\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {Like any human language, C++ provides a way to express concepts. If successf
ul, this medium of expression will be significantly easier and more flexible than the alternatives as problems grow larger and more complex.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can\rquote t just look at C++ as a collection of features; some of the features make no sense in isolation. You can only use the sum of the parts if you are thinking about }{\i design}{
, not simply coding. And to understand C++ in this way, you must understand the problems with C and with programming in general. This book discusses programming problems, why they are problems, an
d the approach C++ has taken to solve such problems. Thus, the set of features I explain in each chapter will be based on the way I see a particular type of problem being solved with the language. In this way I hope to move you, a little at a time, from u
nderstanding C to the point where the C++ mindset becomes your native tongue.
\par Throughout, I\rquote ll be taking the attitude that you want to build a model in your head that allows you to understand the language all the way down to the bare metal; if you encounter a puzzle you\rquote 
ll be able to feed it to your model and deduce the answer. I will try to convey to you the insights which have rearranged my brain to make me start \ldblquote thinking in C++.\rdblquote 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373769}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775931}Prerequisites{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373769}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775931}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the first edition of this book, I decided to assume that s
omeone else had taught you C and that you have at least a reading level of comfort with it. My primary focus was on simplifying what I found difficult \endash 
 the C++ language. In this edition I have added a chapter that is a very rapid introduction to C, assumi
ng that you have some kind of programming experience already. In addition, just as you learn many new words intuitively by seeing them in context in a novel, it\rquote 
s possible to learn a great deal about C from the context in which it is used in the rest of the book.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775932}Thinking in C{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775932}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {For those of you who need a gentler introduction to C than the chapter in this book, I have created with Chuck Allison a CD ROM called \ldblquote Thinking in C: foundations for Java and C++\rdblquote 
 which will introduce you to the aspects of C that are
 necessary for you to move on to C++ or Java (leaving out the nasty bits that C programmers must deal with on a day-to-day basis but that the C++ and Java languages steer you away from). This CD can be ordered at http://www.BruceEckel.com. [Note: the CD w
ill not be available until late Fall 98, at the earliest \endash  watch the Web site for updates]
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373770}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775933}Learning C++{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373770}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775933}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {I clawed my way into C++ from exactly the same position as I expect the readers of this book will: As a C programmer with a very no-nonsense, nuts-and-bo
lts attitude about programming. Worse, my background and experience was in hardware-level embedded programming, where C has often been considered a high-level language and an inefficient overkill for pushing bits around. I discovered later that I wasn
\rquote t even a very good C programmer, hiding my ignorance of structures, }{\b malloc(\~)}{ & }{\b free(\~)}{, }{\b setjmp(\~)}{ & }{\b longjmp(\~)}{, and other \ldblquote sophisticated\rdblquote 
 concepts, scuttling away in shame when the subjects came up in conversation rather than reaching out for new knowledge.
\par When I began my struggle to understand C++, the only decent book was Stroustrup\rquote s self-professed \ldblquote expert\rquote s guide,}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright 
\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ Bjarne Stroustrup}{\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \v\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {Stroustrup, Bjarne}}}{, }{\i The C++ Programming Language}{
, Addison-Wesley, 1986 (first edition).}}}{ \rdblquote  so I was left to simplify the basic concepts on my own. This resulted in my first C++ book,}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright 
\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ }{\i Using C++}{,}{\i  }{ibid.}}}{ which was essentially a brain dump of my experience. That was designed as a reader\rquote s guide, to bring programmers into C and C++ at the same time. Both editions}{
\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ }{\i Using C++}{ and }{\i C++ Inside & Out}{,}{\i  }{ ibid.}}}{
 of the book garnered an enthusiastic response and I still feel it is a valuable resource.
\par At about the same time that }{\i Using C++}{ came out, I began teaching the language. Teaching C++ has become my profession; I\rquote 
ve seen nodding heads, blank faces, and puzzled expressions in audiences all over the world since 1989. As I began giving in-house training with smaller groups of people, I discovered some
thing during the exercises. Even those people who were smiling and nodding were confused about many issues. I found out, by chairing the C++ track at the Software Development Conference for the last three years, that I and other speakers tended to give th
e typical audience too many topics, too fast. So eventually, through both variety in the audience level and the way that I presented the material, I would end up losing some portion of the audience. Maybe it\rquote 
s asking too much, but because I am one of those people resistant to traditional lecturing (and for most people, I believe, such resistance results from boredom), I wanted to try to keep everyone up to speed.
\par For a time, I was creating a number of different presentations in fairly short order. Thus, I en
ded up learning by experiment and iteration (a technique that also works well in C++ program design). Eventually I developed a course using everything I had learned from my teaching experience, one I would be happy giving for a long time. It tackles the l
earning problem in discrete, easy-to-digest steps and for a hands-on seminar (the ideal learning situation), there are exercises following each of the short lessons. 
\par This book developed over the course of two}{\fs24  }{years, and the material in this book has been road-tested in many forms in many different seminars. The feedback that I\rquote 
ve gotten from each seminar has helped me change and refocus the material until I feel it works well as a teaching medium. But it isn\rquote t just a seminar handout \endash  I tried to pack as muc
h information as I could within these pages, and structure it to draw you through, onto the next subject. More than anything, the book is designed to serve the solitary reader, struggling with a new programming language.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373771}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775934}Goals{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373771}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775934}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {My goals in this book are to:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 1. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {Present the material a simple step at a time, so the reader can easily digest each concept before moving on.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 2. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Use examples that are as simple and short as possible. This sometimes prevents me from tackling \ldblquote real-world\rdblquote  problems, \line but I\rquote ve found that beginn
ers are usually happier when they can understand every detail of an example rather than being impressed by the scope of the problem it solves. Also, there\rquote 
s a severe limit to the amount of code that can be absorbed in a classroom situation. For this I will no doubt receive criticism for using \ldblquote toy examples,\rdblquote  but I\rquote 
m willing to accept that in favor of producing something pedagogically useful. Those who want more complex examples can refer to the later chapters of }{\i C++ Inside & Out}{.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ Ibid.}}}{
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 3. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Carefully sequence the presentation of features so that you aren\rquote t seeing something you haven\rquote t been exposed to. Of course, this isn\rquote 
t always possible; in those situations, a brief introductory description will be given.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 4. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Give you what I think is important for you to understand about the language, rather than everything I know. I believe there is an \ldblquote information importance hierarchy,\rdblquote 
 and there are some facts that 95% of programmers will never need to know, but would just confuse people and add to their perception of the complexity of the language \endash 
 and C++ is now considered to be more complex than ADA! To take an example from C, if you memorize the operator precedence table (I never did) you can write clever code. But if you have to think about it, it will confuse the reader/maintain
er of that code. So forget about precedence, and use parentheses when things aren\rquote t clear. This same attitude will be taken with some information in the C++ language, which I think is more important for compiler writers than for programmers.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 5. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Keep each section focused enough so the lecture time \endash  and the time between exercise periods \endash  is small. Not only does this keep the audience\rquote 
 minds more active and involved during a hands-on seminar, but it gives the reader a greater sense of accomplishment.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 6. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Provide the reader with a solid foundation so they can understand the issues well enough to move on to more difficult coursework and books.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 7. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {I\rquote ve endeavored not to use any particular vendor\rquote s version of C++ because, for learning the language, I don\rquote t feel like the de
tails of a particular implementation are as important as the language itself. Most vendors\rquote  documentation concerning their own implementation specifics is adequate.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373772}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775935}Chapters{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373772}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775935}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overview, chapters}}}{C++ is a language where new and different features are bu
ilt on top of an existing syntax. (Because of this it is referred to as a }{\i hybrid}{ object-oriented programming language.) As more people have passed through the learning curve, we\rquote 
ve begun to get a feel for the way C programmers move through the stages of th
e C++ language features. Because it appears to be the natural progression of the C-trained mind, I decided to understand and follow this same path, and accelerate the process by posing and answering the questions that came to me as I learned the language 
and that came from audiences as I taught it.
\par This course was designed with one thing in mind: the way people learn the C++ language. Audience feedback helped me understand which parts were difficult and needed extra illumination. In the areas where I got am
bitious and included too many features all at once, I came to know \endash  through the process of presenting the material \endash  that if you include a lot of new features, you have to explain them all, and the student\rquote 
s confusion is easily compounded. As a result, I\rquote ve taken a great deal of trouble to introduce the features as few at a time as possible; ideally, only one at a time per chapter.
\par The goal, then, is for each chapter to teach a single feature, or a small group of associated features, in such a way that no 
additional features are relied upon. That way you can digest each piece in the context of your current knowledge before moving on. To accomplish this, I leave many C features in place much longer than I would prefer. For example, I would like to be using 
the C++ iostreams IO library right away, instead of using the }{\b printf(\~)}{
 family of functions so familiar to C programmers, but that would require introducing the subject prematurely, and so many of the early chapters carry the C library functions with them. 
This is also true with many other features in the language. The benefit is that you, the C programmer, will not be confused by seeing all the C++ features used before they are explained, so your introduction to the language will be gentle and will mirror 
the way you will assimilate the features if left to your own devices.
\par Here is a brief description of the chapters contained in this book [[ Please note this section will not be updated until all the chapters are in place ]]
\par }{\b (0) The evolution of objects.}{ When projects became too big and too complicated to easily maintain, the \ldblquote software crisis\rdblquote  was born, saying, \ldblquote We can\rquote t get projects done, and if we can they\rquote re too expensive!
\rdblquote  This precipitated a number of responses, which are discussed in this chapter along with the ideas of object-oriented programming (OOP) and how it attempts to solve the software crisis. You\rquote 
ll also learn about the benefits and concerns of adopting the language and suggestions for moving into the world of C++.
\par }{\b (1) Data abstraction.}{ Most features in C++ revolve around this key concept: the ability to create new data types. Not only does this provide superior code organization, but it lays the ground for more powerful OOP abilities. You\rquote 
ll see how this idea is facilitated by the simple act of putting functions inside structures, the details of how to do it, and what kind of code it creates.}{\b 
\par (2) Hiding the implementation. }{You can decide that some of the data and functions in your structure are unavailable to the user of the new type by making them }{\b private}{
. This means you can separate the underlying implementation from the interface that the client programmer sees, and thus allow that implementation to be easily changed without affecting client code. The keyword }{\b class}{ is also introduced as a fanc
ier way to describe a new data type, and the meaning of the word \ldblquote object\rdblquote  is demystified (it\rquote s a variable on steroids).}{\b 
\par (3) Initialization & cleanup. }{One of the most common C errors results from uninitialized variables. The }{\i constructor}{ in C++ allows you to guarantee that variables of your new data type (\ldblquote objects of your class\rdblquote 
) will always be properly initialized. If your objects also require some sort of cleanup, you can guarantee that this cleanup will always happen with the C++ }{\i destructor}{.}{\b 
\par (4) Function overloading & default arguments. }{
C++ is intended to help you build big, complex projects. While doing this, you may bring in multiple libraries that use the same function name, and you may also choose to use the same name with different meanings within a si
ngle library. C++ makes this easy with }{\i function overloading}{
, which allows you to reuse the same function name as long as the argument lists are different. Default arguments allow you to call the same function in different ways by automatically providing default values for some of your arguments.}{\b 
\par (5) Introduction to iostreams.}{ One of the original C++ libraries \endash  the one that provides the essential I/O facility \endash  is called iostreams. Iostreams is intended to replace C\rquote s }{\b stdio.h }{with an I/O library that is eas
ier to use, more flexible, and extensible \endash  you can adapt it to work with your new classes. This chapter teaches you the ins and outs of how to make the best use of the existing iostream library for standard I/O, file I/O, and in-memory formatting.
}{\b 
\par (6) Constants. }{This chapter covers the }{\b const}{ and }{\b volatile}{ keywords that have additional meaning in C++, especially inside classes. It also shows how the meaning of }{\b const}{
 varies inside and outside classes and how to create compile-time constants in classes.}{\b 
\par (7) Inline functions.}{
 Preprocessor macros eliminate function call overhead, but the preprocessor also eliminates valuable C++ type checking. The inline function gives you all the benefits of a preprocessor macro plus all the benefits of a real function call.}{\b 
\par (8) Name control. }{
Creating names is a fundamental activity in programming, and when a project gets large, the number of names can be overwhelming. C++ allows you a great deal of control over names: creation, visibility, placement of storage, and linkage. This
 chapter shows how names are controlled using two techniques. First, the }{\b static}{
 keyword is used to control visibility and linkage, and its special meaning with classes is explored. A far more useful technique for controlling names at the global scope is C++\rquote s }{\b namespace}{
 feature, which allows you to break up the global name space into distinct regions.
\par }{\b (9) References & the copy-constructor.}{ C++ pointers work like C pointers with the additional benefit of stronger C++ type checking. There\rquote s a new way to handle addresses; from Algol and Pascal, C++ lifts the }{\i reference}{
 which lets the compiler handle the address manipulation while you use ordinary notation. You\rquote ll also meet the copy-constructor, which controls the way objects are passed into and out of functions b
y value. Finally, the C++ pointer-to-member is illuminated.}{\b 
\par (10) Operator overloading.}{ This feature is sometimes called \ldblquote syntactic sugar.\rdblquote  It lets you sweeten the syntax for using your type by allowing operators as well as function calls. In this chapter you\rquote 
ll learn that operator overloading is just a different type of function call and how to write your own, especially the sometimes-confusing uses of arguments, return types, and making an operator a member or friend.}{\b 
\par (11) Dynamic object creation.}{ How many planes will an air-traffic system have to handle? How many shapes will a CAD system need? In the general programming problem, you can\rquote 
t know the quantity, lifetime or type of the objects needed by your running program. In this chapter, you\rquote ll learn how C++\rquote s }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ elegantly solve this problem by safely creating objects on the heap.}{\b 
\par (12) Inheritance & composition.}{ Data abstraction allows you to create new types from scratch; with composition and inheritance, you can create new types from existing t
ypes. With composition you assemble a new type using other types as pieces, and with inheritance you create a more specific version of an existing type. In this chapter you\rquote 
ll learn the syntax, how to redefine functions, and the importance of construction and destruction for inheritance & composition.}{\b 
\par (13) Polymorphism & virtual functions.}{ On your own, you might take nine months to discover and understand this cornerstone of OOP. Through small, simple examples you\rquote ll see how to create a family of types with
 inheritance and manipulate objects in that family through their common base class. The }{\b virtual}{ keyword allows you to treat all objects in this family generically, which means the bulk of your code doesn\rquote 
t rely on specific type information. This makes your programs extensible, so building programs and code maintenance is easier and cheaper.
\par }{\b (14) Templates & container classes. }{Inheritance and composition allow you to reuse object code, but that doesn\rquote t solve all your reuse needs. Templates allow you to reuse }{\i source}{
 code by providing the compiler with a way to substitute type names in the body of a class or function. This supports the use of }{\i container class }{
libraries, which are important tools for the rapid, robust development of object-oriented programs. This extensive chapter gives you a thorough grounding in this essential subject.}{\b 
\par (15) Multiple inheritance.}{ This sounds simple at first: A new class is inherited from more than one existing class. However, you can end up with ambiguities and multiple copies of
 base-class objects. That problem is solved with virtual base classes, but the bigger issue remains: When do you use it? Multiple inheritance is only essential when you need to manipulate an object through more than one common base class. This chapter exp
lains the syntax for multiple inheritance, and shows alternative approaches \endash  in particular, how templates solve one common problem. The use of multiple inheritance to repair a \ldblquote damaged\rdblquote 
 class interface is demonstrated as a genuinely valuable use of this feature.}{\b 
\par (16) Exception handling.}{ Error handling has always been a problem in programming. Even if you dutifully return error information or set a flag, the function caller may simply ignore it. Exception handling is a primary feature in C++ that solves this
 problem by allowing you to \ldblquote throw\rdblquote  an object out of your function when a critical error happens. You throw different types of objects for different errors, and the function caller \ldblquote catches\rdblquote 
 these objects in separate error handling routines. If you throw an exception, it cannot be ignored, so you can guarantee that }{\i something}{ will happen in response to your error.}{\b 
\par (17) Run-time type identification. }{Run-time type identification (RTTI) lets you find the exact type of an object when you only have a pointer or reference to the base type. Normally, you\rquote 
ll want to intentionally ignore the exact type of an object and let the virtual function mechanism implement the correct behavior for that type. But occasionally it is very helpful to know the exact type of an objec
t for which you only have a base pointer; often this information allows you to perform a special-case operation more efficiently. This chapter explains what RTTI is for and how to use it.
\par }{\b Appendix}{ }{\b A: Etcetera. }{At this writing, the C++ Standard is unfinished. Although virtually all the features that will end up in the language have been added to the standard, some haven\rquote 
t appeared in all compilers. This appendix briefly mentions some of the other features you should look for in your compiler (or in future releases of your compiler).
\par }{\b Appendix B: Programming guidelines}{. This appendix is a series of suggestions for C++ programming. They\rquote ve been collected over the course of my teaching and programming experience, and also from the insights of other teachers. Many
 of these tips are summarized from the pages of this book.
\par }{\b Appendix C:}{ }{\b Simulating virtual constructors}{. The constructor cannot have any virtual qualities, and this sometimes produces awkward code. This appendix demonstrates two approaches to \ldblquote virtual construction.\rdblquote 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373773}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775936}Exercises{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373773}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775936}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {I\rquote ve discovered that simple exercises are exceptionally useful during a seminar to complete a student\rquote s understanding, so you\rquote ll find a set at the end of each chapter.
\par These are fairly simple, so they can be finished in a reasonable amo
unt of time in a classroom situation while the instructor observes, making sure all the students are absorbing the material. Some exercises are a bit more challenging to keep advanced students entertained. They\rquote 
re all designed to be solved in a short time and are only there to test and polish your knowledge rather than present major challenges (presumably, you\rquote ll find those on your own \endash  or more likely they\rquote ll find you).}{\cf2 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Ref310733137}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373774}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775937}Source code{\*\bkmkend _Ref310733137}
