\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the text of this book, identifiers (function, variable, and class names) will be set in }{\b bold}{. Most keywords will also be set in bold, exce
pt for those keywords which are used so much that the bolding can become tedious, like class and virtual.
\par I use a particular coding style for the examples in this book. It was developed over a number of years, and was inspired by Bjarne Stroustrup\rquote s}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Stroustrup, Bjarne}}}{
 style in his original }{\i The C++ Programming Language}{.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ Ibid.}}}{
 The subject of formatting style is good for hours of hot debate, so I\rquote ll just say I\rquote m not trying to dictate correct style via my examples; I have my own motivation for using the style 
that I do. Because C++ is a free-form programming language, you can continue to use whatever style you\rquote re comfortable with.
\par The programs in this book are files that are automatically extracted from the text of the book, which allows them to be tested to en
sure they work correctly. (I use a special format on the first line of each file to facilitate this extraction; the line begins with the characters \lquote }{\b /\rquote  \lquote /\rquote }{ \lquote }{\b :\rquote  }{
and the file name and path information.) Thus, the code files printed in the book should all wor
k without compiler errors when compiled with an implementation that conforms to Standard C++ (note that not all compilers support all language features). The errors that }{\i should}{ cause compile-time error messages are commented out with the comment }{
\b //! }{so they can be easily discovered and tested using automatic means. Errors discovered and reported to the author will appear first in the electronic version of the book (at }{\b www.BruceEckel.com}{) and later in updates of the book.
\par One of the standards in this book is that all programs will compile and link without errors (although they will sometimes cause warnings). To this end, some of the programs, which only demonstrate a coding example and don\rquote 
t represent stand-alone programs, will have empty }{\b main(\~)}{ functions, like this
