\tqc\tx2610\tqr\tx7380\adjustright \i\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {Chapter 1: Introduction to Objects\tab }{\field{\*\fldinst {\cs39  PAGE }}{\fldrslt {\cs39\lang1024 62}}}{
\par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s38\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\tqc\tx2610\tqr\tx7380\adjustright \i\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\tab \tab }{\field{\*\fldinst {\cs39  PAGE }}{\fldrslt {\cs39\lang1024 29}}}{
\par }}\pard\plain \s1\fi-1080\li990\sa360\sl240\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \cbpat8 \f4\fs96\cf1\kerning18\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373781}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775944}1: Introduction \line to objects{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373781}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775944}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {The genesis of the computer revolution was in a machine. The genesis of our programming languages thus tends to look like that machine. 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {But computers are not so much machines as they are mind amplification tools (\ldblquote bicycles for the mind,\rdblquote 
 as Steve Jobs is fond of saying) and a different kind of expressive medium. As a result, the tools are beginning to look less like machines and more like parts of our minds, and a
lso like other expressive mediums such as writing, painting, sculpture, animation and filmmaking. Object-oriented programming is part of this movement toward the computer as an expressive medium.
\par This chapter will introduce you to the basic concepts of obj
ect-oriented programming (OOP), including an overview of OOP development methods. This chapter, and this book, assume you have had experience in some programming language, although not necessarily C. If you feel you need more preparation in programming an
d the syntax of C before tackling this book, you may want to consider MindView\rquote s \ldblquote Thinking in C: Foundations for C++ and Java\rdblquote  training CD ROM, available at http://www.MindView.net. 
\par This chapter is background and supplementary material. Many people do not 
feel comfortable wading into object-oriented programming without understanding the big picture first. Thus, there are many concepts that are introduced here to give you a solid overview of OOP. However, many other people don\rquote 
t get the big picture concepts until they\rquote ve seen some of the mechanics first; these people may become bogged down and lost without some code to get their hands on. If you\rquote 
re part of this latter group and are eager to get to the specifics of the language, feel free to jump past this chapter \endash 
 skipping it at this point will not prevent you from writing programs or learning the language. However, you will want to come back here eventually, to fill in your knowledge so that you can understand why objects are important and how to design with
 them.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc375545188}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018385}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775945}The progress of abstraction
{\*\bkmkend _Toc375545188}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018385}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775945}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart mark}{\*\bkmkend mark}All programming languages provide abstractions. It can be argued that the complexity of the problems you\rquote 
re able to solve is directly related to the kind and quality of }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {abstraction}}}{abstraction. By \ldblquote kind\rdblquote  I mean \ldblquote what is it that you are abstracting?\rdblquote  A}{s
}{sembly language is a small abstraction of the underlying machine. Many so-called \ldblquote imperative\rdblquote  languages that followed (such as FORTRAN, BASIC, and C) were abstractions of assembly language. These languages are big improv}{e}{
ments over assembly language, but their primary abstraction still requires you to think in terms of the structure of the computer rather than the structure of the problem you are trying to solve. The programmer must establish the association between th
e machine model (in the \ldblquote solution space,\rdblquote  which is the place where you\rquote re modeling that problem, such as a computer) and the model of the problem that is actually being solved (in the \ldblquote problem space,\rdblquote 
 which is the place where the problem act}{u}{ally exists). 
The effort required to perform this mapping, and the fact that it is extrinsic to the programming language, produces programs that are difficult to write and expensive to maintain, and as a side effect created the entire \ldblquote programming methods
\rdblquote  industry.
\par The alternative to modeling the machine is to model the problem you\rquote re tr}{y}{ing to solve. Early languages such as LISP and APL chose particular views of the world (\ldblquote all problems are ultimately lists\rdblquote  or \ldblquote 
all problems are algorithmic\rdblquote ). PROLOG casts all problems into chains of decisions. Languages have been cr}{e}{
ated for constraint-based programming and for programming exclusively by manipulating graphical symbols. (The latter proved to be too restrictive.) Each of these approaches is a good solution to the particular class of problem they\rquote 
re designed to solve, but when you step outside of that domain they b}{e}{come awkward. 
\par The object-oriented approach goes a step further by providing tools for the programmer to represent elements in the }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {problem space}}}{problem
 space. This representation is general enough that the programmer is not constrained to any particular type of problem. We refer to the elements in the problem space and their re}{p}{resentations in the solution space as}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object}}}{ \ldblquote objects.\rdblquote  (Of course, you will also need other objects that don\rquote 
t have problem-space analogs.) The idea is that the program is allowed to adapt itself to the lingo of the problem by adding new types of objects, so when you read the code describing the solution, you\rquote re reading wo
rds that also express the problem. This is a more flexible and po}{w}{erful language abstraction than what we\rquote 
ve had before. Thus OOP allows you to describe the problem in terms of the problem, rather than in terms of the computer where the solution will run. There\rquote 
s still a connection back to the computer, though. Each object looks quite a bit like a little computer; it has a state, and it has operations that you can ask it to perform. However, this doesn\rquote t seem like such a bad analogy to o}{b}{
jects in the real world; they all have characteristics and behaviors. 
\par Some language designers have decided that object-oriented programming i}{t}{self is not adequate to easily solve all programming problems, and advocate the combination of various approaches into }{\i multiparadigm}{ programming languages.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 
\chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ See }{\i Multiparadigm Programming in Leda}{ by Timothy Budd (Addison-Wesley 1995).}}}{
\par Alan Kay summarized five basic characteristics of }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Smalltalk}}}{Smalltalk, the first su}{c}{
cessful object-oriented language and one of the languages upon which C++ is based. These characteristics represent a pure approach to object-oriented programming:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\b\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls253\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls253\adjustright {\b Everything is an object.}{
 Think of an object as a fancy var}{i}{able; it stores data, but you can \ldblquote make requests\rdblquote  to that object, asking it to perform o}{p}{erations on itself. In theory, you can take any conceptual component in the problem you\rquote 
re trying to solve (dogs, buildings, services, etc.) and represent it as an object in your program.}{\kerning18 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\b\fs20\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls253\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls253\adjustright {\b\kerning18 
A program is a bunch of objects telling each other what to do by sending messages}{\kerning18 . To make a request of an object, you \ldblquote send a message\rdblquote  to that object. 
More concretely, you can think of a message as a request to call a function that belongs to a particular object.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\b\fs20\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls253\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls253\adjustright {\b\kerning18 
Each object has its own memory made up of other o}{\b\kerning18 b}{\b\kerning18 jects}{\kerning18 . Put another way, you create a new kind of object by making a pac}{\kerning18 k}{\kerning18 age containing existi
ng objects. Thus, you can build complexity in a program while hiding it behind the simpli}{\kerning18 c}{\kerning18 ity of objects.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\b\fs20\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 4.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls253\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls253\adjustright {\b\kerning18 
Every object has a type}{\kerning18 . Using the parlance, each object is an }{\i\kerning18 instance}{\kerning18  of a }{\i\kerning18 class}{, where \ldblquote class\rdblquote  is synonymous with \ldblquote type}{\kerning18 .\rdblquote 
 The most important distinguishing characteristic of a class is \ldblquote what messages can you send to it?\rdblquote 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\b\fs20\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls253\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls253\adjustright {\b\kerning18 
All objects of a particular type can receive the same messages}{\kerning18 . This is actually a very loaded statement, as you will see later. Because an object of type \ldblquote circle\rdblquote  is also an object of type \ldblquote shape,\rdblquote 
 a circle is guaranteed to receive shape me}{\kerning18 s}{\kerning18 sages. This means you can write code that talks to shapes and automatically handle anything that fits the description of a shape. This }{\i\kerning18 substitutability}{\kerning18 
 is one of the most powerful concepts in OOP.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc375545189}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018386}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775946}An object has an interface
{\*\bkmkend _Toc375545189}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018386}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775946}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Aristotle was probably the first to begin a careful study of the concept of }{\i type}{; he spoke of things such as \ldblquote the class of fishes and the class of birds.\rdblquote 
 The idea that all objects, while being unique, are also part of a class of o}{b}{jects that have characteristics and behaviors in common was directly used in the first object-oriented language, Simula-67, with its fundamental keyword }{\b class}{
 that introduces a new type into a program.
\par Simula, as its name implies, was created for developing sim}{u}{lations such as the classic \ldblquote bank teller problem}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {
\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ You can find an interesting implementation of this problem later in the book.}}}{.\rdblquote  In this, you have a bunch of tellers, customers, accounts, transactions, units of money \endash  a lot of \ldblquote objects.\rdblquote 
 Objects that are identical except for their state during a program\rquote s execution are grouped together into \ldblquote classes of objects\rdblquote  and that\rquote s where the keyword }{\b class}{
 came from. Creating abstract data types (classes) is a fundamental concept in object-oriented programming. Abstract data types work almost exactly like built-in types: You can create variables of a type (called }{\i objects }{or }{\i instances}{
 in object-oriented parlance) and manipulate those variables (called }{\i sending messages}{ or }{\i requests}{; you send a message and the object figures out what to do with it). The members (elements) of each class s
hare some commonality: every account has a balance, every teller can accept a deposit, etc. At the same time, each member has its own state, each account has a different balance, each teller has a name. Thus the tellers, customers, a}{c}{
counts, transactions, etc., can each be represented with a unique entity in the computer program. This entity is the object, and each object belongs to a pa}{r}{ticular class that defines its characteristics and behaviors.
\par So, although what we really do in object-oriented programming is create new data types, virtually all object-oriented programming languages use the \ldblquote class\rdblquote  keyword. When you see the word \ldblquote type\rdblquote  think \ldblquote 
class\rdblquote  and vice versa}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{
 Some people make a distinction, stating that type determines the interface while class is a particular implementation of that interface.}}}{.
\par Since a class describes a set of objects that have identical characteristics (data element
s) and behaviors (functionality), a class is really a data type because a floating point number, for example, also has a set of characteristics and behaviors. The difference is that a programmer defines a class to fit a problem rather than being forced to
 
use an existing data type that was designed to represent a unit of storage in a machine. You extend the programming language by adding new data types specific to your needs. The programming system welcomes the new classes and gives them all the care and t
ype-checking that it gives to built-in types.
\par The object-oriented approach is not limited to building simulations. Whether or not you agree that any program is a simulation of the system you\rquote re designing, the use of OOP techniques can easily reduce a large set of problems to a simple solution.

\par Once a class is established, you can make as many objects of that class as you like, and then manipulate those objects as if they are the el}{e}{ments that exist in the problem you are trying to solve. Indeed, one of the c
hallenges of object-oriented programming is to create a one-to-one mapping between the elements in the problem space and objects in the solution space.
\par But how do you get an object to do useful work for you? There must be a way to make a request of that ob
ject so it will do something, such as complete a transaction, draw something on the screen or turn on a switch. And each object can satisfy only certain requests. The requests you can make of an object are defined by its }{\i interface}{
, and the type is what determines the interface. A simple example might be a representation of a light bulb: 
