\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The interface establishes }{\i what}{ requests you can make for a particular object. However, there must be code somewhere to satisfy that request. This, along with the hidden data, comprises the }{
\i implementation}{. From a procedural pr}{o}{gramming standpoint, it\rquote s not that complicated. A type has a function assoc}{i}{ated with each possible request, and when you make a particular request to an object, that function is ca
lled. This process is usually summarized by saying that you \ldblquote send a message\rdblquote  (make a request) to an object, and the object fi}{g}{ures out what to do with that message (it executes code).
\par Here, the name of the type/class is }{\b Light}{, the name of this particular }{\b Light }{object is }{\b lt}{,}{\b  }{and the requests that you can make of a }{\b Light}{
 object are to turn it on, turn it off, make it brighter or make it dimmer. You create a }{\b Light }{object by simply declaring a name (}{\b lt}{) for that identifier. To send a message to the object, you 
state the name of the object and connect it to the message request with a period (dot). From the standpoint of the user of a pre-defined class, that\rquote s pretty much all there is to programming with objects.
\par The diagram shown above follows the format of the }{\i Unified Modeling Language}{
 (UML). Each class is represented by a box, with the type name in the top portion of the box, any data members that you care to describe in the middle portion of the box, and the }{\i member functions}{ (the functions that belong to this o
bject, which receive any messages you send to that object) in the bottom portion of the box. The \lquote +\rquote 
 signs before the member functions indicate they are public. Very often, only the name of the class and the public member functions are shown in UML design diagrams, and so the middle portion is not shown. If you\rquote 
re only interested in the class name, then the bottom portion doesn\rquote t need to be shown, either.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc375545190}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018387}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775947}The hidden implementation
