\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It is helpful to break up the playing field into }{\i class creators}{ (those who create new data types) and }{\i client programmers}{\cs23\i\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain 
\s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ I\rquote m indebted to my friend Scott Meyers for this term.}}}{
 (the class consumers who use the data types in their applications). The goal of the client programmer is to co}{l}{lect a toolbox full of classes to use for rapid application development. The goal of the class creat
or is to build a class that exposes only what\rquote s necessary to the client programmer and keeps everything else hidden. Why? Because if it\rquote s hidden, the client programmer can\rquote 
t use it, which means that the class creator can change the hidden portion at will w
ithout worrying about the impact to anyone else. The hidden portions usually represent the tender insides of an object that could easily be corrupted by a careless or uninformed client programmer, so hiding the implementation reduces program bugs. The con
cept of implementation hiding cannot be overemphasized.
\par In any relationship it\rquote s important to have boundaries that are respected by all parties involved. When you create a library, you establish a relationship with the client}{\i  }{programmer}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {client programmer}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {programmer, client}}}{, who is also a programmer, but one who is putting together an application by using your library, possibly to build a bigger library.
\par If all the members of a class are available to everyone, then the client pr}{o}{grammer can do anything with that class and there\rquote 
s no way to enforce any rules. Even though you might really prefer that the client programmer not directly manipulate some of the members of your class, without access control there\rquote s no way to prevent it. Everything\rquote s naked to the world.

\par So the first reason for access control is to keep client programmers\rquote  hands off portions they shouldn\rquote t touch \endash 
 parts that are necessary for the internal machinations of the data type but not part of the interface that users need to solve their particular problems. This is actually a service to users because they can easily see what\rquote 
s important to them and what they can ignore.
\par The second reason for access control is to allow the library designer to change the internal workings of the cla
ss without worrying about how it will affect the client programmer. For example, you might implement a particular class in a simple fashion to ease development, and then later discover you need to rewrite it in order to make it run faster. If the interfac
e and implementation are clearly separated and protected, you can easily accomplish this and require only a relink by the user.}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {interface\: separation of interface and implementation}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {separation of interface and implementation}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {implementation\: and interface, separation}}}{
\par C++ uses three explicit keywords to set the boundaries in a class: }{\b public}{, }{\b private}{, }{\b protected}{. Their use and meaning are quite straightforward. These }{\i access specifiers}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {access\: specifiers}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {specifier\: access specifiers}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {controlling access}}}{
determine who can use the definitions that follow. }{\b public}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {public}}}{means the following definitions are available to everyone. The }{\b private}{ }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {private}}}{keyword, on the other hand, means that no one can access those definitions except you, the creator of the type, inside function members of that type. }{\b private}{ is a brick wall b}{e}{
tween you and the client programmer. If someone tries to access a }{\b private}{ member, they\rquote ll get a compile-time error. }{\b protected}{ acts just like }{\b private}{, with the exception that an inheriting class has access to }{\b protected}{
 members, but not }{\b private}{ members. Inheritance will be introduced shortly.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc375545191}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018388}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775948}Reusing \line the implementation
{\*\bkmkend _Toc375545191}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018388}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775948}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Once a class has been created and tested, it should (ideally) represent a u
seful unit of code. It turns out that this reusability is not nearly so easy to achieve as many would hope; it takes experience and insight to produce a good design. But once you have such a design, it begs to be reused. Code reuse is one of the greatest 
advantages that object-oriented programming languages provide.
\par The simplest way to reuse a class is to just use an object of that class directly, but you can also place an object of that class inside a new class. We call this \ldblquote creating a member object.\rdblquote  You
r new class can be made up of any number and type of other objects, whatever is necessary to achieve the functionality desired in your new class. This concept is called }{\i co}{\i m}{\i position}{ (or more generally, }{\i aggregation}{
), since you are composing a new class from existing classes. Som}{e}{times composition is referred to as a \ldblquote has-a\rdblquote  relationship, as in \ldblquote a car has an engine.\rdblquote 
\par }\pard\plain \s51\sb240\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\lang1024 {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1716\shptop0\shpright5976\shpbottom838\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr1\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz10\shplockanchor\shplid1167
