\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There\rquote s a certain debate that can occur about inheritance: Should inheritance override }{\i only}{ base-class functions (and not add new member functions that aren\rquote 
t in the base class)? This would mean that the derived type is }{\i exactly}{ the same type as the base class since it has exactly the same interface. As a r}{e}{
sult, you can exactly substitute an object of the derived class for an object of the base class. This can be thought of as }{\i pure substitution}{, and it\rquote s often referred to as the }{\i substitution principle}{
. In a sense, this is the ideal way to treat inheritance. We often refer to the relationship between the base class and derived classes in this case as an }{\i is-a}{ relationship, because you can say \ldblquote a circle }{\i is a}{ shape.\rdblquote 
 A test for inheritance is whether you can state the is-a relationship about the classes and have it make sense.
\par There are times when you must add new interface elements to a d}{e}{rived type, thus extending the interface and creating a new type. The new type can still be substituted for the base type, but the substitution isn\rquote t pe}{r}{
fect because your new functions are not accessible from the base type. This can be described as an }{\i is-like-a}{ relationship; the new type has the inte}{r}{face of the old type but it also contains other functions, so you can\rquote t really say it
\rquote s exactly the same. For example, consider an air conditioner. Suppose your house is wired with all the controls for cooling; that is, it has an interface that allows you to control cooling. Imagine 
that the air conditioner breaks down and you replace it with a heat pump, which can both heat and cool. The heat pump }{\i is-like-an}{
 air conditioner, but it can do more. Because the control system of your house is designed only to control cooling, it is restri
cted to communication with the cooling part of the new object. The interface of the new object has been extended, and the existing system doesn\rquote t know about anything except the original interface.
