\par }\pard\plain \sb120\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you see the substitution principle it\rquote s easy to feel like this approach (pure substitution) is the only way to do things, and in fact it }{\i is}{
 nice if your design works out that way. But you\rquote ll find that there are times when it\rquote s equally clear that you must add new functions to the interface of a derived class. With inspection both cases should be reasonably obvious.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc375545195}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018392}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775951}Interchangeable objects \line 
with polymorphism{\*\bkmkend _Toc375545195}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018392}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775951}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When dealing with type hierarchies, you often want to treat an object not as the specific type that it is but instead as its base type. This allows you to write code that doesn\rquote 
t depend on specific types. In the shape example, functions manipulate generic shapes without respect to whether they\rquote re circles, squares, tr
iangles, and so on. All shapes can be drawn, erased, and moved, so these functions simply send a message to a shape object; they don\rquote t worry about how the object copes with the message.
\par Such code is unaffected by the addition of new types, and adding new types is the most common way to extend an object-oriented program to handle new situations. For example, you can derive a new subtype of shape called pentagon}{\i  }{
without modifying the functions that deal only with generic shapes. This ability to extend a program easily by deriving new subtypes is important because it greatly improves designs while reducing the cost of software maintenance.
\par There\rquote s a problem, however, with attempting to treat derived-type objects as their generic base types (circles as shapes, b
icycles as vehicles, cormorants as birds, etc.). If a function is going to tell a generic shape to draw itself, or a generic vehicle to steer, or a generic bird to fly, the compiler cannot know at compile-time precisely what piece of code will be executed
. That\rquote s the whole point \endash  when the message is sent, the programmer doesn\rquote t w}{\i ant}{
 to know what piece of code will be executed; the draw function can be applied equally to a circle, square, or triangle, and the object will execute the proper code depending on its specific type. If you don\rquote 
t have to know what piece of code will be executed, then when you add a new subtype, the code it executes can be different without changes to the function call. Therefore, the compiler cannot know precisely what piece of code
 is executed, so what does it do? For example, in the following diagram the }{\b BirdController}{ object just works with generic }{\b Bird}{ objects, and does not know what exact type they are. This is convenient from }{\b BirdController}{\rquote 
s perspective, because it doesn\rquote t have to write special code to determine the exact type of }{\b Bird}{ it\rquote s working with, or that }{\b Bird}{\rquote s behavior. So how does it happen that, when }{\b fly(\~)}{
 is called while ignoring the specific type of }{\b Bird}{, the right behavior will occur?
\par }\pard\plain \s51\sb240\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\lang1024 {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft240\shptop0\shpright7560\shpbottom2695\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr1\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz15\shplockanchor\shplid1173
