6c65643f3f002500250013001300250025002500250025002500220013003000250025002500130013000f0022001600160013000f0022001300220025000f000f00250025002500030000000000}\par}}}{The answer is the primary tw
ist in object-oriented programming: The compiler cannot make a function call in the traditional sense. The function call generated by a non-OOP compiler causes what is called }{\i early binding}{, a term you may not have heard before because you\rquote 
ve never thought 
about it any other way. It means the compiler generates a call to a specific function name, and the linker resolves this call to the absolute address of the code to be executed. In OOP, the program cannot determine the address of the code until runtime, s
o some other scheme is necessary when a message is sent to a generic object.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To solve the problem, object-oriented languages use the concept of }{\i late binding}{. When you send a message to an object, the code being called isn\rquote t determined until runtime. The com
piler does ensure that the function exists and it performs type checking on the arguments and return value (a language where this isn\rquote t true is called }{\i weakly typed}{), but it doesn\rquote t know the exact code to execute.
\par To perform late binding, the compiler inser
ts a special bit of code in lieu of the absolute call. This code calculates the address of the function body, using information stored in the object itself (this process is covered in great detail in Chapter XX). Thus, each object can behave differently a
ccording to the contents of that special bit of code. When you send a message to an object, the object actually does figure out what to do with that message.
\par You state that you want a function to have the flexibility of late-binding properties using the keyword }{\b virtual}{. You don\rquote t need to understand the mechanics of }{\b virtual}{ to use it, but without it you can\rquote 
t do object-oriented programming in C++. In C++, you must reme}{m}{ber to add the }{\b virtual}{ keyword because by default member functions are }{\i not}{ dynamically boun
d. Virtual functions allow you to express the differences in behavior of classes in the same family. Those differences are what cause polymorphic behavior.
\par Consider the shape example. The family of classes (all based on the same uniform interface) was diagrammed earlier in the chapter.
\par To demonstrate polymorphism, we want to write a single piece of code that ignores the specific details of type and talks only to the base class. That code is }{\i decoupled}{ from type-specific information, and thus is simpler to write and easier to u}{n}
{derstand. And, if a new type \endash  a }{\b Hexagon}{, for example \endash }{\b  }{is added through inheritance, the code you write will work just as well for the new type of }{\b Shape}{ as it did on the existing types. Thus the program is }{\i 
extensible}{.
\par If you write a function in C++ (as you will soon learn how to do):
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void doStuff(Shape& s) \{
\par   s.erase();
\par   // ...
\par   s.draw();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This function speaks to any }{\b Shape}{, so it is independent of the specific type of object it\rquote s drawing and erasing (the \lquote }{\b &}{\rquote  means \ldblquote take the address
 of the object that\rquote s passed to }{\b doStuff(\~)}{, but it\rquote s not important that you understand the details of that right now). If in some other part of the program we use the }{\b doStuff(\~)}{ function:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Circle c;
\par Triangle t;
\par Line l;
\par doStuff(c);
\par doStuff(t);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {doStuff(l);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The calls to }{\b doStuff(\~) }{automatically work right, regardless of the exact type of the o}{b}{ject. 
\par This is actually a pretty amazing trick. Consider the line:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {doStuff(c);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {What\rquote s happening here is that a }{\b Circle}{ is being passed into a function that\rquote s expecting a }{\b Shape}{. Since a }{\b Circle}{ }{\i is}{ a }{\b Shape}{ it can be treated as one by 
}{\b doStuff(\~)}{. That is, any message that }{\b doStuff(\~)}{ can send to a }{\b Shape}{, a }{\b Circle}{ can accept. So it is a completely safe and logical thing to do.
\par We call this process of treating a derived type as though it were its base type }{\i upcasting}{. The name }{\i cast }{is used in the sense of casting into a mold and the }{\i up}{
 comes from the way the inheritance diagram is typically arranged, with the base type at the top and the derived classes fanning out downward. Thus, casting to a base type is moving up the inheritance diagram: \ldblquote upcasting.\rdblquote 
