The use cases produce the requirements specifications, by determining all the interactions that the user may have with the system. You try to discover a full set of use-cases for your system, and once you\rquote 
ve done that you have the core of what the system is supposed to do. The nice thing about focusing on use-cases is that they always bring you back to the essentials and keep you from drifting off into issues that aren\rquote 
t critical for getting the job done. That is, if you have a full set of use-cases you can describe your system and move onto the next phase. You probably won\rquote t get it all figured out perfectly at this phase, but that\rquote s OK. Everything will r}
{e}{veal itself in the fullness of time, and if you demand a perfect system specif}{i}{cation at this point you\rquote ll get stuck.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
If you get stuck, you can kick-start this phase by describing the system in a few paragraphs and then looking for nouns and verbs. The nouns become either actors or parts of use cases (or even entire use cases by themselves), and the ver
bs become the interactions between the two. You\rquote ll be surprised at how useful a tool this can be; sometimes it will accomplish the lion\rquote s share of the work for you.
\par Use-cases will identify key features in the system that will reveal some of the fundamental classes you\rquote ll be using. For example, if you\rquote 
re in the fireworks business, you may want to identify Workers, Firecrackers, and Customers; more specifically you\rquote ll need Chemists, Assemblers, and Handlers; AmateurFirecrackers and ProfessionalFirecrackers; B
uyers and Spectators. Even more specifically, you could identify YoungSpectators, OldSpectators, TeenageSpectators, and ParentSpectators.
\par Although it\rquote s a black art, at this point some kind of }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {scheduling, software development}}}{scheduling can be quite useful. You now have an overview of what you\rquote re building so you
\rquote ll probably be able to get some idea of how long it will take. A lot of factors come into play here: if you estimate a long schedule then the company might not d}{e}{cide to build it, or a manager might have already decided how long the pro}{j}{
ect should take and will try to influence your estimate. But it\rquote s best to have an honest schedule from the beginning and deal with the tough decisions early. There have been a lot of attempts to come up with ac
curate scheduling techniques (like techniques to predict the stock market), but probably the best approach is to rely on your experience and intuition. Get a gut feeling for how long it will really take, then double that and add 10 percent. Your gut feeli
ng is probably correct; you }{\i can}{ get something working in that time. The \ldblquote doubling\rdblquote  will turn that into something decent, and the 10 percent will deal with final polis}{h}{ing and details}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote 
\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{
 My personal take on this has changed lately. Doubling and adding 10 percent will give you a reasonably accurate estimate (assuming there are not too many wild-card factors), but you still have to work quite dilligently to finish in 
that time. If you actually want time to really make it elegant and to enjoy yourself in the process, the correct multiplier is more like three or four times, I believe. }}}{. However you want to explain it, and regardless of the moans and manipula
tions that happen when you reveal such a schedule, it just seems to work out that way.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018412}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775957}Phase 2: How will we build it?{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018412}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775957}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In this phase you must come up with a design that describes what the classes look like and how they will interact. An excellent tool in determining classes and interactions is the }{\i 
Class-Responsibility-Collaboration}{ (CRC) card. Part of the value of this technique is that it\rquote s so low-tech: you start out with a set of blank 3\rdblquote  by 5\rdblquote  cards, and you write on them. Each card represents a single cl
ass, and on the card you write:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls262\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls262\adjustright {The name of the class. It\rquote 
s important that this name capture the essence of what the class does, so that it makes sense at a glance.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls262\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls262\adjustright {The \ldblquote responsibilities
\rdblquote  of the class: what it should do. This can typically be summarized by
 just stating the names of the member functions (since those names should be descriptive in a good design), but it does not preclude other notes. If you need to seed the process, look at the problem from a lazy programmer\rquote 
s standpoint: What objects would you like to magically appear to solve your problem?
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls262\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls262\adjustright {The \ldblquote collaborations
\rdblquote  of the class: what other classes does it interact with? \ldblquote Interact\rdblquote  is an intentionally broad term; it could mean aggregation or simply that some other object exists that will perform s
ervices for an object of the class. Collaborations should also consider the audience for this class. For example, if you create a class Firecracker, who is going to observe it, a Chemist or a Spectator? The former will want to know what chemicals go into 
the construction, and the latter will respond to the colors and shapes released when it explodes.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {You may feel like the cards should be bigger because of all the information you\rquote d like to get on them, but they are intentionally small, not only to keep your 
classes small but also to keep you from getting into too much detail too early. If you can\rquote t fit all you need to know about a class on a small card, the class is too complex (either you\rquote 
re getting too detailed, or you should create more than one class). Th
e ideal class should be understood at a glance. The idea of CRC cards is to assist you in coming up with a first cut on the design, so that you can get the big picture and refine the design.
\par One of the great benefits of CRC cards is in communication. It\rquote s 
best done real-time, in a group, without computers. Each person takes responsibility for several classes (which at first have no names or other information), and you run a live simulation by going through your use-cases and deciding what messages go to wh
ich objects to satisfy each use case. As you go through this process, you discover the classes you need along with their responsibilities and collaborations, and you fill out the cards as you do this. When you\rquote 
ve moved through all the use cases, you should have a fairly complete first cut of your design.
\par Before I began using CRC cards, the most successful consulting experiences I had when coming up with an initial design involved standing in front of a team, who hadn\rquote t built an OOP project before, and drawin
g objects on a whiteboard. We talked about how the objects should communicate with each other, and erased some of them and replaced them with other objects (effectively, I was managing all the \ldblquote CRC cards\rdblquote 
 on the whiteboard). The team (who knew what the project was supposed to do) actually created the design; they \ldblquote owned\rdblquote 
 the design rather than having it given to them. All I was doing was guiding the process by asking the right questions, trying out the assumptions and taking the feedback from the team to mo
dify those assumptions. The true beauty of the process was that the team learned how to do object-oriented design not by reviewing abstract examples, but by working on the one design that was most interesting to them at that moment: theirs.
\par Once you\rquote ve come up with a set of CRC cards, you may want to create a more formal description of your design using UML. There are a fair number of books on UML, and you can get the specification at }{\i http://www.rational.com}{. You don\rquote 
t need to use UML, but it can be helpful, especially if you want to put a diagram up on the wall for everyone to ponder, which is a good idea. An alternative to UML is a textual d}{e}{
scription of the objects and their interfaces, but this can be limiting.
\par UML also provides a diagramming notation fo
r describing the dynamic model of your system, for situations where the state transitions of a system or subsystem are dominant enough that they need their own diagrams (such as in a control system), and for describing the data structures, for systems or 
subsystems where data is a dominant factor (such as a database).
\par You\rquote ll know you\rquote re done with phase 2 when you have described the objects and their i}{n}{terfaces. Well, most of them \endash  there are usually a few that slip through the cracks and don\rquote t make themselv
es known until phase 3. But that\rquote s OK. All you are concerned with is that you eventually discover all of your objects. It\rquote s nice to discover them early in the process but OOP provides enough structure so that it\rquote 
s not so bad if you discover them later. In fact, the design of an object tends to happen in five stages, throughout the process of program development.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018413}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373799}Five stages of object design{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373799}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The design life of an object is not limited to the period of time when you\rquote re writing the program. Instead, the design of an object appears over a sequence of stages. It\rquote 
s helpful to have this perspective because you stop expecting perfection right away; instead, you realize that the understanding of what an object does and what it should look like happens over time. This
 view also applies to the design of various types of programs; the pattern for a particular type of program emerges through struggling again and again with that problem (design patterns are covered in Chapter XX). Objects, too, have their patterns that em
erge through understanding, use, and reuse.
\par }{\b 1. Object discovery. }{This stage occurs during the initial analysis of a program. Objects may be discovered by looking for external factors and boundaries, duplication of elements in the system, and the smallest co
nceptual units. Some objects are obvious if you already have a set of class libraries. Commonality between classes suggesting base classes and inheritance may appear right away, or later in the design process.
\par }{\b 2. Object assembly. }{As you\rquote re building an object you\rquote ll discover the need for new members that didn\rquote t appear during discovery. The internal needs of the object may require new classes to support it.
\par }{\b 3. System construction. }{Once again, more requirements for an object may appear at this later stage. As 
you learn, you evolve your objects. The need for communication and interconnection with other objects in the system may change the needs of your classes or require new classes. For example, here you may discover the need for facilitator or helper classes,
 such as a linked list, that contain little or no state information and simply help other classes to function.
\par }{\b 4. System extension. }{As you add new features to a system you may discover that your previous design doesn\rquote t support easy system extension. With th
is new information, you can restructure parts of the system, very possibly adding new classes or class hierarchies.
\par }{\b 5. Object reuse. }{This is the real stress test for a class. If someone tries to reuse it in an entirely new situation, they\rquote ll probably disco
ver some shortcomings. As you change a class to adapt to more new programs, the general principles of the class will become clearer, until you have a truly reusable type.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Guidelines for object development
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {These stages suggest some guidelines when thinking about developing your classes:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls256\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls256\adjustright {
Let a specific problem generate a class, then let the class grow and mature during the solution of other problems.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls256\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls256\adjustright {
Remember, discovering the classes you need (and their interfaces) is the majority of the system design. If you already had those classes, this would be an easy project.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls256\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls256\adjustright {Don\rquote 
t force yourself to know everything at the beginning; learn as you go. That\rquote s the way it will happen anyway.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 4.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls256\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls256\adjustright {
Start programming; get something working so you can prove or disprove your design. Don\rquote t fear procedural-style spaghetti code \endash  classes partition the problem and help control anarchy and entropy. Bad classes do not break good classes.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls256\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls256\adjustright {
Always keep it simple. Little clean objects with obvious utility are better than big complicated interfaces. When decision points come up, use a modified Occam\rquote 
s Razor approach: Consider the choices and select the one that is simplest, because simple classes are almost always best. You can always start small and simple and expand the class interface when you understand it better, but as time goes on, it\rquote 
s difficult to remove elements from a class.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775958}Phase 3: Build it{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018413}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775958}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This is the initial conversion from the rough design to a compiling body of code that can be tested, and especially that will prove or disprove you
r design. This is not a one-pass process, but rather the beginning of a series of writes and rewrites, as you\rquote ll see in phase 4.
\par If you\rquote re reading this book you\rquote re probably a programmer, so now we\rquote re at the part you\rquote ve been trying to get to. By following a plan \endash  no matter how si}{m}{ple and brief \endash 
 and coming up with design structure before coding, you\rquote ll di}{s}{cover that things fall together far more easily than if you dive in and start hacking, and you\rquote ll also realize a great deal of satisfaction. Getting code 
to run and do what you want is fulfilling, and can easily become an obsession. But it\rquote s my exper}{i}{ence that coming up with an elegant solution is deeply satisfying at an e}{n}{tirely different level; it feels closer to art than technology. And }
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {elegance, in programming}}}{elegance always pays off; it\rquote s not a frivolous pursuit. Not only does it give you a pr}{o}{gram that\rquote s easier to build and debug, but it\rquote 
s also easier to understand and maintain, and that\rquote s where the financial value lies.
\par After you build the system and get it running, it\rquote s important to do a reality check, and here\rquote s where the requirements analysis and system specification comes in. Go through your program and make sure that all the requir}{e}{
ments are checked off, and that all the use-cases work the way they\rquote re d}{e}{scribed (an even better approach is to use the requirements analysis and use-cases to generate test code). Now you\rquote re done. Or are you?
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018414}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775959}Phase 4: Iteration{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018414}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775959}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This is the point in the development cycle that has traditionally been called \ldblquote maintenance,\rdblquote  a catch-all term that can mean everything from \ldblquote 
getting it to work the way it was really supposed to in the first place\rdblquote  to \ldblquote adding features that the customer forgot to mention\rdblquote  to the more traditional \ldblquote fixing the bugs that show up\rdblquote  and \ldblquote 
adding new features as the need arises.\rdblquote  So many misconceptions have been applied to the term \ldblquote maintenance\rdblquote  that it has taken on a slightly deceiving quality, partly because it suggests that you\rquote 
ve actually built a pristine program and all you need to do is change parts, oil it and keep it from rusting. Perhaps there\rquote s a better term to describe what\rquote s going on.
\par The term is }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iteration, during software development}}}{\i iteration}{. That is, \ldblquote You won\rquote t get it right the first time, so give yourself the latitude to learn 
and to go back and make changes.\rdblquote  You might need to make a lot of changes as you learn and understand the problem more deeply. The elegance you\rquote 
ll produce if you iterate until you get it right will pay off, both in the short and the long term. Iteration is where your program goes from good to great, and where those issues that you didn\rquote t really understand in the first pass become clear. It
\rquote s also where your classes can evolve from single-project usage to reusable resources.
\par What it means to \ldblquote get it right\rdblquote  isn\rquote 
t just that the program works according to the requirements and the use-cases. It also means that the internal structure of the code makes sense to you, and feels like it fits together well, with no awkward syntax, oversized objects or ungainly exposed 
bits of code. In add}{i}{
tion, you must have some sense that the program structure will survive the changes that it will inevitably go through during its lifetime, and that those changes can be made easily and cleanly. This is no small feat. You must not only understand what you
\rquote re building, but also how the program will evolve (what I call the }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {vector of change}}}{\i vector of change}{
). Fortunately, object-oriented programming languages are particularly adept at supporting this kind of continuing mod}{i}{fication \endash 
 the boundaries created by the objects are what tend to keep the structure from breaking down. They are also what allow you to make changes \endash  ones that would seem drastic in a procedural program \endash  without causing eart}{h}{
quakes throughout your code. In fact, support for iteration might be the most important benefit of OOP.
\par With iteration, you create something that at least approximates what you think you\rquote re building, and then you kick the tires, compare it to your r}{e}{quirements and see where it falls short. Th
en you can go back and fix it by redesigning and re-implementing the portions of the program that didn\rquote t work right.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ }{\fs18 
This is something like \ldblquote rapid prototyping,\rdblquote  where you were supposed to build a quick
-and-dirty version so that you could learn about the system, and then throw away your prototype and build it right. The trouble with rapid prototyping is that people didn\rquote 
t throw away the prototype, but instead built upon it. Combined with the lack of structure in procedural programming, this often leads to messy systems that are expensive to maintain.}}}{
 You might actually need to solve the problem, or an aspect of the problem, several times before you hit on the right solution. (A study of }{\i D}{\i e}{\i sign Patterns}{, described in Chapter XX, is usually helpful here.)
\par Iteration also occurs when you build a system, see that it matches your r}{e}{quirements and then discover it wasn\rquote t actually what you }{\i wanted}{. When you see the system in operation, you find t
hat you really wanted to solve a different problem. If you think this kind of iteration is going to happen, then you owe it to you}{r}{self to build your first version as quickly as possible so you can find out if it\rquote s what you want.
\par Iteration is closely tied to }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {incremental development}}}{\i incremental development}{. Incremental development means that you start with the core of your system and implement it as a fram}{e}{
work upon which to build the rest of the system piece by piece. Then you start adding features one at a time. The trick to this is in designing a fram}{e}{
work that will accommodate all the features you plan to add to it. (See Chapter XX for more insight into this issue.) The advantage is that once you get the core framework working, each feature you ad
d is like a small project in itself rather than part of a big project. Also, new features that are incorporated later in the development or maintenance phases can be added more easily. OOP supports incremental development because if your program is design
ed well, your increments will turn out to be discrete objects or groups of objects.
\par Perhaps the most important thing to remember is that by default \endash  by definition, really \endash  if you modify a class its super- and subclasses will still function. You need not fear modification; it won\rquote 
t necessarily break the program, and any change in the outcome will be limited to subclasses and/or specific collaborators of the class you change.
\par You have to know when to stop iterating the design. Ideally, you achieve target fun
ctionality and are in the process of refinement and addition of new features when the deadline comes along and forces you to stop and ship that version. (Remember, software is a subscription business.)
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018415}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775960}Plans pay off{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018415}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775960}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Of course you wouldn\rquote t build a house without a lot of carefully-drawn plans. If you build a deck or a dog house, your plans won\rquote t be so elaborate but you\rquote 
ll still probably start with some kind of sketches to guide you on your way. Software development has gone to extremes. For a long time, people didn\rquote t have much structure in their development, but then big projects began fai}{l}{
ing. In reaction, we ended up with methodologies that had an intimidating amount of structure and detail, primarily intended for those big projects. These methodologies were too scary to use \endash  it looked like you\rquote 
d spend all your time writing documents and no time programming. (This was often the case.) I hope that what I\rquote ve shown you here suggests a middle path \endash  a sliding scale. Use an approach that fits your needs (and your
 personality). No matter how minimal you choose to make it, }{\i some}{ kind of plan will make a big improvement in your project as opposed to no plan at all. R}{e}{
member that, by most estimates, over 50 percent of projects fail (some estimates go up to 70 percent!).{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373783}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018408}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373795} 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373787}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775961}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373783}Why C++ succeeds{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373787}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775961}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Part of the reason C++ has been so successful is that the goal was not just to turn C into an OOP language (although it started that way), but also to solve many other problems facing developers today, especially those who have large in
vestments in C. Traditionally, OOP languages have suffered from the attitude that you should abandon everything you know and start from scratch with a new set of concepts and a new syntax, arguing that it\rquote 
s better in the long run to lose all the old baggag
e that comes with procedural languages. This may be true, in the long run. But in the short run, a lot of that baggage was valuable. The most valuable elements may not be the existing code base (which, given adequate tools, could be translated), but inste
ad the existing }{\i mind base}{. If you\rquote 
re a functioning C programmer and must drop everything you know about C in order to adopt a new language, you immediately become nonproductive for many months, until your mind fits around the new paradigm. Whereas if you ca
n leverage off of your existing C knowledge and expand upon it, you can continue to be productive with what you already know while moving into the world of object-oriented programming. As everyone has his or her own mental model of programming, this move 
is messy enough as it is without the added expense of starting with a new language model from square one. So the reason for the success of C++, in a nutshell, is economic: It still costs to move to OOP, but C++ may cost less.
\par The goal of C++ is improved pro
ductivity. This productivity comes in many ways, but the language is designed to aid you as much as possible, while hindering you as little as possible with arbitrary rules or any requirement that you use a particular set of features. C++ is designed to b
e practical; language design decisions were based on providing the maximum benefits to the programmer (at least, from the world view of C).
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373788}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775962}A better C{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373788}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775962}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You get an instant win even if you continue to write C code because C++ has closed many holes in the C language and provides better type checking and compile-time analysis. You\rquote 
re forced to declare functions so the compiler can check their use. The need for the preprocessor has virtually been eliminated for value substitution and macros, which removes a set of difficult-to-find bugs. C++ has a feature called }{\i references}{
 that allows more convenient handling of addresses for function arguments and return values. The handling of names is improved through a feature called }{\i function overloading}{, which allows you to us
e the same name for different functions. A feature called }{\i namespaces }{also improves the control of names. There are numerous other small features that improve the safety of C.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373789}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775963}You\rquote re already on the learning curve{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373789}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775963}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The problem with learning a new language is 
productivity: No company can afford to suddenly lose a productive software engineer because he or she is learning a new language. C++ is an extension to C, not a complete new syntax and programming model. It allows you to continue creating useful code, ap
plying the features gradually as you learn and understand them. This may be one of the most important reasons for the success of C++.
\par In addition, all your existing C code is still viable in C++, but because the C++ compiler is pickier, you\rquote ll often find hidden errors when recompiling the code.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373790}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775964}Efficiency{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373790}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775964}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Sometimes it is appropriate to trade execution speed for programmer productivity. A financial model, for example, may be useful for only a short period of time, so it\rquote 
s more important to create the model ra
pidly than to execute it rapidly. However, most applications require some degree of efficiency, so C++ always errs on the side of greater efficiency. Because C programmers tend to be very efficiency-conscious, this is also a way to ensure they won\rquote 
t be able to argue that the language is too fat and slow. A number of features in C++ are intended to allow you to tune for performance when the generated code isn\rquote t efficient enough.
\par Not only do you have the same low-level control as in C (and the ability to directly write assembly language within a C++ program), but anecdotal evidence suggests that the program speed for an object-oriented C++ program tends to be within \'b1
10% of a program written in C, and often much closer. The design produced for an OOP program may actually be more efficient than the C counterpart.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373791}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775965}Systems are easier \line to express and understand{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373791}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775965}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Classes designed to fit the problem tend to express it better. This means that when you write the code, you\rquote re describing your solution in the terms of the problem space (\ldblquote 
put the grommet in the bin\rdblquote ) rather than the terms of the computer, which is the solution space (\ldblquote set the bit in the chip that means that the relay will close\rdblquote 
). You deal with higher-level concepts and can do much more with a single line of code.
\par The other benefit of this ease of expression is maintenance, which (if reports can be believed) takes a huge portion of the cost over a program\rquote s lifetime. If a program is easier to understand, then it\rquote 
s easier to maintain. This can also reduce the cost of creating and maintaining the documentation.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373792}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775966}Maximal leverage with libraries{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373792}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775966}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The fastest way to create a program is to use code that\rquote s already written: a library. A major goal in C++ is to make library use easier. This is accomplished by casting lib
raries into new data types (classes), so that bringing in a library means adding a new types to the language. Because the C++ compiler takes care of how the library is used \endash 
 guaranteeing proper initialization and cleanup, and ensuring functions are called properly \endash  you can focus on what you want the library to do, not how you have to do it. 
\par Because names can be sequestered to portions of your program via C++ namespaces, you can use as many libraries as you want without the kinds of name clashes you\rquote d run into with C.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775967}Source-code reuse with templates{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775967}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There is a significant class of types that require source-code modification in order to reuse them effectively. The }{\i template}{
 feature in C++ performs the source code modification automatically, making it an esp
ecially powerful tool for reusing library code. A type you design using templates will work effortlessly with many other types. Templates are especially nice because they hide the complexity of this type of code reuse from the client programmer.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373793}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775968}Error handling{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373793}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775968}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Error handling in C is a notorious problem, and one that is often ignored \endash  finger-crossing is usually involved. If you\rquote re building a large, complex program, there\rquote 
s nothing worse than having an error buried somewhere with no clue of where it came from. C++ }{\i exception handling}{ (the subject of Chapter XX) is a way to guarantee that an error is noticed and that something happens as a result.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373794}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775969}Programming in the large{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373794}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775969}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Many traditional languages have built-in limitations to program size and complexity. BASIC}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {BASIC language}}}{
, for example, can be great for pulling together quick solutions for certain classes of problems, but if the program gets more than a few pages long or ventures out of the normal problem domain of that language, it\rquote s like trying to run
 through an ever-more viscous fluid. C, too, has these limitations. For example, when a program gets beyond perhaps 50,000 lines of code, name collisions start to become a problem \endash 
 effectively, you run out of function and variable names. Another particularly bad problem is the little holes in the C language \endash  errors can get buried in a large program that are extremely difficult to find.
\par There\rquote s no clear line that tells when your language is failing you, and even if there were, you\rquote d ignore it. You don\rquote t say, \ldblquote My BASIC program just got too big; I\rquote ll have to rewrite it in C!\rdblquote 
 Instead, you try to shoehorn a few more lines in to add that one extra feature. So the extra costs come creeping up on you.
\par C++ is designed to aid }{\i programming in the large}{, that is, to erase those creeping-complexity boundaries between a small program and a large one. You certainly don\rquote t need to use OOP, templates, namespaces, and exception handling when you
\rquote re writing a hello-world style utility program, but those features are there when you need them. And the compiler is aggressive about ferreting out bug-producing errors for small and large programs alike.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373815}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775970}Strategies for transition{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373815}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775970}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you buy into OOP, you next question is probably, \ldblquote How can I get my manager/colleagues/department/peers to start using objects?\rdblquote  Think about how you \endash 
 one independent programmer \endash  would go about learning to use a new language and a new programming paradigm. You\rquote ve done it before. First comes education and examples; then comes a trial project to give you a fe
el for the basics without doing anything too confusing; then try a \ldblquote real world\rdblquote 
 project that actually does something useful. Throughout your first projects you continue your education by reading, asking questions of experts, and trading hints with friends.
 This is the approach many experienced programmers suggest for the switch from C to C++. Switching an entire company will of course introduce certain group dynamics, but it will help at each step to remember how one person would do it. 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775971}Guidelines{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775971}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here are some guidelines to consider when making the transition to OOP and C++:
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {1. Training
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The first step is some form of education. Remember the company\rquote s investment in plain C code, and try not to throw everything into disarray for 6 to 9 months while everyone 
puzzles over how multiple inheritance works. Pick a small group for indoctrination, preferably one composed of people who are curious, work well together, and can function as their own support network while they\rquote re learning C++.
\par An alternative approach tha
t is sometimes suggested is the education of all company levels at once, including overview courses for strategic managers as well as design and programming courses for project builders. This is especially good for smaller companies making fundamental shi
f
ts in the way they do things, or at the division level of larger companies. Because the cost is higher, however, some may choose to start with project-level training, do a pilot project (possibly with an outside mentor), and let the project team become th
e teachers for the rest of the company.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {2. Low-risk project
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Try a low-risk project first and allow for mistakes. Once you\rquote 
ve gained some experience, you can either seed other projects from members of this first team or use the team members as an OOP technic
al support staff. This first project may not work right the first time, so it should not be mission-critical for the company. It should be simple, self-contained, and instructive; this means that it should involve creating classes that will be meaningful 
to the other programmers in the company when they get their turn to learn C++.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {3. Model from success
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Seek out examples of good object-oriented design before starting from scratch. There\rquote s a good probability that someone has solved your problem already, and if they haven\rquote 
t solved it exactly you can probably apply what you\rquote ve learned about abstraction to modify an existing design to fit your needs. This is the general concept of }{\i design patterns, }{covered in Chapter XX.}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {patterns, design}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {design\: patterns}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {4. Use existing class libraries
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The primary economic motivation for switching to C++ is the easy use of existing code in the form of class libraries (in particular, the Standard C++ libraries, which are covered later in this book). The shortest applica
tion development cycle will result when you don\rquote t have to write anything but }{\b main(\~)}{. However, some new programmers don\rquote 
t understand this, are unaware of existing class libraries, or through fascination with the language desire to write classes that may already exist. Your success with OOP and C++ will be optimized if you make an effort to seek out and reuse other people
\rquote s code early in the transition process.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {5. Don\rquote t rewrite existing code in C++
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Although }{\i compiling}{ your C code in C++ usually produces (some
times great) benefits by finding problems in the old code, it is not usually the best use of your time to take existing, functional code and rewrite it in C++ (if you must turn it into objects, you can \ldblquote wrap\rdblquote 
 the C code in C++ classes). There are incremental benefits, especially if the code is slated for reuse. But chances are you aren\rquote 
t going to see the dramatic increases in productivity that you hope for in your first few projects unless that project is a new one. C++ and OOP shine best when taking a project from concept to reality.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373817}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775972}Management obstacles{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373817}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775972}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you\rquote re a manager, your job is to acquire resources for your team, to overcome barriers to your team\rquote 
s success, and in general to try to provide the most productive and enjoyable environment so your team 
is most likely to perform those miracles that are always being asked of you. Moving to C++ falls in all three of these categories, and it would be wonderful if it didn\rquote t cost you anything as well. Although moving to C++ may be cheaper \endash 
 depending on your constraints}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{
 Because of its productivity improvements, the Java language should also be considered here.}}}{ \endash  than the OOP alternatives for team of C programmers (and probably for programmers in other procedural languages), it isn\rquote 
t free, and there are obstacles you should be aware of before trying to sell the move to C++ within your company and embarking on the move itself.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Startup costs
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The cost of moving to C++ is more than just the acquisition of C++ compilers (the GNU C++ compiler, one of the very best, is free). Your medium- and long-term costs will be minimized if you invest in training (and po
ssibly mentoring for your first project) and also if you identify and purchase class libraries that solve your problem rather than trying to build those libraries yourself. These are hard-money costs that must be factored into a realistic proposal. In add
i
tion, there are the hidden costs in loss of productivity while learning a new language and possibly a new programming environment. Training and mentoring can certainly minimize these but team members must overcome their own struggles to understand the iss
u
es. During this process they will make more mistakes (this is a feature, because acknowledged mistakes are the fastest path to learning) and be less productive. Even then, with some types of programming problems, the right classes, and the right developme
nt environment, it\rquote s possible to be more productive while you\rquote re learning C++ (even considering that you\rquote re making more mistakes and writing fewer lines of code per day) than if you\rquote d stayed with C.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Performance issues
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A common question is, \ldblquote Doesn\rquote t OOP automatically make my programs a lot bigger and slower?\rdblquote  The answer is, \ldblquote It depends.\rdblquote 
 Most traditional OOP languages were designed with experimentation and rapid prototyping in mind rather than lean-and-mean operation. Thus, they virtually guaranteed a signifi
cant increase in size and decrease in speed. C++, however, is designed with production programming in mind. When your focus is on rapid prototyping, you can throw together components as fast as possible while ignoring efficiency issues. If you\rquote 
re using any third-party libraries, these are usually already optimized by their vendors; in any case it\rquote s not an issue while you\rquote re in rapid-development mode. When you have a system you like, if it\rquote s small and fast enough, then you
\rquote re done. If not, you begin tuning with a profiling tool, looking first for speedups that can be done with simple applications of built-in C++ features. If that doesn\rquote 
t help, you look for modifications that can be made in the underlying implementation so no code that uses a particular class 
needs to be changed. Only if nothing else solves the problem do you need to change the design. The fact that performance is so critical in that portion of the design is an indicator that it must be part of the primary design criteria. You have the benefit
 of finding this out early through rapid prototyping.
\par As mentioned earlier, the number that is most often given for the difference in size and speed between C and C++ is \'b110%, and often much closer to par. You may actually get a significant improvement in s
ize and speed when using C++ rather than C because the design you make for C++ could be quite different from the one you\rquote d make for C.
\par The evidence for size and speed comparisons between C and C++ tends to be anecdotal and is likely to remain so. Regardles
s of the number of people who suggest that a company try the same project using C and C++, no company is likely to waste money that way unless it\rquote 
s very big and interested in such research projects. Even then, it seems like the money could be better spent.
 Almost universally, programmers who have moved from C (or some other procedural language) to C++ have had the personal experience of a great acceleration in their programming productivity, and that\rquote s the most compelling argument you can find.

\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Common design errors
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
When starting your team into OOP and C++, programmers will typically go through a series of common design errors. This often happens because of too little feedback from experts during the design and implementation of early projects, because no exp
erts have been developed within the company. It\rquote s easy to feel that you understand OOP too early in the cycle and go off on a bad tangent; something that\rquote 
s obvious to someone experienced with the language may be a subject of great internal debate for a novice. Much of this trauma can be skipped by using an outside expert for training and mentoring.
\par On the other hand, the fact that it is easy to make these design errors points to C++\rquote s main drawback: its backwards-compatibility with C (of course, that\rquote s also
 its main strength). To accomplish the feat of being able to compile C code, the language had to make some compromises which have resulted in a number of \ldblquote dark corners.\rdblquote 
 These are a reality, and comprise much of the learning curve for the language. In this book (and in others; see the \ldblquote Recommended Reading\rdblquote 
 appendix) I try to reveal most of the pitfalls you are likely to encounter when working with C++, but you should always be aware that there are some holes in the safety net.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373818}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775973}Summary{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373818}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775973}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This chapter attempts 
to give you a feel for the broad issues of object-oriented programming and C++, including why OOP is different, and why C++ in particular is different, concepts of OOP methodologies, and finally the kinds of issues you will encounter when moving your own 
company to OOP and C++.
\par OOP and C++ may not be for everyone. It\rquote s important to evaluate your own needs and decide whether C++ will optimally satisfy those needs, or if you might be better off with another programming system. If you know that your needs will
 be very specialized for the foreseeable future and if you have specific constraints that may not be satisfied by C++, then you owe it to yourself to investigate the alternatives. Even if you eventually choose C++ as your language, you\rquote 
ll at least understand what the options were and have a clear vision of why you took that direction.
\par You know what a procedural program looks like: data definitions and function calls. To find the meaning of such a program you have to work a little, looking through the functi
on calls and low-level concepts to create a model in your mind. This is the reason we need intermediate representations when designing procedural programs \endash 
 by themselves, these programs tend to be confusing because the terms of expression are oriented more toward the computer than the problem you\rquote re solving.
\par Because C++ adds many new concepts to the C language, your natural assumption may be that, of course, the }{\b main(\~)}{ in a C++ program will be far more complicated than the equivalent C program. Here, you\rquote 
ll be pleasantly surprised: A well-written C++ program is generally far simpler and much easier to understand than the equivalent C program. What you\rquote 
ll see are the definitions of the objects that represent concepts in your problem space (rather than the i
ssues of the computer representation) and messages sent to those objects to represent the activities in that space. One of the delights of object-oriented programming is that, with a well-designed program, it\rquote 
s very easy to understand the code by reading it. Usually there\rquote s a lot less code, as well, because many of your problems will be solved by reusing existing library code.
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373819}\sect }\sectd \margtsxn2707\margbsxn2707\sbkodd\linex0\headery0\footery1440\colsx432\endnhere\titlepg\sectdefaultcl {\footer \pard\plain \s38\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 
