\par }}\pard\plain \s1\fi-1080\li990\sa360\sl240\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \cbpat8 \f4\fs96\cf1\kerning18\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775974}2: Making & using objects{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775974} 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {This chapter will introduce enough C++ syntax and program construction concepts to allow you to write an
d run some simple object-oriented programs. In the subsequent chapter we will cover the basic syntax of C and C++ in detail.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {By seeing this chapter first, you\rquote 
ll get the basic flavor of what it is like to program with objects in C++, and you\rquote ll also discov
er some of the reasons for the enthusiasm surrounding this language. This should be enough to carry you through Chapter 3, which can be a bit exhausting since it contains most of the details of the C language.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {The user-defined data type}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {user-defined type}}}{, or }{\i class}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class}}}{
, is what distinguishes C++ from traditional procedural languages. A class is a new data type that you or someone else creates to solve a particular kind of problem. Once a class is created, anyone can use it without knowing the s
pecifics of how it works, or even how classes are built. This chapter treats classes as if they are just another built-in data type available for use in programs. 
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright {Classes that someone else has created are typically packaged into a library}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar
\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {library}}}{. T
his chapter uses several of the class libraries that come with all C++ implementations. An especially important standard library is iostreams, which (among other things) allows you to read from files and the keyboard, and to write to files and the display
. You\rquote ll also see the very handy }{\b string }{class, and the }{\b vector }{container from the Standard Template Library (STL). By the end of the chapter, you\rquote ll see how easy it is to utilize a pre-defined library of classes.
\par In order to create your first program you must understand the tools used to build applications.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775975}The process of language translation{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775975}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {All computer languages are translated from something that tends to be easy for a human to understand (}{\i source code}{)}{\i  }{into something that is executed on a computer (}{\i machine
 instructions}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i machine instructions}}}{). Traditionally, translators fall into two classes: }{\i interpreters}{ and }{\i compiler}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {\i compiler}}}{\i s}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775976}Interpreters{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775976}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {interpreters}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {An interpreter translates source code into activities (which may comprise groups of mach
ine instructions) and immediately executes those activities. BASIC, for example, has been a popular interpreted language. Traditional BASIC interpreters translate and execute one line at a time, and then forget that the line has been translated. This make
s
 them slow, since they must re-translate any repeated code. BASIC has also been compiled, for speed. More modern interpreters, such as those for the Perl language, translate the entire program into an intermediate language which is then executed by a much
 faster interpreter}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{  The boundary between compilers and interpreters can tend to 
become a bit fuzzy, especially with Perl, which has many of the features and power of a compiled language but the quick turnaround of an interpreted language.}}}{.
\par Interpreters have many advantages. The transition from writing code to executing code is almost immediate, and the source code is always available so the interpreter can be much more specific when an error occurs. The benefits often ci
ted for interpreters are ease of interaction and rapid development (but not necessarily execution) of programs.
\par Interpreted languages often have severe limitations when building large projects (Perl seems to be an exception to this). The interpreter (or a 
reduced version) must always be in memory to execute the code, and even the fastest interpreter may introduce unacceptable speed restrictions. Most interpreters require that the complete source code be brought into the interpreter all at once. Not only do
es this introduce a space limitation, it can also cause more difficult bugs if the language doesn\rquote t provide facilities to localize the effect of different pieces of code.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775977}Compilers{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775977}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {compilers}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A compiler translates source code directly into assembly lan
guage or machine instructions. The eventual end product is a file or files containing machine code. This is an involved process, and usually takes several steps. The transition from writing code to executing code is significantly longer with a compiler.

\par De
pending on the acumen of the compiler writer, programs generated by a compiler tend to require much less space to run, and they run much more quickly. Although size and speed are probably the most often cited reasons for using a compiler, in many situatio
ns they aren\rquote t the most important reasons. Some languages (such as C) are designed to allow pieces of a program to be compiled independently. These pieces are eventually combined into a final }{\i executable}{ program by a tool called the }{\i 
linker}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i linker}}}{. This process is called }{\i separate compilation}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i separate compilation}}}{.
\par Separate compilation has many benefits. A program that, taken all at once, would exceed the limits of the compiler or the compiling environment can be compiled in pieces. Programs can be b
uilt and tested a piece at a time. Once a piece is working, it can be saved and treated as a building block. Collections of tested and working pieces can be combined into }{\i librar}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i 
library, code}}}{\i ies}{ for use by other programmers. As each piece is created, the complexity of the other pieces is hidden. All these features support the creation of large programs}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain 
\s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ Perl is again an exception, since it also provides separate compilation.}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
large programs, creation of}}}{.
\par Compiler debugging}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {debugging}}}{ features have improved significantly over time. Early compilers only generated m
achine code, and the programmer inserted print statements to see what was going on. This is not always effective. Modern compilers can insert information about the source code into the executable program. This information is used by powerful }{\i 
source-level debugger}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i source-level debugger}}}{\i s}{ to show exactly what is happening in a program by tracing its progress through the source code.
\par Some compilers tackle the compilation-speed problem by performing }{\i in-memory compilation}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i in-memory compilation}}}{. Most c
ompilers work with files, reading and writing them in each step of the compilation process. In-memory compilers keep the program in RAM. For small programs, this can seem as responsive as an interpreter. 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775978}The compilation process{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775978}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {compilation process}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To program in C and C++, you need to understand the steps and tools in the compilation process. Some languages (C and C++, in particular) start compilation by running a }{\i preprocessor}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i preprocessor}}}{ on the source code. The preprocessor is a simple program that replaces patterns in the source code with other patterns the programmer has defined (using }{\i preprocessor directives}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i preprocessor\: directives}}}{). Preprocessor directives are used to save typing and to increase the readability of the code (Later in the book, you\rquote 
ll learn how the design of C++ is meant to discourage much of the use of the preprocessor, since it can cause subtle bugs). The pre-processed code is often written to an intermediate file.
\par Compilers usually do their work in two passes. The first pass }{\i parses}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i parsing}}}{
 the pre-processed code. The compiler breaks the source code into small units and organizes it into a structure called a }{\i tree}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i parsing\: parse tree}}}{. In the expression \ldblquote }{
\b A + B}{\rdblquote  the elements \lquote }{\b A}{\rquote , \lquote }{\b +}{\rquote  and \lquote }{\b B}{\rquote  are leaves on the parse tree.
\par A }{\i global optimizer}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i optimizer\:global}}}{ is sometimes used between the first and second passes to produce smaller, faster code.
\par In the second pass, the }{\i code generator}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i code generator}}}{ walks through the parse tree and generates either assem
bly language code or machine code for the nodes of the tree. If the code generator creates assembly code, the assembler must then be run. The end result in both cases is an object module}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
object module}}}{ (a file that typically has an extension of }{\b .o}{ or }{\b .obj}{). A }{\i peephole optimizer}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i optimizer\: peephole}}}{
 is sometimes used in the second pass to look for pieces of code containing redundant assembly-language statements.
\par The use of the word \ldblquote object\rdblquote }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object}}}{ to describe chunks of machine code is an unfo
rtunate artifact. The word came into use before object-oriented programming was in general use. \ldblquote Object\rdblquote  is used in the same sense as \ldblquote goal\rdblquote 
 when discussing compilation, while in object-oriented programming it means \ldblquote a thing with boundaries.\rdblquote 
\par The }{\i linker}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i linker}}}{
 combines a list of object modules into an executable program that can be loaded and run by the operating system. When a function in one object module makes a reference to a function or variable in another object module, the linker resolves these
 references \endash  it makes sure that all the external functions and data you claimed existed during compilation actually do exist}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {resolving references}}}{
. The linker also adds a special object module to perform start-up activities.
\par The linker can search through special files called }{\i libraries}{ in order to resolve all its references. A library}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {library}}}{
 contains a collection of object modules in a single file. A library is created and maintained by a program called a }{\i librarian}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Static type checking}{\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \v\f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {static type checking}}}{

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The compiler performs }{\i type checking}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i type checking}}}{
 during the first pass. Type checking tests for the proper use of arguments in functions, and prevents many kinds of programming errors. Since type checking occurs during compilation rather than when the program is running, it is called }{\i 
static type checking}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i type checking\:static}}}{. 
\par Some object-oriented languages (notably Java}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Smalltalk}}}{) perform some type checking at runtime (}{\i dynamic type checking}{). If combined with static type checking, dynamic type checking}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type checking\: dynamic}}}{ is more powerful than static type checking alone. However, it also adds overhead to program execution.
\par C++ uses static type checking because the language cannot assume any particular runtime s
upport for bad operations. Static type checking notifies the programmer about misuses of types during compilation, and thus maximizes execution speed. As you learn C++ you will see that most of the language design decisions favor the same kind of high-spe
ed, production-oriented programming the C language is famous for.
\par You can disable static type checking in C++. You can also do your own dynamic type checking \endash  you just need to write the code. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775979}Tools for separate compilation{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775979}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Separate compilation is particularly important }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {separate compilation }}}{
when building large projects. In C and C++, a program can be created in small, manageable, independently tested pieces. The most fundamental tool for breaking a program up into pieces is the ability to create name
d subroutines or subprograms. In C and C++, a subprogram is called a }{\i function}{
, and functions are the pieces of code that can be placed in different files, enabling separate compilation. Put another way, the function is the atomic unit of code, since you ca
nnot have part of a function in one file and another part in a different file \endash  the entire function must be placed in a single file (although files can and do contain more than one function).
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright {When you call a function, you typically pass it some }{\i arguments}{, which are values you\rquote 
d like the function to work with during its execution. When the function is finished, you typically get back a }{\i return value}{, a value that the function hands back to you as a result. It\rquote s also possible to write functions that take no ar
guments and return no values.
\par To create a program with multiple files, functions in one file must access functions and data in other files. When compiling a file, the C or C++ compiler must know about the functions and data in the other files, in particula
r their names and proper usage. The compiler ensures that functions and data are used correctly. This process of \ldblquote telling the compiler\rdblquote  the names of external functions and data and what they should look like is called }{\i declaration}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {declaration}}}{. Once yo
u declare a function or variable, the compiler knows how to check to make sure it is used properly.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373820}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775980}Declarations vs. definitions{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373820}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775980}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It\rquote s important to understand the difference between }{\i declarations}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar
\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i declaration}}}{ and }{\i definitions}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar
\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i definition}}}{ because the
se terms will be used precisely throughout the book. Essentially all C and C++ programs require declarations. Before you can write your first program, you need to understand the proper way to write a declaration.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {A }{\i declaration}{ introduces a name \endash  an identifier \endash  to the compiler. It tells the compiler \ldblquote this function or this variable exists somewhere, and here is what it should look like.\rdblquote 
 A }{\i definition}{, on the other hand, says: \ldblquote make this variable here\rdblquote  or \ldblquote make this function here.\rdblquote  It allocates storage for the name. This meaning works whether you\rquote 
re talking about a variable or a function; in either case, at the point of definition the compiler allocates storage. For a variable, the compiler determines how big that variable is and causes space to be generated 
in memory to hold the data for that variable. For a function, the compiler generates code, which ends up occupying storage in memory. 
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {declaration\: vs. definitions}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {definition\: vs declaration}}}{
You can declare a variable or a function in many different places, but there must only be one definition in C and C++ (this is sometimes called the ODR: }{\i one-definition rule}{
). When the linker is uniting all the object modules, it will usually complain if it finds more than one definition for the same function or variable.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {A definition can also be a declaration. If the compiler hasn\rquote t seen the name }{\b x}{ before and you define }{\b int x;}{, the compiler sees the name as a declaration and allocates storage for it all at once.

