\rquote s typically broken up into pieces, each of which is built and maintained by a different pers
on or group. Since C effectively has a single arena where all the identifier and function names live, this means that all the developers must be careful not to accidentally use the same names in situations where they can conflict. This rapidly becomes ted
ious, time-wasting and, ultimately, expensive.
\par Standard C++ has a mechanism to prevent this collison: the }{\b namespace}{ keyword. Each set of C++ definitions in a library or program is \ldblquote wrapped\rdblquote  in a namespace, and if some other definition has an identical name,
 but is in a different namespace, then there is no collision.
\par Namespaces are a convenient and helpful tool, but their presence means you must be aware of them before you can write any programs at all. If you simply include a header file and use some functions or objects from that header, you\rquote 
ll probably get strange-sounding errors when you try to compile the program, to the effect that the compiler cannot find any of the declarations for the items that you just included in the header file! After you see this message a few times you\rquote 
ll become familiar with its meaning (which is: \ldblquote you included the header file but all the declarations are within a namespace and you didn\rquote t tell the compiler that you wanted to use the declarations in that namespace\rdblquote ).
\par There\rquote s a keyword that allows you to say \ldblquote I want to use the declarations and/or definitions in this namespace.\rdblquote  This keyword, appropriately enough, is }{\b using}{
. All of the Standard C++ libraries are wrapped in a single namespace, which is }{\b std}{ (for \ldblquote standard\rdblquote ). As this book uses the standard libraries almost exclusively, you\rquote ll see the following }{\i using directive}{
 in almost every program:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {using namespace std;
