perators. It prints out these arguments in left-to-right order. The special iostream function }{\b endl}{ outputs the line and a newline. With iostreams, you can string together a series of arguments like this, which makes the class easy to use. 
\par In C, text inside double quotes is traditionally called a \ldblquote string}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {string}}}{.\rdblquote 
 However, the Standard C++ library has a powerful class called }{\b string}{ for manipulating text, and so I shall use the more precise term }{\i character array }{for text inside double quotes.
\par The compile
r creates storage for character arrays and stores the ASCII equivalent for each character in this storage. The compiler automatically terminates this array of characters with an extra piece of storage containing the value 0, to indicate the end of the cha
racter array. 
\par Inside a character array, you can insert special characters by using }{\i escape sequences}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {escape sequences}}}
{. These consist of a backslash (}{\b \\}{) followed by a special code. For example }{\b \\n}{ means newline. Your compiler manual or local C guide gives a complete set of escape sequences; others include }{\b \\t}{ (tab), }{\b \\\\}{ (backslash) and }{
\b \\b}{ (backspace).
\par Notice that the entire statement terminates with a semicolon.
\par Character array arguments and constant numbers are mixed together in the above }{\b cout}{ statement. Because the operator }{\b <<}{ is overloaded}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar
