\par Other compilers will have a similar syntax; consult your compiler\rquote s documenation for details.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775989}More about iostreams{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775989} 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
So far you have seen only the most rudimentary aspect of the iostreams class. The output formatting available with iostreams also includes features like number formatting in decimal, octal and hex. Here\rquote s another example of the use of iostreams:

\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C02:Stream2.cpp
\par // More streams features
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   // Specifying formats with manipulators:
\par   cout << "a number in decimal: "
\par        << dec << 15 << endl;
\par   cout << "in octal: " << oct << 15 << endl;
\par   cout << "in hex: " << hex << 15 << endl;
\par   cout << "a floating-point number: "
\par        << 3.14159 << endl;
\par   cout << "non-printing char (escape): "
\par        << char(27) << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This example shows the iostreams class printing numbers in decimal, octal and hexadecimal using iostream }{\i 
manipulators}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostream\: manipulators}}}{(which don\rquote 
t print anything, but change the state of the output stream). The formatting of floating-point numbers is determined automatically, by the compiler. In addition, any character can be sent to a stream object using a }{\i cast}{ to a }{\b char}{ (a }{\b 
char}{ is a data type which holds single characters). This }{\i cast }{looks like a function call: }{\b char(\~)}{, along with the character\rquote s ASCII value. In the above program, the }{\b char(27)}{ sends an \ldblquote escape\rdblquote  to }{\b cout
}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775990}Character array concatenation{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775990}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {An important feature of the C preprocessor is }{\i character array concatenation}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar
\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {string concatenation}}}{. This feature is used in some of the examples in this book.
 If two quoted character arrays are adjacent, and no punctuation is between them, the compiler will paste the character arrays together into a single character array. This is particularly useful when code listings have width restrictions:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C02:Concat.cpp
\par // Character array Concatenation
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   cout << "This is far too long to put on a single "
\par     "line but it can be broken up with no ill effects\\n"
\par     "as long as there is no punctuation separating "
\par     "adjacent character arrays.\\n";
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {At first, the above code can look like an error because there\rquote s no familiar semicolon at the end of each line. Remember that C and C++ are free-form languages, and although you\rquote 
ll usually see a semicolon at the end of each line, the actual requirement is for a semicolon at the end of each statement, and it\rquote s possible for a statement to continue over several lines.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775991}Reading input{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775991}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {iostream\: reading input}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The iostreams classes provide the ability to read input. The object used for standard input}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {standard input}}}{ is }{\b cin}{ (for \ldblquote console input\rdblquote ). }{\b cin}{
 normally expects input from the console, but this input can be redirected from other sources. An example of redirection is shown later in this chapter.
\par The iostreams operator used with }{\b cin}{ is }{\b >>}{. This operator waits for the same kind of input as its argument. For example, if you give it an integer argument, it waits for an integer from the console. Here\rquote s an example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C02:Numconv.cpp
\par // Converts decimal to octal and hex
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int number;
\par   cout << "Enter a decimal number: ";
\par   cin >> number;
\par   cout << "value in octal = 0" << oct << number << endl;
\par   cout << "value in hex = 0x" << hex << number << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This program converts a number typed in by the user into octal and hexadecimal representations.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775992}Simple file manipulation{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775992}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {simple file manipulation}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Standard I/O provides a very simple way to read and write files, called I/O redirection}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar
\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {I/O redirection}}}{. If a program takes input from standard input (}{\b cin}{ for iostreams) and sends its output to standard output (}{\b 
cout}{ for iostreams), that input and output can be redirected. Input can be taken from a file, and output can be sent to a file. To re\_direct I/O on the command line of some operating systems (Unix/Linux & DOS, in particular), use the \lquote }{\b <}{
\rquote  sign to redirect input and the \lquote }{\b >}{\rquote  sign to redirect output. For example, if we have a fictitious program called }{\b fiction}{ that reads from standard input and writes to sta
ndard output, you can redirect standard input from the file }{\b stuff}{ and redirect the output to the file }{\b such}{ with the command:
\par }{\b fiction < stuff > such
\par }{Since the files are opened for you, the job is much easier (although you\rquote ll see later that iostreams has a very simple mechanism for opening files).
\par As a useful example, suppose you want to record the number of times you perform an activity, but the program that records the incidents must be loaded and run many times, and the machine may be turned off, etc. To k
eep a permanent record of the incidents, you must store the data in a file. This file will be called }{\b incident.dat}{
 and will initially contain the character 0. For easy reading, it will always contain ASCII digits representing the number of incidents.
\par The program to increment the number is very simple:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C02:Incr.cpp
\par // Read a number, add one and write it
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int num;
\par   cin >> num;
\par   cout << num + 1;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To test the program, run it, type a number and press the \ldblquote Enter\rdblquote 
 key. The program should print a number one larger than the one you type.
\par While the typical way to use a program that reads from standard input and writes to standard output is within a Unix shell script or DOS batch file, any program can be called from inside a C or C++ program using the Standard C }{\b system(\~)}{ function}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {system() function}}}{, which is declared in the header file }{\b <cstdlib>}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C02:Incident.cpp
\par // Records an incident using INCR
\par #include <cstdlib> // Declare "system()"
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   // Other code here...
\par   system("incr < incident.dat > incident.dat");
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To use the }{\b system(\~)}{
 function, you give it a character array that you would normally type at the operating system command prompt. The command executes and control returns to the program}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ If you find yourself writing programs that use a lot of }{\b system(\~)}{ calls, you might be more productive using the Perl language instead.}}}{.
\par Notice that the file }{\b incident.dat}{ is read and written using I/O redirection. Since the single \lquote }{\b >}{\rquote  is used, the file is overwritten. Although it works fine here, reading and writing the same file isn\rquote t always a saf
e thing to do \endash  if you aren\rquote t careful you can end up with garbage in the file. 
\par If a double \lquote }{\b >>}{\rquote  is used instead of a single \lquote }{\b >\rquote }{, the output is appended to the file (and this program wouldn\rquote t work correctly).
\par This program shows you how easy it is to use plain C library functions in C++: just include the header file and call the function. This upward compatibility from C to C++ is a big advantage if you are learning the language starting from a background in C.

\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775993}Introducing }{\b string}{s{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775993}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {While a character array can be fairly useful, it is quite limited. It\rquote 
s simply a group of characters in memory, but if you want to do anything with it you must manage all the little details. For example, the size of a quoted character array is fixed at compile time. If you have a ch
aracter array and you want to add some more characters to it, you\rquote ll need to understand quite a lot (inluding dynamic memory management, character array copying and concatenation) before you can get your wish. This is exactly the kind of thing we
\rquote d like to have an object do for us.
\par The Standard C++ }{\b string}{ class is designed to take care of (and hide) all the low-level manipulations of character arrays that were previously required of the C++ programmer. These manipulations have been a constant source of time
-wasting and errors since the inception of the C language. So, although an entire chapter is devoted to the }{\b string}{ class later in the book, the }{\b string}{
 is so important and it makes life so much easier that it will be introduced here and used in much of the early part of the book.
\par To use }{\b string}{s you include the C++ header file }{\b <string>}{. The }{\b string}{ class is in the namespace }{\b std}{ so a }{\b using}{ directive is necessary. Because of operator overloading, the syntax for using }{\b string}{
s is quite intuitive:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C02:HelloStrings.cpp
\par // The basics of the Standard C++ string class
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   string s1, s2; // Empty strings
\par   string s3 = "Hello, World."; // Initialized
\par   string s4("I am"); // Also initialized
\par   s2 = "Today"; // Assigning to a string
\par   s1 = s3 + " " + s4; // Combining strings
\par   s1 += " 8 "; // Appending to a string
\par   cout << s1 + s2 + "!" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The first two }{\b string}{s, }{\b s1}{ and }{\b s2}{, start out empty, while }{\b s3}{ and }{\b s4}{ show two equivalent ways to initialize }{\b string}{
 objects from character arrays (you can as easily initialize }{\b string}{ objects from other }{\b string}{ objects).
\par You can assign to any }{\b string}{ object using \lquote }{\b =}{\rquote . This replaces the previous contents of the string with whatever is on the right-hand side, and you don\rquote t have to worry about what happens to the previous contents \endash 
 that\rquote s handled automatically for you. To combine }{\b string}{s you simply use the \lquote }{\b +}{\rquote  operator, which also allows you to combine character arrays with }{\b string}{s. If you want to append either a }{\b string}{
 or a character array to another }{\b string}{, you can use the operator \lquote }{\b +=}{\rquote . Finally, note that iostreams already know what to do with }{\b string}{s, so you can just send a }{\b string}{ (or an expression that produces a }{\b 
string}{, which happens with }{\b s1 + s2 + "!"}{) directly to }{\b cout}{ in order to print it.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775994}Reading and writing files{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775994}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {We could use IO redirection in order to read files and write files, as shown previously. In C, the process of opening and manipulating files requires a lot of language background to prepare yo
u for the complexity of the operations. However, the C++ iostream library provides a very simple way to manipulate files, and so this functionality can be introduced much earlier than it would be in C.
\par To open files for reading and writing, you must include }{\b <fstream>}{. Although this will automatically include }{\b <iostream>}{, it\rquote s generally prudent to explicitly include }{\b <iostream>}{ if you\rquote re planning to use }{\b cin}{, }{\b 
cout}{, etc.
\par To open a file for reading, you create an }{\b ifstream}{ object, which then behaves like }{\b cin}{. To open a file for writing, you create an }{\b ofstream}{ object, which then behaves like }{\b cout}{. Once you\rquote 
ve opened the file, you can read from it or write to it just as you would with any other iostream object. It\rquote s that simple (which is, of course, the whole point).
\par One of the most useful functions in the iostream library is }{\b getline(\~)}{, which allows you to read one line (terminated by a newline) into a }{\b string}{ object}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ There are actually a number of variants of }{\b getline(\~)}{, which will be discussed thoroughly in the iostreams chapter, Chapter XX.}}}{. The first argument is the }{\b ifstream}
{ object you\rquote re reading from, and the second argument is the }{\b string}{ object. When the function call is finished, the }{\b string}{ object will contain the line.
\par Here\rquote s a simple example, which copies the contents of one file into another:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C02:Scopy.cpp
\par // Copy one file to another, a line at a time
\par #include <string>
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   ifstream in("Scopy.cpp"); // Open for reading
\par   ofstream out("Scopy2.cpp"); // Open for writing
\par   string s;
\par   while(getline(in, s)) // Discards newline char
\par     out << s << "\\n"; // ... must add it back
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To open the files, you just hand the }{\b ifstream}{ and }{\b ofstream}{ objects the file names you want to create, as seen above.
\par There is a new concept introduced here, which is the }{\b while}{ loop. Although this will be explained in detail in the next chapter, the basic idea is that the expression in parentheses following the }{\b while}{
 controls the execution of the subsequent statement (which can also be multiple statements, wrapped inside curly braces). As long as the expression in parentheses (in this case, }{\b getline(in, s)}{) produces a \ldblquote true\rdblquote 
 result, then the statement controlled by the }{\b while}{ will continue to execute. It turns out that }{\b getline(\~)}{ will return a value that can be interpreted as \ldblquote true\rdblquote  if another line has been successfully read, and \ldblquote 
false\rdblquote  upon reaching the end of the input. Thus, the above }{\b while}{ loop reads every line in the input file and sends each line to the output file.
\par }{\b getline(\~)}{ reads each line until it discovers a newline (the termination character can be changed, but that won\rquote t be an issue until Chapter XX). However, it discards the newline and doesn\rquote t store it in the resulting }{\b string}{
 object. Thus, if we want the copied file to look just like the source file, we must add the newline back in, as shown.
\par Another interesting example is to copy the entire file into a single }{\b string}{ object:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C02:FillString.cpp
\par // Read an entire file into a single string
\par #include <string>
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   ifstream in("FillString.cpp");
\par   string s, line;
\par   while(getline(in, line))
\par     s += line + "\\n";
\par   cout << s;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Because of the dynamic nature of }{\b string}{s, you don\rquote t have to worry about how much storage to allocate for a }{\b string}{ \endash  you can just keep adding things and the }{\b string}{
 will keep expanding to hold whatever you put into it.
\par One of the nice things about putting an entire file into a }{\b string}{ is that the }{\b string}{
 class has many functions for searching and manipulation which would then allow you to modify the file as a single string. However, this has its limitations. For one thing, it 
is often convenient to treat a file as a collection of lines instead of just a big blob of text. For example, if you want to add line numbering it\rquote s much easier if you have each line as a separate }{\b string}{ object. To accomplish this, we
\rquote ll need another approach.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775995}Introducing }{\b vector{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775995}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {With }{\b string}{s, we can fill up a }{\b string}{ object without knowing how much storage we\rquote re going to need. The problem with reading lines from a file into individual }{\b string }{
objects is that you don\rquote t know up front how many }{\b string}{s you\rquote re going to need \endash  you only know after you\rquote 
ve read the entire file. To solve this problem, we need some sort of holder that will automatically expand to contain as many }{\b string}{ objects as we care to put into it.
\par In fact, why limit ourselves to holding }{\b string}{ objects? It turns out that this kind of problem \endash  not knowing how many of something you have while you\rquote re writing a program \endash  happens a lot. And this \ldblquote container
\rdblquote  object sounds like it would be more useful if it would hold }{\i any kind of object at all!}{ Fortunately, the Standard C++ library has a ready-made solution: the STL container classes. STL stands for \ldblquote Standard Template Library,
\rdblquote  and it\rquote s one of the real powerhouses of Standard C++. Even though the implementation of the STL uses some advanced concepts, and th
e full coverage of the STL is given two large chapters later in this book, this library can also be very potent without knowing a lot about it. It\rquote s so useful that the most basic of the STL containers, the }{\b vector}{
, is introduced in this early chapter and used throughout the introductory part of the book. You\rquote ll find that you can do a tremendous amount just by using the basics of }{\b vector}{
 and not worrying about the underlying implementation (again, an important goal of OOP). Since you\rquote ll learn much more about this and the other containers when you reach the STL chapters, it seems forgiveable if the programs that use }{\b vector }{
in the early portion of the book aren\rquote t exactly what an experienced C++ programmer would do. You\rquote ll find that in most cases, the usage shown here is adequate.
\par The }{\b vector}{ class is a }{\i template}{, which means that it can be efficiently applied to different types. That is, we can create a }{\b vector}{ of }{\b shape}{s, a }{\b vector}{ of }{\b cat}{s, a }{\b vector}{ of }{\b string}{
s, etc. Basically, with a template you can create a \ldblquote class of anything.\rdblquote  To tell the compiler what it is that the class will work with (in this case, what the }{\b vector}{ will hold), you put the name of the desired type in 
\ldblquote angle brackets,\rdblquote  which means \lquote less-than\rquote  and \lquote greater-than\rquote  signs. So a }{\b vector}{ of }{\b string}{ would be denoted }{\b vector<string>}{
. When you do this, you end up with a customized vector that will only hold }{\b string}{ objects, and you\rquote ll get an error message from the compiler if you try to put anything else into it.
\par Since }{\b vector }{expresses the concept of a \ldblquote container,\rdblquote  there must be a way to put things into the container and get things back out of the container. To add a brand-new element on the end of a }{\b vector}{
, you use a the member function }{\b push_back(\~)}{ (remember that, since it\rquote s a member function, you use a \lquote }{\b .}{\rquote  to call it for a particular object). The reason the name of this member function might seem a bit verbose \endash 
 }{\b push_back(\~)}{ instead of something simpler like \ldblquote put\rdblquote  \endash  is because there are other containers and other member functions for putting new elements into containers. For example, the there is an }{\b insert(\~)}{
 member function to put something in the middle of a container. }{\b vector}{ supports this but its use is more complicated and we won\rquote t need to explore it until later in the book. There\rquote s also a }{\b push_front(\~)}{ (not part of }{\b 
vector}{) to put things at the beginning. There are many more member functions in }{\b vector}{ and many more containers in the STL, but you\rquote ll be surprised at how much you can do just knowing about a few simple features.
\par So you can put new elements into a }{\b vector}{ with }{\b push_back(\~)}{, but how do you get these elements back out again? This solution is more clever and elegant \endash  operator overloading is used to make the }{\b vector}{ look like an }{\i array}
{. The array (which will be described more fully in the next chapter) is a data type which is available in virtually every programming language so you should already be somewhat familiar with it. Arrays are }{\i aggregates}{
, which mean they consists of a number of elements clumped together. The distinguishing characteristic of an ar
ray is that these elements are the same size and are arranged to be one right after the other. Most importantly, these elements can be selected by \ldblquote indexing,\rdblquote  which means you can say \ldblquote I want element number n\rdblquote 
 and that element will be produced, usually very quickly. Although there are exceptions in programming languages, the indexing is normally achieved using square brackets, so if you have an array }{\b a}{ and you want to produce element 5, you say }{\b 
a[5]}{.
\par This very compact and powerful indexing notation is incorporated into the }{\b vector}{ using operator overloading, just like \lquote }{\b <<}{\rquote  and \lquote }{\b >>}{\rquote  were incorporated into iostreams. Again, you don\rquote 
t need to know how the overloading was implemented \endash  that\rquote s saved for a later chapter \endash  but it\rquote s helpful if you\rquote re aware that there\rquote s some magic going on under the covers in order to make the }{\b [\~]}{
 work with }{\b vector}{.
\par With that in mind, you can now see a program that uses }{\b vector}{. To use a }{\b vector}{, you include the header file }{\b <vector>}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C02:Fillvector.cpp
\par // Copy an entire file into a vector of string
\par #include <string>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <vector>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<string> v;
\par   ifstream in("Fillvector.cpp");
\par   string line;
\par   while(getline(in, line))
\par     v.push_back(line); // Add the line to the end
\par   // Add line numbers:
\par   for(int i = 0; i < v.size(); i++)
\par     cout << i << ": " << v[i] << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Much of this program is similar to the previous one: a file is opened and lines are read into }{\b string}{ objects one at a time. However, these }{\b string}{ objects are pushed onto the back of the 
}{\b vector v}{. Once the }{\b while}{ loop completes, the entire file is resident in memory, inside }{\b v}{.
\par The next statement in the program is called a }{\b for}{ loop. It is similar to a }{\b while}{ loop except that it adds some extra control. After the }{\b for}{, there is a \ldblquote control expression\rdblquote  inside of parentheses, just like the }{
\b while}{ loop. However, this control expression is in three parts: a part which initializes, one that tests to see if we should exit the loop, and one which changes something, typically to step through a sequence of items. This program shows the }{\b 
for}{ loop in the way you\rquote ll see it most commonly used: the initialization part }{\b int i = 0}{ creates an integer }{\b i}{ to use as a loop counter and gives it an initial value of zero. The testing portion says that to stay in the loop, }{\b i}{
 should be less than the number of elements in the }{\b vector v}{ (this is produced using the member function }{\b size(\~)}{ which I just sort of slipped in here, but you must admit it has a fairly obvious meaning). The final portion uses 
a shorthand for C and C++, the \ldblquote auto-increment\rdblquote  operator, to add one to the value of }{\b i}{. Effectively, }{\b i++}{ says \ldblquote get the value of }{\b i}{, add one to it, and put the result back into }{\b i}{
. Thus, the total effect of the }{\b for}{ loop is to take a variable }{\b i}{ and march it through the values from zero to one less than the size of the }{\b vector}{. For each value of }{\b i}{, the }{\b cout}{
 statement is executed and this builds a line that consists of the value of }{\b i}{ (magically converted to a character array by }{\b cout}{), a colon and a space, the line from the file, and a newline provided by }{\b endl}{
. When you compile and run it you\rquote ll see the effect is to add line numbers to the file.
\par Because of the way that the \lquote }{\b >>}{\rquote  operator works with iostreams, you can easily modify the above program so that it breaks up the input into whitespace-separated words instead of lines:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C02:GetWords.cpp
\par // Break a file into whitespace-separated words
\par #include <string>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <vector>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
