\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {Since C++ is based on C, you must be familiar with the syntax of C in order to program in C++, just as you must be reasonably fluent in algebra in order to tackle calculus.

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you\rquote ve never seen C before}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {procedural language}}}{
, this chapter will give you a decent background in the style of C used in C++. If you are familiar with the style of C described in the first edition of Kernighan & Ritchie (often called K&R C}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe 
{K&R C}}}{) you will find some new and different features in C++ as well as in Standard C. If you are familiar with Standard C, you should skim through this chapter looking for features 
that are particular to C++. Note that there are some fundamental C++ features introduced here, although they are basic ideas that are akin to the features in C. The more sophisticated C++ features will not be introduced until later chapters.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright {This chapter is a fairly fast coverage of C constructs, with the understanding that you\rquote 
ve had some experience programming in another language. If after reading the chapter you still don\rquote t feel comfortable with the fundamentals, you may want to consider purchasing }{\i Thinking in C: Foundations for Java & C++}{
 by Chuck Allison (published by MindView, Inc., and available at }{\b http://www.MindView.net}{, where you\rquote ll find the introductory lecture as a free demonstration). This is a seminar on a CD-ROM, much like the CD packaged with
 this book, and its goal is to take you carefully through the fundamentals of the C language, but focusing on the knowledge necessary for you to be able to move on to the C++ or Java languages rather than trying to make you an expert in all the dark corne
rs of C (one of the reasons for using a higher-level language like C++ or Java is precisely so we can avoid many of these dark corners). It also contains excercises and guided solutions.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451775999}Creating functions{\*\bkmkend _Toc451775999}}{\pard\plain 
\s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \v\f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {creating functions in C and C++}}}{\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \v\f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {function\: creating}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In old (pre-Standard) C, you could call a function with any number or type of arguments, and the compiler wouldn\rquote 
t complain. Everything seemed fine until you ran the program. You got mysterious results (or worse, the program crashed) with no hi
nts as to why. The lack of help with argument passing and the enigmatic bugs that resulted is probably one reason why C was dubbed a \ldblquote high-level assembly language}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
high-level assembly language}}}{.\rdblquote  Pre-Standard C programmers just adapted to it.
\par Standard C and C++ use a feature called }{\i function prototyping}{.}{\i  }{With function prototyping, you must use a description of the types of arguments when declaring and defining a function. This description is the \ldblquote prototype.\rdblquote 
 When the function is called, the compiler uses the prototype to ensure the proper arguments are passed in, and that the return value is treated correctly. If the programmer makes a mistake when calling the function, the compiler catches the mistake.

\par Essentially, you learned about function prototyping 
(without naming it as such) in the previous chapter, since the form of function declaration in C++ requires proper prototyping. In a function prototype, the argument list contains the types of arguments that must be passed to the function and (optionally 
for the declaration) identifiers for the arguments. The order and type of the arguments must match in the declaration, definition and function call. Here\rquote s an example of a function prototype in a declaration:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int translate(float x, float y, float z);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You do not use the same form when declaring variables in function prototypes as you do in ordinary variable definitions. That is, you cannot say: }{\b float x, y, z}{. You must indicate the type of }{
\i each}{ argument. In a function declaration, the following form is also acceptable:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int translate(float, float, float);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Since the compiler doesn\rquote t do anything but check for types when the function is called, the identifiers are only included for clarity, when someone is reading the code.
\par In the function definition, names are required because the arguments are referenced inside the function:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int translate(float x, float y, float z) \{
\par   x = y = z;
\par   // ...
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It turns out this rule only applies to C. In C++, an argument may be unnamed }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {unnamed arguments}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {argument\: unnamed}}}{
in the argument list of the function definition. Since it is unnamed, you cannot use it in the function body, of course. The reason unnamed arguments are allowed is to give the programmer a way to \ldblquote reserve space in the argument list.\rdblquote 
 Whoever uses the f
unction must still call the function with the proper arguments. However, the person creating the function can then use the argument in the future without forcing modification of code that calls the function. This option of ignoring an argument in the list
 is also possible if you leave the name in, but you will get an obnoxious warning message about the value being unused every time you compile the function. The warning is eliminated if you remove the name.
\par C and C++ have two other ways to declare an argument list. If you have an empty argument list you can declare it as }{\b func(\~)}{
 in C++, which tells the compiler there are exactly zero arguments. You should be aware that this only means an empty argument list in C++. In C it means \ldblquote an indeterminate number of arguments (which is a \ldblquote hole\rdblquote 
 in C since it disables type checking in that case). In both C and C++, the declaration }{\b func(void);}{ means an empty argument list. The }{\b void}{ keyword means \ldblquote nothing\rdblquote  in this case (it can also mean \ldblquote no type
\rdblquote  in the case of pointers, as you\rquote ll see later in this chapter).}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {argument\: indeterminate list}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
indeterminate argument list}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {void\: argument list}}}{
\par The other option for argument lists occurs when you don\rquote t know how many arguments or what type of arguments you will have; this is called a variable argument list}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {variable argument list}
}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {argument\: variable list}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: variable argument list}}}{. This \ldblquote uncertain argument list\rdblquote 
 is represented by ellipses (}{\b ...}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {...}}}{. Defining a function with a variable argumen
t list is significantly more complicated than defining a regular function. You can use a variable argument list for a function that has a fixed set of arguments if (for some reason) you want to disable the error checks of function prototyping. Because of 
this, you should restrict your use of variable argument lists to C, and avoid them in C++ (where, as you\rquote ll learn, there are much better alternatives). Handling variable argument lists is described in the library section of your local C guide.

\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Function return values}{\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \v\f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {
returning a value from a function}}}{\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \v\f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {function\: return value}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A C++ function prototype must specify the return value type of the function (in C, if you leave off the return value type it defaults to }{\b int}{). The return type specification prec
edes the function name. To specify that no value is returned, use the }{\b void}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b void}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b return value, void}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b function\: void return value}}}{\b  }{keyword. This will generate an error if you try to return a value from the function. Here are some complete function prototypes:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int f1(void); // Returns an int, takes no arguments
\par int f2(); // Like f1() in C++ but not in Standard C!
\par float f3(float, int, char, double); // Returns a float
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void f4(void); // Takes no arguments, returns nothing
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To return a value from a function, you use the }{\b return}{ statement. }{\b return}{ exits the function, back to the point right after the function call. If }{\b return}{
 has an argument, that argument becomes the return value of the function. If a function says that it will return a particular type, then each }{\b return }{statement must return that type. You can have more than one }{\b return}{
 statement in a function definition:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Return.cpp
\par // Use of "return"
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par char cfunc(int i) \{
\par   if(i == 0)
\par     return 'a';
\par   if(i == 1)
\par     return 'g';
\par   if(i == 5)
\par     return 'z';
\par   return 'c';
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   cout << "type an integer: ";
\par   int val;
\par   cin >> val;
\par   cout << cfunc(val) << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In }{\b cfunc(\~)}{, the first }{\b if}{ that evaluates to }{\b true}{ exits the function via the }{\b return}{
 statement. Notice that a function declaration is not necessary because the function definition appears before it is used in }{\b main(\~)}{, so the compiler knows about it from that function definition.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776000}Using the C function library{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776000}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {C\: function library}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {function\: C library}}}{

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {All the functions in your local C function library are available while you are programming in C++. You should look hard at the function library before defining your own function \endash  there\rquote 
s a good chance that someone has already solved your problem for you, and probably given it a lot more thought and debugging.
\par A word of caution, though: many compilers include a lot of extra functions that make life even easier and are very tempting to use, but are not part of the Standard C library
. If you are certain you will never want to move the application to another platform (and who is certain of that?), go ahead \endash 
use those functions and make your life easier. If you want your application to be portable, you should restrict yourself to Standa
rd library functions. If you must perform platform-specific activities, try to isolate that code in one spot so it can easily be changed when porting to another platform. In C++, platform-specific activities are often encapsulated in a class, which is the
 ideal solution.
\par The formula for using a library function is as follows: first, find the function in your programming reference (many programming references will index the function by category as well as alphabetically). The description of the function shou
ld include a section that demonstrates the syntax of the code. The top of this section usually has at least one }{\b #include}{ line, showing you the header file containing the function prototype. Duplicate this }{\b #include}{
 line in your file, so the function is properly declared}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {declaration\: function}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: declaring}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {header\: file}}}{. Now you can call the function in the same way it appears in the syntax section. If you make a mistake, the compiler will discover it by comparing your function call to 
the function prototype in the header, and tell you about your error. The linker searches the standard library by default, so that\rquote s all you need to do: include the header file, and call the function.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776001}Creating your own libraries with the librarian{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776001} }{\pard\plain 
\s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {creating your own libraries with the librarian }}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {librarian}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {libraries, creating}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can collect your own functions together into a library. Most programming packages come with a librarian that manages groups of object modules. Each librarian has it
s own commands, but the general idea is this: if you want to create a library, make a header file containing the function prototypes for all the functions in your library. Put this header file somewhere in the preprocessor\rquote 
s search path, either in the local directory (so it can be found by }{\b #include "header"}{) or in the include directory (so it can be found by }{\b #include <header>}{
). Now take all the object modules and hand them to the librarian along with a name for the finished library (most librarians require a common extension, such as }{\b .lib}{ or }{\b .a}{
). Place the finished library where the other libraries reside, so the linker can find it. When you use your library, you will have to add something to the command line so the linker }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linker\:
 searching libraries}}}{knows to search the library for the functions you call. You must find all the details in your local manual, since they vary from system to system.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776002}Controlling execution{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776002} }{\pard\plain 
\s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \v\f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {controlling execution }}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This section covers the execution control statements in C+
+. You must be familiar with these statements before you can read and write C or C++ code.
\par C++ uses all C\rquote s execution control statements. These include }{\b if-else}{, }{\b while}{, }{\b do-while}{, }{\b for}{, and a selection statement called }{\b switch}{. C++ also allows the infamous }{\b goto}{, which will be avoided in this book.

\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776003}True and false{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776003}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
All conditional statements use the truth or falsehood of a conditional expression to determine the execution path. An example of a conditional expression is }{\b A == B}{. This uses the conditional operator }{\b ==}{ to see if the variable }{\b A}{
 is equivalent to the variable B}{\b .}{ The expression produces a boolean }{\b true }{or }{\b false}{ (these are keywords only in C++; in C an expression is \ldblquote true\rdblquote  if it evaluates to a nonzero value). Other conditional operators are }
{\b >}{, }{\b <}{, }{\b >=}{, etc. Conditional statements are covered more fully later in this chapter.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776004}if-else{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776004}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {if-else}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {else}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b if-else}{ statement can exist in two forms: with or without the }{\b else}{. The two forms are:
\par }{\b if}{(expression)\line }{\b   statement
\par }{or
\par }{\b if}{(expression)\line }{\b   statement\line else\line   statement
\par }{The \ldblquote expression\rdblquote  evaluates to }{\b true}{ or }{\b false}{. The \ldblquote statement\rdblquote 
 means either a simple statement terminated by a semicolon or compound statement, which is a group of simple statements enclosed in braces. Any time the word \ldblquote statement\rdblquote  is used, it always
 implies that the statement is simple or compound. Note this statement can also be another }{\b if}{, so they can be strung together.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Ifthen.cpp
\par // Demonstration of if and if-else conditionals
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int i;
\par   cout << "type a number and 'Enter'" << endl;
\par   cin >> i;
\par   if(i > 5)
\par     cout << "It's greater than 5" << endl;
\par   else
\par     if(i < 5)
\par       cout << "It's less than 5 " << endl;
\par     else
\par       cout << "It's equal to 5 " << endl;
\par 
\par   cout << "type a number and 'Enter'" << endl;
\par   cin >> i;
\par   if(i < 10)
\par     if(i > 5)  // "if" is just another statement
\par       cout << "5 < i < 10" << endl;
\par     else
\par       cout << "i <= 5" << endl;
\par   else // Matches "if(i < 10)"
\par     cout << "i >= 10" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It is conve
ntional to indent the body of a control flow statement so the reader may easily determine where it begins and ends}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 
\chftn }{ Note that all conventions seem to end after the agreement that some sort of indentation take place. The feud between styles of code formatting is unending. See appendix A for the description of this book\rquote s coding style.}}}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776005}while{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776005}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {while}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b while}{, }{\b do-while}{ and }{\b for}{
 control looping. A statement repeats until the controlling expression evaluates to }{\b false}{. The form of a }{\b while}{ loop is
\par }{\b while}{(expression)\line }{\b   statement
\par }{The expression is evaluated once at the beginning of the loop, and again before each further iteration of the statement.
\par This example stays in the body of the }{\b while}{ loop until you type the secret number or press control-C.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Guess.cpp
\par // Guess a number (demonstrates "while")
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int secret = 15;
\par   int guess = 0;
\par   // "!=" is the "not-equal" conditional:
\par   while(guess != secret) \{ // Compound statement
\par     cout << "guess the number: ";
\par     cin >> guess;
\par   \}
\par   cout << "You guessed it!" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b while}{\rquote s}{\b  }{conditional expression is not restricted to a simple test as in the above example; it can be as complicated as you like as long as it produces a }{\b true}{ or }{\b 
false}{ result. You will even see code where the loop has no body, just a bare semicolon:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {while(/* Do a lot here */)
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  ;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In these cases the programmer has written the conditional expression not only to perform the test but also to do the work.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776006}do-while{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776006}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {do-while}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The form of }{\b do-while}{ is
\par }{\b do\line   statement\line while}{(expression)}{\b ;
\par }{The }{\b do-while}{ is different from the while because the statement always executes at least once, even if the expression evaluates to false the first time. In a regular }{\b while}{, i
f the conditional is false the first time the statement never executes.
\par If a }{\b do-while}{ is used in }{\b Guess.cpp}{, the variable }{\b guess}{ does not need an initial dummy value, since it is initialized by the }{\b cin}{ statement before it is tested:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Guess2.cpp
\par // The guess program using do-while
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int secret = 15;
\par   int guess; // No initialization needed here
\par   do \{
\par     cout << "guess the number: ";
\par     cin >> guess; // Initialization happens
\par   \}   while(guess != secret);
\par   cout << "You got it!" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {For some reason, most programmers tend to avoid }{\b do-while}{ and just work with }{\b while}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776007}for{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776007}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {for}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A }{\b for}{ loop performs initialization before the first iteration. Then it performs conditional testing and, at 
the end of each iteration, some form of \ldblquote stepping.\rdblquote  The form of the }{\b for}{ loop is:
\par }{\b for(}{\i initialization}{\b ;}{ }{\i conditional}{\b ;}{ }{\i step}{\b )}{\line }{\b   statement
\par }{Any of the expressions }{\i initialization}{, }{\i conditional}{ or }{\i step}{ may be empty. The }{\i initialization}{ code executes once at the very beginning. The }{\i conditional}{
 is tested before each iteration (if it evaluates to false at the beginning, the statement never executes). At the end of each loop, the }{\i step}{ executes.
\par }{\b for}{ loops are usually used for \ldblquote counting\rdblquote  tasks:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Charlist.cpp
\par // Display all the ASCII characters
\par // Demonstrates "for"
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 128; i = i + 1)
\par     if (i != 26)  // ANSI Terminal Clear screen
\par       cout << " value: " << i <<
\par       " character: " << 
\par       char(i) << endl; // Type conversion
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You may notice that the variable }{\b i}{
 is defined at the point where it is used, instead of at the beginning of the block denoted by the open curly brace \lquote }{\b \{}{\rquote . This is in contrast to traditional procedural languages (in
cluding C), which require that all variables be defined at the beginning of the block. This will be discussed later in this chapter.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776008}The }{\b break}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {break}}}{ and }{\b continue}{ }{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {continue}}}{
Keywords{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776008} 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Inside the body of any of the looping constructs }{\b while}{, }{\b do-while}{ or }{\b for}{,}{\b  }{
you can control the flow of the loop using }{\b break}{ and }{\b continue}{. }{\b break}{ quits the loop without executing the rest of the statements in the loop. }{\b continue}{
 stops the execution of the current iteration and goes back to the beginning of the loop to begin a new iteration.
\par As an example of the use of }{\b break}{ and }{\b continue}{, this program is a very simple menu system:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Menu.cpp
\par // Simple menu program demonstrating
\par // the use of "break" and "continue"
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   char c; // To hold response
\par   while(true) \{
\par     cout << "MAIN MENU:" << endl;
\par     cout << "l: left, r: right, q: quit -> ";
\par     cin >> c;
\par     if(c == 'q')
\par       break; // Out of "while(1)"
\par     if(c == 'l') \{
\par       cout << "LEFT MENU:" << endl;
\par       cout << "select a or b: ";
\par       cin >> c;
\par       if(c == 'a') \{
\par         cout << "you chose 'a'" << endl;
\par         continue; // Back to main menu
\par       \}
\par       if(c == 'b') \{
\par         cout << "you chose 'b'" << endl;
\par         continue; // Back to main menu
\par       \}
\par       else \{
\par         cout << "you didn't choose a or b!"
\par              << endl;
\par         continue; // Back to main menu
\par       \}
\par     \}
\par     if(c == 'r') \{
\par       cout << "RIGHT MENU:" << endl;
\par       cout << "select c or d: ";
\par       cin >> c;
\par       if(c == 'c') \{
\par         cout << "you chose 'c'" << endl;
\par         continue; // Back to main menu
\par       \}
\par       if(c == 'd') \{
\par         cout << "you chose 'd'" << endl;
\par         continue; // Back to main menu
\par       \}
\par       else \{
\par         cout << "you didn't choose c or d!" 
\par              << endl;
\par         continue; // Back to main menu
\par       \}
\par     \}
\par     cout << "you must type l or r or q!" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   cout << "quitting menu..." << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If the user selects \lquote q\rquote  in the main menu, the }{\b break}{ keyword is used to quit, o
therwise the program just continues to execute indefinitely. After each of the sub-menu selections, the }{\b continue}{ keyword is used to pop back up to the beginning of the while loop.
\par The }{\b while(true)}{ statement is the equivalent of saying \ldblquote do this loop forever.\rdblquote  The }{\b break}{ statement allows you to break out of this infinite while loop when the user types a \lquote q.\rquote  
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776009}switch{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776009}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {switch}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A }{\b switch}{
 statement selects from among pieces of code based on the value of an integral expression. Its form is:
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {\b switch(}{selector}{\b ) \{}{\line   }{\b case }{integral-value1 }{\b :}{ statement; }{\b break;}{\line   }{\b case }{integral-value2 }{\b :}{ statement; }{\b break;}{\line   }{\b case }{integral-value3 }{\b :}{
 statement; }{\b break;}{\line   }{\b case }{integral-value4 }{\b :}{ statement; }{\b break;}{\line   }{\b case }{integral-value5 }{\b :}{ statement; }{\b break;}{\line           (...)\line   }{\b default:}{ statement;\line }{\b \}}{
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright {\i Selector}{ is an expression that produces an integral value. The }{\b switch}{ compares the result of }{\i selector}{ to each }{\i 
integral-value}{. If it finds a match, the corresponding statement (simple or compound) executes. If no match occurs, the }{\b default}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar
\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {default }}}{statement executes.
\par You will notice in the above definition that each }{\b case}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b case}}}{ ends with a }{\b break}{
, which causes execution to jump to the end of the }{\b switch}{ body (the closing brace that completes the }{\b switch}{). This is the conventional way to build a }{\b switch}{ statement, but the }{\b break}{ is optional. If it is missing, your }{\b 
case }{\ldblquote drops through\rdblquote  to the one after it. That is, the code for the following }{\b case}{ statements execute until a }{\b break}{ is encountered. Although you don\rquote t usually want this kind of behavior, it can be u
seful to an experienced programmer.
\par The }{\b switch}{ statement is a very clean way to implement multi-way selection (i.e., selecting from among a number of different execution paths), but it requires a selector that evaluates to an integral value at compile-time
. If you want to use, for example, a }{\b string}{ object as a selector, it won\rquote t work in a }{\b switch}{ statement. For a }{\b string}{ selector, you must instead use a series of }{\b if}{ statements and compare the }{\b string}{
 inside the conditional.
\par The menu example shown previously provides a particularly nice example of a }{\b switch}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Menu2.cpp
\par // A menu using a switch statment
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   bool quit = false;  // Flag for quitting
\par   while(quit == false) \{
\par     cout << "Select a, b, c or q to quit: ";
\par     char response;
\par     cin >> response;
\par     switch(response) \{
\par       case 'a' : cout << "you chose 'a'" << endl;
\par                  break;
\par       case 'b' : cout << "you chose 'b'" << endl;
\par                  break;
\par       case 'c' : cout << "you chose 'c'" << endl;
\par                  break;
\par       case 'q' : cout << "quitting menu" << endl;
\par                  quit = true;
\par                  break;
\par       default  : cout << "Please use a,b,c or q!"
\par                  << endl;
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b quit }{flag is a }{\b bool}{, short for \ldblquote Boolean,\rdblquote  which is a type you\rquote 
ll only find in C++. It can only have the keyword values }{\b true}{ or }{\b false}{.}{\b  }{Selecting \lquote q\rquote  sets the }{\b quit}{ flag to }{\b true}{. The next time the selector is evaluated, }{\b quit == false}{ returns }{\b false}{
 so the body of the }{\b while}{ does not execute.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776010}Recursion{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776010}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Recursion is an interesting and sometimes useful programming technique whereby you call the function that you\rquote re in. Of course, if this is all you do you\rquote ll keep calling the function you
\rquote re in until you run out of memory, so there must be some way to \ldblquote bottom out\rdblquote  the recursive call. In the following example, this \ldblquote bottoming out\rdblquote 
 is accomplished by simply saying that the recursion will only go until the }{\b cat}{ exceeds \lquote Z\rquote :}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 
\chftn }{ Thanks to Kris C. Matson for suggesting this exercise topic.}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:CatsInHats.cpp
\par // Simple demonstration of recursion
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par void removeHat(char cat) \{
\par   for(char c = 'A'; c < cat; c++)
\par     cout << "  ";
\par   if(cat <= 'Z') \{
\par     cout << "cat " << cat << endl;
\par     removeHat(cat + 1); // Recursive call
\par   \} else
\par     cout << "VOOM!!!" << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   removeHat('A');
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In }{\b removeHat(\~)}{, you can see that as long as }{\b cat}{ is less than \lquote Z\rquote , }{\b removeHat(\~)}{ will be called from }{\i within}{ }{\b removeHat(\~)}{
, thus effecting the recursion. Each time }{\b removeHat(\~)}{ is called, its argument is one greater than the current }{\b cat}{ so the argument keeps increasing.
\par Recursion is often used when evaluating some sort of arbitrarily complex problem, since you aren\rquote t restricted to a particular \ldblquote size\rdblquote  for the solution \endash  the function can just keep recursing until it\rquote 
s reached the end of the problem.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid { {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776011}Introduction to operators{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776011}}{\pard\plain 
\s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \v\f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {C & C++ operators}}}{\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright 
\v\f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {operator\: C & C++}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can think of operators as a special type of function (you\rquote 
ll learn that C++ operator overloading treats operators precisely that way). An operator takes one or more arguments and produces a new value. The arguments are in a different form than ordinary function calls, but the effect is the same.
\par From your previous programming experience, you should be reasonably comfortable with the operators that have been used so far. The concepts of addition (}{\b +}{), subtraction and unary minus (}{\b -}{), multiplication (}{\b *}{), division (}{\b /}{
) and assignment(}{\b =}{) all have essentialy the same meaning in any programming language. The full set of operators are enumerated later in this chapter.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776012}Precedence{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776012}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar
\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: precedence}}}{
Operator precedence defines the order in which an expression evaluates when several different operators are present. C and C++ have specific rules to determine the order of evaluation. The easiest to remem
ber is that multiplication and division happen before addition and subtraction. After that, if an expression isn\rquote t transparent to you it probably won\rquote 
t be for anyone reading the code, so you should use parentheses to make the order of evaluation explicit. For example:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {A = X + Y - 2/2 + Z;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {has a very different meaning from the same statement with a particular grouping of parentheses:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {A = X + (Y - 2)/(2 + Z);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {(Try evaluating the result with X = 1, Y = 2 and Z = 3.)
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776013}Auto increment }{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {increment }}}{and decrement{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776013}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright 
\v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {decrement}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
C, and therefore C++, is full of shortcuts. Shortcuts can make code much easier to type, and sometimes much harder to read. Perhaps the C language designers thought it would be easier to understand a tricky piece of code if your eyes didn\rquote 
t have to scan as large an area of print.
\par One of the nicer shortcuts is the auto-increment}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {auto-increment}}}{ and auto-decrement}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {auto-decrement}}}{ operators. You often use these to change loop variables, which control the number of times a loop exe
cutes.
\par The auto-decrement operator is }{\b --}{ and means \ldblquote decrease by one unit.\rdblquote  The auto-increment operator is }{\b ++}{ and means \ldblquote increase by one unit.\rdblquote  If }{\b A}{ is an }{\b int}{, for example, the expression }{\b 
++A}{ is equivalent to (}{\b A = A + 1}{). Auto-increment and auto-decrement operators produce the value of the variable as a result. If the operator appears before the variable, (i.e., }{\b ++A}{
), the operation is first performed and the resulting value is produced. If the operator appears after the variable (i.e. }{\b A++}{), the current value is produced, and then the operation is performed. For example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:AutoIncrement.cpp
\par // Shows use of auto-increment
\par // and auto-decrement operators.
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int i = 0;
\par   int j = 0;
\par   cout << ++i << endl; // Pre-increment
\par   cout << j++ << endl; // Post-increment
\par   cout << --i << endl; // Pre-decrement
\par   cout << j-- << endl; // Post decrement
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you\rquote ve been wondering about the name \ldblquote C++,\rdblquote  now you understand. It implies \ldblquote 
one step beyond C.\rdblquote 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776014}Introduction to data}{\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \v\f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {C++\: data}}}{ types{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776014}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\i Data types }{
define the way you utilize storage (memory) in the programs that you write. By specifying a data type, you tell the compiler how to create a particular piece of storage, and also how to manipulate that storage.
\par Data types can be built-in}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {data type\: built-in}}}{ or abstract}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {data type\: abstract}}}{. A built-in data type}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {built-in data type}}}{ is one that the compiler intrinsically understands, one that is wired directly into the compiler. The types of built
-in data are almost identical in C and C++. In contrast, a user-defined data type}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {user-defined data type}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {data type\: user-defined
}}}{ is one that you or another programmer create as a class. These are commonly referred to as abstract data types}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {abstract data type}}}{
. The compiler knows how to handle built-in types when it starts up; it \ldblquote learns\rdblquote  how to handle abstract data types by reading header files}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {header\: file}}}{
 containing class declarations (you\rquote ll learn about this in later chapters).
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776015}Basic built-in types{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776015}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {built-in types, basic}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {type\: basic built-in}
}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {values, minimum and maximum}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {size, built-in types}}}{
The Standard C specification for built-in types (which C++ inherits) doesn\rquote t say how many bits each of the built-in types 
must contain. Instead, it stipulates the minimum and maximum values that the built-in type must be able to hold. When a machine is based on binary, this maximum value can be directly translated into a minimum number of bits necessary to hold that value. H
o
wever, if a machine uses, for instance, binary-coded decimal (BCD) to represent numbers then the amount of space in the machine required to hold the maximum numbers for each data type will be different. The minimum and maximum values that can be stored in
 the various data types are defined in the system header files }{\b limits.h}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b LIMITS.H}}}{\b  }{and }{\b float.h}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {floating point
\: FLOAT.H}}}{ (in C++ you will generally }{\b #include <climits>}{ and }{\b <cfloat>}{ instead).
\par C and C++ have four basic built-in data types, described here for binary-based machines. A }{\b char}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b char}}}{
 is for character storage and uses a minimum of 8 bits (one byte) of storage. An }{\b int}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b int}}}{ stores an integral number and uses a minimum of two bytes of storage. The }{\b float}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b floating point\: float}}}{ and }{\b double}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b double}}}{ types store floating-point numbers}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {floating point\: numbers}}}{, usually in IEEE floating-point format}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {IEEE floating-point format}}}{. }{\b float}{
 is for single-precision floating point}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {single-precision floating point}}}{ and }{\b double}{ is for double-precision floating point}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {double precision floating point}}}{.
\par As previously mentioned, you can define variables anywhere in a scope, and you can define and initialize them}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {defining\: and initializing variables}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {initializing variables at definition}}}{ at the same time. Here\rquote s how to define variables using the four basic data types:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Basic.cpp
\par // Defining the four basic data
\par // types in C and C++
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   // Definition without initialization:
\par   char protein;
\par   int carbohydrates;
\par   float fiber;
\par   double fat;
\par   // Simultaneous definition & initialization:
\par   char pizza = 'A', pop = 'Z';
\par   int dongdings = 100, twinkles = 150, 
\par     heehos = 200;
\par   float chocolate = 3.14159;
\par   // Exponential notation:
\par   double fudge_ripple = 6e-4; 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The first part of the program defines variables of the four basic data types without initializing them. If you don\rquote 
t initialize a variable, the Standard says that its contents are undefined (usually, this means they contain garbage). The second part of the program defines and initializes variables at the same time (it\rquote 
s always best, if possible, to provide an initialization value at the point of definition). Notice the use of exponential notation}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exponential\: notation}}}{ in the constant 6e-4, meaning: 
\ldblquote 6 times 10 to the minus fourth power.\rdblquote 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374157}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776016}bool}{, }{\b true}{, & }{\b false}{{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374157}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776016}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Before }{\b bool }{became part of Standard C++, everyone tended to use different techniques in order to produce Boolean-like behavior. }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
