\sa120\widctlpar\intbl\adjustright {First operand converts to }{\b bool}{ value\cell }\pard \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright {\row }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par Because there\rquote s a lot of existing code that uses an }{\b int}{ to represent a flag, the compiler will implicitly convert from an }{\b int}{ to a }{\b bool}{. Ideally, the compiler will give you a warning as a suggestion to correct the situation.

\par An idiom that falls under \ldblquote poor programming style\rdblquote  is the use of }{\b ++}{ to set a flag to true. This is still allowed, but }{\i deprecated}{, which means tha
t at some time in the future it will be made illegal. The problem is that you\rquote re making an implicit type conversion from }{\b bool}{ to }{\b int}{, incrementing the value (perhaps beyond the range of the normal }{\b bool}{
 values of zero and one), and then implicitly converting it back again.
\par Pointers (which will be introduced later in this chapter) will also be automatically converted to }{\b bool}{ when necessary.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776017}Specifiers{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776017}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {specifiers}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Specifiers modify the meanings of the basic built-in types, and expand the built-in types to a much larger set. There are four specifiers: }{\b long}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {long}}}{, }{\b short}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {short}}}{, }{\b signed}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b signed}}}{ and }{\b unsigned}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {unsigned}}}{.
\par }{\b long}{ and }{\b short}{ modify the maximum and minimum values that a data type will hold. A plain }{\b int}{ must be at least the size of a }{\b short}{. The size hierarchy for integral types is: }{\b short}{ }{\b int}{, }{\b int}{, }{\b long}{ }{\b 
int}{. All the sizes could conceivably be the same, as long as they satisfy the minimum/maximum value requirements. On a machine with a 64-bit word, for instance, all the data types might be 64 bits.
\par The size hierarchy for floating point numbers}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {floating point\: number size hierarchy}}}{ is: }{\b float}{, }{\b double}{, and }{\b long}{ }{\b double}{. \ldblquote Long float\rdblquote 
 is not a legal type. There are no }{\b short}{ floating-point numbers.
\par The }{\b signed}{ and }{\b unsigned}{ specifiers tell the compiler how to use the sign bit with integral types and characters (floating-point numbers always contain a sign). An }{\b unsigned}{
 number does not keep track of the sign and thus has an extra bit available, so it can store positive numbers twice as large as the positive numbers that can be stored in a }{\b signed}{ number. }{\b signed}{ is the default and is only necessary with }{
\b char}{; }{\b char}{ may or may not default to }{\b signed}{. By specifying }{\b signed}{ }{\b char}{, you force the sign bit to be used. 
\par The following example shows the size of the data types in bytes by using the }{\b sizeof(\~)}{ operator, introduced later in this chapter:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Specify.cpp
\par // Demonstrates the use of specifiers
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   char c;
\par   unsigned char cu;
\par   int i;
\par   unsigned int iu;
\par   short int is;
\par   short iis; // Same as short int
\par   unsigned short int isu;
\par   unsigned short iisu;
\par   long int il;
\par   long iil;  // Same as long int
\par   unsigned long int ilu;
\par   unsigned long iilu;
\par   float f;
\par   double d;
\par   long double ld;
\par   cout 
\par     << "\\n char= " << sizeof(c)
\par     << "\\n unsigned char = " << sizeof(cu)
\par     << "\\n int = " << sizeof(i)
\par     << "\\n unsigned int = " << sizeof(iu)
\par     << "\\n short = " << sizeof(is)
\par     << "\\n unsigned short = " << sizeof(isu)
\par     << "\\n long = " << sizeof(il) 
\par     << "\\n unsigned long = " << sizeof(ilu)
\par     << "\\n float = " << sizeof(f)
\par     << "\\n double = " << sizeof(d)
\par     << "\\n long double = " << sizeof(ld) 
\par     << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Be aware that the results you get by running this program will probably be 
different from one machine/operating system/compiler to the next, since (as previously mentioned) the only thing that must be consistent is that each different type hold the minimum and maximum values specified in the Standard.
\par When you are modifying an }{\b int}{ with }{\b short}{ or }{\b long}{, the keyword }{\b int}{ is optional, as shown above.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776018}Introduction to Pointers{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776018}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Whenever you run a program, it is first loaded (typically from disk) into the computer\rquote s memory. Thus, all elements of your program are located somewhere in memory.
 Memory is typically laid out as a sequential series of memory locations; we usually refer to these locations as eight-bit }{\i bytes }{
but actually the size of each space depends on the architecture of that particular machine and is usually called that machine\rquote s }{\i word size}{. Each space can be uniquely distinguished from all other spaces by its }{\i address}{
. For the purposes of this discussion, we\rquote ll just say that all machines use bytes which have sequential addresses starting at zero and going up to however much memory you have in your computer.
\par Since your program lives in memory while it\rquote s being run, every element of your program has an address. Suppose we start with a simple program:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:YourPets1.cpp
