}{All the variables, and even the function, occupy storage. As you\rquote ll see, it turns out that what an element is and the way you define it usually determines the area of memory where that element is placed.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There is an operator in C and C++ that will tell you the address of an element. This is the \lquote }{\b &}{\rquote  operator. All you do is precede the identifier name with \lquote }{\b &}{\rquote 
 and it will produce the address of that identifier. }{\b YourPets1.cpp}{ can be modified to print out the addresses of all its elements, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:YourPets2.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int dog, cat, bird, fish;
\par 
\par void f(int pet) \{
\par   cout << "pet id number: " << pet << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int i, j, k;
\par   cout << "f(): " << (long)&f << endl;
\par   cout << "dog: " << (long)&dog << endl;
\par   cout << "cat: " << (long)&cat << endl;
\par   cout << "bird: " << (long)&bird << endl;
\par   cout << "fish: " << (long)&fish << endl;
\par   cout << "i: " << (long)&i << endl;
\par   cout << "j: " << (long)&j << endl;
\par   cout << "k: " << (long)&k << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b (long)}{ is a }{\i cast}{. It says \ldblquote don\rquote t treat this as it\rquote s normal type, instead treat it as a }{\b long}{.\rdblquote  The cast isn\rquote t essential, but if it wasn
\rquote t there the addresses would have been printed out in hexadecimal instead, so casting to a }{\b long}{ makes things a little more readable.
\par The results of this program will vary depending on your computer, OS and all sorts of other factors, but it will still give you some interesting insights. For a single run on my computer, the results looked like this:
