}{You can see how the variables that are defined inside }{\b main(\~)}{ are in a different area than the variables defined outside of }{\b main(\~)}{; you\rquote ll understand why as you learn more about the language. Also, }{\b f(\~)}{
 appears to be in its own area; code is typically separated from data in memory.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Another interesting thing to 
note is that variables defined one right after the other appear to be placed contiguously in memory. They are separated by the number of bytes that are required by their data type. Here, the only data type used is }{\b int}{, and }{\b cat}{
 is four bytes away from }{\b dog}{, }{\b bird}{ is four bytes away from }{\b cat}{, etc. So it would appear that, on this machine, an }{\b int}{ is four bytes long.
\par Other than this interesting experiment showing how memory is mapped out, what can you do with an address? The most important thing you can do is sto
re it inside another variable for later use. C and C++ have a special type of variable that holds an address. This variable is called a }{\i pointer}{.
\par The operator that defines a pointer is the same as the one used for multiplication: \lquote }{\b *}{\rquote . The compiler knows that it isn\rquote t multiplication because of the context in which it is used, as you shall see.
\par When you define a pointer, you must specify the type of variable it points to. You start out by giving the type name, then instead of immediately giving an identifier for the variable, you say \ldblquote wait, it\rquote s a pointer\rdblquote 
 by inserting a star between the type and the identifier. So a pointer to an }{\b int}{ looks like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int* ip; // ip points to an int variable
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The association of the \lquote }{\b *}{\rquote  with the type looks sensible and reads easily, but it can actually be a bit deceiving. Your inclination might be to say \ldblquote intpointer\rdblquote 
 as if it is a single discrete type. However, with an }{\b int}{ or other basic data type, it\rquote s possible to say:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int a, b, c;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {whereas with a pointer, you\rquote d }{\i like}{ to say:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int* ipa, ipb, ipc;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {C syntax (and by inheritance, C++ syntax) does not allow such sensible expressions. In the above definitions, only }{\b ipa}{ is a pointer, but }{\b ipb}{ and }{\b ipc}{ are ordinary }{\b int}{
s (you can say that \ldblquote * binds more tightly to the identifer\rdblquote ). Consequently, the best results can be achieved by using only one definition per line: you still get the sensible syntax without the confusion:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int* ipa;
\par int* ipb;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int* ipc;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Since a general guideline for C++ programming is that you should always initialize a variable
 at the point of definition, this form actually works better. For example, the above variables are not initialized to any particular value; they hold garbage. It\rquote s much better to say something like:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int a = 47;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int* ipa = &a;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now both }{\b a}{ and }{\b ipa}{ have been initialized, and }{\b ipa}{ holds the address of }{\b a}{.
\par Once you have an initialized pointer, the most basic thing you can do with it is to use it to modify the value it points to. To access a variable through a pointer, you }{\i dereference}{ the pointer using the same ope
rator that you used to define it, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {*ipa = 100;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now }{\b a}{ contains the value 100 instead of 47.
\par These are the basics of pointers: you can hold an address, and you can use that address to modify the original variable. But the question still remains: why do you want to modify one variable using another variable as a proxy?
\par For this introductory view of pointers, we can put the answer into two broad categories:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls266\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls266\adjustright {To change \ldblquote 
outside objects\rdblquote  from within a function. This is perhaps the most basic use of pointers, and it will be examined here.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls266\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls266\adjustright {
To achieve many other clever programming techniques, which you\rquote ll learn about in portions of the rest of the book.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776019}Modifying the outside object{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776019}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Ordinarily, when you pass an argument to a function, a copy of that argument is made inside the function. This is referred to as }{\i pass-by-value}{.}{\i  }{
You can see the effect of pass-by-value in the following program:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:PassByValue.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par void f(int a) \{
\par   cout << "a = " << a << endl;
\par   a = 5;
\par   cout << "a = " << a << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int x = 47;
\par   cout << "x = " << x << endl;
\par   f(x);
\par   cout << "x = " << x << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In }{\b f(\~)}{, }{\b a}{ is a }{\i local variable}{, so it only exists for the duration of the function call to }{\b f(\~)}{. Because it\rquote s a function argument, the value of }{\b a}{
 is initialized by the arguments that are passed when the function is called; in }{\b main(\~)}{ the argument is }{\b x}{ which has a value of 47, so this value is copied into }{\b a}{ when }{\b f(\~)}{ is called.
\par When you run this program you\rquote ll see:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {x = 47
\par a = 47
\par a = 5
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {x = 47
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Initially, of course, }{\b x}{ is 47. When }{\b f(\~)}{ is called, temporary space is created to hold the variable }{\b a}{ for the duration of the function call, and }{\b a}{
 is initialized by copying the value of }{\b x}{, which is verified by printing it out. Of course, you can change the value of }{\b a}{ and show that it is changed. But when }{\b f(\~)}{ is completed, the temporary space that was created for }{\b a}{
 disappears, and we see that the only connection that ever existed between }{\b a}{ and }{\b x}{ happened when the value of }{\b x}{ was copied into }{\b a}{.
\par When you\rquote re inside }{\b f(\~)}{, }{\b x}{ is the }{\i outside object}{ (my terminology), and changing the local variable does not affect the outside object, naturally enough, since they are two separate locations in storage. But what if you }{\i do
}{ want to modify the outside object? This is where pointers come in handy. In a sense, a pointer is an alias for another variable. So if we pass a }{\i pointer}{
 into a function instead of an ordinary value, we are actually passing an alias to the outside object, enabling the function to modify that outside object, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:PassAddress.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par void f(int* p) \{
\par   cout << "p = " << p << endl;
\par   cout << "*p = " << *p << endl;
\par   *p = 5;
\par   cout << "p = " << p << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int x = 47;
\par   cout << "x = " << x << endl;
\par   cout << "&x = " << &x << endl;
\par   f(&x);
\par   cout << "x = " << x << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now }{\b f(\~)}{ takes a pointer as an argument, and dereferences the pointer during assignment, and this causes the outside object }{\b x}{ to be modified. The output is:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {x = 47
\par &x = 0065FE00
\par p = 0065FE00
\par *p = 47
\par p = 0065FE00
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {x = 5
\par }\pard\plain \s26\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that the value contained in }{\b p }{is the same as address of }{\b x}{ \endash  the pointer }{\b p}{ does indeed point to }{\b x}{. If that isn\rquote t convincing enough, when }{\b p}{
 is dereferenced to assign the value 5, we see that the value of }{\b x}{ is now changed to 5 as well.
\par Thus, passing a pointer into a function will allow that function to modify the outside object. You\rquote ll see plenty of other uses for pointers later, but this is arguably the most basic and possibly the most common use.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776020}Introduction to C++ references{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776020}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Pointers work roughly the same in C and in C++, but C++ adds an additional way to pass an address into a function. This is }{\i pass-by-reference}{
 and it exists in several other programming languages so it was not a C++ invention.
\par Your initial perception of references may be that they are unnecessary \endash  that you could write all your programs without references. In general, this is true, with the exception of a few important places which you\rquote ll learn about later i
n the book. You\rquote 
ll also learn more about references later in the book, but the basic idea is the same as the above demonstration of pointer use: you can pass the address of an argument using a reference. The difference between references and pointers is that }{\i calling
}{ a function that takes references is cleaner, syntactically, than calling a function that takes pointers (and it is exactly this syntactic difference that makes references essential in certain situations). If }{\b PassAddress.cpp}{ is modified to use re
ferences, you can see the difference in the function call in }{\b main(\~)}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:PassReference.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par void f(int& r) \{
\par   cout << "r = " << r << endl;
\par   cout << "&r = " << &r << endl;
\par   r = 5;
\par   cout << "r = " << r << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int x = 47;
\par   cout << "x = " << x << endl;
\par   cout << "&x = " << &x << endl;
\par   f(x); // Looks like pass-by-value, 
\par         // is actually pass by reference
\par   cout << "x = " << x << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In }{\b f(\~)}{\rquote s argument list, instead of saying }{\b int*}{ to pass a pointer, you say }{\b int&}{ to pass a reference. Inside }{\b f(\~)}{, if you just say \lquote }{\b r}{\rquote 
 (which would produce the address if }{\b r}{ were a pointer) you get }{\i the value in the variable that }{\b\i r }{\i references}{. If you assign to }{\b r}{, you actually assign to the variable that }{\b r}{
 references. In fact, the only way to get the address that\rquote s held inside }{\b r}{ is with the \lquote }{\b &}{\rquote  operator.
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{, you can see the key effect of references in the syntax of the call to }{\b f(\~)}{, which is just }{\b f(x)}{. Even though this looks like an ordinary 
pass-by-value, the effect of the reference is that it actually takes the address and passes it in, rather than making a copy of the value. The output is:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {x = 47
\par &x = 0065FE00
\par r = 47
\par &r = 0065FE00
\par r = 5
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {x = 5
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {So you can see that pass-by-reference allows a 
function to modify the outside object, just like passing a pointer does (you can also observe that the reference obscures the fact that an address is being passed \endash 
 this will be examined later in the book). Thus, for this simple introduction you can assume that references are just a syntactically different way (sometimes referred to as \ldblquote syntactic sugar\rdblquote 
) to accomplish the same thing that pointers do: allow functions to change outside objects.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776021}Pointers and references as modifiers{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776021}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {So far, you\rquote ve seen the basic data types }{\b char}{, }{\b int}{, }{\b float}{ and }{\b double}{, along with the specifiers }{\b signed}{, }{\b unsigned}{, }{\b short}{ and }{\b long}{
, which can be used with the basic data types in almost any combination. Now we\rquote ve added pointers and references which are orthogonal to the basic data types and specifiers, so the possible combinations have just tripled:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:AllDefinitions.cpp
\par // All possible combinations of basic data types, 
\par // specifiers, pointers and references
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par void f1(char c, int i, float f, double d);
\par void f2(short int si, long int li, long double ld);
\par void f3(unsigned char uc, unsigned int ui, 
\par   unsigned short int usi, unsigned long int uli);
\par void f4(char* cp, int* ip, float* fp, double* dp);
\par void f5(short int* sip, long int* lip, 
\par   long double* ldp);
\par void f6(unsigned char* ucp, unsigned int* uip, 
\par   unsigned short int* usip, 
\par   unsigned long int* ulip);
\par void f7(char& cr, int& ir, float& fr, double& dr);
\par void f8(short int& sir, long int& lir, 
\par   long double& ldr);
\par void f9(unsigned char& ucr, unsigned int& uir, 
\par   unsigned short int& usir, 
\par   unsigned long int& ulir);
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int main() \{\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Pointers and references also work when passing objects into and out of functions; you\rquote ll learn about this in a later chapter.
\par There\rquote s one other type that works with pointers: }{\b void}{. If you state that a pointer is a }{\b void*}{, it means that any type of address at all can be assigned to that pointer (whereas if you have an }{\b int*}{
, you can only assign the address of an }{\b int}{ variable to that pointer). For example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:VoidPointer.cpp
\par int main() \{
\par   void* vp;
\par   char c;
\par   int i;
\par   float f;
\par   double d;
\par   // The address of ANY type can be
\par   // assigned to a void pointer:
\par   vp = &c;
\par   vp = &i;
\par   vp = &f;
\par   vp = &d;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Once you assign to a }{\b void*}{ you lose any information about what type it is. This means that before you can use the pointer, you must cast it to the correct type:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:CastFromVoidPointer.cpp
\par int main() \{
\par   int i = 99;
\par   void* vp = &i;
\par   // Can't dereference a void pointer:
\par   // *vp = 3; // Compile-time error
\par   // Must cast back to int before dereferencing:
\par   *((int*)vp) = 3;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The cast }{\b (int*)vp}{ takes the }{\b void*}{ and tells the compiler to treat it as an }{\b int*}{, and thus it can be successfully dereferenced. You might observe that this syntax is ugly,
 and it is, but it\rquote s worse than that \endash  the }{\b void*}{ introduces a hole in the language\rquote s type system. That is, it allows, or even promotes the treatment of one type as another type. In the above example, I treat an }{\b int}{
 as an }{\b int}{ by casting }{\b vp}{ to an }{\b int*}{, but there\rquote s nothing that says I can\rquote t cast it to a }{\b char*}{ or }{\b double*}{, which would modify a different amount of storage that had been allocated for the }{\b int}{
, possibly crashing your program. In general, }{\b void}{ pointers should be avoided, and only used in rare special cases, the likes of which you won\rquote t be ready to consider until significantly later in the book.
\par You cannot have a }{\b void}{ reference, for reasons that will be explained in a future chapter.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776022}Scoping{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776022}}{\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \v\f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {scoping}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Scoping rules tell you where a variable is valid, where it is created and where it gets destroyed (i.e., goes out of scope)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
going out of scope}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {variable\: going out of scope}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: going out of scope}}}{
. The scope of a variable extends from the point where it is defined to the first closing brace that matches the closest opening brace before the variable was defined. To illustrate:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Scope.cpp
\par // How variables are scoped
\par int main() \{
\par   int scp1;
\par   // scp1 visible here
\par   \{
\par     // scp1 still visible here
\par     //.....
\par     int scp2;
\par     // scp2 visible here
\par     //.....
\par     \{
\par       // scp1 & scp2 still visible here
\par       //..
\par       int scp3;
\par       // scp1, scp2 & scp3 visible here
\par       // ...
\par     \} // <-- scp3 destroyed here
\par     // scp3 not available here
\par     // scp1 & scp2 still visible here
\par     // ...
\par   \} // <-- scp2 destroyed here
\par   // scp3 & scp2 not available here
\par   // scp1 still visible here
\par   //..
\par \} // <-- scp1 destroyed here
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The above example shows when variables are visible, and when they are unavailable (that is, when they }{\i go out of scope}{). A variable can only be used when inside its scope. Scopes can be nested}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {nested scopes}}}{, indicated by matched pairs of braces inside other matched pairs of braces. Nesting means that you can access a variable in a scope that encloses 
the scope you are in. In the above example, the variable }{\b scp1}{ is available inside all of the other scopes, while }{\b scp3}{ is only available in the innermost scope.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776023}Defining variables on the fly{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776023}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {defining\: data on the fly}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As noted earlier in this chapter, there is a significant difference between C and C++ when defining variables}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {defining\: variables}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {variable\: defining}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C++\: difference with C when defining variables}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {C\: difference with C++ when defining variables}}}{. Both languages require
 that variables be defined before they are used, but C (and many other traditional procedural languages) forces you to define all the variables at the beginning of a scope, so that when the compiler creates a block it can allocate space for those variable
s. 
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright {While reading C code, a block of variable definitions is usually the first thing you see when entering a scope. Declaring all variables
}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {declaring variables\: point of declaration & scope}}}{
 at the beginning of the block requires the programmer to write in a particular way because of the implementation details of the language. Most people don\rquote 
t know all the variables they are going to use before they write the code, so they must keep jumping back to the beginning of the block to insert new variables, which is awkward and causes errors. These variable definitions don\rquote 
t usually mean much to the reader, and actually tend to be confusing because they appear apart from the context in which they are used.
\par C++ (not C) allows you to define variables anywhere in a scope}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {defining\: variable, anywhere in the scope}}}{
, so you can define a variable right before you use it. In addition, you can initialize the variable at the point you define it, which prevents a certain class of errors. Defining variables this way ma
kes the code much easier to write and reduces the errors you get from being forced to jump back and forth within a scope. It makes the code easier to understand because you see a variable defined in the context of its use. This is especially important whe
n you are defining and initializing a variable at the same time \endash  you can see the meaning of the initialization value by the way the variable is used.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {You can also define variables inside the control expressions of }{\b for}{ loops and }{\b while}{ loops, inside the conditional of an }{\b if}{ statement, and inside the selector statement of a }{\b switch}{. Here
\rquote s an example showing on-the-fly variable definitions:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:OnTheFly.cpp
\par // On-the-fly variable definitions
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   //..
\par   \{ // Begin a new scope
\par     int q = 0; // C requires definitions here
\par     //..
\par     // Define at point of use:
\par     for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++) \{ 
\par       q++; // q comes from a larger scope
\par       // Definition at the end of the scope:
\par       int p = 12; 
\par     \}
\par     int p = 1;  // A different p
\par   \} // End scope containing q & outer p
\par   cout << "Type characters:" << endl;
\par   while(char c = cin.get() != 'q') \{
\par     cout << c << " wasn't it" << endl;
\par     if(char x = c == 'a' || c == 'b')
\par       cout << "You typed a or b" << endl;
\par     else
\par       cout << "You typed " << x << endl;
\par   \}
\par   cout << "Type A, B, or C" << endl;
\par   switch(int i = cin.get()) \{
\par     case 'A': cout << "Snap" << endl; break;
\par     case 'B': cout << "Crackle" << endl; break;
\par     case 'C': cout << "Pop" << endl; break;
\par     default: cout << "Not A, B or C!" << endl;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the innermost scope, }{\b p}{ is defined right before the scope ends, so it is really a useless gesture (but it shows you can define a variable anywhere). The }{\b p}{ in the outer scope is in th
e same situation.
\par The definition of }{\b i}{ in the control expression of the }{\b for}{ loop is an example of being able to define a variable }{\i exactly}{ at the point you need it (you can only do this in C++). The scope of }{\b i}{
 is the scope of the expression controlled by the }{\b for}{ loop, so you can turn around and re-use }{\b i}{ in the next }{\b for}{ loop. This is a convenient and commonly-used idiom in C++; }{\b i}{ is the classic name for a loop counter and you don
\rquote t have to keep inventing new names.
\par Although the example also shows variables defined within }{\b while}{, }{\b if}{ and }{\b switch}{ statements, this kind of definition is much less common than those in }{\b for}{
 expressions, possibly because the syntax is so constrained. For example, you cannot have any parentheses. That is, you cannot say:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {while((char c = cin.get()) != 'q')
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The addition of the extra parentheses would seem like a very innocent and useful thing to do, and because you cannot use them, the results are not what you might like. The problem occurs because 
\lquote }{\b !=}{\rquote  has a higher precedence than \lquote }{\b =}{\rquote , so the }{\b char}{ }{\b c }{ends up containing a }{\b bool}{ converted to }{\b char}{. When that\rquote s printed, on many terminals you\rquote 
ll see a smiley-face character.
\par In general, you can consider the ability to define variables within }{\b while}{, }{\b if}{ and }{\b switch}{ statements as being there for completeness, but the only place you\rquote re likely to use this kind of variable definition is in a }{\b for}{
 loop (where you\rquote ll use it quite often).
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776024}Specifying storage allocation{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776024}}{\pard\plain 
\s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \v\f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {specifying storage allocation}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When creating a variable, you have a number of options to specify the lifetime of the variable, how the storage is allocated for that variable, and how the variable is treated by the compiler.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776025}Global variables{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776025}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {global variables}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {variable\: global}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Global variables are defined outside all function bodies a
nd are available to all parts of the program (even code in other files). Global variables are unaffected by scopes and are always available (i.e., the lifetime of a global variable lasts until the program ends). If the existence of a global variable in on
e file is declared using the }{\b extern}{ keyword in another file, the data is available for use by the second file. Here\rquote s an example of the use of global variables:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Global.cpp
\par //\{L\} Global2
\par // Demonstration of global variables
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int globe;
\par void func();
\par int main() \{
\par   globe = 12;
\par   cout << globe << endl;
\par   func(); // Modifies globe
\par   cout << globe << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here\rquote s a file that accesses }{\b globe}{ as an extern:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Global2.cpp \{O\}
\par // Accessing external global variables
\par extern int globe;  
\par // (The linker resolves the reference)
\par void func() \{
\par   globe = 47;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Storage for the variable }{\b globe}{ is created by the definition in }{\b Global.cpp}{, and that same variable is accessed by the code in }{\b Global2.cpp}{. Since the code in }{\b Global2.cpp}{
 is compiled separately from the code in }{\b Global.cpp}{, the compiler must be informed that the variable exists elsewhere by the declaration 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {extern int globe;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you run the program, you\rquote ll see that the call to }{\b func(\~)}{ does indeed affect the single global instance of }{\b globe}{.
\par In }{\b Global.cpp}{, you can see the special comment tag (which is my own design):
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//\{L\} Global2
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This says that to create the final program, the object file with the name }{\b Global2}{ must be linked in (there is no extension becaus
e the extension names of object files differ from one system to the next). In }{\b Global2.cpp}{, the first line has another special comment tag }{\b \{O\}}{ which says \ldblquote don\rquote t try to create an executable out of this file, it\rquote 
s being compiled so that it can be linked into some other executable.\rdblquote  The }{\b ExtractCode.cpp}{ program at the end of this book reads these tags and creates the appropriate }{\b makefile}{ so everything compiles properly (you\rquote 
ll learn about }{\b makefile}{s at the end of this chapter).
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776026}Local variables{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776026}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {local variables}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {variable\: local}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Local variables occur within a scope; they are \ldblquote local\rdblquote  to a function. They are often called }{\i automatic}{
 variables because they automatically come into being when the scope is entered, and automatically go away when the scope closes. The keyword }{\b auto}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b auto keyword}}}{
 makes this explicit, but local variables default to }{\b auto}{ so it is never necessary to declare something as an }{\b auto}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Register variables}{\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \v\f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {register variables}}}
{\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \v\f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {variable\: register}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A register variable is a type of local variable. The }{\b register}{ keyword tells the compiler \ldblquote make accesses to this variable as fast as possible.\rdblquote 
 Increasing the access speed is implementation dependent but, as the name suggests, it is often done by placing the variable in a register. There is n
o guarantee that the variable will be placed in a register or even that the access speed will increase. It is a hint to the compiler.
\par There are restrictions to the use of }{\b register}{ variables. You cannot take or compute the address of a }{\b register}{ variable. A }{\b register}{ variable can only be declared within a block (you cannot have global or }{\b static}{ }{\b register}{
 variables). You can, however, use a }{\b register}{ variable as a formal argument in a function (i.e., in the argument list).
\par Generally, you shouldn\rquote t try to second-guess the compiler\rquote s optimizer, since it will probably do a better job than you can. Thus, the }{\b register}{ keyword is best avoided.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776027}static{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776027}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {\b static}}}{\b 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b static}{ keyword has several distinct meanings. Normally, variables defined local to a function disappear 
at the end of the function scope. When you call the function again, storage for the variables is created anew and the values are re-initialized. If you want a value to be extant throughout the life of a program, you can define a function\rquote 
s local variable to be }{\b static}{ and give it an initial value. The initialization is only performed the first time the function is called, and the data retains its value between function calls. This way, a function can \ldblquote remember\rdblquote 
 some piece of information between function calls.
\par You may wonder why a global variable isn\rquote t used instead. The beauty of a }{\b static}{ variable is that it is unavailable outside the scope of the function, so it can\rquote t be inadvertently changed. This localizes errors.
\par Here\rquote s an example of the use of }{\b static}{ variables:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Static.cpp
\par // Using a static variable in a function
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par void func() \{
\par   static int i = 0;
\par   cout << "i = " << ++i << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   for(int x = 0; x < 10; x++)
\par     func();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Each time }{\b func}{(\~) is called in the for loop, it prints a different value. If the keyword }{\b static}{ is not used, the value printed will always be \lquote 1\rquote .
\par The second meaning of }{\b static}{ is related to the first in the \ldblquote unavailable outside a certain scope\rdblquote  sense. When }{\b static}{ is applied to a function name or to a variable that is outside of all functions, it means \ldblquote 
this name is unavailable outside of this file.\rdblquote  The function name or variable is local to the file; we say it }{\i has file scope}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {file scope}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {variable\: file scope}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {file\: file static}}}{. As a demonstration, compiling and linking the following two files will cause a linker error:

\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:FileStatic.cpp
\par // File scope demonstration. Compiling and 
\par // linking this file with FileStatic2.cpp
\par // will cause a linker error
\par 
\par // File scope means only available in this file:
\par static int fs; 
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   fs = 1;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Even though the variable }{\b fs}{ is claimed to exist as an }{\b extern}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b extern}}}{ in the following file, the linker won\rquote 
t find it because it has been declared }{\b static}{ in }{\b FileStatic.cpp}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:FileStatic2.cpp \{O\}
\par // Trying to reference fs
\par extern int fs;
\par void func() \{
\par   fs = 100;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b static}{ specifier may also be used inside a }{\b class}{. This explanation will be delayed until you learn to create classes, later in the book.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776028}extern{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776028}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {\b extern}}}{\b 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b extern}{ keyword has already been briefly described and demonstrated. It tells the compiler that a variable or a function exists, even if the compiler hasn\rquote 
t yet seen it in the file currently being compiled. This variable or function may be defined in another file or further down in the current file. As an example of the latter:}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
forward declaration}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {declaration\: forward}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Forward.cpp
\par // Forward function & data declarations
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par // This is not actually external, but the 
\par // compiler must be told it exists somewhere:
\par extern int i; 
\par extern void func();
\par int main() \{
\par   i = 0;
\par   func();
\par \}
\par int i; // The data definition
\par void func() \{
\par   i++;
\par   cout << i;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When the compiler encounters the declaration \lquote }{\b extern int i}{\rquote  it knows that the definition for }{\b i}{
 must exist somewhere as a global variable. When the compiler reaches the definition of }{\b i}{, no other declaration is visible so it knows it has found the same }{\b i}{ declared earlier in the file. If you were to define }{\b i}{ as }{\b static}{
, you would be telling the compiler that }{\b i}{ is defined globally (via the }{\b extern}{), but it also has file scope}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {file scope}}}{ (via the }{\b static}{
), so the compiler will generate an error.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Linkage}{\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \v\f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {linkage}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To understand the behavior of C and C++ programs, you need to know about }{\i linkage}{
. In an executing program, an identifier is represented by storage in memory that holds a variable or a compiled function body. Linkage describes this storage it is seen by the linker. There are two types of linkage: }{\i internal linkage}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i internal linkage}}}{ and }{\i external linkage}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {external linkage}}}{.
\par Internal linkage means that storage is created to represent the identifier only for the file being compiled. Other files may use the same identifier name with internal linkage, or for a global variable, and no conflicts will be found by the linker 
\endash  separate storage is created for each identifier. Internal linkage is specified by the keyword }{\b static}{ in C and C++.
\par External linkage means that a si
ngle piece of storage is created to represent the identifier for all files being compiled. The storage is created once, and the linker must resolve all other references to that storage. Global variables and function names have external linkage. These are 
accessed from other files by declaring them with the keyword }{\b extern}{. Variables defined outside all functions (with the exception of }{\b const}{
 in C++) and function definitions default to external linkage. You can specifically force them to have internal linkage using the }{\b static}{ keyword. You can explicitly state that an identifier has external linkage by defining it with the }{\b extern}{
 keyword. Defining a variable or function with }{\b extern}{ is not necessary in C, but it is sometimes necessary for }{\b const}{ in C++.
\par Automatic (local) variables exist only temporarily, on the stack, while a function is being called. The linker doesn\rquote t know about automatic variables}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {automatic variables}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {variable\: automatic}}}{, and they have }{\i no linkage}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linkage\: no}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {no linkage}
}}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776029}Constants{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776029}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {constant}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In old (pre-Standard) C, if you wanted to make a constant, you had to use the preprocessor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {preprocessor}}}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#define PI 3.14159
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Everywhere you used }{\b PI}{, the value 3.14159 was substituted by the preprocessor (you can still use this method in C and C++). 
\par When you use the preprocessor to create constants, you place control of those constants outside the scope of the compiler. No type checking}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type\: checking}}}{ is performed on the name }{\b PI}
{ and you can\rquote t take the address of }{\b PI}{ (so you can\rquote t pass a pointer}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {pointer}}}{ or a reference}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {reference}}}{
 to }{\b PI}{). }{\b PI}{ cannot be a variable of a user-defined type. The meaning of }{\b PI}{ lasts from the point it is defined to the end of the file; the preprocessor doesn\rquote t recognize scoping.
\par C++ introduces the concept of a named constant}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {named constant}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constant\: named}}}{
 that is just like a variable, except its value cannot be changed. The modifier }{\b const}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const}}}{ tells the compiler that a name represents a constant. Any data type, built-in or 
user-defined, may be defined as }{\b const}{. If you define something as }{\b const}{ and then attempt to modify it, the compiler will generate an error.
\par You must specify the type of a }{\b const}{, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {const int x = 10;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In Standard C and C++, you can use a named constant in an argument list, even if the argument it fills is a pointer or a reference (i.e., you can take the address of a }{\b const}{). A }{\b const}{
 has a scope, just like a regular variable, so you can \ldblquote hide\rdblquote  a }{\b const}{ inside a function and be sure that the name will not affect the rest of the program.
\par The }{\b const}{ was taken from C++ and incorporated into Standard C, albeit quite differently. In C, the compiler treats a }{\b const}{ just like a variable that has a special tag attached that says \ldblquote don\rquote t change me.\rdblquote 
 When you define a }{\b const}{ in C, the compiler creates storage for it, so if you define more than one }{\b const}{
 with the same name in two different files (or put the definition in a header file), the linker will generate error messages about conflicts. The intended use of }{\b const}{ in C is quite different from its intended use in C++ (in short, it\rquote 
s nicer in C++).
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Constant values}{\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \v\f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {constant\: values}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In C++, a }{\b const}{ must always have an initialization value (in C, this is not true). Constant values for built-in types are expressed as decimal}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {decimal}}}{, octal}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {octal}}}{, hexadecimal}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {hexadecimal}}}{, or floating-point numbers}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {floating point\: numbers}}}{ (sadly, binary numbers were not considered important), or as characters}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {character}}}{.
\par In the absence of any other clues, the compiler assumes a constant value is a decimal number. The numbers 47, 0 and 1101 are all treated as decimal numbers.
\par A constant value with a leading 0 is treated as an octal number (base 8). Base 8 numbers can only contain digits 0-7; the compiler flags other digits as an error. A legitimate octal number is 017 (15 in base 10).
\par A constant value with a leading 0x is treated as a hexadecimal number (base 16). Base 16 numbers contain the digits 0-9 and a-f or A-F. A legitimate hexadecimal number is 0x1fe (510 in base 10).
\par Floating point numbers can contain decimal points and exponential}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exponential}}}{ powers (represented by e, which means \ldblquote 10 to the power\rdblquote ). Both the decimal point and the }{
\b e}{ are optional. If you assign a constant to a floating-point variable, the compiler will take the constant value and convert it to a floating-point number (this process is one form of what\rquote s called }{\i implicit type conversion}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {implicit type conversion}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type\: implicit conversion}}}{). However, it is a good idea to use either a decimal point or an }{\b e}{
 to remind the reader you are using a floating-point number; some older compilers also need the hint.
\par Legitimate floating-point constant values are: 1e4, 1.0001, 47.0, 0.0 and -1.159e-77. You can add suffixes to force the type of floating-point number: }{\b f}{ or }{\b F}{ forces a }{\b float}{, }{\b L}{ or }{\b l}{ forces a }{\b long}{ }{\b double}{
, otherwise the number will be a }{\b double}{.}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {floating point\: float}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {double}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {long double}}}{
\par }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {character\: constants}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constant\: character}}}{Character constants are characters surrounded by single quotes, as: \lquote }{\b A
}{\rquote , \lquote }{\b 0}{\rquote , \lquote  \lquote . Notice there is a big difference between the character \lquote }{\b 0}{\rquote  (ASCII 96) and the value }{\b 0}{. Special characters are represented with the \ldblquote backslash escape\rdblquote 
: \lquote }{\b \\n}{\rquote  (newline), \lquote }{\b \\t}{\rquote  (tab), \lquote }{\b \\\\}{\rquote  (backslash), \lquote }{\b \\r}{\rquote  (carriage return), \lquote }{\b \\"}{\rquote  (double quotes), \lquote }{\b \\'}{\rquote 
 (single quote), etc. You can also express char constants in octal: \lquote }{\b \\17}{\rquote  or hexadecimal: \lquote }{\b \\xff}{\rquote .
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776030}volatile{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776030}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {\b volatile}}}{\b 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Whereas the qualifier }{\b const}{ tells the compiler \ldblquote this never changes\rdblquote  (which allows the compiler to perform extra optimizations) the qualifier }{\b volatile}{
 tells the compiler \ldblquote you never know when this will change,\rdblquote  and prevents the compiler from performing any optimizations. Use this keyword when you read some value outside the control of your code
, such as a register in a piece of communication hardware. A }{\b volatile}{ variable is always read whenever its value is required, even if it was just read the line before.
\par A special case of some storage being \ldblquote outside the control of your code\rdblquote  is in a multithreaded program. If you\rquote re watching a particular flag that is modified by another thread or process, that flag should be }{\b volatile}{
 so the compiler doesn\rquote t make the assumption that it can optimize away multiple reads of the flag.
\par Note that }{\b volatile}{ may have no effect when a compiler is not optimizing, but may prevent critical bugs when you start optimizing the code (which is when the compiler will begin looking for reduntant reads).
\par The }{\b const}{ and }{\b volatile }{keywords will be further illuminated in a later chapter.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776031}Operators and their use{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776031}}{\pard\plain 
\s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \v\f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {C++\: operators and their use}}}{\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \v\f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {C\: operators and their use}}}{\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \v\f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {operator}
}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This section covers all the operators in C and C++.
\par All operators produce a value from their operands. This value is produced without modifying the operands, except with the assignment, increment and decrement operators. Modifying an operand is called a }{\i side effect}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {side effect}}}{. The most common use for operators that modify their operands is to generate the side effect, but you should keep in min
d that the value produced is available for your use just as in operators without side effects.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776032}Assignment{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776032}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {assignment}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Assignment is performed with the operator }{\b =}{. It means \ldblquote take the right-hand side (often called the }{\i rvalue}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {rvalue}}}{
) and copy it into the left-hand side (often called the }{\i lvalue}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {lvalue}}}{
). An rvalue is any constant, variable, or expression that can produce a value, but an lvalue must be a distinct, named variable (that is, there must be a physical space in which to store data). Fo
r instance, you can assign a constant value to a variable (}{\b A = 4;}{), but you cannot assign anything to constant value \endash  it cannot be an lvalue (you can\rquote t say }{\b 4 = A;}{).
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776033}Mathematical operators{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776033}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {mathematical operators}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The basic mathematical operators are the same as the ones available in most programming languages: addition }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {addition }}}{(}{\b +}{
), subtraction}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {subtraction}}}{ (}{\b -}{), division}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {division}}}{ (}{\b /}{), multiplication}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {multiplication}}}{ (}{\b *}{) and modulus}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {modulus}}}{ (}{\b %}{, this produces the remainder fro
m integer division). Integer division truncates the result (it doesn\rquote t round). The modulus operator cannot be used with floating-point numbers.
\par C & C++ also use a shorthand notation to perform an operation and an assignment at the same time. This is denote
d by an operator followed by an equal sign, and is consistent with all the operators in the language (whenever it makes sense). For example, to add 4 to the variable }{\b x}{ and assign }{\b x}{ to the result, you say: }{\b x += 4;}{. 
\par This example shows the use of the mathematical operators:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Mathops.cpp
\par // Mathematical operators
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par // A macro to display a string and a value.
\par #define PRINT(STR, VAR) \\
\par   cout << STR " = " << VAR << endl
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int i, j, k;
\par   float u,v,w;  // Applies to doubles, too
\par   cout << "enter an integer: ";
\par   cin >> j;
\par   cout << "enter another integer: ";
\par   cin >> k;
\par   PRINT("j",j);  PRINT("k",k);
\par   i = j + k; PRINT("j + k",i);
\par   i = j - k; PRINT("j - k",i);
\par   i = k / j; PRINT("k / j",i);
\par   i = k * j; PRINT("k * j",i);
\par   i = k % j; PRINT("k % j",i);
\par   // The following only works with integers:
\par   j %= k; PRINT("j %= k", j);
\par   cout << "Enter a floating-point number: ";
\par   cin >> v;
\par   cout << "Enter another floating-point number:";
\par   cin >> w;
\par   PRINT("v",v); PRINT("w",w);
\par   u = v + w; PRINT("v + w", u);
\par   u = v - w; PRINT("v - w", u);
\par   u = v * w; PRINT("v * w", u);
\par   u = v / w; PRINT("v / w", u);
\par   // The following works for ints, chars, 
\par   // and doubles too:
\par   u += v; PRINT("u += v", u);
\par   u -= v; PRINT("u -= v", u);
\par   u *= v; PRINT("u *= v", u);
\par   u /= v; PRINT("u /= v", u);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The rvalues of all the assignments can, of course, be much more complex.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Introduction to preprocessor macros}{\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \v\f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {
preprocessor\: macros}}}{\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \v\f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {macro\: preprocessor}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice the use of the macro }{\b PRINT(\~)}{ to save typing (and typing errors!). Preprocessor macros are traditionally named with all uppercase letters, so they stand out \endash  you\rquote 
ll learn later that macros can quickly become dangerous (and they can also be very useful). 
\par The arguments in the parenthesized list following the macro name are substituted in all the code following the closing parenthesis. The preprocessor removes the name }{\b PRINT}{
 and substitutes the code wherever the macro is called, so the compiler cannot generate any error messages using the macro name, and it doesn\rquote 
t do any type checking on the arguments (the latter can be beneficial, as shown in the debugging macros at the end of the chapter).
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776034}Relational operators{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776034}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {relational operators}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {operator\: relational}}}
{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Relational operators establish a relationship between the values of the operands. They produce a Boolean (specified with the }{\b bool }{keyword in C++) }{\b true}{ if the relationship is true, and }{
\b false }{if the relationship is false. The relational operators are: less than (}{\b <}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {less than (<)}}}{, greater than (}{\b >}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {greater than (>)}}}{, less than or equal to (}{\b <=}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {less than or equal to (<=)}}}{, greater than or equal to (}{\b >=}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
greater than or equal to (>=)}}}{, equivalent (}{\b ==}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {equivalent (==)}}}{ and not equivalent (}{\b !=}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {not equivalent (!=)}}}
{.}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {<}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {>}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {<=}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {>=}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {==}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {!=}}}{
 They may be used with all built-in data types in C and C++. They may be given special definitions for user-defined data types in C++ (you\rquote ll learn about this in Chapter XX, on operator overloading).
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776035}Logical operators{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776035}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {logical\: operators}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {operator\: logical}}}{

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The logical operators }{\i and}{ (}{\b &&}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {AND (&&)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {&&}}}{ and }{\i or}{
 (}{\b ||}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {OR (||)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {||}}}{ produce a }{\b true}{ or }{\b false}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
false}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {true}}}{based on the logical relationship of its arguments. Remember that in C and C++, a statement is }{\b true}{ if it has a non-zero value, and }{\b false}{
 if it has a value of zero. If you print a }{\b bool}{, you\rquote ll typically see a \lquote }{\b 1}{\rquote  for }{\b true}{ and \lquote }{\b 0}{\rquote  for }{\b false}{.
\par This example uses the relational and logical operators:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Boolean.cpp
\par // Relational and logical operators.
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int i,j;
\par   cout << "enter an integer: ";
\par   cin >> i;
\par   cout << "enter another integer: ";
\par   cin >> j;
\par   cout << "i > j is " << (i > j) << endl;
\par   cout << "i < j is " << (i < j) << endl;
\par   cout << "i >= j is " << (i >= j) << endl;
\par   cout << "i <= j is " << (i <= j) << endl;
\par   cout << "i == j is " << (i == j) << endl;
\par   cout << "i != j is " << (i != j) << endl;
\par   cout << "i && j is " << (i && j) << endl;
\par   cout << "i || j is " << (i || j) << endl;
\par   cout << " (i < 10) && (j < 10) is "
\par        << ((i < 10) && (j < 10))  << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can replace the definition for }{\b int}{ with }{\b float}{ or }{\b double}{ in the above program. Be aware, how
ever, that the comparison of a floating-point number with the value of zero is very strict: a number that is the tiniest fraction different from another number is still \ldblquote not equal.\rdblquote 
 A floating-point number that is the tiniest bit above zero is still true.}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {floating point\: true and false}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776036}Bitwise operators{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776036}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {bitwise\: operators}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {operator\: bitwise}}}{

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The bitwise operators allow you to manipulate individual bits in a number (since floating point values use a special internal format, the bitwise operat
ors only work with integral numbers). Bitwise operators perform boolean algebra}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {boolean algebra}}}{ on the corresponding bits in the arguments to produce the result.
\par The bitwise }{\i and}{ operator (}{\b &}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {bitwise\: AND operator (&)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {&}}}{ produces a one in t
he output bit if both input bits are one; otherwise it produces a zero. The bitwise }{\i or}{ operator (}{\b |}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {bitwise\: OR operator (|)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {|}}}{ produces a one in the output bit if either input bit is a one and only produces a zero if both input bits are zero. The bitwise }{\i exclusive or}{, or }{\i xor}{ (}{\b ^}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {bitwise\: EXCLUSIVE OR XOR (^)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {XOR}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {^}}}{
 produces a one in the output bit if one or the other input bit is a one, but not both. The bitwise }{\i not}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\i bitwise\: NOT ~}}}{\i  }{(}{\b ~}{, also called the }{\i ones complement}{
 operator}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ones complement operator}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: ones-complement}}}{) is a unary operator}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {unary\: operator}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: unary}}}{ \endash  it only takes one argument (all other bitwise operators are binary operators}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {binary operators}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: binary}}}{). Bitwise }{\i not}{ produces the opposite of the input bit \endash  a one if the input bit is zero, a zero if the input bit is one.

\par Bitwise operators can be combined with the }{\b =}{ sign to unite the operation and assignment: }{\b &=}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {&=}}}{, }{\b |=}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {|=}}}{ and 
}{\b ^=}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {^=}}}{ are all legitimate operations (since }{\b ~}{ is a unary operator it cannot be combined with the }{\b =}{ sign).
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776037}Shift operators{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776037}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {shift operators}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {operator\: shift}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The shift operators also manipulate bits. The left-shift operator (}{\b <<}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {left-shift operator (<<)}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {<<}}}{ produces the operand to the left of the operator shifted to the left by the number of bits specified after the operator. The right-shift operator (}{\b >>}{)}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {right-shift operator (>>)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {>>}}}{ produces the operand to the left of the operator shifted 
to the right by the number of bits specified after the operator. If the value after the shift operator is greater than the number of bits in the left-hand operand, the result is undefined. If the left-hand operand is unsigned, the right shift is a logical
 shift so the upper bits will be filled with zeros. If the left-hand operand is signed, the right shift may or may not be a logical shift (that is, the behavior is undefined).
\par Shifts can be combined with the equal sign (}{\b <<=}{ and }{\b >>=}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {<<=}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {>>=}}}{. The
 lvalue is replaced by the lvalue shifted by the rvalue.
\par Here\rquote s an example that demonstrates the use of all the operators involving bits:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Bitwise.cpp
\par // Demonstration of bit manipulation
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par // Display a byte in binary:
\par void printBinary(const unsigned char val) \{
\par   for(int i = 7; i >= 0; i--)
\par     if(val & (1 << i))
\par       cout << "1";
\par     else
\par       cout << "0";
\par \}
\par 
\par // A macro to save typing:
\par #define PR(STR, EXPR) \\
\par   cout << STR; printBinary(EXPR); cout << endl;  
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   unsigned int getval;
\par   unsigned char a, b;
\par   cout << "Enter a number between 0 and 255: ";
\par   cin >> getval; a = getval;
\par   PR("a in binary: ", a);
\par   cout << "Enter a number between 0 and 255: ";
\par   cin >> getval; b = getval;
\par   PR("b in binary: ", b);
\par   PR("a | b = ", a | b);
\par   PR("a & b = ", a & b);
\par   PR("a ^ b = ", a ^ b);
\par   PR("~a = ", ~a);
\par   PR("~b = ", ~b);
\par   // An interesting bit pattern:
\par   unsigned char c = 0x5A; 
\par   PR("c in binary: ", c);
\par   a |= c;
\par   PR("a |= c; a = ", a);
\par   b &= c;
\par   PR("b &= c; b = ", b);
\par   b ^= a;
\par   PR("b ^= a; b = ", b);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b printBinary(\~)}{ function takes a single byte and displays it bit-by-bit.  The expression }{\b (1 << i)}{
 produces a one in each successive bit position; in binary: 00000001, 00000010, etc. If this bit is bitwise }{\i and}{ed with }{\b val}{ and the result is nonzero, it means there was a one in that position in }{\b val}{.
\par Once again, a preprocessor macro is used to save typing. It prints the string of your choice, the the binary representation of an expression, then a newline.
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{, the variables are }{\b unsigned}{. This is because, generally, you don't want signs when you are working with bytes. An }{\b int}{ must be used instead of a }{\b char}{ for }{\b getval }{because the \ldblquote }{\b cin >>}{\rdblquote 
 statement will otherwise treat the first digit as a character.  By assigning }{\b getval}{ to }{\b a}{ and }{\b b}{, the value is converted to a single byte (by truncating it).
\par The }{\b <<}{ and }{\b >>}{ provide bit-shifting behavior, but when they shift bits off the end of the number, those bits are lost (it\rquote s commonly said that they fall into the mythical }{\i bit bucket}{
, a place where discarded bits end up, presumably so they can be reused\'85). When manipulating bits you can also perform }{\i rotation}{, which means that the bits that fall off one end are inserted back at the other end, as if they\rquote re
 being rotated around a loop. Even though most computer processors provide a machine-level rotate command (so you\rquote ll see it in the assembly language for that processor), there is no direct support for \ldblquote rotate\rdblquote  in C or C++. }
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {rotate}}}{Presumably the designers of C felt justified in leaving \ldblquote rotate\rdblquote 
 off (aiming, as they said, for a minimal language) because you can build your own rotate command. For example, here are functions to perform left and right rotations:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Rotation.cpp \{O\}
\par // Perform left and right rotations
\par 
\par unsigned char rol(unsigned char val) \{
\par   int highbit;
\par   if(val & 0x80) // 0x80 is the high bit only
\par     highbit = 1;
\par   else
\par     highbit = 0;
\par   // Left shift (bottom bit becomes 0):
\par   val <<= 1;
\par   // Rotate the high bit onto the bottom:
\par   val |= highbit;
\par   return val;
\par \}
\par 
\par unsigned char ror(unsigned char val) \{
\par   int lowbit;
\par   if(val & 1) // Check the low bit
\par     lowbit = 1;
\par   else
\par     lowbit = 0;
\par   val >>= 1; // Right shift by one position
\par   // Rotate the low bit onto the top:
\par   val |= (lowbit << 7);
\par   return val;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Try using these functions in }{\b Bitwise.cpp}{. Notice the definitions (or at least declarations) of }{\b rol(\~)}{ and }{\b ror(\~)}{ must be seen by the compiler in }{\b Bitwise.cpp}{
 before the functions are used.
\par The bitwise functions are generally extremely efficient to use because they translate directly into assembly language statements. Sometimes a single C or C++ statement will generate a single line of assembly code.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776038}Unary operators{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776038}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {unary\: operators}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {operator\: unary}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Bitwise }{\i not}{ isn\rquote t the only operator that takes a single argument. Its companion, the }{\i logical not}{ (}{\b !}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {logical\:
 NOT (!)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {!}}}{, will take a }{\b true}{ value and produce a }{\b false}{ value}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {true}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {false}}}{. The unary minus (}{\b -}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {unary\: minus (-)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {-}}}{ and unary plus (}{\b +}{)}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {unary\: plus (+)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {+}}}{ are the same operators as binary minus and plus \endash 
 the compiler figures out which usage is intended by the way you write the expression. For instance, the statement
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {x = -a;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {has an obvious meaning. The compiler can figure out:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {x = a * -b;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {but the reader might get confused, so it is safer to say:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {x = a * (-b);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The unary minus produces the negative of the value. Unary plus provides symmetry with unary minus, although it doesn\rquote t actually do anything.
\par The increment}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {increment}}}{ and decrement}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {decrement}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {++}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {--}}}{ operators (}{\b ++}{ and }{\b --}{) were introduced earlier in this chapter. These are the only operators other than those involving assignment that have side effects}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {side effect}}}{. These operators increase or decrease the variable by one unit, although \ldblquote unit\rdblquote  can have different meanings according to the data type \endash 
 this is especially true with pointers.
\par The last unary operators are the address-of (}{\b &}{)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {address-of (&)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {&}}}{, dereference (}{\b *}{ and }{\b ->}{)}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {dereference (*)}}}{ and cast}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cast}}}{ operators in C and C++, and }{\b new}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b 
new}}}{ and }{\b delete}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b delete}}}{ in C++. Address-of and dereference are used with pointers}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {pointer}}}{
, described in this chapter. Casting is described later in this chapter, and }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ are described in Chapter XX.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776039}The ternary operator{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776039}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {conditional operator}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {ternary operator}}}
{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {operator\:ternary}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The ternary }{\b if-else}{ is unusual because it has 3 operands. It is truly an operator because it produces a value, unlike the ordinary }{\b if-else}{ statement}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {if-else statement}}}{. It consists of three expressions: if the first expression (followed by a }{\b ?}{) evaluates to }{\b true}{, the expression following the }{\b ?}{
 is evaluated and its result becomes the value produced by the operator. If the first expression is }{\b false}{, the third expression (following a }{\b :}{) is executed and its result becomes the value produced by the operator.
\par The conditional operator can be used for its side effects or for the value it produces. Here\rquote s a code fragment that demonstrates both:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {A = --B ? B : (B = -99);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here, the conditional produces the rvalue. }{\b A}{ is assigned to the value of }{\b B}{ if the result of decrementing }{\b B}{ is nonzero. If }{\b B}{ became zero, }{\b A}{ and }{\b B}{
 are both assigned to -99. }{\b B}{ is always decremented, but it is only assigned to -99 if the decrement causes }{\b B}{ to become 0. A similar statement can be used without the \ldblquote }{\b A =}{\rdblquote  just for its side effects:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {--B ? B : (B = -99);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here the second B is superfluous, since the value produced by the operator is unused. An expression is required between the }{\b ?}{ and }{\b :}{
. In this case the expression could simply be a constant that might make the code run a bit faster.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776040}The comma operator{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776040}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {comma operator}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {operator\: comma}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The comma is not restricted to separating variable names in multiple definitions such as 
