\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The statement }{\b a = b}{ will always evaluate to true}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {true}}}{ when }{\b b}{ is non-zero. The variable }{\b a }{
is assigned to the value of }{\b b}{, and the value of }{\b b}{ is also produced by the operator }{\b =}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {=}}}{. Generally you want to use the equivalence}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {equivalence}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {==}}}{ operator }{\b ==}{ inside a conditional statement, not assignment. This one b
ites a lot of programmers (however, some compilers will point out the problem to you, which is very helpful).
\par A similar problem is using bitwise }{\i and}{ and }{\i or}{ instead of their logical counterparts. Bitwise }{\i and}{ and }{\i or}{ use one of the characters (}{\b &}{ or }{\b |}{) while logical }{\i and}{ and }{\i or}{ use two (}{\b &&}{ and }{\b ||}{
). Just as with }{\b =}{ and }{\b ==}{, it\rquote s easy to just type one character instead of two. A useful mnemonic device is to observe that \ldblquote bits are smaller, so they don\rquote t need as many characters in their operators.\rdblquote }
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {bitwise\: AND}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {bitwise\: OR}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {AND}}}{\pard\plain 
