\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The word }{\i cast}{ is used in the sense of \ldblquote casting into a mold.\rdblquote  The compiler will automatically change one type of data i
nto another if it makes sense. For instance, if you assign an integral value to a floating-point variable, the compiler will secretly call a function (or more probably, insert code) to convert the }{\b int}{ to a }{\b float}{
. Casting allows you to make this type conversion explicit, or to force it when it wouldn\rquote t normally happen.
\par To perform a cast, put the desired data type (including all modifiers) inside parentheses to the left of the value. This value can be a variable, a constant, the value produced by an expression or the return value of a function. Here\rquote s an example:

\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int b = 200;
