\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Of course in the above case you wouldn\rquote t really need a cast; you could just say }{\b 200f}{ (and that\rquote 
s typically what the compiler will do for the above expression, in effect). Casts are generally used instead with variables, rather than constants.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776043}sizeof}{ \endash  an operator by itself{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776043} }{\pard\plain 
\s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {sizeof }}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {
operator\: sizeof}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b sizeof(\~)}{ operator stands alone because it satisfies an unusual need. }{\b sizeof(\~)}{
 gives you information about the amount of memory allocated for data items. As described earlier in this chapter, }{\b sizeof(\~)}{ tells you the number of bytes used by any particular va
riable. It can also give the size of a data type (with no variable name):
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {cout << "sizeof(double) = " << sizeof(double);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b sizeof(\~)}{ can also give you the sizes of user-defined data types. This is used later in the book.
