\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {assembly-language\: asm in-line assembly language keyword}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {keyword\:
 asm, for in-line assembly language }}}{This is an escape mechanism that allows you to write assembly code for your hardware within a C++ program. Often you\rquote re able to reference C++ variables within the
 assembly code, which means you can easily communicate with your C++ code and limit the assembly code to that necessary for efficiency tuning or to utilize special processor instructions. The exact syntax of the assembly language is compiler-dependent and
