\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If your compiler complies with Standard C++, it will support these keywords.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776046}Composite type creation{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776046}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The fundamental data types and their variations are essential, but rather primitive. C and C++ provide tools that allow you to compose more sophisticated data types from the fundamental data type
s. As you\rquote ll see, the most important of these is }{\b struct}{, which is the foundation for }{\b class}{ in C++. However, the simplest way to create more sophisticated types is simply to alias a name to another name via }{\b typedef}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776047}Aliasing names with }{\b typedef{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776047}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This keyword promises more than it delivers: }{\b typedef }{suggests \ldblquote type definition\rdblquote  when \ldblquote alias\rdblquote 
 would probably have been a more accurate description, since that\rquote s all it really does. The syntax is:
\par }\pard\plain \s41\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f0\kerning0 typedef existing-type-description alias-name
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {People often use }{\b typedef}{ when data types get slightly complicated, just to prevent extra keystrokes. Here is a commonly-used }{\b typedef}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {typedef unsigned long ulong; 
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now if you say }{\b ulong}{ the compiler knows that you mean }{\b unsigned long}{. You might think that this could as easily be accomp
lished using preprocessor substitution, but there are key situations where the compiler must be aware that you\rquote re treating a name as if it were a type, so }{\b typedef}{ is essential.
\par You can argue that it\rquote s more explicit and therefore more readable to avoid }{\b typedef}{s for primitive types, and indeed programs rapidly become difficult to read when many }{\b typedef}{s are used. However, }{\b typedef}{
s become especially important in C when used with }{\b struct}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776048}Combining variables with }{\b struct{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776048}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A }{\b struct}{ is a way to collect a group of variables into a structure. Once you create a }{\b struct}{, then you can make many instances of this \ldblquote new\rdblquote  type of variable you
\rquote ve invented.  For example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:SimpleStruct.cpp
\par 
\par struct Structure1 \{
\par   char c;
\par   int i;
\par   float f;
\par   double d;
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   struct Structure1 s1, s2;
\par   s1.c = 'a'; // Select an element using a '.'
\par   s1.i = 1;
\par   s1.f = 3.14;
\par   s1.d = 0.00093;
\par   s2.c = 'a';
\par   s2.i = 1;
\par   s2.f = 3.14;
\par   s2.d = 0.00093;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \s32\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs20\cgrid {\f0 The }{\b\f0 struct }{declaration must end with a semicolon. }{\f0 In }{\b\f0 main( )}{, two instances of }{\b Structure1}{ are created: }{\b s1}{ and }{\b s2}{
. Each of these have their own separate versions of }{\b c}{, }{\b i}{, }{\b f}{ and }{\b d}{. So }{\b s1}{ and }{\b s2}{ represent clumps of completely independent variables. To select one of the elements within }{\b s1}{ or }{\b s2}{, you use a \lquote 
}{\b .}{\rquote , syntax you\rquote ve seen in the previous chapter when using C++ }{\b class }{objects \endash  since }{\b class}{es evolved from }{\b struct}{s, this is where that syntax arose.
\par One thing you\rquote ll notice is the awkwardness of the use of }{\b Structure1}{. You can\rquote t just say }{\b Structure1}{ when you\rquote re defininge variables, you must say }{\b struct Structure1}{. This is where }{\b typedef}{
 becomes especially handy:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:SimpleStruct2.cpp
\par // Using typedef with struct
\par typedef struct \{
\par   char c;
\par   int i;
\par   float f;
\par   double d;
\par \} Structure2;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Structure2 s1, s2;
\par   s1.c = 'a';
\par   s1.i = 1;
\par   s1.f = 3.14;
\par   s1.d = 0.00093;
\par   s2.c = 'a';
\par   s2.i = 1;
\par   s2.f = 3.14;
\par   s2.d = 0.00093;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {By using }{\b typedef}{ in this way, you can pretend that }{\b Structure2}{ is a built-in type, like }{\b int}{ or }{\b float}{, when you define }{\b s1}{ and }{\b s2}{ (but notice it only has data 
\endash  characteristics \endash  and does not include behavior, which is what we get with real objects in C++). You\rquote ll notice that the }{\b struct}{ name has been left off at the beginning, because the goal is to create the }{\b typedef}{
. However, there are times when you might need to refer to the }{\b struct}{ during its definition. In those cases, you can actually repeat the name of the }{\b struct}{ as the }{\b struct}{ name and as the }{\b typedef}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:SelfReferential.cpp
\par // Allowing a struct to refer to itself
\par 
\par typedef struct SelfReferential \{
\par   int i;
\par   SelfReferential* sr; // Head spinning yet?
\par \} SelfReferential;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   SelfReferential sr1, sr2;
\par   sr1.sr = &sr2;
\par   sr2.sr = &sr1;
\par   sr1.i = 47;
\par   sr2.i = 1024;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you look at this for awhile, you\rquote ll see that }{\b sr1}{ and }{\b sr2}{ point to each other, as well as each holding a piece of data. 
\par Actually, the }{\b struct}{ name does not have to be the same as the }{\b typedef}{ name, but it is usually done this way as it tends to keep things simpler.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Pointers and }{\b struct}{s
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the above examples, all the }{\b struct}{s are manipulated as objects. However, like any piece of storage you can take the address of a }{\b struct}{ object (as seen in }{\b SelfReferential.cpp}{
 above). To select the elements of a particular }{\b struct}{ object, you use a \lquote }{\b .}{\rquote , as seen above. However, if you have a pointer to a }{\b struct }{object, you must select an element of that object using a different operator: the 
\lquote }{\b ->}{\rquote . Here\rquote s an example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:SimpleStruct3.cpp
\par // Using pointers to structs
\par typedef struct Structure3 \{
\par   char c;
\par   int i;
\par   float f;
\par   double d;
\par \} Structure3;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Structure3 s1, s2;
\par   Structure3* sp = &s1;
\par   sp->c = 'a';
\par   sp->i = 1;
\par   sp->f = 3.14;
\par   sp->d = 0.00093;
\par   sp = &s2; // Point to a different struct object
\par   sp->c = 'a';
\par   sp->i = 1;
\par   sp->f = 3.14;
\par   sp->d = 0.00093;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In }{\b main(\~)}{, the }{\b struct}{ pointer }{\b sp}{ is initially pointing to }{\b s1}{, and the members of }{\b s1}{ are initialized by selecting them with the \lquote }{\b ->}{\rquote 
 (and you use this same operator in order to read those members). But then }{\b sp}{ is pointed to }{\b s2}{, and those variables 
are initialized the same way. So you can see that another benefit of pointers is that they can be dynamically redirected to point to different objects; this provides more flexibility in your programming, as you shall learn.
\par For now, that\rquote s all you need to know about }{\b struct}{s, but}{\b  }{you\rquote ll become much more comfortable with }{\b struct}{s (and especially their more potent successors, }{\b class}{es) as the book progresses.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776049}Clarifying programs with }{\b enum{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776049}}{\pard\plain 
\s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {enum\: clarifying programs with}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {An enumerated data type is a way of attaching names to numbers, thereby giving more meaning to anyone reading the code. The }{\b enum}{
 keyword (from C) automatically enumerates any list of words you give it by assigning them values of 0, 1, 2, etc. You can declare }{\b enum}{ variables (which are always }{\b int}{s). The declaration of an }{\b enum}{ looks similar to a }{\b struct}{
 declaration.
\par An enumerated data type is very useful when you want to keep track of some sort of feature:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Enum.cpp
\par // Keeping track of shapes.
\par 
\par enum ShapeType \{
\par   circle,
\par   square,
\par   rectangle
\par \};  // Must end with a semicolon like a struct
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   ShapeType shape = circle;
\par   // Activities here....
\par   // Now do something based on what the shape is:
\par   switch(shape) \{
\par     case circle:  /* circle stuff */ break;
\par     case square:  /* square stuff */ break;
\par     case rectangle:  /* rectangle stuff */ break;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b shape}{ is a variable of the }{\b ShapeType}{ enumerated data type, and its value is compared with the value in the enumeration. Since }{\b shape}{ is really just an }{\b int}{
, however, it can be any value an }{\b int}{ can hold (including a negative number). You can also compare an }{\b int}{ variable with a value in the enumeration.
\par If you don\rquote t like the way the compiler assigns values, you can do it yourself, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {enum ShapeType \{ 
\par   circle = 10, square = 20, rectangle = 50
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you give values to some names and not to others, the compiler will use the next integral value. For example,
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {enum snap \{ crackle = 25, pop \};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The compiler gives }{\b pop}{ the value 26.
\par You can see how much more readable the code is when you use enumerated data types. However, to some degree this is still an attempt (in C) to accomplish the things that we can do with a }{\b class}{ in C++, so you\rquote ll see }{\b enum}{
 used less in C++.}{\b 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776050}Saving memory with }{\b union}{{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776050} }{\pard\plain 
\s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {union\: saving memory with}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Sometimes a program will handle different types of data using the same variable. In this situation, you have two choices: you can create a }{\b struct}{
 containing all the possible different types you might need to store, or you can use a }{\b union}{. A }{\b union}{ piles all the data into a single space; it figures out the amount of space necessary for the largest item you\rquote ve put in the }{\b 
union}{, and makes that the size of the }{\b union}{. Use a }{\b union}{ to save memory.
\par Anytime you place a value in a }{\b union}{, the value always starts in the same place at the beginning of the }{\b union}{, but only uses as much space as is necessary. Thus, you create a \ldblquote super-variable,\rdblquote 
 capable of holding any of the }{\b union}{ variables. All the addresses of the }{\b union}{ variables are the same (in a class or }{\b struct}{, the addresses are different).
\par Here\rquote s a simple use of a }{\b union}{. Try removing various elements and see what effect it has on the size of the }{\b union}{. Notice that it makes no sense to declare more than one instance of a single data type in a }{\b union}{ (unless you
\rquote re just doing it to use a different name).
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Union.cpp
\par // The size and simple use of a union
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par union packed \{ // Declaration similar to a class
\par   char i;
\par   short j;
\par   int k;
\par   long l;
\par   float f;
\par   double d;  
\par   // The union will be the size of a 
\par   // double, since that's the largest element
\par \};  // Semicolon ends a union, like a struct
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   cout << "sizeof(packed) = " 
\par     << sizeof(packed) << endl;
\par   packed x;
\par   x.i = 'c';
\par   cout << x.i << endl;
\par   x.d = 3.14159;
\par   cout << x.d << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The compiler performs the proper assignment according to the union member you select.
\par Once you perform an assignment, the compiler doesn\rquote t care what you do with the union. In the above example, you could assign a floating-point value to }{\b x}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {x.f = 2.222;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {and then send it to the output as if it were an }{\b int}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {cout << x.i;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This would produce garbage.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776051}Arrays{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776051}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Arrays are a kind of composite type because they allow you to clump a lot of variables together, one right after the other, under a single identifier name. If you say:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int a[10];
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You create storage for 10 }{\b int}{ variables stacked on top of each other, but without unique identifier names for each variable. Instead, they are all lumped under the name }{\b a}{.
\par To access one of these }{\i array elements}{, you use the same square-bracket syntax that you use to define an array:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {a[5] = 47;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {However, you must remember that even though the }{\i size}{ of }{\b a}{ is 10, you select array elements starting at zero (this is sometimes called }{\i zero indexing}{), so you can only s
elect the array elements 0-9, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Arrays.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int a[10];
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++) \{
\par     a[i] = i * 10;
\par     cout << "a[" << i << "] = " << a[i] << endl;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Array access is extremely fast. However, if you index past the end of the array, there is no safety net \endash  you\rquote 
ll step on other variables. The other drawback is the fact that you must define the size of the array at compile time; if you want to change the size at runtime you can\rquote 
t do it with the above syntax (C does have a way to create an array dynamically, but it\rquote s significantly messier). The C++ }{\b vector}{
, introduced in the previous chapter, provides an array-like object that automatic resizes itself, so it is usually a much better solution if your array size cannot be known at compile time.
\par You can make an array of any type, even of }{\b struct}{s:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:StructArray.cpp
\par // An array of struct
\par 
\par typedef struct \{
\par   int i, j, k;
\par \} ThreeDpoint;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   ThreeDpoint p[10];
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++) \{
\par     p[i].i = i + 1;
\par     p[i].j = i + 2;
\par     p[i].k = i + 3;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice how the }{\b struct}{ identifier }{\b i}{ is independent of the }{\b for}{ loop\rquote s }{\b i}{.
\par To see that each element of an array is contiguous with the next, you can print out the addresses like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:ArrayAddresses.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int a[10];
\par   cout << "sizeof(int) = " << sizeof(int) <<endl;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
\par     cout << "&a[" << i << "] = " 
\par       << (long)&a[i] << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you run this program, you\rquote ll see that each element is one }{\b int}{ size away from the previous one. That is, they are stacked one on top of the other.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Pointers and arrays
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The identifier of an array is unlike the identifiers for ordinary variables. For one thing, an array identifier is not an lvalue \endash  you cannot assign to it. It\rquote 
s really just a hook into the square-bracket syntax, and when you give the name of an array, without square brackets, what you get is the starting address of the array:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:ArrayIdentifier.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int a[10];
\par   cout << "a = " << a << endl;
\par   cout << "&a[0] =" << &a[0] << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you run this program you\rquote ll see that the two addresses (which will be printed in hexadecimal, since there is no cast to }{\b long}{) are the same.
\par So one way to look at the array identifier is as a read-only pointer to the beginning of an array. And although we can\rquote t change the array identifier to point somewhere else, we }{\i can}{ create a
nother pointer and use that to move around in the array. In fact, the square-bracket syntax works with regular pointers, as well:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:PointersAndBrackets.cpp
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int a[10];
\par   int* ip = a;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
\par     ip[i] = i * 10;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The fact that naming an array produces its starting address turns out to be quite important when you want to pass an array to a function. If you declare an array as a function argument, what you
\rquote re really declaring is a pointer. So in the following example, }{\b func1(\~)}{ and }{\b func2(\~)}{ effectively have the same argument lists:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:ArrayArguments.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par void func1(int a[], int size) \{
\par   for(int i = 0; i < size; i++)
\par     a[i] = i * i - i;
\par \}
\par 
\par void func2(int* a, int size) \{
\par   for(int i = 0; i < size; i++)
\par     a[i] = i * i + i;
\par \}
\par 
\par void print(int a[], string name, int size) \{
\par   for(int i = 0; i < size; i++)
\par     cout << name << "[" << i << "] = " 
\par       << a[i] << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int a[5], b[5];
\par   // Probably garbage values:
\par   print(a, "a", 5);
\par   print(b, "b", 5);
\par   // Initialize the arrays:
\par   func1(a, 5);
\par   func1(b, 5);
\par   print(a, "a", 5);
\par   print(b, "b", 5);
\par   // Notice the arrays are always modified:
\par   func2(a, 5);
\par   func2(b, 5);
\par   print(a, "a", 5);
\par   print(b, "b", 5);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Even though }{\b func1(\~)}{ and }{\b func2(\~)}{
 declare their arguments differently, the usage is the same inside the function. There are some other issues that this example reveals: arrays cannot be passed by value}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ Unless you take the very strict approach that \ldblquote all argument passing in C/C++ is by value, and the \lquote value\rquote 
 of an array is what is produced by the array identifier: it\rquote s address.\rdblquote  This can be seen as true from the assembly-language standpoint, but I don\rquote 
t think it helps when trying to work with higher-level concepts. The addition of references in C++ makes the \ldblquote all passing is by value\rdblquote  argument more confusing, to the point where I feel it\rquote s more helpful to think in terms of 
\ldblquote passing by value\rdblquote  vs. \ldblquote passing addresses.\rdblquote }}}{, that is, you never automatically get a local copy of the array that you pass into a function. Thus, when you modify an array, you\rquote 
re always modifying the outside object. This can be a bit confusing at first, if you\rquote re expecting the pass-by-value provided with ordinary arguments.
\par You\rquote ll notice that }{\b print(\~)}{
 uses the square-bracket syntax for array arguments. Even though the pointer syntax and the square-bracket syntax are effectively the same when passing arrays as arguments, the square-bracket syntax makes it clearer to the 
reader that you mean for this argument to be an array.
\par Also note that the }{\b size}{ argument is passed in each case. Just passing the address of an array isn\rquote t enough information; you must always be able to know how big the array is inside your function, so you don\rquote 
t run off the end of that array.
\par Arrays can be of any type, including arrays of pointers. In fact, when you want to pass command-line arguments into your program, C & C++ have a special argument list for }{\b main(\~)}{ which looks like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{ // ...
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The first argument is the number of elements in the array which is the second argument. The second argument is always an array of }{\b char*}{
, because the arguments are passed from the command line as character arrays (and remember, an
 array can only be passed as a pointer). Each whitespace-delimited cluster of characters on the command line is turned into a separate array argument. The following program prints out all its command-line arguments by stepping through the array:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:CommandLineArgs.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   cout << "argc = " << argc << endl;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < argc; i++)
\par     cout << "argv[" << i << "] = " 
\par       << argv[i] << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You\rquote ll notice that }{\b argv[0]}{
 is the path and name of the program itself. This allows the program to discover information about itself. It also adds one more to the array of program arguments, so a common error when fetching command-line arguments is to grab }{\b argv[0]}{
 when you want }{\b argv[1]}{.
\par You are not forced to use }{\b argc}{ and }{\b argv}{ as identifiers in }{\b main(\~)}{; those identifiers are only conventions (but it will confuse people if you don\rquote t use them). Also, there are alternate ways to declare }{\b argv}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int main(int argc, char** argv) \{ // ...
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int main(int argc, char argv[][]) \{ // ...
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {All three forms are equivalent, but I find the form used in this book to be the most intuitive when reading the code, since it says, directly, \ldblquote this is an array of character pointers.
\rdblquote 
\par All you get from the comma
nd-line is character arrays; if you want to treat an argument as some other type, you are responsible for converting it, inside your program. To facilitate the conversion to numbers, there are some helper functions in the Standard C library, declared in }
{\b <cstdlib>}{. The simplest ones to use are }{\b atoi(\~)}{, }{\b atol(\~)}{ and }{\b atof(\~)}{, to convert an ASCII character array to an }{\b int}{, }{\b long}{ and }{\b double}{ floating-point value, respectively. Here\rquote s an example using }{
\b atoi(\~)}{ (the other two functions are called the same way):
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:ArgsToInts.cpp
\par // Converting command-line arguments to ints
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   for(int i = 1; i < argc; i++)
\par     cout << atoi(argv[i]) << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In this program, you can put any number of arguments on the command line. You\rquote ll notice that the }{\b for}{ loop starts at the value }{\b 1}{ to skip over the program name at }{\b argv[0]}{
. Also, if you put a floating-point number containing a decimal point on the command line, }{\b atoi(\~)}{ only takes the digits up to the decimal point. If you put non-numbers on the command line, these come back from }{\b atoi(\~)}{ as zero
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Pointer arithmetic
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If all you could do with a pointer that points at an array is treat it as if it were an alias for that array, pointers into arrays wouldn\rquote 
t be very interesting. However, pointers are more flexible than this, since they can be moved around (and remember, the array identifier cannot be moved).
\par }{\i Pointer arithmetic}{ refers to the application of some of the arithmetic operators to
 pointers. The reason pointer arithmetic is a separate subject from ordinary arithmetic is that pointers must conform to special constraints in order to make them behave properly. For example, a common operator to use with pointers is }{\b ++}{, which 
\ldblquote adds one to the pointer.\rdblquote  What this actually means is that the pointer is changed to move to \ldblquote the next value,\rdblquote  whatever that means. Here\rquote s an example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:PointerIncrement.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int i[10];
\par   double d[10];
\par   int* ip = i;
\par   double* dp = d;
\par   cout << "ip = " << (long)ip << endl;
\par   ip++;
\par   cout << "ip = " << (long)ip << endl;
\par   cout << "dp = " << (long)dp << endl;
\par   dp++;
\par   cout << "dp = " << (long)dp << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {For one run on my machine, the output is:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {ip = 6684124
\par ip = 6684128
\par dp = 6684044
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {dp = 6684052
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {What\rquote s interesting here is that even though the operation }{\b ++}{ appears to be the same operation for both the }{\b int*}{ and the }{\b double*}{
, you can see that the pointer has been changed only 4 bytes for the }{\b int*}{ but 8 bytes for the }{\b double*}{. Not coincidently, these are the sizes of }{\b int}{ and }{\b double}{ on my machine. And that\rquote 
s the trick of pointer arithmetic: the compiler figures out the right amount to change the pointer so that it\rquote s pointing to the next element in the array (pointer arithmetic is only meaningful within arrays). This even works with arrays of }{\b 
struct}{s:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:PointerIncrement2.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par typedef struct \{
\par   char c;
\par   short s;
\par   int i;
\par   long l;
\par   float f;
\par   double d;
\par   long double ld;
\par \} Primitives;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Primitives p[10];
\par   Primitives* pp = p;
\par   cout << "sizeof(Primitives) = " 
\par     << sizeof(Primitives) << endl;
\par   cout << "pp = " << (long)pp << endl;
\par   pp++;
\par   cout << "pp = " << (long)pp << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The output for one run on my machine was:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {sizeof(Primitives) = 40
\par pp = 6683764
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {pp = 6683804
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {So you can see the compiler also does the right thing for pointers to }{\b struct}{s (and }{\b class}{es and }{\b union}{s).
\par Pointer arithmetic also works with the operators }{\b --}{, }{\b +}{ and }{\b -}{, but the latter two operators are limited: you cannot add or subtract two pointers. Instead, you must add or subtract an integral value. Here\rquote 
s an example demonstrating the use of pointer arithmetic:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:PointerArithmetic.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par #define P(EXP) \\
\par   cout << #EXP << ": " << EXP << endl;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int a[10];
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
\par     a[i] = i; // Give it index values
\par   int* ip = a;
\par   P(*ip);
\par   P(*++ip);
\par   P(*(ip + 5));
\par   int* ip2 = ip + 5;
\par   P(*ip2);
\par   P(*(ip2 - 4));
\par   P(*--ip2);  
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It begins with another macro, but this one uses a preprocessor feature called }{\i stringizing}{ (implemented with the \lquote }{\b #}{\rquote 
 sign before an expression) which takes any expression and turns it into a character array. This is quite convenient, since it allows the expression to be printed, followed by a colon, followed by the value of the expression. In }{\b main(\~)}{
 you can see the useful shorthand that is produced.
\par Although pre- and postfix versions of }{\b ++}{ and }{\b -- }{are valid with pointers, only t
he prefix versions are used in this example because they are applied before the pointers are dereferenced in the above expressions, so they allow us to see the effects of the operations. Note that only integral values are being added and subtracted; if tw
o pointers were combined this way the compiler would not allow it. 
\par Here is the output of the above program:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {*ip: 0
\par *++ip: 1
\par *(ip + 5): 6
\par *ip2: 6
\par *(ip2 - 4): 2
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {*--ip2: 5
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In all cases, the pointer arithmetic results in the pointer being adjusted to point to the \ldblquote right place,\rdblquote  based on the size of the elements being pointed to.
\par If pointer arithmetic seems a bit overwhelming at first, don\rquote t worry. Most of the time you\rquote ll only need to create arrays and index into them with }{\b [\~]}{, and the most sophisticated pointer arithmetic you\rquote ll usually need is }{\b 
++}{ and }{\b --}{. Pointer arithmetic is generally reserved for more clever and complex programs, and many of the containers in the Standard C++ library hide most of these clever details so you don\rquote t have to worry about them.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776052}Debugging hints{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776052}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
In an ideal environment, you have an excellent debugger available that easily makes the behavior of your program transparent so you can quickly discover errors. However, most debuggers have blind spots, and these will require you to embed cod
e snippets in your program to help you understand what\rquote 
s going on. In addition, you may be developing in an environment (such as an embedded system, which is where I spent my formative years) that has no debugger available, and perhaps very limited feedbac
k (i.e. a one-line LED display). In these cases you become creative in the ways you discover and display information about the execution of your program. This section suggests some techniques for doing this.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776053}Debugging flags{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776053}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {debugging\: flags}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {flags\: debugging}}}{

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you hard-wire your debugging code into a program, you can run into problems. You start to get too much information, which makes the bugs difficult to isolate. When you think you\rquote 
ve found the bug you start tearing out debugging code, only to find you need to put it back in again. You can solve these problems with two types of flags: preprocessor debugging flags and runtime debugging flags.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Preprocessor debugging flags}{\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \v\f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {preprocessor\:
 debugging flags}}}{\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \v\f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {debugging\: preprocessor flags}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {By using the preprocessor to }{\b #define}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {#define}}}{
 one or more debugging flags (preferably in a header file), you can test a flag using an }{\b #ifdef}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b #ifdef}}}{
 statement and conditionally include debugging code. When you think your debugging is finished, you can simply }{\b #undef}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {#undef}}}{
 the flag(s) and the code will automatically be removed (and you\rquote ll reduce the size and runtime overhead of your executable file).
\par It is best to decide on names for debugging flags before you begin building your project so the
 names will be consistent. Preprocessor flags are traditionally distinguished from variables by writing them in all upper case. A common flag name is simply DEBUG (but be careful you don\rquote 
t use NDEBUG, which is reserved in C). The sequence of statements might be:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#define DEBUG // Probably in a header file
\par //...
\par #ifdef DEBUG // Check to see if flag is defined
\par /* debugging code here */
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // DEBUG
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Most C and C++ implementations will also let you }{\b #define}{ and }{\b #undef}{ flags from the compiler command line, so 
you can re-compile code and insert debugging information with a single command (preferably via the makefile, a tool that will be described next). Check your local documentation  for details.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Runtime debugging flags}{\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \v\f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {
run-time debugging flags}}}{\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \v\f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {debugging\: run-time}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
In some situations it is more convenient to turn debugging flags on and off during program execution, especially by setting them when the program starts up using the command line. Large programs are tedious to recompile just to insert debugging code.

\par To turn the debugger on and off dynamically, create }{\b bool}{ flags:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:DynamicDebugFlags.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par // Debug flags aren't necessarily global:
\par bool debug = false;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   for(int i = 0; i < argc; i++)
\par     if(string(argv[i]) == "--debug=on")
\par       debug = true;
\par   bool go = true;
\par   while(go) \{
\par     if(debug) \{
\par       // Debugging code here
\par       cout << "Debugger is now on!" << endl;
\par     \} else \{
\par       cout << "Debugger is now off." << endl;
\par     \}  
\par     cout << "Turn debugger [on/off/quit]: ";
\par     string reply;
\par     cin >> reply;
\par     if(reply == "on") debug = true; // Turn it on
\par     if(reply == "off") debug = false; // Off
\par     if(reply == "quit") break; // Out of 'while'
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This program continues to allow you to turn the debugging flag on and off until you type \ldblquote quit\rdblquote 
 to tell it you want to exit. Notice that it requires that full words be typed in, not just letters (you can shorten it to letter if you wish). Also, a command-line argument can optionally be used to turn debugging on a startup \endash 
 this argument can appear anyplace in the command line, since the startup code in }{\b main(\~)}{ looks at all the arguments. The testing is quite simple because of the expression:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {string(argv[i])
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This takes the }{\b argv[i]}{ character array and creates a }{\b string}{, which then can be easily compared to the right-hand side of the }{\b ==}{. The above program searches for the entire string }
{\b --debug=on}{. You can also look for }{\b --debug=}{ and then see what\rquote s after that, to provide more options. The chapter on the }{\b string}{ class later in the book will show you how to do that.
\par Although a debugging flag is one of the relatively few areas where it makes a lot of sense to use a global variable, there\rquote s nothing that says it must be that way. Notice that the variable is in lower case letters to remind the reader it isn
\rquote t a preprocessor flag.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776054}Turning variables and expressions into string}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {variable\: turning name into a string}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {string
\: turning variable name into}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {stringizing}}}{s{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776054}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When writing debugging code, it is tedious to write print expressions consisting of a character array containing the variable name, followed by the variable. Fortunately, Standard C includes the }{\i 
stringize}{ operator \lquote }{\b #}{\rquote , which was used earlier in this chapter. When you put a }{\b #}{
 before an argument in a preprocessor macro, the preprocessor turns that argument into a character array. This, combined with the fact that character arrays with no intervening punctuation are concatenated into a singl
e character array, allows you to make a very convenient macro for printing the values of variables during debugging:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#define PR(x) cout << #x " = " << x << "\\n";
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you print the variable }{\b a}{ by calling the macro }{\b PR(a)}{, it will have the same effect as the code:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {cout << "a = " << a << "\\n";
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This same process works with entire expressions. The following program uses a macro to create a shorthand that prints the stringized expression and then evaluates the expression and prints the result:

\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:StringizingExpressions.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par #define P(A) cout << #A << ": " << (A) << endl;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int a = 1, b = 2, c = 3;
\par   P(a); P(b); P(c);
\par   P(a + b);
\par   P((c - a)/b);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can see how a technique like this can quickly become indispensible, especially if you have no debugger (or must use multiple development environments). You can also insert an }{\b #ifdef}{
 to cause }{\b P(A)}{ to be defined as \ldblquote nothing\rdblquote  when you want to strip out debugging.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776055}The C }{\b assert( )}{ macro{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776055}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {assert() macro in ANSI C}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {debugging\:
 assert() macro}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the standard header file }{\b <cassert> }{you\rquote ll find }{\b assert(\~)}{, which is a convenient debugging macro. When you use }{\b assert(\~)}{
, you give it an argument that is an expression you are \ldblquote asserting to be true.\rdblquote  The preprocessor generates code that will test the assertion. If the assertion isn\rquote 
t true, the program will stop after issuing an error message telling you what the assertion was and that it failed. Here\rquote s a trivial example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C03:Assert.cpp
\par // Use of the assert() debugging macro
\par #include <cassert>  // Contains the macro
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int i = 100;
\par   assert(i != 100);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The macro originated in Standard C, so it\rquote s also available in the header file }{\b assert.h}{. 
\par When you are finished debugging, you can remove the code generated by the macro by placing the line:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#define NDEBUG
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {#define NDEBUG
\par }}}\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {in the program before the inclusion of }{\b <cassert>}{, or by defining NDEBUG on the compiler command line. NDEBUG is a flag used in }{\b <cassert> }{to change 
the way code is generated by the macros.
\par Later in this book, you\rquote ll see some more sophisticated alternatives to }{\b assert(\~)}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776056}Make}{\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \v\f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {make}}}{: an essential tool for separate compilation{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776056}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When using }{\i separate compilation}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {separate compilation}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {compilation\:
 separate}}}{(breaking code into a number of translation units), you need some way to automatically compile each file and to tell the linker to build all the pieces \endash  along with the appropriate libraries and startup code \endash 
 into an executable file. Most compilers allow you to do this with a single command-line statement. For the Gnu C++ compiler, for example, you might say
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {g++ SourceFile1.cpp SourceFile2.cpp
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The problem with this approach is that the compiler will first compile each individual file, regardless of whether that file }{\i needs}{
 to be rebuilt or not. With many files in a project, it can become prohibitive to recompile everything if you\rquote ve only changed a single file.
\par The solution to this problem, developed on Unix but available everywhere in some form, is a program called }{\b make}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {make}}}{. The }{\b make}{
 utility manages all the individual files in a project by following the instructions in a text file called a }{\b makefile}{. When you edit some of the files in a project and type }{\b make}{, the }{\b make}{ program follows the guidelines in the }{\b 
makefile }{to compare the dates on the source code files to the dates on the corresponding target files, and if a source code file date is more recent than its target file, }{\b make}{ invokes the compiler on the source code file.  }{\b make}{ only recom
piles the source code files that were changed, and any other source-code files that are affected by the modified files. By using }{\b make}{, you don\rquote 
t have to re-compile all the files in your project every time you make a change, nor do you have to check to see that everything was built properly. The }{\b makefile}{ contains all the commands to put your project together. Learning to use }{\b make}{
 will save you a lot of time and frustration. You\rquote ll also discover that }{\b make}{ is the typical way that you install new software on a Linux/Unix machine (although those }{\b makefile}{
s tend to be far more complicated than the ones presented in this book, and you\rquote ll often automatically generate a }{\b makefile}{ for your particular machine as part of the installation process).
\par Because }{\b make}{ is available in some form for virtually all C++ compilers (and even if it isn\rquote t, you can use freely-available }{\b make}{
s with any compiler), it will be the tool used throughout this book. However, compiler vendors have also created their own project building tools}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {project building tools}}}{
. These tools ask you which files are in your project, and determine all the relationships themselves. These tools use something similar to a }{\b makefile}{, generally called a }{\i project file}{, but the programming environment maintains this fil
e so you don\rquote 
t have to worry about it. The configuration and use of project files varies from one development environment to another, so you must find the appropriate documentation on how to use them (although project file tools provided by compiler vendor
s are usually so simple to use that you can learn them by playing around \endash  my favorite form of education). 
\par The }{\b makefile}{s used within this book should work even if you are also using a specific vendor\rquote s project-building tool.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776057}Make activities{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776057}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you type }{\b make}{ (or whatever the name of your \ldblquote make\rdblquote  program happens to be), the }{\b 
make}{ program looks in the current directory for a file named }{\b makefile}{, which you\rquote ve created if it\rquote s your project. This file lists dependencies}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar
\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {make\: dependencies}}}{ between source code files. }{\b make}{
 looks at the dates on files. If a dependent file has an older date than a file it depends on, }{\b make}{ executes the }{\i rule}{ given after the dependency.
\par All comments in }{\b makefile}{s start with a }{\b #}{ and continue to the end of the line.
\par As a simple example, the }{\b makefile}{ for a program called \ldblquote hello\rdblquote  might contain:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {# A comment
\par hello.exe: hello.cpp
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {        mycompiler hello.cpp
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This says that }{\b hello.exe}{ (the target) depends on }{\b hello.cpp}{. When }{\b hello.cpp}{
 has a newer date than }{\b hello.exe}{, }{\b make}{ executes the \ldblquote rule\rdblquote  }{\b mycompiler hello.cpp}{. There may be multiple dependencies and multiple rules. Many }{\b make}{
 programs require that all the rules begin with a tab. Other than that, whitespace is generally ignored so you can format for readability.
\par The rules are not restricted to being calls to the compiler; you can call any program you\rquote d like from within }{\b make}{. By creating groups of interdependent dependency-rule sets, you can modify your source code files, type }{\b make}{
 and be certain that all the affected files will be rebuilt correctly.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Macros
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A }{\b makefile}{ may contain }{\i macros}{ (note that these are completely different from C/C++ preprocessor macros)
}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {make\:macros}}}{. Macros allow convenient string replacement. The }{\b makefile}{
s in this book use a macro to invoke the C++ compiler. For example,
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {CPP = mycompiler
\par hello.exe: hello.cpp
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {        $(CPP) hello.cpp
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b = }{is used to identify }{\b CPP }{as a macro, and the }{\b $}{
 and parentheses expand the macro. In this case, the expansion means that the macro call }{\b $(}{CPP) will be replaced with the string }{\b mycompiler}{. With the above macro, if you want to change to a different compiler called }{\b cpp}{,}{\b  }{
you just change the macro to:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {CPP = cpp
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can also add compiler flags, etc., to the macro, or use separate macros to add compiler flags.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Suffix Rules
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It becomes tedious to tell }{\b make}{ how to invoke the compiler for every single}{\b  cpp}{ file in your project, when you know it\rquote s the same basic process each time. Since }{\b make}{
 is designed to be a time-saver, it also has a way to abbreviate actions, as long as they depend on file name suffixes. These abbreviations are called }{\i suffix rules}{. A suffix rule is the way to teach }{\b make}{
 how to convert a file with one type of extension (}{\b .cpp}{, for example) into a file with another type of extension (}{\b .obj}{ or }{\b .exe}{). Once you teach }{\b make}{ the rules for p
roducing one kind of file from another, all you have to do is tell }{\b make}{ which files depend on which other files. When }{\b make}{ finds a file with a date earlier than the file it depends on, it uses the rule to create a new file. 
\par The suffix rule tells }{\b make}{ that it doesn\rquote t need explicit rules to build everything, but instead it can figure out how to build things based on their file extension. In this case it says: \ldblquote to build a file that ends in }{\b exe}{
 from one that ends in }{\b cpp}{, invoke the following command.\rdblquote  Here\rquote s what it looks like for the above example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {CPP = mycompiler
\par .SUFFIXES: .exe .cpp
\par .cpp.exe:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {        $(CPP) $<
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b .SUFFIXES}{ directive tells }{\b make}{ that it should watch out for any of the following file-name extensions because they have special meaning. Next you see the suffix rule }{\b .cpp.exe}{
 which says: \ldblquote here\rquote s how to convert any file with an extension of }{\b cpp}{ to one with an extension of }{\b exe}{\rdblquote  (when the }{\b cpp}{ file is more recent than the }{\b exe}{ file). As before, the }{\b $(CPP)}{
 macro is used, but then you see something new: }{\b $<}{. Because this begins with a \lquote }{\b $}{\rquote  it\rquote s a macro, but this is one of }{\b make}{\rquote s special built-in macros. The }{\b $< }{
can only be used in suffix rules, and it means \ldblquote whatever prerequisite triggered the rule\rdblquote  (sometimes called the }{\i dependent}{), which in this case translates to: \ldblquote the }{\b cpp}{ file that needs to be compiled.\rdblquote 

\par Once the suffix rules have been set up, you can simply say, for example, \ldblquote }{\b make Union.cpp}{,\rdblquote  and the suffix rule will kick in, even though there\rquote s no mention of \ldblquote Union\rdblquote  anywhere in the }{\b makefile}{. 

\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Default targets
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {After the macros and suffix rules, }{\b make}{ looks for the first \ldblquote target\rdblquote  in a file, and builds that, unless you specify differently. So for the following }{\b makefile}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {CPP = mycompiler
\par .SUFFIXES: .exe .cpp
\par .cpp.exe:
\par         $(CPP) $<
\par target1.exe:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {target2.exe:
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you just type \lquote }{\b make}{\rquote , }{\b target1.exe}{ will be built (using the default suffix rule) because that\rquote s the first target that }{\b make}{ encounters. To build }{\b 
target2.exe}{ you\rquote d have to explicitly say \lquote }{\b make target2.exe}{\rquote . This becomes tedious, so you normally create a default \ldblquote dummy\rdblquote  target which depends on all the rest of the targets, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {CPP = mycompiler
\par .SUFFIXES: .exe .cpp
\par .cpp.exe:
\par         $(CPP) $<
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {all: target1.exe target2.exe
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here, \lquote }{\b all}{\rquote  does not exist, and there\rquote s no file called \lquote }{\b all}{\rquote , so every time you type }{\b make}{, the program sees \lquote }{\b all}{\rquote 
 as the first target in the list (and thus the default target), then it sees that \lquote }{\b all}{\rquote  does not exist so it had better make it by checking all the dependencies. So it looks at }{\b target1.exe}{ and (using the suffix rule) sees w
hether (1) }{\b target1.exe}{ exists and (2) whether }{\b target1.cpp}{ is more recent than }{\b target1.exe}{
, and if so runs the suffix rule (if you provide an explicit rule for a particular target, that rule is used instead). Then it moves on to the next file in the default target list. Thus, by creating a default target list (typically called \lquote }{\b all
}{\rquote  by convention, but you can call it anything) you can cause every executable in your project to be made simply by typing \lquote }{\b make}{\rquote . In addition, you can have other non-default target lists that do other things \endash 
 for example, you could set it up so that typing \lquote }{\b make debug}{\rquote  rebuilds all your files with debugging wired in.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776058}Makefile}{s in this book{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776058}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Using the program }{\b ExtractCode.cpp}{ which is shown in Chapter XX, all the code listings in this
 book are automatically extracted from the ASCII text version of this book and placed in subdirectories according to their chapters. In addition, }{\b ExtractCode.cpp }{creates several }{\b makefile}{
s in each subdirectory (with different names) so that you can simply move into that subdirectory and type }{\b make -f mycompiler.makefile}{ (substituting the name of your compiler for \lquote }{\b mycompiler}{\rquote , the \lquote }{\b -f}{\rquote 
 flag says \ldblquote use what follows as the }{\b makefile}{\rdblquote ). Finally, }{\b ExtractCode.cpp}{ creates a \ldblquote master\rdblquote  }{\b makefile}{ in the root directory where the book\rquote s files have been expanded, and this }{\b 
makefile}{ descends into each subdirectory and calls }{\b make}{ with the appropriate }{\b makefile}{. This way you can compile all the code in the book by invoking a single }{\b make}{ command, and the process will stop whenever 
your compiler is unable to handle a particular file (note that a Standard C++ conforming compiler should be able to compile all the files in this book). Because implementations of }{\b make}{
 vary from system to system, only the most basic, common features are used in the generated }{\b makefile}{s. 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776059}An example }{\b makefile}{{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776059}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As mentioned, the code-extraction tool }{\b ExtractCode.cpp}{ automatically generates }{\b makefile}{
s for each chapter. Because of this, the }{\b makefile}{s for each chapter will not be placed in the book (all the makefiles are packaged with the source code, which is freely available at }{\b http://www.BruceEckel.com}{).}{\b  }{However, it\rquote 
s useful to see an example of a }{\b makefile}{. What follows is a very shortened version of the one that was automatically generated for this chapter by the book\rquote s extraction tool. You\rquote ll find more than one }{\b makefile}{
 in each subdirectory (they have different names \endash  you invoke a specific one with \lquote }{\b make -f}{\rquote ). This one is for Gnu C++:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {CPP = g++
\par OFLAG = -o
\par .SUFFIXES : .o .cpp .c
\par .cpp.o :
\par \tab $(CPP) $(CPPFLAGS) -c $<
\par .c.o :
\par \tab $(CPP) $(CPPFLAGS) -c $<
\par 
\par all: \\
\par \tab Return \\
\par \tab Declare \\
\par \tab Ifthen \\
\par \tab Guess \\
\par \tab Guess2
\par # Rest of the files for this chapter not shown
\par 
\par Return: Return.o 
\par \tab $(CPP) $(OFLAG)Return Return.o 
\par 
\par Declare: Declare.o 
\par \tab $(CPP) $(OFLAG)Declare Declare.o 
\par 
\par Ifthen: Ifthen.o 
\par \tab $(CPP) $(OFLAG)Ifthen Ifthen.o 
\par 
\par Guess: Guess.o 
\par \tab $(CPP) $(OFLAG)Guess Guess.o 
\par 
\par Guess2: Guess2.o 
\par \tab $(CPP) $(OFLAG)Guess2 Guess2.o 
\par 
\par Return.o: Return.cpp 
\par Declare.o: Declare.cpp 
\par Ifthen.o: Ifthen.cpp 
\par Guess.o: Guess.cpp 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Guess2.o: Guess2.cpp
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The macro CPP is set to the name of the compiler. To use a different compiler, you can either edit the }{\b makefile}
{ or change the value of the macro on the command line, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {make CPP=cpp
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Note, however, that }{\b ExtractCode.cpp}{ has an automatic scheme to automatically build }{\b makefile}{s for additional compilers.
\par The second macro }{\b OFLAG}{ is the flag that\rquote s used to indicate the name of the output file. Although many compilers automatically assume the output file has the same base name as the input file, others don\rquote t (su
ch as Linux/Unix compilers, which default to creating a file called }{\b a.out}{).
\par You can see there are two suffix rules here, one for}{\b  cpp}{ files and one for }{\b .c}{ files (in case any C source code needs to be compiled). The default target is }{\b all}{, and each line for this target is \ldblquote continued\rdblquote 
 by using the backslash, up until }{\b Guess2}{, which is the last one in the list and thus has no backslash. There are many more files in this chapter, but only these are shown here for the sake of brevity.
\par The suffix rules take care of creating object files (with a }{\b .o }{extension) from }{\b cpp }{
files, but in general you need to explicitly state rules for creating the executable, because normally an executable is created by linking many different object files and }{\b make}{ cannot guess what those are
. Also, in this case (Linux/Unix) there is no standard extension for executables so a suffix rule won\rquote t work for these simple situation. Thus, you see all the rules for building the final executables explicitly stated.
\par This }{\b makefile}{ takes the absolute safest route of using as few }{\b make}{ features as possible \endash  it only uses the basic }{\b make}{
 concepts of targets and dependencies, as well as macros. This way it is virtually assured of working with as many }{\b make}{ programs as possible. It tends to produce a larger }{\b makefile}{, but that\rquote s not so bad since it\rquote 
s automatically generated by }{\b ExtractCode.cpp}{.
\par There are lots of other }{\b make}{ features that this book will not use, as well as newer and cleverer versions and variations of }{\b make}{ with advanced shortcuts that can save a lot o
f time. Your local documentation may describe the further features of your particular }{\b make}{, and you can learn more about }{\b make}{ from }{\i Managing Projects with Make}{ by Oram & Talbott (O\rquote 
Reilly, 1993). Also, if your compiler vendor does not supply a }{\b make}{ or they use a non-standard }{\b make}{, you can find Gnu make for virtually any platform in existence by searching the Internet for Gnu archives (of which there are many).
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776060}Summary{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776060}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This chapter was a fairly intense tour through all the fundamental features of C++ syntax, most of which are inherited from and in common with C (and result in C++\rquote 
s vaunted backwards compatibility with C). Although some C++ features were introduced here, this tour is primarily intended for people who are conversant in programming, and simply need to be given an introduction to the syntax basics of C and C++. If you
\rquote re already a C programmer, you may have even seen one or two things about C here that were unfamiliar, aside from the C++ features that were most likely new to you. However, if this c
hapter has still seemed a bit overwhelming, you may want to consider going through the CD-ROM course }{\i Thinking in C: Foundations for C++ & Java}{ (which contains lectures, excercises and guided solutions) available at http://www.MindView.net.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776061}Exercises{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776061}
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 1. \tab}}\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls265\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls265\adjustright \fs20\lang1024\cgrid {Create a header file (with an extension of \lquote }{\b .h}{\rquote ). In this file, declare a group of functions by varying the argument lists and return values from among the following: }{\b void}{, }{\b char}{, 
}{\b int}{, and }{\b float}{. Now create a }{\b .cpp}{ file which includes your header file and creates definitions for all these functions. Each definition should simply print out the function name, argument list and return type so you know it\rquote 
s been called. Create a second }{\b .cpp}{ file which includes your header file and defines }{\b int main(\~)}{, containing calls to all your functions. Compile and run your program.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 2. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls265\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls265\adjustright {Write a program that uses two nested }{\b for}{ loops and the modulus operator (}{\b %}{) to detect and print prime numbers (integral numbers that are not evenly divisible by any other numbers except for themselves and 1).
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 3. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls265\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls265\adjustright {Write a program that uses a }{\b while }{loop to read words from standard input (}{\b cin}{) into a }{\b string}{. This is an \ldblquote infinite\rdblquote  }{\b while}{ loop, which you break out of (and exit the program) using a }{\b 
break}{ statement. For each word that is read, evaluate it}{\b  }{by first using a sequence of }{\b if}{ statements to \ldblquote map\rdblquote  an integral value to the word, and then use a }{\b switch}{
 statement that uses that integral value as its selector (this sequence of events is not meant to be good programming style; it\rquote s just supposed to give you exercise with control flow). Inside each }{\b case}{,}{\b  }{
print something meaningful. You must decide what the \ldblquote interesting\rdblquote  words are and what the meaning is. You must also decide what word will signal the end of the program. Test the program by redirecting a file into the program\rquote 
s standard input (if you want to save typing, this file can be your program\rquote s source file).
