\s1\fi-1080\li990\sa360\sl240\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \cbpat8 \f4\fs96\cf1\kerning18\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776062}4: Data abstraction{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373819}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776062}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {
C++ is a productivity enhancement tool. Why else would you make the effort (and it is an effort, regardless of how easy we attempt to make the transition)
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {to switch from some language that you already know and are productive with to a new language where you\rquote re going to be }{\i less}{
 productive for a while, until you get the hang of it? It\rquote s because you\rquote ve become convinced that you\rquote re going to get big gains by using this new tool.
\par Pr
oductivity, in computer programming terms, means that fewer people can make much more complex and impressive programs in less time. There are certainly other issues when it comes to choosing a language, like efficiency (does the nature of the language cau
s
e slowdown and code bloat?), safety (does the language help you ensure that your program will always do what you plan, and handle errors gracefully?), and maintenance (does the language help you create code that is easy to understand, modify and extend?).
 These are certainly important factors that will be examined in this book.
\par But raw productivity means a program that formerly took three of you a week to write now takes one of you a day or two. This touches several levels of economics. You\rquote re happy because
 you get the rush of power that comes from building something, your client (or boss) is happy because products are produced faster and with fewer people, and the customers are happy because they get products more cheaply. The only way to get massive incre
ases in productivity is to leverage off other people\rquote s code. That is, to use libraries.
\par A library}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {library}}}{
 is simply a bunch of code that someone else has written and packaged together. Often, the most minimal package is a file with an extension like }{\b lib}{ and one or more header files to tell your compiler what\rquote 
s in the library. The linker knows how to search through the library file and extract the appropriate compiled code. But that\rquote s only one way to deliver a library. On platforms that span many archit
ectures, like Linux/Unix, often the only sensible way to deliver a library is with source code, so it can be reconfigured and recompiled on the new target.
\par Thus, libraries are probably the most important way to improve productivity, and one of the primary design goals of C++ is to make library use easier. This implies that there\rquote 
s something hard about using libraries in C. Understanding this factor will give you a first insight into the design of C++, and thus insight into how to use it.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373821}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776063}A tiny C-like library
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373821}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776063}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A library usually starts out as a collection of functions, but if you have used third-party C libraries you know there\rquote 
s usually more to it than that because there\rquote s more to life than behavior, actions and functions. There are also characteristics (blue
, pounds, texture, luminance), which are represented by data. And when you start to deal with a set of characteristics in C, it is very convenient to clump them together into a }{\b struct}{
, especially if you want to represent more than one similar thing in your problem space. Then you can make a variable of this }{\b struct}{ for each thing.
\par }\pard \qj\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Thus, most C libraries have a set of }{\b struct}{s and a set of functions that act on those }{\b struct}{
s. As an example of what such a system looks like, consider a programming tool that acts like an array, but whose size can be established at runtime, when it is created. I\rquote ll call it a }{\b CStash}{. Although it\rquote 
s written in C++, it has the style of what you\rquote d write in C:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C04:CLib.h
\par // Header file for a C-like library
\par // An array-like entity created at runtime
\par 
\par typedef struct CStashTag \{
\par   int size;      // Size of each space
\par   int quantity;  // Number of storage spaces
\par   int next;      // Next empty space
\par   // Dynamically allocated array of bytes:
\par   unsigned char* storage;
\par \} CStash;
\par 
\par void initialize(CStash* s, int size);
\par void cleanup(CStash* s);
\par int add(CStash* s, const void* element);
\par void* fetch(CStash* s, int index);
\par int count(CStash* s);
\par void inflate(CStash* s, int increase);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A tag name like }{\b CStashTag}{ is generally used }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
tag name}}}{for a }{\b struct}{ in case you need to reference the }{\b struct }{inside itself. For example, when creating a }{\i linked list}{ (each element in your list contains a pointer to the next element), you need a pointer to the next }{\b struct}{
 variable, so you need a way to identify the type of that pointer within the }{\b struct}{ body. Also, you'll almost universally see the }{\b typedef}{ as shown above for every }{\b struct}{ in a C library. This is done so you can treat the }{\b struct}{
 as if it were a new type and define variables of that }{\b struct}{ like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {CStash A, B, C;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b storage}{ pointer is an }{\b unsigned char*}{. An }{\b unsigned char}{ is the smallest piece of storage a C compiler supports, }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {storage\: sizes}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {size\: storage}}}{
although on some machines it can be the same size as the largest. It\rquote s implementation dependent, but is often one byte long. You might think that because the }{\b CStash}{ is designed to hold any type of variable, a }{\b void*}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {void\: pointer}}}{ would be more appropriate here. However, the purpose is not to treat this storage as a block of some un
known type, but rather as a block of contiguous bytes.
\par The source code for the implementation file (which you may not get if you buy a library commercially \endash  you might get only a compiled }{\b obj}{ or }{\b lib}{ or }{\b dll}{, etc.) looks like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C04:CLib.cpp \{O\}
\par // Implementation of example C-like library
\par // Declare structure and functions:
\par #include "CLib.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <cassert> 
\par using namespace std;
\par // Quantity of elements to add
\par // when increasing storage:
\par const int increment = 100;
\par 
\par void initialize(CStash* s, int sz) \{
\par   s->size = sz;
\par   s->quantity = 0;
\par   s->storage = 0;
\par   s->next = 0;
\par \}
\par 
\par int add(CStash* s, const void* element) \{
\par   if(s->next >= s->quantity) //Enough space left?
\par     inflate(s, increment);
\par   // Copy element into storage,
\par   // starting at next empty space:
\par   int startBytes = s->next * s->size;
\par   unsigned char* e = (unsigned char*)element;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < s->size; i++)
\par     s->storage[startBytes + i] = e[i];
\par   s->next++;
\par   return(s->next - 1); // Index number
\par \}
\par 
\par void* fetch(CStash* s, int index) \{
\par   // Check index boundaries:
\par   assert(0 <= index && index < s->next);
\par   // Produce pointer to desired element:
\par   return &(s->storage[index * s->size]);
\par \}
\par 
\par int count(CStash* s) \{
\par   return s->next;  // Elements in CStash
\par \}
\par 
\par void inflate(CStash* s, int increase) \{
\par   assert(increase > 0);
\par   int newQuantity = s->quantity + increase;
\par   int newBytes = newQuantity * s->size;
\par   int oldBytes = s->quantity * s->size;
\par   unsigned char* b = new unsigned char[newBytes];
\par   for(int i = 0; i < oldBytes; i++)
\par     b[i] = s->storage[i]; // Copy old to new
\par   delete [](s->storage); // Old storage
\par   s->storage = b; // Point to new memory
\par   s->quantity = newQuantity;
\par \}
\par 
\par void cleanup(CStash* s) \{
\par   if(s->storage != 0) \{
\par    cout << "freeing storage" << endl;
\par    delete []s->storage;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
