storage}{ pointer is set to zero, and the }{\b size}{ indicator is also zero \endash  no initial storage is allocated.
\par The }{\b add(\~)}{ function inserts an element into the }{\b CStash}{ at the next available location. First, it checks to see if there is any available space left. If not, it expands the storage using the }{\b inflate(\~)}{ function, described later.

\par Because the compiler doesn\rquote t know the specific type of the variable being stored (all the function gets is a }{\b void*}{), you can\rquote 
t just do an assignment, which would certainly be the convenient thing. Instead, you must copy the variable byte-by-byte. The most straightforward way to perform the copying is with array indexing. Typically, there are already data bytes in }{\b storage}{
, and this is indicated by the value of }{\b next}{. To start with the right byte offset, }{\b next}{ is multiplied by the size of each element (in bytes) to produce }{\b startBytes}{. Then the argument }{\b element}{ is cast to an }{\b unsigned char}{
* so that it can be addressed byte-by-byte and copied into the available }{\b storage}{ space. }{\b next}{ is incremented so that it indicates the next available piece of storage, and the \ldblquote index number\rdblquote  where the 
value was stored so that value can be retrieved using this index number with }{\b fetch(\~)}{.
\par }{\b fetch(\~) }{checks to see that the index isn\rquote t out of bounds and then returns the address of the desired variable, calculated using the }{\b index}{ argument. Since }{\b index }{indicates the number of elements to offset into the }{\b Cstash}{
, it must be multiplied by the number of bytes occupied by each piece to produce the numerical offset in bytes. When this offset is used to index into }{\b storage }{using array indexing, you don\rquote t get the address
, but instead the byte at the address. To produce the address, you must use the address-of operator }{\b &}{. 
\par }{\b count(\~)}{ may look a bit strange at first to a seasoned C programmer. It seems like a lot of trouble to go through to do something that would probably be a lot easier to do by hand. If you have a }{\b struct CStash}{ called }{\b intStash}{
, for example, it would seem much more straightforward to find out how many elements it has by saying }{\b intStash.next}{ instead of making a function call (which has overhead) like }{\b count(&intStash)}{
. However, if you wanted to change the internal representation of }{\b CStash}{ and thus the way the count was calculated, the function call interface allows the necessary flexibility. But alas, most programmers won\rquote t bother to find out about your 
\ldblquote better\rdblquote  design for the library. They\rquote ll look at the }{\b struct}{ and grab the }{\b next}{ value directly, and possibly even change }{\b next}{
 without your permission. If only there were some way for the library designer to have better control over things like this! (Yes, that\rquote s foreshadowing.)
