 dynamic memory allocation}}}{You never know the maximum amount of storage you might need for a }{\b CStash}{, so the memory pointed to by }{\b storage}{ is allocated from the }{\i heap}{. The heap}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {heap}}}{ is a big block of memory used for allocating smaller pieces at runtime. You use the heap when you don\rquote t know the size of the memory you\rquote 
ll need while you\rquote re writing a program. That is, only at runtime will you find out that you need space to hold 200 }{\b Airplane}{ variables instead of 20. In Standard C, dynamic-memory allocation functions are part of and include }{\b malloc(\~)}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {malloc(\~)}}}{, }{\b calloc(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {calloc(\~)}}}
{, }{\b realloc(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {realloc(\~)}}}{, and }{\b free(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {free(\~)}}}{. Instead of library calls, however, C++ has a more sophisticated (albeit simpler to use) approach to dynamic memory which is integrated into the language via the keywords }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{.
\par The }{\b inflate(\~)}{ function uses }{\b new}{ to get a bigger chunk of space for the }{\b CStash}{. In this situation, we will only expand memory and not shrink it, and the }{\b assert(\~)}{ will guarantee that a negative number is not passed to }{\b 
inflate(\~)}{ as the }{\b increase}{ value. The new number of elements that can be held (after }{\b inflate(\~)}{ completes) is calculated as }{\b newQuantity}{, and this is multiplied by the number of bytes per element to produce }{\b newBytes}{
, which will be the number of bytes in the allocation. So that we know how many bytes to copy over from the old location, }{\b oldBytes}{ is calculated using the old }{\b quantity}{.
\par The actual storage allocation occurs in the }{\i new-expression}{, which is the expression involving the }{\b new}{ keyword:
