\par A new-expression returns a }{\i pointer}{ to an object of the exact type that you asked for. So if you say }{\b new Type}{, you get back a pointer to a }{\b Type}{.}{\b  }{If you say }{\b new int}{, you get back a pointer to an }{\b int}{. If you want a }
{\b new}{ }{\b unsigned char}{ array, you get back a pointer to the first element of that array. The compiler will ensure that you assign the return value of the new-expression to a pointer of the correct type.
\par Of course, any time you request memory it\rquote s possible for the request to fail, if there is no more memory. As you will learn, C++ has mechanisms that come into play if the memory-allocation operation is unsuccessful.
\par Once the new storage is allocated, the data in the old storage must be copied to the new storage \endash 
 this is again accomplished with array indexing, copying one byte at a time in a loop. After the data is copied, the old storage must be released so that it can be used by other parts of the program if they need new storage. The }{\b delete}{
 keyword is the complement of }{\b new}{, and must be applied to release any storage that is allocated with }{\b new}{ (if you forget to use }{\b delete}{, that storage remains unavailable, and if this so-called }{\i memory leak }{happens enough, you
\rquote ll run out of memory). In addition, there\rquote s a special syntax when you\rquote re deleting an array. It\rquote 
s as if you must remind the compiler that this pointer is not just pointing to one object, but to an array of objects: you put a set of empty square brackets in front of the pointer to be deleted:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {delete []myArray;
