inflate(\~)}{ has completed its job.
\par Note that the heap manager is fairly primitive. It gives you chunks of memory and takes them back when you }{\b delete}{ them. There\rquote s no inherent facility for }{\i heap compaction}{
, which compresses the heap to provide bigger free chunks. If a program allocates and frees heap storage for a while, you can end up with a }{\i fragmented}{ heap that has
 lots of memory free, but without any pieces that are big enough to allocate the size you\rquote re looking for at the moment. A heap compactor complicates a program because it moves memory chunks around, so your pointers won\rquote 
t retain their proper values. Some operating environments have heap compaction built in, but they require you to use special memory }{\i handles}{ (which can be temporarily converted to pointers, after locking the memory so the heap compactor can\rquote 
t move it) instead of pointers. You can also build your own heap-compaction scheme, but this is not a task to be undertaken lightly.
\par When you create a variable on the stack at compile-time, the storage for that variable is automatically created and freed by the compiler. The compiler knows exactly how much s
torage is needed, and it knows the lifetime of the variables because of scoping. With dynamic memory allocation, however, the compiler doesn\rquote t know how much storage you\rquote re going to need, }{\i and}{ it doesn\rquote 
t know the lifetime of that storage. That is, the storage doesn\rquote t get cleaned up automatically. Therefore, you\rquote re responsible for releasing the storage using }{\b delete}{, }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {free(\~)}}}{which tells the heap manager that storage can be used by the next call to }{\b new}{. The logical place for this to happen in the library is in the }{\b cleanup(\~)}{
 function because that is where all the closing-up housekeeping is done.
\par To test the library, two }{\b CStash}{es are created. The first holds }{\b int}{s and the second holds arrays of 80 }{\b char}{s:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C04:CLibTest.cpp
\par //\{L\} CLib
\par // Test the C-like library
\par #include "CLib.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <cassert>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   // Define variables at the beginning
\par   // of the block, as in C:
\par   CStash intStash, stringStash;
\par   int i;
\par   char* cp;
\par   ifstream in;
\par   string line;
\par   const int bufsize = 80;
\par   // Now remember to initialize the variables:
\par   initialize(&intStash, sizeof(int));
\par   for(i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     add(&intStash, &i);
\par   for(i = 0; i < count(&intStash); i++)
\par     cout << "fetch(&intStash, " << i << ") = "
\par          << *(int*)fetch(&intStash, i)
\par          << endl;
\par   // Holds 80-character strings:
\par   initialize(&stringStash, sizeof(char)*bufsize);
\par   in.open("CLibTest.cpp");
\par   assert(in);
\par   while(getline(in, line))
\par     add(&stringStash, line.c_str());
\par   while((cp = (char*)fetch(&stringStash,i++))!=0)
\par     cout << "fetch(&stringStash, " << i << ") = "
\par          << cp << endl;
\par   cleanup(&intStash);
\par   cleanup(&stringStash);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Following the form required by C, all the variables are created at the beginning of the scope of }{\b main(\~)}{
. Of course, you must remember to initialize the }{\b CStash}{ variables later in the block, by calling }{\b initialize(\~)}{. One of the problems with C libraries is that you must carefully convey to the user the importance of the initialization }
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {initialization}}}{and cleanup }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cleanup}}}{
functions. If these functions aren\rquote t called, there will be a lot of trouble. Unfortunately, the user doesn\rquote t always wonder if initialization and cleanup are mandatory. They know what }{\i they}{ want to accomplish, and they\rquote 
re not as concerned about you jumping up and down saying, \ldblquote Hey, wait, you have to do }{\i this}{ first!\rdblquote  Some users have even been known to initialize the elements of a structure themselves. There\rquote 
s certainly no mechanism in C to prevent it (more foreshadowing).
\par The }{\b intStash}{ is filled up with integers, and the }{\b stringStash}{ is filled with character arrays. These character arrays are produced by opening the source code file, }{\b CLibTest.cpp}{, and reading the lines from it into a }{\b string }{
called }{\b line}{, and then producing a pointer to the character representation of }{\b line}{ using the member function }{\b c_str(\~)}{.}{\b  
\par }{After each }{\b Stash}{ is loaded, it is displayed. The }{\b intStash}{ is printed using a }{\b for}{ loop, which uses }{\b count(\~)}{ to establish its limit. The }{\b stringStash}{ is printed with a }{\b while}{, which breaks out when }{\b fetch(\~)}{
 returns zero to indicate it is out of bounds.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776065}Bad guesses{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776065}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There is one more important issue you should understand before we look at the general problems in creating a C library. Note that the }{\b CLib.h}{
 header file }{\i must}{ be included in any file that refers to }{\b CStash}{ because the compiler can\rquote t even guess at what that structure looks like. However, it }{\i can}{ guess at what a function looks like \endash 
 this sounds like a feature but it turns out to be a major C pitfall.
\par Although you should always declare functions by including a header file, function declarations aren\rquote t essential in C. It\rquote s possible in C (but }{\i not }{in C++) to call a function that you haven\rquote 
t declared. A good compiler will warn you that you probably ought to declare a function first, but it isn\rquote t enforced by the C language standard. This is a dangerous practice, because the C compiler can assume that a function that you call with an }
{\b int}{ argument has an argument list containing }{\b int}{, even if it may actually contain a }{\b float}{.}{\b  }{This can produce bugs that are very difficult find, as you will see.
\par Each separate C file is a }{\i translation unit}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {translation unit}}}{
. That is, the compiler is run separately on each translation unit, and when it is running it is aware of only that unit. Thus, any information you provide by including header files is quite important because it provides the compiler\rquote 
s understanding of the rest of your program. Declarations in header files are particularly important, because everywhere the header is included, the compiler will know exactly what to do. If, for example, you have a declaration in a header file that says 
}{\b void func(float)}{, the compiler knows that if you call that function with an integer argument, it should convert}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {promotion}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {automatic type conversion}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type\: conversion}}}{ the }{
\b int}{ to a }{\b float}{ as it passes the argument (this is called }{\i promotion}{). Without the declaration, the C compiler would simply assume that a function }{\b func(int)}{ existed, it wouldn\rquote t do the promotion, and the wrong data would qui
etly be passed into }{\b func(\~)}{.
\par For each translation unit, the compiler creates an object file, with an extension of }{\b o}{ or }{\b obj}{ or something similar. These object files, along with the necessary start-up code, must be collected by the linker }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linker}}}{into the executable program. During linking, all the external references must be resolved. For example, in }{\b CLibTest.cpp}{, functions like }{\b 
initialize(\~)}{ and }{\b fetch(\~)}{ are declared (that is, the compiler is told what they look like) and used, but not defined. They are defined elsewhere, in }{\b CLib.cpp}{. Thus, the calls in }{\b CLIb.cpp}{
 are external references. The linker must, when it puts all the object files together, take the unresolved external references }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {external\: references}
}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {reference\: external}}}{and find the ad
dresses they actually refer to. Those addresses are put into the executable program to replace the external references.
\par It\rquote s important to realize that in C, the external references that the linker searches for are simply function names, generally with an u
nderscore in front of them. So all the linker has to do is match up the function name where it is called and the function body in the object file, and it\rquote s done. If you accidentally made a call that the compiler interpreted as }{\b func(int)}{
 and there\rquote s a function body for }{\b func(float)}{ in some other object file, the linker will see }{\b _func}{ in one place and \-}{\b _func}{ in another, and it will think everything\rquote s OK. The }{\b func(\~)}{
 at the calling location will push an }{\b int}{ onto the stack, and the }{\b func(\~)}{ function body will expect a }{\b float}{ to be on the stack. If the function only reads the value and doesn\rquote t write to it, it won\rquote 
t blow up the stack. In fact, the }{\b float}{ value it reads off the stack might even make some kind of sense. That\rquote s worse because it\rquote s harder to find the bug.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373825}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776066}What's wrong?
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373825}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776066}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {We are remarkably adaptable, even in situations where perhaps we }{\i shouldn\rquote t}{ adapt. The style of the }{\b CStash}{
 library has been a staple for C programmers, but if you look at it for a while, you might notice that it\rquote s rather . . . awkward. When yo
u use it, you have to pass the address of the structure to every single function in the library. When reading the code, the mechanism of the library gets mixed with the meaning of the function calls, which is confusing when you\rquote 
re trying to understand what\rquote s going on.
\par One of the biggest obstacles, however, to using libraries in C is the problem of }{\i name clashes}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {name\: clashes}}}{.}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {clashes, name}}}{ C has a single name space for functions; that is, when the linker looks for a function name, it looks in a s
ingle master list. In addition, when the compiler is working on a translation unit, it can only work with a single function with a given name.
\par Now suppose you decide to buy two libraries from two different vendors, and each library has a structure that must be initialized and cleaned up. Both vendors decided that }{\b initialize(\~)}{ and }{\b cleanup(\~)}{
 are good names. If you include both their header files in a single translation unit, what does the C compiler do? Fortunately, C gives you an error, telling you there\rquote s
 a type mismatch in the two different argument lists of the declared functions. But even if you don\rquote t include them in the same translation unit, the linker will still have problems. A good linker will detect that there\rquote 
s a name clash, but some linkers take
 the first function name they find, by searching through the list of object files in the order you give them in the link list. (Indeed, this can be thought of as a feature because it allows you to replace a library function with your own version.)
\par In either event, you can\rquote t use two C libraries that contain a function with the identical name. To solve this problem, C library vendors will often prepend a sequence of unique characters to the beginning of all their function names. So }{\b 
initialize(\~)}{ and }{\b cleanup(\~)}{ might become }{\b CStash_initialize(\~)}{ and }{\b CStash_cleanup(\~)}{. This is a logical thing to do because it \ldblquote decorates\rdblquote  the name of the }{\b struct}{
 the function works on with the name of the function.}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {mangling, name}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {name\: mangling}}}{
\par Now it\rquote s time to take the first step toward creating classes in C++. Variable names inside a }{\b struct}{ do not clash with global variable names. So why not take advantage of this for function names, when those functions operate on a particular }
{\b struct}{? That is, why not make functions members of }{\b struct}{s?
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373826}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776067}The basic object
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373826}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776067}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Step one is exactly that. C++ functions can be placed inside }{\b struct}{s as \ldblquote member functions.\rdblquote  Here\rquote 
s what it looks like after converting the C version of }{\b CStash}{ to the C++ }{\b Stash}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C04:CppLib.h
\par // C-like library converted to C++
\par 
\par struct Stash \{
\par   int size;      // Size of each space
\par   int quantity;  // Number of storage spaces
\par   int next;      // Next empty space
\par    // Dynamically allocated array of bytes:
\par   unsigned char* storage;
\par   // Functions!
\par   void initialize(int size);
\par   void cleanup();
\par   int add(const void* element);
\par   void* fetch(int index);
\par   int count();
\par   void inflate(int increase);
