. Instead of requiring you to create a }{\b typedef}{, the C++ compiler turns the name of the structure into a new type name for the program (just as }{\b int}{, }{\b char}{, }{\b float}{ and }{\b double}{ are type names).
\par All the data members are exactly the same as before, but now the functions are inside the body of the }{\b struct}{. In addition, notice that the fir
st argument from the C version of the library has been removed. In C++, instead of forcing you to pass the address of the structure as the first argument to all the functions that operate on that structure, the compiler secretly does this for you. Now the
 only arguments for the functions are concerned with what the function }{\i does}{, not the mechanism of the function\rquote s operation.
\par It\rquote s important to realize that the function code is effectively the same as it was with the C version of the library. The number of arguments are the same (even though you don\rquote t see the structure address being passed in, it\rquote 
s still there), and there\rquote s only one function body for each function. That is, just because you say
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Stash A, B, C;
