\par So the code that\rquote s generated is almost identical to what you would have written for the C version of the library. Interestingly enough, this includes the \ldblquote name decoration\rdblquote  you probably would have done to produce }{\b 
Stash_initialize(\~)}{, }{\b Stash_cleanup(\~)}{, and so on. When the function name is inside the }{\b struct}{, the compiler effectively does the same thing. Therefore, }{\b initialize(\~)}{ inside the structure }{\b Stash}{
 will not collide with a function named }{\b initialize(\~)}{ inside any other structure, or even a global function named }{\b initialize(\~)}{. Most of the time you don\rquote t have to worry about the function name decoration \endash 
 you use the undecorated name. But sometimes you do need to be able to specify that this }{\b initialize(\~)}{ belongs to the }{\b struct}{ }{\b Stash}{, and not to any other }{\b struct}{. In particular, when you\rquote 
re defining the function you need to fully specify which one it is. To accomplish this full specification, C++ has an operator (}{\b ::}{) called the }{\i scope resolution operator}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {scope\: resolution operator}}}{ (named so because names can now be in different scopes: at global scope, or within the scope of a }{\b struct}{). For example, if you want to specify }{\b initialize(\~) }{which belongs to }
{\b Stash}{, you say }{\b Stash::initialize(int size)}{. You can see how the scope resolution operator is used in the function definitions:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C04:CppLib.cpp \{O\}
\par // C library converted to C++
\par // Declare structure and functions:
\par #include "CppLib.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <cassert>
\par using namespace std;
\par // Quantity of elements to add
\par // when increasing storage:
\par const int increment = 100;
\par 
\par void Stash::initialize(int sz) \{
\par   size = sz;
\par   quantity = 0;
\par   storage = 0;
\par   next = 0;
\par \}
\par 
\par int Stash::add(const void* element) \{
\par   if(next >= quantity) // Enough space left?
\par     inflate(increment);
\par   // Copy element into storage,
\par   // starting at next empty space:
\par   int startBytes = next * size;
\par   unsigned char* e = (unsigned char*)element;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < size; i++)
\par     storage[startBytes + i] = e[i];
\par   next++;
\par   return(next - 1); // Index number
\par \}
\par 
\par void* Stash::fetch(int index) \{
\par   // Check index boundaries:
\par   assert(0 <= index && index < next);
\par   // Produce pointer to desired element:
\par   return &(storage[index * size]);
\par \}
\par 
\par int Stash::count() \{
\par   return next; // Number of elements in CStash
\par \}
\par 
\par void Stash::inflate(int increase) \{
\par   assert(increase > 0);
\par   int newQuantity = quantity + increase;
\par   int newBytes = newQuantity * size;
\par   int oldBytes = quantity * size;
\par   unsigned char* b = new unsigned char[newBytes];
\par   for(int i = 0; i < oldBytes; i++)
\par     b[i] = storage[i]; // Copy old to new
\par   delete []storage; // Old storage
\par   storage = b; // Point to new memory
\par   quantity = newQuantity;
\par \}
\par 
\par void Stash::cleanup() \{
\par   if(storage != 0) \{
\par    cout << "freeing storage" << endl;
\par    delete []storage;
\par   \}
