\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There are several other things that are different between C and C++. First, the declarations in the header files}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {header\: files}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {file\: header}}}{ are }{\i required}{
 by the compiler. In C++ you cannot call a function without declaring it first. The compiler will issue an
 error message otherwise. This is an important way to ensure that function calls are consistent between the point where they are called and the point where they are defined. By forcing you to declare}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {declaration}}}{ the function before you call it, the C
++ compiler virtually ensures you will perform this declaration by including the header file. If you also include the same header file in the place where the functions are defined, then the compiler checks to make sure that the declaration in the header a
nd the function definition match up. This means that the header file becomes a validated repository for function declarations and ensures that functions are used consistently throughout all translation units in the project.
\par Of course, global functions }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {global\: functions}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {function\: global}}}{can still be declared by hand every place where they are defined and used. (This is so tedious that it becomes very unlikely.) However, structures must always be declared before they are defined or used, and the mo
st convenient place to put a structure definition is in a header file, except for those you intentionally hide in a file.
\par You can see that all the member functions look almost the same as when they were C functions, except for the scope resolution and the fact that the first argument from the C version of the library is no longer explicit. It\rquote 
s still there, of course, because the function has to be able to work on a particular }{\b struct}{ variable. But notice, inside the member function, that the member selection is also gone! Thus, instead of saying }{\b s\endash >size = sz;}{ you say }{\b 
size = sz;}{ and eliminate the tedious }{\b s\endash >}{, which didn\rquote t really add anything to the meaning of what you were doing anyway. The C++ compiler is apparently doing this for you. Indeed, it is taking the \ldblquote secret\rdblquote 
 first argument (the address of the structure that we were previously passing in by hand) and applying the member selector whenever you refer to one of the data members of a }{\b struct}{. }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {member\: member function selection}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {selection, member function}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: member selection}}}{This means that whenever you are inside the member function of another }{\b struct}{
, you can refer to any member (including another member function) by simply giving its name. The compiler will search through the local structure\rquote s names before looking for a global version of that name. You\rquote 
ll find that this feature means that not only is your code easier to write, it\rquote s a lot easier to read.
\par But what if, for some reason, you }{\i want}{ to be able to get your hands on the address of the structure? In the C version of the library it was easy because each function\rquote s first argument was a }{\b CStash*}{ called }{\b s}{
. In C++, things are even more consistent. There\rquote s a special keyword, called }{\b this}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {this}}}{, which produces the address of the }{\b struct}
{. It\rquote s the equivalent of the \lquote }{\b s}{\rquote  in the C version of the library. So we can revert to the C style of things by saying
