ll see code where people explicitly use }{\b this->}{ everywhere but it doesn\rquote t add anything to the meaning of the code and often indicates an inexperienced programmer. Usually, you don\rquote t use }{\b this}{ very often, but when you need it, it
\rquote s there (some of the examples later in the book will use }{\b this}{).
\par There\rquote s one last item to mention. In C, you could assign a }{\b void*}{ to any other pointer like this:
