\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cast\: casting void pointers}}}{
and there was no complaint from the compiler. But in C++, this statement is not allowed. Why? Because C is not so particular about type information, so it allows you to assign a pointer with an unspecified type to
 a pointer with a specified type. Not so with C++. Type is critical in C++, and the compiler stamps its foot when there are any violations of type information. This has always been important, but it is especially important in C++ because you have member f
unctions in }{\b struct}{s. If you could pass pointers to }{\b struct}{s around with impunity in C++, then you could end up calling a member function for a }{\b struct}{ that doesn\rquote t even logically exist for that }{\b struct}{
! A real recipe for disaster. Therefore, while C++ allows the assignment of any type of pointer to a }{\b void*}{ (this was the original intent of }{\b void*}{, which is required to be large enough to hold a pointer to any type), it will }{\i not}{
