\par This brings up an interesting issue. One of the important goals for C++ is to compile as much existing C code as possible to allow for an easy transition to the new language. However, this doesn\rquote 
t mean any code that C allows will automatically be allowed in C++. }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {compatibility, C & C++}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C\: and C++ compatibility}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C++\: and C compatibility}}}{
There are a number of things the C compiler lets you get away with that are dangerous and error-prone. (We\rquote 
ll look at them as the book progresses.) The C++ compiler generates warnings and errors for these situations. This is often much more of an advantage than a hindrance. In fact, there are many situations w
here you are trying to run down an error in C and just can\rquote t find it, but as soon as you recompile the program in C++, the compiler points out the problem! In C, you\rquote 
ll often find that you can get the program to compile, but then you have to get it to work. In C++, when the program compiles correctly, it often works, too! This is because the language is a lot stricter about type.}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type\: improved type checking}}}{
\par You can see a number of new things in the way the C++ version of }{\b Stash}{ is used, in the following test program:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C04:CppLibTest.cpp
\par //\{L\} CppLib
\par // Test of C++ library
\par #include "CppLib.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Stash intStash;
\par   intStash.initialize(sizeof(int));
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     intStash.add(&i);
\par   for(int i = 0; i < intStash.count(); i++)
\par     cout << "intStash.fetch(" << i << ") = "
\par          << *(int*)intStash.fetch(i)
\par          << endl;
\par   // Holds 80-character strings:
\par   Stash stringStash;
\par   const int bufsize = 80;
\par   stringStash.initialize(sizeof(char) * bufsize);
\par   ifstream in("CppLibTest.cpp");
\par   assure(in, "CppLibTest.cpp");
\par   string line;
\par   while(getline(in, line))
\par     stringStash.add(line.c_str());
\par   int i = 0;
\par   char* cp;
\par   while((cp =(char*)stringStash.fetch(i++)) != 0)
\par     cout << "stringStash.fetch(" << i << ") = "
\par          << cp << endl;
\par   intStash.cleanup();
\par   stringStash.cleanup();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {One thing you\rquote ll notice is that the variables are all defined \ldblquote on the fly\rdblquote  (as introduced in the previous 
chapter). That is, they are defined at any point in the scope, rather than being restricted \endash  as in C \endash  to the beginning of the scope.
\par The code is quite similar to }{\b CLibTest.cpp}{, but when a member function is called, the call occurs using the member selection operator }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {member\:
 member selection operator}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\:member selection}}}{ \lquote }{\b .}{\rquote 
 preceded by the name of the variable. This is a convenient syntax because it mimics the selection of a data member of the structure. The difference is that this is a function member, so it has an argument list.
\par Of course, the call that the compiler }{\i actually}{ generates looks much more like the original C library function. Thus, considering name decoration}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {name\: mangling}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {decoration, name}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
mangling, name}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {name\: decoration}}}{ and the passing of }{\b this}{, the C++ function call }{\b intStash.initialize(sizeof(int), 100)}{
 becomes something like }{\b Stash_initialize(&intStash, sizeof(int), 100)}{. If you ever wonder what\rquote s going on underneath the covers, remember that the original C++ compiler }{\b cfront}{
 from AT&T produced C code as its output, which was then compiled by the underlying C compiler. This approach meant that }{\b cfront}{ could be quickly ported to any machine that had a C compiler, and it helped to rapidly disseminate C++ c
ompiler technology. But because the C++ compiler had to generate C, you know that there must be some way to represent C++ syntax in C.
\par You\rquote ll also notice an additional cast in 
