\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now that you\rquote ve seen an initial example, it\rquote s time to ste
p back and take a look at some terminology. The act of bringing functions inside structures is the root of what C++ adds to C, and it introduces a new way of thinking about structures: as concepts. In C, a structure }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {structure\: C}}}{is an agglomeration of data, a way to package data so you can treat it in a clump. But it\rquote 
s hard to think about it as anything but a programming convenience. The functions that operate on those structures are elsewhere. However, with functions in the package, the structure bec
omes a new creature, capable of describing both characteristics (like a C }{\b struct}{ does) }{\i and}{ behaviors. The concept of an object, a free-standing, bounded entity that can remember }{\i and}{ act, suggests itself.
\par In C++, an object is just a variable, and the purest definition is \ldblquote a region of storage\rdblquote  (this is a more specific way of saying \ldblquote an object must have a unique identifier,\rdblquote 
 which in the case of C++ is a unique memory address). It\rquote s a place where you can store data, and it\rquote s implied that there are also operations that can be performed on this data.
\par Unfortunately there\rquote s not complete consistency across languages when it comes to these terms, although they are fairly well-accepted. You will also sometimes encounter disagreement about what an object-oriented langu
age is, although that seems to be reasonably well sorted out by now. There are languages that are }{\i object-based}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: object-based}}}{
, which means they have objects like the C++ structures-with-functions that you\rquote ve seen so far. This, however, is only 
part of the picture when it comes to an object-oriented language, and languages that stop at packaging functions inside data structures are object-based, not object-oriented.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373828}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776069}Abstract data typing
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373828}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776069}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The ability to package data with functions allows you to create a new data type. This is often called }{\i encapsulation}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote 
\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ This term can cause debate. Some people use it as defined here; others use it to describe }{\i 
implementation hiding}{, discussed in the following chapter.}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {encapsulation}}}{.}{\i  }{
An existing data type may have several pieces of data packaged together. For example a }{\b float}{ has an exponent, a mantissa, and a sign bit. You can tell it to do things: add to another }{\b float}{ or to an }{\b int}{
, and so on. It has characteristics and behavior.
\par The definition of }{\b Stash}{ creates a new data type. You can }{\b add(\~)}{ and }{\b fetch(\~)}{ and }{\b inflate(\~)}{. You create one by saying }{\b Stash s}{, just as you create a }{\b float}{ by saying }{\b float f}{. A }{\b Stash}{ al
so has characteristics and behavior. Even though it acts like a real, built-in data type, we refer to it as an }{\i abstract data type}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {abstract\:
 data type}}}{, perhaps because it allows us to abstract a concept from the problem space into the solution space. In addition, the C++ compiler treats it like a new data type, and if you say a function expects a }{\b Stash}{
, the compiler makes sure you pass a }{\b Stash}{ to that function. So the same level of type checking happens with abstract data types (sometimes called }{\i user-defined types}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {user-defined types}}}{) as with built-in types.
\par You can immediately see a difference, however, in the way you perform operations on objects. You say }{\b object.memberFunction(arglist)}{. This is \ldblquote calling a member function }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {calling a member function}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {member\: calling a member function}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: calling a member}}}{for an object.\rdblquote  But in object-oriented parlance, this is also referred to as \ldblquote sending a message }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {message, sending}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {sending a message}}}{to an object.
\rdblquote  So for a }{\b Stash s}{, the statement }{\b s.add(&i)}{ \ldblquote sends a message to }{\b s}{\rdblquote  saying \ldblquote }{\b add(\~)}{ this to yourself.\rdblquote  }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {OOP\: summarized}}}{In fact, object-oriented programming can be summed up in a single phrase: }{\i sending messages to objects}{. Really, that\rquote s all you do \endash  create a bunch of obje
cts and send messages to them. The trick, of course, is figuring out what your objects and messages }{\i are}{, but once you accomplish this the implementation in C++ is surprisingly straightforward.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373829}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776070}Object details
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373829}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776070}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A question that comes up a lot in seminars is \ldblquote How big is an object, and what does it look like?\rdblquote  The answer is \ldblquote 
about what you expect from a C }{\b struct}{.\rdblquote  In fact, the code the C compiler produces for a C }{\b struct}{ (with no C++ adornments) will usually look }{\i exactly}{ the same as the code produced by a C++ comp
iler. This is reassuring to those C programmers who depend on the details of size and layout in their code, and for some reason directly access structure}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {directly accessing structure}}}{ bytes instead of using identifiers (relying on a particular size and layout for a structure is a nonportable activity).
\par The size of a }{\b struct}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {size\: of a struct}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {struct\: size of}}}{ is the combined size of all its members. Sometimes when the compiler lays out a }{\b struct}{, it adds extra bytes to make the boundaries come out neatly \endash 
 this may increase execution efficiency. In Chapters XX and XX, you\rquote ll see how in some cases \ldblquote secret\rdblquote  pointers are added to the structure, but you don\rquote t need to worry about that right now.
\par You can determine the size of a }{\b struct}{ using the }{\b sizeof}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {size\: sizeof}}}{operator. Here\rquote s a small example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C04:Sizeof.cpp
\par // Sizes of structs
\par #include "CLib.h"
\par #include "CppLib.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par struct A \{
\par   int i[100];
\par \};
\par 
\par struct B \{
\par   void f();
\par \};
\par 
\par void B::f() \{\}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   cout << "sizeof struct A = " << sizeof(A)
\par     << " bytes" << endl;
\par   cout << "sizeof struct B = " << sizeof(B)
\par     << " bytes" << endl;
\par   cout << "sizeof CStash in C = " 
\par     << sizeof(CStash) << " bytes" << endl;
\par   cout << "sizeof Stash in C++ = " 
\par     << sizeof(Stash) << " bytes" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {On my machine (your results may vary) the first print statement produces 200 because each }{\b int}{ occupies two bytes. }{\b struct B}{
 is something of an anomaly because it is a }{\b struct}{ with no data members. In C, this is illegal, but in C++ we need the option of creating a }{\b struct}{
 whose sole task is to scope function names, so it is allowed. Still, the result produced by the second print statement is a somewhat surprising nonzero value}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {struct\: minimum size}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {minimum size of a struct}}}{
. In early versions of the language, the size was zero, but an awkward situation arises when you create such objects: They have the same address as the object created directly after them, and so are not distinct. One of the fundamental
 rules of objects is that each object must have a unique address, so structures with no data members will always have some minimum nonzero size.
\par The last two }{\b sizeof}{ statements show you that the size of the structure in C++ is the same as the size of the equivalent version in C. C++ tries not to add any unnecessary overhead.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373830}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776071}Header file etiquette
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373830}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776071}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you create a }{\b struct}{
 containing member functions, you are creating a new data type. Generally, you want this type to be easily accessible to yourself and others. In addition, you want to separate the interface}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {interface}}}{ (the declaration) from the implementation}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {implementation}}}{
 (the definition of the member functions) so the implementation can be changed without forcing a re-compile of the entire system. You achieve this end by putting the declaration for your new type in a header file.
\par When I first learned to program in C, the header file }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {header\: files}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {file\: header}}}{was a mystery to me. Many C books don\rquote t seem to emphasize it, and the compiler didn\rquote 
t enforce function declarations, so it seemed optional most of the time, except when structures were declared. In C++ the use of header files becomes crystal clear. They are almost mandatory for easy program development, and you put very specific in
formation in them: declarations. The header file tells the compiler what is available in your library. Because you can use the library without the source code for the }{\b cpp}{
 file (you only need the object file or library file), the header file is where the interface specification is stored.
\par Although it is not enforced by the compiler, the best approach to building large projects in C is to use libraries: collect associated functions into the same object module or library, and use a header file to hold all the 
declarations for the functions. It is }{\i de rigueur}{ in C++: you could throw any function into a C library, but the C++ abstract data type determines the functions that are associated by dint of their common access to the data in a }{\b struct}{
. Any member function must be declared in the }{\b struct }{declaration; you cannot put it elsewhere. The use of function libraries was encouraged in C and institutionalized in C++.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776072}Importance of header file}{\pard\plain 
