\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The ellipses specify a }{\i variable argument list}{\cs23\i\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ To write a function definition for a function that takes a true variable argument list, you must use }{\i varargs}{
. This is avoided in C++; you can find details about the use of varargs in your C manual.}}}{, which says: }{\b printf(\~)}{ has some arguments, each of whi
ch has a type, but ignore that. Just take whatever arguments you see and accept them. By using this kind of declaration, you suspend all error checking on the arguments.
\par This practice can cause subtle problems. If you declare functions by hand, in one file
 you may make a mistake. Since the compiler only sees your hand-declaration in that file, it may be able to adapt to your mistake. The program will then link correctly, but the use of the function in that one file will be faulty. This is a tough error to 
find, and is easily avoided by using a header file.
\par If you place all your function declarations in a header file, and include that header everywhere you use the function and where you define the function, you ensure a consistent declaration across the whole
 system. You also ensure that the declaration and the definition match by including the header in the definition file.
\par If a }{\b struct}{ is declared in a header file in C++, you }{\i must}{ include the header file everywhere a }{\b struct}{ is used and where }{\b struct}{ member fun
ctions are defined. The C++ compiler will give an error message if you try to call a regular function, or call or define a member function, without declaring it first. By enforcing the proper use of header files, the language ensures consistency in librar
ies, and reduces bugs by forcing the same interface to be used everywhere.
\par The header is a contract between you and the user of your library. The contract describes your data structures, and states the arguments and return values for the function calls. It says, \ldblquote Here\rquote s what my library does.\rdblquote 
 The user needs some of this information to develop the application and the compiler needs all of it to generate proper code. The user of the }{\b struct}{ simply includes the header file, creates objects (instances) of that }{\b struct}{
, and links in the object module or library (i.e.: the compiled code).
\par The compiler enforces the contract by requiring you to declare all structures and functions before they are used and, in the case of member functions, before they are defined. Thus, you\rquote 
re forced to put the declarations in the header and to include the header in the file where the member functions are defined and the file(s) where they are used. Because a single header file describing your library is included throughout the system, t
he compiler can ensure consistency and prevent errors.
\par There are certain issues that you must be aware of in order to organize your code properly}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {code organization\: header files}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {organization\: code in header files}}}{ and write effective header files. The first issue concerns what you can put into header files. The basic rule is 
\ldblquote only declarations}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {declaration}}}{,\rdblquote 
 that is, only information to the compiler but nothing that allocates storage by generating code or creating variables. This is because 
the header file will typically be included in several translation units in a project, and if storage for one identifier is allocated in more than one place, the linker will come up with a multiple definition error (this is C++\rquote s }{\i 
one definition rule}{: you can declare things as many times as you want, but there can be only one actual definition for each thing).
\par This rule isn\rquote t completely hard and fast. If you define a variable that is \ldblquote file static\rdblquote }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {file\: static}}}{
 (has visibility only within a file) inside a header file, there will be multiple instances of that data across the project, but the linker won\rquote t have a collision}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ However, in Standard C++ file static is a deprecated feature.}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {collision, linker}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linker\: collision}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ambiguity}}}{. Basically, you don\rquote t want to do anything in the header file that will cause an ambiguity at link time.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776073}The multiple\_declaration }{\pard\plain 
\s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {re-declaration of classes, preventing}}}{problem{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776073}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The second header-file issue is this: }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
multiple inclusion of header files}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {header\: file, multiple inclusion}}}{when you put a }{\b struct}{ declara
tion in a header file, it is possible for the file to be included more than once in a complicated program. Iostreams are a good example. Any time a }{\b struct}{ does I/O it may include one of the iostream headers. If the }{\b cpp }{
file you are working on uses more than one kind of }{\b struct}{ (typically including a header file for each one), you run the risk of including the istreams header more than once and re-declaring streams.
\par The compiler considers the redeclaration of a structure (this includes both }{\b struct}{s and }{\b class}{es)}{\b  }{
to be an error, since it would otherwise allow you to use the same name for different types. To prevent this error when multiple header files are included, you need to build some intelligence into your header files using the preprocessor (Standard C++ h
eader files like }{\b <iostream>}{ already have this \ldblquote intelligence\rdblquote ).
\par Both C and C++ allow you to redeclare a function, as long as the two declarations match, but neither will allow the redeclaration of a structure}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
function\: declaring}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {structure\: declaring}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {structure\: redeclaring}}}{. In C++ this rule is especially important because if the compiler allowed you to redeclare a structure and the two declarations differed, which one would it use?
\par The problem of redeclaration comes up quite a bit in C++ bec
ause each data type (structure with functions) generally has its own header file, and you have to include one header in another if you want to create another data type that uses the first one. In any }{\b cpp }{file in your project, it\rquote 
s very likely that you\rquote ll i
nclude several files that include the same header file. During a single compilation, the compiler can see the same header file several times. Unless you do something about it, the compiler will see the redeclaration of your structure and report a compile-
time error. To solve the problem, you need to know a bit more about the preprocessor.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776074}The preprocessor directives \line }{\b #define}{, }{\b #ifdef}{
