\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {#ifndef}}}{, which will yield a true }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {true}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {false}}}{if the label has not been defined (this is the one we will use in header files).
\par There are other useful features in the C preprocessor. You should check your local documentation for the full set. 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776075}A standard for header file}{\pard\plain 
\s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {standard for each class header file}}}{\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \v\f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {header\: formatting standard}}}{s{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776075}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In each header file that contains a structure, you should first check to see if this header has already been included in this particular }{\b cpp}{
 file. You do this by testing a preprocessor flag. If the flag isn\rquote t set, the file wasn\rquote t included and you should set the flag (so the structure can\rquote 
t get re-declared) and declare the structure. If the flag was set then that type has already been declared so you should just ignore the code that declares it. Here\rquote s how the header file should look:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#ifndef HEADER_FLAG
\par #define HEADER_FLAG
\par // Type declaration here...
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // HEADER_FLAG
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As you can see, the first time the header file is included, the con
tents of the header file (including your type declaration) will be included by the preprocessor. All the subsequent times it is included \endash  in a single compilation unit \endash 
 the type declaration will be ignored. The name HEADER_FLAG can be any unique name, but
 a reliable standard to follow is to capitalize the name of the header file and replace periods with underscores (leading underscores are reserved for system names). Here\rquote s an example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C04:Simple.h
\par // Simple header that prevents re-definition
\par #ifndef SIMPLE_H
\par #define SIMPLE_H
\par 
\par struct Simple \{
\par   int i,j,k;
\par   initialize() \{ i = j = k = 0; \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // SIMPLE_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Although the }{\b SIMPLE_H}{ after the }{\b #endif}{ is commented out and thus ignored by the preprocessor, it is useful for documentation.
\par These preprocessor statements that prevent multiple inclusion are often referred to as }{\i include guards}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373831}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776076}Namespaces in headers
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776076}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You\rquote ll notice that }{\i using directives}{ are present in nearly all the }{\b cpp}{ files in this book, usually in the form:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {using namespace std;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Since }{\b std }{
is the namespace that surrounds the entire Standard C++ library, this particular using directive allows the names in the Standard C++ library to be used without qualification. However, you\rquote 
ll virtually never see a using directive in a header file (at least
, not outside of a scope). The reason is that the using directive eliminates the protection of that particular namespace, and the effect lasts until the end of the current compilation unit. If you put a using directive (outside of a scope) in a header fil
e, it means that this loss of  \ldblquote namespace protection\rdblquote  will occur with any file that includes this header, which often means other header files. Thus, if you start putting using directives in header files, it\rquote 
s very easy to end up \ldblquote turning off\rdblquote  namespaces practically everywhere, and thereby neutralizing the beneficial effects of namespaces. 
\par In short: don\rquote t do it.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776077}Using headers in projects{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373831}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776077}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {code organization}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {organization\: code}}}{When building a project in C++, you\rquote ll usually create it by bringing tog
ether a lot of different types (data structures with associated functions). You\rquote ll usually put the declaration for each type or group of associated types in a separate header file}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {header\: files}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {file\: header}}}{, then define the functions for that t
ype in a translation unit. When you use that type, you must include the header file to perform the declarations properly.
\par Sometimes that pattern will be followed in this book, but more often the examples will be very small, so everything \endash  the structure declarations, function definitions, and the }{\b main(\~)}{ function \endash 
 may appear in a single file. However, keep in mind that you\rquote ll want to use separate files and header files in practice.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373832}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776078}Nested structures
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373832}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776078}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {nested\: structures}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {structure\: nested}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {stack}}}{
The convenience of taking data and function names out of the global name space extends to structures. You can nest a structure within another structure, and therefore keep associated elements together. The declaration syntax is what you would expect, a
s you can see in the following structure, which implements a push-down stack as a very simple linked list }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linked list}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {list\: linked}}}{so it \ldblquote never\rdblquote  runs out of memory:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C04:Stack.h
\par // Nested struct in linked list
\par #ifndef STACK_H
\par #define STACK_H
\par 
\par struct Stack \{
\par   struct Link \{
\par     void* data;
\par     Link* next;
\par     void initialize(void* dat, Link* nxt);
\par   \}* head;
\par   void initialize();
\par   void push(void* dat);
\par   void* peek();
\par   void* pop();
\par   void cleanup();
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // STACK_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The nested }{\b struct}{ is called }{\b Link}{, and it contains a pointer to the next }{\b Link}{ in the list and a pointer to the data stored in the }{\b 
Link}{. If the }{\b next}{ pointer is zero, it means you\rquote re at the end of the list.
\par Notice that the }{\b head}{ pointer is defined right after the declaration for }{\b struct Link}{, instead of a separate definition }{\b Link* head}{
. This is a syntax that came from C, but it emphasizes the importance of the semicolon after the structure declaration \endash  the semicolon indicates the end of the list of definitions of that structure type. (Usually the list is empty.)
\par The nested structure has its own }{\b initialize(\~)}{ function, like all the structures presented so far, to ensure proper initialization. }{\b Stack}{ has both an }{\b initialize(\~)}{ and }{\b cleanup(\~)}{ function, as well as }{\b push(\~)}{
, which takes a pointer to the data you wish to store (it assumes this has been allocated on the heap), and }{\b pop(\~)}{, which returns the }{\b data}{ pointer from the top of the }{\b Stack}{ and removes the top element. (If you }{\b pop(\~)}{
 an element, then }{\i you}{ are responsible for destroying the object pointed to by the }{\b data}{.) The }{\b peek(\~)}{ function also returns the }{\b data}{ pointer from the top element, but it leaves the top element on the }{\b Stack}{.
\par }{\b cleanup(\~)}{ goes through the }{\b Stack}{ and removes each element }{\i and}{ frees the }{\b data}{ pointer (thus the }{\b data}{ objects }{\i must}{ be on the heap). Notice there\rquote s something you have to keep track of, a bit: if you }{\b 
pop(\~)}{ the element, you must call }{\b delete}{, but if you don\rquote t, then }{\b cleanup(\~)}{ will call }{\b delete}{. The subject of \ldblquote who\rquote s responsible for the memory\rdblquote  is not even that simple, as we\rquote 
ll see in later chapters.
\par Here are the definitions for the member functions:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C04:Nested.cpp \{O\}
\par // Linked list with nesting
\par #include "Stack.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par void 
\par Stack::Link::initialize(void* dat, Link* nxt) \{
\par   data = dat;
\par   next = nxt;
\par \}
\par 
\par void Stack::initialize() \{ head = 0; \}
\par 
\par void Stack::push(void* dat) \{
\par   Link* newLink = new Link();
\par   newLink->initialize(dat, head);
\par   head = newLink;
\par \}
\par 
\par void* Stack::peek() \{ return head->data; \}
\par 
\par void* Stack::pop() \{
\par   if(head == 0) return 0;
\par   void* result = head->data;
\par   Link* oldHead = head;
\par   head = head->next;
\par   delete oldHead;
\par   return result;
\par \}
\par 
\par void Stack::cleanup() \{
\par   Link* cursor = head;
\par   while(head) \{
\par     cursor = cursor->next;
\par     delete head->data; // Assumes a 'new'!
\par     delete head;
\par     head = cursor;
\par   \}
\par   head = 0; // Officially empty
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The first definition is particularly interesting because it shows you how to define a member of a nested structure. You simply use an additional level of scope resolution, to specify the name of the enclosing }{\b struct}{. }{\b Stack::Link::initialize(\~
)}{ takes the arguments and assigns them to its members.
\par }{\b Stack::initialize(\~)}{ sets }{\b head}{ to zero, so the object knows it has an empty list.
\par }{\b Stack::push(\~)}{ takes the argument, which is a pointer to the variable you want to keep track of, and pushes it on the }{\b Stack}{. First, it uses }{\b new}{ to allocate storage for the }{\b Link}{ it will insert at the top. Then it calls }{\b 
Link}{\rquote s }{\b initialize(\~)}{ function to assign the appropriate values to the members of the }{\b Link}{. Notice that the }{\b next}{ pointer is assigned to the current }{\b head}{; then }{\b head}{ is assigned to the new }{\b Link}{
 pointer. This effectively pushes the }{\b Link}{ in at the top of the list.
\par }{\b Stack::pop(\~)}{ captures the }{\b data}{ pointer at the current top of the }{\b Stack}{; then it moves the }{\b head}{ pointer down and deletes the old top of the }{\b Stack}{, finally returning the captured pointer.
\par }{\b Stack::cleanup(\~)}{ creates a }{\b cursor}{ to move through the }{\b Stack}{ and }{\b delete}{ both the }{\b data}{ in each link and the link itself. After it\rquote s finished destroying all the links, }{\b head}{
 is set to zero. This not only indicates that the }{\b Stack}{ is empty, but if }{\b cleanup(\~)}{ is called a second time it will not wander off and try to }{\b delete}{ inappropriate storage (which would be a run-time error, and might cause diff
icult-to-find bugs).
\par Here\rquote s an example to test the }{\b Stack}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C04:StackTest.cpp
\par //\{L\} Nested
\par //\{T\} NestTest.cpp
\par // Test of nested linked list
\par #include "Stack.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1); // File name is argument
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]);
\par   Stack textlines;
\par   textlines.initialize();
\par   string line;
\par   // Read file and store lines in the Stack:
\par   while(getline(in, line))
\par     textlines.push(new string(line));
\par   // Pop the lines from the Stack and print them:
\par   string* s;
\par   while((s = (string*)textlines.pop()) != 0) \{
\par     cout << *s << endl;
\par     delete s; 
\par   \}
\par   textlines.cleanup();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This is very similar to the earlier example, but it pushes lines from a file (as }{\b string}{ pointers)}{\b  }{on the }{\b Stack}{
 and then pops them off, which results in the file being printed out in reverse order. Note that the }{\b pop(\~) }{member function returns a }{\b void* }{and this must be cast back to a }{\b string* }{before it can be used. To print the }{\b string}{
, the pointer is dereferenced.
\par As }{\b textlines }{is being filled, the contents of }{\b line}{ is \ldblquote cloned\rdblquote  for each }{\b push(\~)}{ by making a }{\b new string(line)}{. The value returned from the new-expression is a pointer to the new }{\b string}{ tha
t was created and that copied the information from }{\b line}{. If you had simply passed the address of }{\b line}{ to }{\b push(\~)}{, you would end up with a }{\b Stack}{ filled with identical addresses, all pointing to }{\b line}{. You\rquote 
ll learn more about this \ldblquote cloning\rdblquote  process later in the book.
\par The file name is taken from the command line}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {command line}}}{
. To guarantee that there are enough arguments on the command line, you see a second function used from the }{\b require.h}{ header file: }{\b requireArgs(\~)}{, which compares }{\b argc}{ to the desired nu
mber of arguments and prints an appropriate error message and exits the program if there aren\rquote t enough arguments.}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {arguments\: command line}}}{

\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373833}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776079}Global scope resolution
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373833}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776079}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {global\: scope resolution}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: global scope resolution}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {scope\: resol
ution, global}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {resolution\: global scope}}}{
The scope resolution operator gets you out of situations where the name the compiler chooses by default (the \ldblquote nearest\rdblquote  name) isn\rquote t what you want. For example, suppose you have a structure with a local identifier }{\b a}{
, and you want to select a global identifier }{\b a}{
 from inside a member function. The compiler would default to choosing the local one, so you must tell it to do otherwise. When you want to specify a global name using scope resolution, you use the operator with nothing in front of it. Here\rquote 
s an example that shows global scope resolution for both a variable and a function:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C04:Scoperes.cpp \{O\}
\par // Global scope resolution
\par int a;
\par void f() \{\}
\par 
\par struct S \{
\par   int a;
\par   void f();
\par \};
\par 
\par void S::f() \{
\par   ::f();  // Would be recursive otherwise!
\par   ::a++;  // Select the global a
\par   a--;    // The a at struct scope
\par \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Without scope resolution in }{\b S::f(\~)}{, the compiler would default to selecting the member versions of }{\b f(\~)}{ and }{\b A}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373834}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776080}Summary
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373834}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776080}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In this chapter, you\rquote ve learned the fundamental \ldblquote twist\rdblquote 
 of C++: that you can place functions inside of structures. This new type of structure is called an }{\i abstract data type}{, and variables you create using this structure are called }{\i objects}{, or }{\i instances}{, of that type. Calling a member fu
nction for an object is called }{\i sending a message}{ to that object. The primary action in object-oriented programming is sending messages to objects.
\par Although packaging data and functions together is a significant benefit for code organization and makes library use easier because it prevents name clashes by hiding the names, there\rquote 
s a lot more you can do to make programming safer in C++. In the next chapter, you\rquote ll learn how to protect some members of a }{\b struct}{ so that only you can manipulate them. This establi
shes a clear boundary between what the user of the structure can change and what only the programmer may change.
