[[[ Readers: Questions have not been distributed through chapter yet. The questions will be repeated here, along with the answers . If you think of questions which might be useful to include, please note them.]]]
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 1. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 1.}{\f26 \tab }{What is object oriented programming, in one sentence?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: Sending messages to objects
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 2. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 2.}{\f26 \tab }{ In C++, how is the C language extended to embody this primary concept of object-oriented programming?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: Functions are added to structs, so structures describe not only an agglomeration of da
ta, but an agglomeration of data and functions. We often say this means that a structure has }{\i characteristics}{ (described by the data) and }{\i behaviors}{ (described by the functions).
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 3. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 3.}{\f26 \tab }{What is the difference between a structure and an object?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: A structure is a description of a type, and an object is a variable of that new type.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 4. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 4.}{\f26 \tab }{Can you compile C code with C++?
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 5. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {\f26\fs24 5.}{\f26 \tab }{Does a structure occupy storage? Do the functions in a structure require storage for each function, in each object?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: No. A member function is like an ordinary function except for its name, so there\rquote 
s only one piece of storage allocated for that function regardless of how many variables you create. The only difference is that the name is scoped within the name of the structure.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 6. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 6.}{\f26 \tab }{What are the two basic differences between an ordinary C function and a member function (inside a structure) in C++?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: The first is that name is scoped within the structure so it\rquote 
s no longer a global name, and the second is that the address of the structure is secretly passed in as the first argument. Other than that, it\rquote s just like an ordinary global function.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 7. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 7.}{\f26 \tab }{In a function definition, How do you tell the compiler \ldblquote this is a member function of a particular class, and not an ordinary function definition.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: With the scope resolution operator }{\b ::}{
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 8. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 8.}{\f26 \tab }{What is a translation unit?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: A single file which generates code. Generally, these have a file name extension of}{\b  cpp}{, to distinguish them from header files (with extensions of }
{\b .h}{). The compiler is invoked once for each CPP file to create an object module (this name is an artifact, it is the objective of the compilation rather than anything object-oriented).
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 9. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 9.}{\f26 \tab }{What does the linker do?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: The primary job of the linker is to resolve references between function calls. These refere
nces often span a group of translation units and may also include various libraries (a file that often has the extension }{\b lib}{), which are collections of }{\b obj}{
 files. The linker takes all these and binds them into an executable which contains the necessary code to resolve the function calls.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 10. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\fs24 10.}{\tab How does the linker tell the difference between an ordinary C function call, and a call to a member function which happens to have the same name?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: When the compiler generates internal names, it does something which is often referred to as }{\i name decoration}{
. When a function is a member of a structure, the name of the structure is decorated in with the name of the function, thereby creating an internal name which is completely distinct from a function of the same name that
 is global, or one that is a member of another structure. Therefore the linker cannot confuse the two, and neither can the compiler.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 11. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 11.}{\tab How does a member function know which object it is supposed to be working on? That is, what syntax do you use if you have an object and want to call a member function for }{\i 
that}{ object, rather than for another object?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: Just like in C, if you have a structure and you want to select a member of that structure, you give the name of the variable, followed by the \lquote }{
\b .}{\rquote  (dot) operator, followed by the name of the member function. Of course, since it\rquote s a function, it is followed by the argument list.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 12. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 12.}{\tab What syntax do you use if you have a pointer to a structure, and you want to call a member function?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: Just like in C, if you have a pointer to a structure and you want to select a member of that structure, you use the \lquote }{\b ->}{\lquote 
 (arrow) operator. Again, since it\rquote s a member }{\i function}{ you must use the argument list. Also, since it\rquote s a function it can have a return value.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 13. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 13.}{\tab What kind of code does the compiler generate when you call a member function? That is, how is the information passed into the function about what object is called?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: The information about what object is called is secretly passed in as the first argument of the function.
 That is, whenever you have a member function you do not explicitly put down the fact that the first argument of the member function is the address of the object, but the compiler generates that information because it knows it\rquote 
s looking at a member functio
n. Also, when you call the member function for a particular object, the compiler takes the address of that object and passes that address as the first argument to the member function. All this happens quietly. You can think of this whole process as the co
mpiler generating some extra code for you so you can use a more convenient syntax.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 14. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 14.}{\tab What is the name of the address of the object, referred to from inside the member function? 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: The name the keyword }{\b this}{. Inside a member function (and }{\i only}{ inside a member function) the word }{\b this}{ means \ldblquote 
the starting address of the current object.\rdblquote  The code that selects members using }{\b this}{ is a much closer approximation to what the compiler generates when you make an ordinary member function call.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 15. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 15.}{\tab How do you call a member function from within another member function?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: You simply call it. Since you\rquote 
re inside a member function of that class, the compiler first looks for another member function of the same class before it goes looking for a global function of the same name.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 16. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 16.}{\tab Suppose you have a class, and you're inside a function }{\b f()}{, and there's a member function of the same class called }{\b g()}{, and there's also a global function }{\b g()}{
. How do you call the global function }{\b g()}{ so the member function isn't automatically called instead.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
A: Use the global scope-resolution operator, that is the scope-resolution operator with nothing preceding the operator. This refers to the global version of a function.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 17. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 17.}{\f26 \tab }{How does the compiler insure the consistency of structures across translation units?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
A: The compiler implicitly forces you to put structure declarations (that is, descriptions of the structures) inside header files. The compiler must see the description of the structure before you can use that class by either declaring a varia
ble of that class (that is, an object), or call a member function for that variable. In addition, the compiler complains if it sees a multiple declaration. Therefore, the intelligent thing to do is put the declaration of a class into a single header file,
 and put the definitions into a single CPP file. Then everywhere you use that class you must include the header file.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 18. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 18.}{\tab In C, you must use }{\b typedef}{ if you want to be able to treat a }{\b struct}{ name as if it were a built-in type. This is a common practice in C 
programming, especially when creating libraries. Is this necessary in C++? How does C++ treat the same situation?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: The C++ compiler automatically creates the equivalent of a }{\b typedef}{ (although it generally treats it differently internally). You can then t
reat the structure name as if it were a true native type.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 19. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 19.}{\tab Why does the compiler only allow you to declare a structure once during the compilation of a particular translation unit?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: If it saw the declaration more than once, it wouldn\rquote t know which was the correct declaration. Thus, it prevents you from redeclaring a class.

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 20. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 20.}{\tab It's possible with multiple levels of }{\b #include}{
s that a class can be declared more than once. What is the common practice used to prevent the accidental multiple declaration of a class?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: The preprocessor is ordinarily used by creating a \ldblquote decorated name\rdblquote 
 of your own, generally by using the name of the file and the extension, and mixing in underscores. Leading underscores are not used because those are reserved in the Standard C library.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 21. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 21.}{\tab How does the combination of header files and the requirement that you must declare header files before they are used increase the safety of your programs?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: C allows you to call functions without first declaring them. C++ effectively forces you 
to place the class declaration in a header file (although you could certainly not include header files and just declare the class by hand in each translation unit where it is used \endash 
 but that would be ridiculous) and you have to declare functions with prototypes before calling them. This way, the compiler can insure the function is called properly including proper type checking on the arguments. Since the information wasn\rquote 
t there in C, it was possible to make a mis-call which was a very subtle and difficult error to detect. In C++ you get an error message.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 22. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 22.}{\tab What is the difference between a declaration and a definition?\line A:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 23. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {\f26\fs24 23.}{\tab What should you never put into a header file?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: Although there are a few semi-exceptions (which have their own sensibility, and which you\rquote 
ll see throughout the book), you should never put anything into a header file which causes the compiler to allocate storage. This includes variable/objects and function bodies. Put another way, only declarations should go into header files, never de
finitions. The linker must never see multiple definitions for the same piece of storage. If you put a function definition, or an object definition into a header file, then that storage will be allocated anywhere the header file is included. Then when the 
linker comes along to create the final program by resolving all the references, it will find multiple definitions for the object/variable and cause a multiple definition error. There must only be a single definition for a particular function or variable.

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 24. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 24.}{\tab When the C++ compiler sees a structure which was originally written for a C compiler, what does it do differently than what the C compiler does?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A: The only difference is the implied }{\b typedef}{ performed for the structure\rquote s tagname.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 25. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\f26\fs24 25.}{\tab Why is the semicolon at the end of a structure declaration so important?\line 
A: The semicolon indicates the end of all object definitions for this particular structure. If the semicolon isn't there, the next name the compiler sees it will assume is the definition of an object of that structure type.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 26. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {\f26\fs24 26.}{\tab What's the difference between C and C++ prototypes?
\par }}}{
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 22. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls114\adjustright {Create a }{\b Stack}{ that holds }{\b Stash}{es. Each }{\b Stash}{ will hold 5 lines from an input file. Create the }{\b Stash}{es using }{\b new}{. Read a file into your }{\b Stack}{
, then reprint it in its original form by extracting it from the }{\b Stack}{. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 23. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls114\adjustright {Modify the previous exercise so that you create a }{\b struct}{ that encapsulates the }{\b Stack}{ of }{\b Stash}{es. The user should only add and get lines via member functions, but under the covers the }{\b struct }{happens to use a 
}{\b Stack}{ of }{\b Stash}{es.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 24. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls114\adjustright {Create a }{\b struct}{ that holds an }{\b int}{ and a pointer to another instance of the same }{\b struct}{. Write a function that takes the address of one of these }{\b struct}{s and an }{\b int}{
 indicating the length of the list you want created. This function will make makes a whole chain of these }{\b struct}{s (a }{\i linked list}{), starting from the argument (the }{\i head}{ of the list), with each one pointing to the next. Make the new }{
\b struct}{s using }{\b new}{, and put the count (which object number this is) in the }{\b int}{. In the last }{\b struct }{in the list, put a zero value in the pointer to indicate that it\rquote 
s the end. Write a second function that takes the head of your list and moves through to the end, printing out both the pointer value and the }{\b int}{ value for each one.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 25. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls114\adjustright {Repeat the previous exercise, but put the functions inside a }{\b struct}{ instead of using \ldblquote raw\rdblquote  }{\b struct}{s and functions.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 26. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls114\adjustright {Write a function which takes two }{\b int}{ arguments, }{\b rows}{ and }{\b columns}{. This function returns a pointer to a dynamically-allocated two-dimensional array of }{\b float}{. As a hint, the first call to }{\b new}{ is:\line }
{\b new float[rows][]}{. This must be followed by multiple calls to }{\b new}{ in order to create all the storage for the }{\b rows}{. Write a second function that takes this \ldblquote matrix\rdblquote  and frees the storage using }{\b delete}{
. Now convert the code into a }{\b struct}{ called }{\b matrix}{.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\sect }\sectd \margtsxn2707\margbsxn2707\sbkodd\linex0\headery0\footery1440\colsx432\endnhere\titlepg\sectdefaultcl {\footer \pard\plain 
