\par }}\pard\plain \s1\fi-1080\li990\sa360\sl240\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \cbpat8 \f4\fs96\cf1\kerning18\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373836}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776082}
5: Hiding the implementation{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373836}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776082}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {A typical C library contains a }{\b struct}{ and some associated functions to act on that }{\b struct}{. So far, you
've seen how C++ takes functions that are }{\i conceptually}{ associated and makes them }{\i literally}{ associated, by 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {putting the function declarations inside the scope of the }{\b struct}{, changing the way functions are called for the }{\b struct}{
, eliminating the passing of the structure address as the first argument, and adding a new type name to the program (so you don\rquote t have to create a }{\b typedef}{ for the }{\b struct}{ tag).
\par These are all convenient \endash  they help you organize your code and make it easier to write and read. However, ther
e are other important issues when making libraries easier in C++, especially the issues of safety and control. This chapter looks at the subject of boundaries in structures.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373837}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776083}Setting limits
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373837}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776083}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In any relationship it\rquote s important to have boundaries that are resp
ected by all parties involved. When you create a library, you establish a relationship with the }{\i client programmer}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {client programmer}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {programmer, client}}}{ who uses that library to build an application or another library.
\par In a C }{\b struct}{, as with most things in C, there are no rules. Client programmers can do anything they want with that }{\b struct}{, and there\rquote 
s no way to force any particular behaviors. For example, even though you saw in the last chapter the importance of the functions named }{\b initialize(\~)}{ and }{\b cleanup(\~)}{, the client programmer has the option not to call those functions. (We
\rquote ll look at a better approach in the next chapter.) And even though you would really prefer that the client programmer not directly manipulate some of the members of your }{\b struct}{, in C there\rquote s no way to prevent it. Everything\rquote 
s naked to the world.
\par There are two reasons for controlling access }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {controlling\: access}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {access\: control}}}{to members. The first is to keep the client programmer\rquote s hands off tools they shouldn\rquote t
 touch, tools that are necessary for the internal machinations of the data type, but not part of the interface the client programmer needs to solve their particular problems. This is actually a service to client programmers because they can easily see wha
t\rquote s important to them and what they can ignore.
\par The second reason for access control is to allow the library designer to change the internal workings of the structure without worrying about how it will affect the client programmer. In the }{\b Stack}{ example in t
he last chapter, you might want to allocate the storage in big chunks, for speed, rather than creating new storage each time an element is added. If the interface and implementation are clearly separated and protected, you can accomplish this and require 
only a relink by the client programmer.}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {interface\: separation of interface and implementation}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {separation of interface & implementation}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {implementation
\: and interface, separation}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373838}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776084}C++ access control
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373838}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776084}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {C++ introduces three new keywords to set the boundaries in a structure: }{\b public}{, }{\b private}{, and }{\b protected}{
. Their use and meaning are remarkably straightforward. These }{\i access specifiers}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {access\: specifiers}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {specifier\: access}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {control\: access}}}{
are used only in a structure declaration, and they change the boundary for all the declarations that follow them. Whenever you use an access specifier, it must be followed by a colon.
\par }{\b public}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {public}}}{means all member declarations that follow are available to everyone. }{\b public}{ members are like }{\b struct}{
 members. For example, the following }{\b struct}{ declarations are identical:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C05:Public.cpp
\par // Public is just like C's struct
\par 
\par struct A \{
\par   int i;
\par   char j;
\par   float f;
\par   void func();
\par \};
\par 
\par void A::func() \{\}
\par 
\par struct B \{
\par public:
\par   int i;
\par   char j;
\par   float f;
\par   void func();
\par \};
\par 
\par void B::func() \{\}  
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   A a; B b;
\par   a.i = b.i = 1;
\par   a.j = b.j = 'c';
\par   a.f = b.f = 3.14159;
\par   a.func();
\par   b.func();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b private}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {private}}}{
keyword, on the other hand, means that no one can access that member except you, the creator of the type, inside function members of that type. }{\b private}{ is a brick wall between you and the client programmer; if someone tries to access a }{\b private
}{ member, they\rquote ll get a compile-time error. In }{\b struct B}{ in the above example, you may want to make portions of the representation (that is, the data members) hidden, accessible only to you:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C05:Private.cpp
\par // Setting the boundary
\par 
\par struct B \{
\par private:
\par   char j;
\par   float f;
\par public:
\par   int i;
\par   void func();
\par \};
\par 
\par void B::func() \{
\par   i = 0;
\par   j = '0';
\par   f = 0.0;
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   B b;
\par   b.i = 1;    // OK, public
\par //!  b.j = '1';  // Illegal, private
\par //!  b.f = 1.0;  // Illegal, private
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Although }{\b func(\~)}{ can access any member of }{\b B}{ (because }{\b func(\~)}{ is itself a member of }{\b B}{, thus automatically 
granting it permission), an ordinary global function like }{\b main(\~)}{ cannot. Of course, neither can member functions of other structures. Only the functions that are clearly stated in the structure declaration (the \ldblquote contract\rdblquote 
) can have access to }{\b private}{ members.
\par There is no required order for access specifiers}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {order\: for access specifiers}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {access\: order for specifiers}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {specifier\: order for access}}}{
, and they may appear more than once. They affect all the members declared after them and before the next access specifier.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373839}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776085}protected
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373839}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776085}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The last access specifier is }{\b protected}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
protected}}}{. }{\b protected}{ acts just like }{\b private}{, with one exception that we can\rquote t really talk about right now: \ldblquote Inherited\rdblquote  structures (which cannot access }{\b private }{members) are granted access to }{\b 
protected}{ members. But inheritance won\rquote t be introduced until Chapter XX, so this doesn\rquote t have any meaning to you. For the current purposes, consider }{\b protected}{ to be just like }{\b private}{
; it will be clarified when inheritance is introduced.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373840}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776086}Friends
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373840}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776086}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {What if you want to explicitly grant access to a function that isn\rquote 
t a member of the current structure? This is accomplished by declaring that function a }{\b friend}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {friend}}}{\i inside}{
 the structure declaration. It\rquote s important that the }{\b friend}{ declaration occ
urs inside the structure declaration because you (and the compiler) must be able to read the structure declaration and see every rule about the size and behavior of that data type. And a very important rule in any relationship is \ldblquote 
who can access my private implementation?\rdblquote 
\par The class controls which code has access to its members. There\rquote s no magic way to \ldblquote break in\rdblquote  from the outside if you aren\rquote t a }{\b friend}{; you can\rquote t declare a new class and say \ldblquote hi, I\rquote 
m a friend of }{\b Bob}{!\rdblquote  and expect to see the }{\b private}{ and }{\b protected}{ members of }{\b Bob}{.
\par You can declare a global function as a }{\b friend}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {global\: friend function}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: global friend}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {friend\: global function}}}{
, and you can also declare a member function of another structure}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {member\: friend function}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {friend\: member function}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: friend member}}}{
, or even an entire structure, as a }{\b friend}{. Here\rquote s an example }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {structure\: friend}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {friend\: structure}}}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C05:Friend.cpp
\par // Friend allows special access
\par 
\par // Declaration (incomplete type specification):
\par struct X;
\par 
\par struct Y \{
\par   void f(X*);
\par \};
\par 
\par struct X \{ // Definition
\par private:
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   void initialize();
\par   friend void g(X*, int); // Global friend
\par   friend void Y::f(X*);  // Struct member friend
\par   friend struct Z; // Entire struct is a friend
\par   friend void h();
\par \};
\par 
\par void X::initialize() \{ 
\par   i = 0; 
\par \}
\par 
\par void g(X* x, int i) \{ 
\par   x->i = i; 
\par \}
\par 
\par void Y::f(X* x) \{ 
\par   x->i = 47; 
\par \}
\par 
\par struct Z \{
\par private:
\par   int j;
\par public:
\par   void initialize();
\par   void g(X* x);
\par \};
\par 
\par void Z::initialize() \{ 
\par   j = 99;
\par \}
\par 
\par void Z::g(X* x) \{ 
\par   x->i += j; 
\par \}
\par 
\par void h() \{
\par   X x;
\par   x.i = 100; // Direct data manipulation
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   X x;
\par   Z z;
\par   z.g(&x);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b struct Y}{ has a member function }{\b f(\~)}{ that will modify an object of type }{\b X}{. This is a bit of a conundrum because 
the C++ compiler requires you to declare everything before you can refer to it, so }{\b struct Y}{ must be declared before its member }{\b Y::f(X*)}{ can be declared as a friend in }{\b struct X}{. But for }{\b Y::f(X*)}{ to be declared, }{\b struct X}{
 must be declared first!
\par Here\rquote s the solution. Notice that }{\b Y::f(X*)}{ takes the }{\i address}{ of an }{\b X}{ object}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {address of an object}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: address of}}}{
. This is critical because the compiler always knows how to pass an address, which is of a fixed size regardless of the object being passed, even if it doesn\rquote 
t have full information about the size of the type. If you try to pass the whole object, however, the compiler must see the entire structure definition of }{\b X}{, to know the size and how to pass it, before it allows you to declare a function such as }{
\b Y::g(X)}{. 
\par By passing the address of an }{\b X}{, the compiler allows you to make an }{\i incomplete type specification}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {incomplete type specification}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type\: incomplete type specification}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
specification\: incomplete type}}}{of }{\b X}{ prior to declaring }{\b Y::f(X*)}{. This is accomplished in the declaration 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {struct }{\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl
\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {declaration\: structure}}}{\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 
{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {structure\: declaration}}}{X;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The declaration simply tells the compiler there\rquote s a }{\b struct}{ by that name, so it\rquote s OK to refer to it as long as you don\rquote 
t require any more knowledge than the name.
\par Now, in }{\b struct X}{, the function }{\b Y::f(X*)}{ can be declared as a }{\b friend}{ with no problem. If you tried to declare it before the compiler had seen the full specification for }{\b Y}{
, it would have given you an error. This is a safety feature to ensure consistency and eliminate bugs.
\par Notice the two other }{\b friend}{ functions. The first declares an ordinary global function }{\b g(\~)}{ as a }{\b friend}{. But }{\b g(\~)}{ has not been previously declared at the global scope! It turns out that }{\b friend}{
 can be used this way to simultaneously declare the function }{\i and}{ give it }{\b friend}{ status. This extends to entire structures: 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {friend struct Z;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {is an incomplete type specification for }{\b Z}{, and it gives the entire structure }{\b friend}{ status.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373841}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776087}Nested friends
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373841}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776087}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {nested\: friends}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {friend\: nested}}}{Making a structure nested doesn\rquote t automatically give it access to }{\b private}{
 members. To accomplish this you must follow a particular form: first define the nested structure, then declare it as a }{\b friend}{ using full scoping. The structure definition must be separate from the }{\b friend}{
 declaration, otherwise it would be seen by the compiler as a nonmember. Here\rquote s an example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C05:NestFriend.cpp
\par // Nested friends
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <cstring> // memset()
\par using namespace std;
\par const int sz = 20;
\par 
\par struct Holder \{
\par private:
\par   int a[sz];
\par public:
\par   void initialize();
\par   struct Pointer \{
\par   private:
\par     Holder* h;
\par     int* p;
\par   public:
\par     void initialize(Holder* h);
\par     // Move around in the array:
\par     void next();
\par     void previous();
\par     void top();
\par     void end();
\par     // Access values:
\par     int read();
\par     void set(int i);
\par   \};
\par   friend Holder::Pointer;
\par \};
\par 
\par void Holder::initialize() \{
\par  memset(a, 0, sz * sizeof(int));
\par \}
\par 
\par void Holder::Pointer::initialize(Holder* h) \{
\par   h = h;
\par   p = h->a;
\par \}
\par 
\par void Holder::Pointer::next() \{
\par   if(p < &(h->a[sz - 1])) p++;
\par \}
\par 
\par void Holder::Pointer::previous() \{
\par   if(p > &(h->a[0])) p--;
\par \}
\par 
\par void Holder::Pointer::top() \{
\par   p = &(h->a[0]);
\par \}
\par 
\par void Holder::Pointer::end() \{
\par   p = &(h->a[sz - 1]);
\par \}
\par 
\par int Holder::Pointer::read() \{
\par   return *p;
\par \}
\par 
\par void Holder::Pointer::set(int i) \{
\par   *p = i;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Holder h;
\par   Holder::Pointer hp, hp2;
\par   int i;
\par 
\par   h.initialize();
\par   hp.initialize(&h);
\par   hp2.initialize(&h);
\par   for(i = 0; i < sz; i++) \{
\par     hp.set(i);
\par     hp.next();
\par   \}
\par   hp.top();
\par   hp2.end();
\par   for(i = 0; i < sz; i++) \{
\par     cout << "hp = " << hp.read()
\par       << ", hp2 = " << hp2.read() << endl;
\par     hp.next();
\par     hp2.previous();
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b struct Holder}{ contains an array of }{\b int}{s and the }{\b Pointer}{ allows you to access them. Because }{\b Pointer}{
 is strongly associated with }{\b Holder}{, it\rquote s sensible to make it a member structure of }{\b Holder}{. Once }{\b Pointer }{is defined, it is granted access to the private members of }{\b Holder}{ by saying:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {friend Holder::Pointer;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that the }{\b struct }{keyword is not necessary because the compiler already knows what }{\b Pointer}{ is.
\par Because }{\b Pointer}{ is a separate class from }{\b Holder}{, you can make more than one of them in }{\b main(\~)}{ and use them to select different parts of the array. Because }{\b Pointer}{ is a class instead of a raw C po
inter, you can guarantee that it will always safely point inside the }{\b Holder}{.
\par The Standard C library function }{\b memset(\~)}{ (in }{\b <cstring>}{) is used for convenience in the above program. It sets all memory starting at a particular address (the first argument) to a particular value (the second argument) for }{\b n}{
 bytes past the starting address (}{\b n}{ is the third argument). Of course, you could have simply used a loop to iterate through all the memory, but }{\b memset(\~)}{ is available, well-tested (so it\rquote s less likely you\rquote ll in
troduce an error) and probably more efficient than if you coded it by hand.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373842}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776088}Is it pure?
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373842}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776088}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The class definition gives you an audit trail, so you can see from looking at the class which functions have permission to modify the private parts of the class. If a function is a }{\b friend}{, it means that it isn\rquote 
t a member, but you want to give permission to modify private data anyway, and it must be listed in the class definition so everyone can see that it\rquote s one of the privileged functions. 
\par C++ is a hybrid object-oriented language, not a pure one, and }{\b friend}{ was added to get around practical problems that crop up. It\rquote s fine to point out that this makes the language less \ldblquote pure,\rdblquote  because C++ }{\i is}{
 designed to be pragmatic, not to aspire to an abstract ideal.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373843}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776089}Object layout
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373843}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776089}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Chapter XX stated that a }{\b struct}{
 written for a C compiler and later compiled with C++ would be unchanged. This referred primarily to the object layout of the }{\b struct}{, that is, where the storage for the individual variables is positioned in the memory allocated 
for the object. If the C++ compiler changed the layout }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {access\: access specifiers and object layout}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: layout, and access control}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
layout, object, and access control}}}{of C }{\b struct}{s, then any C code you wrote that inadvisably took advantage of knowledge of the positions of variables in the }{\b struct}{ would break.
\par When you start using access specifiers, however, you\rquote ve moved completely into the C++ realm, and things change a bit. Within a particular \ldblquote access block\rdblquote  (a group of declarations delim
ited by access specifiers), the variables are guaranteed to be laid out contiguously, as in C. However, the access blocks themselves may not appear in the object in the order that you declare them. Although the compiler will }{\i usually}{
 lay the blocks out exactly as you see them, there is no rule about it, because a particular machine architecture and/or operating environment may have explicit support for }{\b private}{ and }{\b protected}{
 that might require those blocks to be placed in special memory locations. The language specification doesn\rquote t want to restrict this kind of advantage.
\par Access specifiers are part of the structure and don\rquote t affect the objects created from the structure. All of the access specification information disappears before the program is run; generall
y this happens during compilation. In a running program, objects become \ldblquote regions of storage\rdblquote 
 and nothing more. If you really want to, you can break all the rules and access the memory directly, as you can in C. C++ is not designed to prevent you from doing unwise things. It just provides you with a much easier, highly desirable alternative.

\par In general, it\rquote s not a good idea to depend on anything that\rquote s implementation-specific when you\rquote re writing a program. When you must, those specifics should be encapsulate
d inside a structure, so any porting changes are focused in one place.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373844}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776090}The class
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373844}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776090}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Access control is often referred to as }{\i implementation hiding}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {implementation\: hiding}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {hiding\: implementation hiding}}}{. Including functions within structures (encapsulation}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {encapsulation}}}{
) produces a data type with characteristics and behaviors, but access control puts boundaries within that data type, for two important reasons. The first is to establish what the client programmers can and can\rquote t use. You can build y
our internal mechanisms into the structure without worrying that client programmers will think it\rquote s part of the interface they should be using.
\par This feeds directly into the second reason, which is to separate the interface from the implementation. }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {interface\: and implementation, separation}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {separation of interface and implementation}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
implementation\: and interface, separation}}}{ If the structure is used in a set of programs, but the client programmers can\rquote t do anything but send messages to the }{\b public}{ interface, then you can change anything that\rquote s }{\b private}{
 without requiring modifications to their code.
\par Encapsulation and implementation hiding, taken together, invent something more than a C }{\b struct}{. We\rquote re now in the world of object-oriented programmin
g, where a structure is describing a class of objects, as you would describe a class of fishes or a class of birds: Any object belonging to this class will share these characteristics and behaviors. That\rquote 
s what the structure declaration has become, a description of the way all objects of this type will look and act.
\par In the original OOP }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {OOP}}}{language, Simula-67}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {Simula-67}}}{, the keyword }{\b class}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class}}}{
was used to describe a new data type. This apparently inspired Stroustrup to choose the same keyword for C++, to emphasize that this was the focal point of the whole language: the creation of new data types that are more than just C }{\b struct}{
s with functions. This certainly seems like adequate justification for a new keyword.
\par However, the use of }{\b class}{ in C++ comes close to being an unnecessary keyword. It\rquote s identical to the }{\b struct}{ keyword in absolutely every way except one: }{\b class}{ defaults to }{\b private}{, whereas }{\b struct}{ defaults to }{\b 
public}{. Here are two structures that produce the same result:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C05:Class.cpp \{O\}
\par // Similarity of struct and class
\par 
\par struct A \{
\par private:
\par   int i, j, k;
\par public:
\par   int f();
\par   void g();
\par \};
\par 
\par int A::f() \{ 
\par   return i + j + k; 
\par \}
\par 
\par void A::g() \{ 
\par   i = j = k = 0; 
\par \}
\par 
\par // Identical results are produced with:
\par 
\par class B \{
\par   int i, j, k;
\par public:
\par   int f();
\par   void g();
\par \};
\par 
\par int B::f() \{ 
\par   return i + j + k; 
\par \}
\par 
\par void B::g() \{ 
\par   i = j = k = 0; 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b class}{ is the fundamental OOP concept in C++. It is one of the keywords that will }{\i not }{be set in bold in this book \endash 
 it becomes annoying with a word repeated as often as \ldblquote class.\rdblquote  The shift to classes is so important that I suspect Stroustrup\rquote s preference would have been to throw }{\b struct}{
 out altogether, but the need for backwards compatibility with C wouldn\rquote t allow that.
\par Many people prefer a style of creating classes that is more }{\b struct}{-like than class-like, because you override the \ldblquote default-to-}{\b private}{\rdblquote  behavior of the class by starting out with }{\b public}{ elements:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class X \{
\par public:
\par   void interface_function();
\par private:
\par   void private_function();
\par   int internal_representation;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The logic behind this is that it makes more sense for the reader to see the members of interest first, then they can ignore anything that says }{\b private}{
. Indeed, the only reasons all the other members must be declared in the class at all are so the compiler knows how big the objects are and can allocate them properly, and so it can guarantee consistency.
\par The examples in this book, however, will put the }{\b private}{ members first, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class X \{
\par   void private_function();
\par   int internal_representation;
\par public:
\par   void interface_function();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Some people even go to the trouble of decorating their own private names:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class Y \{
\par public:
\par   void f();
\par private:
\par   int mX;  // "Self-decorated" name
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Because }{\b mX}{ is already hidden in the scope of }{\b Y}{, the }{\b m}{
 is unnecessary. However, in projects with many global variables (something you should strive to avoid, but which is sometimes inevitable in existing projects) it is helpful to be able to distinguish, inside a member function definition, which
 data is global and which is a member.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373845}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776091}Modifying }{\b Stash}{
 to use access control{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373845}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776091}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
It makes sense to take the examples from the previous chapter and modify them to use classes and access control. Notice how the client programmer portion of the interface is now clearly distinguished, so there\rquote 
s no possibility of client programmers accidentally manipulating a part of the class that they shouldn\rquote t.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C05:Stash.h
\par // Converted to use access control
\par #ifndef STASH_H
\par #define STASH_H
\par 
\par class Stash \{
\par   int size;      // Size of each space
\par   int quantity;  // Number of storage spaces
\par   int next;      // Next empty space
\par   // Dynamically allocated array of bytes:
\par   unsigned char* storage;
\par   void inflate(int increase);
\par public:
\par   void initialize(int size);
\par   void cleanup();
\par   int add(void* element);
\par   void* fetch(int index);
\par   int count();
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // STASH_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b inflate(\~)}{ function has been made }{\b private}{ because it is used only by the }{\b add(\~)}{
 function and is thus part of the underlying implementation, not the interface. This means that, sometime later, you can change the underlying implementation to use a different system for memory management.
\par Other than the name of the include file, the above header is the only thing that\rquote s been changed for this example. The implementation file and test file are the same.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373846}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776092}Modifying }{\b Stack}{ to use 
\line access control{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373846}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776092}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As a second example, here\rquote s the }{\b Stack}{ turned into a class. Now the nested data structure is }{\b private}{
, which is nice because it ensures that the client programmer will neither have to look at it nor be able to depend on the internal representation of the }{\b Stack}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C05:Stack.h
\par // Nested structs via linked list
\par #ifndef STACK_H
\par #define STACK_H
\par 
\par class Stack \{
\par   struct Link \{
\par     void* data;
\par     Link* next;
\par     void initialize(void* dat, Link* nxt);
\par   \}* head;
\par public:
\par   void initialize();
\par   void push(void* dat);
\par   void* peek();
\par   void* pop();
\par   void cleanup();
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // STACK_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As before, the implementation doesn\rquote t change and so is not repeated here. The test, too, is identical. The only thing that\rquote 
s been changed is the robustness of the class interface. The real value of access control is during development, to prevent you from crossing boundaries. In fact, the compiler is the only thing that knows about the protection level of c
lass members. There is no access control information mangled into the member name that carries through to the linker. All the protection checking is done by the compiler; it has vanished by runtime.}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {access\: control, run-time}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {control\: access and run-time
}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time, access control}}}{
\par Notice that the interface presented to the client programmer is now truly that of a push-down stack}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {push-down stack}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {stack\: push-down}}}{. It happens to be implemented as a linked list}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linked list}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {list\: linked}}}{
, but you can change that without affecting what the client programmer interacts with, or (more importantly) a single line of client code.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373847}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776093}Handle classes
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373847}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776093}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Access control in C++ allows you to separate interface from implementation, but the implementation hiding }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {implementation\: hiding}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {hiding\: implementation hiding}
}}{is only partial. The compiler must still see the declarations for all parts of an object in order to create and manipulate it properly. You could imagine a programming langua
ge that requires only the public interface of an object and allows the private implementation to be hidden, but C++ performs type checking statically (at compile time) as much as possible. This means that you\rquote ll learn as early as possible if there
\rquote s an error. It also means your program is more efficient. However, including the private implementation has two effects: The implementation is visible even if you can\rquote t easily access it, and it can cause needless recompilation}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {needless recompilation}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {compilation\: needless}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {reducing recompilation}}}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373848}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776094}Hiding the implementation
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373848}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776094}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Some projects cannot afford to have their implementation visible to the client programmer. It may show strategic information in a library header file that the company doesn\rquote t want 
available to competitors. You may be working on a system where security}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {security}}}{ is an issue \endash  an encryption algorithm, for example 
\endash  and you don\rquote t want to expose any clues in a header file that might help people to crack the code. Or you may be putting your library in a \ldblquote hostile\rdblquote  environment, where the programmers}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {hostile programmers}}}{ will directly access the private components anyway, using pointers }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec 
}\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {pointer}}}{and casting}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cast}}}{. In all these situations, it\rquote s valuable to have the actual 
structure compiled inside an implementation file rather than exposed in a header file.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373849}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776095}Reducing recompilation
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373849}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776095}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The project manager in your programming environment will cause a recompilation of a file if that file is touched (that is, modified) }{\i or}{ if another
 file it\rquote s dependent upon \endash  that is, an included header file \endash  is touched. This means that any time you make a change to a class, whether it\rquote s to the public interface or the private member declarations, you\rquote 
ll force a recompilation of anything that include
s that header file. For a large project in its early stages this can be very unwieldy because the underlying implementation may change often; if the project is very big, the time for compiles can prohibit rapid turnaround.
\par The technique to solve this is sometimes called }{\i handle classes }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {handle classes}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\: handle}}}{or the \ldblquote Cheshire Cat\rdblquote }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Cheshire Cat}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Cat, Cheshire}}}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ This name is attributed to John Carolan}{\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {Carolan, John}}}{
, one of the early pioneers in C++, and of course, Lewis Carroll}{\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {Carroll, Lewis}}}{
. This technique can also be seen as a form of the \ldblquote proxy\rdblquote  design pattern, described in Chapter XX.}}}{ \endash  everything about the implementation disappears except for a single pointer, the \ldblquote smile.\rdblquote 
 The pointer refers to a structur
e whose definition is in the implementation file along with all the member function definitions. Thus, as long as the interface is unchanged, the header file is untouched. The implementation can change at will, and only the implementation file needs to be
 recompiled and relinked with the project.
\par Here\rquote s a simple example demonstrating the technique. The header file contains only the public interface and a single pointer of an incompletely specified class:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C05:Handle.h
\par // Handle classes
\par #ifndef HANDLE_H
\par #define HANDLE_H
\par 
\par class Handle \{
\par   struct Cheshire; // Class declaration only
\par   Cheshire* smile;
\par public:
\par   void initialize();
\par   void cleanup();
\par   int read();
\par   void change(int);
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // HANDLE_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This is all the client programmer is able to see. The line 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {struct Cheshire;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {is an }{\i incomplete type specification}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
incomplete type specification}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type\: incomplete type specification}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {specification\: incomplete type}}}{or a }{\i class declaration}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\: declaration}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {declaration\: class}}}{(A }{\i class definition}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\:
 definition}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {definition\: class}}}{includes the body of the class.) It tells the compiler that }{\b Cheshire}{ is a structure name, but it doesn
\rquote t give any details about the }{\b struct}{. This is only enough information to create a pointer to the }{\b struct}{; you can\rquote 
t create an object until the structure body has been provided. In this technique, that structure body is hidden away in the implementation file:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C05:Handle.cpp \{O\}
\par // Handle implementation
\par #include "Handle.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par 
\par // Define Handle's implementation:
\par struct Handle::Cheshire \{
\par   int i;
\par \};
\par 
\par void Handle::initialize() \{
\par   smile = new Cheshire();
\par   require(smile != 0);
\par   smile->i = 0;
\par \}
\par 
\par void Handle::cleanup() \{
\par   delete smile;
\par \}
\par 
\par int Handle::read() \{
\par   return smile->i;
\par \}
\par 
\par void Handle::change(int x) \{
\par   smile->i = x;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b Cheshire}{ is a nested structure, so it must be defined with scope resolution}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {scope\: resolution}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {resolution\: scope}}}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {struct Handle::Cheshire \{
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In }{\b Handle::initialize(\~)}{, storage is allocated for a }{\b Cheshire}{ structure, and in }{\b Handle::cleanup(\~)}{
 this storage is released. This storage is used in lieu of all the data elements you\rquote d normally put into the }{\b private}{ section of the class. When you compile }{\b Handle.cpp}{, this structure definition is hidde
n away in the object file where no one can see it. If you change the elements of }{\b Cheshire}{, the only file that must be recompiled is }{\b Handle.cpp}{ because the header file is untouched.
\par The use of }{\b Handle}{ is like the use of any class: include the header, create objects, and send messages.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C05:UseHandle.cpp
\par //\{L\} Handle
\par // Use the Handle class
\par #include "Handle.h"
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Handle u;
\par   u.initialize();
\par   u.read();
\par   u.change(1);
\par   u.cleanup();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The only thing the client programmer can access is the
 public interface, so as long as the implementation is the only thing that changes, the above file never needs recompilation. Thus, although this isn\rquote t perfect implementation hiding, it\rquote s a big improvement.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373850}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776096}Summary
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373850}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776096}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Access control in C++ gives valuable cont
rol to the creator of a class. The users of the class can clearly see exactly what they can use and what to ignore. More important, though, is the ability to ensure that no client programmer becomes dependent on any part of the underlying implementation o
f a class. If you know this as the creator of the class, you can change the underlying implementation with the knowledge that no client programmer will be affected by the changes because they can\rquote t access that part of the class.
\par When you have the ability to change the underlying implementation, you can not only improve your design }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {design\: and mistakes}}}{
at some later time, but you also have the freedom to make mistakes}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {mistakes, and design}}}{. No matter how carefully you plan and design, you\rquote 
ll make mistakes. Knowing that it\rquote s relatively safe to make these mistakes means you\rquote ll be more experimental, you\rquote ll learn faster, and you\rquote ll finish your project sooner.
\par The public interface to a class is what the client programmer }{\i does}{ see, so that is the most important part of the class to get \ldblquote right\rdblquote  during analysis and design. But even that allows you some leeway for change. If you don
\rquote t get the interface right the first time, you can }{\i add}{ more functions}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: adding more to a design}}}{, as long as you don\rquote 
t remove any that client programmers have already used in their code.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373851}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776097}Exercises{\*\bkmkstart AAA}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373851}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776097}{\*\bkmkend AAA}
