Chapter 4 made a significant improvement in library use by taking all the scattered components of a typical C library and encapsulating them into a structure (an abstract data type, called a class from now on). 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
This not only provides a single unified point of entry into a library component, but it also hides the names of the functions within the class name. In Chapter 5, access control (implementation hiding) was introduced. This gives the class designer a 
way to establish clear boundaries for determining what the client programmer is allowed to manipulate and what is off limits. It means the internal mechanisms of a data type\rquote 
s operation are under the control and discretion of the class designer, and it\rquote s clear to client programmers what members they can and should pay attention to.
\par Together, encapsulation and implementation hiding make a significant step in improving the ease of library use. The concept of \ldblquote new data type\rdblquote  they provide is better in some ways
 than the existing built-in data types inherited from C. The C++ compiler can now provide type-checking guarantees for that data type and thus ensure a level of safety when that data type is being used.
\par When it comes to safety, however, there\rquote s a lot more the compiler can do for us than C provides. In this and future chapters, you\rquote 
ll see additional features engineered into C++ that make the bugs in your program almost leap out and grab you, sometimes before you even compile the program, but usually in the f
orm of compiler warnings and errors. For this reason, you will soon get used to the unlikely sounding scenario that a C++ program that compiles usually runs right the first time.
\par Two of these safety issues are initialization and cleanup. A large segment of C bugs occur when the programmer forgets to initialize or clean up a variable. This is especially true with libraries, when client programmers don\rquote 
t know how to initialize a }{\b struct}{, or even that they must. (Libraries often do not include an initialization function, so the client programmer is forced to initialize the }{\b struct}{
 by hand.) Cleanup is a special problem because C programmers are used to forgetting about variables once they are finished, so any cleaning up that may be necessary for a library\rquote s }{\b struct}{ is often missed.
\par In C++ the concept of initialization and cleanup is essential to making library use easy and to eliminating the many subtle bugs that occur when the client programmer forgets to perform these activities. This chapter examines the featur
es in C++ that help guarantee proper initialization and cleanup.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373853}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776099}
Guaranteed initialization with the constructor{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373853}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776099}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Both the }{\b Stash}{ and }{\b Stack}{ classes have had functions called }{\b initialize(\~)}{
, which hint that it should be called before using the object in any oth
er way. Unfortunately, this means the client programmer must ensure proper initialization. Client programmers are prone to miss details like initialization in their headlong rush to make your amazing library solve their problem. In C++ initialization is t
oo important to leave to the client programmer. The class designer can guarantee initialization of every object by providing a special function called the }{\i constructor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {initialization\: initializing with the constructor}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor}}}{. If a class has 
a constructor, the compiler automatically calls that constructor at the point an object is created, before client programmers can even get their hands on the object. The constructor call isn\rquote 
t even an option for the client programmer; it is performed by the compiler at the point the object }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: definition point}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {definition\: object}}}{is defined.
\par The next challenge is what to name this function. There are two issues. The first is that any name you use is something that can potentially clash with a
 name you might like to use as a member in the class. The second is that because the compiler is responsible for calling the constructor, it must always know which function to call. The solution Stroustrup chose seems the easiest and most logical: The nam
e of the constructor }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: name}}}{
is the same as the name of the class. It makes sense that such a function will be called automatically on initialization.
\par Here\rquote s a simple class with a constructor:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class X \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   X();  // Constructor
\par \};
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now, when an object is defined,
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void f() \{
\par   X a;
\par   // ...
\par \}
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {the same thing happens as if }{\b a}{ were an }{\b int}{: Storage is allocated for the object. But when the program reaches the }{\i sequence point}{ }
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {sequence point}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {point, sequence}}}{
(point of execution) where }{\b a}{ is defined, the constructor is called automatically. That is, the compiler quietly inserts the call to }{\b X::X(\~)}{ for the object }{\b a}{
 at its point of definition. Like any member function, the first (secret) argument to the constructor is the address of the object for which it is being called.
\par Like any function, the constructor can have arguments}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: arguments}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {arguments\: constructor}}}{ to allow you to specify }{\i how}{ an object is created, give it initialization values, and so on. Constru
ctor arguments provide you with a way to guarantee that all parts of your object are initialized to appropriate values. For example, if the class }{\b Tree}{
 has a constructor that takes a single integer argument denoting the height of the tree, you must then create a tree object like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Tree t(12);  // 12-foot tree
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If }{\b tree(int)}{ is your only constructor, then the compiler won\rquote t let you create an object any other way. (We\rquote 
ll look at multiple constructors and different ways to call constructors in the next chapter.)
\par That\rquote s really all there is to a constructor: It\rquote 
s a specially named function that is called automatically by the compiler for every object. However, it eliminates a large class of problems and makes the code easier to read. In the preceding code fragment, for example, you don\rquote 
t see an explicit function call to some }{\b initialize(\~)}{ function that is conceptually separate from definition. In C++, definition and initialization are unified concepts \endash  you can\rquote t have one without the other.
\par Both the constructor and destructor are very unusual types of functions: They have no return value}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: return value}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {return\: constructor return value}}}{. This is distinctly different from a }{\b void}{ return value, where the function returns nothing but you still ha
ve the option to make it something else. Constructors and destructors return nothing and you don\rquote 
t have an option. The acts of bringing an object into and out of the program are special, like birth and death, and the compiler always makes the function call
s itself, to make sure they happen. If there were a return value, and if you could select your own, the compiler would somehow have to know what to do with the return value, or the client programmer would have to explicitly call constructors and destructo
rs, which would eliminate their safety.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373854}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776100}
Guaranteed cleanup with the destructor{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373854}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776100}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As a C programmer, you often think about the importance of initialization, but it\rquote 
s rarer to think about cleanup. After all, what do you need to do to clean up an }{\b int}{? Just forget about it. However, with libraries, just \ldblquote letting go\rdblquote  of an object once you\rquote 
re done with it is not so safe. What if it modifies some piece of hardware, or puts something on the screen, or allocates storage on the heap? If you just forget about it, your ob
ject never achieves closure upon its exit from this world. In C++, cleanup is as important as initialization and is therefore guaranteed with the destructor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {destructor}}}{.
\par The syntax for the destructor is similar to that for the constructor: The class name is used for the name of the function. However, the destructor is distinguished from the constructor by a leading tilde (}{\b ~}{
). In addition, the destructor never has any arguments }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {arguments\: destructor}}}{because destruction never needs any options. Here\rquote 
s the declaration for a destructor:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class Y \{
\par public:
\par   ~Y();
\par \};
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The destructor is called automatically by the compiler when the object goes out of scope}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {destructor\: scope}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {scope}}}{. You can see where the constructor gets called by the point of 
definition of the object, but the only evidence for a destructor call is the closing brace of the scope that surrounds the object. Yet the destructor is called, even when you use }{\b goto}{ }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {goto}}}{to jump out of a scope. (}{\b goto}{ still exists in C++, for backwa
rd compatibility with C and for the times when it comes in handy.) You should note that a nonlocal }{\b goto}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {non-local goto}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {goto\: non-local}}}{, implemented by the Standard C library functions }{\b setjmp(\~)}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {setjmp(\~)}}}{and }{\b longjmp(\~)}{, }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {longjmp(\~)}}}{doesn\rquote 
t cause destructors to be called. (This is the specification, even if your compiler doesn\rquote t implement it that way. Relying on a feature that isn\rquote t in the specification means your code is nonportable.)
\par Here\rquote s an example demonstrating the features of constructors and destructors you\rquote ve seen so far:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C06:Constructor1.cpp
\par // Constructors & destructors
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Tree \{
\par   int height;
\par public:
\par   Tree(int initialHeight);  // Constructor
\par   ~Tree();  // Destructor
\par   void grow(int years);
\par   void printsize();
\par \};
\par 
\par Tree::Tree(int initialHeight) \{
\par   height = initialHeight;
\par \}
\par 
\par Tree::~Tree() \{
\par   cout << "inside Tree destructor" << endl;
\par   printsize();
\par \}
\par 
\par void Tree::grow(int years) \{
\par   height += years;
\par \}
\par 
\par void Tree::printsize() \{
\par   cout << "Tree height is " << height << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   cout << "before opening brace" << endl;
\par   \{
\par     Tree t(12);
\par     cout << "after Tree creation" << endl;
\par     t.printsize();
\par     t.grow(4);
\par     cout << "before closing brace" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   cout << "after closing brace" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here\rquote s the output of the above program:
