\par after Tree creation
\par Tree height is 12
\par before closing brace
\par inside Tree destructor
\par Tree height is 16
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {after closing brace
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can see that the destructor is automatically called at the closing brace of the scope that encloses it.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373855}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776101}
Elimination of the definition block{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373855}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776101}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {definition\: block}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {block, definition}}}{In C}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C}}}{
, you must always define all the variables at the beginning of a block, after the opening brace. This is not an uncommon requirement in programming languages, and the reason given has always been that it\rquote s \ldblquote good programming style.
\rdblquote  On this point, I have my suspicions. It has always seemed inconvenient to me, as a programmer, to pop back t
o the beginning of a block every time I need a new variable. I also find code more readable when the variable definition is close to its point of use.}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe 
{variable\: point of definition}}}{
\par Perhaps these arguments are stylistic. In C++, however, there\rquote s a sig
nificant problem in being forced to define all objects at the beginning of a scope. If a constructor exists, it must be called when the object is created. However, if the constructor takes one or more initialization arguments, how do you know you will hav
e that initialization information at the beginning of a scope? In the general programming situation, you won\rquote t. Because C has no concept of }{\b private}{
, this separation of definition and initialization is no problem. However, C++ guarantees that when an object is created, it is simultaneously initialized. This ensures you will have no uninitialized objects running around in your system. C doesn\rquote 
t care; in fact, C }{\i encourages}{ this practice by requiring you to define variables at the beginning of a block before you necessarily have the initialization information.
\par Generally C++ will not allow you to create an object before you have the initialization information for the constructor, so you don\rquote t have to define variables at the beginning of a scope. In fact, the style
 of the language would seem to encourage the definition of an object as close to its point of use as possible. In C++, any rule that applies to an \ldblquote object\rdblquote 
 automatically refers to an object of a built-in type, as well. This means that any class object or v
ariable of a built-in type can also be defined at any point in a scope. It also means that you can wait until you have the information for a variable before defining it, so you can always define and initialize at the same time:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C06:DefineInitialize.cpp
\par // Defining variables anywhere
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class G \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   G(int ii);
\par \};
\par 
\par G::G(int ii) \{ i = ii; \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   cout << "initialization value? ";
\par   int retval = 0;
\par   cin >> retval;
\par   require(retval != 0);
\par   int y = retval + 3;
\par   G g(y);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can see that some code is executed, then }{\b retval}{ is defined, initialized and used to capture user input, then }{\b y}{ and }{\b g}{
 are defined. C, on the other hand, would never allow a variable to be defined anywhere except at the beginning of the scope.
\par Generally, you should define variables as close to their point of use as possible, and always initialize them when they are defined. (This is a stylistic suggestion for built-in types, wh
ere initialization is optional.) This is a safety issue. By reducing the duration of the variable\rquote 
s availability within the scope, you are reducing the chance it will be misused in some other part of the scope. In addition, readability is improved because the reader doesn\rquote 
t have to jump back and forth to the beginning of the scope to know the type of a variable.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373856}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776102}for}{ loops
