\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In C++, you will often see a }{\b for}{ loop }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
for loop}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {loop, for}}}{counter defined right inside the }{\b for}{ expression:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {for(int j = 0; j < 100; j++) \{
\par     cout << "j = " << j << endl;
\par \}
\par for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     cout << "i = " << i << endl;
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The above statements are important special cases, which cause confusion to new C++ programmers.
\par The variables }{\b i}{ and }{\b j}{ are defined directly inside the }{\b for}{ expression (which you cannot do in C). They are then available for use in the }{\b for}{ loop. It\rquote 
s a very convenient syntax because the context removes all question about the purpose of }{\b i}{ and }{\b j}{, so you don\rquote t need to use such ungainly names as }{\b i_loop_counter}{ for clarity.
\par The problem is the lifetime of the variables}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {lifetime\: of variables}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {variable\: lifetime of variables}}}{, which was formerly determined }{\i by the enclosing scope}{. This is a situation where a design decision was made from a compiler-writer\rquote 
s view of what is logical because as a programmer you obviously intend }{\b i}{ to be used only inside the statement(s) of the }{\b for}{ loop. Unfortunately, however, if you previously took this approach and said
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     cout << "i = " << i << endl;
\par // ....
\par for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)\{
\par     cout << "i = " << i << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {(with or without curly braces) within the same scope, compilers written for the old specification gave you a multiple-definition error for }{\b i}{
. The Standard C++ specification says that the lifetime of a loop counter defined within the control expression of a }{\b for}{
 loop lasts until the end of the controlled expression, so the above statements will work with a conforming compiler. Watch out, though, for local variables that hide variables in the enclosing scope.
\par I find small scopes an indicator of good design. If you have several pages for a single function, perhaps you\rquote re trying to do too much with that function. More granular functions are not only more useful, but it\rquote s also easier to find bugs}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {bugs, finding}}}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373857}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776103}Storage allocation
