\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
A variable can now be defined at any point in a scope, so it might seem initially that the storage for a variable may not be defined until its point of definition. It\rquote s more likely that the compiler w
ill follow the practice in C of allocating all the storage for a block at the opening brace of that block. It doesn\rquote t matter because, as a programmer, you can\rquote 
t get the storage (a.k.a. the object) until it has been defined. Although the storage is allocated}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {storage\: allocation}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {allocation\: storage}}}{ at the beginning of the block, the constructor call doesn\rquote 
t happen until the sequence point where the object is defined because the identifier isn\rquote t available until then. The compiler even checks to make sure you don\rquote 
t put the object definition (and thus the constructor call) where the sequence point }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {sequence point}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {point, sequence}}}{only conditionally passes through it, such as in a }{\b switch}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {switch}}}{statement or somewhere a }{\b goto}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {goto}}}{c
an jump past it. Uncommenting the statements in the following code will generate a warning or an error:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C06:Nojump.cpp \{O\}
\par // Can't jump past constructors
\par 
\par class X \{
\par public:
\par   X() \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par void f(int i) \{
\par   if(i < 10) \{
\par    //! goto jump1; // Error: goto bypasses init
\par   \}
\par   X x1;  // Constructor called here
\par  jump1:
\par   switch(i) \{
\par     case 1 :
\par       X x2;  // Constructor called here
\par       break;
\par   //! case 2 : // Error: case bypasses init
\par       X x3;  // Constructor called here
\par       break;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the above code, both the }{\b goto}{ and the }{\b switch}{
 can potentially jump past the sequence point where a constructor is called. That object will then be in scope even if the constructor hasn\rquote t been called, so the compiler gives an error message. This once again guarantees }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {guaranteed initialization}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {initialization\: guaranteed}}}
{that an object cannot be created unless it is also initialized.
\par All the storage allocation discussed here happens, of course, on the stack}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {stack}}}{. The storage is allocated by th
e compiler by moving the stack pointer \ldblquote down\rdblquote  (a relative term, which may indicate an increase or decrease of the actual stack pointer }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {pointer\: stack}}}{value, depending on your machine). Objects can also be allocated on the heap, but that\rquote s the subject of Chapter XX.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373858}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776104}Stash}{
 with constructors and destructors{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373858}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776104}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The examples from previous chapters have obvious functions that map to constructors and destructors: }{\b initialize(\~)}{ and }{\b cleanup(\~)}{. Here
\rquote s the }{\b Stash}{ header using constructors and destructors:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C06:Stash2.h
\par // With constructors & destructors
\par #ifndef STASH2_H
\par #define STASH2_H
\par 
\par class Stash \{
\par   int size;      // Size of each space
\par   int quantity;  // Number of storage spaces
\par   int next;      // Next empty space
\par   // Dynamically allocated array of bytes:
\par   unsigned char* storage;
\par   void inflate(int increase);
\par public:
\par   Stash(int size);
\par   ~Stash();
\par   int add(void* element);
\par   void* fetch(int index);
\par   int count();
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // STASH2_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The only member function definitions that are changed are }{\b initialize(\~)}{ and }{\b cleanup(\~)}{
, which have been replaced with a constructor and destructor:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C06:Stash2.cpp \{O\}
\par // Constructors & destructors
\par #include "Stash2.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <cassert>
\par using namespace std;
\par const int increment = 100;
\par 
\par Stash::Stash(int sz) \{
\par   size = sz;
\par   quantity = 0;
\par   storage = 0;
\par   next = 0;
\par \}
\par 
\par int Stash::add(void* element) \{
\par   if(next >= quantity) // Enough space left?
\par     inflate(increment);
\par   // Copy element into storage,
\par   // starting at next empty space:
\par   int startBytes = next * size;
\par   unsigned char* e = (unsigned char*)element;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < size; i++)
\par     storage[startBytes + i] = e[i];
\par   next++;
\par   return(next - 1); // Index number
\par \}
\par 
\par void* Stash::fetch(int index) \{
\par   assert(0 <= index && index < next);
\par   // Produce pointer to desired element:
\par   return &(storage[index * size]);
\par \}
\par 
\par int Stash::count() \{
\par   return next; // Number of elements in CStash
\par \}
\par 
\par void Stash::inflate(int increase) \{
\par   assert(increase > 0);
\par   int newQuantity = quantity + increase;
\par   int newBytes = newQuantity * size;
\par   int oldBytes = quantity * size;
\par   unsigned char* b = new unsigned char[newBytes];
\par   for(int i = 0; i < oldBytes; i++)
\par     b[i] = storage[i]; // Copy old to new
\par   delete [](storage); // Old storage
\par   storage = b; // Point to new memory
\par   quantity = newQuantity;
\par \}
\par 
\par Stash::~Stash() \{
\par   if(storage != 0) \{
\par    cout << "freeing storage" << endl;
\par    delete []storage;
\par   \}
\par \} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Looking at }{\b inflate(\~)}{, you might ask why the \ldblquote primitive\rdblquote  }{\b assert(\~)}{ is still being used after the }{\b require.h}{ funct
ions have already been introduced. The distinction is important: in this book, }{\b assert(\~)}{ will be used to watch for programmer errors. This makes sense because the output of a failed }{\b assert(\~)}{
 is not particularly end-user friendly and should only be seen by programmers, while the }{\b require.h}{ functions (which will be shown later in the book) are specifically designed to be reasonably useful for end-users.
\par Because }{\b inflate(\~)}{ is private, the only way an }{\b assert(\~)}{ could occur is if one of the other member functions accidentally passed an incorrect value to }{\b inflate(\~)}{. If you are certain this can\rquote 
t happen, you could consider removing the }{\b assert(\~)}{, but you might keep in mind that until the class is stable, there\rquote s always the possibility that new code might be added to the class which could cause errors. The cost of the }{\b assert(
\~)}{ is low (and can be removed by defining }{\b NDEBUG}{) and the value of code robustness is high.
\par Notice, in the following test program, how the definitions for }{\b Stash}{ objects appear right before they are needed, and how the initialization appears as part of the definition, in the constructor argument list:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C06:Stash2Test.cpp
\par //\{L\} Stash2
\par // Constructors & destructors
\par #include "Stash2.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Stash intStash(sizeof(int));
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     intStash.add(&i);
\par   for(int i = 0; i < intStash.count(); i++)
\par     cout << "intStash.fetch(" << i << ") = "
\par          << *(int*)intStash.fetch(i)
\par          << endl;
\par   const int bufsize = 80;
\par   Stash stringStash(sizeof(char) * bufsize);
\par   ifstream in("StashTest3.cpp");
\par   assure(in, "StashTest3.cpp");
\par   string line;
\par   while(getline(in, line))
\par     stringStash.add((char*)line.c_str());
\par   int i = 0;
\par   char* cp;
\par   while((cp = (char*)stringStash.fetch(i++))!=0)
\par     cout << "stringStash.fetch(" << i << ") = "
\par          << cp << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Also notice how the }{\b cleanup(\~)}{ calls have been eliminated, but the destructors are still automatically }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {automatic\: destructor calls}}}{called when }{\b intStash}{ and }{\b stringStash}{ go out of scope.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373859}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776105}Stack}{
 with constructors & destructors{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373859}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776105}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Reimplementing the linked list }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linked list}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {list\: linked}}}{(inside }{\b Stack}{)}{\b  }{with constructors and destructors shows up a significant problem. Here\rquote s th
e modified header file:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C06:Stack3.h
\par // With constructors/destructors
\par #ifndef STACK3_H
\par #define STACK3_H
\par 
\par class Stack3 \{
\par   struct Link \{
\par     void* data;
\par     Link* next;
\par     Link(void* dat, Link* nxt);
\par     ~Link();
\par   \}* head;
\par public:
\par   Stack3();
\par   ~Stack3();
\par   void push(void* dat);
\par   void* peek();
\par   void* pop();
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // STACK3_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that although }{\b Stack3}{ has a constructor and destructor, the nested class }{\b Link}{ does not. This has nothing to do with the fact that it
\rquote s nested. The problem arises when it is used:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C06:Stack3.cpp \{O\}
\par // Constructors/destructors
\par #include "Stack3.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par Stack3::Link::Link(void* dat, Link* nxt) \{
\par   data = dat;
\par   next = nxt;
\par \}
\par 
\par Stack3::Link::~Link() \{
\par   delete data;
\par \}
\par 
\par Stack3::Stack3() \{ head = 0; \}
\par 
\par void Stack3::push(void* dat) \{
\par   head = new Link(dat,head);
\par \}
\par 
\par void* Stack3::peek() \{ return head->data; \}
\par 
\par void* Stack3::pop() \{
\par   if(head == 0) return 0;
\par   void* result = head->data;
\par   Link* oldHead = head;
\par   head = head->next;
\par   delete oldHead;
\par   return result;
\par \}
\par 
\par Stack3::~Stack3() \{
\par   Link* cursor = head;
\par   while(head) \{
\par     cursor = cursor->next;
\par     delete head;
\par     head = cursor;
\par   \}
\par   head = 0; // Officially empty
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The nested }{\b struct Link}{ has its own constructor. The }{\b Link::Link(\~)}{ constructor simply initializes the }{\b data}{ and }{\b next}{
 pointers, so in }{\b Stack3::push(\~)}{ the line
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {head = new Link(dat,head);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {not only allocates a new link, but neatly initializes the pointers for that link.
\par Because the allocation and cleanup are hidden within }{\b Stack3}{ \endash  it\rquote s part of the underlying implementation \endash  you don\rquote t see the effect in the test program:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C06:Stack3Test.cpp
\par //\{L\} Stack3
\par // Constructors/destructors
\par #include "Stack3.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1); // File name is argument
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]);
\par   Stack3 textlines;
\par   string line;
\par   // Read file and store lines in the stack:
\par   while(getline(in, line))
\par     textlines.push(new string(line));
\par   // Pop the lines from the stack and print them:
\par   string* s;
\par   while((s = (string*)textlines.pop()) != 0) \{
\par     cout << s << endl;
\par     delete s; 
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The constructor and destructor for }{\b textlines}{ are ca
lled automatically, so the user of the class can focus on what to do with the object and not worry about whether or not it will be properly initialized and cleaned up.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373860}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776106}Aggregate initialization
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373860}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776106}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {An }{\i aggregate}{ is just what it sounds like: a bunch of things clumped together. This definition includes aggregates of mixed types, like }{\b struct}{
s and }{\b class}{es. An array is an aggregate of a single type.
\par Initializing aggregates can be error-prone and tedious. C++ }{\i aggregate initialization}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {aggregate\: initialization}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {initialization\: aggregate}}}{ makes it much safer. When you create an object that\rquote 
s an aggregate, all you must do is make an assignment, and the initialization will be taken care of by the compiler. This assignment }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {assignment}}}{
comes in several flavors, depending on the type of aggregate you\rquote re dealing with, but in all cases the elements in the assignment must be surrounded by curly braces. For an array of built-in types this is quite simple:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int a[5] = \{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 \};
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you try to give more initializers }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {initialization
\: initializers for array elements}}}{than there are array elements, the compiler gives an error message. But what happens if you give }{\i fewer}{ initializers, such as
\par int b[6] = \{0\};
\par Here, the compiler will use the first initializer for the first array element, and then use zero for all the elements without initializers. Notice this initialization behavior doesn\rquote 
t occur if you define an array without a list of initializers. So the above expression is a very succinct way to initialize }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {initialization\:
 initializing to zero}}}{an array to zero}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {array\: initializing to zero}}}{, without using a }{\b for}{
 loop, and without any possibility of an off-by-one error }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {off-by-one error}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {error\: off-by-one}}}{(Depending on the compiler, it may also be more efficient than the }{\b for}{ loop.)
\par A second shorthand for arrays is }{\i automatic counting}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {automatic\: counting, and arrays}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {counting\: automatic, and arrays}}}{, where you let the compiler determine the size of the array based on the number of initializers:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int c[] = \{ 1, 2, 3, 4 \};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Now if you decide to add another element to the array, you simply add another initializer. If you can set your code up so it needs to be changed in only one spot, you reduce the chance of errors during modification. But how 
do you determine the size of the array? The expression }{\b sizeof c / sizeof *c}{ (size of the entire array divided by the size of the first element) does the trick in a way that doesn\rquote t need to be changed if the array size }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {array\: calculating size}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {calculating array size}}}{
changes:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {for(int i = 0; i < sizeof c / sizeof *c; i++)
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  c[i]++;
