\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Y y2[] = \{ Y(1), Y(2), Y(3) \};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You get three objects and three constructor calls. Any}{\i  }{time you have a constructor, whether it\rquote s a }{\b struct}{ with all members }{\b public}{
 or a }{\b class}{ with }{\b private}{ data members, all the initialization must go through the constructor, even if you\rquote re using aggregate initialization.
\par Here\rquote s a second example showing multiple constructor arguments:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C06:Multiarg.cpp
\par // Multiple constructor arguments
\par // with aggregate initialization
\par 
\par class X \{
\par   int i, j;
\par public:
\par   X(int ii, int jj) \{
\par     i = ii;
\par     j = jj;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   X xx[] = \{ X(1,2), X(3,4), X(5,6), X(7,8) \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that it looks like an explicit but unnamed constructor is called for each object in the array.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373861}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776107}Default constructors
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373861}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776107}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A }{\i default constructor}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {default\: constructor}
}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: default}}}{is one that can be called with no arguments. A default constructor is used to create a \ldblquote vanilla object,
\rdblquote  but it\rquote s also very important when the compiler is told to create an object but isn\rquote t given any details. For example, if you take }{\b Y}{ and use it in a definition like this,
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Y y4[2] = \{ Y(1) \};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {the compiler will complain that it cannot find a default constructor. The second object in the array wants to be created with no arguments, and that\rquote 
s where the compiler looks for a default constructor. In fact, if you simply define an array of }{\b Y}{ objects,
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Y y5[7];
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {or an individual object,
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Y y;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
the compiler will complain because it must have a default constructor to initialize every object in the array. (Remember, if you have a constructor the compiler ensures it is }{\i always}{ called, regardless of the situation.)
\par The default constructor is so important that }{\i if}{ (and only if) there are no constructors for a structure (}{\b struct}{ or }{\b class}{), the compiler will automatically create one for you. So this works:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class Z \{
\par   int i;  // private
\par \}; // No constructor
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Z z, z2[10];
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If any constructors are defined, however, and there\rquote s no default constructor, the above object definitions will generate compile-time errors.
\par You might think that the default constructor should do some intelligent initialization, like setting all the memory for the object to zero. But it doesn\rquote t \endash  that would add extra overhead but be out of the programmer\rquote 
s control. This would mean, for example, that if you compiled C code under C++, the effect would be different. If you want the memory to be initialized to zero, you must do it yourself.
\par The automatic creation of default constructors }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {automatic\: creation of default constructors}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {creating\: automatic default constructors}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {default\:
 automatic creation of constructors}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: automatic creation of default}}}{
was not simply a feature to make life easier for new C++ programmers. It\rquote s virtually required to aid backward compatibility with existing C code, which is a critical issue in C++. In C, it\rquote s not uncommon to create an array of }{\b struct}{
s. Without the default constructor, this would cause a compile-time error in C++.
\par If you had to modify your C code to recompile it under C++ just because of stylistic issues, you might not bother. When you move C code to C++, you wil
l almost always have new compile-time error messages, but those errors are because of genuine bad C code that the C++ compiler can detect because of its stronger rules. In fact, a good way to find obscure errors in a C program is to run it through a C++ c
ompiler.}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {compiling C with C++}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C\:
 compiling with C++}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C++\: compiling C}}}{{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373862}
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776108}Summary{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373862}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776108}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The seemingly elaborate mechanisms provided by C++ should give you a strong hint about the critical importance placed on initialization and cleanup in the languag
e. As Stroustrup was designing C++, one of the first observations he made about productivity in C was that a very significant portion of programming problems are caused by improper initialization of variables. These kinds of bugs are very hard to find, an
d similar issues apply to improper cleanup. Because constructors and destructors allow you to }{\i guarantee}{
 proper initialization and cleanup (the compiler will not allow an object to be created and destroyed without the proper constructor and destructor calls), you get complete control and safety.
\par Aggregate initialization is included in a similar vein \endash  it prevents you from making typical initialization mistakes with aggregates of built-in types and makes your code more succinct.
\par Safety during coding is a big issue in C++. Initialization and cleanup are an important part of this, but you\rquote ll also see other safety issues as the book progresses.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373863}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776109}Exercises
