7: Function overloading & default arguments{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373864}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776110}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {One of the important features in any programming language is the convenient use of names. 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
When you create an object (a variable), you give a name to a region of storage. A function is a name for an action. By using names that you make up to describe the system at hand, you create a program that is easier for people to understand and change. It
\rquote s a lot like writing prose \-\-\endash  the goal is to communicate with your readers.
\par A problem arises when mapping the concept of nuance }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {nuance, and overloading}}}{in human language onto a prog
ramming language. Often, the same word expresses a number of different meanings, depending on context. That is, a single word has multiple meanings \endash  it\rquote s }{\i overloaded}{
. This is very useful, especially when it comes to trivial differences. You say \ldblquote wash the shirt, wash the car.\rdblquote  It would be silly to be forced to say, \ldblquote shirt_wash the shirt, car_wash the car\rdblquote 
 just so the hearer doesn\rquote t have to make any distinction about the action performed. Most human languages are redundant, so even if you miss a few words, you can still determine the meaning. We don\rquote t need unique identifiers \endash 
 we can deduce meaning from context}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {context, and overloading}}}{.
\par Most programming languages, however, require that you have a unique identifier for each function. If you have three different types of data you want to print, }{\b int}{, }{\b char}{, and }{\b float}{
, you generally have to create three different function names, for example, }{\b print_int(\~)}{, }{\b print_char(\~)}{, and }{\b print_float(\~)}{. This loads extra work on you as you write the program, and on readers as they try to understand it.
\par In C++, another factor forces the overloading of function names: the constructor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: and overloading}}}{. Because the constructor\rquote 
s name is predetermined by the name of the class, there can be only one constructor na
me. But what if you want to create an object in more than one way? For example, suppose you build a class that can initialize itself in a standard way and also by reading information from a file. You need two constructors, one that takes no arguments (the
 }{\i default}{ constructor) and one that takes a character array as an argument, which is the name of the file to initialize the object. Both are constructors, so they must have the same name \endash 
 the name of the class. Thus function overloading is essential to allow the same function name, the constructor in this case, to be used with different argument types.
\par Although function overloading is a must for constructors, it\rquote s a general convenience and can be used with any function, not just class member functions. In ad
dition, function overloading means that if you have two libraries that contain functions of the same name, the chances are they won\rquote t conflict as long as the argument lists are different. We\rquote 
ll look at all these factors in detail throughout this chapter.
\par The theme of this chapter is convenient use of function names. Function overloading allows you to use the same name for different functions, but there\rquote s a second way to make calling a function more convenient. What if you\rquote 
d like to call the same function in
 different ways? When functions have long argument lists, it can become tedious to write and confusing to read the function calls when most of the arguments are the same for all the calls. A very commonly used feature in C++ is called }{\i 
default arguments}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {default\: arguments}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
arguments\: default}}}{. A default argument is one the compiler inserts if the person calling a function doesn\rquote t specify it. Thus the calls }{\b f(\ldblquote hello\rdblquote )}{, }{\b f(\ldblquote hi\rdblquote , 1)}{ and }{\b f(\ldblquote howdy
\rdblquote , 2, \lquote c\rquote )}{ can all be calls to the same function. They could also be calls to three overloaded functions, but when the argument lists are this similar, you\rquote ll usually want similar behavior that calls for a single function.

\par Function overloading and default arguments really aren\rquote t very complicated. By the time you reach the end of this chapter, you\rquote ll understand when to use them and the underlying mechanisms used during compiling and linking to implement them.

\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373865}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776111}More name decoration
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373865}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776111}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In Chapter 4 the concept of }{\i name decoration}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
name\: mangling}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {mangling, name}}}{was introduced. (Sometimes the more gentle term }{\i decoration}{ }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {name\: decoration}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {decoration, name}}}{
is used.) In the code
