\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {the function }{\b f(\~)}{ inside the scope of }{\b class X}{ does not clash with the global version of }{\b f(\~)}{. T
he compiler performs this scoping by manufacturing different internal names for the global version of }{\b f(\~)}{ and }{\b X::f(\~)}{. In Chapter 4 it was suggested that the names are simply the class name \ldblquote decorated\rdblquote 
 together with the function name, so the internal names the compiler uses might be }{\b _f}{ and }{\b _X_f}{. It turns out that function name decoration involves more than the class name.
\par Here\rquote s why. Suppose you want to overload two function names
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void print(char);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void print(float);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It doesn\rquote t matter whether they are both inside a class or at the global scope. The compiler can\rquote 
t generate unique internal identifiers if it uses only the scope of the function names. You\rquote d end up with }{\b _print}{ in both cases. The idea of an overloaded function is that you use the same function n
ame, but different argument lists. Thus, for overloading to work the compiler must decorate the names of the argument }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {arguments\: and name mangling}
}}{types with the function name. The above functions, defined at global scope, produce internal names that might look something like }{\b _print_char}{ and }{\b _print_float}{. It\rquote s worth noting there is no standard }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {name\: mangling, no standard for}}}{for the way names must be decorated by the compiler, so you will see very different results from one compiler to another. 
(You can see what it looks like by telling the compiler to generate assembly-language output.) This, of course, causes problems if you want to buy compiled libraries for a particular compiler and linker, but those problems can also exist because of the wa
y different compilers generate code}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {library\: issues with different compilers}}}{.
\par That\rquote s really all there is to function overloading: You can use the same function name for different functions, as long as the argument lists are different. The compiler dec
orates the name, the scope, and the argument lists to produce internal names for it and the linker to use.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373866}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776112}Overloading on return values
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373866}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776112}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It\rquote s common to wonder \ldblquote why just scopes and argument lists? Why not return values?\rdblquote  It seems at first that it would make 
sense to also decorate the return value with the internal function name. Then you could overload on return values}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\: on return values}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {return\: overloading on return values}}}{, as well:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void f();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int f();
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This works fine when the compiler can unequivocally determine the meaning from the context, as in }{\b int x = f(\~);}{. However, in C you\rquote 
ve always been able to call a function and ignore the return value. How can the compiler distinguish which call is meant in this case? Possibly worse is the d
ifficulty the reader has in knowing which function call is meant. Overloading solely on return value is a bit too subtle, and thus isn\rquote t allowed in C++.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373867}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776113}Type-safe linkage
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373867}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776113}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There is an added benefit to all this name decoration. A particularly sticky problem 
in C occurs when the client programmer misdeclares a function, or, worse, a function is called without declaring it first, and the compiler infers the function declaration from the way it is called. Sometimes this function declaration is correct, but when
 it isn\rquote t, it can be a very difficult bug to find.
\par Because all functions }{\i must}{ be declared before they are used in C++, the opportunity for this problem to pop up is greatly diminished. The compiler refuses to declare a function automatically for you, so it\rquote 
s likely you will include the appropriate header file. However, if for some reason you still manage to misdeclare a function, either by declaring it yourself by hand or by including the wrong header file (perhaps one that is out of date), the name-decorat
ion provides a safety net that is often referred to as }{\i type-safe linkage}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type\: type-safe linkage}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linkage\: type-safe}}}{.
\par Consider the following scenario. In one file is the definition for a function:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C07:Def.cpp \{O\}
\par // Function definition
\par void f(int) \{\}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the second file, the function is misdeclared and then called:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C07:Use.cpp
\par //\{L\} Def
\par // Function misdeclaration
\par void f(char);
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par //!  f(1); // Causes a linker error
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Even though you can see that the function is actually }{\b f(int)}{, the compiler doesn\rquote t know this because it was told \endash 
 through an explicit declaration \endash  that the function is }{\b f(char)}{. Thus, the compilation is successful. In C, the linker would also be successful, but }{\i not}{ in C++. Because the compiler decorates
 the names, the definition becomes something like }{\b f_int}{, whereas the use of the function is }{\b f_char}{. When the linker tries to resolve the reference to }{\b f_char}{, it can find only }{\b f_int}{
, and it gives you an error message. This is type-safe linkage. Although the problem doesn\rquote 
t occur all that often, when it does it can be incredibly difficult to find, especially in a large project. This is one of the cases where you can find a difficult error in a C program simply by running it through the C++ compiler.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373868}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776114}Overloading example
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373868}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776114}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Consider the examples we\rquote 
ve been looking at so far in this series, modified to use function overloading. As stated earlier, an immediately useful place for overloading is in constructors. You can see this in the following version of the }{\b Stash}{ class:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C07:Stash3.h
\par // Function overloading
\par #ifndef STASH3_H
\par #define STASH3_H
\par 
\par class Stash \{
\par   int size;      // Size of each space
\par   int quantity;  // Number of storage spaces
\par   int next;      // Next empty space
\par   // Dynamically allocated array of bytes:
\par   unsigned char* storage;
\par   void inflate(int increase);
\par public:
\par   Stash(int size); // Zero quantity
\par   Stash(int size, int initQuant);
\par   ~Stash();
\par   int add(void* element);
\par   void* fetch(int index);
\par   int count();
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // STASH3_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The first }{\b Stash(\~)}{ constructor is the same as before, but the second one has a }{\b Quantity}{
 argument to indicate the initial quantity of storage places to be allocated. In the definition, you can see that the internal value of }{\b quantity}{ is set to zero, along with the }{\b storage}{ pointer:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C07:Stash3.cpp \{O\}
\par // Function overloading
\par #include "Stash3.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <cassert>
\par using namespace std;
\par const int increment = 100;
\par 
\par Stash::Stash(int sz) \{
\par   size = sz;
\par   quantity = 0;
\par   next = 0;
\par   storage = 0;
\par \}
\par 
\par Stash::Stash(int sz, int initQuant) \{
\par   size = sz;
\par   quantity = 0;
\par   next = 0;
\par   storage = 0;
\par   inflate(initQuant);
\par \}
\par 
\par Stash::~Stash() \{
\par   if(storage != 0) \{
\par    cout << "freeing storage" << endl;
\par    delete []storage;
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par int Stash::add(void* element) \{
\par   if(next >= quantity) // Enough space left?
\par     inflate(increment);
\par   // Copy element into storage,
\par   // starting at next empty space:
\par   int startBytes = next * size;
\par   unsigned char* e = (unsigned char*)element;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < size; i++)
\par     storage[startBytes + i] = e[i];
\par   next++;
\par   return(next - 1); // Index number
\par \}
\par 
\par void* Stash::fetch(int index) \{
\par   assert(0 <= index && index < next);
\par   // Produce pointer to desired element:
\par   return &(storage[index * size]);
\par \}
\par 
\par int Stash::count() \{
\par   return next; // Number of elements in CStash
\par \}
\par 
\par void Stash::inflate(int increase) \{
\par   assert(increase > 0);
\par   int newQuantity = quantity + increase;
\par   int newBytes = newQuantity * size;
\par   int oldBytes = quantity * size;
\par   unsigned char* b = new unsigned char[newBytes];
\par   for(int i = 0; i < oldBytes; i++)
\par     b[i] = storage[i]; // Copy old to new
\par   delete [](storage); // Old storage
\par   storage = b; // Point to new memory
\par   quantity = newQuantity;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you use the first constructor no memory is allocated for }{\b storage}{. The allocation happens the first time you try to }{\b add(\~)}{
 an object and any time the current block of memory is exceeded inside }{\b add(\~)}{.
\par This is demonstrated in the test program, which exercises both constructors:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C07:Stash3Test.cpp
\par //\{L\} Stash3
\par // Function overloading
\par #include "Stash3.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Stash intStash(sizeof(int));
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     intStash.add(&i);
\par   for(int i = 0; i < intStash.count(); i++)
\par     cout << "intStash.fetch(" << i << ") = "
\par          << *(int*)intStash.fetch(i)
\par          << endl;
\par   const int bufsize = 80;
\par   Stash stringStash(sizeof(char) * bufsize, 100);
\par   ifstream in("CppLibTest.cpp");
\par   assure(in, "CppLibTest.cpp");
\par   string line;
\par   while(getline(in, line))
\par     stringStash.add((char*)line.c_str());
\par   int i = 0;
\par   char* cp;
\par   while((cp = (char*)stringStash.fetch(i++))!=0)
\par     cout << "stringStash.fetch(" << i << ") = "
\par          << cp << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The constructor call for }{\b stringStash }{
uses a second argument; presumably you know something special about the problem that allows you to choose an initial size for the }{\b Stash}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373869}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776115}Default arguments
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373869}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776115}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Examine the two constructors for }{\b Stash(\~)}{. They don\rquote t seem all that dif
ferent, do they? In fact, the first constructor seems to be the special case of the second one with the initial }{\b size}{
 set to zero. In this situation it seems a bit of a waste of effort to create and maintain two different versions of a similar function.
\par C++ provides a remedy with }{\i default arguments}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {default\: arguments}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {arguments\: default}}}{. A default argument is a value given in the declaration that the compiler automatically inserts if you don\rquote t provide a value in the function call. In the }{\b Stash}{ exam
ple, we can replace the two functions:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  Stash(int size); // Zero quantity
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  Stash(int size, int quantity);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {with the single declaration
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  Stash(int size, int quantity = 0);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b Stash(int)}{ definition is simply removed \endash  all that is necessary is the single }{\b Stash(int, int)}{ definition.
\par Now, the two object definitions
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  Stash A(100), B(100, 0);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {will produce exactly the same results. The identical constructor is called in both cases, but for }{\b A}{
, the second argument is automatically substituted by the compiler when it sees the first argument is an }{\b int}{
 and there is no second argument. The compiler has seen the default argument, so it knows it can still make the function call if it substitutes this second argument, which is what you\rquote ve told it to do by making it a default.
\par Default arguments are a convenience, as function overloading is a convenience. Both features allow you to use a single name in different situations. The difference is that the compiler is substituting arguments when you don\rquote t want to put them in y
ourself. The preceding example is a good place to use default arguments instead of function overloading; otherwise you end up with two or more functions that have similar signatures and similar behaviors. Obviously, if the functions have very different be
haviors, it usually doesn\rquote t make sense to use default arguments.
\par There are two rules you must be aware of when using default arguments. First, only trailing arguments may be defaulted. That is, you can\rquote t have a default argument followed by a nondefault argu
ment. Second, once you start using default arguments, all the remaining arguments must be defaulted. (This follows from the first rule.)
\par Default arguments are only placed in the declaration of a function, which is placed in a header file}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {header\: files}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {file\: header}}}{
. The compiler must see the default value before it can use it. Sometimes people will place the commented values of the default arguments in the function definition, for documentation purposes
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void fn(int x /* = 0 */) \{ // ...
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Default arguments can make arguments declared without identifiers look a bit funny. You can end up with
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void f(int x, int = 0, float = 1.1);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In C++ you don\rquote t need identifiers in the function definition, either:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void f(int x, int, float f) \{ /* ... */ \}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the function body, }{\b x}{ and }{\b f}{
 can be referenced, but not the middle argument, because it has no name. The calls must still use a placeholder, though: }{\b f(1)}{ or }{\b f(1,2,3.0)}{. This syntax allows you to put the argument in as a placeholder without using it. The idea is tha
t you might want to change the function definition to use it later, without changing all the function calls. Of course, you can accomplish the same thing by using a named argument, but if you define the argument for the function body without using it, mos
t compilers will give you a warning message, assuming you\rquote ve made a logical error. By intentionally leaving the argument name out, you suppress this warning. 
\par More important, if you start out using a function argument and later decide that you don\rquote t need it
, you can effectively remove it without generating warnings, and yet not disturb any client code that was calling the previous version of the function.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776116}unions{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776116}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As you\rquote ve seen, the only difference between }{\b struct }{and }{\b class }{in C++ is that }{\b struct }{defaults to }{\b public }{and }{\b class }{
defaults to }{\b private}{. A }{\b struct}{ can also have constructors and destructors, as you might expect. But it turns out that a }{\b union}{ can also have a constructor, destructor, member functions and even access control:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C07:UnionClass.cpp
\par //  Unions with constructors and member functions
\par #include<iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par union U \{
\par private: // Access control too!
\par   int i;
\par   float f;
\par public:  
\par   U(int a) \{ i = a; \}
\par   U(float b) \{ f = b;\}
\par   ~U() \{ f = 0; \}
\par   int read_int() \{ return i; \}
\par   float read_float() \{ return f; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   U X(12), Y(1.9F);
\par   cout << X.read_int() << endl;
\par   cout << Y.read_float() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You might think from the above code that the only difference between a }{\b union}{ and a }{\b class}{ is the way the data is stored. However, a }{\b union}{
 cannot be used as a base class during inheritance, which is quite limiting from an object-oriented design standpoint (you\rquote ll learn about inheritance in Chapter XX).
\par Although the member functions civilize access to the }{\b union}{ somewhat, there is still no way to prevent the client programmer from selecting the wrong element type once the }{\b union}{ is initialized. A \ldblquote safe\rdblquote  }{\b union}{
 can be encapsulated in a class like this. Notice how the }{\b enum}{ clarifies the code, and how overloading comes in handy with the constructors:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C07:SuperVar.cpp
\par // A super-variable
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class SuperVar \{
\par   enum \{
\par     character,
\par     integer,
\par     floating_point
\par   \} vartype;  // Define one
\par   union \{  // Anonymous union
\par     char c;
\par     int i;
\par     float f;
\par   \};
\par public:
\par   SuperVar(char ch) \{
\par     vartype = character;
\par     c = ch;
\par   \}
\par   SuperVar(int ii) \{
\par     vartype = integer;
\par     i = ii;
\par   \}
\par   SuperVar(float ff) \{
\par     vartype = floating_point;
\par     f = ff;
\par   \}
\par   void print();
\par \};
\par 
\par void SuperVar::print() \{
\par   switch (vartype) \{
\par     case character:
\par       cout << "character: " << c << endl;
\par       break;
\par     case integer:
\par       cout << "integer: " << i << endl;
\par       break;
\par     case floating_point:
\par       cout << "float: " << f << endl;
\par       break;
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   SuperVar A('c'), B(12), C(1.44F);
\par   A.print();
\par   B.print();
\par   C.print();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the above code, the }{\b enum}{
 has no type name (it is an untagged enumeration). This is acceptable if you are going to immediately define instances of the }{\b enum}{, as is done here. There is no need to refer to the }{\b enum\rquote s}{ type in the future, so the type is optional.

\par The }{\b union}{ has no type name and no variable name. This is called an }{\i anonymous union}{, and creates space for the }{\b union}{ but doesn\rquote t require accessing the }{\b union}{
 elements with a variable name and the dot operator. For instance, if your anonymous }{\b union}{ is:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {union \{ int i, float f \};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {you access members by saying:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {i = 12;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {f = 1.22;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {just like other variables. The only difference is that both variables occupy the same space. If the anonymous }{\b union}{
 is at file scope (outside all functions and classes) then it must be declared }{\b static}{ so it has internal linkage.}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {union\:
 anonymous at file scope}}}{
\par Although }{\b SuperVar}{ is now safe, its value is a bit dubious because the reason for using a }{\b union}{ in the first place is to save space, and the addition of }{\b vartype}{
 takes up quite a bit of space, so the savings are effectively eliminated. There are a couple of alternatives to make this scheme workable: if the }{\b vartype}{ was controlling more than one }{\b union}{ instance \endash  if they were all the same type 
\endash  then you\rquote d only need one for the group and it wouldn\rquote t take up more space. A more useful approach is to have }{\b #ifdef}{s around all the }{\b vartype}{ code so you can use it during development and test
ing to guarantee things are being used correctly, and then eliminate the extra space and time overhead for shipping code.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373871}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776117}Summary
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373871}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776117}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Both function overloading and default arguments provide a convenience for calling function names. It can seem confusing at tim
es to know which technique to use. For example, in the }{\b BitVector}{ class it seems like the two }{\b bits(\~)}{ functions could be combined into a single version:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int bits(int sz = -1);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you called it without an argument, the function would check for the -1 defaul
t and interpret that as meaning that you wanted it to tell you the current number of bits. The use appears to be the same as the previous scheme. However, there are a number of significant differences that jump out, or at least should make you feel uncomf
ortable.
\par Inside }{\b bits(\~)}{ you\rquote ll have to do a conditional based on the value of the argument. If you have to }{\i look}{
 for the default rather than treating it as an ordinary value, that should be a clue that you will end up with two different functions inside one:
 one version for the normal case, and one for the default. You might as well split it up into two distinct function bodies and let the compiler do the selection. This results in a slight increase in efficiency, because the extra argument isn\rquote 
t passed and the extra code for the conditional isn\rquote t executed. The slight efficiency increase for two functions could make a difference if you call the function many times.
\par You do lose something when you use a default argument in this case. First, the default has to be something you wouldn\rquote t ordinarily use, -1 in this case. Now you can\rquote 
t tell if a negative number is an accident or a default substitution. Second, there\rquote s only one return value with a single function, so the compiler loses the information that was available for the overloaded functions. Now, if you say
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int i = bv1.set(10);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {the compiler will accept it and no longer sees something that you, as the class designer, might want, to be an error.
\par And consider the plight of the client programmer, always. Which desi
gn will make more sense to users of your class as they peruse the header file? What does a default argument of -1 suggest? Not much. The two separate functions are much clearer because one takes a value and doesn\rquote 
t return anything and the other doesn\rquote t take a value but returns something. Even without documentation, it\rquote s far easier to guess what the two different functions do.
\par As a guideline, you shouldn\rquote t use a default argument as a flag upon which to conditionally execute code. You should instead break the
 function into two or more overloaded functions if you can. A default argument should be a value you would ordinarily put in that position. It\rquote 
s a value that is more likely to occur than all the rest, so client programmers can generally ignore it or use it only if they want to change it from the default value.
\par The default argument is included to make function calls easier, especially when those functions have many arguments with typical values. Not only is it much easier to write the calls, it\rquote s easier to r
ead them, especially if the class creator can order the arguments so the least-modified defaults appear latest in the list.
\par An especially important use of default arguments is when you start out with a function with a set of arguments, and after it\rquote s been 
used for a while you discover you need to add arguments. By defaulting all the new arguments, you ensure that all client code using the previous interface is not disturbed. }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: expanding the function interface}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {interface\: expanding function interface}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373872}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776118}Exercises
