\par }}\pard\plain \s1\fi-1080\li990\sa360\sl240\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \cbpat8 \f4\fs96\cf1\kerning18\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628674}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373902}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776119}8: Constants
{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628674}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373902}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776119}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {The concept of }{\i constant}{ (expressed by the }{\b const}{ keyword) was created to allow the programmer to draw a line between what changes and what doesn\rquote t.

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This provides safety and control in a C++ programming project. Since its origin, it has taken on a number of different purposes. In the meantime it trickled back into the C
 language where its meaning was changed. All this can seem a bit confusing at first, and in this chapter you\rquote ll learn when, why, and how to use the }{\b const}{ keyword. At the end there\rquote s a discussion of }{\b volatile}{
, which is a near cousin to }{\b const}{ (because they both concern change) and has identical syntax.
\par The first motivation for }{\b const}{ seems to have been to eliminate the use of preprocessor }{\b #define}{s for value substitution. It has since been put to use for pointers, function arguments, and return types, and class 
objects and member functions. All of these have slightly different but conceptually compatible meanings and will be looked at in separate sections.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628675}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373903}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776120}Value substitution
{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628675}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373903}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776120}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When programming in C, the preprocessor }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {preprocessor\: value substitution}}}{is liberal
ly used to create macros and to substitute values. }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {substitution, value}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {value\: preprocessor value substitution}}}{
 Because the preprocessor simply does text replacement and has no concept nor facility for type checking, preprocessor value substitution introduces subtle problems that can be avoided in C++ by using }{\b const}{ }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const}}}{values.
\par The typical use of the preprocessor to substitute values for names in C looks like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#define BUFSIZE 100
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b BUFSIZE}{ is a name that doesn\rquote t occupy storage and can be placed in a header file to provide a single value for all translation units that use it. It\rquote 
s very important to use value substitution instead of so-called \ldblquote magic numbers\rdblquote }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {magic numbers}}}{ to support code maintenance. If you use magic numbers in you
r code, not only does the reader have no idea where the numbers come from or what they represent, but if you decide to change a value, you must perform hand editing, and you have no trail to follow to ensure you don\rquote t miss one.
\par Most of the time, }{\b BUFSIZE}{ will behave like an ordinary variable, but not all the time. In addition, there\rquote s no type information. This can hide bugs that are very difficult to find. C++ uses }{\b const}{
 to eliminate these problems by bringing value substitution into the domain of the compiler. Now you can say
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {const int bufsize = 100;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can use }{\b bufsize }{anyplace where the compiler must know the value at compile time }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constant\: compile time}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {compile time\: constants}}}{so it can perform }{\i constant folding}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constant\: folding}}}{, which m
eans the compiler will reduce a complex constant expression to a simple one by performing the necessary calculations at compile time. This is especially important in array definitions:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {char buf[bufsize];
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can use }{\b const}{ for all the built-in types (}{\b char}{, }{\b int}{, }{\b float}{, and }{\b double}{) and their variants (as well as class objects, as you\rquote 
ll see later in this chapter). You should always use }{\b const}{ instead of }{\b #define}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {#define}}}{ value substitution.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373904}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776121}const}{ in header files{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373904}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776121}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To use }{\b const}{ instead of }{\b #define}{, you must be able to place }{\b const}{ definitions inside header files }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {header\: files}}}{
as you can with }{\b #define}{. This way, you can place the definition for a }{\b const}{ in a single place and distribute it to a translation unit by including the header file. A }{\b const}{ in C++ defaults to }{\i internal linkage}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linkage\: internal}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {internal linkage}}}{
; that is, it is visible only within the file where it is defined and cannot be seen at link time by other translation units. You must always assign a value to a }{\b const}{ when you define it, }{\i except}{ when you make an explicit declaration using }{
\b extern}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {extern}}}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {extern const bufsize;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The C++ compiler avoids creating storage for a }{\b const}{, but instead holds the definition in its symbol table, although the above }{\b extern}{ forces storage to be all
ocated, as do certain other cases, such as taking the address of a }{\b const}{. When the }{\b const}{ is used, it is folded in at compile time.
\par Of course, this goal of never allocating storage for a }{\b const}{ cannot always be achieved, especially with complicated structures. In these cases, the compiler creates storage, which prevents constant folding. This is why }{\b const}{
 must default to internal linkage, that is, linkage only }{\i within}{ that particular translation unit; otherwise, linker errors would occur with complicated }{\b const}{s because they allocate storage in multiple }{\b cpp}{
 files. The linker sees the same definition in multiple object files, and complains. However, a }{\b const}{ defaults to internal linkage, so the linker doesn\rquote t try to link those definitions across translation units,
 and there are no collisions. With built-in types, which are used in the majority of cases involving constant expressions, the compiler can always perform constant folding.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373905}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776122}Safety }{\b const}{s{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373905}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776122}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The use of }{\b const}{ is not limited to replacing }{\b #define}{s in constant exp
ressions. If you initialize a variable with a value that is produced at runtime and you know it will not change for the lifetime of that variable, it is good programming practice to make it a }{\b const}{
 so the compiler will give you an error message if you accidentally try to change it. Here\rquote s an example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C08:Safecons.cpp
\par // Using const for safety
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par const int i = 100;  // Typical constant
\par const int j = i + 10; // Value from const expr
\par long address = (long)&j; // Forces storage
\par char buf[j + 10]; // Still a const expression
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   cout << "type a character & CR:";
\par   const char c = cin.get(); // Can't change
\par   const char c2 = c + 'a';
\par   cout << c2;
\par   // ...
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can see that }{\b i}{ is a compile-time }{\b const}{, but }{\b j}{ is calculated from }{\b i}{. However, because }{\b i}{ is a }{\b const}{, the calculated value for }{\b j}{
 still comes from a constant expression and is itself a compile-time constant. The very next line requires the address of }{\b j}{ and therefore forces the compiler to allocate storage for }{\b j}{. Yet this doesn\rquote t prevent the use of }{\b j}{
 in the determination of the size of }{\b buf}{ because the compiler knows }{\b j}{ is }{\b const}{ and that the value is valid even if storage was allocated to hold that value at some point in the program.
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{, you see a different kind of }{\b const}{ in the identifier }{\b c}{ because the value cannot be known at compile time. This means storage is required, and the compiler doesn\rquote 
t attempt to keep anything in its symbol table (the same behavior as in C). The initialization must still happen at the point of definition, and once the initialization occurs, the value cannot be changed. You can see that }{\b c2}{ is calculated from }{
\b c}{ and also that scoping works for }{\b const}{s}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {scoping\: consts}}}{ as it does for any other type \endash  yet another improvement over the use of }{\b #define}{.
\par As a matter of practice, if you think a value shouldn\rquote t change, you should make it a }{\b const}{. This not only provides insurance against inadvertent changes, it also allows the compiler to generate more efficient code by eliminating s
torage and memory reads. 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373906}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776123}Aggregates{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373906}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776123}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It\rquote s possible to use }{\b const}{ for aggregates, }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {aggregate\: const aggregates}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: for aggregates}}}{but you\rquote re virtually assured that the compiler will not be sophisticated enough to keep an aggregate in its symbol table, so storage will be allocated. In these situations, }{\b const}{ means 
\ldblquote a piece of storage that cannot be changed.\rdblquote  However, the value cannot be used at compile time because the compiler is not required to know the contents of storage at compile time. Thus, you cannot say
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C08:Constag.cpp \{O\}
\par // Constants and aggregates
\par 
\par const int i[] = \{ 1, 2, 3, 4 \};
\par 
\par //! float f[i[3]]; // Illegal
\par 
\par struct S \{ int i, j; \};
\par 
\par const S s[] = \{ \{ 1, 2 \}, \{ 3, 4 \} \};
\par 
\par //! double d[s[1].j]; // Illegal
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In an array defini
tion, the compiler must be able to generate code that moves the stack pointer to accommodate the array. In both of the illegal definitions, the compiler complains because it cannot find a constant expression in the array definition.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373907}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776124}Differences with C{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373907}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776124}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {[[ This section needs rewriting to finish incorporating material taken from elsewhere ]]
\par Constants were introduced in early versions of C++ while the Standard C specification was still being finished. It was then seen as a good idea and included in C. But somehow, }{\b const}{ in C }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {C\: const}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: in C}}}{came to mean \ldblquote an ordinary variable that cannot be changed.\rdblquote 
 In C, it always occupies storage and its name is global. The C compiler cannot treat a }{\b const }{as a compile-time constant. In C, if you say
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {const bufsize = 100;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {char buf[bufsize];
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {you will get an error, even though it seems like a rational thing to do. Because }{\b bufsize}{ occupies storage somewhere, the C compiler cannot know the value at compile time. You can optionally say

\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {const bufsize;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {in C, but not in C++, and the C compiler accepts it as a declaration indicating there is storage allocated elsewhere. Because C defaults to external linkage }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linkage\: external}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {external\: linkage}}}{for }{\b const}{s, this makes sense. C++ defaults to internal linkage }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {linkage\: internal}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {internal linkage}}}{for }{\b const}{s so if you want to accomplish the same thing in C++, you must explicitly change the linkage to external using }{\b extern}{:

\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {extern const bufsize; // Declaration only
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This line also works in C.
\par In C++, a }{\b const}{ doesn\rquote t necessarily create storage. In C a }{\b const}{ always creates storage. Whether or not storage is reserved for a }{\b const}{ in C++ depends on how it is used. In general, if a }{\b const}{
 is used simply to replace a name with a value (just as you would use a }{\b #define}{), then storage doesn\rquote t have to be created for the }{\b const}{
. If no storage is created (this depends on the complexity of the data type and the sophistication of the compiler), the values may be folded into the code for greater efficiency after type checking, not before, as with }{\b #define}{
. If, however, you take an address of a }{\b const}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: address of}}}{ (even unknowingly, by passing it to a function that takes a reference argument) or you define it as }{\b extern}{
, then storage is created for the }{\b const}{.
\par In C++, a }{\b const}{ that is outside all functions has file scope}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {file\: file scope}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {scope\: file}}}{
 (i.e., it is invisible outside the file). That is, it defaults to internal linkage. This is very different from all other identifiers in C++ (and from }{\b const}{ in C!) that default to external linkage. Thus, if you declare a }{\b const}{
 of the same name in two different files and you don\rquote t take the address or define that name as extern, the ideal compiler won\rquote t allocate storage for the }{\b const}{, but simply fold it into the code}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constant\: folding}}}{ (admittedly very difficult for complicated types). Because }{\b const}{ has implied file scope, you can put it in header files (in C++ only) with no conflicts at link time.
\par Since a }{\b const}{ in C++ defaults to internal linkage}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {internal linkage}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linkage\: internal}}}{, you can\rquote t just define a 
}{\b const}{ in one file and reference it as an extern in another file. To give a }{\b const}{ external linkage}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {external linkage}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
linkage\: external}}}{ so it can be referenced from another file, you must explicitly define it as extern}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {extern}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: extern}
}}{, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {extern const int x = 1;
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that by giving it an initializer and saying it is extern, you force storage to be created for the }{\b const}{ (although the compiler still has the option of do
ing constant folding here). The initialization establishes this as a definition, not a declaration. The declaration:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {extern const int x;
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {in C++ means that the definition exists elsewhere (again, this is not necessarily true in C). You can now see why C++ requires a }{\b const}{
 definition to have an initializer: the initializer distinguishes a declaration from a definition (in C it\rquote s always a definition, so no initializer is necessary). With an external }{\b const}{ declaration, the compiler cannot do constant folding be
cause it doesn\rquote t know the value.
\par The C approach to }{\b const}{ is not very useful, and if you want to use a named value inside a constant expression (one that must be evaluated at compile time), C almost }{\i forces }{you to use }{\b #define}{ in the preprocessor.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628676}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373908}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776125}Pointers{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628676}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373908}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776125}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Pointers can be made }{\b const}{. The compiler will still endeavor to prevent storage allocation and do constant folding when dealing with }{\b const}{ pointers}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {pointer\: and const}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: and pointers}}}{, but these features seem less useful in this case. More imp
ortantly, the compiler will tell you if you attempt changes using such a pointer later in your code, which adds a great deal of safety.
\par When using }{\b const}{ with pointers, you have two options: }{\b const}{ can be applied to what the pointer is pointing to, or the }{\b const}{
 can be applied to the address stored in the pointer itself. The syntax for these is a little confusing at first but becomes comfortable with practice.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373909}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776126}Pointer to }{\b const}{{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373909}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776126}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The trick with a pointer definition, as with any complicated definition, is to read it starting at the identifier and working your way out. The }{\b const}{ specifier binds to the thing it is 
\ldblquote closest to.\rdblquote  So if you want to prevent any changes to the element you are pointing to, you write a definition like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {const int* x;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Starting from the identifier, we read \ldblquote }{\b x}{ is a pointer, which points to a }{\b const}{ }{\b int}{.\rdblquote  Here, no initialization is required because you\rquote re saying that }{
\b x}{ can point to anything (that is, it is not }{\b const}{), but the thing it points to cannot be changed.
\par Here\rquote s the mildly confusing part. You might think that to make the pointer itself unchangeable, that is, to prevent any change to the address contained inside }{\b x}{, you would simply move the }{\b const}{ to the other side of the }{\b int}{
 like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int const* x;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It\rquote s not all that crazy to think that this should read \ldblquote }{\b x}{ is a }{\b const}{ pointer to an }{\b int}{.\rdblquote  However, the way it }{\i actually}{ reads is \ldblquote }{\b x}
{ is an ordinary pointer to an }{\b int}{ that happens to be }{\b const}{.\rdblquote  That is, the }{\b const}{ has bound itself to the }{\b int}{ again, and the effect is the same as the previous defi
nition. The fact that these two definitions are the same is the confusing point; to prevent this confusion on the part of your reader, you should probably stick to the first form.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373910}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776127}const}{ pointer{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373910}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776127}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To make the pointer itself a }{\b const}{, you must place the }{\b const}{ specifier to the right of the }{\b *}{, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int d = 1;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int* const x = &d;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\i Now}{ it reads: \ldblquote }{\b x}{ is a pointer, which is }{\b const}{ that points to an }{\b int}{.\rdblquote  Because the pointer itself is now the }{\b const}{
, the compiler requires that it be given an initial value that will be unchanged for the life of that pointer. It\rquote s OK, however, to change what that value points to by saying 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {*x = 2;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can also make a }{\b const}{ pointer to a }{\b const}{ object using either of two legal forms:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int d = 1;
\par const int* const x = &d;  // (1)
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int const* const x2 = &d; // (2)
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now neither the pointer nor the object can be changed.
\par Some people argue that the second form is more consistent because the }{\b const}{ is always placed to the right of what it modifies. You\rquote ll have to decide which is clearer for your particular coding style.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Formatting
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This book makes a point of only putting one pointer definition on a line, and initializing each pointer at the point of definition whenever possible. Because of this, the formatting style of 
\ldblquote attaching\rdblquote  the \lquote }{\b *}{\rquote  to the data type is possible:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int* u = &w;
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\i as if}{ }{\b int*}{ were a discrete type unto itself. This makes the code easier to understand, but unfortunately that\rquote s not actually the way things work. The \lquote }{\b *}{\rquote 
 in fact binds to the identifier, not the type. It can be placed anywhere between the type name and the identifier. So you can do this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int* u = &w, v = 0;
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {which creates an }{\b int* u}{, as before, and a nonpointer }{\b int v}{. Because readers often find this confusing, it is best to follow the form shown in this book.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373911}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776128}Assignment and type checking{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373911}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776128}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {C++ is very particular about type checking, and this extends to pointer assignments. You can assign the address of a non-}{\b const}{ object to a }{\b const}{ pointer because you\rquote 
re simply promising not to change something that is OK to change. However, you can\rquote t assign the address of a }{\b const}{ object to a non-}{\b const}{ pointer because then you\rquote 
re saying you might change the object via the pointer. Of course, you can always use a cast to force such an assignment, but this is bad programming practice because you are then breaking the }{\b const}{
ness of the object, along with any safety promised by the }{\b const}{. For example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int d = 1;
\par const int e = 2;
\par int* u = &d; // OK -- d not const
\par int* v = &e; // Illegal -- e const
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int* w = (int*)&e; // Legal but bad practice
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Although C++ helps prevent errors it, does not protect you from yourself if you want to break the safety mechanisms.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Character array literals
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The place where strict }{\b const}{ness is not enforced is with character array literals}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: and string literals}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {string\: literals}}}{. You can say
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {char* cp = "howdy";
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {and the compiler will accept it without complaint. This is technically an error because a character array literal (}{\b \ldblquote howdy\rdblquote }{
 in this case) is created by the compiler as a constant character array, and the result of the quoted character array is its starting address in memory.
\par So character array literals are actually constant character arrays. Of course, the compiler lets you get away with treating them as non-}{\b const}{ because there\rquote s so much existing C code that relies
 on this. However, if you try to change the values in a character array literal, the behavior is undefined, although it will probably work on many machines.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628677}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373912}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776129}Function arguments \line & return values
{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628677}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373912}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776129}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The use of }{\b const}{ to specify function arguments }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {arguments\: const}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
function\: const function arguments}}}{and return values}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: for function arguments and return values}}}{
 is another place where the concept of constants can be confusing. If you are passing objects }{\i by value}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {passing\: objects by value}}}{, specifying }{\b const}{
 has no meaning to the client (it means that the passed argument cannot be modified inside the function). If you are returning an object of a user-defined type by value as a }{\b const}{, it means the returned value cannot be modified. If you are p
assing and returning }{\i addresses}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {passing\: and returning addresses}}}{, }{\b const}{ is a promise that the destination of the address will not be changed.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373913}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776130}Passing by }{\b const}{ value{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373913}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776130}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can specify that function arguments are }{\b const}{ when passing them by value, such as
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void f1(const int i) \{
\par   i++; // Illegal -- compile-time error
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {but what does this mean? You\rquote re making a promise that the original value of the variable will not be changed by the function }{\b x(\~)}{
. However, because the argument is passed by value, you immediately make a copy of the original variable, so the promise to the client is implicitly kept.
\par Inside the function, the }{\b const}{ takes on meaning: the argument cannot be changed. So it\rquote s really a tool for the creator of the function, and not the caller.
\par To avoid confusion to the caller, you can make the argument a }{\b const}{ }{\i inside}{ the function, rather than in the argument list. You could do this with a pointer, but a nicer syntax is achieved with the }{\i reference}{
, a subject that will be fully developed in Chapter XX. Briefly, a reference is like a constant pointer that is automatically dereferenced, so it has the effect of being an alias to an object. To create a reference, you use the }{\b &}{
 in the definition. So the nonconfusing function definition looks like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void f2(int ic) \{
\par   const int& i = ic;
\par   i++;  // Illegal -- compile-time error
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Again, you\rquote ll get an error message, but this time the }{\b const}{ness of the local object is not part of the function signature; it only has meaning to the implementation of the function so it
\rquote s hidden from the client.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373914}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776131}Returning by }{\b const}{ value{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373914}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776131}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {return\: returning by const value}}}{A similar truth holds for the return value. If you return by value from a function, as a }{\b const}{

\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {const int g();
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {you are promising that the original variable (inside the function frame) will not be modified. And again, because you\rquote re returning it by value, it\rquote 
s copied so the original value is automatically not modified.
\par At first, this can make the specification of }{\b const}{ seem meaningless. You can see the apparent lack of effect of returning }{\b const}{s by value in this example: 
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C08:Constval.cpp
\par // Returning consts by value
\par // has no meaning for built-in types
\par 
\par int f3() \{ return 1; \}
\par const int f4() \{ return 1; \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   const int j = f3(); // Works fine
\par   int k = f4(); // But this works fine too!
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {For built-in types, it doesn\rquote t matter whether you return by value as a }{\b const}{, so you should avoid confusing the client programmer by leaving off the }{\b const}{
 when returning a built-in type by value.
\par Returning by value as a }{\b const}{ becomes important when you\rquote re dealing with user-defined types. If a function returns a class object by value as a }{\b const}{, the return value of that function cannot be an lvalue }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {lvalue}}}{(that is, it cannot be assigned to or otherwise modified). For example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C08:Constret.cpp
\par // Constant return by value
\par // Result cannot be used as an lvalue
\par 
\par class X \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   X(int ii = 0) \{ i = ii; \}
\par   void modify() \{ i++; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par X f5() \{
\par   return X();
\par \}
\par 
\par const X f6() \{
\par   return X();
\par \}
\par 
\par void f7(X& x) \{ // Pass by non-const reference
\par   x.modify();
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   f5() = X(1); // OK -- non-const return value
\par   f5().modify(); // OK
\par // Causes compile-time errors:
\par //!  f7(f5());
\par //!  f6() = X(1);
\par //!  f6().modify();
\par //!  f7(f6());
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b f5(\~)}{ returns a non-}{\b const}{ }{\b X}{ object, while }{\b f6(\~)}{ returns a }{\b const X}{ object. Only the non-}{\b const}{ return value can be used as an lvalue. Thus, it\rquote 
s important to use }{\b const }{when returning an object by value if you want to prevent its use as an lvalue.
\par The reason }{\b const}{ has no meaning when you\rquote re returning a built-in type by value is that the compiler already prevents it from being an lvalue (because it\rquote s always a value, and not a variable). Only when you\rquote 
re returning objects of user-defined types by value does it become an issue.
\par The function }{\b f7(\~)}{ takes its argument as a non-}{\b const}{ }{\i reference}{ (an additional way of handling addresses in C++ which is the subject of Chapter XX). This is effectively the same as taking a non-}{\b const}{ pointer; it\rquote 
s just that the syntax is different. The reason this won\rquote t compile in C++ is because of the creation of a temporary.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Temporaries
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Sometimes, during the evaluation of an expression, the compiler must create }{\i temporary objects}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: temporary}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {temporary\: object}}}{. These are objects like any other: they require storage and they must be constructed and destroyed. The difference is that you never see them \endash 
 the compiler is responsible for deciding that they\rquote re needed and the details of their existence. But there is one thing about temporaries: they\rquote re automatically }{\b const}{. Because you usually won\rquote 
t be able to get your hands on a temporary object, telling it to do something that will change that temporary is almost certainly a mistake because you won\rquote t be able to use that information. By making all temporaries automatically }{\b const}{
, the compiler informs you when you make that mistake.
\par In the above example, }{\b f5(\~)}{ returns a non-}{\b const}{ }{\b X}{ object. But in the expression:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {f7(f5());
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {the compiler must manufacture a temporary object to hold the return value of }{\b f5(\~)}{ so it can be passed to }{\b f7(\~)}{. This would be fine if }{\b f7(\~)}{ took it\rquote 
s argument by value; then the temporary would be copied into }{\b f7(\~)}{ and it wouldn\rquote t matter what happened to the temporary }{\b X}{. However, }{\b f7(\~)}{ takes its argument }{\i by reference}{, whic
h means in this example takes the address of the temporary }{\b X}{. Since }{\b f7(\~)}{ doesn\rquote t take it\rquote s argument by }{\b const}{
 reference, it has permission to modify the temporary object. But the compiler knows that the temporary will vanish as soon as the expression evaluation is complete, and thus any modifications you make to the temporary }{\b X}{
 will be lost. By making all temporary objects automatically }{\b const}{, this situation causes a compile-time error so you don\rquote t get caught by what would be a very difficult bug to find.
\par The way the constness of class objects is preserved is shown later in the chapter.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373915}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776132}Passing and returning addresses{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373915}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776132}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {passing\: and returning addresses}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {addresses\: passing and returning}}}{
If you pass or return a pointer (or a reference), it\rquote s possible for the client programmer to take the pointer and modify the original value. If you make the pointer a }{\b const}{
, you prevent this from happening, which may be an important factor. In fact, whenever you\rquote re passing an address into a function, you should make it a }{\b const}{ if at all possible. If you don\rquote t, you\rquote 
re excluding the possibility of using that function with a pointer to a }{\b const}{.
\par The choice of whether to return a pointer to a }{\b const}{ depends on what you want to allow your client programmer to do with it. Here\rquote s an example that demonstrates the use of }{\b const}{ pointers as function arguments and return values:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C08:Constp.cpp
\par // Constant pointer arg/return
\par 
\par void t(int*) \{\}
\par 
\par void u(const int* cip) \{
\par //!  *cip = 2; // Illegal -- modifies value
\par   int i = *cip; // OK -- copies value
\par //!  int* ip2 = cip; // Illegal: non-const
\par \}
\par 
\par const char* v() \{
\par   // Returns address of static character array:
\par   return "result of function v()";
\par \}
\par 
\par const int* const w() \{
\par   static int i;
\par   return &i;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int x = 0;
\par   int* ip = &x;
\par   const int* cip = &x;
\par   t(ip);  // OK
\par //!  t(cip); // Not OK
\par   u(ip);  // OK
\par   u(cip); // Also OK
\par //!  char* cp = v(); // Not OK
\par   const char* ccp = v(); // OK
\par //!  int* ip2 = w(); // Not OK
\par   const int* const ccip = w(); // OK
\par   const int* cip2 = w(); // OK
\par //!  *w() = 1; // Not OK
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The function }{\b t(\~)}{ takes an ordinary non-}{\b const}{ pointer as an argument, and }{\b u(\~)}{ takes a }{\b const}{ pointer. Inside }{\b u(\~)}{
 you can see that attempting to modify the destination of the }{\b const}{ pointer is illegal, but you can of course copy the information out into a non-}{\b const}{ variable. The compiler also prevents you from creating a non-}{\b const}{
 pointer using the address stored inside a }{\b const}{ pointer.
\par The functions }{\b v(\~)}{ and }{\b w(\~)}{ test return value semantics}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {semantics, return value}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {return\: return value semantics}
}}{. }{\b v(\~)}{ returns a }{\b const}{ }{\b char*}{ that is created from a character array literal. This statement actually produces the address of the character array literal, after the compiler creates it and stores it in the static
 storage area. As mentioned earlier, this character array is technically a constant, which is properly expressed by the return value of }{\b v(\~)}{.
\par The return value of }{\b w(\~)}{ requires that both the pointer and what it points to be a }{\b const}{. As with }{\b v(\~)}{, the value returned by }{\b w(\~)}{ is valid after the function returns only because it is }{\b static}{
. You never want to return pointers to local stack variables because they will be invalid after the function returns and the stack is cleaned up. (Another common pointer you migh
t return is the address of storage allocated on the heap, which is still valid after the function returns.)
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{, the functions are tested with various arguments. You can see that }{\b t(\~)}{ will accept a non-}{\b const}{ pointer argument, but if you try to pass it a pointer to a }{\b const}{, there\rquote s no promise that }{\b t(\~)}{
 will leave the pointer\rquote s destination alone, so the compiler gives you an error message. }{\b u(\~)}{ takes a }{\b const}{ pointer, so it will accept both types of arguments. Thus, a function that takes a }{\b const}{ pointer
 is more general than one that does not.
\par As expected, the return value of }{\b v(\~)}{ can be assigned only to a }{\b const}{ pointer. You would also expect that the compiler refuses to assign the return value of }{\b w(\~)}{ to a non-}{\b const}{ pointer, and accepts a }{\b const int* const}{
, but it might be a bit surprising to see that it also accepts a }{\b const int*}{
, which is not an exact match to the return type. Once again, because the value (which is the address contained in the pointer) is being copied, the promise that the original variable is untouched is automatically kept. Thus, the second }{\b const}{ in }{
\b const}{ }{\b int* const}{ is only meaningful when you try to use it as an lvalue, in which case the compiler prevents you.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Standard argument passing
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In C it\rquote s very common to pass by value, and when y
ou want to pass an address your only choice is to use a pointer. However, neither of these approaches is preferred in C++. Instead, your first choice when passing an argument is to pass by reference, and by }{\b const}{
 reference at that. To the client programmer, the syntax is identical to that of passing by value, so there\rquote s no confusion about pointers \endash  they don\rquote 
t even have to think about the problem. For the creator of the class, passing an address is virtually always more efficient than passing an entire class object, and if you pass by }{\b const}{
 reference it means your function will not change the destination of that address, so the effect from the client programmer\rquote s point of view is exactly the same as pass-by-value.
\par Because of the syntax of references (it looks like pass-by-value) it\rquote s possible to pass a temporary}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {temporary\: passing a temporary object to a function}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {passing\: temporaries}}}{ object to a function that takes a reference, whereas you can never pass a temporary object to a function that takes a pointer \endash 
 the address must be explicitly taken. So passing by reference produces a new situation that never occurs in C: a temporary, which is always }{\b const}{, can have its }{\i address}{
 passed to a function. This is why, to allow temporaries to be passed to functions by reference the argument must be a }{\b const}{ reference}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: const reference arguments}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: const reference function arguments}}}{. The following example demonstrates this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C08:Consttmp.cpp
\par // Temporaries are const
\par 
\par class X \{\};
\par 
\par X f() \{ return X(); \} // Return by value
\par 
\par void g1(X&) \{\} // Pass by non-const reference
\par void g2(const X&) \{\} // Pass by const reference
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   // Error: const temporary created by f():
\par //!  g1(f());
\par   // OK: g2 takes a const reference:
\par   g2(f());
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628678}f(\~)}{ returns an object of }{\b class X}{ }{\i by value}{. That means when you immediately take the return value of }{\b f(\~)}{
 and pass it to another function as in the calls to }{\b g1(\~)}{ and }{\b g2(\~)}{, a temporary is created and that temporary is }{\b const}{. Thus, the call in }{\b g1(\~)}{ is an error because }{\b g1(\~)}{ doesn\rquote t take a }{\b const}{
 reference, but the call to }{\b g2(\~)}{ is OK.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373916}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776133}Classes{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628678}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373916}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776133}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This section shows the two ways to use }{\b const}{ with classes. You may want to create a local }{\b const}{ in a class to use inside constant expressions that will be evaluate
d at compile time. However, the meaning of }{\b const}{ is different inside classes, so you must use an alternate technique with enumerations to achieve the same effect.
\par You can also make a class object }{\b const}{ (and as you\rquote ve just seen, the compiler always makes temporary class objects }{\b const}{). But preserving the }{\b const}{ness of a class object is more complex. The compiler can ensure the }{\b const}{
ness of a built-in type but it cannot monitor the intricacies of a class. To guarantee the }{\b const}{ness of a class object, the }{\b const}{ member function is introduced: Only a }{\b const}{ member function }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {member\: const member function}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: const member}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: member function}}}{may be called for a }{\b 
const}{ object. 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373917}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776134}const}{ and }{\b enum}{ in classes{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373917}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776134}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: and enum in classes}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {enum\: and const in classes}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\: const and enum in}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\: compile-time constants in}}}{One of the places you\rquote d like to use a }{\b const}{
 for constant expressions is inside classes. The typical example is when you\rquote re creating an array inside a class and you want to use a }{\b const}{ instead of a }{\b #define}{
 to establish the array size and to use in calculations involving the array. The array size is something you\rquote d like to keep hidden inside the class, so if you used a name like }{\b size}{, for example, you could use tha
t name in another class without a clash. The preprocessor treats all }{\b #define}{s as global from the point they are defined, so this will not achieve the desired effect. 
\par Initially, you probably assume that the logical choice is to place a }{\b const}{ inside the class. This doesn\rquote t produce the desired result. Inside a class, }{\b const}{
 partially reverts to its meaning in C. It allocates storage within each class object and represents a value that is initialized once and then cannot change. The use of }{\b const}{ inside a class means \ldblquote 
This is constant for the lifetime of the object.\rdblquote  However, each different object may contain a different value for that constant.
\par Thus, when you create a }{\b const}{ inside a class, you cannot give it an initial value. This initialization must occur in the constructor, of course, but in a special place in the constructor. Because a }{\b const}{
 must be initialized at the point it is created, inside the main body of the constructor the }{\b const}{ must }{\i already }{be initialized. Otherwise you\rquote re left with the choice of wai
ting until some point later in the constructor body, which means the }{\b const}{ would be un-initialized for a while. Also, there\rquote s nothing to keep you from changing the value of the }{\b const}{ at various places in the constructor body.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {The constructor initializer list
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The special initialization point is called the }{\i constructor initializer list}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {initialization\: constructor initializer list}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {list\: constructor initializer}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: initializer list}}}{, and it was originally developed for use in inheritanc
e (an object-oriented subject of a later chapter). The constructor initializer list \endash  which, as the name implies, occurs only in the definition of the constructor \endash  is a list of \ldblquote constructor calls\rdblquote 
 that occur after the function argument list and a colon, b
ut before the opening brace of the constructor body. This is to remind you that the initialization in the list occurs before any of the main constructor code is executed. This is the place to put all }{\b const}{ initializations, so the proper form for }{
\b const}{ inside a class is
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class Fred \{
\par   const int size;
\par public:
\par   Fred();
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Fred::Fred() : size(100) \{\}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The form of the constructor initializer list shown above is at first confusing because you\rquote re not used to seeing a built-in type treated as if it has a constructor.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\ldblquote Constructors\rdblquote  for built-in types
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
As the language developed and more effort was put into making user-defined types look like built-in types, it became apparent that there were times when it was helpful to make built-in types look like user-defined type
s. In the constructor initializer list, you can treat a built-in type as if it has a constructor, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class B \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   B(int ii); 
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {B::B(int ii) : i(ii) \{\}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This is especially critical when initializing }{\b const}{ data members }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: initializing data members}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {data\: initializing const members}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {member\: initializing const data members}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
initialization\: initializing const data members}}}{because they must be initialized before the function body is entered.
\par It made sense to extend this \ldblquote constructor\rdblquote  for built-in types (which simply means assignment) to the general case. Now you can say
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {float pi(3.14159);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It\rquote s often useful to encapsulate a built-in type inside a class to guarantee initialization with the constructor. For example, here\rquote s an }{\b Integer}{ class:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class Integer \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   Integer(int ii = 0); 
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Integer::Integer(int ii) : i(ii) \{\}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now if you make an array of }{\b integer}{s, they are all automatically initialized to zero:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {integer i[100];
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This initialization }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {initialization}}}{isn\rquote t necessarily more costly than a }{\b for}{ loop or }{\b memset(\~)}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {memset(\~)}}}{. Many compilers easily optimize this to a very fast process.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373918}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776135}Compile-time constants in classes{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373918}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776135}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Because storage is allocated in the class object, the compiler cannot know what the contents of the }{\b const}{ are, so it cannot be used as a compile-time constant}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constant\: compile-time inside classes}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\: compile-time constant inside}}}{. This means that, for constant expressions inside classes, }{
\b const}{ becomes as useless as it is in C. You cannot say
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class Bob \{
\par   const int size = 100;  // Illegal
\par   int array[size];  // Illegal
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//...
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The meaning of }{\b const}{ inside a class is \ldblquote This value is }{\b const}{ for the lifetime of this particular object, not for the class as a whole.\rdblquote  How the
n do you create a class constant that can be used in constant expressions? A common solution is to use an untagged }{\b enum}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {enum\: untagged}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {untagged enum}}}{with no instances. An enumeration must have all its values established at compile time, it\rquote s local to the class, and its values are available for constant expressions. Thus, you will commonly see
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class Bunch \{
\par   enum \{ size = 1000 \};
\par   int i[size];
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The use of }{\b enum}{ here is guaranteed to occupy no storage in the object, and the enumerators are all evaluated at compile time. You can also explicitly establish the values of the enumerators:

\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {enum \{ one = 1, two = 2, three \};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {With integral }{\b enum}{ types, the compiler will continue counting from the last value, so the enumerator }{\b three}{ will get the value 3.
\par Here\rquote s an example that shows the use of }{\b enum}{ inside a container that represents a }{\b Stack}{ of string pointers:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C08:SStack.cpp
\par // enum inside classes
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class StringStack \{
\par   enum \{ size = 100 \};
\par   const string* Stack[size];
\par   int index;
\par public:
\par    StringStack();
\par    void push(const string* s);
\par    const string* pop();
\par \};
\par 
\par StringStack::StringStack() : index(0) \{
\par   memset(Stack, 0, size * sizeof(string*));
\par \}
\par 
\par void StringStack::push(const string* s) \{
\par   if(index < size)
\par     Stack[index++] = s;
\par \}
\par 
\par const string* StringStack::pop() \{
\par   if(index > 0) \{
\par     const string* rv = Stack[--index];
\par     Stack[index] = 0;
\par     return rv;
\par   \}
\par   return 0;
\par \}
\par 
\par string iceCream[] = \{
\par   "pralines & cream",
\par   "fudge ripple",
\par   "jamocha almond fudge",
\par   "wild mountain blackberry",
\par   "raspberry sorbet",
\par   "lemon swirl",
\par   "rocky road",
\par   "deep chocolate fudge"
\par \};
\par 
\par const int iCsz = 
\par   sizeof iceCream / sizeof *iceCream;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   StringStack SS;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < iCsz; i++)
\par     SS.push(&iceCream[i]);
\par   const string* cp;
\par   while((cp = SS.pop()) != 0)
\par     cout << *cp << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that }{\b push(\~)}{ takes a }{\b const}{ }{\b string*}{ as an argument, }{\b pop(\~)}{ returns a }{\b const}{ }{\b string*}{, and }{\b StringStack}{ holds }{\b const string*}{
. If this were not true, you couldn\rquote t use a }{\b StringStack}{ to hold the pointers in }{\b iceCream}{. However, it also prevents you from doing anything that will change the objects contained by }{\b StringStack}{
. Of course, not all containers are designed with this restriction.
\par Although you\rquote ll often see the }{\b enum}{ technique in legacy code, C++ also has the }{\b static const}{ which produces a more flexible compile-time constant inside a class. This is described in Chapter XX.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Type checking for enumerations
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {C\rquote s enumerations }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {enumeration\: type checking}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type\:
 type checking for enumerations}}}{are fairly primitive, simply associating integral values with names, but providing no type checking. In C++, as you may have come to expect by now, the concept of type is fundamental, and this is true with en
umerations. When you create a named enumeration, you effectively create a new type just as you do with a class: The name of your enumeration becomes a reserved word for the duration of that translation unit. 
\par In addition, there\rquote s stricter type checking for enumerations in C++ than in C. You\rquote ll notice this in particular if you have an instance of an enumeration }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {increment\:
 incrementing and enumeration}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {enumeration\: incrementing}}}{\b color}{ called }{\b a}{. In C you can say }{\b a++}{ but in C++ you can\rquote t. This is beca
use incrementing an enumeration is performing two type conversions, one of them legal in C++ and one of them illegal. First, the value of the enumeration is implicitly cast from a }{\b color}{ to an }{\b int}{, then the value is incremented, then the }{
\b int}{ is cast back into a }{\b color}{. In C++ this isn\rquote t allowed, because }{\b color}{ is a distinct type and not equivalent to an }{\b int}{. This makes sense because how do you know the increment of }{\b blue}{
 will even be in the list of colors? If you want to increment a }{\b color}{, then it should be a class (with an increment operation) and not an }{\b enum}{. Any time you write code that assumes an implicit conversion to an }{\b enum}{
 type, the compiler will flag this inherently dangerous activity.
\par Unions }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {unions, additional type checking}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type\: type checking for unions}}}{have similar additional type checking.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373919}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776136}const}{ objects & member functions{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373919}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776136}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: const objects and member functions}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: const member functions}
}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {member\: const member functions}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: const member}}}{Class member functions can be made }{\b const}{
. What does this mean? To understand, you must first grasp the concept of }{\b const}{ objects.
\par A }{\b const}{ object is defined the same for a user-defined type as a built-in type. For example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {const int i = 1;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {const blob B(2);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here, }{\b B}{ is a }{\b const}{ object of type }{\b blob}{. Its constructor is called with an argument of two. For the compiler to enforce }{\b constness}{
, it must ensure that no data members of the object are changed during the object\rquote s lifetime. It can easily ensure that no public data is modified, but how is it to know which member functions will change the data and which ones are \ldblquote safe
\rdblquote  for a }{\b const}{ object?
\par If you declare a member function }{\b const}{, you tell the compiler the function can be called for a }{\b const}{ object. A member function that is not specifically declared }{\b const}{
 is treated as one that will modify data members in an object, and the compiler will not allow you to call it for a }{\b const}{ object.
\par It doesn\rquote t stop there, however. Just }{\i claiming}{ a function is }{\b const}{ inside a class definition doesn\rquote t guarantee the member function definition will act that way, so the compiler forces you to reiterate the }{\b const}{
 specification when defining the function. (The }{\b const}{ becomes part of the function signature, so both the compiler and linker check for }{\b const}{ness.) Then it enforces }{\b const}{
ness during the function definition by issuing an error message if you try to change any members of the object }{\i or}{ call a non-}{\b const}{ member function. Thus, any member function you declare }{\b const}{
 is guaranteed to behave that way in the definition.
\par Preceding the function declaration with }{\b const}{ means the return value is }{\b const}{, so that isn\rquote t the proper syntax. You must place the }{\b const}{ specifier }{\i after}{ the argument list. For example,
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class X \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   int f() const;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b const}{ keyword must be repeated in the definition using the same form, or the compiler sees it as a different function:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int X::f() const \{ return i; \}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If }{\b f(\~)}{attempts to change }{\b i}{ in any way }{\i or}{ to call another member function that is not }{\b const}{, the compiler flags it as an error.
\par Any function that doesn\rquote t modify member data should be declared as }{\b const}{, so it can be used with }{\b const}{ objects.
\par Here\rquote s an example that contrasts a }{\b const}{ and non-}{\b const}{ member function:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C08:Quoter.cpp
\par // Random quote selection
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <cstdlib> // Random number generator
\par #include <ctime> // To seed random generator
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Quoter \{
\par   int lastquote;
\par public:
\par   Quoter();
\par   int lastQuote() const;
\par   const char* quote();
\par \};
\par 
\par Quoter::Quoter()\{
\par   lastquote = -1;
\par   srand(time(0)); // Seed random number generator
\par \}
\par 
\par int Quoter::lastQuote() const \{
\par   return lastquote;
\par \}
\par 
\par const char* Quoter::quote() \{
\par   static const char* quotes[] = \{
\par     "Are we having fun yet?",
\par     "Doctors always know best",
\par     "Is it ... Atomic?",
\par     "Fear is obscene",
\par     "There is no scientific evidence "
\par     "to support the idea "
\par     "that life is serious",
\par   \};
\par   const int qsize = sizeof quotes/sizeof *quotes;
\par   int qnum = rand() % qsize;
\par   while(lastquote >= 0 && qnum == lastquote)
\par     qnum = rand() % qsize;
\par   return quotes[lastquote = qnum];
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Quoter q;
\par   const Quoter cq;
\par   cq.lastQuote(); // OK
\par //!  cq.quote(); // Not OK; non const function
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 20; i++)
\par     cout << q.quote() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Neither constructors nor destructors can be }{\b const}{ member functions because they virtually always perform some modification on the object during initialization and cleanup. The }{\b quote(\~)}{
 member function also cannot be }{\b const}{ because it modifies the data member }{\b lastquote}{ in the return statement. However, }{\b lastQuote(\~)}{ makes no modifications, and so it can be }{\b const}{ and can be safely called for the }{\b const}{
 object }{\b cq}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\b mutable}{: bitwise vs. memberwise const
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {mutable\: bitwise vs. memberwise const}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: mutable}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: memberwise}}}{What if you want to create a }{\b const}{ member function, but you\rquote 
d still like to change some of the data in the object? This is sometimes referred to as the difference between }{\i bitwise }{\b const}{\i  }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {bitwise\: const}}}{and }{\i memberwise }{\b const}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {memberwise\: memberwise const}}}{\i . }{Bitwise }{\b const}{ means that every bit in the object is permanent, so a bit image of the object will never change. Memberwise }{\b const}{
 means that, although the entire object is conceptually constant, there may be changes on a member-by-member basis. However, if the compiler is told that an object is }{\b const}{
, it will jealously guard that object. There are two ways to change a data member inside a }{\b const}{ member function.}{\i 
\par }{The first approach is the historical one and is called }{\i casting away constness}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: casting away}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cast\:
 casting away constness}}}{. It is performed in a rather odd fashion. You take }{\b this}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {this}}}{
 (the keyword that produces the address of the current object) and you cast it to a pointer to an object of the current type. It would seem that }{\b this}{ is }{\i already}{ such a pointer, but it\rquote s a }{\b const}{
 pointer, so by casting it to an ordinary pointer, you remove the }{\b const}{ness for that operation. Here\rquote s an example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C08:Castaway.cpp
\par // "Casting away" constness
\par 
\par class Y \{
\par   int i, j;
\par public:
\par   Y() \{ i = j = 0; \}
\par   void f() const;
\par \};
\par 
\par void Y::f() const \{
\par //!    i++; // Error -- const member function
\par     ((Y*)this)->j++; // OK: cast away const-ness
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   const Y yy;
\par   yy.f(); // Actually changes it!
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This approach works and you\rquote ll see it used in legacy code, but it is not the preferred technique. The problem is that this lack of }{\b const}{
ness is hidden away in a member function of an object, so the client programmer has no clue that it\rquote s happening unless she has access to the source code (and actually goes looking for it). To put everything out in the open, you should use the }{\b 
mutable}{ keyword in the class declaration to specify that a particular data member may be changed inside a }{\b const}{ object:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C08:Mutable.cpp
\par // The "mutable" keyword
\par 
\par class Y \{
\par   int i;
\par   mutable int j;
\par public:
\par   Y() \{ i = j = 0; \}
\par   void f() const;
\par \};
\par 
\par void Y::f() const \{
\par //! i++; // Error -- const member function
\par     j++; // OK: mutable
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   const Y yy;
\par   yy.f(); // Actually changes it!
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now the user of the class can see from the declaration which members are likely to be modified in a }{\b const}{ member function.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373920}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776137}ROMability{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373920}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776137}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If an object is defined as }{\b const}{, it is a candidate to be placed in read-only memory (ROM)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {memory\: read-only (ROM)}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ROM\: read-only memory}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {read-only memory (ROM)}}}{
, which is often an important consideration in embedded systems programming. Simply making an object }{\b const}{, however, is not enough \endash  the requirements for ROMability }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ROM\:
 ROMability}}}{are much more strict. Of course, the object must be bitwise-}{\b const}{, rather than memberwise-}{\b const}{. This is easy to see if memberwise }{\b const}{ness is implemented only through the }{\b mutable}{
 keyword, but probably not detectable by the compiler if }{\b const}{ness is cast away inside a }{\b const}{ member function. In addition,
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 7. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls114\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {The }{\b class}{ or }{\b struct}{ must have no user-defined constructors or destructor.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 8. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {There can be no base classes (covered in the future chapter on inheritance) or member objects with user-defined constructors or destructors.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The effect of a write operation on any part of a }{\b const}{
 object of a ROMable type is undefined. Although a suitably formed object may be placed in ROM, no objects are ever }{\i required}{ to be placed in ROM.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628679}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373921}
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776138}volatile}{{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628679}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373921}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776138}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The syntax of }{\b volatile}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {volatile}}}{
is identical to that for }{\b const}{, but }{\b volatile}{ means \ldblquote This data may change outside the knowledge of the compiler.\rdblquote  Somehow, the environment is changing the data (possibly through multitasking), and }{\b volatile}{
 tells the compiler not to make any assumptions about the data \endash  this is particularly important during optimization. If the compiler says, \ldblquote I read the data into a register earlier, and I haven\rquote t touched that register,\rdblquote 
 normally it wouldn\rquote t need to read the data again. But if the data is }{\b volatile}{, the compiler cannot make such an assumption because the data may have been changed by another process, }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {multitasking and volatile}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {process}}}{
and it must reread the data rather than optimizing the code.
\par You can create }{\b volatile}{ objects just as you create }{\b const}{ objects. You can also create }{\b const}{ }{\b volatile}{ objects, which can\rquote 
t be changed by the programmer but instead change through some outside agency. Here is an example that might represent a class to associate with some piece of communication hardware:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C08:Volatile.cpp
\par // The volatile keyword
\par 
\par class Comm \{
\par   const volatile unsigned char byte;
\par   volatile unsigned char flag;
\par   enum \{ bufsize = 100 \};
\par   unsigned char buf[bufsize];
\par   int index;
\par public:
\par   Comm();
\par   void isr() volatile;
\par   char read(int index) const;
\par \};
\par 
\par Comm::Comm() : index(0), byte(0), flag(0) \{\}
\par 
\par // Only a demo; won't actually work
\par // as an interrupt service routine:
\par void Comm::isr() volatile \{
\par   if(flag) flag = 0;
\par   buf[index++] = byte;
\par   // Wrap to beginning of buffer:
\par   if(index >= bufsize) index = 0;
\par \}
\par 
\par char Comm::read(int index) const \{
\par   if(index < 0 || index >= bufsize)
\par     return 0;
\par   return buf[index];
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   volatile Comm Port;
\par   Port.isr(); // OK
\par //!  Port.read(0); // Not OK;
\par                 // read() not volatile
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As with }{\b const}{, you can use }{\b volatile}{ for data members, member functions, and objects themselves. You can call only }{\b volatile}{
 member functions for }{\b volatile}{ objects.
\par The reason that }{\b isr(\~)}{ can\rquote t actually be used as an interrupt service routine }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {interrupt\: interrupt service routine (ISR)}}}{
is that in a member function, the address of the current object (}{\b this}{) must be secretly passed, and an ISR generally wants no arguments at all. To solve this problem, you can make }{\b isr(\~)}{ a }{\b static}{ member function}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {member\: static member function}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: static member
}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {static\: member function}}}{, a subject covered in a future chapter.
\par The syntax of }{\b volatile}{ is identical to }{\b const}{, so discussions of the two are often treated together. To indicate the choice of either one, the two are referred to in combination as the }{\i c\_v qualifier}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {qualifier, c\_v}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {c\_v qualifier}}}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628680}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373922}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776139
}Summary{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628680}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373922}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776139}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b const}{
 keyword gives you the ability to define objects, function arguments and return values, and member functions as constants, and to eliminate th
e preprocessor for value substitution without losing any preprocessor benefits. All this provides a significant additional form of type checking and safety in your programming. The use of so-called }{\i const correctness}{ }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {correctness, const}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: const correctness}}}{
(the use of }{\b const}{ anywhere you possibly can) has been a lifesaver for projects.
\par Although you can ignore }{\b const}{ and continue to use old C coding practices, it\rquote s there to help you. Chapters 9 & 10 begin using references heavily, and there you\rquote ll see even more about how critical it is to use }{\b const}{
 with function arguments.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373923}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776140}Exercises
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373923}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776140}
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 1. \tab}}\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls202\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
