\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\f4\fs28\cgrid {\xe {efficiency}}}{. If the efficiency of C++ were dramatically less than C, there would be a significant contingent of programmers who couldn\rquote t justify its use.

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In C, one of the ways to preserve efficiency is through the use of }{\i macros}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {preprocessor\: macros}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {macro\: preprocessor}}}{
, which allow you to make what looks like a function call without the normal overhead of the fun
ction call. The macro is implemented with the preprocessor rather than the compiler proper, and the preprocessor replaces all macro calls directly with the macro code, so there\rquote 
s no cost involved from pushing arguments, making an assembly-language CALL, re
turning arguments, and performing an assembly-language RETURN. All the work is performed by the preprocessor, so you have the convenience and readability of a function call but it doesn\rquote t cost you anything.
\par There are two problems with the use of preprocessor macros in C++. The first is also true with C: A macro looks like a function call, but doesn\rquote 
t always act like one. This can bury difficult-to-find bugs. The second problem is specific to C++: The preprocessor has no permission to access }{\b private}{ data. This means preprocessor macros are virtually useless as class member functions.
\par To retain the efficiency of the preprocessor macro, but to add the safety and class scoping of true functions, C++ has the }{\i inline function}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
function\: inline}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inline\: function}}}{. In this chapter, we\rquote 
ll look at the problems of preprocessor macros in C++, how these problems are solved with inline functions, and guidelines and insights on the way inlines work.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593211}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628683}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373925
