\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b require.h }{macros have been used up to this point without defining them (although }{\b assert(\~)}{ has also bee
n used to help detect programmer errors, where it\rquote s appropriate). Now it\rquote 
s time to define this header file. Inline functions are convenient here because they allow everything to be placed in a header file, which simplifies the process of using the package. You just include the header file and you don\rquote 
t need to worry about linking.
\par You should note that exceptions (presented in detail in Chapter XX) provide a much more effective way of handling many kinds of errors \endash  especially those that you\rquote d like to recove
r from, instead of just halting the program. The conditions that }{\b require.h}{ handles, however, are ones which prevent the continuation of the program, such as if the user doesn\rquote 
t provide enough command-line arguments or a file cannot be opened.
\par The following header file will be placed in the book\rquote s root directory so it\rquote s easily accessed from all chapters.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: :require.h
\par // Test for error conditions in programs
\par // Local "using namespace std" for old compilers
\par #ifndef REQUIRE_H
\par #define REQUIRE_H
\par #include <cstdio>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <fstream>
\par 
\par inline void require(bool requirement, 
\par   const char* msg = "Requirement failed") \{
\par   using namespace std;
\par   if (!requirement) \{
\par     fprintf(stderr, "%s", msg);
\par     exit(1);
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par inline void requireArgs(int argc, int args, 
\par   const char* msg = "Must use %d arguments") \{
\par   using namespace std;
\par    if (argc != args + 1) \{
\par      fprintf(stderr, msg, args);
\par      exit(1);
\par    \}
\par \}
\par 
\par inline void requireMinArgs(int argc, int minArgs,
\par   const char* msg = 
\par     "Must use at least %d arguments") \{
\par   using namespace std;
\par   if(argc < minArgs + 1) \{
\par     fprintf(stderr, msg, minArgs);
\par     exit(1);
\par   \}
\par \}
\par   
\par inline void assure(std::ifstream& in, 
\par   const char* filename = "") \{
\par   using namespace std;
\par   if(!in) \{
\par     fprintf(stderr,
\par       "Could not open file %s", filename);
\par     exit(1);
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par inline void assure(std::ofstream& in, 
\par   const char* filename = "") \{
\par   using namespace std;
\par   if(!in) \{
\par     fprintf(stderr,
\par       "Could not open file %s", filename);
\par     exit(1);
\par   \}
\par \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // REQUIRE_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593216}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628688}The default values provide reasonable messages that can be changed if necessary.
\par In the definitions for }{\b requireArgs(\~) }{and }{\b requireMinArgs(\~)}{, one is added to the number of arguments you need on the command line because }{\b argc}{ always includes t
he name of the program being executed as the zeroth argument, and so always has a value that is one more than the number of actual arguments on the command line.
\par Note the use of local \ldblquote }{\b using namespace std}{\rdblquote  declarations within each function. This is because some compilers at the time of this writing incorrectly did not include the C standard library functions in }{\b namespace std}{
, so explicit qualification would cause a compile-time error. The local declaration allows }{\b require.h}{ to work with both correct and incorrect libraries.
\par Here\rquote s a simple program to test }{\b require.h}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C09:ErrTest.cpp
\par // Testing require.h
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   int i = 1;
\par   require(i, "value must be nonzero");
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1);
\par   requireMinArgs(argc, 1);
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]); // Use the file name
\par   ifstream nofile("nofile.xxx");
\par   assure(nofile); // The default argument
\par   ofstream out("tmp.txt");
\par   assure(out);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
