\par }}\pard\plain \s1\fi-1080\li990\sa360\sl240\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \cbpat8 \f4\fs96\cf1\kerning18\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373940}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776159}10: Name control{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373940}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776159}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {Creating names is a fundamental activity in programming, and when a project gets large the number of names can easily be overwhelming
. C++ allows you a great deal of control over both the creation and visibility of names, where storage for those names is placed, and linkage for names.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b static}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {static}}}{keyword was overloaded in C before people knew what the term \ldblquote overload\rdblquote  meant, 
and C++ has added yet another meaning. The underlying concept with all uses of }{\b static}{ seems to be \ldblquote something that holds its position\rdblquote 
 (like static electricity), whether that means a physical location in memory or visibility within a file.
\par In this chapter, you\rquote ll learn how }{\b static}{ controls storage and visibility, and an improved way to control access to names via C++\rquote s }{\i namespace}{ feature. You\rquote 
ll also find out how to use functions that were written and compiled in C.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593219}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628691}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373941}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776160}
Static elements from C{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593219}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628691}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373941}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776160}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In both C and C++ the keyword }{\b static}{ has two basic meanings, which unfortunately often step on each other\rquote s toes:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li2520\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls178\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls178\adjustright {
Allocated once at a fixed address; that is, the object is created in a special }{\i static data area}{ }{\pard\plain \fi-360\li2520\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls178\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls178\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {data\: static area}}}
{\pard\plain \fi-360\li2520\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls178\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls178\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {static\: data area}}}{rather than on the stack each time a function is called. This is the concept of }{\i static storage}
{\pard\plain \fi-360\li2520\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls178\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls178\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {storage\: static}}}{\pard\plain \fi-360\li2520\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls178\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls178\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {static\: storage}}}{. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li2520\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls179\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls179\adjustright {
Local to a particular translation unit (and class scope in C++, as you will see later). Here, }{\b static}{ controls the }{\i visibility}{ }{\pard\plain \fi-360\li2520\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls179\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls179\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {visibility}}}{of a name, so that name cannot be seen outside the translation unit or class. This also describes the concept of }{\i linkage}{\pard\plain \fi-360\li2520\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls179\pnrnot0\pndec }
\ls179\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linkage}}}{, which determines what names the linker will see.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {This section will look at the above meanings of }{\b static}{ as they were inherited from C.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373942}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776161}static}{ variables inside functions{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373942}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776161}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {static\: variables inside functions}}}{
Normally, when you create a variable inside a function, the compiler allocates storage for that variable each time the function is called by moving the stack pointer}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {stack\: pointer}}}{
 down an appropriate amount. If there is an initializer for the variable, the initialization is performed each time that sequence point is passed.
\par Sometimes, however, you want to retain a value between function calls. You could accomplish this by making a global variable, but that variable would not be under the sole control of the function. C and C++ allow you to create a }{\b static}{
 object inside a function; the storage for this object is not on the stack but instead in the program\rquote 
s static storage area. This object is initialized once the first time the function is called and then retains its value between function invocations. For example, the following function returns the next character in the string each time the function is ca
lled:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C10:Statfun.cpp
\par // Static vars inside functions
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par char onechar(const char* string = 0) \{
\par   static const char* s;
\par   if(string) \{
\par     s = string;
\par     return *s;
\par   \}
\par   else
\par     require(s, "un-initialized s");
\par   if(*s == '\\0')
\par     return 0;
\par   return *s++;
\par \}
\par 
\par char* a = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz";
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   // Onechar(); // require() fails
\par   onechar(a); // Initializes s to a
\par   char c;
\par   while((c = onechar()) != 0)
\par     cout << c << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b static char* s}{ holds its value between calls of }{\b onechar(\~)}{
 because its storage is not part of the stack frame of the function, but is in the static storage area of the program. When you call }{\b onechar(\~)}{ with a }{\b char*}{ argument, }{\b s}{ is assigned 
to that argument, and the first character of the string is returned. Each subsequent call to }{\b onechar(\~)}{ }{\i without}{ an argument produces the default value of zero for }{\b string}{
, which indicates to the function that you are still extracting characters from the previously initialized value of }{\b s}{
. The function will continue to produce characters until it reaches the null terminator of the string, at which point it stops incrementing the pointer so it doesn\rquote t overrun the end of the string.
\par But what happens if you call }{\b onechar(\~)}{ with no arguments and without previously initializing the value of }{\b s}{? In the definition for }{\b s}{, you could have provided an initializer,
\par static char* s = 0;
\par but if you do not provide an initializer for a static variable of a built-in type}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {static\: initializer for a variable of a built-in type}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {variable\: initializer for a static variable of a built-in type}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {built-in type\: initializer for a static variable }}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {initialization\: initializer for a static variable of a built-in type}}}{, the compiler guarantees that variable will be initialized to zero (converted to the proper type) at program start-up. So in }{\b onechar(\~)}{
, the first time the function is called, }{\b s}{ is zero. In this case, the }{\b if(!s)}{ conditional will catch it.
\par The above initialization for }{\b s}{ is very simple, but initialization for static objects (like all other objects) can be arbitrary expressions involving constants and previously declared variables and functions.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\b static}{ class objects inside functions
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The rules are the same for stat
ic objects of user-defined types, including the fact that some initialization is required for the object. However, assignment to zero has meaning only for built-in types; user-defined types must be initialized with constructor calls. Thus, if you don
\rquote t specify constructor arguments when you define the static object, the class must have a default constructor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: default}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {default\: constructor}}}{. For example,
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C10:Funobj.cpp
\par // Static objects in functions
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class X \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   X(int ii = 0) : i(ii) \{\} // Default
\par   ~X() \{ cout << "X::~X()" << endl; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par void f() \{
\par   static X x1(47);
\par   static X x2; // Default constructor required
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   f();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The static objects of type }{\b X}{ inside }{\b f(\~)}{
 can be initialized either with the constructor argument list or with the default constructor. This construction occurs the first time control passes through the definition, and only the first time.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Static object destructors
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Destructors for static objects (all objects with static storage, not just local static objects as in the above example) are called when }{\b main(\~)}{ exits or when the Standard C library function }{
\b exit(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C\: Standard library function exit(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {library\: Standard C function exit(\~)}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exit(\~) Standard C library function}}}{\b  }{is explicitly called, }{\b main(\~)}{ in most implementations calls }{\b exit(\~)}{ when it terminates. This means that it can be dangerous to call }{\b exit(\~
)}{ inside a destructor because you can end up with infinite recursion. Static object destructors are }{\i not}{ called if you exit the program using the Standard C library function }{\b abort(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {C\: Standard library function abort(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {library\: Standard C function abort(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {abort(\~)\: Standard C library function}
}}{.
\par You can specify actions to take place when leaving }{\b main(\~)}{ (or calling }{\b exit(\~)}{) by using the Standard C library function }{\b atexit(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C\: Standard library function atexit(\~)
}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {library\: Standard C function atexit(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {atexit(\~)\: Standard C library function}}}{. I
n this case, the functions registered by }{\b atexit(\~) }{may be called before the destructors for any objects constructed before leaving }{\b main(\~)}{ (or calling }{\b exit(\~)}{).
\par Destruction of static objects }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {static\: destruction of objects}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: destruction of static}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {destructor\: destruction of static objects}}}{
occurs in the reverse order of initialization. However, only objects that have been constructed are destroyed. Fortunately, the programming system keeps track of initialization order and the objects t
hat have been constructed. Global objects are always constructed}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: global constructor}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {global\: object constructor}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: global object}}}{ before }{\b main(\~)}{ is entered, so this last statement applies only to static objects that are lo
cal to functions. If a function containing a local static object }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {static\: local object}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: local static}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {local\: static object}}}{is never called, the constructor for that object is never executed, so the destructor is also not executed. For example,
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C10:StaticDestructors.cpp
\par // Static object destructors
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("statdest.out"); // Trace file
\par 
\par class Obj \{
\par   char c; // Identifier
\par public:
\par   Obj(char cc) : c(cc) \{
\par     out << "Obj::Obj() for " << c << endl;
\par   \}
\par   ~Obj() \{
\par     out << "Obj::~Obj() for " << c << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par Obj a('a'); // Global (static storage)
\par // Constructor & destructor always called
\par 
\par void f() \{
\par   static Obj b('b');
\par \}
\par 
\par void g() \{
\par   static Obj c('c');
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   out << "inside main()" << endl;
\par   f(); // Calls static constructor for b
\par   // g() not called
\par   out << "leaving main()" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In }{\b Obj}{, the }{\b char c}{ acts as an identifier so the constructor and destructor can print out information about the object they\rquote re working on. The }{\b Obj a}{
 is a global object, so the constructor is always called for it before }{\b main(\~)}{ is entered, but the constructors for the }{\b static Obj b}{ inside }{\b f(\~)}{ and the }{\b static Obj C}{ inside }{\b g(\~)}{
 are called only if those functions are called.
\par To demonstrate which constructors and destructors are called, inside }{\b main(\~)}{ only }{\b f(\~)}{ is called. The output of the program is
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Obj::Obj() for a
\par inside main()
