\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The constructor for }{\b a}{ is called before }{\b main(\~)}{ is entered, and the constructor for }{\b b}{ is called only because }{\b f(\~)}{ is called. When }{\b main(\~)}{
 exits, the destructors for the objects that have been constructed are called in reverse order of their construction. This means that if }{\b g(\~)}{ }{\i is}{ called, the order in which the destructors for }{\b b}{ and }{\b c}{
 are called depends on whether }{\b f(\~)}{ or }{\b g(\~) }{is called first.
\par Notice that the trace file }{\b ofstream}{ object }{\b out}{ is also a static object}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ofstream\: as a static object}}}{. It is important that its definition (as opposed to an }{\b extern}{
 declaration) appear at the beginning of the file, before there is any possible use of }{\b out}{. Otherwise you\rquote ll be using an object before it is properly initialized.
\par In C++ the constructor for a global static object is called before }{\b main(\~)}{ is entered, so you now have a simple and portable way to execute code before entering }{\b main(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {main(\~)\:
 executing code before entering}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {executing code\: before entering main(\~)}}}{ and to execute code with the destructor after exiting }{\b main(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {executing code\: after exiting main(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {main(\~)\: executing code after exiting}}}{. In C this was always a trial that required you to root around in the compiler vendor
\rquote s assembly-language startup code.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373943}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776162}Controlling linkage{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373943}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776162}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {controlling\: linkage}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linkage\: controlling}}}{Ordinarily, any name at }{
\i file scope}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {scope\: file}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {file\: scope}}}{
(that is, not nested inside a class or function) is visible throughout all translation units in a program. This is often called }{\i external linkage}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linkage\: external}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {external\: linkage}}}{because at link time the name is visible to the linker everywhere, external to that translation unit. Global variables and ordinary functions have external linkage.
\par There are times when you\rquote d like to limit the visibility of a name. You might like to have a variable at file scope so all the functions in that file can use it, but you don\rquote 
t want functions outside that file to see or access that variable, or to inadvertently cause name clashes with identifiers outside the file.
\par An object or function name at file scope that is explicitly declared }{\b static}{ is local to its translation unit (in the terms of this book, the}{\b  cpp}{ file where the declaration occurs); that name has }{\i internal linkage}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linkage\: internal}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {internal linkage}}}{. This means you can use the same name in other translation units without a name clash.
\par One advantage to internal linkage is that the name can be placed in a header file without worrying that there will be a clash at link time. Names that are commonly placed in header files, such as }{\b const}{ definitions and }{\b inline}{
 functions, default to internal linkage. (However, }{\b const}{ defaults to internal linkage only in C++; in C it defaults to external linkage.) Note that linkage refers only to elements that have addresses at link/load ti
me; thus, class declarations and local variables have no linkage}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linkage\: no}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {no linkage}}}{.
