cifier is almost never used because it tells the compiler that this is a local variable. The compiler can always determine this fact from the context in which the variable is defined, so }{\b auto}{ is redundant.
\par A }{\b register}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {storage\: register storage class specifier}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {register}}}{variable is a local (}{\b auto}{
) variable, along with a hint to the compiler that this particular variable will be heavily used, so the compiler ought to keep it in a register if it can. Thus, it is an optimization aid. Various compilers 
respond differently to this hint; they have the option to ignore it. If you take the address of the variable, the }{\b register}{ specifier will almost certainly be ignored. You should avoid using }{\b register}{
 because the compiler can usually do a better job at of optimization than you.
