\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {extern int x;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int y = x + 1;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {For all static objects, the linking-loading mechanism guarantees a static initialization to zero}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {initialization\: static to zero}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {static\:
 initialization to zero}}}{ before the dynamic initialization specified by the programmer takes place. In the previous example, zeroing of the storage occupied by the }{\b fstream out}{ object has no special meaning
, so it is truly undefined until the constructor is called. However, with built-in types, initialization to zero }{\i does}{ have meaning, and if the files are initialized in the order they are shown above, }{\b y}{
 begins as statically initialized to zero, so }{\b x}{ becomes one, and }{\b y}{ is dynamically initialized to two. However, if the files are initialized in the opposite order, }{\b x}{ is statically initialized to zero, }{\b y}{
 is dynamically initialized to one, and }{\b x}{ then becomes two.
\par Programmers must be aware of this because they can create a program with static initialization dependencies and get it working on one platform, but move it to another compiling environment where it suddenly, mysteriously, doesn\rquote t work.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373953}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776172}What to do{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373953
}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776172}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There are three approaches to dealing with this problem:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 1. \tab}}\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls275\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls275\adjustright \fs20\lang1024\cgrid {Don\rquote t do it. Avoiding static initializer dependencies is the best solution.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 2. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls275\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls275\adjustright {If you must do it, put the critical static object definitions in a single file, so you can portably control their initialization by putting them in the correct order.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 3. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls275\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls275\adjustright {If you\rquote re convinced it\rquote s unavoidable to scatter static objects across translation units \endash  as in the case of a library, where you can\rquote t control the programmer who uses it \endash 
 there is a technique pioneered by Jerry Schwarz}{\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls275\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls275\adjustright \v\fs20\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {Schwarz, Jerry}}}{ while creating the iostream li
brary (because the definitions for }{\b cin}{, }{\b cout}{, and }{\b cerr}{ live in a separate file).
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This technique requires an additional class in your library header file. This class is responsible for the dynamic initialization of your library\rquote 
s static objects. Here is a simple example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C10:Depend.h
\par // Static initialization technique
\par #ifndef DEPEND_H
\par #define DEPEND_H
\par #include <iostream>
\par extern int x; // Declarations, not definitions
\par extern int y;
\par 
\par class Initializer \{
\par   static int init_count;
\par public:
\par   Initializer() \{
\par     std::cout << "Initializer()" << std::endl;
\par     // Initialize first time only
\par     if(init_count++ == 0) \{
\par       std::cout << "performing initialization"
\par            << std::endl;
\par       x = 100;
\par       y = 200;
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par   ~Initializer() \{
\par     std::cout << "~Initializer()" << std::endl;
\par     // Clean up last time only
\par     if(--init_count == 0) \{
\par       std::cout << "performing cleanup" 
\par         << std::endl;
\par       // Any necessary cleanup here
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // The following creates one object in each
\par // file where DEPEND.H is included, but that
\par // object is only visible within that file:
\par static Initializer init;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // DEPEND_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The declarations for }{\b x}{ and }{\b y}{ announce only that these objects exist, but don\rquote t allocate storage for them. However, the definit
ion for the }{\b Initializer init}{ allocates storage for that object in every file where the header is included, but because the name is }{\b static}{
 (controlling visibility this time, not the way storage is allocated because that is at file scope by default), it is only visible within that translation unit, so the linker will not complain about multiple definition errors.
\par Here is the file containing the definitions for }{\b x}{, }{\b y}{, and }{\b init_count}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C10:Depdefs.cpp \{O\}
\par // Definitions for DEPEND.H
\par #include "Depend.h"
\par // Static initialization will force
\par // all these values to zero:
\par int x;
\par int y;
\par int Initializer::init_count;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {(Of course, a file static instance of }{\b init}{ is also placed in this file.) Suppose that two other files are created by the library user:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C10:Depend.cpp \{O\}
\par // Static initialization
\par #include "Depend.h"
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {and
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C10:Depend2.cpp
\par //\{L\} Depdefs Depend
\par // Static initialization
\par #include "Depend.h"
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   cout << "inside main()" << endl;
\par   cout << "leaving main()" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now it doesn\rquote t matter which translation unit is initialized first. The first time a translation unit containing }{\b Depend.h }{is initialized, }{\b 
init_count}{ will be zero so the initialization will be performed. (This depends heavily on the fact that glo
bal objects of built-in types are set to zero before any dynamic initialization takes place.) For all the rest of the translation units, the initialization will be skipped. Cleanup happens in the reverse order, and }{\b ~Initializer(\~)}{
 ensures that it will happen only once.
\par This example used built-in types as the global static objects. The technique also works with classes, but those objects must then be dynamically initialized by the }{\b Initializer}{ class. One way to do this is to create the classes without constru
ctors and destructors, but instead with initialization and cleanup member functions using different names. A more common approach, however, is to have pointers to objects and to create them dynamically on the heap inside }{\b Initializer(\~)}{
. This requires the use of two C++ keywords, }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{, which will be explored in Chapter XX.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593223}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628695}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373954
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776173}Alternate linkage specifications{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593223}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628695}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373954}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776173}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {alternate linkage specification}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {linkage\: alternate linkage specification}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C\:
 linking compiled C code with C++}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C++\: linking compiled C code with C++}}}{What happens if you\rquote 
re writing a program in C++ and you want to use a C library? If you make the C function declaration,
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {float f(int a, char b);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {the C++ compiler will decorate this name to something like }{\b _f_int_int}{ to support fun
ction overloading (and type-safe linkage). However, the C compiler that compiled your C library has most definitely }{\i not}{ decorated the name, so its internal name will be }{\b _f}{. Thus, the linker will not be able to resolve your C++ calls to }{\b 
f(\~)}{.
\par The escape mechanism provided in C++ is the }{\i alternate linkage specification}{, which was produced in the language by overloading the }{\b extern}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {extern\: to link C code}}}{keyword. The }{\b extern}{ is followed by a string that specifies the linkage you want for the declaration, followed by the declaration itself:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {extern "C" float f(int a, char b);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This tells the compiler to give C linkage to }{\b f(\~)}{; that is, don\rquote t decorate the name}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {name\: mangling}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {mangling, name}}}{
. The only two types of linkage specifications supported by the standard are }{\b \ldblquote C\rdblquote }{ and }{\b \ldblquote C++,\rdblquote }{ but compiler vendors have the option of supporting other languages in the same way.
\par If you have a group of declarations with alternate linkage, put them inside braces, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {extern "C" \{
\par   float f(int a, char b);
\par   double d(int a, char b);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}
