\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {The code generated for the rest of the function expects the memory to be laid out exactly this way, so it can carefully pick from the function argume
nts and local variables without touching the return address. I shall call this block of memory, which is everything used by a function in the process of the function call, the }{\i function frame}{.
\par You might think it reasonable to try to return values on the stack. The compiler could simply push it, and the function could return an offset to indicate how far down in the stack the return value begins.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Re-entrancy
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The problem occurs because functions in C and C++ support interrupts; that is, the languages are }{\i re-entrant}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {re-entrant}}}{. They also support recursive function calls. This means that at any point in the execution of a program an interrupt }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {interrupt}}}{can occur without disturbing the program. Of course, the person who writes the interrupt service routine (ISR)}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {interrupt\: interrupt service routine (ISR)}}}{ is responsible for saving and restoring all the registers he uses, but if the ISR needs to use any memory that
\rquote s further down on the stack, that must be a safe thing to do. (You can think of an ISR as an ordinary function with no arguments and }{\b void}{
 return value that saves and restores the CPU state. An ISR function call is triggered by some hardware event rather than an explicit call from within a program.)
\par Now imagine what would happen if the called function tried to return values on the stack from an ordinary function. You can\rquote t touch any part of the stack that\rquote 
s above the return address, so the function would have to push the values below the return address. But when the assembly-language RETURN 
is executed, the stack pointer must be pointing to the return address (or right below it, depending on your machine), so right before the RETURN, the function must move the stack pointer up, thus clearing off all its local variables. If you\rquote 
re trying to re
turn values on the stack below the return address, you become vulnerable at that moment because an interrupt could come along. The ISR would move the stack pointer down to hold its return address and its local variables and overwrite your return value.

\par To solve this problem, the caller could be responsible for allocating the extra storage on the stack for the return values }{\i before}{ calling the function. However, C was not designed this way, and C++ must be compatible. As you\rquote 
ll see shortly, the C++ compiler uses a more efficient scheme.
\par Your next idea might be to return the value in some global data area, but this doesn\rquote t work either. Re-entrancy means that any function can interrupt any other function, }{\i including the same function you\rquote re currently inside}{. Thus
, if you put the return value in a global area, you might return into the same function, which would overwrite that return value. The same logic applies to recursion}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {recursion}}}{.
\par The only safe place to return values is in the registers, so you\rquote re back to the problem of what to do when the registers aren\rquote t large enough to hold the return value. The answer is to push the address of the return value\rquote 
s destination on the stack as one of the function arguments, and let the function copy the return information directly into the destination. This not only solves all the problems, it\rquote s more efficient. It\rquote s also the reason that, in }{\b 
PassStruct.cpp}{, the compiler pushes the address of }{\b B2}{ before the call to }{\b bigfun(\~)}{ in }{\b main(\~)}{. If you look at the assembly output for }{\b bigfun(\~)}{
, you can see it expects this hidden argument and performs the copy to the destination }{\i inside}{ the function.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Bitcopy versus initialization
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {bitcopy}}}{So far, so good. There\rquote 
s a workable process for passing and returning large simple structures.
 But notice that all you have is a way to copy the bits from one place to another, which certainly works fine for the primitive way that C looks at variables. But in C++ objects can be much more sophisticated than a patch of bits; they have meaning. This 
meaning may not respond well to having its bits copied.
\par Consider a simple example: a class that knows how many objects of its type exist at any one time. From Chapter XX, you know the way to do this is by including a }{\b static}{ data member:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C11:HowMany.cpp
\par // Class counts its objects
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("HowMany.out");
\par 
\par class HowMany \{
\par   static int object_count;
\par public:
\par   HowMany() \{
\par     object_count++;
\par   \}
\par   static void print(const char* msg = 0) \{
\par     if(msg) out << msg << ": ";
\par     out << "object_count = "
\par          << object_count << endl;
\par   \}
\par   ~HowMany() \{
\par     object_count--;
\par     print("~HowMany()");
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int HowMany::object_count = 0;
\par 
\par // Pass and return BY VALUE:
\par HowMany f(HowMany x) \{
\par   x.print("x argument inside f()");
\par   return x;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   HowMany h;
\par   HowMany::print("after construction of h");
\par   HowMany h2 = f(h);
\par   HowMany::print("after call to f()");
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The class }{\b HowMany}{ contains a }{\b static}{ }{\b int}{ and a }{\b static}{ member function }{\b print(\~)}{ to report the value of that }{\b int}{
, along with an optional message argument. The constructor increments the count each time an object is created, and the destructor decrements it.
\par The output, however, is not what you would expect:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {after construction of h: object_count = 1
\par x argument inside f(): object_count = 1
\par ~HowMany(): object_count = 0
\par after call to f(): object_count = 0
\par ~HowMany(): object_count = -1
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {~HowMany(): object_count = -2
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {After }{\b h}{ is created, the object count is one, which is fine. But after the call to }{\b f(\~) }{you wou
ld expect to have an object count of two, because }{\b h2}{
 is now in scope as well. Instead, the count is zero, which indicates something has gone horribly wrong. This is confirmed by the fact that the two destructors at the end make the object count go negative, something that should never happen.
\par Look at the point inside }{\b f(\~)}{, which occurs after the argument is passed by value. This means the original object }{\b h}{ exists outside the function frame, and there\rquote s an additional object }{\i inside}{ the function frame, which i
s the copy that has been passed by value. However, the argument has been passed using C\rquote s primitive notion of bitcopying, whereas the C++ }{\b HowMany}{
 class requires true initialization to maintain its integrity, so the default bitcopy fails to produce the desired effect.
\par When the local object goes out of scope at the end of the call to }{\b f(\~)}{, the destructor is called, which decrements }{\b object_count}{, so outside the function, }{\b object_count}{ is zero. The creation of }{\b h2}{
 is also performed using a bitcopy, so the constructor isn\rquote t called there, either, and when }{\b h}{ and }{\b h2}{ go out of scope, their destructors cause the negative values of }{\b object_count}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373964}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776183}Copy-construction
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373964}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776183}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The problem occurs because the compiler makes an assumption about how to create }{\i a new object from an existing object}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: creating a new object from an existing object}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
creating\: a new object from an existing object}}}{. When you pass an object by value, you create a new object, the passed object inside the function frame, from an existing object, the o
riginal object outside the function frame. This is also often true when returning an object from a function. In the expression 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {HowMany h2 = f(h);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b h2}{, a previously unconstructed object, is created from the return value of }{\b f(\~)}{, so again a new object is created from an existing one.
\par The compiler\rquote s assumption is that you want to perform this creation using a bitcopy, and in many cases this may work fine but in }{\b HowMany}{ it doesn\rquote t fly because the meaning of initialization goes beyond simply copying. Another co
mmon example occurs if the class contains pointers \endash  what do they point to, and should you copy them or should they be connected to some new piece of memory?
\par Fortunately, you can intervene in this process and prevent the compiler from doing a bitcopy. You do this by defining your own function to be used whenever the compiler needs to make a new object from an existing object. Logically enough, you\rquote 
re making a new object, so this function is a constructor, and also logically enough, the single argument to this constructor has to do with the object you\rquote re constructing from. But that object can\rquote 
t be passed into the constructor by value because you\rquote re trying to define the function that handles passing by value, and syntactically it doesn\rquote t make sense to pass a pointer because, after all, you\rquote 
re creating the new object from an existing }{\i object}{. Here, references come to the rescue, so you take the reference of the source object. This function is called the }{\i copy-constructor}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {copy-constructor}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: copy}}}{
 and is often referred to as }{\b X(X&)}{, which is its appearance for a class called }{\b X}{.
\par If you create a copy-constructor, the compiler will not perform a bitcopy when creating a new object from an existing one. It will always call your copy-constructor. So, if you don\rquote 
t create a copy-constructor, the compiler will do something sensible, but you have the choice of taking over complete control of the process.
\par Now it\rquote s possible to fix the problem in }{\b HowMany.cpp}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C11:HowMany2.cpp
\par // The copy-constructor
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <cstring>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("HowMany2.out");
\par 
\par class HowMany2 \{
\par   enum \{ bufsize = 30 \};
\par   char name[bufsize]; // Object identifier
\par   static int object_count;
\par public:
\par   HowMany2(const char* id = 0) \{
\par     if(id) strncpy(name, id, bufsize);
\par     else *name = 0;
\par     ++object_count;
\par     print("HowMany2()");
\par   \}
\par   // The copy-constructor:
\par   HowMany2(const HowMany2& h) \{
\par     strncpy(name, h.name, bufsize);
\par     strncat(name, " copy", bufsize - strlen(name));
\par     ++object_count;
\par     print("HowMany2(HowMany2&)");
\par   \}
\par   // Can't be static (printing name):
\par   void print(const char* msg = 0) const \{
\par     if(msg) out << msg << endl;
\par     out << '\\t' << name << ": "
\par         << "object_count = "
\par         << object_count << endl;
\par   \}
\par   ~HowMany2() \{
\par     --object_count;
\par     print("~HowMany2()");
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int HowMany2::object_count = 0;
\par 
\par // Pass and return BY VALUE:
\par HowMany2 f(HowMany2 x) \{
\par   x.print("x argument inside f()");
\par   out << "returning from f()" << endl;
\par   return x;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   HowMany2 h("h");
\par   out << "entering f()" << endl;
\par   HowMany2 h2 = f(h);
\par   h2.print("h2 after call to f()");
\par   out << "call f(), no return value" << endl;
\par   f(h);
\par   out << "after call to f()" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There are a number of new twists thrown in here so you can get a better idea of what\rquote s happening. First, the character buffer }{\b name}{
 acts as an object identifier so you can figure out which object the information is being printed about. In the constructor, you can put an identifier string (usually the name of the object) that is copied to }{\b name}{
 using the Standard C library function }{\b strncpy(\~)}{, which only copies a certain number of characters, preventing overrun of the buffer.
\par Next is the copy-constructor, }{\b HowMany2(HowMany2&)}{. The copy-constructor can create a new object only from an existing one, so the existing object\rquote s name is copied to }{\b name}{, followed by the word \ldblquote copy\rdblquote 
 so you can see where it came from. Note the use of the Standard C library function }{\b strncat(\~)}{ to copy a maximum number of characters into }{\b name}{, again to prevent overrunning the end of the buffer.
\par Inside the copy-constructor, the object count is incremented just as it is inside the normal constructor. This means you\rquote ll now get an accurate object count when passing and returning by value.
\par The }{\b print(\~)}{ function has been modified to print out a message, the object identifier, and the object count. It must now access the }{\b name}{ data of a particular object, so it can no longer be a }{\b static}{ member function}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {member\: static member function}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: static member
}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {static\: member function}}}{.
\par Inside }{\b main(\~)}{, you can see a second call to }{\b f(\~)}{ has been added. However, this call uses the common C approach of ignoring the return value. But now that you know how the value is returned (that is, code }{\i inside}{
 the function handles the return process, putting the result in a destination whose address is passed as a hidden argument), you might wonder what happens when the return value is ignored. The output of the program will throw some illumination on this.

\par Before showing the output, here\rquote s a little program that uses iostreams to add line numbers to any file:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C11:Linenum.cpp
\par // Add line numbers
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <strstream>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1, "Usage: linenum file\\n"
\par     "Adds line numbers to file");
\par   strstream text;
\par   \{
\par     ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par     assure(in, argv[1]);
\par     text << in.rdbuf(); // Read in whole file
\par   \} // Close file
\par   ofstream out(argv[1]); // Overwrite file
\par   assure(out, argv[1]);
\par   const int bsz = 100;
\par   char buf[bsz];
\par   int line = 0;
\par   while(text.getline(buf, bsz)) \{
\par     out.setf(ios::right, ios::adjustfield);
\par     out.width(2);
\par     out << ++line << ") " << buf << endl;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The entire file is read into a }{\b strstream}{ (which can be both written to and read from) and the }{\b ifstream}{ is closed with scoping. Then an }{\b 
ofstream}{ is created for the same file, overwriting it. }{\b getline(\~)}{ fetches a line at a time from the }{\b strstream}{ and line numbers are added as the line is written back into the file.
\par The line numbers are printed right-aligned in a field width of two, so the output still lines up in its original configuration. You can change the program to add an optional second command-line argument that allows the user to select a field width, }{\i 
or}{ you can be more clever and count all the lines in the file to determine the field width automatically.
\par When }{\b Linenum.cpp}{ is applied to }{\b HowMany2.out}{, the result is
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid { 1) HowMany2()
\par  2)   h: object_count = 1
\par  3) entering f()
\par  4) HowMany2(HowMany2&)
\par  5)   h copy: object_count = 2
\par  6) x argument inside f()
\par  7)   h copy: object_count = 2
\par  8) returning from f()
\par  9) HowMany2(HowMany2&)
\par 10)   h copy copy: object_count = 3
\par 11) ~HowMany2()
\par 12)   h copy: object_count = 2
\par 13) h2 after call to f()
\par 14)   h copy copy: object_count = 2
\par 15) call f(), no return value
\par 16) HowMany2(HowMany2&)
\par 17)   h copy: object_count = 3
\par 18) x argument inside f()
\par 19)   h copy: object_count = 3
\par 20) returning from f()
\par 21) HowMany2(HowMany2&)
\par 22)   h copy copy: object_count = 4
\par 23) ~HowMany2()
\par 24)   h copy: object_count = 3
\par 25) ~HowMany2()
\par 26)   h copy copy: object_count = 2
\par 27) after call to f()
\par 28) ~HowMany2()
\par 29)   h copy copy: object_count = 1
\par 30) ~HowMany2()
\par 31)   h: object_count = 0
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As you would expect, the first thing that happens is the normal constructor is called for }{\b h}{, which increments the object count to one. But then, as }{
\b f(\~)}{ is entered, the copy-constructor is quietly called by the compiler to perform the pass-by-value. A new object is created, which is the copy of }{\b h}{ (thus the name \ldblquote h copy\rdblquote ) inside the function frame of }{\b f(\~)}{
, so the object count becomes two, courtesy of the copy-constructor.
\par Line eight indicates the beginning of the return from }{\b f(\~)}{. But before the local variable \ldblquote h copy\rdblquote 
 can be destroyed (it goes out of scope at the end of the function), it must be copied into the return value, which happens to be }{\b h2}{. A previously unconstructed object (}{\b h2}{) is created from an existing object (the local variable inside }{\b 
f(\~)}{), so of course the copy-constructor is used again in line nine. Now the name becomes \ldblquote h copy copy\rdblquote  for }{\b h2}{\rquote s identifier because it\rquote s being copied from the copy that is the local object inside }{\b f(\~)}{
. After the object is returned, but before the function ends, the object count becomes temporarily three, but then the local object \ldblquote h copy\rdblquote  is destroyed. After the call to }{\b f(\~)}{
