\par It turns out the compiler can create a temporary object whenever it needs one to properly evaluate an expression. In this case it creates one you don\rquote t even see to act as the destination for the ignored return value of }{\b f(\~)}{
. The lifetime of this temporary object }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {lifetime\: of temporary objects}}}{is as short as possible so the landscape doesn\rquote 
t get cluttered up with temporaries waiting to be destroyed, taking up valuable resources. In some cases, the temporary might be immediately passed to another function, but in this case it isn\rquote t needed after the function call, so as s
oon as the function call ends by calling the destructor for the local object (lines 23 and 24), the temporary object is destroyed (lines 25 and 26).
\par Now, in lines 28-31, the }{\b h2}{ object is destroyed, followed by }{\b h}{, and the object count goes correctly back to zero.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373965}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776184}Default copy-constructor
