\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {copy-constructor\: default}}}{Because the copy-constructor implements pass and return by value, it\rquote 
s important that the compiler will create one for you in the case of simple structures \endash  effectively, the same thing it does in C. However, all you\rquote ve seen so far is the default primitive behavior: a bitcopy.
\par When more complex types are involved, the C++ compiler will still automatically create a copy-constructor if you don\rquote t make one. Again, however, a bitcopy }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {bitcopy}}}{doesn\rquote t make sense, because it doesn\rquote t necessarily implement the proper meaning.
\par Here\rquote s an example to show the more intelligent approach the compiler takes. Suppose you create a new class composed of objects of several existing classes. This is called, appropriately enough, }{\i composition}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\: composition}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {composition}}}{, and it\rquote 
s one of the ways you can make new classes from existing classes. Now take the role of a naive user who\rquote s trying to solve a problem quickly by creating the new class this way. You don\rquote t know about copy-constructors, so you don\rquote 
t create one. The example demonstrates what the compiler does while creating the default copy-constructor for your new class:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C11:Autocc.cpp
\par // Automatic copy-constructor
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <cstring>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class WithCC \{ // With copy-constructor
\par public:
\par   // Explicit default constructor required:
\par   WithCC() \{\}
\par   WithCC(const WithCC&) \{
\par     cout << "WithCC(WithCC&)" << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class WoCC \{ // Without copy-constructor
\par   enum \{ bsz = 30 \};
\par   char buf[bsz];
\par public:
\par   WoCC(const char* msg = 0) \{
\par     memset(buf, 0, bsz);
\par     if(msg) strncpy(buf, msg, bsz);
\par   \}
\par   void print(const char* msg = 0) const \{
\par     if(msg) cout << msg << ": ";
\par     cout << buf << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Composite \{
\par   WithCC withcc; // Embedded objects
\par   WoCC wocc;
\par public:
\par   Composite() : wocc("Composite()") \{\}
\par   void print(const char* msg = 0) \{
\par     wocc.print(msg);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Composite c;
\par   c.print("contents of c");
\par   cout << "calling Composite copy-constructor"
\par        << endl;
\par   Composite c2 = c;  // Calls copy-constructor
\par   c2.print("contents of c2");
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The class }{\b WithCC}{ contains a copy-constructor, which simply announces it has been called, and this brings up an interesting issue. In the class }{\b 
Composite}{, an object of }{\b WithCC}{ is created using a default constructor. If there were no constructors at all in }{\b WithCC}{, the compiler would automatically create a default constructor}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: default}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {default\: constructor}}}{
, which would do nothing in this case. However, if you add a copy-constructor, you\rquote ve told the compiler you\rquote re going to handle constructor creation, so it no longer creates a default constructor for you and will complain unless you explicitl
y create a default constructor as was done for }{\b WithCC}{.
\par The class }{\b WoCC}{ has no copy-constructor, but its constructor will store a message in an internal buffer that can be printed out using }{\b print(\~)}{. This constructor is explicitly called in }{\b Composite}{\rquote s}{\b  }{const
ructor initializer list (briefly introduced in Chapter XX and covered fully in Chapter XX). The reason for this becomes apparent later.
\par The class }{\b Composite}{ has member objects of both }{\b WithCC}{ and }{\b WoCC }{(note the embedded object }{\b WOCC}{ is initialized in the constructor-initializer list, as it must be), and no explicitly defined copy-constructor. However, in }{\b 
main(\~)}{ an object is created using the copy-constructor in the definition:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Composite c2 = c;
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The copy-constructor for }{\b Composite }{is created automatically by the compiler, and the output of the program reveals how it is created.
\par To create a copy-constructor for a class that uses composition (and inheritance, which is introduced in Chapter XX), the compiler recursively calls the copy-constructors for all the membe
r objects and base classes. That is, if the member object also contains another object, its copy-constructor is also called. So in this case, the compiler calls the copy-constructor for }{\b WithCC}{
. The output shows this constructor being called. Because }{\b WoCC}{ has no copy-constructor, the compiler creates one for it, which is the default behavior of a bitcopy, and calls that inside the }{\b Composite}{ copy-constructor. The call to }{\b 
Composite::print(\~)}{ in main shows that this happens because the contents of }{\b c2.WOCC}{ are identical to the contents of }{\b c.WOCC}{. The process the compiler goes through to synthesize a copy-constructor is called }{\i memberwise initialization}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {initialization\: memberwise}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {memberwise\:
 initialization}}}{.
\par It\rquote s best to always create your own copy-constructor rather than letting the compiler do it for you. This guarantees it will be under your control.
