{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776187}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A similar exercise produces the pointer-to-member syntax for member functions. A pointer to a function is defined like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int (*fp)(float);
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The parentheses around }{\b (*fp)}{
 are necessary to force the compiler to evaluate the definition properly. Without them this would appear to be a function that returns an }{\b int*}{. 
\par To define and use a pointer to a member function, parentheses play a similarly important role. If you have a function inside a structure:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {struct simple2 \{ int f(float); \};
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {you define a pointer to that member function by inserting the class name and scope resolution operator into an ordinary function pointer definition:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int (simple2::*fp)(float);
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can also initialize it when you create it, or at any other time:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int (simple2::*fp)(float) = &simple2::f;
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As with normal functions, the }{\b &}{ is optional; you can give the function identifier without an argument list to mean the address:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {fp = simple2::f;
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {An example
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The value of a pointer is that you can change what it points to at runtime, which provides an important flexibility in your programming because throug
h a pointer you can select or change }{\i behavior}{
 at runtime. A pointer-to-member is no different; it allows you to choose a member at runtime. Typically, your classes will have only member functions publicly visible (data members are usually considered part o
f the underlying implementation), so the following example selects member functions at runtime.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C11:Pmem.cpp
\par // Pointers to members
\par 
\par class Widget \{
\par public:
\par   void f(int);
\par   void g(int);
\par   void h(int);
\par   void i(int);
\par \};
\par 
\par void Widget::h(int) \{\}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Widget w;
\par   Widget* wp = &w;
\par   void (Widget::*pmem)(int) = &Widget::h;
\par   (w.*pmem)(1);
\par   (wp->*pmem)(2);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Of course, it isn\rquote t particularly reasonable to expect the casual user to create such complicated expressions. If the user must directly
 manipulate a pointer-to-member, then a }{\b typedef}{ is in order. To really clean things up, you can use the pointer-to-member as part of the internal implementation mechanism. Here\rquote s the preceding example using a pointer-to-member }{\i inside}{
 the class. All the user needs to do is pass a number in to select a function.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {
\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ Thanks to Owen Mortensen}{\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {Mortensen, Owen}}}{ for this example}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C11:Pmem2.cpp
\par // Pointers to members
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Widget \{
\par   void f(int) const \{cout << "Widget::f()\\n";\}
\par   void g(int) const \{cout << "Widget::g()\\n";\}
\par   void h(int) const \{cout << "Widget::h()\\n";\}
\par   void i(int) const \{cout << "Widget::i()\\n";\}
\par   enum \{ _count = 4 \};
\par   void (Widget::*fptr[_count])(int) const;
\par public:
\par   Widget() \{
\par     fptr[0] = &Widget::f; // Full spec required
\par     fptr[1] = &Widget::g;
\par     fptr[2] = &Widget::h;
\par     fptr[3] = &Widget::i;
\par   \}
\par   void select(int i, int j) \{
\par     if(i < 0 || i >= _count) return;
\par     (this->*fptr[i])(j);
\par   \}
\par   int count() \{ return _count; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Widget w;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < w.count(); i++)
\par     w.select(i, 47);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the class interface and in }{\b main(\~)}{
, you can see that the entire implementation, including the functions themselves, has been hidden away. The code must even ask for the }{\b count(\~)}{ of functions. This way, the class implementor can change t
he quantity of functions in the underlying implementation without affecting the code where the class is used.
\par The initialization of the pointers-to-members in the constructor may seem overspecified. Shouldn\rquote t you be able to say
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {fptr[1] = &g;
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {because the name }{\b g}{ occurs in the member function, which is automatically in the scope of the class? The problem is this doesn\rquote 
t conform to the pointer-to-member syntax, which is required so everyone, especially the compiler, can figure out what\rquote s going on. Similarly, when the pointer-to-member is dereferenced, it seems like
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {(this->*fptr[i])(j);
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {is also over-specified; }{\b this}{
 looks redundant. Again, the syntax requires that a pointer-to-member always be bound to an object when it is dereferenced.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593231}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628703}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373969
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776188}Summary{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593231}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628703}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373969}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776188}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Pointers in C++ are remarkably similar to pointers in C, which is good. Otherwise a lot of C code wouldn\rquote 
t compile properly under C++. The only compiler errors you will produce is where dangerous assignments occur. If these are in fact what are intended, the compiler errors can be removed with a simple (and explicit!) cast.
\par C++ also adds the }{\i reference}{ from Algol and Pascal, which is like a constant pointer that is automatically dereferenced by the compiler. A reference holds an address, but you treat it like an object. Refere
nces are essential for clean syntax with operator overloading (the subject of the next chapter), but they also add syntactic convenience for passing and returning objects for ordinary functions.
\par The copy-constructor takes a reference to an existing object 
of the same type as its argument, and it is used to create a new object from an existing one. The compiler automatically calls the copy-constructor when you pass or return an object by value. Although the compiler will automatically create a copy-construc
tor for you, if you think one will be needed for your class you should always define it yourself to ensure that the proper behavior occurs. If you don\rquote t want the object passed or returned by value, you should create a private copy-constructor.

\par Pointers-to-m
embers have the same functionality as ordinary pointers: You can choose a particular region of storage (data or function) at runtime. Pointers-to-members just happen to work with class members rather than global data or functions. You get the programming 
flexibility that allows you to change behavior at runtime.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373970}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776189}Exercises
