\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The difference is the arguments for this function don\rquote t appear inside parentheses, but instead surrounding or next to characters you\rquote ve always 
thought of as immutable operators.
\par There are two differences between the use of an operator and an ordinary function call. The syntax is different; an operator is often \ldblquote called\rdblquote  by placing it between or sometimes after the arguments. The second difference 
is that the compiler determines what \ldblquote function\rdblquote  to call. For instance, if you are using the operator }{\b +}{ with floating-point arguments, the compiler \ldblquote calls\rdblquote 
 the function to perform floating-point addition (this \ldblquote call\rdblquote  is typically the act of inserting in-line code, or a floating-point coprocessor instruction). If you use operator }{\b +}{
 with a floating-point number and an integer, the compiler \ldblquote calls\rdblquote  a special function to turn the }{\b int}{ into a }{\b float}{, and then \ldblquote calls\rdblquote  the floating-point addition code.
\par But in C++, it\rquote s possible to define new operators that work with classes. This definition is just like an ordinary function definition except the name of the function begins with the keyword }{\b operator}{ }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {keyword\: operator}}}{and ends with the operator itself. That\rquote s the only difference, and it becomes a function }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: operator overloading}}}{like any other function, which the compiler calls when it sees the appropriate pattern.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593234}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628706}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373972
