\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It\rquote s very tempting to become overenthusiastic with operator overloading. It\rquote s a fun toy, at first. But remember it\rquote s }{\i only}{
 syntactic sugar, another way of calling a function. Looking at it this way, you have no reason to overload an operator except that it will make the code involving your class easier to write and especially }{\i read}{
. (Remember, code is read much more than it is written.) If this isn\rquote t the case, don\rquote t bother.
\par Another common response to operator overloading is panic: Suddenly, C operators have no familiar meaning anymore. \ldblquote Everything\rquote s changed and all my C code will do different things !\rdblquote  This isn\rquote 
t true. All the operators used in expressions that contain only built-in data types cannot be changed. You can never overload operators such that
