(zero arguments for unary, one for binary \endash  the object becomes the left-hand argument).
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here\rquote s a small class that shows the syntax for operator overloading:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Opover.cpp
\par // Operator overloading syntax
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Integer \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   Integer(int ii) \{ i = ii; \}
\par   const Integer
\par   operator+(const Integer& rv) const \{
\par     cout << "operator+" << endl;
\par     return Integer(i + rv.i);
\par   \}
\par   Integer&
\par   operator+=(const Integer& rv)\{
\par     cout << "operator+=" << endl;
\par     i += rv.i;
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   cout << "built-in types:" << endl;
\par   int i = 1, j = 2, k = 3;
\par   k += i + j;
\par   cout << "user-defined types:" << endl;
\par   Integer I(1), J(2), K(3);
\par   K += I + J;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The two overloaded operators are defined as inline member functions that announce when they are called. The single argument is what appears on the right-hand side of the operator for binary operators. Unary operators have no arguments when 
defined as member functions. The member function is called for the object on the left-hand side of the operator.
\par For nonconditional operators (conditionals usually return a Boolean value) you\rquote ll almost always want to return }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {return\: overloaded operator return type}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: overloaded return type}}}{an object or reference of the same type you\rquote re operating on if the two arguments are the same type. If they\rquote 
re not, the interpretation of what it should produce is up to you. This way complex expressions can be built up:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  K += I + J;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b operator+}{ produces a new }{\b Integer}{ (a temporary) that is used as the }{\b rv}{ argument for the }{\b operator+=}{. This temporary is destroyed as soon as it is no longer needed.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593236}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628708}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373974}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776193}
Overloadable operators{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593236}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628708}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373974}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776193}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Although you can overload }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: overloading, which operators can be overloaded}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\: which operators can be overloaded}}}{almost all the operators available in C, the use is fairly restrictive. In particular, you cannot combine operators that currently have no meaning in C (such as }{\b **}{
 to represent exponentiation), you cannot change the evaluation precedence of operators, and you cannot change the number of arguments an operator takes. This makes sense \endash  all these actions would produce operators that confuse meaning rathe
r than clarify it.
\par The next two subsections give examples of all the \ldblquote regular\rdblquote  operators, overloaded in the form that you\rquote ll most likely use.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373975}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776194}Unary operators{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373975}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776194}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The following example shows the syntax to overload all the unary operators}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: unary overloading examples}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {unary\: examples of all overloaded unary operators}}}{, both in the form of global functions and member functions. These will expand upon the }{\b Integer }{class shown previously and add a new }{\b byte }{
class. The meaning of your particular operators will depend on the way you want to use them, but consider the client programmer before doing something unexpected.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Unary.cpp
\par // Overloading unary operators
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Integer \{
\par   long i;
\par   Integer* This() \{ return this; \}
\par public:
\par   Integer(long ll = 0) : i(ll) \{\}
\par   // No side effects takes const& argument:
\par   friend const Integer&
\par     operator+(const Integer& a);
\par   friend const Integer
\par     operator-(const Integer& a);
\par   friend const Integer
\par     operator~(const Integer& a);
\par   friend Integer*
\par     operator&(Integer& a);
\par   friend int
\par     operator!(const Integer& a);
\par   // Side effects don't take const& argument:
\par   // Prefix:
\par   friend const Integer&
\par     operator++(Integer& a);
\par   // Postfix:
\par   friend const Integer
\par     operator++(Integer& a, int);
\par   // Prefix:
\par   friend const Integer&
\par     operator--(Integer& a);
\par   // Postfix:
\par   friend const Integer
\par     operator--(Integer& a, int);
\par \};
\par 
\par // Global operators:
\par const Integer& operator+(const Integer& a) \{
\par   cout << "+Integer\\n";
\par   return a; // Unary + has no effect
\par \}
\par const Integer operator-(const Integer& a) \{
\par   cout << "-Integer\\n";
\par   return Integer(-a.i);
\par \}
\par const Integer operator~(const Integer& a) \{
\par   cout << "~Integer\\n";
\par   return Integer(~a.i);
\par \}
\par Integer* operator&(Integer& a) \{
\par   cout << "&Integer\\n";
\par   return a.This(); // &a is recursive!
\par \}
\par int operator!(const Integer& a) \{
\par   cout << "!Integer\\n";
\par   return !a.i;
\par \}
\par // Prefix; return incremented value
\par const Integer& operator++(Integer& a) \{
\par   cout << "++Integer\\n";
\par   a.i++;
\par   return a;
\par \}
\par // Postfix; return the value before increment:
\par const Integer operator++(Integer& a, int) \{
\par   cout << "Integer++\\n";
\par   Integer r(a.i);
\par   a.i++;
\par   return r;
\par \}
\par // Prefix; return decremented value
\par const Integer& operator--(Integer& a) \{
\par   cout << "--Integer\\n";
\par   a.i--;
\par   return a;
\par \}
\par // Postfix; return the value before decrement:
\par const Integer operator--(Integer& a, int) \{
\par   cout << "Integer--\\n";
\par   Integer r(a.i);
\par   a.i--;
\par   return r;
\par \}
\par 
\par void f(Integer a) \{
\par   +a;
\par   -a;
\par   ~a;
\par   Integer* ip = &a;
\par   !a;
\par   ++a;
\par   a++;
\par   --a;
\par   a--;
\par \}
\par 
\par // Member operators (implicit "this"):
\par class Byte \{
\par   unsigned char b;
\par public:
\par   Byte(unsigned char bb = 0) : b(bb) \{\}
\par   // No side effects: const member function:
\par   const Byte& operator+() const \{
\par     cout << "+Byte\\n";
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par   const Byte operator-() const \{
\par     cout << "-Byte\\n";
\par     return Byte(-b);
\par   \}
\par   const Byte operator~() const \{
\par     cout << "~Byte\\n";
\par     return Byte(~b);
\par   \}
\par   Byte operator!() const \{
\par     cout << "!Byte\\n";
\par     return Byte(!b);
\par   \}
\par   Byte* operator&() \{
\par     cout << "&Byte\\n";
\par     return this;
\par   \}
\par   // Side effects: non-const member function:
\par   const Byte& operator++() \{ // Prefix
\par     cout << "++Byte\\n";
\par     b++;
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par   const Byte operator++(int) \{ // Postfix
\par     cout << "Byte++\\n";
\par     Byte before(b);
\par     b++;
\par     return before;
\par   \}
\par   const Byte& operator--() \{ // Prefix
\par     cout << "--Byte\\n";
\par     --b;
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par   const Byte operator--(int) \{ // Postfix
\par     cout << "Byte--\\n";
\par     Byte before(b);
\par     --b;
\par     return before;
\par   \}
\par \};
