\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The functions are grouped according to the way their arguments are passe
d. Guidelines for how to pass and return arguments are given later. The above forms (and the ones that follow in the next section) are typically what you\rquote ll use, so start with them as a pattern when overloading your own operators.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Increment & decrement
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {decrement\: overloading operator}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {increment\: overloading operator}}}{
The overloaded }{\b ++}{ and }{\b \endash  \endash  }{operators present a dilemma because you want to be able to call different functions depending on whether they appear before (prefix) or after (postfix) the object they\rquote 
re acting upon. The solution is simple, but some people find it a bit confusing at first. When the compiler sees, for example, }{\b ++a}{ (a preincrement), it generates a call to }{\b operator++}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {overloading\: operator++}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\:++}}}{\b (a)}{; but when it sees }{\b a++,}{ it generates a call to }{\b operator++(a, int)}{
. That is, the compiler differentiates between the two forms by making different function calls. In }{\b Unary.cpp}{ for the member function versions, if the compiler sees }{\b ++b}{, it generates a call to }{\b B::operator++(\~)}{; and if it sees }{\b 
b++}{ it calls }{\b B::operator++(int)}{.
\par The user never sees the result of her action except that a different function gets called for the prefix }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {prefix operator increment & decrement}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: prefix increment & decrement}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {postfix operator increment & decrement}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\:
 postfix increment & decrement}}}{and postfix versions. Underneath, however, the two functions calls have different signatures, so they link to two different function bodies. The compiler passes a dummy constant value for the }{\b int}{
 argument (which is never given an identifier because the value is never used) to generate the different signature for the postfix version.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373976}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776195}Binary operators{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373976}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776195}
