\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can see that }{\b operator=}{ is only allowed to be a member function. This is explained later.
\par Notice that all the assignment operators have code to check for self-assignment}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {self-assignment\: check for in operator overloading}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {check for self-assignment in operator overloading}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: overloading, check for self-assignment}}}{, as a general guideline. In some cases this is not necessary; for example, with }
{\b operator+=}{ you may }{\i want}{ to say }{\b A+=A}{ and have it add }{\b A}{ to itself. The most important place to check for self-assignment is }{\b operator=}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: =}}}{
 because with complicated objects disastrous results may occur. (In some cases it\rquote s OK, but you should always keep it in mind when writing }{\b operator=}{.)
\par All of the operators shown in the previous two examples are overloaded to handle a single type. It\rquote 
s also possible to overload operators to handle mixed types, so you can add apples to oranges, for example. Before you start on an exhaustive overloading of operators, however, you
 should look at the section on automatic type conversion later in this chapter. Often, a type conversion in the right place can save you a lot of overloaded operators.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373977}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776196}Arguments & return values{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373977}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776196}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: overloading, arguments and return values}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {arguments\:
 and return values, operator overloading}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {return\: operator overloading arguments and return values}}}{It may seem a little confusing at first when you look at }{\b Unary.cpp}{ and }{\b 
Binary.cpp}{ and see all the different ways that arguments are passed and returned. Although you }{\i can}{
 pass and return arguments any way you want to, the choices in these examples were not selected at random. They follow a very logical pattern, the same one you\rquote ll want to use in most of your choices.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls261\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls261\adjustright {As with any function argumen
t, if you only need to read from the argument and not change it, default to passing it as a }{\b const}{ reference}{\pard\plain \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls261\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls261\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {reference\: const}}}{\pard\plain \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls261\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls261\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: reference}}}{. Ordinary arithmetic operations (like }{\b + }{and }{\b \endash }
{, etc.) and Booleans will not change their arguments, so pass by }{\b const }{reference is predominantly what you\rquote ll use. When the function is a class member, this translates to making it a }{\b const}{
 member function. Only with the operator-assignments (like }{\b +=}{) and the }{\b operator=}{, which change the left-hand argument, is the left argument }{\i not}{ a constant, but it\rquote s still passed in as an address because it will be changed.

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls261\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls261\adjustright {
The type of return value you should select depends on the expected meaning of the operator. (Again, you can do anything you want with the arguments and return
 values.) If the effect of the operator is to produce a new value, you will need to generate a new object as the return value. For example, }{\b Integer::operator+}{ must produce an }{\b Integer}{
 object that is the sum of the operands. This object is returned by value as a }{\b const}{, so the result cannot be modified as an lvalue.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls261\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls261\adjustright {All the assignment operators}
{\pard\plain \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls261\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls261\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: assignment}}}{\pard\plain \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls261\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls261\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {assignment\: operators}}}{ modify the lvalue. To allow the result of the assignment to be used in chained expressions, like }{\b A=B=C}{, it\rquote s expe
cted that you will return a reference to that same lvalue that was just modified. But should this reference be a }{\b const}{ or non}{\b const}{? Although you read }{\b A=B=C}{ from left to right, the compiler parses it from right to left, so you\rquote 
re not forced to return a non}{\b const}{ to support assignment chaining. However, people do sometimes expect to be able to perform an operation on the thing that was just assigned to, such as }{\b (A=B).func(\~);}{ to call }{\b func(\~)}{ on }{\b A}{
 after assigning }{\b B}{ to it. Thus the return value for all the assignment operators should be a non}{\b const}{ reference to the lvalue.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 4.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls261\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls261\adjustright {For the logical operators}
{\pard\plain \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls261\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls261\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: logical}}}{\pard\plain \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls261\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls261\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {logical\: operators}}}{, everyone expects to get at worst an }{\b int}{ back, and at best a }{\b bool}{
. (Libraries developed before most compilers supported C++\rquote s built-in }{\b bool}{ will use }{\b int}{ or an equivalent }{\b typedef}{).
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls261\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls261\adjustright {
The increment and decrement operators }{\pard\plain \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls261\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls261\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {decrement\: and increment operators}}}{\pard\plain 
\fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls261\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls261\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: increment and decrement}}}{\pard\plain \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls261\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls261\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {increment\: and decrement operators}}}{present a dilemma because of the pr
e- and postfix versions. Both versions change the object and so cannot treat the object as a }{\b const}{
. The prefix version returns the value of the object after it was changed, so you expect to get back the object that was changed. Thus, with prefix you can just return }{\b *this}{ as a reference. The postfix version is supposed to return the value }{\i 
before}{ the value is changed, so you\rquote re forced to create a separate object to represent that value and return it. Thus, with postfix you must return by value if you want to preserve the expected meaning. (Note that you\rquote 
ll often find the increment and decrement operators returning an }{\b int}{ or }{\b bool}{ to indicate, for example, whether an iterator is at the end of a list). Now the question is: Should these be returned as }{\b const}{ or non}{\b const}{
? If you allow the object to be modified and someone writes }{\b (++A).func(\~);}{, }{\b func(\~)}{ will be operating on }{\b A}{ itself, but with }{\b (A++).func(\~);}{, }{\b func(\~)}{ operates on the temporary object returned by the postfix }{\b 
operator++}{. Temporary objects are automatically }{\b const}{, so this would be flagged by the compiler, but for consistency\rquote s sake it may make more sense to make them both }{\b const}{
, as was done here. Because of the variety of meanings you may want to give the increment and decrement operators, they will need to be considered on a case-by-case basis.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Return by value as }{\b const}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {return\: by value as const}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: return by value as const}}}{
Returning by value as a }{\b const}{ can seem a bit subtle at first, and so deserves a bit more explanation. Consider the binary }{\b operator+}{. If you use it in an expression such as }{\b f(A+B)}{, the result of }{\b A+B}{
 becomes a temporary object that is used in the call to }{\b f(\~)}{. Because it\rquote s a temporary, it\rquote s automatically }{\b const}{, so whether you explicitly make the return value }{\b const}{ or not has no effect.
\par However, it\rquote s also possible for you to send a message to the return value of }{\b A+B}{, rather than just passing it to a function. For example, you can say }{\b (A+B).g(\~)}{, where }{\b g(\~)}{ is some member function of }{\b Integer}{
, in this case. By making the return value }{\b const}{, you state that only a }{\b const}{ member function can be called for that return value. This is }{\b const}{
-correct, because it prevents you from storing potentially valuable information in an object that will most likely be lost.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\b return}{ efficiency
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {return\: efficiency when creating and returning objects}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {efficiency\:
 when creating and returning objects}}}{When new objects are created to return by value, notice the form used. In }{\b operator+}{, for example:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {return Integer(left.i + right.i);
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This may look at first like a \ldblquote function call to a constructor,\rdblquote  but it\rquote s not. The syntax is that of a temporary object; the statement says \ldblquote make a temporary }{\b 
Integer}{ object and return it.\rdblquote  Because of this, you might think that the result is the same as creating a named local object and returning that. However, it\rquote s quite different. If you were to say instead:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Integer tmp(left.i + right.i);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {return tmp;
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {three things will happen. First, the }{\b tmp}{ object is created including its constructor call. Then, the copy-constructor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {copy
-constructor}}}{ copies the }{\b tmp}{ to the location of the outside return value. Finally, the destructor is called for }{\b tmp}{ at the end of the scope.
\par In contrast, the \ldblquote returning a temporary\rdblquote  approach works quite differently. When the compiler sees you do this, it knows that you have no other need for the object it\rquote s creating than to return it so it builds the object }{\i 
directly}{ into the location of the outside return value. This requires only a single ordinary constructor call (no copy-constructor is necessary) and there\rquote s no destructor call because you never actually create a local object. Thus, while it doesn
\rquote t cost anything but programmer awareness, it\rquote s significantly more efficient.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373978}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776197}Unusual operators{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373978}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776197}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {unusual operator overloading}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: unusual overloaded}}}{
Several additional operators have a slightly different syntax for overloading.
\par The subscript, }{\b operator[ ]}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: []}}}{, must be a member function and it requires a single argument. Because it implies that the object acts like an array, you will 
often return a reference from this operator, so it can be used conveniently on the left-hand side of an equal sign. This operator is commonly overloaded; you\rquote ll see examples in the rest of the book.
\par The comma operator}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: comma}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {comma operator}}}{
 is called when it appears next to an object of the type the comma is defined for. However, }{\b operator, }{is }{\i not }{called for function argument lists, only for objects that are out in the open, separated by commas. There doesn\rquote 
t seem to be a lot of practical uses for this operator; it\rquote s in the language for consistency. Here\rquote s an example showing how the comma function can be called when the comma appears }{\i before}{ an object, as well as after:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Comma.cpp
\par // Overloading the \lquote ,\rquote  operator
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class After \{
\par public:
\par   const After& operator,(const After&) const \{
\par     cout << "After::operator,()" << endl;
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Before \{\};
\par 
\par Before& operator,(int, Before& b) \{
\par   cout << "Before::operator,()" << endl;
\par   return b;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   After a, b;
\par   a, b;  // Operator comma called
\par 
\par   Before c;
\par   1, c;  // Operator comma called
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The global function allows the comma to be placed before the object in question. The usage shown is fairly obscure and question
able. Although you would probably use a comma-separated list as part of a more complex expression, it\rquote s too subtle to use in most situations.
\par The }{\i function call}{ }{\b operator(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\: function call operator(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: (\~), function call}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: function call operator(\~)}}}{ must be a member function, and it is unique in that it allows any number of arguments. It makes your object look like it\rquote 
s actually a function name, so it\rquote s probably best used for types that only have a single operation, or at least an especially prominent one.
\par The operators }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ control dynamic storage allocation, and can be overloaded. This very important topic is covered in the next chapter.
\par The }{\b operator\endash >*}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\: operator->* (pointer-to-member)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: ->*}}}{
is a binary operator that behaves like all the other binary operators. It is provided for those situations when you want to mimic the behavior provided by the built-in }{\i pointer-to-member}{ syntax, described in the previous chapter.
\par The }{\i smart pointer}{ }{\b operator\endash >}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\: smart pointer operator->}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: -> smart pointer}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {smart pointer operator->}}}{ is designed to be used when you want to make an object appear to be a pointer. This is especially useful if you want to \ldblquote wrap\rdblquote 
 a class around a pointer to make that pointer safe, or in the common usage of an }{\i iterator}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iterator}}}{, which is an object that moves through a }{\i collection}{ }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {collection}}}{or }{\i container }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {container}}}{of other objects and selects them one at a time, wi
thout providing direct access to the implementation of the container. (You\rquote ll often find containers and iterators in class libraries.)
\par A smart pointer must be a member function. It has additional, atypical constraints: It must return either an object (or r
eference to an object) that also has a smart pointer or a pointer that can be used to select what the smart pointer arrow is pointing at. Here\rquote s a simple example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Smartp.cpp
\par // Smart pointer example
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <cstring>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Obj \{
\par   static int i, j;
\par public:
\par   void f() \{ cout << i++ << endl; \}
\par   void g() \{ cout << j++ << endl; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Static member definitions:
\par int Obj::i = 47;
\par int Obj::j = 11;
\par 
\par // Container:
\par class ObjContainer \{
\par   enum \{ sz = 100 \};
\par   Obj* a[sz];
\par   int index;
\par public:
\par   ObjContainer() \{
\par     index = 0;
\par     memset(a, 0, sz * sizeof(Obj*));
\par   \}
\par   void add(Obj* obj) \{
\par     if(index >= sz) return;
\par     a[index++] = obj;
\par   \}
\par   friend class Sp;
\par \};
\par 
\par // Iterator:
\par class Sp \{
\par   ObjContainer* oc;
\par   int index;
\par public:
\par   Sp(ObjContainer* objc) \{
\par     index = 0;
\par     oc = objc;
\par   \}
\par   // Return value indicates end of list:
\par   int operator++() \{ // Prefix
\par     if(index >= oc->sz) return 0;
\par     if(oc->a[++index] == 0) return 0;
\par     return 1;
\par   \}
\par   int operator++(int) \{ // Postfix
\par     return operator++(); // Use prefix version
\par   \}
\par   Obj* operator->() const \{
\par     if(oc->a[index]) return oc->a[index];
\par     static Obj dummy;
\par     return &dummy;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   const int sz = 10;
\par   Obj o[sz];
\par   ObjContainer oc;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < sz; i++)
\par     oc.add(&o[i]); // Fill it up
\par   Sp sp(&oc); // Create an iterator
\par   do \{
\par     sp->f(); // Smart pointer calls
\par     sp->g();
\par   \} while(sp++);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The class }{\b Obj}{ defines the objects that are manipulated in this program. The functions }{\b f(\~)}{ and }{\b g(\~)}{ simply print out interesting values using }{\b static}{
 data members. Pointers to these objects are stored inside containers of type }{\b ObjContainer}{ using its }{\b add(\~)}{ function. }{\b ObjContainer}{ looks like an array of pointers, but you\rquote ll notice there\rquote s 
no way to get the pointers back out again. However, }{\b Sp}{ is declared as a }{\b friend}{ class, so it has permission to look inside the container. The }{\b Sp}{ class looks very much like an intelligent pointer \endash  you can move it forward using }
{\b operator++}{ (you can also define an }{\b operator\endash  \endash }{), it won\rquote t go past the end of the container it\rquote s pointing to, and it returns (via the smart pointer operator) the value it\rquote 
s pointing to. Notice that an iterator is a custom fit for the container it\rquote s created for \endash  unlike a pointer, there isn\rquote t a \ldblquote general purpose\rdblquote 
 iterator. Containers and iterators are covered in more depth in Chapter XX.
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{, once the container }{\b oc}{ is filled with }{\b Obj}{ objects, an iterator }{\b SP}{ is created. The smart pointer calls happen in the expressions:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {   sp->f(); // Smart pointer calls
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {   sp->g();
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here, even though }{\b sp}{ doesn\rquote t actually have }{\b f(\~)}{ and }{\b g(\~)}{ member functions, the smart pointer mechanism calls those functions for the }{\b Obj*}{ that is returned by }{\b 
Sp::operator\endash >}{. The compiler performs all the checking to make sure the function call works properly.
\par Although the underlying mechanics of the smart pointer are more complex than the other operators, the goal is exactly the same \endash  to provide a more convenient syntax for the users of your classes.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373979}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776198}Operators you can\rquote t overload{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373979}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776198}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: operators you can\rquote t overload}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\:
 operators you can\rquote t overload}}}{There are certain operators in the available set that cannot be overloaded. The general reason for the restriction is safety: If these operators were overlo
adable, it would somehow jeopardize or break safety mechanisms. Often it makes things harder, or confuses existing practice.
\par The member selection }{\b operator.}{. Currently, the dot has a meaning for any member in a class, but if you allow it to be overloaded, then you couldn\rquote t access members in the normal way; instead you\rquote 
d have to use a pointer and the arrow operator }{\b \endash >}{.
\par The pointer to member dereference }{\b operator.*}{. For the same reason as }{\b operator.}{.
\par There\rquote s no exponentiation operator}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exponentiation, no operator}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: no exponentiation}}}{
. The most popular choice for this was }{\b operator** }{from Fortran, but this raised difficult parsing questions. Also, C has no exponentiation operator, so C++ didn\rquote t seem to need one either because you can always perfor
m a function call. An exponentiation operator would add a convenient notation, but no new language functionality, to account for the added complexity of the compiler.
\par There are no user-defined operators}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: no user-defined}}}{. That is, you can\rquote t make up new operators that aren\rquote 
t currently in the set. Part of the problem is how to determine precedence, and part of the problem is an insufficient need to account for the necessary trouble.
\par You can\rquote t change the precedence rules. They\rquote re hard enough to remember as it is, without letting people play with them.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593237}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628709}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373980}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776199}
Nonmember operators{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593237}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628709}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373980}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776199}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {member\: vs. non-member operators}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: member vs. non-member}}}{
In some of the previous examples, the operators may be members or nonmembers, and it doesn\rquote t seem to make much difference. This usually raises the question, \ldblquote Which should I choose?\rdblquote  In general, if it doesn\rquote 
t make any difference, they should be members, to emphasize the association between the operator and its class. When the left-hand operand is an object of the current class, it works fine. 
\par This isn\rquote t always the case \endash  sometimes you want the left-hand operand to be an object of some other class. A very common place to see this is when the operators }{\b <<}{ and }{\b >>}{ are overloaded for iostreams}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inserter\: and extractor, overloading for iostreams}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {extractor\: and inserter, overloading for iostreams}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\: << and >> for iostreams}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: overloading << and >>}}}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Iosop.cpp
\par // Iostream operator overloading
\par // Example of non-member overloaded operators
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <strstream>
\par #include <cstring>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class IntArray \{
\par   enum \{ sz = 5 \};
\par   int i[sz];
\par public:
\par   IntArray() \{
\par     memset(i, 0, sz* sizeof(*i));
\par   \}
\par   int& operator[](int x) \{
\par     require(x >= 0 && x < sz,
\par            "operator[] out of range");
\par     return i[x];
\par   \}
\par   friend ostream&
\par     operator<<(ostream& os,
\par                const IntArray& ia);
\par   friend istream&
\par     operator>>(istream& is, IntArray& ia);
\par \};
\par 
\par ostream& operator<<(ostream& os,
\par                     const IntArray& ia)\{
\par   for(int j = 0; j < ia.sz; j++) \{
\par     os << ia.i[j];
\par     if(j != ia.sz -1)
\par       os << ", ";
\par   \}
\par   os << endl;
\par   return os;
\par \}
\par 
\par istream& operator>>(istream& is, IntArray& ia)\{
\par   for(int j = 0; j < ia.sz; j++)
\par     is >> ia.i[j];
\par   return is;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   istrstream input("47 34 56 92 103");
\par   IntArray I;
\par   input >> I;
