to copying a register at the machine level. In addition, the copy-constructor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {copy-constructor\: vs. operator=}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\:
 = vs. copy-constructor}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: copy-constructor vs. operator=}}}{ (from the previous chapter) can also be invoked when using the }{\b =}{ sign:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {MyType b;
\par MyType a = b;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {a = b;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the second line, the object}{\b  a}{ is being }{\i defined}{. A new object is being created where one didn\rquote 
t exist before. Because you know by now how defensive the C++ compiler is about object initialization, you know that a constructor must always be called at the point where an object is defined. But which constructor? }{\b a}{
 is being created from an existing }{\b MyType}{ object, so there\rquote s only one choice: the copy-constructor. So even though an equal sign is involved, the copy-constructor is called.
\par In the third line, things are different. On the left side of the equal sign, there\rquote s a previously initialized object. Clearly, you don\rquote t call a constructor for an object that\rquote s already been created. In this case }{\b MyType::operator=
}{ is called for }{\b a}{, taking as an argument whatever appears on the right-hand side. (You can have multiple }{\b operator=}{ functions to take different right-hand arguments.)
\par This behavior is not restricted to the copy-constructor. Any time you\rquote re initializing an object using an }{\b =}{
 instead of the ordinary function-call form of the constructor, the compiler will look for a constructor that accepts whatever is on the right-hand side:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:FeeFi.cpp
\par // Copying vs. initialization
\par 
\par class Fi \{
\par public:
\par   Fi() \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Fee \{
\par public:
\par   Fee(int) \{\}
\par   Fee(const Fi&) \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Fee f = 1; // Fee(int)
\par   Fi fi;
\par   Fee fum = fi; // Fee(Fi)
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When dealing with the }{\b =}{ sign, it\rquote s important to keep this distinction in mind: If the object hasn\rquote t been created yet, initialization is required; otherwise the assignment }{\b 
operator=}{ is used.
\par It\rquote s even better to avoid writing code that uses the }{\b =}{ for initialization; instead, always use the explicit constructor form; the last line becomes 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  Fee fum(fi);
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This way, you\rquote ll avoid confusing your readers.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373983}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776202}Behavior of }{\b operator=}{{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373983}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776202}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: = behavior of}}}{In }{\b Binary.cpp}{, you saw that }{\b operator=}{
 can be only a member function. It is intimately connected to the object on the left side of the }{\b =}{, and if you could define }{\b operator=}{ globally, you could try to redefine the built-in }{\b =}{ sign:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int operator=(int, MyType); // Global = not allowed!
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The compiler skirts this whole issue by forcing you to make }{\b operator=}{ a member function.
\par When you create an }{\b operator=}{, you must copy all the necessary information from the right-hand object into yourself to perform whatever you consider \ldblquote assignment\rdblquote  for your class. For simple objects, this is obvious:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Simpcopy.cpp
\par // Simple operator=()
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Value \{
\par   int a, b;
\par   float c;
\par public:
\par   Value(int aa = 0, int bb = 0, float cc = 0.0) \{
\par     a = aa;
\par     b = bb;
\par     c = cc;
\par   \}
\par   Value& operator=(const Value& rv) \{
\par     a = rv.a;
\par     b = rv.b;
\par     c = rv.c;
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par   friend ostream&
\par     operator<<(ostream& os, const Value& rv) \{
\par       return os << "a = " << rv.a << ", b = "
\par         << rv.b << ", c = " << rv.c;
\par     \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Value A, B(1, 2, 3.3);
\par   cout << "A: " << A << endl;
\par   cout << "B: " << B << endl;
\par   A = B;
\par   cout << "A after assignment: " << A << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here, the object on the left side of the }{\b =}{ copies all the elements of the object on the right, then returns a reference to itself, so a more complex expression can be created.
\par A common mistake was made in this example. When you\rquote re assigning two objects of the same type, you should always check first for self-assignment: Is the object being assigned to itself? In some cases, such as this one, it\rquote 
s harmless if you perform the assignment operations anyway, but if changes are made to the implementation of the class it, can make a difference, and if you don\rquote t do it as a matter of habit, you may forget and cause hard-to-find bugs.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Pointers in classes
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {pointer\: in classes}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\: pointers in}}}{
What happens if the object is not so simple? For example, what if the object contains pointers to other objects? Simply copying a pointer}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {copying\: pointers}}}{ means you\rquote 
ll end up with two objects pointing to the same storage location. In situations like these, you need to do bookkeeping of your own.
\par There are two common approaches to this problem. The simplest technique is to copy whatever the pointer refers to when you do an assignment or a copy-constructor. This is very straightforward:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Copymem.cpp \{O\}
\par // Duplicate during assignment
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <cstring>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class WithPointer \{
\par   char* p;
\par   enum \{ blocksz = 100 \};
\par public:
\par   WithPointer() \{
\par     p = (char*)malloc(blocksz);
\par     require(p != 0);
\par     memset(p, 1, blocksz);
\par   \}
\par   WithPointer(const WithPointer& wp) \{
\par     p = (char*)malloc(blocksz);
\par     require(p != 0);
\par     memcpy(p, wp.p, blocksz);
\par   \}
\par   WithPointer&
\par   operator=(const WithPointer& wp) \{
\par     // Check for self-assignment:
\par     if(&wp != this)
\par       memcpy(p, wp.p, blocksz);
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par   ~WithPointer() \{
\par     free(p);
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}; ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This shows the four functions you will always need to define when your class contains pointers: all necessary ordinary constructors, the copy-constructor, }{\b operator=}{ (either define it 
or disallow it), and a destructor. The }{\b operator=}{ checks for self-assignment as a matter of course, even though it\rquote s not strictly necessary here. This virtually eliminates the possibility that you\rquote 
ll forget to check for self-assignment if you }{\i do}{ change the code so that it matters.
\par Here, the constructors allocate the memory and initialize it, the }{\b operator=}{ copies it, and the destructor frees the memory. However, if you\rquote re dealing with a lot of memory or a high overhead to initialize that memory, you may want 
to avoid this copying. A very common approach to this problem is called }{\i reference counting}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {counting\: reference}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {reference\:
 reference counting}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {memory\: management, reference counting}}}{. You make the block of memory smart, so it knows how many 
objects are pointing to it. Then copy-construction or assignment means attaching another pointer to an existing block of memory and incrementing the reference count. Destruction means reducing the reference count and destroying the object if the reference
 count goes to zero.
\par But what if you want to write to the block of memory? More than one object may be using this block, so you\rquote d be modifying someone else\rquote s block as well as yours, which doesn\rquote 
t seem very neighborly. To solve this problem, an additional technique called }{\i copy-on-write}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {copy-on-write (COW)}}}{
is often used. Before writing to a block of memory, you make sure no one else is using it. If the reference count is greater than one, you must make yourself a personal copy of that block before writing it, so you don\rquote t disturb someone else\rquote 
s turf. Here\rquote s a simple example of reference counting and copy-on-write:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Refcount.cpp
\par // Reference count, copy-on-write
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <cstring>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Counted \{
\par   class MemBlock \{
\par     enum \{ size = 100 \};
\par     char c[size];
\par     int refcount;
\par   public:
\par     MemBlock() \{
\par       memset(c, 1, size);
\par       refcount = 1;
\par     \}
\par     MemBlock(const MemBlock& rv) \{
\par       memcpy(c, rv.c, size);
\par       refcount = 1;
\par     \}
\par     void attach() \{ ++refcount; \}
\par     void detach() \{
\par       require(refcount != 0);
\par       // Destroy object if no one is using it:
\par       if(--refcount == 0) delete this;
\par     \}
\par     int count() const \{ return refcount; \}
\par     void set(char x) \{ memset(c, x, size); \}
\par     // Conditionally copy this MemBlock.
\par     // Call before modifying the block; assign
\par     // resulting pointer to your block;
\par     MemBlock* unalias() \{
\par       // Don't duplicate if not aliased:
\par       if(refcount == 1) return this;
\par       --refcount;
\par       // Use copy-constructor to duplicate:
\par       return new MemBlock(*this);
\par     \}
\par   \}* block;
\par public:
\par   Counted() \{
\par     block = new MemBlock; // Sneak preview
\par   \}
\par   Counted(const Counted& rv) \{
\par     block = rv.block; // Pointer assignment
\par     block->attach();
\par   \}
\par   void unalias() \{ block = block->unalias(); \}
\par   Counted& operator=(const Counted& rv) \{
\par     // Check for self-assignment:
\par     if(&rv == this) return *this;
\par     // Clean up what you're using first:
\par     block->detach();
\par     block = rv.block; // Like copy-constructor
\par     block->attach();
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par   // Decrement refcount, conditionally destroy
\par   ~Counted() \{ block->detach(); \}
\par   // Copy-on-write:
\par   void write(char value) \{
\par     // Do this before any write operation:
\par     unalias();
\par     // It's safe to write now.
\par     block->set(value);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Counted A, B;
\par   Counted C(A);
\par   B = A;
\par   C = C;
\par   C.write('x');
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The nested class }{\b MemBlock}{ is the block of memory pointed to. (Notice the pointer }{\b block}{ defined at the end of the nested class.
) It contains a reference count and functions to control and read the reference count. There\rquote s a copy-constructor so you can make a new }{\b MemBlock}{ from an existing one.
\par The }{\b attach(\~)}{ function increments the reference count of a }{\b MemBlock}{ to indicate there\rquote s another object using it. }{\b detach(\~) }{
decrements the reference count. If the reference count goes to zero, then no one is using it anymore, so the member function destroys its own object by saying }{\b delete this}{.
\par You can modify the memory with the }{\b set(\~)}{ function, but before you make any modifications, you should ensure that you aren\rquote t walking on a }{\b MemBlock }{that some other object is using. You do this by calling }{\b Counted::unalias(\~)}{
, which in turn calls }{\b MemBlock::unalias(\~)}{.}{\b  }{The latter function will return the }{\b block}{
 pointer if the reference count is one (meaning no one else is pointing to that block), but will duplicate the block if the reference count is more than one.
\par This example includes a sneak preview of the next chapter. Instead of C\rquote s }{\b malloc(\~)}{ and }{\b free(\~)}{ to create and destroy the objects, the special C++ operators }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ are used. For this example, you can think of }
{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ just like }{\b malloc(\~)}{ and }{\b free(\~)}{, except }{\b new}{ calls the constructor after allocating memory, and }{\b delete}{ calls the destructor before freeing the memory.
\par The copy-constructor, instead of creating its own memory, assigns }{\b block}{ to the }{\b block}{ of the source object. Then, because there\rquote s now an additional object using that block of memory, it increments the reference count by calling }{\b 
MemBlock::attach(\~)}{.
\par The }{\b operator=}{ deals with an object that has already been created on the left side of the }{\b =}{, so it must first clean that up by calling }{\b detach(\~)}{ for that }{\b MemBlock}{, which will destroy the old }{\b MemBlock }{
if no one else is using it. Then }{\b operator=}{ repeats the behavior of the copy-constructor. Notice that it first checks to detect whether you\rquote re assigning the same object to itself.
\par The destructor calls }{\b detach(\~)}{ to conditionally destroy the }{\b MemBlock}{.
\par To implement copy-on-write, you must control all the actions that write to your block of memory. This means you can\rquote t ever hand a raw pointer to the outside world. Instead you say, \ldblquote Tell me what you want done and I\rquote 
ll do it for you!\rdblquote  For example, the }{\b write(\~)}{ member function allows you to change the values in the block of memory. But first, it uses }{\b unalias(\~)}{ to prevent the modification of an aliased block (a block with more than one }{\b 
Counted}{ object using it).
\par }{\b main(\~)}{ tests the various functions that must work correctly to implement reference counting: the constructor, copy-constructor, }{\b operator=}{, and destructor. It also tests the copy-on-write by calling the }{\b write(\~)}{ function for object 
}{\b C}{, which is aliased to }{\b A}{\rquote s memory block. 
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Tracing the output
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To verify that the behavior of this scheme is correct, the best approach is to add information and functionality to the class to generate a trace output that can be analyzed. Here\rquote s }{\b 
Refcount.cpp}{ with added trace information}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {trace information, adding to program}}}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:RefcountTrace.cpp
\par // Refcount.cpp w/ trace info
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <cstring>
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par ofstream out("rctrace.out");
\par 
\par class Counted \{
\par   class MemBlock \{
\par     enum \{ size = 100 \};
\par     char c[size];
\par     int refcount;
\par     static int blockcount;
\par     int blocknum;
\par   public:
\par     MemBlock() \{
\par       memset(c, 1, size);
\par       refcount = 1;
\par       blocknum = blockcount++;
\par     \}
\par     MemBlock(const MemBlock& rv) \{
\par       memcpy(c, rv.c, size);
\par       refcount = 1;
\par       blocknum = blockcount++;
\par       print("copied block");
\par       out << endl;
\par       rv.print("from block");
\par     \}
\par     ~MemBlock() \{
\par       out << "\\tdestroying block "
\par           << blocknum << endl;
\par     \}
\par     void print(const char* msg = "") const \{
\par       if(*msg) out << msg << ", ";
\par       out << "blocknum:" << blocknum;
\par       out << ", refcount:" << refcount;
\par     \}
\par     void attach() \{ ++refcount; \}
\par     void detach() \{
\par       require(refcount != 0);
\par       // Destroy object if no one is using it:
\par       if(--refcount == 0) delete this;
\par     \}
\par     int count() const \{ return refcount; \}
\par     void set(char x) \{ memset(c, x, size); \}
\par     // Conditionally copy this MemBlock.
\par     // Call before modifying the block; assign
\par     // resulting pointer to your block;
\par     MemBlock* unalias() \{
\par       // Don't duplicate if not aliased:
\par       if(refcount == 1) return this;
\par       --refcount;
\par       // Use copy-constructor to duplicate:
\par       return new MemBlock(*this);
\par     \}
\par   \}* block;
\par   enum \{ sz = 30 \};
\par   char ident[sz];
\par public:
\par   Counted(const char* id = "tmp") \{
\par     block = new MemBlock; // Sneak preview
\par     strncpy(ident, id, sz);
\par   \}
\par   Counted(const Counted& rv) \{
\par     block = rv.block; // Pointer assignment
\par     block->attach();
\par     strncpy(ident, rv.ident, sz);
\par     strncat(ident, " copy", sz - strlen(ident));
\par   \}
\par   void unalias() \{ block = block->unalias(); \}
\par   void addname(const char* nm) \{
\par     strncat(ident, nm, sz - strlen(ident));
\par   \}
\par   Counted& operator=(const Counted& rv) \{
\par     print("inside operator=\\n\\t");
\par     if(&rv == this) \{
\par       out << "self-assignment" << endl;
\par       return *this;
\par     \}
\par     // Clean up what you're using first:
\par     block->detach();
\par     block = rv.block; // Like copy-constructor
\par     block->attach();
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par   // Decrement refcount, conditionally destroy
\par   ~Counted() \{
\par     out << "preparing to destroy: " << ident
\par         << "\\n\\tdecrementing refcount ";
\par     block->print();
\par     out << endl;
\par     block->detach();
\par   \}
\par   // Copy-on-write:
\par   void write(char value) \{
\par     unalias();
\par     block->set(value);
\par   \}
\par   void print(const char* msg = "") \{
\par     if(*msg) out << msg << " ";
\par     out << "object " << ident << ": ";
\par     block->print();
\par     out << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int Counted::MemBlock::blockcount = 0;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Counted A("A"), B("B");
\par   Counted C(A);
\par   C.addname(" (C) ");
\par   A.print();
\par   B.print();
\par   C.print();
\par   B = A;
\par   A.print("after assignment\\n\\t");
\par   B.print();
\par   out << "Assigning C = C" << endl;
\par   C = C;
\par   C.print("calling C.write('x')\\n\\t");
\par   C.write('x');
\par   out << "\\n exiting main()" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now }{\b MemBlock}{ contains a }{\b static}{ data member }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {member\: static data member}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {static\: data member}}}{\b blockcount }{to keep track of the number of blocks created, and to create a unique number (stored in }{\b blocknum}{)}{\b  }{for each block so you can tell them apart. The destructor announces which bloc
k is being destroyed, and the }{\b print(\~)}{ function displays the block number and reference count.
\par The }{\b Counted}{ class contains a buffer }{\b ident}{ to keep track of information about the object. The }{\b Counted}{ constructor creates a }{\b new}{ }{\b MemBlock}{ object and assigns the result (a pointer to the }{\b MemBlock}{
 object on the heap) to }{\b block}{. The identifier, copied from the argument, has the word \ldblquote copy\rdblquote  appended to show where it\rquote s copied from. Also, the }{\b addname(\~)}{
 function lets you put additional information about the object in }{\b ident}{ (the actual identifier, so you can see what it is as well as where it\rquote s copied from).
\par Here\rquote s the output:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {object A: blocknum:0, refcount:2
\par object B: blocknum:1, refcount:1
\par object A copy (C) : blocknum:0, refcount:2
\par inside operator=
\par    object B: blocknum:1, refcount:1
\par   destroying block 1
\par after assignment
\par    object A: blocknum:0, refcount:3
\par object B: blocknum:0, refcount:3
\par Assigning C = C
\par inside operator=
\par    object A copy (C) : blocknum:0, refcount:3
\par self-assignment
\par calling C.write('x')
\par    object A copy (C) : blocknum:0, refcount:3
\par copied block, blocknum:2, refcount:1
\par from block, blocknum:0, refcount:2
\par exiting main()
\par preparing to destroy: A copy (C)
\par   decrementing refcount blocknum:2, refcount:1
\par   destroying block 2
\par preparing to destroy: B
\par   decrementing refcount blocknum:0, refcount:2
\par preparing to destroy: A
\par   decrementing refcount blocknum:0, refcount:1
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  destroying block 0
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {By studying the output, tracing through the source code, and experimenting with the program, you\rquote ll deepen your understanding of these techniques.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Automatic }{\b operator=}{ creation
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {automatic\: creation of operator=}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: =, automatic creation}}}{
Because assigning an object to another object }{\i of the same type}{ is an activity most people expect to be possible, the compiler will automatically create a }{\b type::operator=(type)}{ if you don\rquote 
t make one. The behavior of this operator mimics that of the automatically created copy-constructor: If the class contains objects (or is inherited from another class), the }{\b operator=}{ for those objects is called recursively. This is called }{\i 
memberwise assignment}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {assignment\: memberwise}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {memberwise\: assignment}}}{.}{\i  }{For example,
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Autoeq.cpp
\par // Automatic operator=()
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Bar \{
\par public:
\par   Bar& operator=(const Bar&) \{
\par     cout << "inside Bar::operator=()" << endl;
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par }\pard \s24\qr\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright {
\par }\pard \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright {class MyType \{
\par   Bar b;
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   MyType a, b;
\par   a = b; // Prints: "inside Bar::operator=()"
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The automatically generated }{\b operator=}{ for }{\b MyType}{ calls }{\b Bar::operator=}{.
\par Generally you don\rquote t want to let the compiler do this for you. With classes of any sophistication (especially if they contain pointers!) you want to explicitly create an }{\b operator=}{. If you really don\rquote 
t want people to perform assignment, declare }{\b operator=}{ as a }{\b private}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: =as a private function}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
disallowing assignment}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {assignment\: disallowing}}}{ function. (You don\rquote t need to define it unless you\rquote re using it inside the class.)
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593239}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628711}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373984}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776203}
Automatic type conversion{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593239}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628711}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373984}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776203}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In C and C++, if the compiler sees an expression or function call using a type that isn\rquote 
t quite the one it needs, it can often perform an automatic type conversion from the type it has to the type it wants.}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {automatic type conversion}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type\: automatic type conversion}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {conversion\: automatic type conversion}}}{
 In C++, you can achieve this same effect for user-defined types by defining automatic type-conversion functions. These functions come in two flavors: a particular type of constructor and an overloaded operator.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373985}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776204}Constructor conversion{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373985}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776204}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you define a constructor that takes as its single argument an object (or reference) of another type, that constructor allows the compiler to perform an automatic type conversion. For example,

\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Autocnst.cpp
\par // Type conversion constructor
\par 
\par class One \{
\par public:
\par   One() \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Two \{
\par public:
\par   Two(const One&) \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par void f(Two) \{\}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   One one;
\par   f(one); // Wants a Two, has a One
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When the compiler sees }{\b f(\~)}{ called with a }{\b One}{ object, it looks at the declaration for }{\b f(\~)}{ and notices it wants a }{\b Two}{. Then it looks to see if there\rquote 
s any way to get a }{\b Two}{ from a }{\b One}{, and it finds the constructor }{\b Two::Two(One)}{, which it quietly calls. The resulting }{\b Two}{ object is handed to }{\b f(\~)}{.
\par In this case, automatic type conversion has saved you from the trouble of defining two overloaded versions of }{\b f(\~)}{. However, the cost is the hidden constructor call to }{\b Two}{, which may matter if you\rquote 
re concerned about the efficiency of calls to }{\b f(\~)}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Preventing constructor conversion
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {explicit\: keyword to prevent automatic type conversion}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
preventing automatic type conversion with the keyword explicit}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {automatic type conversion\: preventing with the keyword explicit}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe 
{type\: preventing automatic type conversion with the keyword explicit}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {conversion\: preventing automatic type conversion with the keyword explicit}}}{
There are times when automatic type conversion via the constructor can cause problems. To turn it off, you modify the constructor by prefacing with the keyword }{\b explicit}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ At the time of this writing, }{\b explicit}{ was a new keyword in the language. Your compiler may not support it yet.}}}{ (which only
 works with constructors). Used to modify the constructor of class }{\b Two}{ in the above example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class One \{
\par public:
\par   One() \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Two \{
\par public:
\par   explicit Two(const One&) \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par void f(Two) \{\}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   One one;
\par //!  f(one); // No auto conversion allowed
\par   f(Two(one)); // OK -- user performs conversion
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {By making }{\b Two}{\rquote s constructor explicit, the compiler is told not to perform any automatic conversion using that particular constructor (other non-}{\b explicit}{
 constructors in that class can still perform automatic conversions). If the user wants to make the conversion happen, the code must be written out. In the above code, }{\b f(Two(one))}{ creates a temporary object of type }{\b Two}{ from }{\b one}{
, just like the compiler did in the previous version.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373986}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776205}Operator conversion{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373986}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776205}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The second way to effect automatic type conversion is through operator overloading}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: type conversion overloading}}}{
. You can create a member function that takes the current type and converts it to the desired type using the }{\b operator}{ keyword followed by the type you want to convert to. This form of operator overloading is unique because you don\rquote 
t appear to specify a return type \endash  the return type is the }{\i name}{ of the operator you\rquote re overloading. Here\rquote s an example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Opconv.cpp
\par // Op overloading conversion
\par 
\par class Three \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   Three(int ii = 0, int = 0) : i(ii) \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Four \{
\par   int x;
\par public:
\par   Four(int xx) : x(xx) \{\}
\par   operator Three() const \{ return Three(x); \}
\par \};
\par 
\par void g(Three) \{\}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Four four(1);
\par   g(four);
\par   g(1);  // Calls Three(1,0)
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
With the constructor technique, the destination class is performing the conversion, but with operators, the source class performs the conversion. The value of the constructor technique is you can add a new conversion path to an existing system as you
\rquote re creating a new class. However, creating a single-argument constructor }{\i always}{ defines an automatic type conversion (even if it\rquote s got more than one argument, if the rest of the arguments are defaulted), which may not be what 
you want. In addition, there\rquote s no way to use a constructor conversion from a user-defined type to a built-in type; this is possible only with operator overloading.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Reflexivity
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {reflexivity, in operator overloading}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\: operator overloadin
g reflexivity}}}{One of the most convenient reasons to use global overloaded operators rather than member operators }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\: global operators vs. member operators}}}{
is that in the global versions, automatic type conversion may be applied to either op
erand, whereas with member objects, the left-hand operand must already be the proper type. If you want both operands to be converted, the global versions can save a lot of coding. Here\rquote s a small example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Reflex.cpp
\par // Reflexivity in overloading
\par 
\par class Number \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   Number(int ii = 0) : i(ii) \{\}
\par   const Number
\par   operator+(const Number& n) const \{
\par     return Number(i + n.i);
\par   \}
\par   friend const Number
\par     operator-(const Number&, const Number&);
\par \};
\par 
\par const Number
\par   operator-(const Number& n1,
\par             const Number& n2) \{
\par     return Number(n1.i - n2.i);
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Number a(47), b(11);
\par   a + b; // OK
\par   a + 1; // 2nd arg converted to Number
\par //! 1 + a; // Wrong! 1st arg not of type Number
\par   a - b; // OK
\par   a - 1; // 2nd arg converted to Number
\par   1 - a; // 1st arg converted to Number
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Class }{\b Number}{ has a member }{\b operator+}{ and a friend }{\b operator\endash }{. Because there\rquote s a constructor that takes a single }{\b int}{ argument, an }{\b int}{
 can be automatically converted to a }{\b Number}{, but only under the right conditions. In }{\b main(\~)}{, you can see that adding a }{\b Number}{ to another }{\b Number}{ works fine because it\rquote 
s an exact match to the overloaded operator. Also, when the compiler sees a }{\b Number}{ followed by a }{\b +}{ and an }{\b int}{, it can match to the member function }{\b Number::operator+}{ and convert the }{\b int}{ argument to a }{\b Number}{
 using the constructor. But when it sees an }{\b int}{ and a }{\b +}{ and a }{\b Number}{, it doesn\rquote t know what to do because all it has is }{\b Number::operator+}{, which requires that the left operand already be a }{\b Number}{ object. Thus 
the compiler issues an error.
\par With the }{\b friend}{ }{\b operator\endash }{, things are different. The compiler needs to fill in both its arguments however it can; it isn\rquote t restricted to having a }{\b Number}{ as the left-hand argument. Thus, if it sees }{\b 1 \endash  a}{
, it can convert the first argument to a }{\b Number}{ using the constructor.
\par Sometimes you want to be able to restrict the use of your operators by making them members. For example, when multiplying a matrix by a vector, the vector must go on the right. But if you want your operators t
o be able to convert either argument, make the operator a friend function.
\par Fortunately, the compiler will not take }{\b 1 \endash  1}{ and convert both arguments to }{\b Number}{ objects and then call }{\b operator\endash }{. That would mean that existing C code might suddenly start to work differently. The compiler matches the 
\ldblquote simplest\rdblquote  possibility first, which is the built-in operator for the expression }{\b 1 \endash  1}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373987}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776206}A perfect example: strings{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373987}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776206}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {An example where automatic type conversion is extremely helpful occurs with a }{\b string}{ class}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\: string}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {string\: string class example}}}{. Without automatic type conversion, if you wanted to use all the existing string functions from the Standard C library, you\rquote 
d have to create a member function for each one, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Strings1.cpp
\par // No auto type conversion
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <cstring>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Stringc \{
\par   char* s;
\par public:
\par   Stringc(const char* S = "") \{
\par     s = (char*)malloc(strlen(S) + 1);
\par     require(s != 0);
\par     strcpy(s, S);
\par   \}
\par   ~Stringc() \{ free(s); \}
\par   int strcmp(const Stringc& S) const \{
\par     return ::strcmp(s, S.s);
\par   \}
\par   // ... etc., for every function in string.h
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Stringc s1("hello"), s2("there");
\par   s1.strcmp(s2);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here, only the }{\b strcmp(\~)}{ function is created, but you\rquote d have to create a corresponding function for every one in }{\b <cstring> }{
that might be needed. Fortunately, you can provide an automatic type conversion allowing access to all the functions in }{\b <cstring>}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Strings2.cpp
\par // With auto type conversion
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <cstring>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Stringc \{
\par   char* s;
\par public:
\par   Stringc(const char* S = "") \{
\par     s = (char*)malloc(strlen(S) + 1);
\par     require(s != 0);
\par     strcpy(s, S);
\par   \}
\par   ~Stringc() \{ free(s); \}
\par   operator const char*() const \{ return s; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Stringc s1("hello"), s2("there");
\par   strcmp(s1, s2); // Standard C function
\par   strspn(s1, s2); // Any string function!
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now any function that takes a }{\b char*}{ argument can also take a }{\b Stringc}{ argument because the compiler knows how to make a }{\b char*}{ from a }{\b Stringc}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373988}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776207}Pitfalls in automatic type conversion{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373988}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776207}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {pitfalls\: in automatic type conversion}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {automatic type conversion\:
 pitfalls}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\: pitfalls in automatic type conversion}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {conversion\: pitfalls in automatic type conversion}}}{
Because the compiler must choose how to quietly perform a type conversion, it can get into trouble if you don\rquote t design your conversions correctly. A simple and obvious situation occurs with a class }{\b X}{
 that can convert itself to an object of class }{\b Y}{ with an }{\b operator Y(\~)}{. If class }{\b Y}{ has a constructor that takes a single argument of type }{\b X}{
, this represents the identical type conversion. The compiler now has two ways to go from }{\b X}{ to }{\b Y}{, so it will generate an ambiguity error when that conversion occurs:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Ambig.cpp
\par // Ambiguity in type conversion
\par 
\par class Y; // Class declaration
\par 
\par class X \{
\par public:
\par   operator Y() const; // Convert X to Y
\par \};
\par 
\par class Y \{
\par public:
\par   Y(X); // Convert X to Y
\par \};
\par 
\par void f(Y);
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   X x;
\par //! f(x); // Error: ambiguous conversion
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The obvious solution to this problem is not to do it: Just provide a single path for automatic conversion from one type to another.
\par A more difficult problem to spot occurs when you provide automatic conversion to more than one type. This is sometimes called }{\i fan-out}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {fan-out, automatic type conversion}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: fan-out in automatic type conversion}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\: fan-out in automatic type conversion}}}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:Fanout.cpp
\par // Type conversion fanout
\par 
\par class A \{\};
\par class B \{\};
\par 
\par class C \{
\par public:
\par   operator A() const;
\par   operator B() const;
\par \};
\par 
\par // Overloaded h():
\par void h(A);
\par void h(B);
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   C c;
\par //! h(c); // Error: C -> A or C -> B ???
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Class }{\b C}{ has automatic conversions to both }{\b A}{ and }{\b B}{. The insidious thing about this is that there\rquote 
s no problem until someone innocently comes along and creates two overloaded versions of }{\b h(\~)}{. (With only one version, the code in }{\b main(\~)}{ works fine.)
\par Again, the solution \endash  and the general watchword with automatic type conversion \endash  is to only provide a single automatic conversion from one type to another. You can have conversions to other types; they just shouldn\rquote t be }{\i automatic
}{. You can create explicit function calls with names like }{\b make_A(\~)}{ and }{\b make_B(\~)}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Hidden activities
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Automatic type conversion can introduce more underlying activities than you may expect. As a little brain teaser, look at this modification of }{\b FeeFi.cpp}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C12:FeeFi2.cpp
\par // Copying vs. initialization
\par 
\par class Fi \{\};
\par 
\par class Fee \{
\par public:
\par   Fee(int) \{\}
\par   Fee(const Fi&) \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Fo \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   Fo(int x = 0) \{ i = x; \}
\par   operator Fee() const \{ return Fee(i); \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Fo fo;
\par   Fee fiddle = fo;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There is no constructor to create the }{\b Fee fiddle}{ from a }{\b Fo}{ object. However, }{\b Fo}{ has an automatic type conversion to a }{\b Fee}{. There\rquote s no copy-constructor to create a }{
\b Fee}{ from a }{\b Fee}{, but this is one of the special functions the compiler can create for you. (The default constructor, copy-constructor, }{\b operator=}{, and destructor can be created automatically.) So for the relatively innocuous statement

\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Fee fiddle = FO;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {the automatic type conversion operator is called, and a copy-constructor is created.
\par Automatic type conversion should be used carefully. It\rquote s excellent when it significantly reduces a coding task, but it\rquote s usually not worth using gratuitously.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593240}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628712}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373989}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776208}Summary
{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593240}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628712}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373989}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776208}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The whole reason for the existence of operator overloading is for those situations when it makes life easier. There\rquote s nothing particularly magical
 about it; the overloaded operators are just functions with funny names, and the function calls happen to be made for you by the compiler when it spots the right pattern. But if operator overloading doesn\rquote 
t provide a significant benefit to you (the creator of the class) or the user of the class, don\rquote t confuse the issue by adding it.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373990}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776209}Exercises{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373990}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776209}
