{\pntxta . }}\ls114\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {You call }{\b malloc(\~)}{, which requests a block of memory from the pool. (This code may actually be part of }{\b malloc(\~)}{.)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 5. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls114\adjustright {The pool is searched for a block of memory large enough to satisfy the request. This is done by checking a map or directory of some sort that shows which blocks are currently in use and which blocks are available. It
\rquote s a quick process, but it may take several tries so it might not be deterministic \endash  that is, you can\rquote t necessarily count on }{\b malloc(\~)}{\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls114\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {malloc(\~)\: and time}}}{ always taking exactly the same amount of time.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 6. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls114\adjustright {Before a pointer to that block is returned, the size and location of the block must be recorded so further calls to }{\b malloc(\~)}{ won\rquote t use it, and so that when you call }{\b free(\~)}{\pard\plain 
\s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls114\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {free(\~)}}}{, the system knows how much memory to release.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The way all this is implemented can vary widely. For example, there\rquote 
s nothing to prevent primitives for memory allocation being implemented in the processor. If you\rquote re curious, you can write test programs to try to guess the way your }{\b malloc(\~)}{
 is implemented. You can also read the library source code, if you have it.
