\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now that }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ have been introduced (as well as many other subjects), the }{\b Stash}{ and }{\b Stack }{examples from the early
 part of this book can be rewritten using all the features discussed in the book so far. Examining the new code will also give you a useful review of the topics.
\par At this point in the book, neither the }{\b Stash}{ nor }{\b Stack}{ classes will \ldblquote own\rdblquote }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {container\: ownership}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ownership\: container}}}{ the objects they point to; that is, when the }{\b Stash}{ or }{\b Stack}{ object goes out of scope, it will not call }{\b delete}{
 for all the objects it points to. The reason this is not possible is because, in an attempt to be generic, they hold }{\b void}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {void\: pointer}}}{
pointers}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {pointer\: void}}}{. If you }{\b delete}{ a }{\b void}{ pointer, the only thing that happens is the memory gets released, because there
\rquote s no type information and no way for the compiler to know what destructor to call. When a pointer is returned from the }{\b Stash}{ or }{\b Stack}{
 object, you must cast it to the proper type before using it. These problems will be dealt with in the next chapter [[??]], and in Chapter XX.
\par Because the container doesn\rquote t own the pointer, the user must be responsible for it. This means there\rquote s a serious problem if you add pointers to objects created on the stack }{\i and }{
objects created on the heap to the same container because a delete-expression is unsafe for a pointer that hasn\rquote t been allocated on the heap. (And when yo
u fetch a pointer back from the container, how will you know where its object has been allocated?). Thus, you must be sure that objects stored in the upcoming versions of }{\b Stash }{and }{\b Stack }{
are only made on the heap, either through careful programming or by creating classes that can only be built on the heap.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374000}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776218}Stash}{ for pointers
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374000}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776218}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This version of the }{\b Stash}{
 class, which you last saw in Chapter XX, is changed to reflect all the new material introduced since Chapter XX. In addition, the new }{\b PStash}{ holds }{\i pointers}{ to objects that exist by themselves on the heap, whereas the old }{\b Stash}{
 in Chapter XX and earlier copied the }{\i objects}{ into the }{\b Stash}{ container. With the introduction of }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{, it\rquote s easy and safe to hold pointers to objects that have been created on the heap.
\par Here\rquote s the header file for the \ldblquote pointer }{\b Stash}{\rdblquote :
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C13:PStash.h
\par // Holds pointers instead of objects
\par #ifndef PSTASH_H
\par #define PSTASH_H
\par 
\par class PStash \{
\par   int quantity; // Number of storage spaces
\par   int next; // Next empty space
\par    // Pointer storage:
\par   void** storage;
\par   void inflate(int increase);
\par public:
\par   PStash() \{
\par     quantity = 0;
\par     storage = 0;
\par     next = 0;
\par   \}
\par   // No ownership:
\par   ~PStash() \{ delete []storage; \}
\par   int add(void* element);
\par   void* operator[](int index) const; // Fetch
\par   // Number of elements in Stash:
\par   int count() const \{ return next; \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // PSTASH_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The underlying data elements are fairly similar, but now }{\b storage}{ is an array of }{\b void }{
pointers, and the allocation of storage for that array is performed with }{\b new}{ instead of }{\b malloc(\~)}{. In the expression
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void** st = new void*[quantity + increase];
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {the type of object allocated is a }{\b void*}{, so the expression allocates an array of }{\b void}{ pointers.
\par The destructor deletes the storage where the }{\b void}{ pointers are held, rather than attempting to delete what they point at (which, as previously noted, will release their storage and not call the destructors because a }{\b void}{ pointer}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {pointer\: void}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {void\: pointer}}}{
 has no type information).
\par The other change is the replacement of the }{\b fetch(\~)}{ function with }{\b operator[ ]}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: []}}}{
, which makes more sense syntactically. Again, however, a }{\b void*}{ is returned, so the user must remember what types are stored in the container and cast the pointers when fetching them out (a problem which will be repaired in future chapters).
\par Here are the member function definitions:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C13:PStash.cpp \{O\}
\par // Pointer Stash definitions
\par #include "PStash.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <cstring> // 'mem' functions
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int PStash::add(void* element) \{
\par   const int inflateSize = 10;
\par   if(next >= quantity)
\par     inflate(inflateSize);
\par   storage[next++] = element;
\par   return(next - 1); // Index number
\par \}
\par 
\par // Operator overloading replacement for fetch
\par void* PStash::operator[](int index) const \{
\par   if(index >= next || index < 0)
\par     return 0;  // Out of bounds
\par   // Produce pointer to desired element:
\par   return storage[index];
\par \}
\par 
\par void PStash::inflate(int increase) \{
\par   const int psz = sizeof(void*);
\par   void** st = new void*[quantity + increase];
\par   memset(st, 0, (quantity + increase) * psz);
\par   memcpy(st, storage, quantity * psz);
\par   quantity += increase;
\par   delete []storage; // Old storage
\par   storage = st; // Point to new memory
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b add(\~)}{ function is effectively the same as before, except that the pointer is stored instead of a copy of the whole object, which, as you\rquote 
ve seen, actually requires a copy-constructor for normal objects.
\par The }{\b inflate(\~)}{ code is modified to handle the allocation of an array of }{\b void*}{ instead of the previous design which was on
ly working with raw bytes. Here, instead of using the prior approach of copying by array indexing, the Standard C library function }{\b memset(\~)}{ is first used to set all the new memory to zero (this is not strictly necessary, since the }{\b PStash}{
 is presumably managing all the memory correctly \endash  but it usually doesn\rquote t hurt to throw in a bit of extra care). Then }{\b memcpy(\~)}{ moves the existing data from the old location to the new. Often, functions like }{\b memset(\~)}{ and }{
