\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The new-handler function must take no arguments and have }{\b void}{ return value. The }{\b while}{ loop will keep allocating }{\b int}{
 objects (and throwing away their return addresses) until the free store is exhausted. At the very next call to }{\b new}{, no storage can be allocated, so the new-handler will be called.
\par Of course, you can write more sophisticated new-handlers, even one to try to reclaim memory (commonly known as a }{\i garbage collector}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
garbage collector}}}{). This is not a job for the novice programmer.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593247}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628719}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374005
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776223}Overloading new & delete{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593247}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628719}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374005}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776223}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\: new & delete}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {new\: overloading new and delete}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {delete\:
 overloading new & delete}}}{When you create a new-expression}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {new\: new-expression}}}{, two things occur: First, storage is allocated using the }{\b 
operator new}{, then the constructor is called. In a delete-expression}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {delete\: delete-expression}}}{
, the destructor is called, then storage is deallocated using the }{\b operator delete}{. The constructor and destructor calls are never under your control (otherwise you might accidentally subvert them), but you }{\i can }{
change the storage allocation functions }{\b operator new}{ and }{\b operator delete}{.
\par The memory allocation }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {allocation\: memory}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
memory\: allocation}}}{system used by }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ is designed for general-purpose use. In special situations, however, it doesn\rquote 
t serve your needs. The most common reason to change the allocator is efficiency: You might be creating and destroying so many objects of a particular class that it has become a speed bottleneck. C++ allows you to overload }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{
 to implement your own storage allocation scheme, so you can handle problems like this.
\par Another issue is heap fragmentation: By allocating objects of different sizes it\rquote s possible to break up the heap so that you effe
ctively run out of storage. That is, the storage might be available, but because of fragmentation no piece is big enough to satisfy your needs. By creating your own allocator for a particular class, you can ensure this never happens.
\par In embedded and real-time systems, a program may have to run for a very long time with restricted resources. Such a system may also require that memory allocation always take the same amount of time, and there\rquote 
s no allowance for heap exhaustion or fragmentation. A custom memory allocator is the solution; otherwise programmers will avoid using }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ altogether in such cases and miss out on a valuable C++ asset.
\par When you overload }{\b operator new}{ and }{\b operator delete}{, it\rquote s important to remember that you\rquote re changing only the way }{\i raw storage is allocated}{. The compiler will simply call your }{\b new}{
 instead of the default version to allocate storage, then call the constructor for that storage. So, although the compiler allocates storage }{\i and}{ calls the constructor when it sees }{\b new}{, all you can change when you overload }{\b new}{
 is the storage allocation portion. (}{\b delete}{ has a similar limitation.)
\par When you overload }{\b operator}{ }{\b new}{, you also replace the behavior when it runs out of memory, so you must decide what to do in your }{\b operator new}{: return z
ero, write a loop to call the new-handler and retry allocation, or (typically) throw a }{\b bad_alloc}{ exception (discussed in Chapter XX).
\par Overloading }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ is like overloading any other operator. However, you have a choice of overloading the global allocator or using a different allocator for a particular class.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374006}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776224}Overloading global new & delete
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374006}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776224}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\: global new and delete}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {global\: overloading global new and delete}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {new\:
 overloading global new and delete}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {delete\: overloading global new and delete}}}{This is the drastic approach, when the global versions of }{\b new}
{ and }{\b delete}{ are unsatisfactory for the whole system. If you overload the global versions, you make the defaults completely inaccessible \endash  you can\rquote t even call them from inside your redefinitions.
\par The overloaded }{\b new}{ must take an argument of }{\b size_t}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {size\: size_t}}}{
 (the Standard C standard type for sizes). This argument is generated and passed to you by the compiler and is the size of the object you\rquote re responsible for allocatin
g. You must return a pointer either to an object of that size (or bigger, if you have some reason to do so), or to zero if you can\rquote t find the memory (in which case the constructor is }{\i not}{ called!). However, if you can\rquote 
t find the memory, you should probably do something more drastic than just returning zero, like calling the new-handler or throwing an exception, to signal that there\rquote s a problem.
\par The return value of }{\b operator new}{ is a }{\b void*}{, }{\i not}{ a pointer to any particular type. All you\rquote ve done is produce memory, not a finished object \endash  that doesn\rquote 
t happen until the constructor is called, an act the compiler guarantees and which is out of your control.
\par The }{\b operator delete}{ takes a }{\b void*}{ to memory that was allocated by }{\b operator new}{. It\rquote s a }{\b void*}{ because you get that pointer }{\i after}{
 the destructor is called, which removes the object-ness from the piece of storage. The return type is }{\b void}{.
\par Here\rquote s a very simple example showing how to overload the global }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C13:GlobalNew.cpp
\par // Overload global new/delete
\par #include <cstdio>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par void* operator new(size_t sz) \{
\par   printf("operator new: %d Bytes\\n", sz);
\par   void* m = malloc(sz);
\par   if(!m) puts("out of memory");
\par   return m;
\par \}
\par 
\par void operator delete(void* m) \{
\par   puts("operator delete");
\par   free(m);
\par \}
\par 
\par class S \{
\par   int i[100];
\par public:
\par   S() \{ puts("S::S()"); \}
\par   ~S() \{ puts("S::~S()"); \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   puts("creating & destroying an int");
\par   int* p = new int(47);
\par   delete p;
\par   puts("creating & destroying an s");
\par   S* s = new S;
\par   delete s;
\par   puts("creating & destroying S[3]");
\par   S* sa = new S[3];
\par   delete []sa;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here you can see the general form for overloading }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{. These use the Standard C library functions }{\b malloc(\~)}{ and }{\b free(\~
)}{ for the allocators (which is probably what the default }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ use, as well!). However, they also print out messages about what they are doing. Notice that }{\b printf(\~)}{ and }{\b puts(\~)}{ are used rather than }{\b iostreams}
{. Thus, when an }{\b iostream}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: limitations with overloaded global new & delete}}}{object is created (like the global }{\b cin}{, }{\b 
cout}{, and }{\b cerr}{), they call }{\b new}{ to allocate memory. With }{\b printf(\~)}{, you don\rquote t get into a deadlock because it doesn\rquote t call }{\b new }{to initialize itself.
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{, objects of built-in types are created to prove that the overloaded }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ are also called in that case. Then a single object of type }{\b s}{ is created, followed by an array. For the array, you\rquote 
ll see that extra memory is requested to put information about the number of objects in the array. In all cases, the global overloaded versions of }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ are used.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374007}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776225}
Overloading new & delete for a class{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374007}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776225}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\: new and delete for a class}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {new\: overloading new and delete for a class}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {delete\: overloading new and delete for a class}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\: overloading new and delete for a class}}}{Although you don\rquote 
t have to explicitly say }{\b static}{, when you overload }{\b new }{and }{\b delete}{ for a class, you\rquote re creating }{\b static}{ member functions. Again, the syntax is the same as overloading any other operator. When the compiler sees you use }{
\b new}{ to create an object of your class, it chooses the member }{\b operator new}{ over the global version. However, the global versions of }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ are used for all other types of objects (unless they have their own }{\b new}{ and 
}{\b delete}{).
\par In the following example, a very primitive storage allocation system }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {heap\: simple storage allocation system}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {memory\: simple storage allocation system}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {storage\:
 simple allocation system}}}{is created for the class }{\b Framis}{. A chunk of memory is set aside in the static data area at program start-up, and that memory is used to allocate space for objects of type }{\b Framis}{
. To determine which blocks have been allocated, a simple array of bytes is used, one byte for each block:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C13:Framis.cpp
\par // Local overloaded new & delete
\par #include <cstddef> // Size_t
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <new>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("Framis.out");
\par 
\par class Framis \{
\par   char c[10]; // To take up space, not used
\par   static unsigned char pool[];
\par   static unsigned char alloc_map[];
\par public:
\par   enum \{ psize = 100 \};  // # of frami allowed
\par   Framis() \{ out << "Framis()\\n"; \}
\par   ~Framis() \{ out << "~Framis() ... "; \}
\par   void* operator new(size_t) throw(bad_alloc);
\par   void operator delete(void*);
\par \};
\par unsigned char Framis::pool[psize * sizeof(Framis)];
\par unsigned char Framis::alloc_map[psize] = \{0\};
\par 
\par // Size is ignored -- assume a Framis object
\par void* Framis::operator new(size_t) 
\par   throw(bad_alloc) \{
\par   for(int i = 0; i < psize; i++)
\par     if(!alloc_map[i]) \{
\par       out << "using block " << i << " ... ";
\par       alloc_map[i] = 1; // Mark it used
\par       return pool + (i * sizeof(Framis));
\par     \}
\par   out << "out of memory" << endl;
\par   throw bad_alloc();
\par \}
\par 
\par void Framis::operator delete(void* m) \{
\par   if(!m) return; // Check for null pointer
\par   // Assume it was created in the pool
\par   // Calculate which block number it is:
\par   unsigned long block = (unsigned long)m
\par     - (unsigned long)pool;
\par   block /= sizeof(Framis);
\par   out << "freeing block " << block << endl;
\par   // Mark it free:
\par   alloc_map[block] = 0;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Framis* f[Framis::psize];
\par   for(int i = 0; i < Framis::psize; i++)
\par     f[i] = new Framis;
\par   new Framis; // Out of memory
\par   delete f[10];
\par   f[10] = 0;
\par   // Use released memory:
\par   Framis* x = new Framis;
\par   delete x;
\par   for(int j = 0; j < Framis::psize; j++)
\par     delete f[j]; // Delete f[10] OK
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The pool of memory for the }{\b Framis}{ heap is created by allocating an array of bytes large enough to hold }{\b psize}{ }{\b Framis}{
 objects. The allocation map is }{\b psize}{ bytes long, so there\rquote s one byte for every bl
ock. All the bytes in the allocation map are initialized to zero using the aggregate initialization trick of setting the first element to zero so the compiler automatically initializes all the rest.
\par The local }{\b operator new}{ has the same form as the global one. All it does is search through the allocation map looking for a zero byte, then sets that byte to one to indicate it\rquote 
s been allocated and returns the address of that particular block. If it can\rquote t find any memory, it issues a message and returns zero (Notice that the new-handler}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {new\: new-handler}}}{ is not called and no exceptions are thrown because the behavior when you run out of memory}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {memory\:
 exhausting heap}}}{ is now under your control.) In this example, it\rquote s OK to use iostreams }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: and global overloaded new & delete}}}{
because the global }{\b operator new}{ and }{\b delete}{ are untouched.
\par The }{\b operator delete}{ assumes the }{\b Framis}{ address was created in the pool. This is a fair assumption, because the local }{\b operator new}{ will be called whenever you create a single }{\b Framis}{ object on the heap \endash 
 but not an array. Global }{\b new}{ is used in that case. So the user might accidentally have called }{\b operator delete}{ without using the empty bracket syntax to indicate array destruction. This would cause a problem. Also, the
 user might be deleting a pointer to an object created on the stack. If you think these things could occur, you might want to add a line to make sure the address is within the pool and on a correct boundary.
\par }{\b operator delete}{ calculates which block in the pool this pointer represents, and then sets the allocation map\rquote s flag for that block to zero to indicate the block has been released.
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{, enough }{\b Framis}{ objects are dynamically allocated to run out of memory; this checks the out-of-memory behavior. Then one of the objects is freed, and another one is created to show that the released memory is reused.
\par Because this allocation scheme is specific to }{\b Framis}{ objects, it\rquote s probably much faster than the general-purpose memory allocation scheme used for the default }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374008}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776226}Overloading new & delete for arrays
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374008}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776226}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\: new and delete for arrays}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {new\: overloading new and delete for arrays}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe 
{delete\: overloading new and delete for arrays}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {array\: overloading new and delete for arrays}}}{If you overload operator }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete
}{ for a class, those operators are called whenever you create an object of that class. However, if you create an }{\i array}{ of those class objects, the global }{\b operator}{ }{\b new}{ is called to allocate enough storage for the array a
ll at once, and the global }{\b operator}{ }{\b delete}{ is called to release that storage. You can control the allocation of arrays of objects by overloading the special array versions of }{\b operator new[ ]}{ and }{\b operator delete[ ]}{
 for the class. Here\rquote s an example that shows when the two different versions are called:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C13:ArrayNew.cpp
\par // Operator new for arrays
\par #include <new> // Size_t definition
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream trace("ArrayNew.out");
\par 
\par class Widget \{
\par   int i[10];
\par public:
\par   Widget() \{ trace << "*"; \}
\par   ~Widget() \{ trace << "~"; \}
\par   void* operator new(size_t sz) \{
\par     trace << "Widget::new: "
\par          << sz << " bytes" << endl;
\par     return ::new char[sz];
\par   \}
\par   void operator delete(void* p) \{
\par     trace << "Widget::delete" << endl;
\par     ::delete []p;
\par   \}
\par   void* operator new[](size_t sz) \{
\par     trace << "Widget::new[]: "
\par          << sz << " bytes" << endl;
\par     return ::new char[sz];
\par   \}
\par   void operator delete[](void* p) \{
\par     trace << "Widget::delete[]" << endl;
\par     ::delete []p;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   trace << "new Widget" << endl;
\par   Widget* w = new Widget;
\par   trace << "\\ndelete Widget" << endl;
\par   delete w;
\par   trace << "\\nnew Widget[25]" << endl;
\par   Widget* wa = new Widget[25];
\par   trace << "\\ndelete []Widget" << endl;
\par   delete []wa;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here, the global versions of }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ are called so the effect is the same as having no overloaded versions of }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete
}{ except that trace information is added. Of course, you can use any memory allocation scheme you want in the overloaded }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{.
\par You can see that the array versions of }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ are the same as the individual-object versions with the addition of the brackets. In both cases you\rquote 
re handed the size of the memory you must allocate. The size handed to the array version will be the size of the entire array. It\rquote s worth keeping in mind that the }{\i only}{ thing the overloaded operator }{\b new}{
 is required to do is hand back a pointer to a large enough memory block. Although you may perform initialization on that memory, normally that\rquote s the job of the constructor that will automatically be called for your memory by the compiler.
\par The constructor and destructor simply print out characters so you can see when they\rquote ve been called. Here\rquote s what the trace file looks like for one compiler:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {new Widget
\par Widget::new: 20 bytes
\par *
\par delete Widget
\par ~Widget::delete
\par 
\par new Widget[25]
\par Widget::new[]: 504 bytes
\par *************************
\par delete []Widget
\par ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Widget::delete[]
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Creating an individual object requires 20 bytes, as you might expect. (This machine uses two bytes for an }{\b int}{). The }{\b operator new}{
 is called, then the constructor (indicated by the }{\b *}{). In a complementary fashion, calling }{\b delete}{ causes the destructor to be called, then the }{\b operator delete}{.
\par When an array of }{\b Widget}{ objects is created, the array version of }{\b operator new}{
 is used, as promised. But notice that the size requested is four more bytes than expected. This extra four bytes is where the system keeps information about the array, in particular, the number of objects in the array. That way, when you say
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {delete []Widget;
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {the brackets tell the compiler it\rquote 
s an array of objects, so the compiler generates code to look for the number of objects in the array and to call the destructor that many times.
\par You can see that, even though the array }{\b operator new}{ and }{\b operator delete}{ are only called once for the entire array chunk, the default constructor and destructor are called for each object in the array.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374009}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776227}Constructor calls
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374009}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776227}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Considering that
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {MyType* f = new MyType;
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {calls }{\b new}{ to allocate a }{\b MyType}{-sized piece of storage, then invokes the }{\b MyType}{
 constructor on that storage, what happens if all the safeguards fail and the value returned by }{\b operator new}{ is zero? The constructor }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
constructor\: and operator new, out of memory}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {new\: operator new and constructor, out of memory}}}{
is not called in that case, so although you still have an unsuccessfully created object, at least you haven\rquote t invoked the constructor and handed it a zero pointer. Here\rquote s an example to prove it:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C13:NoMemory.cpp
\par // Constructor isn't called
\par // If new returns 0
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <new> // size_t definition
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par void my_new_handler() \{
\par   cout << "new handler called" << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par class NoMemory \{
\par public:
\par   NoMemory() \{
\par     cout << "NoMemory::NoMemory()" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   void* operator new(size_t sz) throw(bad_alloc)\{
\par     cout << "NoMemory::operator new" << endl;
\par     throw bad_alloc(); // "Out of memory"
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   set_new_handler(my_new_handler);
\par   NoMemory* nm = new NoMemory;
\par   cout << "nm = " << nm << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When the program runs, it prints only the message from }{\b operator new}{. Because }{\b new}{
 returns zero, the constructor is never called so its message is not printed.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374010}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776228}Object placement
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374010}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776228}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There are two other, less common, uses for overloading }{\b operator new}{.}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {new\: operator new placement specifier}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {new\: overloaded, can take multiple arguments}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {placement\: operator new placement specifier}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\:
 new placement specifier}}}{
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 1. \tab}}\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls223\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls223\adjustright \fs20\lang1024\cgrid {You may want to place an object in a specific location in memory. This is especially important with hardware-oriented embedded systems }{\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls223\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls223\adjustright \v\fs20\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {embedded\: systems}}}{where an object may be synonymous with a particular piece of hardware.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 2. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {You may want to be able to choose from different allocators when calling }{\b new}{.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Both of these situations are solved with the same mechanism: The overloaded }{\b operator new}{ can take more than one argument. As you\rquote 
ve seen before, the first argument is always the size of the object, which is secretly calculated and passed by the compiler. But the other
 arguments can be anything you want: the address you want the object placed at, a reference to a memory allocation function or object, or anything else that is convenient for you.
\par The way you pass the extra arguments to }{\b operator new}{ during a call may seem slightly curious at first: You put the argument list (}{\i without}{ the }{\b size_t }{argument, which is handled by the compiler) after the keyword }{\b new}{
 and before the class name of the object you\rquote re creating. For example,
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {X* xp = new(a) X;
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {will pass }{\b a}{ as the second argument to }{\b operator new}{. Of course, this can work only if such an }{\b operator new}{ has been declared.
\par Here\rquote s an example showing how you can place an object at a particular location:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C13:PlacementNew.cpp
\par // Placement with operator new
\par #include <cstddef> // Size_t
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class X \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   X(int ii = 0) : i(ii) \{\}
\par   ~X() \{
\par     cout << "X::~X()" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   void* operator new(size_t, void* loc) \{
\par     return loc;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int l[10];
\par   X* xp = new(l) X(47); // X at location l
\par   xp->X::~X(); // Explicit destructor call
\par   // ONLY use with placement!
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that }{\b operator new}{ only returns the pointer that\rquote 
s passed to it. Thus, the caller decides where the object is going to sit, and the constructor is called for that memory as part of the new-expression.
\par A dilemma occurs when you want to destroy the object. There\rquote s only one version of }{\b operator delete}{, so there\rquote s no way to say, \ldblquote Use my special deallocator for this object.\rdblquote  You want to call the destruc
tor, but you don\rquote t want the memory to be released by the dynamic memory mechanism because it wasn\rquote t allocated on the heap.
\par The answer is a very special syntax: You can explicitly call the destructor, as in
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {xp->X::~X(); // Explicit destructor call
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {destructor\: explicit destructor call}}}{
A stern warning is in order here. Some people see this as a way to destroy objects at some time before the end of the scope, rather than either adjusting the scope or (more correctly) using dynamic object creation if they want the object\rquote 
s lifetime to be determined at runtime. You will have serious problems if you call the destructor this way for an object created on the stack because the destructor will be called again at the end of the scope. If you call the destructor th
is way for an object that was created on the heap, the destructor will execute, but the memory won\rquote t be released, which probably isn\rquote 
t what you want. The only reason that the destructor can be called explicitly this way is to support the placement syntax for }{\b operator new}{.
\par Although this example shows only one additional argument, there\rquote s nothing to prevent you from adding more if you need them for other purposes.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593248}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628720}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374011
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776229}Summary{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593248}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628720}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374011}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776229}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It\rquote s convenient and optimally efficient to create automatic objects on the stack, but to
 solve the general programming problem you must be able to create and destroy objects at any time during a program\rquote s execution, particularly to respond to information from outside the program. Although C\rquote 
s dynamic memory allocation will get storage from the heap, it doesn\rquote t provide the ease of use and guaranteed construction necessary in C++. By bringing dynamic object creation into the core of the language with }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{
, you can create objects on the heap as easily as making them on the stack. In addition, you get a great deal of flexibility. You can change the behavior of }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ if they don\rquote t suit your needs, particularly if they aren
\rquote t efficient enough. Also, you can modify what happens when the heap runs out of storage. (However, }{\i exception handling}{, described in Chapter XX, also comes into play here.)
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374012}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776230}Exercises
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374012}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776230}
