\par }}\pard\plain \s1\fi-1080\li990\sa360\sl240\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \cbpat8 \f4\fs96\cf1\kerning18\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593250}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628722}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374013}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776231}
14: Inheritance & composition{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593250}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628722}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374013}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776231}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {One of the most compelling features about C++ is code reuse}{\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \v\f4\fs28\cgrid {\xe {reuse\: code reuse}}}
{\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \v\f4\fs28\cgrid {\xe {code re-use}}}{. But to be revolutionary, you need to be able to do a lot more than copy code and change it.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {That\rquote s the C approach, and it hasn\rquote 
t worked very well. As with most everything in C++, the solution revolves around the class. You reuse code by creating new classes, but instead of creating them from scratch, you use existing classes that someone else has built and debugged.
\par The trick is to use the classes without soiling the existing code. In this chapter you\rquote ll see two ways to accomplish this. The first is quite straightforward: You simply create objects of your existing class inside the new class. This is called }{
\i composition}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {composition}}}{because the new class is composed of objects of existing classes.
\par The second approach is more subtle. You create a new class as a }{\i type of}{ an existing class. You literally take the form of the existing class and add code to it, without modifying the existing class. This magical act is called }{\i inheritance}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance}}}{, and most of the work is done by the compiler. Inheritance is one of the cornerstones of object-oriented programmi
ng and has additional implications that will be explored in the next chapter.
\par It turns out that much of the syntax and behavior are similar for both composition and inheritance (which makes sense; they are both ways of making new types from existing types). In this chapter, you\rquote ll learn about these code reuse mechanisms.

\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593251}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628723}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374014}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776232}
Composition syntax{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593251}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628723}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374014}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776232}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Actually, you\rquote ve been using composition all along to create classes. You\rquote ve just been composing classes using built-in types. It turns out to be almost as easy to use com
position with user-defined types.
\par Consider an existing class that is valuable for some reason:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:Useful.h
\par // A class to reuse
\par #ifndef USEFUL_H
\par #define USEFUL_H
\par 
\par class X \{
\par   int i;
\par   enum \{ factor = 11 \};
\par public:
\par   X() \{ i = 0; \}
\par   void set(int ii) \{ i = ii; \}
\par   int read() const \{ return i; \}
\par   int permute() \{ return i = i * factor; \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // USEFUL_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The data members are }{\b private}{ in this class, so it\rquote s completely safe to embed an object of type }{\b X}{ as a }{\b public}{ object in a new class, which mak
es the interface straightforward:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:Compose.cpp
\par // Reuse code with composition
\par #include "Useful.h"
\par 
\par class Y \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   X x; // Embedded object
\par   Y() \{ i = 0; \}
\par   void f(int ii) \{ i = ii; \}
\par   int g() const \{ return i; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Y y;
\par   y.f(47);
\par   y.x.set(37); // Access the embedded object
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Accessing the member functions of the embedded object}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {embedded\: object}}}{ (referred to as a }{\i subobject}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {subobject}}}{) simply requires another member selection.
\par It\rquote s probably more common to make the embedded objects }{\b private}{, so they become part of the underlying implementation (which means you can change the implementation if you want). The }{\b public}{
 interface functions for your new class then involve the use of the embedded object, but they don\rquote t necessarily mimic the object\rquote s interface:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:Compose2.cpp
\par // Private embedded objects
\par #include "Useful.h"
\par 
\par class Y \{
\par   int i;
\par   X x; // Embedded object
\par public:
\par   Y() \{ i = 0; \}
\par   void f(int ii) \{ i = ii; x.set(ii); \}
\par   int g() const \{ return i * x.read(); \}
\par   void permute() \{ x.permute(); \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Y y;
\par   y.f(47);
\par   y.permute();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here, the }{\b permute(\~)}{ function is carried through to the new class interface, but the other member functions of }{\b X}{ are used within the members of }{\b Y}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593252}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628724}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374015}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776233}I
nheritance syntax{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593252}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628724}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374015}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776233}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\v\fs16 {\*\atnid BE}{\*\atnauthor BE}\chatn {\*\annotation \pard\plain \s31\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst {PAGE \\# "'Page: '#'
\line '"}}{\fldrslt }}{\fs16 \chatn }{Change example so it doesn\rquote t use the words \ldblquote base\rdblquote  and \ldblquote derived.\rdblquote  Too confusing.}}}{The syntax for composition is obvious, but to perform inheritance there\rquote 
s a new and different form.
\par When you inherit, you are saying, \ldblquote This new class is like that old class.\rdblquote  You state this in code by giving the name of the class, as usual, but before the opening brace of the class body, you put a colon and the name of the }{\i 
base class}{ (or classes, for multiple inheritance). When you do this, you automatically get all the data members and member functions in the base class. Here\rquote s an example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:Inherit.cpp
\par // Simple inheritance
\par #include "Useful.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Y : public X \{
\par   int i; // Different from X's i
\par public:
\par   Y() \{ i = 0; \}
\par   int change() \{
\par     i = permute(); // Different name call
\par     return i;
\par   \}
\par   void set(int ii) \{
\par     i = ii;
\par     X::set(ii); // Same-name function call
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   cout << "sizeof(X) = " << sizeof(X) << endl;
\par   cout << "sizeof(Y) = "
\par        << sizeof(Y) << endl;
\par   Y D;
\par   D.change();
\par   // X function interface comes through:
\par   D.read();
\par   D.permute();
\par   // Redefined functions hide base versions:
\par   D.set(12);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In }{\b Y}{ you can see inheritance going on, which means that }{\b Y}{ will contain all the data elements in }{\b X}{ and all the member functions in }{\b X}{. In fact, }{\b Y}{ contain
s a subobject of }{\b X}{ just as if you had created a member object of }{\b X}{ inside }{\b Y}{ instead of inheriting from }{\b X}{. Both member objects and base class storage are referred to as subobjects}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {subobject}}}{.
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{ you can see that the data elements are added because the }{\b sizeof(Y)}{ is twice as big as }{\b sizeof(X)}{.
\par You\rquote ll notice that the base class is preceded by }{\b public}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {public\: inheritance}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\: public}}}{
. During inheritance, everything defaults to }{\b private}{, which means all the }{\b public}{ members of the base class are }{\b private}{ in the derived class. This is almost never what you want; the desired result is to keep all the }{\b public}{
 members of the base class }{\b public}{ in the derived class. You do this by using the }{\b public}{ keyword during inheritance.
\par In }{\b change(\~)}{, the base-class }{\b permute(\~)}{ function is called. The derived class has direct access to all the }{\b public }{base-class functions.
\par The }{\b set(\~)}{ function in the derived class }{\i redefines}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: redefinition during inheritance}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\:
 function redefinition}}}{the }{\b set(\~)}{ function in the base class. That is, if you call the functions }{\b read(\~)}{ and }{\b permute(\~)}{ for an object of type }{\b Y}{, you\rquote 
ll get the base-class versions of those functions (you can see this happen inside }{\b main(\~)}{), but if you call }{\b set(\~)}{ for a }{\b Y}{ object, you get the redefined version. This means that if you don\rquote 
t like the version of a function you get during inheritance, you can change what it does. (You can also add completely new functions like }{\b change(\~)}{.)
\par However, when you\rquote re redefining a function, you may still want to call the base-class version. If, inside }{\b set(\~)}{, you simply call }{\b set(\~)}{ you\rquote ll get the local version of the function \endash 
 a recursive function call. To call the base-class version, you must explicitly name it, using the base-class name and the scope resolution operator}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\:
 scope resolution operator for calling base-class functions}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {scope\: resolution operator, for calling base-class functions}}}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593253}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628725}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374016}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776234}
The constructor initializer list{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593253}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628725}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374016}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776234}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You\rquote ve seen how important it is in C++ to guarantee proper initialization, and it\rquote 
s no different during composition and inheritance. When an object is created, the compiler guarantees that constructors for all its subobjects are called. In the examples so far, all the subobjects have default constructors, and that\rquote 
s what the compiler automatically calls. But what happens if your subobjects }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {subobject}}}{don\rquote 
t have default constructors, or if you want to change a default argument in a constructor? This is a problem because the new class constructor doesn\rquote t have permission to access the }{\b private}{ data elements of the subobject, so it can\rquote 
t initialize them directly.
\par The solution is simple: Call the constructor for the subobject. C++ provides a special syntax for this, the }{\i constructor initializer list}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {initialization\: constructor initializer list}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {list\: constructor initializer list}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: initializer list}}}{
. The form of the constructor initializer list echoes the act of inheritance. With inheritance, you put the base classes after a colon and 
before the opening brace of the class body. In the constructor initializer list, you put the calls to subobject constructors after the constructor argument list and a colon, but before the opening brace of the function body. For a class }{\b MyType}{
, inherited from }{\b Bar}{, this might look like
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {MyType::MyType(int i) : Bar(i) \{ // ...
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {if }{\b Bar}{ has a constructor that takes a single }{\b int}{ argument.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374017}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776235}Member object initialization{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374017}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776235}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {member\: member object initialization}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {composition\:
 member object initialization}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {initialization\: member object initialization}}}{
It turns out that you use this very same syntax for member object initialization when using composition. For composition, you give the names of the objects rather than the class names. If you have more than one c
onstructor call in the initializer list, you separate the calls with commas:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {MyType2::MyType2(int i) : Bar(i), memb(i+1) \{ // ...
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This is the beginning of a constructor for class }{\b MyType2}{, which is inherited from }{\b Bar }{and contains a member object called }{\b memb}{
. Note that while you can see the type of the base class in the constructor initializer list, you only see the member object identifier.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374018}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776236}Built-in types in the initializer list{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374018}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776236}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The constructor initializer list allows you to explicitly call the constructors for member objects. In fact, there\rquote 
s no other way to call those constructors. The idea is that the constructors are all called before you get into the body of the new class\rquote s constructor. That way, any calls you make to member functions of subobjects will 
always go to initialized objects. There\rquote s no way to get to the opening brace of the constructor without }{\i some}{
 constructor being called for all the member objects and base-class objects, even if the compiler must make a hidden call to a default constructor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor}}}{
. This is a further enforcement of the C++ guarantee that no object (or part of an object) can get out of the starting gate without its constructor being called.
\par This idea that all the member objects are initialized by the opening brace of 
the constructor is a convenient programming aid, as well. Once you hit the opening brace, you can assume all subobjects are properly initialized and focus on specific tasks you want to accomplish in the constructor. However, there\rquote 
s a hitch: What about embedded objects of built-in types, which don\rquote t }{\i have}{ constructors?
\par To make the syntax consistent, you\rquote re allowed to treat built-in types as if they have a single constructor, which takes a single argument: a variable of the same type as the variable you\rquote re initializing. Thus, you can say
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class X \{
\par   int i;
\par   float f;
\par   char c;
\par   char* s;
\par public:
\par   X() : i(7), f(1.4), c(\lquote x\rquote ), s("howdy") \{\}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  // ...
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The action of these \ldblquote pseudoconstructor calls\rdblquote }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {pseudoconstructor, for built-in types}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {built-in type\: pseudoconstructor calls for}}}{ is to perform a simple assignment. It\rquote s a convenient technique and a good coding style, so you\rquote ll often see it used.
\par It\rquote s even possible to use the pseudoconstructor syntax when creating a variable of this type outside of a class:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int i(100);
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This makes built-in types act a little bit more like objects. Remember, though, that these are not real constructors. In particular, if you don\rquote 
t explicitly make a pseudo-constructor call, no initialization is performed.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593254}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628726}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374019}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776237}
Combining composition & inheritance{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593254}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628726}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374019}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776237}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {composition\: combining composition & inheritance}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\:
 combining composition & inheritance}}}{Of course, you can use the two together. The following example shows the creation of a more complex class, using both inheritance and composition.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:Combined.cpp
\par // Inheritance & composition
\par 
\par class A \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   A(int ii) : i(ii) \{\}
\par   ~A() \{\}
\par   void f() const \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class B \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   B(int ii) : i(ii) \{\}
\par   ~B() \{\}
\par   void f() const \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class C : public B \{
\par   A a;
\par public:
\par   C(int ii) : B(ii), a(ii) \{\}
\par   ~C() \{\} // Calls ~A() and ~B()
\par   void f() const \{  // Redefinition
\par     a.f();
\par     B::f();
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   C c(47);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b C}{ inherits from }{\b B}{ and has a member object (\ldblquote is composed of\rdblquote ) }{\b A}{. You can see the c
onstructor initializer list contains calls to both the base-class constructor and the member-object constructor.
\par The function }{\b C::f(\~)}{ redefines }{\b B::f(\~)}{ that it inherits, and also calls the base-class version. In addition, it calls }{\b a.f(\~)}{. Notice that the o
nly time you can talk about redefinition of functions is during inheritance; with a member object you can only manipulate the public interface of the object, not redefine it. In addition, calling }{\b f(\~)}{ for an object of class }{\b C}{
 would not call }{\b a.f(\~)}{ if }{\b C::f(\~)}{ had not been defined, whereas it }{\i would}{ call }{\b B::f(\~)}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Automatic destructor calls
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {destructor\: automatic destructor calls}}}{
Although you are often required to make explicit constructor calls in the initializer list, you never need to make explicit destructor calls because there\rquote s only one destructor for any class, and it doesn\rquote 
t take any arguments. However, the compiler still ensures that all destructors are called, and that means all the destructors in the entire hierarchy, starting with the most-derived destructor and working back to the root.
\par It\rquote s worth emphasizing that constructors and destructors are quite unusual in that every one in the hierarchy is called, whereas with a normal member function only that function is called, but not any of the base
-class versions. If you also want to call the base-class version of a normal member function that you\rquote re overriding, you must do it explicitly.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374020}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776238}Order of constructor & destructor calls{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374020}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776238}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It\rquote s interesting to know the order of constructor and destructor calls }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: order of constructor and destructor calls}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {destructor\: order of constructor and destructor calls}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {order\: of constructor and destructor calls}}}{
when an object has many subobjects. The following example shows exactly how it works:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:Order.cpp
\par // Constructor/destructor order
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("order.out");
\par 
\par #define CLASS(ID) class ID \{ \\
\par public: \\
\par   ID(int) \{ out << #ID " constructor\\n"; \} \\
\par   ~ID() \{ out << #ID " destructor\\n"; \} \\
\par \};
\par 
\par CLASS(Base1);
\par CLASS(Member1);
\par CLASS(Member2);
\par CLASS(Member3);
\par CLASS(Member4);
\par 
\par class Derived1 : public Base1 \{
\par   Member1 m1;
\par   Member2 m2;
\par public:
\par   Derived1(int) : m2(1), m1(2), Base1(3) \{
\par     out << "Derived1 constructor\\n";
\par   \}
\par   ~Derived1() \{
\par     out << "Derived1 destructor\\n";
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Derived2 : public Derived1 \{
\par   Member3 m3;
\par   Member4 m4;
\par public:
\par   Derived2() : m3(1), Derived1(2), m4(3) \{
\par     out << "Derived2 constructor\\n";
\par   \}
\par   ~Derived2() \{
\par     out << "Derived2 destructor\\n";
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Derived2 d2;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {First, an }{\b ofstream}{
 object is created to send all the output to a file. Then, to save some typing and demonstrate a macro technique that will be replaced by a much improved technique in Chapter XX, a macro is created to build some of
 the classes, which are then used in inheritance and composition. Each of the constructors and destructors report themselves to the trace file. Note that the constructors are not default constructors; they each have an }{\b int}{
 argument. The argument itself has no identifier; its only job is to force you to explicitly call the constructors in the initializer list. (Eliminating the identifier prevents compiler warning messages.)
\par The output of this program is
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Base1 constructor
\par Member1 constructor
\par Member2 constructor
\par Derived1 constructor
\par Member3 constructor
\par Member4 constructor
\par Derived2 constructor
\par Derived2 destructor
\par Member4 destructor
\par Member3 destructor
\par Derived1 destructor
\par Member2 destructor
\par Member1 destructor
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Base1 destructor
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can see that construction starts 
at the very root of the class hierarchy, and that at each level the base class constructor is called first, followed by the member object constructors. The destructors are called in exactly the reverse order of the constructors \endash 
 this is important because of potential dependencies.
\par It\rquote s also interesting that the order of constructor calls for member objects is completely unaffected by the order of the calls in the constructor initializer list. The order is determined by the order that the member objects are
 declared in the class. If you could change the order of constructor calls via the constructor initializer list, you could have two different call sequences in two different constructors, but the poor destructor wouldn\rquote 
t know how to properly reverse the order of the calls for destruction, and you could end up with a dependency problem.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374021}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776239}Name hiding{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374021}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776239}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {name\: hiding, during inheritance}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {hiding\: name hiding during inheritance }
}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\: name hiding during inheritance }}}{If a base class has a function name that\rquote s overloaded several times, redefining that function name in the derived class will hide }{\i 
all}{ the base-class versions. That is, they become unavailable in the derived class:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:Hide.cpp
\par // Name hiding during inheritance
\par 
\par class Homer \{
\par public:
\par   int doh(int) const \{ return 1; \}
\par   char doh(char) const \{ return 'd';\}
\par   float doh(float) const \{ return 1.0; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Bart : public Homer \{
\par public:
\par   class Milhouse \{\};
\par   void doh(Milhouse) const \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Bart b;
\par //! b.doh(1); // Error
\par //! b.doh('x'); // Error
\par //! b.doh(1.0); // Error
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Because }{\b Bart}{ redefines }{\b doh(\~)}{, none of the base-class versions can be called for a }{\b Bart}{ object. In each case, the compiler attempts to convert the argument into a }{\b Milhouse}{
 object and complains because it can\rquote t find a conversion.
\par As you\rquote ll see in the next chapter, it\rquote s far more common to redefine functions using exactly the same signature and return type as in the base class.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374022}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776240}Functions that don\rquote t automatically inherit{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374022}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776240}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Not all functions are autom
atically inherited from the base class into the derived class. Constructors and destructors deal with the creation and destruction of an object, and they can know what to do with the aspects of the object only for their particular level, so all the constr
uctors }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor}}}{and destructors }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {destructor}}}{
in the entire hierarchy must be called. Thus, constructors and destructors don\rquote t inherit.
\par In addition, the }{\b operator=}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: =}}}{doesn\rquote t inherit because it performs a constructor-like activity.
 That is, just because you know how to initialize all the members of an object on the left-hand side of the }{\b =}{ from an object on the right-hand side doesn\rquote t mean that initialization will still have meaning after inheritance.
\par In lieu of inheritance, these functions are synthesized by the compiler if you don\rquote t create them yourself. (With constructors, you can\rquote t create }{\i any }{
constructors for the default constructor and the copy-constructor to be automatically created.) This was briefly described in Chapter XX. The synthesized constructors use memberwise initialization and the synthesized }{\b operator=}{
 uses memberwise assignment. Here\rquote s an example of the functions that are created by the compiler rather than inherited:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:Ninherit.cpp
\par // Non-inherited functions
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("ninherit.out");
\par 
\par class Root \{
\par public:
\par   Root() \{ out << "Root()\\n"; \}
\par   Root(Root&) \{ out << "Root(Root&)\\n"; \}
\par   Root(int) \{ out << "Root(int)\\n"; \}
\par   Root& operator=(const Root&) \{
\par     out << "Root::operator=()\\n";
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par   class Other \{\};
\par   operator Other() const \{
\par     out << "Root::operator Other()\\n";
\par     return Other();
\par   \}
\par   ~Root() \{ out << "~Root()\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Derived : public Root \{\};
\par 
\par void f(Root::Other) \{\}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Derived d1;  // Default constructor
\par   Derived d2 = d1; // Copy-constructor
\par //! Derived d3(1); // Error: no int constructor
\par   d1 = d2; // Operator= not inherited
\par   f(d1); // Type-conversion IS inherited
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {All the constructors and the }{\b operator=}{ announce themselves so you can see when they\rquote re used by the compiler. In addition, the }{\b operator Other(\~)}{
 performs automatic type conversion from a }{\b Root}{ object to an object of the nested class }{\b Other}{. The class }{\b Derived}{ simply inherits from }{\b Root}{ and creates no functions (to see how the compiler responds). The function }{\b f(\~)}{
 takes an }{\b Other}{ object to test the automatic type conversion function.
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{, the default constructor and copy-constructor are created and the }{\b Root}{ versions are called as part of the constructor-
call hierarchy. Even though it looks like inheritance, new constructors are actually created. As you might expect, no constructors with arguments are automatically created because that\rquote s too much for the compiler to intuit.
\par The }{\b operator=}{ is also synthesized as a new function in }{\b Derived}{ using memberwise assignment because that function was not explicitly written in the new class.
\par Because of all these rules about rewriting functions that handle object creation, it may seem a little strange at first that the automatic type conversion operator }{\i is}{ inherited. But it\rquote s not too unreasonable \endash 
 if there are enough pieces in }{\b Root}{ to make an }{\b Other}{ object, those pieces are still there in anything derived from }{\b Root}{ and the type conversion operator is still valid (even though you may in fact want to redefine it).
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593255}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628727}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374023}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776241}
Choosing composition vs. inheritance{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593255}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628727}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374023}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776241}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {composition\: choosing composition vs. inheritance}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\:
 choosing composition vs. inheritance}}}{Both composition and inheritance place subobjects}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {subobject}}}{
 inside your new class. Both use the constructor initializer list to construct these subobjects. You may now be wondering what the difference is between the two, and when to choose one over the other.
\par Composition is generally used when you want the features of an existing class inside your new class, but not its interface. That is, you embed an object that you\rquote 
re planning on using to implement features of your new class, but the user of your new class sees the interface you\rquote ve defined rather than the interface from the original class. For this effect, you embed }{\b private}{
 objects of existing classes inside your new class.
\par Sometimes it makes sense to allow the class user to directly access the composition of your new class, that is, to make the member objects }{\b public}{
. The member objects use implementation hiding themselves, so this is a safe thing to do and when the user knows you\rquote re assembling a bunch of parts, it makes the interface easier to understand. A }{\b car}{ object is a good example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:Car.cpp
\par // Public composition
\par 
\par class Engine \{
\par public:
\par   void start() const \{\}
\par   void rev() const \{\}
\par   void stop() const \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Wheel \{
\par public:
\par   void inflate(int psi) const \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Window \{
\par public:
\par   void rollup() const \{\}
\par   void rolldown() const \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Door \{
\par public:
\par   Window window;
\par   void open() const \{\}
\par   void close() const \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Car \{
\par public:
\par   Engine engine;
\par   Wheel wheel[4];
\par   Door left, right; // 2-door
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Car car;
\par   car.left.window.rollup();
\par   car.wheel[0].inflate(72);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Because the composition of a car is part of the analysis of the problem (and not simply part of the underlying design), making the members public assists the client programmer\rquote 
s understanding of how to use the class and requires less code complexity for the creator of the class.
\par With a little thought, you\rquote ll also see that it would make no sense to compose a car using a vehicle object \endash  a car doesn\rquote t contain a vehicle, it }{\i is}{ a vehicle. The }{\i is-a}{ relationship is expressed with inheritance, and the 
}{\i has-a}{ relationship is expressed with composition.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374024}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776242}Subtyping{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374024}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776242}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {subtyping}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\: subtyping}}}{
Now suppose you want to create a type of }{\b ifstream}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ifstream}}}{object that not only opens a file but also keeps track of the name of the file. You can use composition and embed both an }
{\b ifstream}{ and a }{\b strstream}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {strstream}}}{into the new class:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:FName1.cpp
\par // An fstream with a file name
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <strstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class FName1 \{
\par   ifstream file;
\par   enum \{ bsize = 100 \};
\par   char buf[bsize];
\par   ostrstream fname;
\par   int nameset;
\par public:
\par   FName1() : fname(buf, bsize), nameset(0) \{\}
\par   FName1(const char* filename)
\par     : file(filename), fname(buf, bsize) \{
\par       assure(file, filename);
\par       fname << filename << ends;
\par       nameset = 1;
\par   \}
\par   const char* name() const \{ return buf; \}
\par   void name(const char* newname) \{
\par     if(nameset) return; // Don't overwrite
\par     fname << newname << ends;
\par     nameset = 1;
\par   \}
\par   operator ifstream&() \{ return file; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   FName1 file("FName1.cpp");
\par   cout << file.name() << endl;
\par   // Error: rdbuf() not a member:
\par //!  cout << file.rdbuf() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There\rquote s a problem here, however. An attempt is made to allow the use of the }{\b FName1}{ object anywhere an }{\b ifstream}{
 object is used, by including an automatic type conversion operator from }{\b FName1}{ to an }{\b ifstream&}{. But in main, the line
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {cout << file.rdbuf() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {will not compile because automatic type conversion happens only in function calls, not during member selection. So this approach won\rquote t work.
\par A second approach is to add the definition of }{\b rdbuf(\~)}{ to }{\b FName1}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {filebuf* rdbuf() \{ return file.rdbuf(); \}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This will work if there are only a few functions you want to bring through from the }{\b ifstream}{ class. In that case you\rquote re only using part of the class, and composition }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {composition}}}{is appropriate.
\par But what if you want everything in the class to come through? This is called }{\i subtyping}{ because you\rquote re making a new type from an existing type, and yo
u want your new type to have exactly the same interface as the existing type (plus any other member functions you want to add), so you can use it everywhere you\rquote 
d use the existing type. This is where inheritance is essential. You can see that subtyping solves the problem in the preceding example perfectly:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:FName2.cpp
\par // Subtyping solves the problem
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <strstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class FName2 : public ifstream \{
\par   enum \{ bsize = 100 \};
\par   char buf[bsize];
\par   ostrstream fname;
\par   int nameset;
\par public:
\par   FName2() : fname(buf, bsize), nameset(0) \{\}
\par   FName2(const char* filename)
\par     : ifstream(filename), fname(buf, bsize) \{
\par       assure(*this, filename);
\par       fname << filename << ends;
\par       nameset = 1;
\par   \}
\par   const char* name() const \{ return buf; \}
\par   void name(const char* newname) \{
\par     if(nameset) return; // Don't overwrite
\par     fname << newname << ends;
\par     nameset = 1;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   FName2 file("FName2.cpp");
\par   assure(file, "FName2.cpp");
\par   cout << "name: " << file.name() << endl;
\par   const int bsize = 100;
\par   char buf[bsize];
\par   file.getline(buf, bsize); // This works too!
\par   file.seekg(-200, ios::end);
\par   cout << file.rdbuf() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now any member function that works with an }{\b ifstream}{ object also works with an }{\b FName2}{ object. That\rquote s because an }{\b FName2}{ }{\i is}{ a type of }{\b ifstream}{; it doesn\rquote 
t simply contain one. This is a very important issue that will be explored at the end of this chapter and in the next one.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374025}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776243}Specialization{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374025}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776243}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {specialization}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\: specialization}}}{
When you inherit, you take an existing class and make a special version of it. Generally, this means you\rquote re taking a general-purpose class and specializing it for a particular need.
\par For example, consider the }{\b Stack}{ class from the previous chapter. One of the problems with that class is that you had to perform a cast every time you fetched a pointer from the container. This is not only tedious, it\rquote s unsafe \endash 
 you could cast the pointer to anything you want.
\par An approach that seems better at first glance is to specialize the general }{\b Stack}{ class using inheritance. Here\rquote s an example that uses the class from the previous chapter:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:InheritStack.cpp
\par //\{L\} ../C13/Stack4
\par // Specializing the Stack class
\par #include "../C13/Stack4.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class StringList : public Stack \{
\par public:
\par   void push(string* str) \{
\par     Stack::push(str);
\par   \}
\par   string* peek() const \{
\par     return (string*)Stack::peek();
\par   \}
\par   string* pop() \{
\par     return (string*)Stack::pop();
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   ifstream file("InheritStack.cpp");
\par   assure(file, "InheritStack.cpp");
\par   string line;
\par   StringList textlines;
\par   while(getline(file,line))
\par     textlines.push(new string(line));
\par   string* s;
\par   while((s = textlines.pop()) != 0) // No cast!
\par     cout << *s << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b Stack4.h}{ header file is brought in from Chapter XX. (The Stack4 object file must be linked in as well.)
\par }{\b Stringlist}{ specializes }{\b Stack}{ so that }{\b push(\~)}{ will accept only }{\b String}{ pointers. Before, }{\b Stack }{would accept }{\b void}{
 pointers, so the user had no type checking to make sure the proper pointers were inserted. In addition, }{\b peek(\~)}{ and }{\b pop(\~)}{ now return }{\b String}{ pointers rather than }{\b void}{ pointers, so no cast is necessary to use the pointer.

\par Amazingly enough, this extra type-checking safety is free! The compiler is being given extra type information, that it uses at compile-time, but the functions are inline and no extra code is generated.
\par Unfortunately}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ownership}}}{, inheritance doesn\rquote t solve all the problems with this container class. The destructor still causes trouble. You\rquote ll remember from Chapter XX that the }{
\b Stack::~Stack(\~)}{ destructor moves through the list and calls }{\b delete}{ for all the pointers. The problem is, }{\b delete}{ is called for }{\b void}{ pointers, which only releases the memory and doesn\rquote t call the destructors (because }{\b 
void*}{ has no type information). If a }{\b Stringlist::~Stringlist(\~)}{ destructor is created to move through the list and call }{\b delete}{ for all the }{\b String}{ pointers in the list, the problem is solved }{\i if
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls251\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls251\adjustright {The }{\b Stack}{
 data members are made }{\b protected}{ so the }{\b Stringlist}{ destructor can access them. (}{\b protected}{ is described a bit later in the chapter.)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls251\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls251\adjustright {The }{\b Stack}{
 base class destructor is removed so the memory isn\rquote t released twice.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls251\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls251\adjustright {
No more inheritance is performed, because you\rquote d end up with the same dilemma again: multiple destructor calls versus an incorrect destructor call (to a }{\b String}{ object rather than what the class derived from }{\b Stringlist}{ might contain).

\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {This issue will be revisited in the next chapter, but will not be fully solved until templates are introduced in Chapter XX.
\par A more important observation to make about this example is that it }{\i changes the interface}{ of the }{\b Stack}{ in the process of inheritance. If the interface is different, then a }{\b Stringlist}{ really isn\rquote t a }{\b Stack}{
, and you will never be able to correctly use a }{\b Stringlist}{ as a }{\b Stack}{. This questions the use of inheritance here: if you\rquote re not creating a }{\b Stringlist}{ that }{\i is-a}{ type of }{\b Stack}{, then
 why are you inheriting? A more appropriate version of }{\b Stringlist}{ will be shown later in the chapter.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374026}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776244}private}{ inheritance{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374026}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776244}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {private\: private inheritance}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\: private inheritance}}}{
You can inherit a base class privately by leaving off the }{\b public}{ in the base-class list, or by explicitly saying }{\b private}{ (probably a better policy because it is clear to the user that you mean it). When you inherit privately, you\rquote re 
\ldblquote implementing in terms of\rdblquote ; that is, you\rquote re creating a new class that has all the data and functionality of the base class, but that functionality is hidden, so it\rquote 
s only part of the underlying implementation. The class user has no access to the underlying functionality, and an object cannot be treated as a member of the base class (as it was in }{\b FName2.cpp}{).
\par You may wonder what the purpose of }{\b private}{ inheritance is, because the alternative of creating a }{\b private}{ object in the new class seems more appropriate. }{\b private}{ inheritance is included in the language for completeness, but if for no o
ther reason than to reduce confusion, you\rquote ll usually want to use a }{\b private}{ member rather than }{\b private}{ inheritance. However, there may occasionally be situations where you want to produce part of the same interface as the base class }{
\i and}{ disallow the treatment of the object as if it were a base-class object. }{\b private}{ inheritance provides this ability.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Publicizing privately inherited members
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you inherit privately, all the }{\b public}{ members of the base class become }{\b private}{. If you want any of them to be visible, just say their names (no arguments or return values) in the }{
\b public}{ section of the derived class:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:Privinh.cpp
\par // Private inheritance
\par 
\par class Base1 \{
\par public:
\par   char f() const \{ return 'a'; \}
\par   int g() const \{ return 2; \}
\par   float h() const \{ return 3.0; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Derived : Base1 \{ // Private inheritance
\par public:
\par   Base1::f; // Name publicizes member
\par   Base1::h;
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Derived d;
\par   d.f();
\par   d.h();
\par //! d.g(); // Error -- private function
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Thus, }{\b private}{ inheritance is useful if you want to hide part of the functionality of the base class.
\par You should think carefully before using }{\b private}{ inheritance instead of member objects; }{\b private}{ inheritance has particular complications when combined with runtime type identification (the subject of Chapter XX).
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593256}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628728}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374027}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776245}protected}{
{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593256}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628728}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374027}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776245}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now that you\rquote ve been introduced to inheritance, the keyword }{\b protected}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {protected}}}{ finally has meaning. In an ideal world, }{
\b private}{ members would always be hard-and-fast }{\b private}{, but in real projects there are times when you want to make something hidden from the world at large and yet allow access for members of derived classes. The }{\b protected}{
 keyword is a nod to pragmatism; it says, \ldblquote This is }{\b private}{ as far as the class user is concerned, but available to anyone who inherits from this class.\rdblquote 
\par The best approach is to leave the data members }{\b private}{ \endash  you should always preserve your right to change the underlying implementation. You can then allow controlled access to inheritors of your class through }{\b protected }{
member functions:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:Protect.cpp \{O\}
\par // The protected keyword
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Base \{
\par   int i;
\par protected:
\par   int read() const \{ return i; \}
\par   void set(int ii) \{ i = ii; \}
\par public:
\par   Base(int ii = 0) : i(ii) \{\}
\par   int value(int m) const \{ return m*i; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Derived : public Base \{
\par   int j;
\par public:
\par   Derived(int jj = 0) : j(jj) \{\}
\par   void change(int x) \{ set(x); \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}; ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You will find examples of the need for }{\b protected}{ in examples later in this book.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374028}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776246}protected}{ inheritance{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374028}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776246}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {protected\: inheritance}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\: protected inheritance}}}{When you
\rquote re inheriting, the base class defaults to }{\b private}{, which means that all the public member functions are }{\b private}{ to the user of the new class. Normally, you\rquote ll make the inheritance }{\b public}{ so the interf
ace of the base class is also the interface of the derived class. However, you can also use the }{\b protected}{ keyword during inheritance.
\par Protected derivation means \ldblquote implemented-in-terms-of\rdblquote  to other classes but \ldblquote is-a\rdblquote  for derived classes and friends. It\rquote s something you don\rquote t use very often, but it\rquote 
s in the language for completeness.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593257}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628729}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374029}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776247}
Multiple inheritance{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593257}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628729}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374029}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776247}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can inherit from one class, so it would seem to make sense to inherit from more than one class at a time. Indeed you can, but whether it makes sense as par
t of a design is a subject of continuing debate. One thing is generally agreed upon: You shouldn\rquote t try this until you\rquote ve been programming quite a while and understand the language thoroughly. By that time, you\rquote 
ll probably realize that no matter how much you think you absolutely must use multiple inheritance, you can almost always get away with single inheritance. 
\par Initially, multiple inheritance seems simple enough: You add more classes in the base-class list during inheritance, separated by commas. However
, multiple inheritance introduces a number of possibilities for ambiguity, which is why Chapter XX is devoted to the subject.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593258}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628730}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374030}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776248}
Incremental development{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593258}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628730}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374030}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776248}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {One of the advantages of inheritance is that it supports }{\i incremental development}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {development, incremental}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {incremental development}}}{
 by allowing you to introduce new code without causing bugs in existing code and isolating new bugs to the new code. By inheriting from an existing, functional class and adding data members and member functions (and redef
ining existing member functions) you leave the existing code \endash  that someone else may still be using \endash  untouched and unbugged. If a bug happens, you know it\rquote 
s in your new code, which is much shorter and easier to read than if you had modified the body of existing code.
\par It\rquote s rather amazing how cleanly the classes are separated. You don\rquote 
t even need the source code for the member functions to reuse the code, just the header file describing the class and the object file or library file with the compiled member functions. (This is true for both inheritance and composition.)
\par It\rquote s important to realize that program development is an incremental process, just like human learning. You can do as much analysis as you want, but you still won\rquote t know all the answers when you set out on a project. You\rquote 
ll have much more success \endash  and more immediate feedback \endash  if you start out to \ldblquote grow\rdblquote  your project as an organic, evolutionary creature, rather than constructing it all at once like a glass-box skyscraper.
\par Although inheritance fo
r experimentation is a useful technique, at some point after things stabilize you need to take a new look at your class hierarchy with an eye to collapsing it into a sensible structure. Remember that underneath it all, inheritance is meant to express a re
lationship that says, \ldblquote This new class is a }{\i type of}{ that old class.\rdblquote 
 Your program should not be concerned with pushing bits around, but instead with creating and manipulating objects of various types to express a model in the terms given you by the problem\rquote s space.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593259}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628731}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374031}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776249}Upcasting
{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593259}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628731}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374031}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776249}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {upcasting}}}{Earlier in the chapter, you saw how an object of a class derived from }{\b ofstream}{
 has all the characteristics and behaviors of an }{\b ofstream}{ object. In }{\b FName2.cpp}{, any }{\b ofstream }{member function could be called for an }{\b FName2}{ object.
\par The most important aspect of inheritance is not that it provides member functions for the new class, however. It\rquote s the relationship expressed between the new class and the base class. This relationship can be summarized by saying, \ldblquote 
The new class }{\i is a type of}{ the existing class.\rdblquote  
\par This description is not just a fanciful way of explaining inheritance \endash  it\rquote s supported directly by the compiler. As an example, consider a base class called }{\b Instrument}{ that represents musical instruments and a derived class called }{
\b Wind}{. Because inheritance means that all the functions in the base class are also available in the derived class, any message you can send to the base class can also be sent to the derived class. So if the }{\b Instrument}{ class has a }{\b play(\~)}
{ member function, so will }{\b Wind}{ instruments. This means we can accurately say that a }{\b Wind}{ object is also a type of }{\b Instrument}{. The following example shows how the compiler supports this notion:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:Wind.cpp
\par // Inheritance & upcasting
\par enum note \{ middleC, Csharp, Cflat \}; // Etc.
\par 
\par class Instrument \{
\par public:
\par   void play(note) const \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Wind objects are Instruments
\par // because they have the same interface:
\par class Wind : public Instrument \{\};
\par 
\par void tune(Instrument& i) \{
\par   // ...
\par   i.play(middleC);
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Wind flute;
\par   tune(flute); // Upcasting
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {What\rquote s interesting in this example is the }{\b tune(\~)}{ function, which accepts an }{\b Instrument}{ reference. However, in }{\b main(\~)}{ the }{\b tune(\~)}{
 function is called by giving it a }{\b Wind}{ object. Given that C++ is very particular about type checking, it seems strange that a function that accepts one type will readily accept another type, until you realize that a }{\b Wind}{ object is also an }
{\b Instrument}{ object, and there\rquote s no function that }{\b tune(\~)}{ could call for an }{\b Instrument}{ that isn\rquote t also in }{\b Wind}{. Inside }{\b tune(\~)}{, the code works for }{\b Instrument}{ and anything derived from }{\b Instrument}
{, and the act of converting a }{\b Wind}{ object, reference, or pointer into an }{\b Instrument}{ object, reference, or pointer is called }{\i upcasting}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374032}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776250}Why \ldblquote upcasting\rdblquote ?{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374032}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776250}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The reason for the term is historical and is based on the way class inheritance diagrams }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\: class inheritance diagrams}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {diagram\: class inheritance diagrams}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\: inheritance diagrams}}}{have traditionally been drawn: with the root at the to
p of the page, growing downward. (Of course, you can draw your diagrams any way you find helpful.) The inheritance diagram for }{\b Wind.cpp}{ is then:
