\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {Casting from derived to base moves }{\i up}{ on the inheritance diagram, so it\rquote s commonly referred to as upcasting. Upcasting is always safe because you\rquote 
re going from a more specific type to a more general type \endash  the only thing that can occur to the class interface is that it can lose member functions, not gain them. This is why the compiler allow
s upcasting without any explicit casts or other special notation.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Downcasting
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can also perform the reverse of upcasting, called }{\i downcasting}{, but this involves a dilemma that is the subject of Chapter XX.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776251}Upcasting and the copy-constructor (not indexed){\*\bkmkend _Toc451776251}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
If you allow the compiler to synthesize a copy-constructor for a derived class, it will automatically call the base-class copy-constructor, and then the copy-constructors for all the member objects (or perform a bitcopy on built-in types) so you\rquote 
ll get the right behavior:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:Ccright.cpp
\par // Correctly synthesizing the CC
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Parent \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   Parent(int ii) : i(ii) \{
\par     cout << "Parent(int ii)\\n";
\par   \}
\par   Parent(const Parent& b) : i(b.i) \{
\par     cout << "Parent(Parent&)\\n";
\par   \}
\par   Parent() :i(0) \{ cout << "Parent()\\n"; \}
\par   friend ostream&
\par     operator<<(ostream& os, const Parent& b) \{
\par     return os << "Parent: " << b.i << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Member \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   Member(int ii) : i(ii) \{
\par     cout << "Member(int ii)\\n";
\par   \}
\par   Member(const Member& m) : i(m.i) \{
\par     cout << "Member(Member&)\\n";
\par   \}
\par   friend ostream&
\par     operator<<(ostream& os, const Member& m) \{
\par     return os << "Member: " << m.i << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Child : public Parent \{
\par   int i;
\par   Member m;
\par public:
\par   Child(int ii) : Parent(ii), i(ii), m(ii) \{
\par     cout << "Child(int ii)\\n";
\par   \}
\par   friend ostream&
\par     operator<<(ostream& os, const Child& d)\{
\par     return os << (Parent&)d << d.m
\par               << "Child: " << d.i << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Child d(2);
\par   cout << "calling copy-constructor: " << endl;
\par   Child d2 = d; // Calls copy-constructor
\par   cout << "values in d2:\\n" << d2;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b operator<<}{ for }{\b Child}{ is interesting because of the way that it calls the }{\b operator<<}{ for the }{\b Parent}{ part within it: by casting the }{\b Child }{object to a }{\b Parent&}
{ (if you cast to a }{\b Parent}{ object instead of a reference you\rquote ll end up creating a temporary):
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {return os << (Parent&)d << d.m
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Since the compiler then sees it as a }{\b Parent}{, it calls the }{\b Parent}{ version of }{\b operator<<}{.
\par You can see that }{\b Child}{ has no explicitly-defined copy-constructor. The compiler then synthesizes the copy-constructor (since that is one of the four functions it will synthesize, along with the default constructor \endash  if you don\rquote 
t create any constructors \endash  the }{\b operator=}{ and the destructor) by calling the }{\b Parent}{ copy-constructor and the }{\b Member}{ copy-constructor. This is shown in the output 
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Parent(int ii)
\par Member(int ii)
\par Child(int ii)
\par calling copy-constructor:
\par Parent(Parent&)
\par Member(Member&)
\par values in d2:
\par Parent: 2
\par Member: 2
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Child: 2
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {However, if you try to write your own copy-constructor for }{\b Child }{and you make an innocent mistake and do it badly:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Child(const Child& d) : i(d.i), m(d.m) \{\}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\i default}{ constructor will be automatically called, since that\rquote s what the compiler falls back on when it has no other choice of constructor to call (remember that }{\i some}{
 constructor must always be called for every object, regardless of whether it\rquote s a subobject of another class). The output will then be:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Parent(int ii)
\par Member(int ii)
\par Child(int ii)
\par calling copy-constructor:
\par Parent()
\par Member(Member&)
\par values in d2:
\par Parent: 0
\par Member: 2
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Child: 2
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This is probably not what you expect, since generally you\rquote ll want the base-class portion to be copied from the existing object to the new object as part of copy-construction.
\par To repair the problem you must remember to properly call the base-class copy-constructor (as the compiler does) whenever you write your own copy-constructor. This can seem a little strange-looking at first but it\rquote s another example of upcasting:

\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  Child(const Child& d)
\par     : Parent(d), i(d.i), m(d.m) \{
\par     cout << "Child(Child&)\\n";
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  \}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The strange part is where the }{\b Parent}{ copy-constructor is called: }{\b Parent(d)}{. What does it mean to pass a }{\b Child}{ object to a }{\b Parent}{ constructor? Here\rquote s the trick: }{\b 
Child}{ is inherited from }{\b Parent}{, so a }{\b Child}{ reference }{\i is}{ a }{\b Parent}{ reference. So the base-class copy-constructor upcasts a reference to }{\b Child}{ to a reference to }{\b Parent}{
 and uses it to perform the copy-construction. When you write your own copy constructors you\rquote ll generally want to do this.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374033}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776252}Composition vs. inheritance (revisited){\*\bkmkend _Toc312374033}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776252}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {composition\: vs. inheritance}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\: vs. composition}}}{
One of the clearest ways to determine whether you should be using composition or inheritance is by asking whether you\rquote ll ever need to upcast from your new class. Earlier in this chapter, the }{\b Stack}{
 class was specialized using inheritance. However, chances are the }{\b Stringlist}{ objects will be used only as }{\b String}{ containers, and never upcast, so a more appropriate alternative is composition:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C14:InheritStack2.cpp
\par //\{L\} ../C13/Stack4
\par // Composition vs. inheritance
\par #include "../C13/Stack4.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class StringList \{
\par   Stack stack; // Embed instead of inherit
\par public:
\par   void push(string* str) \{
\par     stack.push(str);
\par   \}
\par   string* peek() const \{
\par     return (string*)stack.peek();
\par   \}
\par   string* pop() \{
\par     return (string*)stack.pop();
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   ifstream file("InheritStack2.cpp");
\par   assure(file, "InheritStack2.cpp");
\par   string line;
\par   StringList textlines;
\par   while(getline(file,line))
\par     textlines.push(new string(line));
\par   string* s;
\par   while((s = textlines.pop()) != 0) // No cast!
\par     cout << *s << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The file is identical to }{\b Inhstack.cpp}{, except that a }{\b Stack}{ object is embedded in }{\b Stringlist}{, and member functions are called for the embedded object. There\rquote 
s still no time or space overhead because the subobject takes up the same amount of space, and all the additional type checking happens at compile time.
\par You can also use }{\b private}{ inheritance to express \ldblquote implemented in terms of.\rdblquote  The method you use to create the }{\b Stringlist}{ class is not critical in this situation \endash  they all solve the proble
m adequately. One place it becomes important, however, is when multiple inheritance}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\: multiple}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {multiple inheritance}
}}{ might be warranted. In that case, if you can detect a class where composition can be used instead of inheritance, you may be able to eliminate the need for multiple inheritance.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374034}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776253}Pointer & reference upcasting{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374034}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776253}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In }{\b Wind.cpp}{, the upcasting occurs during the function call \endash  a }{\b Wind}{ object outside the function has its reference taken and becomes an }{\b Instrument}{ reference inside th
e function. Upcasting can also occur during a simple assignment to a pointer or reference:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Wind w;
\par Instrument* ip = &w; // Upcast
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Instrument& ir = w; // Upcast
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Like the function call, neither of these cases require an explicit cast.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374035}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776254}A crisis{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374035}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776254}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Of course, any upcast loses type information about an object. If you say
