\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {the compiler can deal with }{\b ip}{\b\i  }{only as an }{\b Instrument}{ pointer and nothing else. That is, it cannot know that }{\b ip}{ }{\i actually}{ happens to point to a }{\b Wind}{
 object. So when you call the }{\b play(\~)}{ member function by saying 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {ip->play(middleC);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {the compiler can know only that it\rquote s calling }{\b play(\~)}{ for an }{\b Instrument}{ pointer, and call the base-class version of }{\b Instrument::play(\~)}{
 instead of what it should do, which is call }{\b Wind::play(\~)}{. Thus you won\rquote t get the correct behavior.
\par This is a significant problem; it is solved in the next chapter by introducing the third cornerstone of object-oriented programming: polymorphism (implemented in C++ with }{\b virtual}{ functions).
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593260}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628732}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374036}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776255}Summary
{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593260}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628732}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374036}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776255}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Both inheritan
ce and composition allow you to create a new type from existing types, and both embed subobjects of the existing types inside the new type. Typically, however, you use composition to reuse existing types as part of the underlying implementation of the new
 type and inheritance when you want to reuse the interface as well as the implementation. If the derived class has the base-class interface, it can be }{\i upcast}{ to the base, which is critical for polymorphism as you\rquote ll see in the next chapter.

\par Although code reuse through composition and inheritance is very helpful for rapid project development, you\rquote 
ll generally want to redesign your class hierarchy before allowing other programmers to become dependent on it. Your goal is a hierarchy where each class has a specific use and is neither too big (encompassing so much functionality that it\rquote 
s unwieldy to reuse) nor annoyingly small (you can\rquote t use it by itself or without adding functionality). Your finished classes should themselves be easily reused.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374037}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776256}Exercises{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374037}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776256}
