{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374038}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776257}15: Polymorphism & virtual functions{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593262}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628734}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374038}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776257}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {Polymorphism (implemented in C++ with }{\b virtual}{ function
s) is the third essential feature of an object-oriented programming language, after data abstraction and inheritance. 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It provides another dimension of separation of interface from implementation, to decouple }{\i what}{ from }{\i how}{. Polymorphism allows improved cod
e organization and readability as well as the creation of }{\i extensible}{ programs that can be \ldblquote grown\rdblquote  not only during the original creation of the project, but also when new features are desired.
\par Encapsulation creates new data types by combining characteristics and behaviors. Implementation hiding separates the interface from the implementation by making the details }{\b private}{
. This sort of mechanical organization makes ready sense to someone with a procedural programming background. But virtual functions deal with decoupling in terms of }{\i types}{
. In the last chapter, you saw how inheritance allows the treatment of an object as its own type }{\i or}{ its base type. This ability is critical because it allows many types (derived from the same base type) to be treated as if the
y were one type, and a single piece of code to work on all those different types equally. The virtual function allows one type to express its distinction from another, similar type, as long as they\rquote 
re both derived from the same base type. This distinction is expressed through differences in behavior of the functions you can call through the base class.
\par In this chapter, you\rquote ll learn about virtual functions starting from the very basics, with simple examples that strip away everything but the \ldblquote virtualness\rdblquote  of the program.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374039}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776258}Evolution of C++ programmers
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374039}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776258}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {C programmers}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C\: C programmers learning C++}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C++\: C programmers learning C++}}}{ seem to acquire C++ in three steps. First, as simply a \ldblquote better C,\rdblquote 
 because C++ forces you to declare all functions before using them and is much pickier about how variables are used. You can often find the errors in a C program simply by compiling it with a C++ compiler.
\par The second step is \ldblquote object-based\rdblquote  C++}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C++\: object-based C++}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: object-based C++}}}{. This means 
that you easily see the code organization benefits of grouping a data structure together with the functions that act upon it, the value of constructors and destructors, and perhaps some simple inheritance. Most programmers who have been working with C for
 a while quickly see the usefulness of this because, whenever they create a library, this is exactly what they try to do. With C++, you have the aid of the compiler.
\par You can get stuck at the object-based level because it\rquote s very easy to get to and you get a lot of benefit without much mental effort. It\rquote s also easy to feel like you\rquote re creating data types \endash 
 you make classes, and objects, and you send messages to those objects, and everything is nice and neat.
\par But don\rquote t be fooled. If you stop here, you\rquote re missing out on the greatest part of the language, which is the jump to true object-oriented programming. You can do this only with virtual functions.
\par Virtual functions}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: virtual functions}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {virtual\: function}}}{ enhance the concept of type rather than just encapsulating code inside structures and behind walls, so they are without a doubt the most difficult concept for the new C++ programmer to fathom. However, they\rquote 
re also the turning point in the understanding of object-oriented programming. If you don\rquote t use virtual functions, you don\rquote t understand OOP yet.
\par Because the virtual function is intimately bound with the concept of type, and type is at the core of object-oriented programming, there is no analog to the virtual function in a traditional proce
dural language. As a procedural programmer, you have no referent with which to think about virtual functions, as you do with almost every other feature in the language. Features in a procedural language can be understood on an algorithmic level, but virtu
al functions can be understood only from a design viewpoint.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593263}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628735}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374040
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776259}Upcasting{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593263}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628735}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374040}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776259}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
In the last chapter you saw how an object can be used as its own type or as an object of its base type. In addition, it can be manipulated through an address of the base type. Taking the
 address of an object (either a pointer or a reference) and treating it as the address of the base type is called }{\i upcasting}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {upcasting}}}{
 because of the way inheritance trees are drawn with the base class at the top.
\par You also saw a problem arise, which is embodied in the following code:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C15:Wind2.cpp
\par // Inheritance & upcasting
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par enum note \{ middleC, Csharp, Cflat \}; // Etc.
\par 
\par class Instrument \{
\par public:
\par   void play(note) const \{
\par     cout << "Instrument::play" << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Wind objects are Instruments
\par // because they have the same interface:
\par class Wind : public Instrument \{
\par public:
\par   // Redefine interface function:
\par   void play(note) const \{
\par     cout << "Wind::play" << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par void tune(Instrument& i) \{
\par   // ...
\par   i.play(middleC);
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Wind flute;
\par   tune(flute); // Upcasting
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The function }{\b tune(\~)}{ accepts (by reference) an }{\b Instrument}{, but also without complaint anything derived from }{\b Instrument}{. In }{\b main(\~
)}{, you can see this happening as a }{\b Wind}{ object is passed to }{\b tune(\~)}{, with no cast necessary. This is acceptable; the interface in }{\b Instrument}{ must exist in }{\b Wind}{, because }{\b Wind}{ is publicly inherited from }{\b Instrument}
{. Upcasting from }{\b Wind}{ to }{\b Instrument}{ may \ldblquote narrow\rdblquote  that interface, but it cannot make it any less than the full interface to }{\b Instrument}{.
\par The same arguments are true when dealing with pointers; the only difference is that the user must explicitly take the addresses of objects as they are passed into the function.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593264}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628736}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374041
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776260}The problem{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593264}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628736}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374041}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776260}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The problem with }{\b Wind2.cpp}{ can be seen by running the program. The output is }{\b Instrument::play}{
. This is clearly not the desired output, because you happen to know that the object is actually a }{\b Wind}{ and not just an }{\b Instrument}{. The call should resolve to }{\b Wind::play}{. For that matter, any object of a class derived from }{\b 
Instrument}{ should have its version of }{\b play}{ used, regardless of the situation.
\par However, the behavior of }{\b Wind2.cpp}{ is not surprising, given C\rquote s approach to functions. To understand the issues, you need to be aware of the concept of }{\i binding}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374042}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776261}Function call binding
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374042}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776261}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: function call binding}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {binding\: function call binding}}}{Connecting a function call to a function body is called }{\i binding}{
. When binding is performed before the program is run (by the compiler and linker), it\rquote s called }{\i early binding}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {early binding}}}{
. You may not have heard the term before because it\rquote s never been an option with procedural languages: C compilers have only one kind of function call, and that\rquote s early binding.
\par The problem in the above program is caused by early binding because the compiler cannot know the correct function to call when it has only an }{\b Instrument}{ address.
\par The solution is called }{\i late binding}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {binding\: late binding}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {late binding}}}{, which means the binding occurs at runtime, based on the type of the object. Late binding is also called }{\i dynamic binding}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {dynamic\: binding}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {binding\: dynamic binding}}}{ or }{\i runtime binding}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time binding}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {binding\: run-time binding}}}{
. When a language implements late binding, there must be some mechanism to determine the type of the object at runtime and call the appropriate member function. That is, the compiler still doesn\rquote 
t know the actual object type, but it inserts code that finds out and calls the correct funct
ion body. The late-binding mechanism varies from language to language, but you can imagine that some sort of type information must be installed in the objects themselves. You\rquote ll see how this works later.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593265}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628737}
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374043}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776262}virtual}{ functions{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593265}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628737}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374043}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776262}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To cause late binding to occur for a particular function, C++ requires that you use the }{\b virtual}{ keyword}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {virtual\: keyword}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {keyword\: virtual}}}{
 when declaring the function in the base class. Late binding occurs only with }{\b virtual}{ functions, and only when you\rquote re using an address of the base class where those }{\b virtual}{
 functions exist, although they may also be defined in an earlier base class.
\par To create a member function as }{\b virtual}{, you simply precede the declaration }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {declaration\: virtual}}}{of the function with the keyword }{\b 
virtual}{. You don\rquote t repeat it for the function definition, and you don\rquote t }{\i need}{ to repeat it in any of the derived-class function redefinitions (though it does no harm to do so). If a function is declared as }{\b virtual}{
 in the base class, it is }{\b virtual}{ in all the derived classes. The redefinition of a }{\b virtual }{function in a derived class is often called }{\i overriding}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overriding}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {virtual\: function overriding}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: virtual function overriding}}}{\i .}{
\par To get the desired behavior from }{\b Wind2.cpp}{, simply add the }{\b virtual}{ keyword in the base class before }{\b play(\~)}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C15:Wind3.cpp
\par // Late binding with virtual
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par enum note \{ middleC, Csharp, Cflat \}; // Etc.
\par 
\par class Instrument \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void play(note) const \{
\par     cout << "Instrument::play" << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Wind objects are Instruments
\par // because they have the same interface:
\par class Wind : public Instrument \{
\par public:
\par   // Redefine interface function:
\par   void play(note) const \{
\par     cout << "Wind::play" << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par void tune(Instrument& i) \{
\par   // ...
\par   i.play(middleC);
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Wind flute;
\par   tune(flute); // Upcasting
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This file is identical to }{\b Wind2.cpp}{ except for the addition of the }{\b virtual}{
 keyword, and yet the behavior is significantly different: Now the output is }{\b Wind::play}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374044}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776263}Extensibility
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374044}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776263}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {With }{\b play(\~)}{ defined as }{\b virtual}{ in the base class, you can add as many new types as you want to the system without changing the }{\b tune(\~)}
{ function. In a well-designed OOP program, most or all of your functions will follow the model of }{\b tune(\~)}{ and communicate only with the base-class interface}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {base\: base-class interface}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {interface\: base-class interface}}}{. Such a program is }{\i extensible}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {extensible program}}}{ because you can add new functionality by inheriting new data t
ypes from the common base class. The functions that manipulate the base-class interface will not need to be changed at all to accommodate the new classes.
\par Here\rquote s the instrument example with more virtual functions and a number of new classes, all of which work correctly with the old, unchanged }{\b tune(\~)}{ function:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C15:Wind4.cpp
\par // Extensibility in OOP
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par enum note \{ middleC, Csharp, Cflat \}; // Etc.
\par 
\par class Instrument \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void play(note) const \{
\par     cout << "Instrument::play" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   virtual char* what() const \{
\par     return "Instrument";
\par   \}
\par   // Assume this will modify the object:
\par   virtual void adjust(int) \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Wind : public Instrument \{
\par public:
\par   void play(note) const \{
\par     cout << "Wind::play" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   char* what() const \{ return "Wind"; \}
\par   void adjust(int) \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Percussion : public Instrument \{
\par public:
\par   void play(note) const \{
\par     cout << "Percussion::play" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   char* what() const \{ return "Percussion"; \}
\par   void adjust(int) \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Stringed : public Instrument \{
\par public:
\par   void play(note) const \{
\par     cout << "Stringed::play" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   char* what() const \{ return "Stringed"; \}
\par   void adjust(int) \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Brass : public Wind \{
\par public:
\par   void play(note) const \{
\par     cout << "Brass::play" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   char* what() const \{ return "Brass"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Woodwind : public Wind \{
\par public:
\par   void play(note) const \{
\par     cout << "Woodwind::play" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   char* what() const \{ return "Woodwind"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Identical function from before:
\par void tune(Instrument& i) \{
\par   // ...
\par   i.play(middleC);
\par \}
\par 
\par // New function:
\par void f(Instrument& i) \{ i.adjust(1); \}
\par 
\par // Upcasting during array initialization:
\par Instrument* A[] = \{
\par   new Wind,
\par   new Percussion,
\par   new Stringed,
\par   new Brass
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Wind flute;
\par   Percussion drum;
\par   Stringed violin;
\par   Brass flugelhorn;
\par   Woodwind recorder;
\par   tune(flute);
\par   tune(drum);
\par   tune(violin);
\par   tune(flugelhorn);
\par   tune(recorder);
\par   f(flugelhorn);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can see that another inheritance level has been added beneath }{\b Wind}{, but the }{\b virtual}{
 mechanism works correctly no matter how many levels there are. The }{\b adjust(\~)}{ function is }{\i not}{ redefined for }{\b Brass}{ and }{\b Woodwind}{. When this happens, the previous definition is automatically used \endash  the com
piler guarantees there\rquote s always }{\i some}{ definition for a virtual function, so you\rquote ll never end up with a call that doesn\rquote t bind to a function body. (This would spell disaster.)
\par The array }{\b A[ ]}{ contains pointers to the base class }{\b Instrument}{, so upcasting occurs during the process of array initialization. This array and the function }{\b f(\~)}{ will be used in later discussions.
\par In the call to }{\b tune(\~)}{, upcasting }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {upcasting}}}{is performed on each different type of object, yet the desired behavior always takes place. T
his can be described as \ldblquote sending a message}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {sending a message}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {message, sending}}}{ to an object and letting the object worry about what to do with it.\rdblquote  The }{\b virtual}{ function is the lens to use when you\rquote re trying to analyze a project: Where should the
 base classes occur, and how might you want to extend the program? However, even if you don\rquote t discover the proper base class interfaces and virtual functions at the initial creation of the program, you\rquote 
ll often discover them later, even much later, when you set out to extend or otherwise maintain the program. This is not an analysis or design error; it simply means you didn\rquote 
t have all the information the first time. Because of the tight class modularization in C++, it isn\rquote t a large problem when this occurs 
because changes you make in one part of a system tend not to propagate to other parts of the system as they do in C.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593266}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628738}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374045
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776264}How C++ implements late binding{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593266}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628738}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374045}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776264}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {How can late binding}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {late binding\: implementing}}}{
 happen? All the work goes on behind the scenes by the
 compiler, which installs the necessary late-binding mechanism when you ask it to (you ask by creating virtual functions). Because programmers often benefit from understanding the mechanism of virtual functions in C++, this section will elaborate on the w
ay the compiler implements this mechanism.
\par The keyword }{\b virtual}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {virtual\: and late binding}}}{
tells the compiler it should not perform early binding. Instead, it should automatically install all the mechanisms necessary to perform late binding. This means that if you call }{\b play(\~)}{ for a }{\b Brass}{ object }{\i 
through an address for the base-class }{\b Instrument}{, you\rquote ll get the proper function.
\par To accomplish this, the compiler creates a single table (called the VTABLE}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {VTABLE}}}{) for each class that contains }{\b virtual}{ func
tions. The compiler places the addresses of the virtual functions for that particular class in the VTABLE. In each class with virtual functions, it secretly places a pointer, called the }{\i vpointer}{ }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {vpointer, abbreviated as VPTR}}}{(abbreviated as VPTR}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {VPTR
}}}{), which points to the VTABLE for that object. When you make a virtual function call through a base-class pointer (that is, when you make a polymorphic call}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {polymorphism\: polymorphic function call}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: polymorphic function call}}}{), the
