\par Each time you create a class that contains virtual functions, or you derive from a class that contains virtual functions, the compiler creates a VTABLE }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {VTABLE}}}{for that class, seen on the right of the diagram. In that table it places the addresses of all the functions that are declared virtual in this class or in the base class. If you don\rquote 
t redefine a function that was declared virtual in the base class, the compiler uses the address of the base-class version in the derived class. (You can see this in the }{\b adjust}{ entry in the }{\b Brass}{ VTABLE.) Then it places the VPTR }
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {VPTR}}}{(discovered in }{\b Sizes.cpp}{
) into the class. There is only one VPTR for each object when using simple inheritance like this. The VPTR must be initialized to point to the starting address of the appropriate VTABLE. (This happens in the constructor, which you\rquote 
ll see later in more detail.)
\par Once the VPTR is initialized to the proper VTABLE, the object in effect \ldblquote knows\rdblquote  what type it is. But this self-knowledge is worthless unless it is used at the point a virtual function is called.
