\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In }{\b b1\endash >f(\~)}{ and }{\b b2.f(\~)}{ addresses are used, which means the information is incomplete: }{\b b1 }{and }{\b b2 }{
can represent the address of a }{\b Base}{ }{\i or}{ something derived from }{\b Base}{, so the virtual mechanism must be used. When calling }{\b b3.f(\~)}{ there\rquote s no ambiguity. The compiler knows the exact type and that it\rquote 
s an object, so it can\rquote t possibly be an object derived from }{\b Base}{ \endash  it\rquote s }{\i exactly}{ a }{\b Base}{. Thus early binding is probably used. However, if the compiler doesn\rquote 
t want to work so hard, it can still use late binding and the same behavior will occur.
