 why is it an option? Why do I even need to know about it?\rdblquote 
\par This is a good question, and the answer is part of the fundamental philosophy of C++: \ldblquote Because it\rquote s not quite as efficient.\rdblquote }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {virtual\: and efficiency}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {efficiency\: and virtual functions}}}{ Y
ou can see from the previous assembly-language output that instead of one simple CALL to an absolute address, there are two more sophisticated assembly instructions required to set up the virtual function call. This requires both code space and execution 
time.
\par Some object-oriented languages have taken the approach that late binding is so intrinsic to object-oriented programming that it should always take place, that it should not be an option, and the user shouldn\rquote 
t have to know about it. This is a design decision when creating a language, and that particular path is appropriate for many languages.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{Smalltalk, for instance, uses this approach with great success.}}}{
 However, C++ comes from the C heritage, where efficiency is critical. After all, C was created to replace assembly language for the implementation of an operating system (thereby rendering that operating system \endash  Unix \endash 
 far more portable than its predecessors). One of the main reasons for the invention of C++ was to make C programmers more efficient.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{At Bell labs, where C++ was invented, there are a }{\i lot}{ of C programmers. Making them all more efficient, even just a bit, saves the company many millions.}}}{
 And the first question asked when C programmers encounter C++ is \ldblquote What kind of size and speed impact will I get?\rdblquote  If the answer were, \ldblquote Everything\rquote s great except for function calls when you\rquote 
ll always have a little extra overhead,\rdblquote  many people would stick with C rather than make the change to C++. In addition, inline functions }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
inline\: functions}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: inline }}}{
would not be possible, because virtual functions must have an address to put into the VTABLE. So the virtual function is an option, }{\i and}{ the language defaults to nonvirtual, which is the fastest config
uration. Stroustrup stated that his guideline was \ldblquote If you don\rquote t use it, you don\rquote t pay for it.\rdblquote 
\par Thus the }{\b virtual }{keyword is provided for efficiency tuning. When designing your classes, however, you shouldn\rquote t be worrying about efficiency tuning. If you\rquote re go
ing to use polymorphism, use virtual functions everywhere. You only need to look for functions to make non-virtual when looking for ways to speed up your code (and there are usually much bigger gains to be had in other areas).
\par Anecdotal evidence suggests t
hat the size and speed impacts of going to C++ are within 10% of the size and speed of C, and often much closer to the same. The reason you might get better size and speed efficiency is because you may design a C++ program in a smaller, faster way than yo
u would using C.
