\par If you have a genuine abstract class (like }{\b Instrument}{), objects of that class almost always have no meaning. That is, }{\b Instrument}{ is meant to express only the interface, and not a particular implementation, so creating an }{\b Instrument}{
 object makes no sense, and you\rquote ll probably want to prevent the user from doing it. This can be accomplished by making all the virtual functions in }{\b Instrument}{
 print error messages, but this delays the information until runtime and requires reliable exhaustive testing on the part of the user. It is much better to catch the problem at compile time.
\par C++ provides a mechanism for doing this called the }{\i pure virtual function}{. Here is the syntax used for a declaration:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {virtual void X() = 0;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {By doing this, you tell the compiler to reserve a slot for a function in the VTABLE}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {VTABLE}}}{, but not to put an address in that particular slot. If only one function in a class is declared as pure virtual, the VTABLE is incomplete. A class containing pure virtual functions is called a }{\i 
pure abstract base class}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {pure\: abstract base classes and pure virtual functions}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {abstract\: pure abstract base class}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {base\: pure abstract base class}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\: pure abstract base class}}}{.
\par If the VTABLE for a class is inc
omplete, what is the compiler supposed to do when someone tries to make an object of that class? It cannot safely create an object of a pure abstract class, so you get an error message from the compiler if you try to make an object of a pure abstract clas
s. Thus, the compiler ensures the purity of the abstract class, and you don\rquote t have to worry about misusing it.
\par Here\rquote s }{\b Wind4.cpp}{ modified to use pure virtual functions:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C15:Wind5.cpp
