\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {VTABLE\: inheritance and the VTABLE}}}{You can imagine what happens when you perform inh
eritance and redefine some of the virtual functions. The compiler creates a new VTABLE for your new class, and it inserts your new function addresses, using the base-class function addresses for any virtual functions you don\rquote 
t redefine. One way or another, there\rquote s always a full set of function addresses in the VTABLE, so you\rquote ll never be able to make a call to an address that isn\rquote t there (which would be disastrous).
\par But what happens when you inherit and add new virtual functions in the }{\i derived}{ class}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {adding new virtual functions in the derived class}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {virtual\: adding new virtual functions in the derived class}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: adding new virtual functions in the derived class}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {derived\: adding new virtual functions in the derived class}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\: adding new virtual functions in the derived class}}}{? Here\rquote s a simple example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C15:Addv.cpp
