\par Notice the compiler maps the location of the }{\b value}{ address into exactly the same spot in the }{\b Derived }{VTABLE as it is in the }{\b Base}{ VTABLE. Similarly, if a class is inherited from }{\b Derived}{, its version of }{\b shift}{
 would be placed in its VTABLE in exactly the same spot as it is in }{\b Derived}{. This is because (as you saw with the assembly-language example) the compiler generates code that uses a simple numerical offset into the VTABLE to select t
he virtual function. Regardless of what specific subtype the object belongs to, its VTABLE is laid out the same way, so calls to the virtual functions will always be made the same way.
\par In this case, however, the compiler is working only with a pointer to a base-class object. The base class has only the }{\b value(\~)}{
 function, so that is the only function the compiler will allow you to call. How could it possibly know that you are working with a }{\b Derived}{ object, if it has only a pointer to a base-class object? Tha
t pointer might point to some other type, which doesn\rquote t have a }{\b shift}{
 function. It may or may not have some other function address at that point in the VTABLE, but in either case, making a virtual call to that VTABLE address is not what you want to do. So it\rquote 
s fortunate and logical that the compiler protects you from making virtual calls to functions that exist only in derived classes.
\par There are some less-common cases where you may know that the pointer actually points to an object of a specific subclass. If
 you want to call a function that only exists in that subclass, then you must cast the pointer. You can remove the error message produced by the previous program like this:
