\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here, you happen to know that }{\b B[1]}{ points to a }{\b Derived}{ object, but generally you don\rquote 
t know that. If your problem is set up so that you must know the exact types of all objects, you should rethink it, because you\rquote re probably not using virtual functions properly. However, there are some situations where the d
esign works best (or you have no choice) if you know the exact type of all objects kept in a generic container. This is the problem of }{\i run-time type identification}{ (RTTI)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type\: run-time type identification (RTTI)}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {RTTI\: run-time type identification (RTTI)}}}{\b .}{
\par Run-time type identification is all about casting base-class pointers }{\i down}{ to derived-class pointers (\ldblquote up\rdblquote  and \ldblquote down\rdblquote  are relative to a typical class diagram, with the base class at the top). Casting }{\i up}
{ happens automatically, with no coercion, because it\rquote s completely safe. Casting }{\i down}{ is unsafe because there\rquote 
s no compile time information about the actual types, so you must know exactly what type the object really is. If you cast it into the wrong type, you\rquote ll be in trouble.
\par Chapter XX describes the way C++ provides run-time type information.
