\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {slicing\: object slicing}}}{There is a distinct difference between passing addresses and passing values when treating objects polymorphically. All the examples you
\rquote ve seen here, and virtually all the examples you should see, pass addresses and not values. This is because addresses all have the same size,}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{Actually, not all pointers are the same size o
n all machines. In the context of this discussion, however, they can be considered to be the same.}}}{ so passing the address of an object of a derived type (which is usually bigger) is
 the same as passing the address of an object of the base type (which is usually smaller). As explained before, this is the goal when using polymorphism \endash 
 code that manipulates objects of a base type can transparently manipulate derived-type objects as well.
\par If you use an object instead of a pointer or reference as the recipient of your upcast, something will happen that may surprise you: the object is \ldblquote sliced\rdblquote 
 until all that remains is the subobject that corresponds to your destination. In the following example you can see what\rquote s left after slicing by examining the size of the objects:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C15:Slice.cpp
\par // Object slicing
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Base \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   Base(int ii = 0) : i(ii) \{\}
\par   virtual int sum() const \{ return i; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Derived : public Base \{
\par   int j;
\par public:
\par   Derived(int ii = 0, int jj = 0)
\par     : Base(ii), j(jj) \{\}
\par   int sum() const \{ return Base::sum() + j; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par void call(Base b) \{
\par   cout << "sum = " << b.sum() << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Base b(10);
\par   Derived d(10, 47);
\par   call(b);
\par   call(d);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The function }{\b call(\~)}{ is passed an object of type }{\b Base}{ }{\i by value}{. It then calls the virtual function }{\b sum(\~)}{ for the }{\b Base}{
 object. In }{\b main(\~)}{, you might expect the first call to produce 10, and the second to produce 57. In fact, both calls produce 10.
\par Two things are happening in this program. First, }{\b call(\~) }{accepts only a }{\b Base}{ object, so all the code inside the function body will manipulate only members associated with }{\b Base}{. Any calls to }{\b call(\~)}{ will cause an obj
ect the size of }{\b Base}{ to be pushed on the stack and cleaned up after the call. This means that if an object of a class inherited from }{\b Base}{ is passed to }{\b call(\~)}{, the compiler accepts it, but it copies only the }{\b Base}{
 portion of the object. It }{\i slices}{ the derived portion off of the object, like this:
