\par Now you may wonder about the virtual function call. Here, the virtual function makes use of portions of both }{\b Base}{ (which still exists) and }{\b Derived}{, which no longer exists because it was slice
d off! So what happens when the virtual function is called?
\par You\rquote re saved from disaster precisely because the object is being passed by value. Because of this, the compiler thinks it knows the precise type of the object (and it does, here, because any infor
mation that contributed extra features to the objects has been lost). In addition, when passing by value,}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {passing\: by value}}}{
 it uses the copy-constructor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {copy-constructor}}}{ for a }{\b Base }{object, which initializes the VPTR to the }{\b Base}{ VTABLE and copies only the 
}{\b Base}{ parts of the object. There\rquote s no explicit copy-constructor here, so the compiler synthesizes one. Under all interpretations, the object truly becomes a }{\b Base}{ during slicing.
\par Object slicing actually removes part of the object rather than simply changing the meaning of an address as when using a pointer or reference. Because of this, upcasting into an object is not often done; in fact, it\rquote 
s usually something to watch out for and prevent. You can explicitly prevent object slicing by putting pure virtual functions in the base class; this will cause a compile-time error message for an object slice.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593270}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628742}
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374055}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776274}virtual}{ functions & constructors{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593270}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628742}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374055}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776274}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {virtual\: virtual functions inside constructors}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: virtual functions & constructors}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe 
{constructor\: virtual functions & constructors}}}{When an object containing virtual functions is created, its VPTR }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {VPTR}}}{
must be initialized to point to the proper VTABLE}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {VTABLE}}}{. This must be done before there\rquote 
s any possibility of calling a virtual function. As you might guess, because the constructor has the job of bringing an object into existence, it is also the constructor\rquote 
s job to set up the VPTR. The compiler secretly inserts code into the beginning of the constructor that initializes the VPTR. In fact, even if you don\rquote 
t explicitly create a constructor for a class, the compiler will create one for you with the proper VPTR initialization code (if you have virtual functions). This has several implications.
\par The first concerns efficiency. The reason for }{\b inline}{ functions is to reduce the calling overhead for small functions. If C++ didn\rquote t provide }{\b inline}{ functions, the preprocessor might be used to create these \ldblquote macros.\rdblquote 
 However, the preprocessor has no concept of access or classes, and therefore couldn\rquote t be used to create member function macros. In addition, with constructors that must have hidden code inserted by the compiler, a preprocessor macro wouldn\rquote 
t work at all.
\par You must be aware when hunting for efficiency}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {efficiency\: constructor}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: efficiency}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inline\: constructor efficiency}}}{
 holes that the compiler is inserting hidden code into your constructor function. Not only must it initialize the VPTR, it must also check the value of }{\b this}{ (in case the }{\b operator}{ }{\b new}{ returns zero) and call bas
e-class constructors. Taken together, this code can impact what you thought was a tiny inline function call. In particular, the size of the constructor can overwhelm the savings you get from reduced function-call overhead. If you make a lot of inline cons
tructor calls, your code size can grow without any benefits in speed.
\par Of course, you probably won\rquote t make all tiny constructors non-inline right away, because they\rquote re much easier to write as inlines. But when you\rquote re tuning your code, remember to remove inline constructors.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374056}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776275}Order of constructor calls
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374056}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776275}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {order\: of constructor calls}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: order of constructor calls}}}{
The second interesting facet of constructors and virtual functions concerns the order of constructor calls and the way virtual calls are made within constructors.
\par All base-class constructors are always called in the constructor for an inherited class. This makes sense because the constructor has a special job: to see that the object is built properly. A derived class has access only to 
its own members, and not those of the base class; only the base-class constructor can properly initialize its own elements. Therefore it\rquote s essential that all constructors get called; otherwise the entire object wouldn\rquote 
t be constructed properly. That\rquote s why the compiler enforces a constructor call for every portion of a derived class. It will call the default constructor if you don\rquote 
t explicitly call a base-class constructor in the constructor initializer list. If there is no default constructor, the compiler will complain. (In this example, }{\b class X}{
 has no constructors so the compiler can automatically make a default constructor.)
\par The order of the constructor calls is important. When you inherit, you know all about the base class and can access any }{\b public}{ and }{\b protected}{
 members of the base class. This means you must be able to assume that all the members of the base class are valid when you\rquote re in the derived class. In a normal member function, construction has already taken place, so all the members of all parts 
of the object have been built. }{\caps i}{nside the constructor, however, you must be able to assume that all members that you use have been built. The only way to guarantee this is for the base-class constructor to be called first. Then when you\rquote 
re in the derived-class constructor, all the members you can access in the base class have been initialized. \ldblquote Knowing all members are valid\rdblquote 
 inside the constructor is also the reason that, whenever possible, you should initialize all member objects (that is, objects placed i
n the class using composition) in the constructor initializer list. If you follow this practice, you can assume that all base class members }{\i and}{ member objects of the current object have been initialized.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374057}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776276}
Behavior of virtual functions inside constructors{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374057}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776276}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {virtual\:
 behavior of virtual functions inside constructors}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: behavior of virtual functions inside constructors}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: behavior of virtual functions inside constructors}}}{The hierarchy of constructor calls brings up an interesting dilemma. What happens if you\rquote 
re inside a constructor and you call a virtual function? Inside an ordinary member function you can imagine what will happen \endash  the virtual call is resolved at runtime because the object cannot know whether it belongs to the c
lass the member function is in, or some class derived from it. For consistency, you might think this is what should happen inside constructors.
\par This is not the case. If you call a virtual function inside a constructor, only the local version of the function is used. That is, the virtual mechanism doesn\rquote t work within the constructor.
\par This behavior makes sense for two reasons. Conceptually, the constructor\rquote s job is to bring the object into existence (which is hardly an ordinary feat). Inside any constructor, the object may only be partially formed \endash 
 you can only know that the base-class objects have been initialized, but you cannot know which classes are inherited from you. A virtual function call, however, reaches \ldblquote forward\rdblquote  or \ldblquote outward\rdblquote  into the inheritance h
ierarchy. It calls a function in a derived class. If you could do this inside a constructor, you\rquote d be calling a function that might manipulate members that hadn\rquote t been initialized yet, a sure recipe for disaster.
\par The second reason is a mechanical one. When a constructor is called, one of the first things it does is initialize its VPTR}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {VPTR}}}{
. However, it can only know that it is of the \ldblquote current\rdblquote  type. The constructor code is completely ignorant of whether or not the object is in the base of another clas
s. When the compiler generates code for that constructor, it generates code for a constructor of that class, not a base class and not a class derived from it (because a class can\rquote t know who inherits it). So the VPTR it uses must be for the VTABLE}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {VTABLE}}}{ of that class. The VPTR remains initialized to that VTABLE for the rest of the object\rquote s lifetime }{\i unless}{ this isn\rquote 
t the last constructor call. If a more-derived constructor is called afterwards, that constructor sets the VPTR to }{\i its}{ VTABLE, and so on, u
ntil the last constructor finishes. The state of the VPTR is determined by the constructor that is called last. This is another reason why the constructors are called in order from base to most-derived.
\par But while all this series of constructor calls is tak
ing place, each constructor has set the VPTR to its own VTABLE. If it uses the virtual mechanism for function calls, it will produce only a call through its own VTABLE, not the most-derived VTABLE (as would be the case after }{\i all}{
 the constructors were calle
d). In addition, many compilers recognize that a virtual function call is being made inside a constructor, and perform early binding because they know that late-binding will produce a call only to the local function. In either event, you won\rquote 
t get the results you might expect from a virtual function call inside a constructor.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593271}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628743}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374058
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776277}Destructors and }{\b virtual}{ destructors{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593271}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628743}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374058}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776277}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {virtual\: destructors and virtual destructors}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {destructor\: destructors and virtual destructors}}}{Constructors cannot be made explicitly virt
ual (and the technique in Appendix B only simulates virtual constructors), but destructors can and often must be virtual.
\par The constructor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor}}}{ has the special job of putting an object together piece-by-piece, first by calling the base construc
tor, then the more derived constructors in order of inheritance. Similarly, the destructor also has a special job \endash 
 it must disassemble an object that may belong to a hierarchy of classes. To do this, the compiler generates code that calls all the destructors, but in the }{\i reverse}{
 order that they are called by the constructor. That is, the destructor starts at the most-derived class and works its way down to the base class. This is the safe and desirable thing to do: The current destructor always knows that t
he base-class members are alive and active because it knows what it is derived from. Thus, the destructor can perform its own cleanup, then call the next-down destructor, which will perform }{\i its}{ own cleanup}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cleanup}}}{, knowing what it is derived from, but not what is derived from it.
\par You should keep in mind that constructors and destructors are the only places where this hierarchy of calls must happen (and thus the proper hierarchy is automatically generated by the compiler). In all other functions, only }{\i that}{
 function will be called, whether it\rquote s virtual or not. The only way for base-class versions of the same function to be called in ordinary functions (virtual or not) is if you }{\i explicitly}{ call that function.
\par Normally, the action of the destructor is quit
e adequate. But what happens if you want to manipulate an object through a pointer to its base class (that is, manipulate the object through its generic interface)? This is certainly a major objective in object-oriented programming. The problem occurs whe
n you want to }{\b delete}{ a pointer of this type for an object that has been created on the heap with }{\b new}{. If the pointer is to the base class, the compiler can only know to call the base-class version of the destructor during }{\b delete}{
. Sound familiar? This is the same problem that virtual functions were created to solve for the general case. Fortunately virtual functions work for destructors as they do for all other functions except constructors.
\par Even though the destructor, like the constructor, is an \ldblquote exceptional\rdblquote  function, it is possible for the destructor to be virtual because the object already knows what type it is (whereas it doesn\rquote 
t during construction). Once an object has been constructed, its VPTR is initialized, so virtual function calls can take place.
\par F
or a time, pure virtual destructors were legal and worked if you combined them with a function body, but in the final C++ standard function bodies combined with pure virtual functions were outlawed. This means that a virtual destructor cannot be pure, and
 must have a function body because (unlike ordinary functions) all destructors in a class hierarchy are always called. Here\rquote s an example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C15:Pvdest.cpp
\par // Pure virtual destructors
\par // require a function body.
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Base \{
\par public:
\par   virtual ~Base() \{
\par     cout << "~Base()" << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Derived : public Base \{
\par public:
\par   ~Derived() \{
\par     cout << "~Derived()" << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Base* bp = new Derived; // Upcast
\par   delete bp; // Virtual destructor call
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
As a guideline, any time you have a virtual function in a class, you should immediately add a virtual destructor (even if it does nothing). This way, you ensure against any surprises later.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374059}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776278}Virtuals in destructors
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374059}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776278}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {virtual\: virtual function calls in destructors}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {destructor\: virtual function calls in destructors}}}{There\rquote 
s something that happens during destruction that you might not immediately expect. If you\rquote re inside an ordinary member function and you call a virtual function, that fu
nction is called using the late-binding mechanism. This is not true with destructors, virtual or not. Inside a destructor, only the \ldblquote local\rdblquote  version of the member function is called; the virtual mechanism is ignored.
\par Why is this? Suppose the virtual mechanism }{\i were}{ used inside the destructor. Then it would be possible for the virtual call to resolve to a function that was \ldblquote further out\rdblquote 
 (more derived) on the inheritance hierarchy than the current destructor. But destructors are called from the \ldblquote outside in\rdblquote  (fr
om the most-derived destructor down to the base destructor), so the actual function called would rely on portions of an object that has }{\i already been destroyed}{! Thus, the compiler resolves the calls at compile-time and calls only the \ldblquote 
local\rdblquote  version of the function. Notice that the same is true for the constructor (as described earlier), but in the constructor\rquote s case the information wasn\rquote 
t available, whereas in the destructor the information (that is, the VPTR) is there, but is isn\rquote t reliable.
