\ls209\adjustright \fs20\lang1024\cgrid {Create a very simple \ldblquote shape\rdblquote  hierarchy}{\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls209\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls209\adjustright \v\fs20\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {
shape\: hierarchy}}}{: a base class called }{\b Shape}{ and derived classes called }{\b Circle}{, }{\b Square}{, and }{\b Triangle}{. In the base class, make a virtual function called }{\b draw(\~),}{
 and redefine this in the derived classes. Create an array of pointers to }{\b Shape}{ objects you create on the heap (and thus perform upcasting of the pointers), and call }{\b draw(\~)}{
 through the base-class pointers, to verify the behavior of the virtual function. If your debugger supports it, single-step through the example.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 2. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls209\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls209\adjustright {Modify Exercise 1 so }{\b draw(\~)}{ is a pure virtual function. Try creating an object of type }{\b Shape}{.}{\b  }{Try to call the pure virtual function inside the constructor and see what happens. Give }{\b draw(\~)}{ a definition.

