\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iterator\: and containers}}}{
Suppose you want to create a stack. In C, you would make a data structure and associated functions, but of course in C++ you package the two together into an abstract data type. This }{\b stack}{ class will hold }{\b int}{s, to keep it simple:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C16:IStack.cpp
\par // Simple integer stack
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class IStack \{
\par   enum \{ ssize = 100 \};
\par   int stack[ssize];
\par   int top;
\par public:
\par   IStack() : top(0) \{ stack[top] = 0; \}
\par   void push(int i) \{
\par     if(top < ssize) stack[top++] = i;
\par   \}
\par   int pop() \{
\par     return stack[top > 0 ? --top : top];
\par   \}
\par   friend class IStackIter;
\par \};
\par 
\par // An iterator is a "super-pointer":
\par class IStackIter \{
\par   IStack& S;
\par   int index;
\par public:
\par   IStackIter(IStack& is)
\par     : S(is), index(0) \{\}
\par   int operator++() \{ // Prefix form
\par     if (index < S.top - 1) index++;
\par     return S.stack[index];
\par   \}
\par   int operator++(int) \{ // Postfix form
\par     int returnval = S.stack[index];
\par     if (index < S.top - 1) index++;
\par     return returnval;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par // For interest, generate Fibonacci numbers:
\par int fibonacci(int n) \{
\par   const int sz = 100;
\par   require(n < sz);
\par   static int f[sz]; // Initialized to zero
\par   f[0] = f[1] = 1;
\par   // Scan for unfilled array elements:
\par   int i;
\par   for(i = 0; i < sz; i++)
\par     if(f[i] == 0) break;
\par   while(i <= n) \{
\par     f[i] = f[i-1] + f[i-2];
\par     i++;
\par   \}
\par   return f[n];
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   IStack is;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 20; i++)
\par     is.push(fibonacci(i));
\par   // Traverse with an iterator:
\par   IStackIter it(is);
\par   for(int j = 0; j < 20; j++)
\par     cout << it++ << endl;
\par   for(int k = 0; k < 20; k++)
\par     cout << is.pop() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The class }{\b IStack}{
 is an almost trivial example of a push-down stack. For simplicity it has been created here with a fixed size, but you can also modify it to automatically expand by allocating memory off the heap. (This will be demonstrated in later examples.)
\par The second class, }{\b IStackIter}{, is an example of an }{\i iterator}{, which you can think of as a superpointer that has been customized to work only with an }{\b IStack}{. Notice that }{\b IStackIter}{ is a }{\b friend}{ of }{\b IStack}{
, which gives it access to all the }{\b private}{ elements of }{\b IStack}{.
\par Like a pointer, }{\b IStackIter}{\rquote s job is to move through an }{\b IStack}{ and retrieve values. In this simple example, the }{\b IStackIter}{ can move only forward (using both the pre- and postfix forms of the }{\b operator++}{
) and it can fetch only values. However, there is no boundary to the way an iterator can be defined. It is perfectly acceptable for an iterator to move around any way within its associated container and to cause the contained values to 
be modified. However, it is customary that an iterator is created with a constructor that attaches it to a single container object and that it is not reattached during its lifetime. (Most iterators are small, so you can easily make another one.)
\par To make the example more interesting, the }{\b fibonacci(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {fibonacci(\~)}}}{
 function generates the traditional rabbit-reproduction numbers. This is a fairly efficient implementation, because it never generates the numbers more than once. (Although if you\rquote ve been out of school awhile, you\rquote 
ve probably figured out that you don\rquote t spend your days researching more efficient implementations of algorithms, as textbooks might lead you to believe.)
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{ you can see the creation and use of the stack and its associated iterator. Once you have the classes built, they\rquote re quite simple to use.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374064}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776283}The need for containers
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374064}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776283}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Obviously an integer stack isn\rquote t a crucial tool. The real need for containers comes when you start making objects on the heap using }{\b new}{
 and destroying them with }{\b delete}{. In the general programming problem, you don\rquote t know how many objects you\rquote re going to need while you\rquote re writing the program. For example, in an air-traffic control system you don\rquote 
t want to limit the number of planes your system can handle. You don\rquote t want the program to abort just because you exceed some number. In a computer-aided design system, you\rquote 
re dealing with lots of shapes, but only the user determines (at runtime) exactly how many shapes you\rquote re going to need. Once you notice this tendency, you\rquote ll discover lots of examples in your own programming situations.
\par C programmers who rely on virtual memory to handle their \ldblquote memory management\rdblquote  often find the idea of }{\b new}{, }{\b delete,}{ and container classes disturbing. Apparently, one practice in C is to create a hu
ge global array, larger than anything the program would appear to need. This may not require much thought (or awareness of }{\b malloc(\~)}{ and }{\b free(\~)}{), but it produces programs that don\rquote t port well and can hide subtle bugs.
\par In addition, if you create a huge global array of objects in C++, the constructor and destructor overhead can slow things down significantly. The C++ approach works much better: When you need an object, create it with }{\b new,}{
 and put its pointer in a container. Later on, fish it out and do something to it. This way, you create only the objects you absolutely need. And generally you don\rquote 
t have all the initialization conditions at the start-up of the program; you have to wait until something happens in the environment before you can actually create the object.
\par So in the most common situation, you\rquote ll create a container that holds pointers to some objects of interest. You will create those objects using }{\b new}{
 and put the resulting pointer in the container (potentially upcasting it in the process), fishing it out later when you want to do something with the object. This technique produces the most flexible, general sort of program.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593276}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628748}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374065
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776284}Overview of templates{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593276}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628748}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374065}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776284}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now a problem arises. You have an }{\b IStack}{, which holds integers. But you want a stack that holds shapes 
or airliners or plants or something else. Reinventing your source-code every time doesn\rquote t seem like a very intelligent approach with a language that touts reusability. There must be a better way.
\par There are three techniques for source-code reuse: the C way, presented here for contrast; the Smalltalk approach, which significantly affected C++; and the C++ approach: templates.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374066}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776285}The C approach
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374066}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776285}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Of course you\rquote re trying to get away from the C approach because it\rquote s messy and error prone and completely inelegant. You
 copy the source code for a }{\b Stack}{ and make modifications by hand, introducing new errors in the process. This is certainly not a very productive technique.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374067}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776286}The Smalltalk approach
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374067}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776286}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Smalltalk }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Smalltalk}}}{
took a simple and straightforward approach: You want to reuse code, so use inheritance. To implement this, each container class holds items of the generic base class }{\b object}{
. But, as mentioned before, the library in Smalltalk is of fundamental importance, so fundamental, in fact, that you don\rquote t ever create 
a class from scratch. Instead, you must always inherit it from an existing class. You find a class as close as possible to the one you want, inherit from it, and make a few changes. Obviously this is a benefit because it minimizes your effort (and explain
s why you spend a lot of time learning the class library before becoming an effective Smalltalk programmer).
\par But it also means that all classes in Smalltalk end up being part of a single inheritance tree. You must inherit from a branch of this tree when creating a new class. Most of the tree is already there (it\rquote 
s the Smalltalk class library), and at the root of the tree is a class called }{\b object}{ \endash  the same class that each Smalltalk container holds.
\par This is a neat trick because it means that every class in the Smalltalk class hierarchy is derived from }{\b object}{
, so every class can be held in every container, including that container itself. This type of single-tree hierarchy based on a fundamental generic type (often named }{\b object}{) is referred to as an \ldblquote object-based hierarchy}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: object-based hierarchy}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {hierarchy\:
 object-based hierarchy}}}{.\rdblquote  You may have heard this term before and assumed it was some new fundamental concept in OOP, like polymorphism. It just means a class tree with }{\b object}{ (or some simil
ar name) at its root and container classes that hold }{\b object}{.
\par Because the Smalltalk class library had a much longer history and experience behind it than C++, and the original C++ compilers had }{\i no}{ container class libraries, it seemed like a good idea to dup
licate the Smalltalk library in C++. This was done as an experiment with a very early C++ implementation,}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ The OOPS library, by Keith Gorlen while he was at NIH. Generally available from public sources.}}}{
 and because it represented a significant body of code, many people began using it. In the process of trying to use the container classes, they discovered a problem.
\par The problem was that in Smalltalk, you could force people to derive everything from a single hierarchy, but in C++ you can\rquote t. You might have your nice object-based hierarchy with its container classes, but then you might buy a set of shape 
classes or airline classes from another vendor who didn\rquote 
t use that hierarchy. (For one thing, the hierarchy imposes overhead, which C programmers eschew.) How do you shoehorn a separate class tree into the container class in your object-based hierarchy? Here\rquote s what the problem looks like:
