\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Now }{\b oshape}{ has }{\b shape}{\rquote s characteristics and behaviors, but because it is also derived from }{\b object}{ it can be placed in }{\b container}{.
\par But multiple inheritance wasn\rquote t originally part of C++. Once the container problem was seen, there came a great deal of pressure to add the feature. Other programmers felt (and still feel) multiple inheritance wasn\rquote t a good idea and that
 it adds unneeded complexity to the language. An oft-repeated statement at that time was, \ldblquote C++ is not Smalltalk,\rdblquote  which, if you knew enough to translate it, meant \ldblquote Don\rquote 
t use object based hierarchies for container classes.\rdblquote  But in the end}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {
\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{  We\rquote ll probably never know the full story because control of the language was still within AT&T at the time.}}}{ the pressure pers
isted, and multiple inheritance was added to the language. Compiler vendors followed suit by including object-based container-class hierarchies, most of which have since been replaced by template versions. You can argue that multiple inheritance is needed
 for solving general programming problems, but you\rquote ll see in the next chapter that its complexity is best avoided except in certain cases.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374068}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776287}The template approach
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374068}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776287}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Although an object-based hierarchy with multiple inheritance is conceptually straightforward, it turns out to be painful to use. In his original book}{
\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ }{\i The C++ Programming Language}{ by Bjarne Stroustrup}
{\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {Stroustrup, Bjarne}}}{ (1st edition, Addison-Wesley, 1986).}}}{
 Stroustrup demonstrated what he considered a preferable alternative to the object-based hierarchy. Container classes were created as large preprocessor macros}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {template\: preprocessor macros for parameterized types, instead of templates}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {macro\:
 preprocessor macros for parameterized types, instead of templates}}}{ with arguments that could be substituted for your desired type. When you wanted to create a container to hold a particular type, you made a couple of macro calls.
\par Unfortunately, this approach was confused by all the existing Smalltalk literature, and it was a bit unwieldy. Basically, nobody got it.
\par In the meantime, Stroustrup and the C++ team at Bell Labs had modified his original macro approach, simplifying it and moving it from the domain of the preprocessor into the compiler itself. This new code-substitution device is called a }{\b template}{
\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ The inspiration for templates appears to be ADA generics.}}}{
, and it represents a completely different way to reuse code: Instead of reusing object code, as with inheritance and composition, a template reuses }{\i source code}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {reuse\: source code reuse with templates}}}{. The container no longer holds a generic base class called }{\b object}{, but instead an unspecified parameter. When you use a template, the parameter is substituted }{\i by the compiler}{
, much like the old macro approach, but cleaner and easier to use.
\par Now, instead of worrying about inheritance or composition when you want to use a container class, you take the template version of the container and stamp out a specific version for your particular problem, like this:
