\par   Array<float> fa;
\par   fa[0] = 1.414;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that in the member function definition the class name is now qualified with the template argument type: }{\b Array<T>}{
. You can imagine that the compiler does indeed carry both the name and the argument type(s) in some decorated form.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Header files
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {header\: header files and templates}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {template\: and header files}}}{Even if you create non-inline function definitions, you\rquote ll generally want to put all declarations }{\i and}{
 definitions for a template in a header file. This may seem to violate the normal header file rule of \ldblquote Don\rquote t put in anything that allocates storage\rdblquote 
 to prevent multiple definition errors at link time, but template definitions are special. Anything preceded by }{\b template<...>}{ means the compiler won\rquote t allocate storage for it at that point, but will instead wait until it\rquote 
s told to (by a template instantiation), and that somewhere in the compiler and linker there\rquote s a mechanism for removing multiple definitions}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
template\: and multiple definitions}}}{ of an identical template. So you\rquote ll almost always put the entire template declaration }{\i and}{ definition in the header file, for ease of use.
\par There are times when you may need to place the template definitions in a separate }{\b cpp}{ file to satisfy special needs (for example, forcing template instantiations to exist in only a single Windows }{\b dll}{
 file). Most compilers have some mechanism to allow this; you\rquote ll have to investigate your particular compiler\rquote s documentation to use it.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374071}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776290}The stack as a template
