\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here is the container and iterator from }{\b Istack.cpp}{, implemented as a generic container class using templates:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C16:Stackt.h
\par // Simple stack template
\par #ifndef STACKT_H
\par #define STACKT_H
\par template<class T> class StacktIter; // Declare
\par 
\par template<class T>
\par class Stackt \{
\par   static const int ssize = 100;
\par   T stack[ssize];
\par   int top;
\par public:
\par   Stackt() : top(0) \{ stack[top] = 0; \}
\par   void push(const T& i) \{
\par     if(top < ssize) stack[top++] = i;
\par   \}
\par   T pop() \{
\par     return stack[top > 0 ? --top : top];
\par   \}
\par   friend class StacktIter<T>;
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class T>
\par class StacktIter \{
\par   Stackt<T>& s;
\par   int index;
\par public:
\par   StacktIter(Stackt<T>& is)
\par     : s(is), index(0) \{\}
\par   T& operator++() \{ // Prefix form
\par     if (index < s.top - 1) index++;
\par     return s.stack[index];
\par   \}
\par   T& operator++(int) \{ // Postfix form
\par     int returnIndex = index;
\par     if (index < s.top - 1) index++;
\par     return s.stack[returnIndex];
\par   \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // STACKT_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that anywhere a template\rquote s class name is referred to, it must be accompanied by its template argument list}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {template\: argument list}}}{, as in }{\b Stackt<T>& s}{
. You can imagine that internally, the arguments in the template argument list are also being decorated to produce a unique class name for each template instantiation.
\par Also notice that a template makes certain assumptions about the objects it is holding. For example, }{\b Stackt}{ assumes there is some sort of assignment operation for }{\b T}{ inside the }{\b push(\~)}{ function. You could say that a template 
\ldblquote implies an interface\rdblquote  for the types it is capable of holding.
\par Here\rquote s the revised example to test the template:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C16:Stackt.cpp
\par // Test simple stack template
\par #include "Stackt.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par // For interest, generate Fibonacci numbers:
\par int fibonacci(int n) \{
\par   const int sz = 100;
\par   require(n < sz);
\par   static int f[sz]; // Initialized to zero
\par   f[0] = f[1] = 1;
\par   // Scan for unfilled array elements:
\par   int i;
\par   for(i = 0; i < sz; i++)
\par     if(f[i] == 0) break;
\par   while(i <= n) \{
\par     f[i] = f[i-1] + f[i-2];
\par     i++;
\par   \}
\par   return f[n];
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Stackt<int> is;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 20; i++)
\par     is.push(fibonacci(i));
\par   // Traverse with an iterator:
\par   StacktIter<int> it(is);
\par   for(int j = 0; j < 20; j++)
\par     cout << it++ << endl;
\par   for(int k = 0; k < 20; k++)
\par     cout << is.pop() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The only difference is in the creation of }{\b is}{ and }{\b it}{
: You specify the type of object the stack and iterator should hold inside the template argument list.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374072}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776291}Constants in templates
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374072}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776291}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constant\: constants in templates}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {template\: constants in templates}}}{
Template arguments are not restricted to class types; you can also use built-in types. The values of these arguments then become compile-time constants for that particular instantiation of the template. You can even use default values for these arguments:

\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C16:Mblock.cpp
\par // Built-in types in templates
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par template<class T, int size = 100>
\par class Mblock \{
\par   T array[size];
\par public:
\par   T& operator[](int index) \{
\par     require(index >= 0 && index < size);
\par     return array[index];
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Number \{
\par   float f;
\par public:
\par   Number(float ff = 0.0f) : f(ff) \{\}
\par   Number& operator=(const Number& n) \{
\par     f = n.f;
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par   operator float() const \{ return f; \}
\par   friend ostream&
\par     operator<<(ostream& os, const Number& x) \{
\par       return os << x.f;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class T, int sz = 20>
\par class Holder \{
\par   Mblock<T, sz>* np;
\par public:
\par   Holder() : np(0) \{\}
\par   T& operator[](int i) \{
\par     require(i >= 0 && i < sz);
\par     if(!np) np = new Mblock<T, sz>;
\par     return np->operator[](i);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Holder<Number, 20> h;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 20; i++)
\par     h[i] = i;
\par   for(int j = 0; j < 20; j++)
\par     cout << h[j] << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Class }{\b Mblock}{ is a checked array of objects; you cannot index out of bounds. (Again, the exception approach in Chapter XX may be more appropriate than 
}{\b assert(\~)}{ in this situation.)
\par The class }{\b Holder}{ is much like }{\b Mblock}{ except that it has a pointer to an }{\b Mblock}{ instead of an embedded object of type }{\b Mblock}{
. This pointer is not initialized in the constructor; the initialization is delayed until the first access. You might use a technique like this if you are creating a lot of objects, but not accessing them all, and want to save storage.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593278}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628750}
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374073}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776292}Stash}{ and }{\b stack}{ \line as templates{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593278}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628750}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374073}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776292}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {stash\: stash and stack as templates}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {stack\: stash and stack as templates}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {template\:
 stash and stack as templates}}}{It turns out that the }{\b Stash}{ and }{\b Stack}{ classes that have been updated periodically throughout this book are actually container}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {container}}}{ classes, so it makes sense to convert them to templates. But first, one other important issue arises with container classes: When a container releases a pointer to an object
, does it destroy that object? For example, when a container object goes out of scope, does it destroy all the objects it points to?
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374074}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776293}The ownership problem
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374074}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776293}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This issue is commonly referred to as }{\i ownership}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe 
{container\: ownership}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ownership}}}{. Containers that hold entire objects don\rquote 
t usually worry about ownership because they clearly own the objects they contain. But if your container holds pointers (which is more common with C++, especially with polymorphism), then it\rquote s very likely those pointers may a
lso be used somewhere else in the program, and you don\rquote 
t necessarily want to delete the object because then the other pointers in the program would be referencing a destroyed object. To prevent this from happening, you must consider ownership when designing and using a container.
\par Many programs are very simple, and one container holds pointers to objects that are used only by that container. In this case ownership is very straightforward: The container owns its objects. Generally, you\rquote ll want this to be the 
default case for a container because it\rquote s the most common situation. 
\par The best approach to handling the ownership problem is to give the client programmer the choice. This is often accomplished by a constructor argument that defaults to indicating ownershi
p (typically desired for simple programs). In addition there may be read and set functions to view and modify the ownership of the container. If the container has functions to remove an object, the ownership state usually affects that removal, so you may 
also find options to control destruction in the removal function. You could conceivably also add ownership data for every element in the container, so each position would know whether it needed to be destroyed; this is a variant of reference counting}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {reference\: reference counting}}}{ where the container and not the object knows the number of references pointing to an object.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374075}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776294}Stash}{ as a template
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374075}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776294}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The \ldblquote stash\rdblquote  class that has been evolving throughout the book (last seen in Chapter XX) is an ideal candidate for 
a template. Now an iterator has been added along with ownership operations:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C16:TStash.h
\par // PSTASH using templates
\par #ifndef TSTASH_H
\par #define TSTASH_H
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par 
\par // More convenient than nesting in TStash:
\par enum Owns \{ no = 0, yes = 1, Default \};
\par // Declaration required:
\par template<class Type, int sz> class TStashIter;
\par 
\par template<class Type, int chunksize = 20>
\par class TStash \{
\par   int quantity;
\par   int next;
\par   Owns _owns; // Flag
\par   void inflate(int increase = chunksize);
\par protected:
\par   Type** storage;
\par public:
\par   TStash(Owns owns = yes);
\par   ~TStash();
\par   Owns owns() const \{ return _owns; \}
\par   void owns(Owns newOwns) \{ _owns = newOwns; \}
\par   int add(Type* element);
\par   int remove(int index, Owns d = Default);
\par   Type* operator[](int index);
\par   int count() const \{ return next; \}
\par   friend class TStashIter<Type, chunksize>;
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class Type, int sz = 20>
\par class TStashIter \{
\par   TStash<Type, sz>& ts;
\par   int index;
\par public:
\par   TStashIter(TStash<Type, sz>& TS)
\par     : ts(TS), index(0) \{\}
\par   TStashIter(const TStashIter& rv)
\par     : ts(rv.ts), index(rv.index) \{\}
\par   // Jump interator forward or backward:
\par   void forward(int amount) \{
\par     index += amount;
\par     if(index >= ts.next) index = ts.next -1;
\par   \}
\par   void backward(int amount) \{
\par     index -= amount;
\par     if(index < 0) index = 0;
\par   \}
\par   // Return value of ++ and -- to be
\par   // used inside conditionals:
\par   int operator++() \{
\par     if(++index >= ts.next) return 0;
\par     return 1;
\par   \}
\par   int operator++(int) \{ return operator++(); \}
\par   int operator--() \{
\par     if(--index < 0) return 0;
\par     return 1;
\par   \}
\par   int operator--(int) \{ return operator--(); \}
\par   operator int() \{
\par     return index >= 0 && index < ts.next;
\par   \}
\par   Type* operator->() \{
\par     Type* t = ts.storage[index];
\par     if(t) return t;
\par     require(0,"TStashIter::operator->return 0");
\par     return 0; // To allow inlining
\par   \}
\par   // Remove the current element:
\par   int remove(Owns d = Default)\{
\par     return ts.remove(index, d);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class Type, int sz>
\par TStash<Type, sz>::TStash(Owns owns) : _owns(owns) \{
\par   quantity = 0;
\par   storage = 0;
\par   next = 0;
\par \}
\par 
\par // Destruction of contained objects:
\par template<class Type, int sz>
\par TStash<Type, sz>::~TStash() \{
\par   if(!storage) return;
\par   if(_owns == yes)
\par     for(int i = 0; i < count(); i++)
\par       delete storage[i];
\par   free(storage);
\par \}
\par 
\par template<class Type, int sz>
\par int TStash<Type, sz>::add(Type* element) \{
\par   if(next >= quantity)
\par     inflate();
\par   storage[next++] = element;
\par   return(next - 1); // Index number
\par \}
\par 
\par template<class Type, int sz>
\par int TStash<Type, sz>::remove(int index, Owns d)\{
\par   if(index >= next || index < 0)
\par     return 0;
\par   switch(d) \{
\par     case Default:
\par       if(_owns != yes) break;
\par     case yes:
\par       delete storage[index];
\par     case no:
\par       storage[index] = 0; // Position is empty
\par   \}
\par   return 1;
\par \}
\par 
\par template<class Type, int sz> inline
\par Type* TStash<Type, sz>::operator[](int index) \{
\par   // Remove check for shipping application:
\par   require(index >= 0 && index < next);
\par   return storage[index];
\par \}
\par 
\par template<class Type, int sz>
\par void TStash<Type, sz>::inflate(int increase) \{
\par   void* v =
\par     realloc(storage, (quantity+increase)*sizeof(Type*));
\par   require(v != 0);  // Was it successful?
\par   storage = (Type**)v;
\par   quantity += increase;
\par \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // TSTASH_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b enum owns}{ is global, although you\rquote d normally want to nest it inside the class. Here it\rquote s m
ore convenient to use, but you can try moving it if you want to see the effect.
\par The }{\b storage}{ pointer is made }{\b protected}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {protected}}}{
so inherited classes can directly access it. This means that the inherited classes may become dependent on the specific implementation of }{\b TStash}{, but as you\rquote ll see in the }{\b Sorted.cpp}{ example, it\rquote s worth it.
\par The }{\b own }{flag indicates whether the container defaults to owning its objects. If so, in the destructor each object whose pointer is in the container is destroyed. This is st
raightforward; the container knows the type it contains. You can also change the default ownership in the constructor or read and modify it with the overloaded }{\b owns(\~)}{ function.
\par You should be aware that if the container holds pointers to a base-class type, that type should have a }{\b virtual}{ destructor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {virtual\: destructor}
}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {destructor\: virtual destructor}}}{ to ensure proper cleanup of derived objects whose addresses have been upcast when placing them in the container.

\par The }{\b TStashIter}{ follows the iterator 
model of bonding to a single container object for its lifetime. In addition, the copy-constructor allows you to make a new iterator pointing at the same location as the existing iterator you create it from, effectively making a bookmark into the container
. The }{\b forward(\~)}{ and }{\b backward(\~)}{
 member functions allow you to jump an iterator by a number of spots, respecting the boundaries of the container. The overloaded increment and decrement operators move the iterator by one place. The smart pointer is used to o
perate on the element the iterator is referring to, and }{\b remove(\~)}{ destroys the current object by calling the container\rquote s }{\b remove(\~)}{.
\par The following example creates and tests two different kinds of }{\b Stash}{ objects, one for a new class called }{\b Int}{ that announces its construction and destruction and one that holds objects of the class }{\b String}{ from Chapter XX.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C16:TStashTest.cpp
\par // Test TStash
\par #include "TStash.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("tstest.out");
\par 
\par class Int \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   Int(int ii = 0) : i(ii) \{
\par     out << ">" << i << endl;
\par   \}
\par   ~Int() \{ out << "~" << i << endl; \}
\par   operator int() const \{ return i; \}
\par   friend ostream&
\par     operator<<(ostream& os, const Int& x) \{
\par       return os << x.i;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   TStash<Int> intStash; // Instantiate for Int
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 30; i++)
\par     intStash.add(new Int(i));
\par   TStashIter<Int> intIter(intStash);
\par   intIter.forward(5);
\par   for(int j = 0; j < 20; j++, intIter++)
\par     intIter.remove(); // Default removal
\par   for(int k = 0; k < intStash.count(); k++)
\par     if(intStash[k]) // Remove() causes "holes"
\par       out << *intStash[k] << endl;
\par 
\par   ifstream file("TStashTest.cpp");
\par   assure(file, "TStashTest.cpp");
\par   // Instantiate for String:
\par   TStash<string> stringStash;
\par   string line;
\par   while(getline(file, line))
\par     stringStash.add(new string(line));
\par   for(int u = 0; u < stringStash.count(); u++)
\par     if(stringStash[u])
\par       out << *stringStash[u] << endl;
\par   TStashIter<string> it(stringStash);
\par   int j = 25;
\par   it.forward(j);
\par   while(it) \{
\par     out << j++ << ": " << it->c_str() << endl;
\par     it++;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In both cases an iterator }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iterator}}}{
is created and used to move through the container. Notice the elegance produced by using these constructs: You aren\rquote t assailed with the implementation details of using an array. You tell the container and iterator objects }{\i what}{
 to do, not how. This makes the solution easier to conceptualize, to build, and to modify.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374076}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776295}stack}{ as a template
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374076}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776295}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b Stack}{ class, last seen in Chapter XX, is also a container and is also best expressed as a template with an associated iterator. Here\rquote 
s the new header file:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C16:TStack.h
\par // Stack using templates
\par #ifndef TSTACK_H
\par #define TSTACK_H
\par 
\par // Declaration required:
\par template<class T> class TStackIterator;
\par 
\par template<class T> class TStack \{
\par   struct Link \{
\par     T* data;
\par     Link* next;
\par     Link(T* dat, Link* nxt) \{
\par       data = dat;
\par       next = nxt;
\par     \}
\par   \}* head;
\par   int _owns;
\par public:
\par   TStack(int own = 1) : head(0), _owns(own) \{\}
\par   ~TStack();
\par   void push(T* dat) \{
\par     head = new Link(dat,head);
\par   \}
\par   T* peek() const \{ return head->data; \}
\par   T* pop();
\par   int owns() const \{ return _owns; \}
\par   void owns(int newownership) \{
\par     _owns = newownership;
\par   \}
\par   friend class TStackIterator<T>;
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class T> T* TStack<T>::pop() \{
\par   if(head == 0) return 0;
\par   T* result = head->data;
\par   Link* oldHead = head;
\par   head = head->next;
\par   delete oldHead;
\par   return result;
\par \}
\par 
\par template<class T> TStack<T>::~TStack() \{
\par   Link* cursor = head;
\par   while(head) \{
\par     cursor = cursor->next;
\par     // Conditional cleanup of data:
\par     if(_owns) delete head->data;
\par     delete head;
\par     head = cursor;
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par template<class T> class TStackIterator \{
\par   TStack<T>::Link* p;
\par public:
\par   TStackIterator(const TStack<T>& tl)
\par     : p(tl.head) \{\}
\par   TStackIterator(const TStackIterator& tl)
\par     : p(tl.p) \{\}
\par   // operator++ returns boolean indicating end:
\par   int operator++() \{
\par     if(p->next)
\par       p = p->next;
\par     else p = 0; // Indicates end of list
\par     return int(p);
\par   \}
\par   int operator++(int) \{ return operator++(); \}
\par   // Smart pointer:
\par   T* operator->() const \{
\par     if(!p) return 0;
\par     return p->data;
\par   \}
\par   T* current() const \{
\par     if(!p) return 0;
\par     return p->data;
\par   \}
\par   // int conversion for conditional test:
\par   operator int() const \{ return p ? 1 : 0; \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // TSTACK_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You\rquote 
ll also notice the class has been changed to support ownership, which works now because the class knows the exact type (or at least the base type, which will work assuming virtual destructors are used). As with }{\b TStash}{,}{\b  }{the default}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ownership}}}{ is for the container to destroy its objects but you can change this by either modifying the constructor argument or using the }{\b owns(
\~)}{ read/write member functions.
\par The iterator }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iterator}}}{is very simple and very small \endash  the size of a single pointer. When you create a }{\b TStackIterator}{, it\rquote 
s initialized to the head of the linked list, and you can only increment it forward through the list. If you want to start over at the beginning, you create a new it
erator, and if you want to remember a spot in the list, you create a new iterator from the existing iterator pointing at that spot (using the copy-constructor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {copy-constructor}}}{).
\par To call functions for the object referred to by the iterator, you can use the smart pointer}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {smart pointer operator->}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {pointer\: smart pointer}}}{ (a very common sight in iterators) or a function called }{\b current(\~)}{ that }{\i looks}{
 identical to the smart pointer because it returns a pointer to the current object, but is different because the smart pointer performs the extra levels of dereferencing (see Chapter XX). Finally, the }{\b operator int }{
indicates whether or not you are at the end of the list and allows the iterator to be used in conditional statements.
\par The entire implementation is contained in the header file, so there\rquote s no separate }{\b cpp}{ file. Here\rquote s a small test that also exercises the iterator:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C16:TStackTest.cpp
\par // Use template list & iterator
\par #include "TStack.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   ifstream file("TStackTest.cpp");
\par   assure(file, "TStackTest.cpp");
\par   TStack<string> textlines;
\par   // Read file and store lines in the list:
\par   string line;
\par   while(getline(file, line))
\par     textlines.push(new string(line));
\par   int i = 0;
\par   // Use iterator to print lines from the list:
\par   TStackIterator<string> it(textlines);
\par   TStackIterator<string>* it2 = 0;
\par   while(it) \{
\par     cout << *it.current() << endl;
\par     it++;
\par     if(++i == 10) // Remember 10th line
\par       it2 = new TStackIterator<string>(it);
\par   \}
\par   cout << *(it2->current()) << endl;
\par   delete it2;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A }{\b TStack}{ is instantiated to hold }{\b String}{
 objects and filled with lines from a file. Then an iterator is created and used to move through the linked list. The tenth line is remembered by copy-constructing a second iterator from the first; later this line is printed and the iterator \endash 
 created dynamically \endash  is destroyed. Here, dynamic object creation}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {dynamic\: object creation}}}{ is used to control the lifetime of the object.

\par This is very similar to earlier test examples for the }{\b Stack}{ class, but now the contained objects are properly destroyed when the }{\b TStack}{ is destroyed.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593280}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628752}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374087
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776296}Polymorphism & containers{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593280}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628752}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374087}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776296}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {polymorphism\: and containers}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {container\: and polymorphism }}}{It\rquote 
s common to see polymorphism, dynamic object creation and containers used together in a true object-oriented program. Containers and dynamic object creation solve the problem of not knowing how many or what type of objects you\rquote ll need, 
and because the container is configured to hold pointers to base-class objects, an upcast occurs every time you put a derived-class pointer into the container (with the associated code organization and extensibility benefits). The following example is a l
ittle simulation of trash recycling. All the trash is put into a single bin, then later it\rquote s sorted out into separate bins. There\rquote 
s a function that goes through any trash bin and figures out what the resource value is. Notice this is not the most elegant wa
y to implement this simulation; the example will be revisited in Chapter XX when Run-Time Type Identification (RTTI) is explained:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C16:Recycle.cpp
\par // Containers & polymorphism
\par #include "TStack.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("recycle.out");
\par 
\par enum TrashType \{ AluminumT, PaperT, GlassT \};
\par 
\par class Trash \{
\par   float _weight;
\par public:
\par   Trash(float wt) : _weight(wt) \{\}
\par   virtual TrashType trashType() const = 0;
\par   virtual const char* name() const = 0;
\par   virtual float value() const = 0;
\par   float weight() const \{ return _weight; \}
\par   virtual ~Trash() \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Aluminum : public Trash \{
\par   static float val;
\par public:
\par   Aluminum(float wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   TrashType trashType() const \{ return AluminumT; \}
\par   virtual const char* name() const \{
\par     return "Aluminum";
\par   \}
\par   float value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(int newval) \{
\par     val = newval;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par float Aluminum::val = 1.67;
\par 
\par class Paper : public Trash \{
\par   static float val;
\par public:
\par   Paper(float wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   TrashType trashType() const \{ return PaperT; \}
\par   virtual const char* name() const \{
\par     return "Paper";
\par   \}
\par   float value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(int newval) \{
\par     val = newval;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par float Paper::val = 0.10;
\par 
\par class Glass : public Trash \{
\par   static float val;
\par public:
\par   Glass(float wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   TrashType trashType() const \{ return GlassT; \}
\par   virtual const char* name() const \{
\par     return "Glass";
\par   \}
\par   float value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(int newval) \{
\par     val = newval;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par float Glass::val = 0.23;
\par 
\par // Sums up the value of the Trash in a bin:
\par void sumValue(const TStack<Trash>& bin,ostream& os)\{
\par   TStackIterator<Trash> tally(bin);
\par   float val = 0;
\par   while(tally) \{
\par     val += tally->weight() * tally->value();
\par     os << "weight of " << tally->name()
\par         << " = " << tally->weight() << endl;
\par     tally++;
\par   \}
\par   os << "Total value = " << val << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   srand(time(0)); // Seed random number generator
\par   TStack<Trash> bin; // Default to ownership
\par   // Fill up the Trash bin:
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 30; i++)
\par     switch(rand() % 3) \{
\par       case 0 :
\par         bin.push(new Aluminum(rand() % 100));
\par         break;
\par       case 1 :
\par         bin.push(new Paper(rand() % 100));
\par         break;
\par       case 2 :
\par         bin.push(new Glass(rand() % 100));
\par         break;
\par     \}
\par   // Bins to sort into:
\par   TStack<Trash> glassBin(0); // No ownership
\par   TStack<Trash> paperBin(0);
\par   TStack<Trash> alBin(0);
\par   TStackIterator<Trash> sorter(bin);
\par   // Sort the Trash:
\par   // (RTTI offers a nicer solution)
\par   while(sorter) \{
\par     // Smart pointer call:
\par     switch(sorter->trashType()) \{
\par       case AluminumT:
\par         alBin.push(sorter.current());
\par         break;
\par       case PaperT:
\par         paperBin.push(sorter.current());
\par         break;
\par       case GlassT:
\par         glassBin.push(sorter.current());
\par         break;
\par     \}
\par     sorter++;
\par   \}
\par   sumValue(alBin, out);
\par   sumValue(paperBin, out);
\par   sumValue(glassBin, out);
\par   sumValue(bin, out);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This uses the classic structure of virtual functions in the base class that are redefined in the derived class. The container }{\b TStack}{
 is instantiated for }{\b Trash}{, so it holds }{\b Trash}{ pointers, which are pointers to the base class. However, it will also hold pointers to objects of classes derived from }{\b Trash}{, as you can see in the call to }{\b push(\~)}{
. When these pointers are added, they lose their specific identities and become simply }{\b Trash}{ pointers (they are }{\i upcast}{). However, because of polymorphism }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {polymorphism}}}{the proper behavior still occurs when the virtual function}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {virtual\: function}}}{ is called through the }{\b tally
}{ and }{\b sorter}{ iterators. (Notice the use of the iterator\rquote s smart pointer}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {smart pointer operator->}}}{
, which causes the virtual function call.)
\par The }{\b Trash}{ class also includes a }{\b virtual}{ destructor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {virtual\: destructor}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {destructor\: virtual destructor}}}{, something you should automatically add to any class with }{\b virtual}{ functions. When the }{\b bin}{
 container goes out of scope, the container\rquote s destructor calls all the }{\b virtual}{ destructors for the objects it contains, and thus properly cleans everything up.
\par Because container class templates}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {container\: container class templates and virtual functions}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\: container class templates and virtual functions}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
template\: container class templates and virtual functions}}}{ are rarely subject to the inheritance and upcasting you see with \ldblquote ordinary\rdblquote  classes, you\rquote ll almost never see }{\b virtual}{
 functions in these types of classes. Their reuse is implemented with templates, not with inheritance.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\cs29\b\v\f17\fs16\lang1033 {\*\atnid BE}{\*\atnauthor Bruce Eckel}\chatn 
{\*\annotation \pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst {PAGE \\# "'Page: '#'\line '"}{\cs29\fs16 
  }}{\fldrslt }}{\cs29\fs16 \chatn }{a long journey
\par }\pard\plain \s31\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {This is the end of a long process, the development of the container class concept and C++ support for it. N
ow you can easily create complex programs. As an example, create a callback object, and a list of them which can all be called (see DDJ article)
\par }}}{{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593283}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628755}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374094}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776297}Summary{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593283}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628755}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374094}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776297}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Container classes are an essential part of object-oriented programming; they are another way to simplify and hide the details of a program and to speed the process of program development. In addition, they provide a great deal of safety and flexibi
lity by replacing the primitive arrays and relatively crude data structure techniques found in C.
\par Because the client programmer needs containers, it\rquote s essential that they be easy to use. This is where the }{\b template}{ comes in. With templates the syntax for so
urce-code reuse (as opposed to object-code reuse provided by inheritance and composition) becomes trivial enough for the novice user. In fact, reusing code with templates is notably easier than inheritance and composition.
\par Although you\rquote ve learned about creating container and iterator classes in this book, in practice it\rquote 
s much more expedient to learn the containers and iterators that come with your compiler or, failing that, to buy a library from a third-party vendor.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ See, for example, Rogue Wave}{\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {Rogue Wave}}}{, which has a well-designed set of C++ tools for all platforms.}}}{ The standard C++ library includes a very complete but nonexhaustive set of containers and iterators.
\par The issues involved with container-class design have been touched upon in this chapter, but you may have gathered that they can go much further. A complicated container-class library may cover 
all sorts of additional issues, including persistence (introduced in Chapter XX) and garbage collection (introduced in Chapter XX), as well as additional ways to handle the ownership problem.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374095}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776298}Exercises
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374095}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776298}
