\par }{\b Localization Library}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Standard C++ libraries\: localization library}}}{. This allows you to localize strings in your program to adapt to usage in different countries}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {adapting to usage in different countries, Standard C++ localization library}}}{, including money, numbers, date, time, and so on.
\par }{\b Containers Library}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Standard C++ libraries\: containers library}}}{. This includes the Standard Template Library (described in the next section of this appendix) and also the }{\b bits}{ }
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Standard C++ libraries\: bits bit vector}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {bits\: bit vector in the Standard C++ libraries}}}{and }{\b bit_string}{ }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Standard C++ libraries\: bit_string bit vector}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {bit_string\: bit vector in the Standard C++ libraries}}}{classes in }{\b <bits>}{ and }{\b 
<bitstring>}{, respectively. Both }{\b bits}{ and }{\b bit_string}{ are more complete implementations of the bitvector concept introduced in Chapter XX. The }{\b bits}{
 template creates a fixed-sized array of bits that can be manipulated with all the bitwise operators, as well as member functions like }{\b set(\~)}{, }{\b reset(\~)}{, }{\b count(\~)}{, }{\b length(\~)}{, }{\b test(\~)}{, }{\b any(\~)}{, and }{\b none(\~
)}{. There are also conversion operators }{\b to_ushort(\~)}{, }{\b to_ulong(\~)}{, and }{\b to_string(\~)}{. 
\par The }{\b bit_string}{ class is, by contrast, a dynamically sized array of bits, with similar operations to }{\b bits}{, but also with additional operations that make it act somewhat like a }{\b string}{. There\rquote 
s a fundamental difference in bit weighting: With }{\b bits}{, the right-most bit (bit zero) is the least significant bit, but with }{\b bit_string}{, the right-most bit is the }{\i most}{ significant bit. There are no conversions between }{\b bits}{ and 
}{\b bit_string}{. You\rquote ll use }{\b bits}{ for a space-efficient set of on-off flags and }{\b bit_string}{ for manipulating arrays of binary values (like pixels).
