One of the biggest time-wasters in C is character arrays: keeping track of the difference between static quoted strings and arrays created on the stack and the heap, and the fact that sometimes you\rquote re passing around a }{\b char*}{
 and sometimes you must copy the whole array.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart StringsChapter}{\*\bkmkend StringsChapter}(This is the general problem of }{\i shallow copy}{ vs. }{\i deep copy}{
.) Especially because string manipulation is so common, character arrays are a great source of misunderstandings and bugs.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\adjustright {Despite t
his, creating string classes remained a common exercise for beginning C++ programmers for many years. The Standard C++ library }{\b string}{ class solves the problem of character array manipulation once and for all}{\b ,}{
 keeping track of memory even during assignments and copy-constructions. You simply don\rquote t need to think about it.
\par This chapter examines the Standard C++ }{\b string}{ class, beginning with a look at what constitutes a C++ string and how the C++ version differs from a traditional C character array.  You\rquote ll learn about operations and manipulations using }{\b 
string}{ objects, and see how C++ }{\b string}{s accommodate variation in character sets and string data conversion.
\par Handling text is perhaps one of the oldest of all programming applications, so it\rquote s not surprising that the C++ }{\b string}{
 draws heavily on the ideas and terminology that have long been used for this purpose in C and other languages. As you begin to acquaint yourself with C++ }{\b string}{s this fact should be reassuring, in the respect that no matter what programming i
diom you choose, there are really only about three things you can do with a }{\b string}{: create or modify the sequence of characters stored in the }{\b string}{, detect the presence or absence of elements within the }{\b string}{
, and translate between various schemes for representing }{\b string}{ characters.
\par You\rquote ll see how each of these jobs is accomplished using C++ }{\b string}{ objects.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776302}What\rquote s in a string{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776302}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In C, a string is simply an array of characters that always includes a binary zero (often called the }{\i null terminator}{) as its final 
array element. There are two significant differences between C++ }{\b string}{s and their C progenitors. First, C++ }{\b string}{ objects associate the array of characters which constitute the }{\b string}{
 with methods useful for managing and operating on it. A }{\b string}{ also contains certain \ldblquote housekeeping\rdblquote  information about the size and storage location of its data. Specifically, a C++ }{\b string}{
 object knows its starting location in memory, its content, its length in characters, and the length in characters to which it can grow before the }{\b string}{
 object must resize its internal data buffer. This gives rise to the second big difference between C }{\b char}{ arrays and C++ }{\b string}{s. C++ }{\b string}{s do not include a null terminator, nor do the C++ }{\b string}{
 handling member functions rely on the existence of a null terminator to perform their jobs. C++ }{\b string}{
s greatly reduce the likelihood of making three of the most common and destructive C programming errors: overwriting array bounds, trying to access arrays through uninitialized or incorrectly valued pointers, and leaving pointers \ldblquote dangling
\rdblquote  after an array ceases to occupy the storage that was once allocated to it.
\par The exact implementation of memory layout for the string class is not defined by the C++ Standard. This architecture is intended to be fle
xible enough to allow differing implementations by compiler vendors, yet guarantee predictable behavior for users. In particular, the exact conditions under which storage is allocated to hold data for a string object are not defined. String allocation rul
es were formulated to allow but not require a reference-counted implementation, but whether or not the implementation uses reference counting, the semantics must be the same. To put this a bit differently, in C, every }{\b char}{
 array occupies a unique physical region of memory. In C++, individual }{\b string}{
 objects may or may not occupy unique physical regions of memory, but if reference counting is used to avoid storing duplicate copies of data, the individual objects must look and act as though they do exclusively 
own unique regions of storage. For example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:StringStorage.cpp
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   string s1("12345");
\par   // Set the iterator indicate the first element
\par   string::iterator it = s1.begin();
\par   // This may copy the first to the second or 
\par   // use reference counting to simulate a copy 
\par   string s2 = s1;
\par   // Either way, this statement may ONLY modify first
\par   *it = '0';
\par   cout << "s1 = " << s1 << endl;
\par   cout << "s2 = " << s2 << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Reference counting may serve to make an implementation more memory efficient, but it is transparent to users of the }{\b string}{ class.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc424692472}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776303}Creating and initializing C++ strings{\*\bkmkend _Toc424692472}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776303}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Creating and initializing }{\b string}{s is a straightforward proposition, and fairly flexible as well. In the example shown below, the first }{\b string}{, }{\b imBlank}{\lang1024\kerning18 , }{
is declared but contains no initial value. Unlike a C }{\b char}{ array, which would contain a random and meaningless bit pattern until initialization, }{\b imBlank}{\lang1024\kerning18  }{does contain}{\lang1024\kerning18  meaningful information. This }{
\b\lang1024\kerning18 string}{\lang1024\kerning18  object has been initialized to hold \ldblquote no characters,\rdblquote  and can properly report its 0 length and absence of data elements through the use of class member functions.}{
\par The next }{\b string}{, }{\b heyMom}{, is initialized by the literal argument "Where are my socks?". This form of initialization uses a quoted character array as a parameter to the }{\b string}{ constructor. By contrast, }{\b standardReply}{
 is simply initialized with an assignment. The last }{\b string}{ of the group, }{\b useThisOneAgain}{, is initialized using an existing C++ }{\b string}{ object. Put another way, this example illustrates that }{\b string}{ objects let you:
