\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice the final line of example above. C++ allows }{\b string}{
 initialization techniques to be mixed in a single statement, a flexible and convenient feature. Also note that the last initializer copies }{\i just one character}{ from the source }{\b string}{.
\par Another slightly more subtle initialization technique involves the use of the }{\b string}{ iterators }{\b string.begin(\~)}{ and }{\b string.end(\~).}{ This treats a }{\b string}{ like a }{\i container}{ object (which you\rquote ve seen primarily in the
 form of }{\b vector}{ so far in this book \endash  you\rquote ll see many more containers soon) which has }{\i iterators}{ indicating the start and end of the \ldblquote container.\rdblquote  This way you can hand a }{\b string}{
 constructor two iterators and it will copy from one to the other into the new }{\b string}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:StringIterators.cpp
\par #include <string>
