\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776304}Operating on strings{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776304}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you\rquote ve programmed in C, you are accustomed to the convenience of a large family of functions for writing, searching, rearranging, and copying }{\b char}{
 arrays. However, there are two unfortunate aspects of the Standard C library functions for handling }{\b char}{ arrays. First, there are three loosely organized families of them: the \ldblquote plain\rdblquote 
 group, the group that manipulates the characters }{\i without}{ respect to case, and the ones which require you to supply a count of the number of characters to be considered in the operation at hand. The roster of function names in the C }{\b char}{
 array handling library literally runs to several pages, and though the kind and number of arguments to the functions are somewhat consistent within each of the three groups, to use them properly you must be ver
y attentive to details of function naming and parameter passing.
\par The second inherent trap of the standard C }{\b char}{ array tools is that they all rely explicitly on the assumption that the character array includes a null terminator. If by oversight or error the null is omitted or overwritten, there\rquote 
s very little to keep the C }{\b char}{ array handling functions from manipulating the memory beyond the limits of the allocated space, sometimes with disastrous results. 
\par C++ provides a vast improvement in the convenience and safety of }{\b string}{ objects. For purposes of actual string handling operations, there are a modest two or three dozen member function names. It\rquote 
s worth your while to become acquainted with these. Each function is overloaded, so you don\rquote t have to learn a new }{\b string}{ member function name simply because of small differences in their parameters.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776305}Appending, inserting and concatenating strings{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776305}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {One of the most valuable and convenient aspects of C++ strings is that they grow as needed, without intervention on the p
art of the programmer. Not only does this make string handling code inherently more trustworthy, it also almost entirely eliminates a tedious \ldblquote housekeeping\rdblquote  chore \endash 
 keeping track of the bounds of the storage in which your strings live. For example, if you create a string object and initialize it with a string of 50 copies of \lquote X\rquote , and later store in it 50 copies of \ldblquote Zowie\rdblquote 
, the object itself will reallocate sufficient storage to accommodate the growth of the data. Perhaps nowhere is this property more appreciated than when the strings manipulated in your code will change in size, but when you don\rquote 
t know big the change is. Appending, concatenating, and inserting strings often give rise to this circumstance, but the string member functions }{\b append(\~)}{ and }{\b insert(\~)}{ transparently reallocate storage when a string grows.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:StrSize.cpp
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   string bigNews("I saw Elvis in a UFO. ");
\par   cout << bigNews << endl;
\par   // How much data have we actually got?
\par   cout << "Size = " << bigNews.size() << endl;
\par   // How much can we store without reallocating
\par   cout << "Capacity = " 
\par     << bigNews.capacity() << endl;
\par   // Insert this string in bigNews immediately
\par   // following bigNews[1]
\par   bigNews.insert(1, " thought I ");
\par   cout << bigNews << endl;
\par   cout << "Size = " << bigNews.size() << endl;
\par   cout << "Capacity = " 
\par     << bigNews.capacity() << endl;
\par   // Make sure that there will be this much space
\par   bigNews.reserve(500);
\par   // Add this to the end of the string
\par   bigNews.append("I've been working too hard.");
\par   cout << bigNews << endl;
\par   cout << "Size = " << bigNews.size() << endl;
\par   cout << "Capacity = " 
\par     << bigNews.capacity() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
