\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {I saw Elvis in a UFO.
\par Size = 21
\par Capacity = 31
\par I thought I saw Elvis in a UFO.
\par Size = 32
\par Capacity = 63
\par I thought I saw Elvis in a UFO. I've been 
\par working too hard.
\par Size = 66
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Capacity = 511
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This example demonstrates that even though you can safely relinquish much of the responsibility for allocating and managing the memory your }{\b string}{s occupy, C++ }{\b string}{
s provide you with several tools to monitor and manage their size. The }{\b size(\~)}{, }{\b resize(\~)}{, }{\b capacity(\~)}{, and }{\b reserve(\~)}{ member functions can be very useful when its necessary to work back and forth
 between data contained in C++ style strings and traditional null terminated C }{\b char}{ arrays. Note the ease with which we changed the size of the storage allocated to the string.
\par The exact fashion in which the }{\b string}{ member functions will allocate space for 
your data is dependent on the implementation of the library. When one implementation was tested with the example above, it appeared that reallocations occurred on even word boundaries, with one byte held back. The architects of the }{\b string}{
 class have endeavored to make it possible to mix the use of C }{\b char}{ arrays and C++ string objects, so it is likely that figures reported by }{\b StrSize.cpp}{
 for capacity reflect that in this particular implementation, a byte is set aside to easily accommodate the insertion of a null terminator. 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776306}Replacing string characters{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776306}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b insert(\~) }{is particularly nice because it absolves you of making sure the insertion of characters in a string won\rquote 
t overrun the storage space or overwrite the characters immediately following the insertion poin
t. Space grows and existing characters politely move over to accommodate the new elements. Sometimes, however, this might not be what you want to happen. If the data in string needs to retain the ordering of the original characters relative to one another
 or must be a specific constant size, use the }{\b replace(\~)}{ function to overwrite a particular sequence of characters with another group of characters. There are quite a number of overloaded versions of }{\b replace(\~)}{
, but the simplest one takes three arguments: a
n integer telling where to start in the string, an integer telling how many characters to eliminate from the original string, and the replacement string (which can be a different number of characters than the eliminated quantity). Here\rquote 
s a very simple example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:StringReplace.cpp
\par // Simple find-and-replace in strings
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   string s("A piece of text");
\par   string tag("$tag$");
\par   s.insert(8, tag + ' ');
\par   cout << s << endl;
\par   int start = s.find(tag);
\par   cout << "start = " << start << endl;
\par   cout << "size = " << tag.size() << endl;
\par   s.replace(start, tag.size(), "hello there");
\par   cout << s << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b tag}{ is first inserted into }{\b s}{ (notice that the insert happens }{\i before}{ the value indicating the insert point, and that an extra space was added after }{\b tag}{
), then it is found and replaced.
\par You should actually check to see if you\rquote ve found anything before you perform a }{\b replace(\~)}{.}{\b  }{The above example replaces with a }{\b char*}{, but there\rquote s an overloaded version that replaces with a }{\b string}{.}{\b  }{Here
\rquote s a more complete demonstration }{\b replace(\~)
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\lang1033\kerning0 //: C17:Replace.cpp
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par void replaceChars(string& modifyMe, 
\par   string findMe, string newChars)\{
\par   // Look in modifyMe for the "find string"
\par   // starting at position 0
\par   int i = modifyMe.find(findMe, 0);
\par   // Did we find the string to replace?
\par   if(i != string::npos)
\par     // Replace the find string with newChars
\par     modifyMe.replace(i,newChars.size(),newChars);
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   string bigNews = 
\par    "I thought I saw Elvis in a UFO. "
\par    "I have been working too hard.";
\par   string replacement("wig");
\par   string findMe("UFO");
\par   // Find "UFO" in bigNews and overwrite it:
\par   replaceChars(bigNews, findMe,  replacement);
\par   cout << bigNews << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\lang1033\kerning0 \} ///:~
