\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {working too hard.
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If replace doesn\rquote t find the search string, it returns }{\b npos}{. }{\b npos}{ is a static constant member of the }{\b basic_string}{ class.
\par Unlike }{\b insert(\~)}{, }{\b replace(\~)}{ won\rquote t grow the }{\b string}{\rquote s storage space if you copy new characters into the middle of an existing series of array elements. However, it }{\i will}{ grow the storage space if you make a 
\ldblquote replacement\rdblquote  that writes beyond the end of an existing array. Here\rquote s an example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:ReplaceAndGrow.cpp
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   string bigNews("I saw Elvis in a UFO. "
