\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {been working too hard.wig
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that }{\b replace(\~)}{ expands the array to accommodate the growth of the string due to \ldblquote replacement\rdblquote  beyond the bounds of the existing array.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Simple character replacement using the STL replace(\~) algorithm
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You may have been hunting through this chapter trying to do something relatively simple like replace all the instances of one character with a different character. Upon finding the above section 
on replacing, you thought you found the answer but then you started seeing groups of characters and counts and other things that looked a bit too complex. Doesn\rquote t }{\b string}{ have a way to just replace one character with another everywhere?

\par The answer comes in observing that the }{\b string}{ class doesn\rquote t solve all these problems alone. It contains a significant number of functions, but there other problems it doesn\rquote 
t solve. These are relegated to the STL algorithms, and enabled because the }{\b string}{ class can look just like an STL container, which is what the STL algorithms want to work with. All the STL algorithms specify a \ldblquote range\rdblquote 
 of elements within a container that the algorithm will work upon. Usually that range is just \ldblquote from the beginning of the container to the end.\rdblquote  A }{\b string}{
 object looks like a container of characters, and to get the beginning of the range you use }{\b string::begin(\~)}{ and to get the end of the range you use }{\b string::end(\~)}{. The following example shows the use of the STL }{\b replace(\~)}{
 algorithm to replace all the instances of \lquote X\rquote  with \lquote Y\rquote :
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:StringCharReplace.cpp
\par #include <string>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   string s("aaaXaaaXXaaXXXaXXXXaaa");
\par   cout << s << endl;
\par   replace(s.begin(), s.end(), 'X', 'Y');
\par   cout << s << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that this }{\b replace(\~)}{ is }{\i not}{ called as a member function of }{\b string}{. Also, unlike the }{\b string::replace(\~)}{
 functions which only perform one replacement, the STL replace is replacing all instances of one character with another.
\par The STL }{\b replace(\~)}{ algorithm only works with single objects (in this case, }{\b char}{ objects), and will not perform replacements of quoted }{\b char}{ arrays or of }{\b string}{ objects.
\par Since a }{\b string}{ looks like an STL container, there are a number of other STL algorithms that can be applied to it, which may solve other problems you have that are not directly addressed by the }{\b string}{
 member functions. See Chapter XX for more information on the STL algorithms.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776307}Concatenation using non-member overloaded operators{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776307}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {One of the most delightful discoveries awaiting a C programmer learning about C++ }{\b string}{ handling is how simply }{\b string}{s can be combined and appended using }{\b operator+}{ and }{\b 
operator+=}{.}{\b  }{These operators make combining }{\b string}{s syntactically equivalent to adding numeric data.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:AddStrings.cpp
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   string s1("This ");
\par   string s2("That ");
\par   string s3("The other ");
\par   // operator+ concatenates strings
\par   s1 = s1 + s2;
\par   cout << s1 << endl;
\par   // Another way to concatenates strings
