\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
To compare a subset of the characters in one or both strings, you add arguments that define where to start the comparison and how many characters to consider. For example, we can use the overloaded version of }{\b compare(\~)}{:
\par }{\b s1.compare(s1artPos, s1NumberChars, s2, s2StartPos, s2NumberChars);
\par }{If we substitute the above version of }{\b compare(\~)}{ in the previous program so that it only looks at the first two characters of each string, the program becomes:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:Compare2.cpp
\par // Overloaded compare()
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   string first("This");
\par   string second("That");
\par   // Compare first two characters of each string:
\par   switch(first.compare(0, 2, second, 0, 2)) \{
\par     case 0: // The same
\par       cout << first << " and " << second <<
\par         " are lexically equal" << endl;
\par       break;
\par     case -1: // Less than
\par       first.swap(second);
\par       // Fall through this case...
\par     case 1: // Greater than
\par       cout << first <<
\par         " is lexically greater than " <<
\par         second << endl;
