\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {which is true, for the first two characters of \ldblquote This\rdblquote  and \ldblquote That.\rdblquote 
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Indexing with }{\b [ ]}{ vs. }{\b at(\~)}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the examples so far, we have used C style array indexing syntax to refer to an individual character in a string. C++ strings provide an alternative to the }{\b s[n] }{notation: the }{\b at(\~)}{
 member. These two idioms produce the same result in C++ if all goes well:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:StringIndexing.cpp
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par int main()\{
\par   string s("1234");
\par   cout << s[1] << " ";
\par   cout << s.at(1) << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
