\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {2 2
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {However, there is one important difference between }{\b [ ]}{ and }{\b at(\~)}{. When you try to reference an array element that is out of bounds, }{\b at(\~) }{
will do you the kindness of throwing an exception, while ordinary }{\b [ ] }{subscripting syntax will leave you to your own devices:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:BadStringIndexing.cpp
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main()\{
\par   string s("1234");
\par   // Runtime problem: goes beyond array bounds:
\par   cout << s[5] << endl;
\par   // Saves you by throwing an exception:
\par   cout << s.at(5) << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Using }{\b at(\~) }{in place of }{\b [ ] }{will give you a chance to gracefully recover from references to array elements that don\rquote t exist. }{\b at(\~)}{ throws an object of class }{\b 
out_of_range. }{By catching this object in an exception handler, you can take appropriate remedial actions such as recalculating the offending subscript or growing the array. (You can read more about Exception Handling in Chapter XX)
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776313}Using iterators{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776313}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the example program }{\b NewFind.cpp}{, we used a lot of messy and rather tedious C }{\b char}{ array handling code to change the case of the characters in a string and then sea
rch for the occurrence of matches to a substring. Sometimes the \ldblquote quick and dirty\rdblquote  method is justifiable, but in general, you won\rquote 
t want to sacrifice the advantages of having your string data safely and securely encapsulated in the C++ object where it lives. 
\par Here is a better, safer way to handle case insensitive comparison of two C++ string objects. Because no data is copied out of the objects and into C style strings, you don\rquote t have to use pointers and you don\rquote 
t have to risk overwriting the bounds of an ordinary character array. In this example, we use the string }{\b iterator}{
. Iterators are themselves objects which move through a collection or container of other objects, selecting them one at a time, but never providing direct access to the implementation of the container. Iterators are }{\i not}{
 pointers, but they are useful for many of the same jobs.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:CmpIter.cpp
\par // Find a group of characters in a string
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par // Case insensitive compare function:
\par int 
\par stringCmpi(const string& s1, const string& s2) \{
\par   // Select the first element of each string:
\par   string::const_iterator 
\par     p1 = s1.begin(), p2 = s2.begin();
\par   // Don\rquote t run past the end:
\par   while(p1 != s1.end() && p2 != s2.end()) \{
\par     // Compare upper-cased chars:
\par     if(toupper(*p1) != toupper(*p2))
\par       // Report which was lexically  greater:
\par       return (toupper(*p1)<toupper(*p2))? -1 : 1;
\par     p1++;
\par     p2++;
\par   \}
\par   // If they match up to the detected eos, say 
\par   // which was longer. Return 0 if the same.
\par   return(s2.size() - s1.size());
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   string s1("Mozart");
\par   string s2("Modigliani");
\par   cout << stringCmpi(s1, s2) << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that the iterators }{\b p1}{ and }{\b p2}{ use the same syntax as C pointers \endash  the \lquote }{\b *}{\rquote  operator makes the }{\i value of}{ e
lement at the location given by the iterators available to the }{\b toupper(\~) }{function. }{\b toupper(\~) }{doesn\rquote t actually change the content of the element in the string. In fact, it can\rquote t. This definition of }{\b p1}{
 tells us that we can only use the elements }{\b p1}{ points to as constants. 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {string::const_iterator p1 = s1.begin();
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The way }{\b toupper(\~)}{ and the iterators are used in this example is called a }{\i case preserving }{case insensitive comparison. This means that the string didn\rquote 
t have to be copied or rewritten to accommodate case insensitive comparison. Both of the strings retain their original data, unmodified.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Iterating in reverse
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Just as the standard C pointer gives us the increment (++) and decrement (--) operators to make pointer arithmetic a bit more convenient, C++ string iterators come in two basic varieties. You\rquote 
ve seen }{\b end(\~)}{ and }{\b begin(\~)}{, which are the tools for moving forward through a string one element at a time. The reverse iterators }{\b rend(\~)}{ and }{\b rbegin(\~)}{ allow you to step backwards through a string. Here\rquote s how 
they work:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:RevStr.cpp
\par // Print a string in reverse
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par int main() \{
\par   string s("987654321");
\par   // Use this iterator to walk backwards:
\par   string::reverse_iterator rev;
\par }\pard \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \outlinelevel0\adjustright {  // "Incrementing" the reverse iterator moves 
\par   // it to successively lower string elements:
\par }\pard \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright {  for(rev = s.rbegin(); rev != s.rend(); rev++)
\par     cout << *rev << " ";
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The output from }{\b RevStr.cpp}{ looks like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Reverse iterators act like pointers to elements of the string\rquote s character array, }{\i except that when you apply the increment operator to them, they move backward rather than forward}{. }{\b 
rbegin(\~)}{ and }{\b rend(\~)}{ supply string locations that are consistent with this behavior, to wit, }{\b rbegin(\~)}{ locates the position just beyond the end of the string, and }{\b rend(\~)}{
 locates the beginning. Aside from this, the main thing to remember about reverse iterators is that they }{\i aren\rquote t}{ type equivalent to ordinary iterators. For example, if a member function parameter list inc
ludes an iterator as an argument, you can\rquote t substitute a reverse iterator to get the function to perform it\rquote s job walking backward through the string. Here\rquote s an illustration:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {// The compiler won\rquote t accept this
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {string sBackwards(s.rbegin(), s.rend());
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The string constructor won\rquote t accept reverse iterators in place of forward iterators in its parameter list. This is also true of string members such as }{\b copy(\~)}{, }{\b insert(\~),}{ and }{
\b assign(\~).}{
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776314}Strings and character traits{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776314}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {We seem to have worked our way around the margins of case insensitive string comparisons using C++ string objects, so maybe it\rquote s time to ask the obvious question: \ldblquote Why isn\rquote 
t case-insensitive comparison part of the standard }{\b string}{ class ?\rdblquote  The answer provides interesting background on the true nature of C++ string objects. 
\par Consider what it means for a character to have \ldblquote case.\rdblquote  Written Hebrew, Farsi, and Kanji don\rquote 
t use the concept of upper and lower case, so for those languages this idea has no meaning at all. This the first impediment to built-in C++ 
support for case-insensitive character search and comparison: the idea of case sensitivity is not universal, and therefore not portable. 
\par It would seem that if there were a way of designating that some languages were \ldblquote all uppercase\rdblquote  or \ldblquote all lowercase\rdblquote  we could design a generalized solution. However, some languages which employ the concept of 
\ldblquote case\rdblquote  }{\i also}{ change the meaning of particular characters with diacritical marks: the cedilla in Spanish, the circumflex in French, and the umlaut in German. For this rea
son, any case-sensitive collating scheme that attempts to be comprehensive will be nightmarishly complex to use. 
\par Although we usually treat the C++ }{\b string}{ as a class, this is really not the case. }{\b string}{ is a }{\b typedef}{ of a more general constituent, the }{\b basic_string<}{\~}{\b >}{ template. Observe how }{\b string}{
 is declared in the standard C++ header file:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {typedef basic_string<char> string;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To really understand the nature of strings, it\rquote s helpful to delve a bit deeper and look at the template on which it is based. Here\rquote s the declaration of the }{\b basic_string<}{\~}{\b >}{
 template:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {template<class charT,
\par   class traits = char_traits<charT>,
\par   class allocator = allocator<charT> >
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  class basic_string;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Earlier in this book, templates were examined in a great deal of detail. The main thing to notice about the two declarations above are that the }{\b string}{ type is created when the }{\b basic_string
}{ template is instantiated with }{\b char. }{Inside the }{\b basic_string<}{\~}{\b > }{template declaration, the line 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class traits = char_traits<charT>,
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {tells us that the behavior of the class made from the }{\b basic_string<}{\~}{\b > }{template is specified by a class based on the template }{\b char_traits<}{\~}{\b >}{. Thus, the }{\b basic_string<}
{\~}{\b >}{ template provides for cases where you need string oriented classes that manipulate types other than }{\b char}{ (wide characters or unicode, for example). To do this, the }{\b char_traits<}{\~}{\b > }{
template controls the content and collating behaviors of a variety of character sets using the character comparison functions }{\b eq(\~)}{ (equal), }{\b ne(\~)}{ (not equal), and }{\b lt(\~)}{ (less than) upon which the }{\b basic_string<}{\~}{\b > }{
string comparison functions rely.
\par This is why the string class doesn\rquote t include case insensitive member functions: That\rquote s not in its job description. To change the way the string class treats character comparison, you must supply a different of }{\b char_traits<}{\~}{\b >}{
 template, because that defines the behavior of the individual character comparison member functions. 
\par This information can be used to make a new type of }{\b string }{class that ignores case. First, we\rquote ll define a new case insensitive }{\b char_traits<}{\~}{\b >}{ template that inherits the existing one. Next, we\rquote 
ll override only the members we need to change in order to make character-by-character comparison case insensitive. (In addition to the three lexical character comparison members mentioned above, we\rquote ll also have to supply new implementation of }{
\b find(\~)}{ and }{\b compare(\~)}{.) Finally, we\rquote ll }{\b typedef}{ a new class based on }{\b basic_string}{, but using the case insensitive }{\b ichar_traits}{ template for its second argument.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:ichar_traits.h
\par // Creating your own character traits
\par #ifndef ICHAR_TRAITS_H
\par #define ICHAR_TRAITS_H
\par #include <string>
\par #include <cctype>
\par 
\par struct ichar_traits : std::char_traits<char> \{
\par   // We'll only change character by 
\par   // character comparison functions
\par   static bool eq(char c1st, char c2nd) \{
\par     return 
\par       std::toupper(c1st) == std::toupper(c2nd);
\par   \}
\par   static bool ne(char c1st, char c2nd) \{
\par     return 
\par       std::toupper(c1st) != std::toupper(c2nd);
\par   \}
\par   static bool lt(char c1st, char c2nd) \{
\par     return 
\par       std::toupper(c1st) < std::toupper(c2nd);
\par   \}
\par   static int compare(const char* str1, 
\par     const char* str2, size_t n) \{
\par     for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) \{
\par       if(std::tolower(*str1)>std::tolower(*str2))
\par         return 1;
\par       if(std::tolower(*str1)<std::tolower(*str2))
\par         return -1;
\par       if(*str1 == 0 || *str2 == 0)
\par         return 0;
\par       str1++; str2++; // Compare the other chars
\par     \}
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   static const char* find(const char* s1, 
\par     int  n, char c) \{
\par     while(n-- > 0 &&  
\par       std::toupper(*s1) != std::toupper(c))
\par       s1++;
\par     return s1;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // ICHAR_TRAITS_H  ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If we }{\b typedef }{an }{\b istring}{ class like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {typedef basic_string<char, ichar_traits, 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  allocator<char> > istring;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Then this }{\b istring}{ will act like an ordinary }{\b string}{ in every way, except that it will make all comparisons without respect to case. Here\rquote s an example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:ICompare.cpp
\par #include "ichar_traits.h"
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par typedef basic_string<char, ichar_traits, 
\par   allocator<char> > istring;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   // The same letters except for case:
\par   istring first = "tHis";
\par   istring second = "ThIS";
\par   cout << first.compare(second) << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The output from the program is \ldblquote 0\rdblquote , indicating that the strings compare as equal. This is just a simple example \endash  in order to make }{\b istring}{ fully equivalent to }{\b 
string}{, we\rquote d have to create the other functions necessary to support the new }{\b istring}{ type.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776315}A string application{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776315}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {My friend Daniel (who designed the cover and page layout for this book) does a lot of work with Web pages. One tool he uses creates a \ldblquote site map\rdblquote 
 consisting of a Java applet to display the map and an HTML tag that invoked the applet and provided it with the necessary data to create the map. Daniel wanted to use this data to create 
an ordinary HTML page (sans applet) that would contain regular links as the site map. The resulting program turns out to be a nice practical application of the }{\b string}{ class, so it is presented here.
\par The input is an HTML file that contains the usual stuff along with an applet tag with a parameter that begins like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {<param name="source_file" value="
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The rest of the line contains encoded information about the site map, all combined into a single line (it\rquote s rather long, but fortunately }{\b string}{ objects don\rquote t
 care). Each entry may or may not begin with a number of \lquote }{\b #}{\rquote  signs; each of these indicates one level of depth. If no \lquote }{\b #}{\rquote  sign is present the entry will be considered to be at level one. After the \lquote }{\b #}{
\rquote  is the text to be displayed on the page, followed by a \lquote }{\b %}{\rquote  and the URL to use as the link. Each entry is terminated by a \lquote }{\b *}{\rquote . Thus, a single entry in the line might look like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {###|Useful Art%./Build/useful_art.html*
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The \lquote }{\b |}{\rquote  at the beginning is an artifact that needs to be removed.
\par My solution was to create an }{\b Item}{
 class whose constructor would take input text and create an object that contains the text to be displayed, the URL and the level. The objects essentially parse themselves, and at that point you can read any value you want. In }{\b main(\~)}{, the input 
file is opened and read until the line contains the parameter that we\rquote re interested in. Everything but the site map codes are stripped away from this }{\b string}{, and then it is parsed into }{\b Item}{ objects:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C17:SiteMapConvert.cpp
\par // Using strings to create a custom conversion
\par // program that generates HTML output
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Item \{
\par   string id, url;
\par   int depth;
\par   string removeBar(string s) \{
\par     if(s[0] == '|')
\par       return s.substr(1);
\par     else return s;
\par   \}
\par public:
\par   Item(string in, int& index) : depth(0) \{
\par     while(in[index] == '#' && index < in.size())\{
\par       depth++;
\par       index++;
\par     \}
\par     // 0 means no '#' marks were found:
\par     if(depth == 0) depth = 1;
\par     while(in[index] != '%' && index < in.size())
\par       id += in[index++];
\par     id = removeBar(id);
\par     index++; // Move past '%'
\par     while(in[index] != '*' && index < in.size())
\par       url += in[index++];
\par     url = removeBar(url);
\par     index++; // To move past '*'
\par   \}
\par   string identifier() \{ return id; \}
\par   string path() \{ return url; \}
\par   int level() \{ return depth; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1,
\par     "usage: SiteMapConvert inputfilename");
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]);
\par   ofstream out("plainmap.html");
\par   string line;
\par   while(getline(in, line)) \{
\par     if(line.find("<param name=\\"source_file\\"")
\par        != string::npos) \{
\par       // Extract data of from start of sequence
\par       // until the terminating quote mark:
\par       line = line.substr(line.find("value=\\"") 
\par              + string("value=\\"").size());
\par       line = line.substr(0, 
\par                line.find_last_of("\\""));
\par       int index = 0;
\par       while(index < line.size()) \{
\par         Item item(line, index);
\par         string startLevel, endLevel;
\par         if(item.level() == 1) \{
\par           startLevel = "<h1>";
\par           endLevel = "</h1>";
\par         \} else
\par           for(int i = 0; i < item.level(); i++)
\par             for(int j = 0; j < 5; j++)
\par               out << "&nbsp;";
\par         string htmlLine = "<a href=\\""
\par           + item.path() + "\\">"
\par           + item.identifier() + "</a><br>";
\par         out << startLevel << htmlLine 
\par             << endLevel << endl;
\par       \}
\par       break; // Out of while loop
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b Item}{ contains a private member function }{\b removeBar(\~)}{ that is used internally to strip off the leading bars, if they appear.
\par The constructor for }{\b Item}{ initializes }{\b depth}{ to }{\b 0}{ to indicate that no \lquote #\rquote  signs were found yet; if none are found then it is assumed the }{\b Item}{ should be displayed at level one. Each character in the }{\b string}{
 is examined using }{\b operator[\~]}{ to find the }{\b depth}{, }{\b id}{ and }{\b url}{ values. The other member functions simply return these values.
\par After opening the files, }{\b main(\~)}{ uses }{\b string::find(\~)}{ to locate the line containing the site map data. At this point, }{\b substr(\~)}{ is used in order to strip off the information before and after the site map data. The subsequent }{\b 
while}{ loop performs the parsing, but notice that the value }{\b index}{ is passed }{\i by reference}{ into the }{\b Item}{ constructor, and that constructor increments }{\b index}{ as it parses each new }{\b Item}{, thus moving forward in the sequence.

\par If an }{\b Item}{ is at level one, then an HTML }{\b h1}{ tag is used, otherwise the elements are indented using HTML non-breaking spaces. Note in the initialization of }{\b htmlLine}{ how easy it is to construct a }{\b string}{ \endash 
 you can just combine quoted character arrays and other }{\b string}{ objects using }{\b operator+}{.
\par When the output is written to the destination file, }{\b startLevel}{ and }{\b endLevel}{ will only produce results if they have been given any value other than their default initialization values.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776316}Summary{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776316}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {C++ string objects provide developers with a number of great advantages over their C counterparts. For the most part, the }{\b string}{ class makes referring to strin
gs through the use of character pointers unnecessary. This eliminates an entire class of software defects that arise from the use of uninitialized and incorrectly valued pointers. C++ strings dynamically and transparently grow their internal data storage 
s
pace to accommodate increases in the size of the string data. This means that when the data in a string grows beyond the limits of the memory initially allocated to it, the string object will make the memory management calls that take space from and retur
n space to the heap. Consistent allocation schemes prevent memory leaks and have the potential to be much more efficient than \ldblquote roll your own\rdblquote  memory management.
\par The }{\b string}{ class member functions provide a fairly comprehensive set of tools for creating, modifying, and searching in strings. }{\b string}{
 comparisons are always case sensitive, but you can work around this by copying string data to C style null terminated strings and using case insensitive string comparison functions, temporarily converting the data he
ld in sting objects to a single case, or by creating a case insensitive string class which overrides the character traits used to create the }{\b basic_string}{ object.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776317}Exercises{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776317}
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 1. \tab}}\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls236\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls236\adjustright \fs20\lang1024\cgrid {A palindrome is a word or group of words that read the same forward and backward. For example \ldblquote madam\rdblquote  or \ldblquote wow\rdblquote 
. Write a program that takes a string argument from the command line and returns TRUE if the string was a palindrome.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 2. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls114\adjustright {Sometimes the input from a file stream contains a two character sequence to represent a newline. These two chara
cters (0x0a 0x0d) produce extra blank lines when the stream is printed to standard out. Write a program that finds the character 0x0d (ASCII carriage return) and deletes it from the string.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 3. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls114\adjustright {Write a program that reverses the order of the characters in a string.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\sect }\sectd \margtsxn2707\margbsxn2707\sbkodd\linex0\headery0\footery1440\colsx432\endnhere\titlepg\sectdefaultcl \pard\plain 
\s1\fi-1080\li990\sa360\sl240\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \cbpat8 \f4\fs96\cf1\kerning18\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628662}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373873}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776318}
18: Iostreams{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628662}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373873}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776318}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {So far in this book we\rquote 
ve used the old reliable C standard I/O library, a perfect example of a library that begs to be turned into a class.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In fact, there\rquote s much more you can do with the general I/O problem than just take standard I/O and turn it into a class. Wouldn\rquote 
t it be nice if you could make all the usual \ldblquote receptacles\rdblquote  \endash  standard I/O, files and even blocks of memory \endash  look the same, so you need to remember only one interface? That\rquote s the idea behind iostreams. They\rquote 
re much easier, safer, and often more efficient than the assorted functions from the Standard C stdio library.
\par Iostream is usually the first class library that new C++ programmers learn to use. This chapter explores the }{\i use}{ of iostreams, so they can replace the C I/O functions through the rest of the book. In future chapters, you\rquote 
ll see how to set up your own classes so they\rquote re compatible with iostreams.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628663}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373874}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776319
}Why iostreams?{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628663}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373874}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776319}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You may wonder what\rquote s wrong with the good old C library. And why not \ldblquote wrap\rdblquote  the C library in a class }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {wrapping, class}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {class\: wrapping}}}{
and be done with it? Indeed, there are situations when this is the perfect thing to do, when you want to make a C library a bit safer and easier to use. For example, suppose you want to make sure a stdio }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {stdio}}}{file is always safely opened and properly closed, without relying on the user to remember to call the }{\b close(\~)}{ function:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C18:FileClass.h
\par // Stdio files wrapped
\par #ifndef FILECLAS_H
\par #define FILECLAS_H
\par #include <cstdio>
\par 
\par class FileClass \{
\par   std::FILE* f;
\par public:
\par   FileClass(const char* fname, const char* mode="r");
\par   ~FileClass();
\par   std::FILE* fp();
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // FILECLAS_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In C when you perform file I/O, you work with a naked pointer to a FILE }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {FILE, stdio}}}{\b struct}{, but this class wraps around the 
pointer and guarantees it is properly initialized and cleaned up using the constructor and destructor. The second constructor argument is the file mode, which defaults to \ldblquote r\rdblquote  for \ldblquote read.\rdblquote 
\par To fetch the value of the pointer to use in the file I/O functions, you use the }{\b fp(\~)}{ access function. Here are the member function definitions:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C18:FileClass.cpp \{O\}
\par // Implementation
\par #include "FileClass.h"
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par FileClass::FileClass(const char* fname, const char* mode)\{
\par   f = fopen(fname, mode);
\par   if(f == NULL) \{
\par     printf("%s: file not found\\n", fname);
\par     exit(1);
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par FileClass::~FileClass() \{ fclose(f); \}
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {FILE* FileClass::fp() \{ return f; \} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
