\par This is not a terrible solution by any means. It\rquote s quite functional, and you could imagine making similar classes for standard (console) I/O and for in-core formatting (reading/writing a piece of memory rather than a file or the console).
\par The big stumbling block is the runtime interpreter}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {printf(\~)\: run-time interpreter}}}{ }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time interpreter for printf(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {interpreter, printf(
\~) run-time}}}{used for the variable-argument list functions. This is the code that parses through your format string at runtime and grabs and interprets arguments from the variable argument list. It\rquote s a problem for four reasons.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 1. \tab}}\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls226\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls226\adjustright \fs20\lang1024\cgrid {Even if you use only a fraction of the functionality of the interpreter, the whole thing gets loaded. So if you say:\line }{\f2 printf("%c", 'x');}{\line you\rquote 
ll get the whole package, including the parts that print out floating-point numbers and strings. There\rquote s no option for reducing the amount of space used by the program.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 2. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {Because the interpretation happens at runtime there\rquote s a performance overhead you can\rquote t get rid of. It\rquote s frustrating because all the information is }{\i there}{
 in the format string at compile time, but it\rquote s not evaluated until runtime. However, if you could parse the arguments in the format string at comp
ile time you could make hard function calls that have the potential to be much faster than a runtime interpreter (although the }{\b printf(\~)}{ family of functions is usually quite well optimized).
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 3. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {A worse problem occurs because the evaluation of the format string doesn\rquote t happen until runtime: there can be no compile-time error checking}{\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls7\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {compile time\: error checking}}}{\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0
\pndec }\ls7\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {error\: compile-time checking}}}{. You\rquote re probably very familiar with this problem if you\rquote ve tried to find bugs that came from using the wrong number or type of arguments in a }{\b 
printf(\~)}{ statement. C++ makes a big deal out of compile-time error checking to find errors early and make your life easier. It seems a shame to throw it away for an I/O library, especially because I/O is used a lot.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 4. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {For C++, the most important problem is that the }{\b printf(\~)}{ family of functions is not particularly extensible}{\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls7\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {extensible program}}}{. They\rquote re really designed to handle the four basic data types }{\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls7\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {data\: C data types}}}{in C }{\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls7\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {C\: basic data types}}}{(}{\b char}{, }{\b int}{, }{\b float}{, }{\b double}{
 and their variations). You might think that every time you add a new class, you could add an overloaded }{\b printf(\~)}{ and }{\b scanf(\~)}{
 function (and their variants for files and strings) but remember, overloaded functions must have different types in their argument lists and the }{\b printf(\~)}{ family hides its type information in the format string and in the variable argument list}
{\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls7\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {variable\: variable argument list}}}{\pard\plain 
\s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls7\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {arguments\: variable argument list}}}{. For a language like C++, whose goal is to be ab
le to easily add new data types, this is an ungainly restriction.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628664}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373876}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776321
