, }{\b eof(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {eof(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: eof(}{
\b \~}{)}}}{, }{\b fail(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {fail(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
iostreams\: fail(\~)}}}{, and }{\b bad(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {bad(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: bad(\~)}}}{. These return state information based on the }{\b eofbit}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {eofbit}}}{ }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: eofbit}}}{(indicates the buffer is at the end of sequence), the }{\b failbit}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {failbit}}}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: failbit}}}{(indicates s
ome operation has failed because of formatting issues or some other problem that does not affect the buffer) and the }{\b badbit}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {badbit}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: badbit}}}{ (indicates something has gone wrong with the buffer).
\par However, as mentioned earlier, the state of an input stream generally gets corrupted in weird ways only when you\rquote 
re trying to do input to specific types and the type read from the input is inconsistent with what is expected. Then of course you have the problem of what to do with the input stream 
to correct the problem. If you follow my advice and read input a line at a time or as a big glob of characters (with }{\b read(\~)}{) and don\rquote t attempt to use the input formatting functions except in simple cases, then all you\rquote 
re concerned with is whether you\rquote re at the end of the input (EOF). Fortunately, testing for this turns out to be simple and can be done inside of conditionals, such as }{\b while(cin)}{ or }{\b if(cin)}{. For now you\rquote 
ll have to accept that when you use an input stream object in this context, the right value is safely, correctly and magically produced to indicate whether the object has reached the end of the input. You can also use the Boolean NOT operator }{\b !}{
, as in }{\b if(!cin)}{, to indicate the stream is }{\i not}{ OK; that is, you\rquote ve probably reached the end of input and should quit trying to read the stream.
\par There are times when the stream becomes not-OK, but you understand this condition and want to go on using it. For example, if you reach the end of an input file, the }{\b eofbit}{ and }{\b failbit}{ are set, so a conditional on
 that stream object will indicate the stream is no longer good. However, you may want to continue using the file, by seeking to an earlier position and reading more data. To correct the condition, simply call the }{\b clear(\~)}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {clear(\~)}}}{ member function.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ Newer implementations of iostreams will still support this style of handling errors, but in some cases will also throw exceptions.}}}{
