\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Manipulating files with iostreams is much easier and safer than using }{\b cstdio }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {STDIO.H}}}{in C. All you do to open a file is create an object; the constructor does the work. You don\rquote t have to explicitly close a file (although you can, using the }{\b close(\~)}{
 member function) because the destructor will close it when the object goes out of scope.
\par To create a file that defaults to input, make an }{\b ifstream}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ifstream}}}{object. To create one that defaults to output, make an }{\b ofstream}{ }
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ofstream}}}{object.
\par Here\rquote s an example that shows many of the features discussed so far. Note the inclusion of }{\b <fstream> }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {FSTREAM.H}}}{
to declare the file I/O classes; this also includes }{\b <iostream>}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {IOSTREAM.H}}}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C18:Strfile.cpp
\par // Stream I/O with files
\par // The difference between get() & getline()
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <fstream>  
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   const int sz = 100; // Buffer size;
\par   char buf[sz];
\par   \{
\par     ifstream in("Strfile.cpp"); // Read
\par     assure(in, "Strfile.cpp"); // Verify open
\par     ofstream out("Strfile.out"); // Write
\par     assure(out, "Strfile.out");
\par     int i = 1; // Line counter
\par 
\par     // A less-convenient approach for line input:
\par     while(in.get(buf, sz)) \{ // Leaves \\n in input
\par       in.get(); // Throw away next character (\\n)
\par       cout << buf << endl; // Must add \\n
\par       // File output just like standard I/O:
\par       out << i++ << ": " << buf << endl;
\par     \}
\par   \} // Destructors close in & out
\par 
\par   ifstream in("Strfile.out");
\par   assure(in, "Strfile.out");
\par   // More convenient line input:
\par   while(in.getline(buf, sz)) \{ // Removes \\n
\par     char* cp = buf;
\par     while(*cp != ':')
\par       cp++;
\par     cp += 2; // Past ": "
\par     cout << cp << endl; // Must still add \\n
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The creation of both the }{\b ifstream}{ and }{\b ofstream}{ are followed by an }{\b assure(\~)}{
 to guarantee the file has been successfully opened. Here again the object, used in a situation where the compiler expects an integral result, produces a value that indicates success or failure. (To do this, an automatic typ
e conversion member function is called. These are discussed in Chapter XX.)
\par The first }{\b while}{ loop demonstrates the use of two forms of the }{\b get(\~)}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {get(\~)}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: get(\~)}}}{function. The first gets characters into a buffer and puts a zero terminator in the buffer when either }{\b sz \endash  1}{
 characters have been read or the third argument (defaulted to }{\b \lquote \\n\rquote }{) is encountered. }{\b get(\~)}{ leaves the terminator character in the input stream, so this terminator must be thrown away via }{\b in.get(\~)}{ using the form of }
{\b get(\~)}{ with no argument, which fetches a single byte and returns it as an }{\b int}{. You can also use the }{\b ignore(\~)}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ignore(\~)}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: ignore(\~)}}}{member function, which has two defaulted arguments. The first is the number of characters to throw away, and
 defaults to one. The second is the character at which the }{\b ignore(\~)}{ function quits (after extracting it) and defaults to EOF.
\par Next you see two output statements that look very similar: one to }{\b cout }{and one to the file }{\b out}{. Notice the convenience here; you don\rquote t need to worry about what kind of object you\rquote 
re dealing with because the formatting statements work the same with all }{\b ostream}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ostream}}}{
objects. The first one echoes the line to standard output, and the second writes the line out to the new file and includes a line number.
\par To demonstrate }{\b getline(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {getline(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe 
{iostreams\: getline(\~)}}}{, it\rquote s interesting to open the file we just created and strip off the line numbers. To ensure the file is properly closed before opening it to read, you have t
wo choices. You can surround the first part of the program in braces to force the }{\b out}{ object out of scope, thus calling the destructor and closing the file, which is done here. You can also call }{\b close(\~)}{
 for both files; if you want, you can even reuse the }{\b in}{ object by calling the }{\b open(\~)}{ member function (you can also create and destroy the object dynamically on the heap as is in Chapter XX).
\par The second }{\b while}{ loop shows how }{\b getline(\~)}{ removes the terminator character (its third argument, which defaults to }{\b \lquote \\n\rquote }{) from the input stream when it\rquote s encountered. Although }{\b getline(\~)}{, like }{\b get(\~
)}{, puts a zero in the buffer, it still doesn\rquote t insert the terminating character.
