\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {buffering, iostream}}}{Whenever you create a new class, you should endeavor to hide the details of the underlying implement
ation as possible from the user of the class. Try to show them only what they need to know and make the rest }{\b private }{to avoid confusion. Normally when using iostreams you don\rquote 
t know or care where the bytes are being produced or consumed; indeed, this is different depending on whether you\rquote re dealing with standard I/O, files, memory, or some newly created class or device.
\par There comes a time, however, when it becomes important to be able to send messages to the part of the iostream that produces and consumes b
ytes. To provide this part with a common interface and still hide its underlying implementation, it is abstracted into its own class, called }{\b streambuf}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {streambuf}}}{. Each iostream object contains a pointer to some kind of }{\b streambuf}{. (The kind depends on whether it deals with standard I/O, files, memory, etc.) You can access the }{\b streambuf }{
directly; for example, you can move raw bytes into and out of the }{\b streambuf}{, without formatting them through the enclosing iostream. This is accomplished, of course, by calling member functions for the }{\b streambuf}{ object.
\par Currently, the most important thing for you to know is that every iostream object contains a pointer to a }{\b streambuf}{ object, and the }{\b streambuf}{ has some member functions you can call if you need to.
\par To allow you to access the }{\b streambuf}{, every iostream object has a member function called }{\b rdbuf(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {rdbuf(\~)}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: rdbuf(\~)}}}{ that returns the pointer to the object\rquote s }{\b streambuf}{. This way you can call any member function for the underlying }{\b 
streambuf}{. However, one of the most interesting things you can do with the }{\b streambuf}{ pointer is to connect it to another iostream object using the }{\b <<}{
 operator. This drains all the bytes from your object into the one on the left-hand side of the }{\b <<}{. This means if you want to move all the bytes from one iostream to another, you don\rquote 
t have to go through the tedium (and potential coding errors) of reading them one byte or one line at a time. It\rquote s a much more elegant approach.
\par For example, here\rquote s a very simple program that opens a file and sends the contents out to standard output (similar to the previous example):
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C18:Stype.cpp
\par // Type a file to standard output
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1); // Must have a command line
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]); // Ensure file exists
\par   cout << in.rdbuf(); // Outputs entire file
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {After making sure there is an argument on the command line, an }{\b ifstream}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {ifstream}}}{is created using this argument. The open will fail if the file doesn\rquote t exist, and this failure is caught by the }{\b assert(in)}{.
\par All the work really happens in the statement
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {cout << in.rdbuf();
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {which causes the entire contents of the file to be sent to }{\b cout}{
. This is not only more succinct to code, it is often more efficient than moving the bytes one at a time.
