ve seen, zero-argument manipulators are quite easy to create. But what if you want to create a manipulator that takes arguments? The iostream library has a rather convoluted and confusing way to do this, but Jerry Schwarz}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Schwarz, Jerry}}}{, the creator of the iostream library, suggests}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ In a private conversation.}}}{ a scheme he calls }{\i effectors}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: effectors}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {effectors}}}{. An effector is a simple class whose c
onstructor performs the desired operation, along with an overloaded }{\b operator<<}{ that works with the class. Here\rquote s an example with two effectors. The first outputs a truncated character string, and the second prints a number in binary }
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: binary printing}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {binary\:
 printing}}}{(the process of defining an overloaded }{\b operator<<}{ will not be discussed until Chapter XX):
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628671}//: C18:Effector.txt
\par // (Should be "cpp" but I can't get it to compile with
\par // My windows compilers, so making it a txt file will
\par // keep it out of the makefile for the time being)
\par // Jerry Schwarz's "effectors"
\par #include<iostream>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <climits> // ULONG_MAX
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par // Put out a portion of a string:
\par class Fixw \{
\par   string str;
\par public:
\par   Fixw(const string& s, int width)
\par     : str(s, 0, width) \{\}
\par   friend ostream& 
\par   operator<<(ostream& os, Fixw& fw) \{
\par     return os << fw.str;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par typedef unsigned long ulong;
\par 
\par // Print a number in binary:
\par class Bin \{
\par   ulong n;
\par public:
\par   Bin(ulong nn) \{ n = nn; \}
\par   friend ostream& operator<<(ostream&, Bin&);
\par \};
\par 
\par ostream& operator<<(ostream& os, Bin& b) \{
\par   ulong bit = ~(ULONG_MAX >> 1); // Top bit set
\par   while(bit) \{
\par     os << (b.n & bit ? '1' : '0');
\par     bit >>= 1;
\par   \}
\par   return os;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   char* string =
\par     "Things that make us happy, make us wise";
\par   for(int i = 1; i <= strlen(string); i++)
\par     cout << Fixw(string, i) << endl;
\par   ulong x = 0xCAFEBABEUL;
\par   ulong y = 0x76543210UL;
\par   cout << "x in binary: " << Bin(x) << endl;
\par   cout << "y in binary: " << Bin(y) << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The constructor for }{\b Fixw}{ creates a shortened copy of its }{\b char*}{
 argument, and the destructor releases the memory created for this copy. The overloaded }{\b operator<<}{ takes the contents of its second argument, the }{\b Fixw}{ object, and inserts it into the first argument, the }{\b ostream}{, then returns the }{\b 
ostream}{ so it can be used in a chained expression. When you use }{\b Fixw}{ in an expression like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {cout << Fixw(string, i) << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
