 function, which takes the number of seconds and returns a pointer to the resulting }{\b tm}{. This }{\b tm}{, however, is a }{\b static}{ structure inside the }{\b localtime(\~)}{ function, which is rewritten every time }{\b localtime(\~)}{
 is called. To copy the contents into the }{\b tm}{ }{\b struct}{ inside }{\b DataPoint}{, you might think you must copy each element individually. However, all you must do is a structure assignment, and the compiler will take care of the rest.
 This means the right-hand side must be a structure, not a pointer, so the result of }{\b localtime(\~)}{ is dereferenced. The desired result is achieved with
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {d.setTime(*localtime(&timer));
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
