\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To check the validity of the data stored in binary format, it is read from the disk and
 put in text form in DATA2.TXT, so that file can be compared to DATA.TXT for verification. In the following program, you can see how simple this data recovery is. After the test file is created, the records are read at the command of the user.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C18:Datascan.cpp
\par //\{L\} Datalog
\par // Verify and view logged data
\par #include "DataLogger.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <strstream>
\par #include <iomanip>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   ifstream bindata("data.bin", ios::binary);
\par   assure(bindata, "data.bin");
\par   // Create comparison file to verify data.txt:
\par   ofstream verify("data2.txt");
\par   assure(verify, "data2.txt");
\par   DataPoint d;
\par   while(bindata.read(
\par     (unsigned char*)&d, sizeof d))
\par     d.print(verify);
\par   bindata.clear(); // Reset state to "good"
\par   // Display user-selected records:
\par   int recnum = 0;
\par   // Left-align everything:
\par   cout.setf(ios::left, ios::adjustfield);
\par   // Fixed precision of 4 decimal places:
\par   cout.setf(ios::fixed, ios::floatfield);
\par   cout.precision(4);
\par   for(;;) \{
\par     bindata.seekg(recnum* sizeof d, ios::beg);
\par     cout << "record " << recnum << endl;
\par     if(bindata.read(
\par       (unsigned char*)&d, sizeof d)) \{
\par       cout << asctime(&(d.getTime()));
\par       cout << setw(11) << "Latitude"
\par            << setw(11) << "Longitude"
\par            << setw(10) << "Depth"
\par            << setw(12) << "Temperature"
\par            << endl;
\par       // Put a line after the description:
\par       cout << setfill('-') << setw(43) << '-'
\par            << setfill(' ') << endl;
\par       cout << setw(11) << d.getLatitude()
\par            << setw(11) << d.getLongitude()
\par            << setw(10) << d.getDepth()
\par            << setw(12) << d.getTemperature()
\par            << endl;
\par     \} else \{
\par       cout << "invalid record number" << endl;
\par       bindata.clear(); // Reset state to "good"
\par     \}
\par     cout << endl
\par       << "enter record number, x to quit:";
\par     char buf[10];
\par     cin.getline(buf, 10);
\par     if(buf[0] == 'x') break;
\par     istrstream input(buf, 10);
\par     input >> recnum;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b ifstream}{ }{\b bindata}{ is created from DATA.BIN as a binary file, with the }{\b ios::nocreate}{ flag on to cause the }{\b assert(\~)}{
 to fail if the file doesn\rquote t exist. The }{\b read(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: read(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {read(\~)}}}{ statement reads a single record and places it directly into the }{\b DataPoint d}{. (Again, if }{\b DataPoint}{ contained pointers this would result in meaningless pointer values.) This }{\b read(\~)}{
 action will set }{\b bindata}{\rquote s }{\b failbit}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: failbit}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec 
}\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {failbit}}}{when the end of the file is reached, which will cause the }{\b while}{ statement to fail. At this point, however, you can\rquote t move the get pointer}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: get pointer}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {get pointer}}}{
 back and read more records because the state of the stream won\rquote t allow further reads. So the }{\b clear(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iostreams\: clear(\~)}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {clear(\~)}}}{ function is called to reset the }{\b failbit}{.
\par Once the record is read in from disk, you can do anything you want with it, such as perform calculations or make graphs. Here, it is displayed to further exercise your knowledge of iostream formatting.
\par The rest of the program displays a record number (represented by }{\b recnum}{) selected by the user. As before, the precision is fixed at four decimal places, but this time everything is left justified.
\par The formatting of this output looks different from before:
