\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To make sure the labels and the data columns line up, the labels are put in the same width fields as the columns, using }{\b setw(\~)}{
. The line in between is generated by setting the fill character to \lquote -\rquote , the width to the desired line width, and outputting a single \lquote -\rquote .
\par If the }{\b read(\~)}{ fails, you\rquote ll end up in the }{\b else}{ part, which tells the user the record number was invalid. Then, because the }{\b failbit}{ was set, it must be reset with a call to }{\b clear(\~)}{ so the next }{\b read(\~)}{
 is successful (assuming it\rquote s in the right range).
\par Of course, you can also open the binary data file for writing as well as reading. This way you can retriev
e the records, modify them, and write them back to the same location, thus creating a flat-file database management system. In my very first programming job, I also had to create a flat-file DBMS \endash 
 but using BASIC on an Apple II. It took months, while this took minutes. Of course, it might make more sense to use a packaged DBMS now, but with C++ and iostreams you can still do all the low-level operations that are necessary in a lab.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628672}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373900}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776346}
Counting editor{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776346}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Often you have some editing task where you must go through 
and sequentially number something, but all the other text is duplicated. I encountered this problem when pasting digital photos into a Web page \endash 
 I got the formatting just right, then duplicated it, then had the problem of incrementing the photo number for each one. So I replaced the photo number with XXX, duplicated that, and wrote the following program to find and replace the \ldblquote XXX
\rdblquote  with an incremented count. Notice the formatting, so the value will be \ldblquote 001,\rdblquote  \ldblquote 002,\rdblquote  etc.:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C18:NumberPhotos.cpp
\par // Find the marker "XXX" and replace it with an
\par // incrementing number whereever it appears. Used
\par // to help format a web page with photos in it
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <sstream>
\par #include <iomanip>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 2);
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]);
\par   ofstream out(argv[2]);
\par   assure(out, argv[2]);
\par   string line;
\par   int counter = 1;
\par   while(getline(in, line)) \{
\par     int xxx = line.find("XXX");
\par     if(xxx != string::npos) \{
\par       ostringstream cntr;
\par       cntr << setfill('0') << setw(3) << counter++;
\par       line.replace(xxx, 3, cntr.str());
\par     \}
\par     out << line << endl;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776347}Breaking up big files{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776347}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This program was created to break up big files
 into smaller ones, in particular so they could be more easily downloaded from an Internet server (since hangups sometimes occur, this allows someone to download a file a piece at a time and then re-assemble it at the client end). You\rquote 
ll note that the program also creates a reassembly batch file for DOS (where it is messier), whereas under Linux/Unix you simply say something like \ldblquote }{\b cat *piece* > destination.file}{\rdblquote .
\par This program reads the entire file into memory, which of course relies on having a 32-bit opera
ting system with virtual memory for big files. It then pieces it out in chunks to the smaller files, generating the names as it goes. Of course, you can come up with a possibly more reasonable strategy that reads a chunk, creates a file, reads another chu
nk, etc.
\par Note that this program can be run on the server, so you only have to download the big file once and then break it up once it\rquote s on the server.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C18:Breakup.cpp
\par // Breaks a file up into smaller files for 
\par // easier downloads
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <iomanip>
\par #include <strstream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1);
\par   ifstream in(argv[1], ios::binary);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]);
\par   in.seekg(0, ios::end); // End of file
\par   long fileSize = in.tellg(); // Size of file
\par   cout << "file size = " << fileSize << endl;
\par   in.seekg(0, ios::beg); // Start of file
\par   char* fbuf = new char[fileSize];
\par   require(fbuf != 0);
\par   in.read(fbuf, fileSize);
\par   in.close();
\par   string infile(argv[1]);
\par   int dot = infile.find('.');
\par   while(dot != string::npos) \{
\par     infile.replace(dot, 1, "-");
\par     dot = infile.find('.');
\par   \}
\par   string batchName(
\par     "DOSAssemble" + infile + ".bat");
\par   ofstream batchFile(batchName.c_str());
\par   batchFile << "copy /b ";
\par   int filecount = 0;
\par   const int sbufsz = 128;
\par   char sbuf[sbufsz];
\par   const long pieceSize = 1000L * 100L;
\par   long byteCounter = 0;
\par   while(byteCounter < fileSize) \{
\par     ostrstream name(sbuf, sbufsz);
\par     name << argv[1] << "-part" << setfill('0') 
\par       << setw(2) << filecount++ << ends;
\par     cout << "creating " << sbuf << endl;
\par     if(filecount > 1) 
\par       batchFile << "+";
\par     batchFile << sbuf;
\par     ofstream out(sbuf, ios::out | ios::binary);
\par     assure(out, sbuf);
\par     long byteq;
\par     if(byteCounter + pieceSize < fileSize)
\par       byteq = pieceSize;
\par     else
\par       byteq = fileSize - byteCounter;
\par     out.write(fbuf + byteCounter, byteq);
\par     cout << "wrote " << byteq << " bytes, ";
\par     byteCounter += byteq;
\par     out.close();
\par     cout << "ByteCounter = " << byteCounter 
\par       << ", fileSize = " << fileSize << endl;
\par   \}
\par   batchFile << " " << argv[1] << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
