19: Templates in depth{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776350}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart AdvancedTemplatesChapter}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776351}{\*\bkmkend AdvancedTemplatesChapter}Nontype template a
rguments{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776351}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here is a random number generator class that always produces a unique number and overloads }{\b operator(\~)}{ to produce a familiar function-call syntax:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:Urand.h
\par // Unique random number generator
\par #ifndef URAND_H
\par #define URAND_H
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par 
\par template<int upperBound>
\par class Urand \{
\par   int used[upperBound];
\par   bool recycle;
\par public:
\par   Urand(bool recycle = false);
\par   int operator()(); // The "generator" function
\par \};
\par 
\par template<int upperBound>
\par Urand<upperBound>::Urand(bool recyc) 
\par   : recycle(recyc) \{
\par   memset(used, 0, upperBound * sizeof(int));
\par   srand(time(0)); // Seed random number generator
\par \}
\par 
\par template<int upperBound>
\par int Urand<upperBound>::operator()() \{
\par   if(!memchr(used, 0, upperBound)) \{
\par     if(recycle)
\par       memset(used,0,sizeof(used) * sizeof(int));
\par     else
\par       return -1; // No more spaces left
\par   \}
\par   int newval;
\par   while(used[newval = rand() % upperBound])
\par     ; // Until unique value is found
\par   used[newval]++; // Set flag
\par   return newval;
\par \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // URAND_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The uniqueness of }{\b Urand}{ is produced by keeping a map of all the numbers possible in the random space (the upper bound is set with the template argument) and marking each one off as it\rquote 
s used. The optional second constructor argument allows you to reuse the numbers once they\rquote re all used up. Notice that this implementation is optimized for speed by allocating the entire map, regardless of how many numbers you\rquote 
re going to need. If you want to optimize for size, you can change the underlying implementation so it allocate
s storage for the map dynamically and puts the random numbers themselves in the map rather than flags. Notice that this change in implementation will not affect any client code.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776352}Default template arguments{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776352}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776353}The typename keyword{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776353}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Consider the following:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:TypenamedID.cpp
\par // Using 'typename' to say it's a type, 
\par // and not something other than a type
\par 
\par template<class T> class X \{
\par   // Without typename, you should get an error:
\par   typename T::id i;
\par public:
\par   void f() \{ i.g(); \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Y \{
\par public:
\par   class id \{
\par   public:
\par     void g() \{\}
\par   \};
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Y y;
\par   X<Y> xy;
\par   xy.f();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The template definition assumes that the class }{\b T}{ that you hand it must have a nested identifier of some kind called }{\b id}{. But }{\b id}{ could be a member object of }{\b T}{, in which 
case you can perform operations on }{\b id }{directly, but you couldn\rquote t \ldblquote create an object\rdblquote  of \ldblquote the type }{\b id}{.\rdblquote  However, that\rquote s exactly what is happening here: the identifier }{\b id }{
is being treated as if it were actually a nested type inside }{\b T}{. In the case of class }{\b Y}{, }{\b id}{ is in fact a nested type, but (without the }{\b typename }{keyword) the compiler can\rquote t know that when it\rquote s compiling }{\b X}{.

\par If, when it sees an identifier in a template, the compiler has the option of treating that identifier as a type or as something other than a
 type, then it will assume that the identifier refers to something other than a type. That is, it will assume that the identifier refers to an object (including variables of primitive types), an enumeration or soemthing similar. However, it will not 
\endash  cannot \endash  just assume that it is a type. Thus, the compiler gets confused when we pretend it\rquote s a type.
\par The }{\b typename}{ keyword tells the compiler to interpret a particular name as a type. It must be used for a name that:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls245\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls245\adjustright {
Is a qualified name, one that is nested within another type.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls245\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls245\adjustright {
Depends on a template argument. That is, a template argument is somehow involved in the name. The template argument causes the ambiguity when the compiler makes the simplest assumption: that the name refers to something other than a type.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {Because the default behavior of the compiler is to assume that a name that fits the above two points is not a type, you must use }{\b typename}{
 even in places where you think that the compiler ought to be able to figure out the right way to interpret the name on its own. In the above example, when the compiler sees }{\b T::id}{, it knows (because of the }{\b typename}{ keyword) that }{\b id}{
 refers to a nested type and thus it can create an object of that type.
\par The short version of the rule is: if your type is a qualified name that involves a template argument, you must use }{\b typename}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776354}Typedefing a typename{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776354}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b typename}{ keyword does not automatically create a }{\b typedef}{. A line which reads:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {typename Seq::iterator It;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {causes a variable to be declared of type }{\b Seq::iterator}{. If you mean to make a }{\b typedef}{, you must say:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {typedef typename Seq::iterator It;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776355}Using }{\b typename}{ instead of }{\b class}{{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776355}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {With the introduction of the }{\b typename}{ keyword, you now have the option of using }{\b typename}{ instead of }{\b class}{ in the template argument list of a template definit
ion. This may produce code which is clearer:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:UsingTypename.cpp \{O\}
\par // Using 'typename' in the template argument list
\par 
\par template<typename T> class X \{
\par   typename T::id i;
\par public:
\par   void f() \{ i.g(); \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}; ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You\rquote ll probably see a great deal of code which does not use }{\b typename}{ in this fashion, since the keyword was added to the language a relativly long time after templates were introduced.

\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593282}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628754}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374089}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776356}
Function templates{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593282}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628754}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374089}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776356}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: function templates}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {template\: function templates}}}{
A class template describes an infinite set of classes, and the most common place you\rquote 
ll see templates is with classes. However, C++ also supports the concept of an infinite set of functions, which is sometimes useful. The syntax is virtually identical, except that you create a function instead of a class.
\par The clue that you should create a function template is, as you might suspect, if you find you\rquote re creating a number of functions that look identical except that they are dealing with different types. The classic example of a fu
nction template is a sorting function.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ See }{\i C++ Inside & Out}{
 (Osborne/McGraw-Hill, 1993) by the author, Chapter 10.}}}{ However, a function template is useful in all sorts of places, as demonstrated in the first example that follows. The second example shows a function template used with containers and iterators. 

\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374090}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776357}A memory allocation system{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374090}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776357}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {memory\: a memory allocation system}}}{There\rquote s a few things you can do to make the raw memory allocation routines }{\b malloc(\~)}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {malloc(\~)}}}{, }{\b calloc(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {calloc(\~)}}}{ and }{\b realloc(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
realloc(\~)}}}{ safer. The following function template produces one function }{\b getmem(\~)}{ that either allocates a new piece of memory or resizes an existing piece (like }{\b realloc(\~)}{). In addition, it zeroes }{\i only the new memory}{
, and it checks to see that the memory is successfully allocated. Also, you only tell it the number of elements of the type you want, not the number of bytes, so the possibility of a programmer error is reduced. Here\rquote s the header file:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:Getmem.h
\par // Function template for memory
\par #ifndef GETMEM_H
\par #define GETMEM_H
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <cstring>
\par 
\par template<class T>
\par void getmem(T*& oldmem, int elems) \{
\par   typedef int cntr; // Type of element counter
\par   const int csz = sizeof(cntr); // And size
\par   const int tsz = sizeof(T);
\par   if(elems == 0) \{
\par     free(&(((cntr*)oldmem)[-1]));
\par     return;
\par   \}
\par   T* p = oldmem;
\par   cntr oldcount = 0;
\par   if(p) \{ // Previously allocated memory
\par     // Old style:
\par     // ((cntr*)p)--; // Back up by one cntr
\par     // New style:
\par     cntr* tmp = reinterpret_cast<cntr*>(p);
\par     p = reinterpret_cast<T*>(--tmp);
\par     oldcount = *(cntr*)p; // Previous # elems
\par   \}
\par   T* m = (T*)realloc(p, elems * tsz + csz);
\par   require(m != 0);
\par   *((cntr*)m) = elems; // Keep track of count
\par   const cntr increment = elems - oldcount;
\par   if(increment > 0) \{
\par     // Starting address of data:
\par     long startadr = (long)&(m[oldcount]);
\par     startadr += csz;
\par     // Zero the additional new memory:
\par     memset((void*)startadr, 0, increment * tsz);
\par   \}
\par   // Return the address beyond the count:
\par   oldmem = (T*)&(((cntr*)m)[1]);
\par \}
\par 
\par template<class T>
\par inline void freemem(T * m) \{ getmem(m, 0); \}
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // GETMEM_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To be able to zero only the new memory, a counter indicating the number of elements allocated is attached to the beginning of each block of memory. The }{\b typedef cntr}{ is the type of this coun
ter; it allows you to change from }{\b int}{ to }{\b long}{ if you need to handle larger chunks (other issues come up when using }{\b long}{, however \endash  these are seen in compiler warnings).
\par A pointer reference is used for the argument }{\b oldmem}{ because the outside variable (a pointer) must be changed to point to the new block of memory. }{\b oldmem}{ must point to zero (to allocate new memory) or to an existing block of memory }{\i 
that was created with }{\b getmem(\~)}{. This function assumes you\rquote re using it properly, but for debugging you could add an additional tag next to the counter containing an identifier, and check that identifier in }{\b getmem(\~)}{
 to help discover incorrect calls.
\par If the number of elements requested is zero, the storage is freed. There\rquote s an additional function template }{\b freemem(\~)}{ that aliases this behavior.
\par You\rquote ll notice that }{\b getmem(\~)}{ is very low-level \endash  there are lots of casts and byte manipulations. For example, the }{\b oldmem}{ pointer doesn\rquote t point to the true beginning of the memory block, but just }{\i past}{
 the beginning to allow for the counter. So to }{\b free(\~)}{ the memory block, }{\b getmem(\~)}{ must back up the pointer by the amount of space occupied by }{\b cntr}{. Because }{\b oldmem}{ is a }{\b T*}{, it must first be cast to a }{\b cntr*}{
, then indexed backwards one place. Finally the address of that location is produced for }{\b free(\~)}{ in the expression:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {free(&(((cntr*)oldmem)[-1]));
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Similarly, if this is previously allocated memory, }{\b getmem(\~)}{ must back up by one }{\b cntr}{
 size to get the true starting address of the memory, and then extract the previous number of elements. The true starting address is required inside }{\b realloc(\~)}{
. If the storage size is being increased, then the difference between the new number of elements and the old number is used to calculate the starting address and the amount of memory to zero in }{\b memset(\~)}{
. Finally, the address beyond the count is produced to assign to }{\b oldmem}{ in the statement:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {oldmem = (T*)&(((cntr*)m)[1]);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Again, because }{\b oldmem}{ is a reference to a pointer, this has the effect of changing the outside argument passed to }{\b getmem(\~)}{.
\par Here\rquote s a program to test }{\b getmem(\~)}{. It allocates storage and fills it up with values, then increases that amount of storage:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:Getmem.cpp
\par // Test memory function template
\par #include "Getmem.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int* p = 0;
\par   getmem(p, 10);
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++) \{
\par     cout << p[i] << ' ';
\par     p[i] = i;
\par   \}
\par   cout << '\\n';
\par   getmem(p, 20);
\par   for(int j = 0; j < 20; j++) \{
\par     cout << p[j] << ' ';
\par     p[j] = j;
\par   \}
\par   cout << '\\n';
\par   getmem(p, 25);
\par   for(int k = 0; k < 25; k++)
\par     cout << p[k] << ' ';
\par   freemem(p);
\par   cout << '\\n';
\par 
\par   float* f = 0;
\par   getmem(f, 3);
\par   for(int u = 0; u < 3; u++) \{
\par     cout << f[u] << ' ';
\par     f[u] = u + 3.14159;
\par   \}
\par   cout << '\\n';
\par   getmem(f, 6);
\par   for(int v = 0; v < 6; v++)
\par     cout << f[v] << ' ';
\par   freemem(f);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {After each }{\b getmem(\~)}{, the values in memory are printed out to show that the new ones have been zeroed. 
\par Notice that a different version of }{\b getmem(\~)}{ is instantiated for the }{\b int}{ and }{\b float}{ pointers. You might think that because all the manipulations are so low-level you could get away with a single non-template function and pass a }{\b 
void*&}{ as }{\b oldmem}{. This doesn\rquote t work because then the compiler must do a conversion from your type to a }{\b void*}{. To take the reference, it makes a temporary. This produces an error because then you\rquote 
re modifying the temporary pointer, not the pointer you want to change. So the function template is necessary to produce the exact type for the argument.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776358}Type induction in function templates{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776358} 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As a simple but very useful example, consider the following:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: :arraySize.h
\par // Uses template type induction to 
\par // discover the size of an array
\par #ifndef ARRAYSIZE_H
\par #define ARRAYSIZE_H
\par 
\par template<typename T, int size>
\par int asz(T (&)[size]) \{ return size; \}
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // ARRAYSIZE_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This actually figures out the size of an array as a compile-time constant value, without using any }{\b sizeof(\~)}{
 operations! Thus you can have a much more succinct way to calculate the size of an array at compile time:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:ArraySize.cpp
\par // The return value of the template function
\par // asz() is a compile-time constant
\par #include "../arraySize.h"
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int a[12], b[20];
\par   const int sz1 = asz(a);
\par   const int sz2 = asz(b);
\par   int c[sz1], d[sz2];
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Of course, just making a variable of a built-in type a }{\b const}{ does not guarantee it\rquote s actually a compile-time constant, but if it\rquote 
s used to define the size of an array (as it is in the last line of }{\b main(\~)}{), then it }{\i must}{ be a compile-time constant.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776359}Taking the address of a generated function template{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776359} 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There are a number of situations where you need to take the address of a function. For example, you may have a function that takes an argument of a pointer to another function. Of course it\rquote 
s possible that this other function might be generated from a template function so you need some way to take that kind of address}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {
\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ I am indebted to Nathan Myers for this example.}}}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:TemplateFunctionAddress.cpp
\par // Taking the address of a function generated
\par // from a template.
\par 
\par template <typename T> void f(T*) \{\}
\par 
\par void h(void (*pf)(int*)) \{\}
\par 
\par template <class T> 
\par   void g(void (*pf)(T*)) \{\}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   // Full type exposition:
\par   h(&f<int>);
\par   // Type induction:
\par   h(&f);
\par   // Full type exposition:
\par   g<int>(&f<int>);
\par   // Type inductions:
\par   g(&f<int>);
\par   g<int>(&f);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This example demonstrates a number of different issues. First, even though you\rquote re using templates, the signatures must match \endash  the function }{\b h(\~)}{
 takes a pointer to a function that takes an }{\b int*}{ and returns }{\b void}{, and that\rquote s what the template }{\b f}{ produces. Second, the function that wants 
the function pointer as an argument can itself be a template, as in the case of the template }{\b g}{.
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{ you can see that type induction works here, too. The first call to }{\b h(\~)}{ explicitly gives the template argument for }{\b f}{, but since }{\b h(\~)}{ says that it will only take the address of a function that takes an }{\b int*}{
, that part can be induced by the compiler. With }{\b g(\~)}{ the situation is even more interesting because there are two templates involved. The compiler cannot induce the type with nothing to go on, but if either }{\b f}{ or }{\b g}{ is given }{\b int}
{, then the rest can be induced.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374091}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776360}Applying a function to a{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374091}n STL sequence
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776360}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {applying a function to a container}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\:
 applying a function to a container}}}{Suppose you want to take an STL sequence container (which you\rquote ll learn more about in subsequent chapters; for now we can just use the familiar }{\b vector}{
) and apply a function to all the objects it contains. Because a }{\b vector}{ can contain any type of object, you need a function that works with any type of }{\b vector}{ and any type of object it contains:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:applySequence.h
\par // Apply a function to an STL sequence container
\par 
\par // 0 arguments, any type of return value:
\par template<class Seq, class T, class R>
\par void apply(Seq& sq, R (T::*f)()) \{
\par   typename Seq::iterator it = sq.begin();
\par   while(it != sq.end()) \{
\par     ((*it)->*f)();
\par     it++;
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par // 1 argument, any type of return value:
\par template<class Seq, class T, class R, class A>
\par void apply(Seq& sq, R(T::*f)(A), A a) \{
\par   typename Seq::iterator it = sq.begin();
\par   while(it != sq.end()) \{
\par     ((*it)->*f)(a);
\par     it++;
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par // 2 arguments, any type of return value:
\par template<class Seq, class T, class R, 
\par          class A1, class A2>
\par void apply(Seq& sq, R(T::*f)(A1, A2),
\par     A1 a1, A2 a2) \{
\par   typename Seq::iterator it = sq.begin();
\par   while(it != sq.end()) \{
\par     ((*it)->*f)(a1, a2);
\par     it++;
\par   \}
\par \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {// Etc., to handle maximum likely arguments ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b apply(\~) }{function template takes a reference to the container class and a pointer-to-member for a member function of the objects contained in th
e class. It uses an iterator to move through the }{\b Stack}{ and apply the function to every object. If you\rquote ve (understandably) forgotten the pointer-to-member}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {pointer\: to member}}}{
 syntax, you can refresh your memory at the end of Chapter XX.
\par Notice that there are no STL header files (or any header files, for that matter) included in }{\b applySequence.h}{, so it is actually not limited to use with an STL sequence. However, it does make assumptions (primarily, the name and behavior of the }{
\b iterator}{) that apply to STL sequences.
\par You can see there is more than one version of }{\b apply(\~)}{, so it\rquote 
s possible to overload function templates. Although they all take any type of return value (which is ignored, but the type information is required to match the pointer-to-member), each version takes a different number of arguments, and because it\rquote 
s a template, those arguments can be of any type. The only limitation here is that there\rquote s no \ldblquote super template\rdblquote  to create templates for you; thus you must decide how many arguments will ever be required.
\par To test the various overloaded versions of }{\b apply(\~)}{, the class }{\b Gromit}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{
 A reference to the British animated short }{\i The Wrong Trousers}{ by Nick Park}{\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \v\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {Park, Nick}}}{.}}}{
 is created containing functions with different numbers of arguments:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:Gromit.h
\par // The techno-dog. Has member functions 
\par // with various numbers of arguments.
\par #include <iostream>
\par 
\par class Gromit \{ 
\par   int arf;
\par public:
\par   Gromit(int arf = 1) : arf(arf + 1) \{\}
\par   void speak(int) \{
\par     for(int i = 0; i < arf; i++)
\par       std::cout << "arf! ";
\par     std::cout << std::endl;
\par   \}
\par   char eat(float) \{
\par     std::cout << "chomp!" << std::endl;
\par     return 'z';
\par   \}
\par   int sleep(char, double) \{
\par     std::cout << "zzz..." << std::endl;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   void sit(void) \{\}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}; ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now the }{\b apply(\~)}{ template functions can be combined with a }{\b vector<Gromit*>}{ to make a container that will call member functions of the contained objects, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:applyGromit.cpp
\par // Test applySequence.h
\par #include "Gromit.h"
\par #include "applySequence.h"
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<Gromit*> dogs;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
\par     dogs.push_back(new Gromit(i));
\par   apply(dogs, &Gromit::speak, 1);
\par   apply(dogs, &Gromit::eat, 2.0f);
\par   apply(dogs, &Gromit::sleep, 'z', 3.0);
\par   apply(dogs, &Gromit::sit);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Although the definition of }{\b apply(\~)}{ is somewhat complex and not something you\rquote 
d ever expect a novice to understand, its use is remarkably clean and simple, and a novice could easily use it knowing only}{\i  what}{ it is intended to accomplish, not }{\i how}{. This is the type of division you should strive for in all of your pro
gram components: The tough details are all isolated on the designer\rquote s side of the wall, and users are concerned only with accomplishing their goals, and don\rquote t see, know about, or depend on details of the underlying implementation
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374092}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776361}Template-templates{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776361}
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:TemplateTemplate.cpp
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par // As long as things are simple, 
\par // this approach works fine:
\par template<typename C>
\par void print1(C& c) \{
\par   typename C::iterator it;
\par   for(it = c.begin(); it != c.end(); it++)
\par     cout << *it << " ";
\par   cout << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par // Template-template argument must 
\par // be a class; cannot use typename:
\par template<typename T, template<typename> class C>
\par void print2(C<T>& c) \{
\par   copy(c.begin(), c.end(), 
\par     ostream_iterator<T>(cout, " "));
\par   cout << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<string> v(5, "Yow!");
\par   print1(v);
\par   print2(v);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776362}Member function templates{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776362}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It\rquote s also possible to make }{\b apply(\~)}{ a }{\i member function template}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {member\: member function template}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: member function template}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {template\: member function template}}}{
 of the class. That is, a separate template definition from the class\rquote  template, and yet a member of the class. This may produce a cleaner syntax:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {dogs.apply(&Gromit::sit);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This is analogous to the act (in Chapter XX) of bringing ordinary functions inside a class.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright 
\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ Check your compiler version information to see if it supports member function templates.}}}{
\par The definition of the }{\b apply(\~)}{ functions turn out to be cleaner, as well, because they are members of the container. To accomplish this, a new container is inherited from one of t
he existing STL sequence containers and the member function templates are added to the new type. However, for maximum flexibility we\rquote d like to be able to use any of the STL sequence containers, and for this to work a }{\i template-template}{
 must be used, to tell the compiler that a template argument is actually a template, itself, and can thus take a type argument and be instantiated. Here is what it looks like after bringing the }{\b apply(\~)}{
 functions into the new type as member functions:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:applyMember.h
\par // applySequence.h modified to use 
\par // member function templates
\par 
\par template<class T, template<typename> class Seq>
\par class SequenceWithApply : public Seq<T*> \{
\par public:
\par   // 0 arguments, any type of return value:
\par   template<class R>
\par   void apply(R (T::*f)()) \{
\par     iterator it = begin();
\par     while(it != end()) \{
\par       ((*it)->*f)();
\par       it++;
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par   // 1 argument, any type of return value:
\par   template<class R, class A>
\par   void apply(R(T::*f)(A), A a) \{
\par     iterator it = begin();
\par     while(it != end()) \{
\par       ((*it)->*f)(a);
\par       it++;
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par   // 2 arguments, any type of return value:
\par   template<class R, class A1, class A2>
\par   void apply(R(T::*f)(A1, A2), 
\par     A1 a1, A2 a2) \{
\par     iterator it = begin();
\par     while(it != end()) \{
\par       ((*it)->*f)(a1, a2);
\par       it++;
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}; ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Because they are members, the }{\b apply(\~)}{ functions don\rquote t need as many arguments, and the }{\b iterator}{ class doesn\rquote t need to be qualified. Also, }{\b begin(\~)}{ and }{\b end(\~)
}{ are now member functions of the new type and so look cleaner as well. However, the basic code is still the same.
\par You can see how the function calls are also simpler for the client programmer:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:applyGromit2.cpp
\par // Test applyMember.h
\par #include "Gromit.h"
\par #include "applyMember.h"
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   SequenceWithApply<Gromit, vector> dogs;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
\par     dogs.push_back(new Gromit(i));
\par   dogs.apply(&Gromit::speak, 1);
\par   dogs.apply(&Gromit::eat, 2.0f);
\par   dogs.apply(&Gromit::sleep, 'z', 3.0);
\par   dogs.apply(&Gromit::sit);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Conceptually, it reads more sensibly to say that you\rquote re calling }{\b apply(\~)}{ for the }{\b dogs}{ container.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776363}Why virtual member template functions are disallowed{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776363}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776364}Nested template classes{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776364}
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374093}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776365}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374092}Template specializations
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776365}
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776366}Full specialization{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776366}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776367}Partial Specialization{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776367}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776368}A practical example{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776368}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {template\: and inheritance}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\: templates}}}{There\rquote 
s nothing to prevent you from using a class template in any way you\rquote d use an ordinary class. For example, you can easily inherit from a template, and you can create a new template that instantiates and inherits from an existing template. If the }{
\b vector }{class does everything you want, but you\rquote d also like it to sort itself, you can easily reuse the code and add value to it:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:Sorted.h
\par // Template specialization
\par #ifndef SORTED_H
\par #define SORTED_H
\par #include <vector>
\par 
\par template<class T>
\par class Sorted : public std::vector<T> \{
\par public:
\par   void sort();
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class T>
\par void Sorted<T>::sort() \{ // A bubble sort
\par   for(int i = size(); i > 0; i--)
\par     for(int j = 1; j < i; j++)
\par       if(at(j-1) > at(j)) \{
\par         // Swap the two elements:
\par         T t = at(j-1);
\par         at(j-1) = at(j);
\par         at(j) = t;
\par       \}
\par \}
\par 
\par // Partial specialization for pointers:
\par template<class T>
\par class Sorted<T*> : public std::vector<T*> \{
\par public:
\par   void sort();
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class T>
\par void Sorted<T*>::sort() \{
\par   for(int i = size(); i > 0; i--)
\par     for(int j = 1; j < i; j++)
\par       if(*at(j-1) > *at(j)) \{
\par         // Swap the two elements:
\par         T* t = at(j-1);
\par         at(j-1) = at(j);
\par         at(j) = t;
\par       \}
\par \}
\par 
\par // Full specialization for char*:
\par template<>
\par void Sorted<char*>::sort() \{
\par   for(int i = size(); i > 0; i--)
\par     for(int j = 1; j < i; j++)
\par       if(strcmp(at(j-1), at(j)) > 0) \{
\par         // Swap the two elements:
\par         char* t = at(j-1);
\par         at(j-1) = at(j);
\par         at(j) = t;
\par       \}
\par \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // SORTED_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b Sorted}{ template imposes a restriction on all classes it is instantiated for: They must contain a }{\b >}{ operator. In }{\b SString}{ this is added explicitly, but in }{\b Integer}{
 the automatic type conversion }{\b operator int}{ provides a path to the built-in }{\b >}{ operator. When a template }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {template\: requirements of template classes}}}{
provides more functionality for you, the trade-off is usually that it puts more requirements on your class. Sometimes you\rquote 
ll have to inherit the contained class to add the required functionality. Notice the value of using an overloaded operator here \endash  the }{\b Integer}{ class can rely on its underlying implementation to provide the functionality.
\par The default }{\b Sorted}{ template only works with objects (including objects of built-in types). However, it won\rquote t sort pointers to objects so the partial specialization is necessary. Even then, the code generated by the partial specialization won
\rquote t sort an array of }{\b char*}{. To solve this, the full specialization compares the }{\b char*}{ elements using }{\b strcmp(\~)}{ to produce the proper behavior.
\par Here\rquote s a test for }{\b Sorted.h}{ that uses the unique random number generator introduced earlier in the chapter:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:Sorted.cpp
\par // Testing template specialization
\par #include "Sorted.h"
\par #include "Urand.h"
\par #include "../arraySize.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par char* words[] = \{
\par   "is", "running", "big", "dog", "a",
\par \};
\par char* words2[] = \{
\par   "this", "that", "theother",
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Sorted<int> is;
\par   Urand<47> rand;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 15; i++)
\par     is.push_back(rand());
\par   for(int l = 0; l < is.size(); l++)
\par     cout << is[l] << ' ';
\par   cout << endl;
\par   is.sort();
\par   for(int l = 0; l < is.size(); l++)
\par     cout << is[l] << ' ';
\par   cout << endl;
\par 
\par   // Uses the template partial specialization:
\par   Sorted<string*> ss;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < asz(words); i++)
\par     ss.push_back(new string(words[i]));
\par   for(int i = 0; i < ss.size(); i++)
\par     cout << *ss[i] << ' ';
\par   cout << endl;
\par   ss.sort();
\par   for(int i = 0; i < ss.size(); i++)
\par     cout << *ss[i] << ' ';
\par   cout << endl;
\par   
\par   // Uses the full char* specialization:
\par   Sorted<char*> scp;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < asz(words2); i++)
\par     scp.push_back(words2[i]);
\par   for(int i = 0; i < scp.size(); i++)
\par     cout << scp[i] << ' ';
\par   cout << endl;
\par   scp.sort();
\par   for(int i = 0; i < scp.size(); i++)
\par     cout << scp[i] << ' ';
\par   cout << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Each of the template instantiations uses a different version of the template. }{\b Sorted<int>}{ uses the \ldblquote ordinary,\rdblquote  non-specialized template. }{\b Sorted<string*>}{
 uses the partial specialization for pointers. Lastly, }{\b Sorted<char*>}{ uses the full specialization for }{\b char*}{. Note that without this full specialization, you could be fooled into thinking that things were working correctly because the }{\b 
words}{ array would still sort out to \ldblquote a big dog is running\rdblquote  since the partial specialization would end up comparing the first character of each array. However, }{\b words2}{
 would not sort out correctly, and for the desired behavior the full specialization is necessary.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Pointer specialization
\par Partial ordering of function templates
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374085}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776369}Design & efficiency{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374085}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776369}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {design\: and efficiency}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {efficiency\: design}}}{In }{\b Sorted}{
, every time you call }{\b add(\~)}{ the element is inserted and the array is resorted. Here, the horribly inefficient and greatly deprecated (but easy to understand and code) bubble sort }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
bubble sort}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {sort\: bubble sort}}}{is used. This is perfectly appropriate, because it\rquote s part of the }{\b private}{ implementation. During program development, your priorities are to

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls252\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls252\adjustright {Get the class interfaces correct.

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls252\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls252\adjustright {
Create an accurate implementation as rapidly as possible so you can:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls252\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls252\adjustright {Prove your design.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {Very often, you will discover problems with the class interface only when you assemble your initial \ldblquote rough draft\rdblquote  of the working system. You may also discover the need for \ldblquote helper
\rdblquote  classes like containers and iterators during system assembly and during your first-pass implementation. Sometimes it\rquote s very difficult to discover these kinds of issues during analysis \endash  your goal in 
analysis should be to get a big-picture design that can be rapidly implemented}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {rapid development}}}{
 and tested. Only after the design has been proven should you spend the time to flesh it out completely and worry about performance issues. If the design fails, or if performance is not a problem, the bubble sort is good enough, and you haven\rquote 
t wasted any time. (Of course, the ideal solution is to use someone else\rquote s sorted container; the Standard C++ template library is the first place to look.)
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374086}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776370}Preventing template bloat{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374086}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776370}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {template\: preventing template bloat}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {bloat, preventing template bloat}}}{
Each time you instantiate a template, the code in the template is generated anew (except for }{\b inline}{ functions). If some of the functionality of a template does 
not depend on type, it can be put in a common base class to prevent needless reproduction of that code. For example, in Chapter XX in }{\b InheritStack.cpp}{ inheritance was used to specify the types that a }{\b Stack}{ could accept and produce. Here
\rquote s the templatized version of that code:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:Nobloat.h
\par // Templatized InheritStack.cpp
\par #ifndef NOBLOAT_H
\par #define NOBLOAT_H
\par #include "Stack4.h"
\par 
\par template<class T>
\par class NBStack : public Stack \{
\par public:
\par   void push(T* str) \{
\par     Stack::push(str);
\par   \}
\par   T* peek() const \{
\par     return (T*)Stack::peek();
\par   \}
\par   T* pop() \{
\par     return (T*)Stack::pop();
\par   \}
\par   ~NBStack();
\par \};
\par 
\par // Defaults to heap objects & ownership:
\par template<class T>
\par NBStack<T>::~NBStack() \{
\par   T* top = pop();
\par   while(top) \{
\par     delete top;
\par     top = pop();
\par   \}
\par \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // NOBLOAT_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As before, the inline functions generate no code and are thus \ldblquote free.\rdblquote 
 The functionality is provided by creating the base-class code only once. However, the ownership problem has been solved here by adding a destructor (which }{\i is}{ type-d
ependent, and thus must be created by the template). Here, it defaults to ownership. Notice that when the base-class destructor is called, the stack will be empty so no duplicate releases will occur.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776371}Explicit instantiation{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374093}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776371}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {controlling\: template instantiation}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {template\: controlling instantiation}
}}{At times it is useful to explicitly instantiate a template; that is, to tell the compiler to lay down the code for a specific version of that template even though you\rquote re not creating an object at that point. To do this, you reuse the }{\b 
template}{ keyword as follows:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {template class Bobbin<thread>;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {template void sort<char>(char*[]);
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here\rquote s a version of the }{\b Sorted.cpp}{ example that explicitly instantiates a template before using it:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:ExplicitInstantiation.cpp
\par #include "Urand.h"
\par #include "Sorted.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par // Explicit instantiation:
\par template class Sorted<int>;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Sorted<int> is;
\par   Urand<47> rand1;
\par   for(int k = 0; k < 15; k++)
\par     is.push_back(rand1());
\par   is.sort();
\par   for(int l = 0; l < is.size(); l++)
\par     cout << is[l] << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In this example, the explicit instantiation doesn\rquote t really accomplish anything; the program would work the same without it. Explicit instantiation is only for special cases where e
xtra control is needed.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776372}Explicit specification of template functions{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776372}
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776373}Controlling template instantiation{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776373}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Normally templates are not instantiated until they are needed. For function templates this just means the point at which you call the function, but for class templates it\rquote 
s more granular than that: each individual member function of the template is not instantiated until the first point of use. This means that only the member functions you actually use will be instantiated, which is quite important since 
it allows greater freedom in what the template can be used with. For example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C19:DelayedInstantiation.cpp
\par // Member functions of class templates are not
\par // instantiated until they're needed.
\par 
\par class X \{
\par public:
\par   void f() \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Y \{
\par public:
\par   void g() \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par template <typename T> class Z \{
\par   T t;
\par public:
\par   void a() \{ t.f(); \}
\par   void b() \{ t.g(); \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Z<X> zx;
\par   zx.a(); // Doesn't create Z<X>::b()
\par   Z<Y> zy;
\par   zy.b(); // Doesn't create Z<Y>::a()
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here, even though the template purports to use both }{\b f(\~)}{ and }{\b g(\~)}{ member functions of }{\b T}{, the fact that the program compiles shows you that it only generates }{\b Z<X>::a(\~)}{
 when it is explicitly called for }{\b zx}{ (if }{\b Z<X>::b(\~)}{ were also generated at the same time, a compile-time error message would be generated). Similarly, the call to }{\b zy.b(\~)}{ doesn\rquote t generate }{\b Z<Y>::a(\~)}{. As a result, the 
}{\b Z}{ template can be used with }{\b X}{ and }{\b Y}{, whereas if all the member functions were generated when the class was first created it would significantly limit the use of many templates.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776374}The inclusion vs. separation models{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776374}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776375}The export keyword{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776375}
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776376}Template programming idioms{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776376}
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776377}The \ldblquote curiously-recurring template\rdblquote {\*\bkmkend _Toc451776377}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776378}Traits{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776378}
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776379}Summary{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776379}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {One of the greatest weaknesses of C++ templates will be shown to you when you try to write code that u
ses templates, especially STL code (introduced in the next two chapters), and start getting compile-time error messages. When you\rquote 
re not used to it, the quantity of inscrutable text that will be spewed at you by the compiler will be quite overwhelming. After a while you\rquote ll adapt (although it always feels a bit barbaric), and if it\rquote 
s any consolation, C++ compilers have actually gotten a lot }{\i better}{ about this \endash  previously they would only give the line where you tried to instantiate the template, and most of t
hem now go to the line in the template definition that caused the problem.
\par The issue is that }{\i a template implies an interface}{. That is, even though the }{\b template}{ keyword says \ldblquote I\rquote ll take any type,\rdblquote  the code in a template definition actually requires that cert
ain operators and member functions be supported \endash  that\rquote s the interface. So in reality, a template definition is saying \ldblquote I\rquote ll take any type that supports this interface.\rdblquote 
 Things would be much nicer if the compiler could simply say \ldblquote hey, this type that you\rquote re trying to instantiate the template with doesn\rquote t support that interface \endash  can\rquote t do it.\rdblquote 
 The Java language has a feature called }{\b interface}{ that would be a perfect match for this (Java, however, has no parameterized type mechanism), but it will be many years
, if ever, before you will see such a thing in C++ (at this writing the C++ Standard has only just been accepted and it will be a while before all the compilers even achieve compliance). Compilers can only get so good at reporting template instantiation e
rrors, so you\rquote ll have to grit your teeth, go to the first line reported as an error and figure it out.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {\sect }\sectd \margtsxn2707\margbsxn2707\sbkodd\linex0\headery0\footery1440\colsx432\endnhere\titlepg\sectdefaultcl \pard\plain \s1\fi-1080\li990\sa360\sl240\slmult0
\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \cbpat8 \f4\fs96\cf1\kerning18\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776380}20: STL Containers & Iterators{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776380}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {\i {\*\bkmkstart STLContainersChapter}{\*\bkmkend STLContainersChapter}Container classes }{
are the solution to a specific kind of code reuse problem. They are building blocks used to create object-oriented programs \endash  they make the internals of a program much easier to construct.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A container class describes an object that holds other objects. Container classes are so important that they were considered fundamental to early object-oriented language
s. In Smalltalk, for example, programmers think of the language as the program translator together with the class library, and a critical part of that library is the container classes. So it became natural that C++ compiler vendors also include a containe
r class library. You\rquote ll note that the }{\b vector}{ was so useful that it was introduced in its simplest form very early in this book.
\par Like many other early C++ libraries, early container class libraries followed Smalltalk\rquote s }{\i object-based hierarchy}{, which worked well for Smalltalk, but turned out to be awkward and difficult to use in C++. Another approach was required.

\par This chapter attempts to slowly work you into the concepts of the C++ }{\i Standard Template Library }{(STL), which is a powerful library of containers (as well as }{\i algorithms}{
, but these are covered in the following chapter). In the past, I have taught that there is a relatively small subset of elements and ideas that you need to understand in order to get much of the usefulness from the STL. Although this can
 be true it turns out that understanding the STL more deeply is important to gain the full power of the library. This chapter and the next probe into the STL containers and algorithms.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374160}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593281}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628753}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374088}
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc375545199}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018396}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776381}Containers and iterators{\*\bkmkend _Toc375545199}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018396}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776381}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you don\rquote t know how many objects you\rquote re going to need to solve a pa}{r}{ticular problem, or how long they will last, you also don\rquote 
t know how to store those objects. How can you know how much space to create? You can\rquote t, since that information isn\rquote t known until run time.
\par The solution to most problems in o
bject-oriented design seems flippant: you create another type of object. For the storage problem, the new type of object holds other objects, or pointers to objects. Of course, you can do the same thing with an array, but there\rquote 
s more. This new type of object, which is typically referred to in C++ as a }{\i container}{ (also called a }{\i collection}{ in some languages), will expand itself whenever necessary to accommodate ever}{y}{thing you place inside it. So you don\rquote 
t need to know how many objects you\rquote re going to hold in a collection. You just create a collection object and let it take care of the details.
\par Fortunately, a good OOP language comes with a set of containers as part of the package. In C++, it\rquote s the Standard Template Library (STL). In some libraries, a generic co
ntainer is considered good enough for all needs, and in others (C++ in particular) the library has different types of containers for different needs: a vector for consistent access to all elements, and a linked list for consistent insertion at all element
s, for example, so you can choose the particular type that fits your needs. These may include sets, queues, hash t}{a}{bles, trees, stacks, etc.
\par All containers have some way to put things in and get things out. The way that you place something into a container is fairly obvious. There\rquote s a function called \ldblquote push\rdblquote  or \ldblquote add\rdblquote 
 or a similar name. Fetching things out of a container is not always as apparent; if it\rquote s an array-like entity such as a ve}{c}{tor, you might be able to use an indexing operator or function. But in many situations this doesn\rquote 
t make sense. Also, a single-selection function is restrictive. What if you want to manipulate or compare a group of elements in the container?
\par The solution is an }{\i iterator}{, which is an object whose job is to select the elements within a container and present them to the user of the iterator. As a class, it also provides a level of a}{b}{
straction. This abstraction can be used to separate the details of the container from the code that\rquote s accessing that container. The container, via the 
iterator, is abstracted to be simply a sequence. The iterator allows you to traverse that sequence without worrying about the underlying structure \endash  that is, whether it\rquote 
s a vector, a linked list, a stack or something else. This gives you the flex}{i}{bility to easily change the underlying data structure without disturbing the code in your program. 
\par From the design standpoint, all you really want is a sequence that can be m}{a}{nipulated to solve your problem. If a single type of sequence satisfied all of your needs, there\rquote d be no reason to have different kinds. There are two re}{a}{
sons that you need a choice of containers. First, containers provide different types of interfaces and external behavior. A stack has a different interface and behavior than that of a queue, 
which is different than that of a set or a list. One of these might provide a more flexible solution to your problem than the other. Second, different containers have different efficiencies for certain operations. The best example is a vector and a list. 
Both are simple sequences that can have identical interfaces and external behaviors. But certain operations can have radically different costs. Randomly accessing elements in a vector is a co}{n}{
stant-time operation; it takes the same amount of time regardless of the el}{e}{
ment you select. However, in a linked list it is expensive to move through the list to randomly select an element, and it takes longer to find an element if it is further down the list. On the other hand, if you want to insert an el}{e}{ment in the 
middle of a sequence, it\rquote s much cheaper in a list than in a vector. These and other operations have different efficiencies depending upon the u}{n}{
derlying structure of the sequence. In the design phase, you might start with a list and, when tuning for performance, change to a vector. Because of the abstraction via iterators, you can change from one to the other with minimal impact on your code.

\par In the end, remember that a container is only a storage cabinet to put objects in. If that cabinet solves all of your needs, it doesn\rquote t really matter }{\i how}{ it is implemented (a basic concept with most types of objects). If you\rquote 
re working in a programming environment that has built-in overhead due to other fa}{c}{tors, then the cost difference between a vector and a linked list might not matter. You might need only one type of sequence. You can even imagine the \ldblquote 
perfect\rdblquote  container abstraction, which can automatically change its unde}{r}{lying implementation according to the way it is used.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776382}STL reference documentation{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776382}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You will notice that this chapter does not contain exhaustive documentation describing each of the member functions in each STL container. Although I describe the member functions that I use, I
\rquote ve left the full descriptions to others: there are at least two very good on
-line sources of STL documentation in HTML format that you can keep resident on your computer and view with a Web browser whenever you need to look something up. The first is the Dinkumware library (which covers the entire Standard C and C++ library) ment
ioned at the beginning of this book section (page XXX). The second is the freely-downloadable SGI STL and documentation, freely downloadable at http://www.sgi.com/Technology/STL/. These should provide complete references when you\rquote 
re writing code. In addition, the STL books listed in Appendix XX will provide you with other resources.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776383}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593281}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628753}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374088}
The Standard Template Library{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776383} {\*\bkmkend _Toc312374160}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Standard C++ libraries\: standard template library (STL)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {template\:
 standard template library (STL)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {library\: standard template library (STL)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {STL\: standard template library}}}{The C++ STL}{
\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ Contributed to the C++ Standard by Alexander Stepanov}{\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar\adjustright \v\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {Stepanov, Alexander}}}{ and Meng Lee}{\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \v\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {Lee, Meng}}}{ at Hewlett-Packard.}}}{
 is a powerful library intended to satisfy the vast bulk of your needs for containers and algorithms, but in a completely portable fashion. This means that not only are your programs easier to port
 to other platforms, but that your knowledge itself does not depend on the libraries provided by a particular compiler vendor (and the STL is likely to be more tested and scrutinized than a particular vendor\rquote 
s library). Thus, it will benefit you greatly to look first to the STL for containers and algorithms, }{\i before}{ looking at vendor-specific solutions.
\par A fundamental principle of software design is that }{\i all problems can be simplified by introducing an extra level of indirection}{. This simplicity is achieved in the STL using }{\i iterators}{
 to perform operations on a data structure while knowing as little as possible about that structure, thus producing data structure independence. With the STL, this means that any operation that can be performed on an array of objec
ts can also be performed on an STL container of objects and vice versa. The STL containers work just as easily with built-in types as they do with user-defined types. If you learn the library, it will work on everything.
\par The drawback to this independence is that you\rquote ll have to take a little time at first getting used to the way things are done in the STL. However, the STL uses a consistent pattern, so once you fit your mind around it, it doesn\rquote 
t change from one STL tool to another.
\par Consider an example using the STL }{\b set}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {standard template library\: set class example}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {set\: STL set class example}}}{
class. A set will allow only one of each object value to be inserted into itself. Here is a simple }{\b set}{ created to work with }{\b int}{s by providing }{\b int}{ as the template argument to }{\b set}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:Intset.cpp
\par // Simple use of STL set
\par #include <set>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par  set<int> intset;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 25; i++)
\par     for(int j = 0; j < 10; j++)
\par       // Try to insert multiple copies:
\par       intset.insert(j);
\par   // Print to output:
\par  copy(intset.begin(), intset.end(),
\par    ostream_iterator<int>(cout, "\\n"));
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b insert(\~)}{ member does all the work: it tries putting the new element in and rejects it if it\rquote s already there. Very often the
 activities involved in using a set are simply insertion and a test to see whether it contains the element. You can also form a union, intersection, or difference of sets, and test to see if one set is a subset of another.
\par In this example, the values 0 - 9 are inserted into the set 25 times, and the results are printed out to show that only one of each of the values is actually retained in the set.
\par The }{\b copy(\~)}{ function is actually the instantiation of an STL template function, of which there are many. These template functions are generally referred to as \ldblquote the STL Algorithms\rdblquote 
 and will be the subject of the following chapter. However, several of the algorithms are so useful that they will be introduced in this chapter. Here, }{\b copy(\~) }{shows the use of iterators. The }{\b set}{ member functions }{\b begin(\~)}{ and }{\b 
end(\~)}{ produce iterators as their return values. These are used by }{\b copy(\~)}{ as beginning and ending points for its operation, which is simply to move between the boundaries established by the iterators and copy the ele
ments to the third argument, which is also an iterator, but in this case, a special type created for iostreams. This places }{\b int}{ objects on }{\b cout}{ and separates them with a newline.
\par Because of its genericity, }{\b copy(\~)}{ is certainly not restricted to printing on a stream. It can be used in virtually any situation: it needs only three iterators to talk to. All of the algorithms follow the form of }{\b copy(\~)}{
 and simply manipulate iterators (the use of iterators is the \ldblquote extra level of indirection\rdblquote ).
\par Now consider taking the form of }{\b Intset.cpp}{ and reshaping it to display a list of the words used in a document. The solution becomes remarkably simple.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:WordSet.cpp
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <string>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <set>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1);
\par   ifstream source(argv[1]);
\par   assure(source, argv[1]);
\par   string word;
\par   set<string> words;
\par   while(source >> word)
\par     words.insert(word);
\par   copy(words.begin(), words.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<string>(cout, "\\n"));
\par   cout << "Number of unique words:" 
\par     << words.size() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The only substantive difference here is that }{\b string}{ is used instead of }{\b int}{. The words are pulled from a file, but everything else is the same as in }{\b Intset.cpp}{. The }{\b operator>>
}{ returns a whitespace-separated group of characters each time it is called, until there\rquote s no more input from the file. So it approximately breaks an input stream up into words. Each }{\b string}{ is placed in the }{\b set }{using }{\b insert(\~)}
{, and the }{\b copy(\~)}{ function is used to display the results. Because of the way }{\b set}{ is implemented (as a tree), the words are automatically sorted.
\par Consider how much effort it would be to accomplish the same task in C, or even in C++ without the STL.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776384}The basic concepts{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776384}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The primary idea in the STL is the }{\i container}{ (also known as a }{\i collection}{
), which is just what it sounds like: a place to hold things. You need containers because objects are constantly marching in and out of your program and there must be someplace to put them while they\rquote re around. You can\rquote 
t make named local objects because in a typical program you don\rquote t know how many, or what type, or the lifetime of the objects you\rquote re working with. So you need a container that will expand whenever necessary to fill your needs.
\par All the containers in the STL hold objects and expand themselves. In addition, they hold your objects in a particular way. The difference between one container and another is the way the objects are held and how the sequence is created. Let\rquote s
 start by looking at the simplest containers.
\par A }{\b vector}{ is a linear sequence that allows rapid random access to its elements. However, it\rquote s expensive to insert an element in the middle of the sequence, and is also expensive when it allocates additional storage. A }{\b deque}{
 is also a linear sequence, and it allows random access that\rquote s nearly as fast as}{\b  vector}{, but it\rquote s significantly faster when it needs to allocate new storage, and you can easily add new elements at either end (}{\b vector }{
only allows the addition of elements at its tail). A }{\b list}{ the third type of basic linear sequence, but it\rquote s expensive to move around randomly and cheap to insert an element in the middle. Thus }{\b list}{, }{\b deque}{ and }{\b vector}{
 are very similar in their basic functionality (they all hold linear sequences), but different in the cost of their activities. So for your first shot at a program, you could choose any one, and only experiment with the others if you\rquote 
re tuning for efficiency.
\par Many of the problems you set out to solve will only require a simple linear sequence like a }{\b vector}{, }{\b deque}{ or }{\b list}{. All three have a member function }{\b push_back(\~)}{
 which you use to insert a new element at the back of the sequence (}{\b deque}{ and }{\b list}{ also have }{\b push_front(\~)}{).
\par But now how do you retrieve those elements? With a }{\b vector}{ or }{\b deque}{, it is possible to use the indexing }{\b operator[ ]}{, but that doesn\rquote t work with }{\b list}{. Since it would be nicest to learn a single interface, we\rquote 
ll often use the one defined for all STL containers: the }{\i iterator}{.
\par An iterator is a class that abstracts the process of moving through a sequence. It allows you to select each element of a sequence }{\i without knowing the underlying structure of that sequence}{
. This is a powerful feature, partly because it allows us to learn a single interface that works with all containers, and partly because it allows containers to be used interchangeably.
\par One more observation and you\rquote re ready for another example. Even though the STL containers hold objects by value (that is, they hold the whole object inside themselves) that\rquote s probably not the way you\rquote ll generally use them if you
\rquote re doing object-oriented programming. That\rquote s because in OOP, most of the time you\rquote ll create objects on the heap with }{\b new}{ and then }{\i upcast}{ the address to the base-class type, later manipulating it as a 
pointer to the base class. The beauty of this is that you don\rquote t worry about the specific type of object you\rquote 
re dealing with, which greatly reduces the complexity of your code and increases the maintainability of your program. This process of upcasting is what you try to do in OOP with polymorphism, so you\rquote ll usually be using containers of pointers.

\par Consider the classic \ldblquote shape\rdblquote  example where shapes have a set of common operations, and you have different types of shapes. Here\rquote s what it looks like using the STL }{\b vector}{ to hold pointers to various types of }{\b Shape}{
 created on the heap:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:Stlshape.cpp
\par // Simple shapes w/ STL
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Shape \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void draw() = 0;
\par   virtual ~Shape() \{\};
\par \};
\par 
\par class Circle : public Shape \{
\par public:
\par   void draw() \{ cout << "Circle::draw\\n"; \}
\par   ~Circle() \{ cout << "~Circle\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Triangle : public Shape \{
\par public:
\par   void draw() \{ cout << "Triangle::draw\\n"; \}
\par   ~Triangle() \{ cout << "~Triangle\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Square : public Shape \{
\par public:
\par   void draw() \{ cout << "Square::draw\\n"; \}
\par   ~Square() \{ cout << "~Square\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par typedef std::vector<Shape*> Container;
\par typedef Container::iterator Iter;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Container shapes;
\par   shapes.push_back(new Circle);
\par   shapes.push_back(new Square);
\par   shapes.push_back(new Triangle);
\par   for(Iter i = shapes.begin();
\par       i != shapes.end(); i++)
\par     (*i)->draw();
\par   // ... Sometime later:
\par   for(Iter i = shapes.begin();
\par       i != shapes.end(); i++)
\par     delete *i;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The creation of }{\b Shape}{, }{\b Circle}{, }{\b Square}{ and }{\b Triangle}{ should be fairly familiar. }{\b Shape}{ is a pure abstract base class (because of the }{\i pure specifier}{ }{\b =0}{
) that defines the interface for all types of }{\b shapes}{. The derived classes redefine the }{\b virtual}{ function }{\b draw(\~)}{ to perform the appropriate operation. Now we\rquote d like to create a bunch of different types of }{\b Shape}{
 object, but where to put them? In an STL container, of course. For convenience, this }{\b typedef}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {typedef std::vector<Shape*> Container;
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {creates an alias for a }{\b vector}{ of }{\b Shape*}{, and this }{\b typedef}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {typedef Container::iterator Iter;
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {uses that alias to create another one, for }{\b vector<Shape*>::iterator}{. Notice that the }{\b container}{ type name must be used to produce the appropriate iterator, which is defined as a nested 
