\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This highlights what could be seen as a flaw in the STL: there\rquote s no facility in any of the STL containers to automatically }{\b delete}{ the pointers they contain, so you must do it by hand. It
\rquote s as if the assumption of the STL designers was that containers of pointers weren\rquote t an interesting problem, although I assert that it is one of the more common things you\rquote ll want to do.
\par Automatically deleting a pointer turns out to be a rather aggressive thing to do because of the }{\i multiple membership}{ problem. If a container holds a pointer to an object, it\rquote 
s not unlikely that pointer could also be in another container. A pointer to an }{\b Aluminum}{ object in a list of }{\b Trash}{ pointers could also reside in a list of }{\b Aluminum}{
