\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {    strings.push_back(line);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The operation that\rquote s being performed on this file is to add line numbers. A }{\b stringstream}{ provides easy conversion from an }{\b int}{ to a }{\b string}{ of characters representing that }{
\b int}{.
\par Assembling }{\b string}{ objects is quite easy, since }{\b operator+}{ is overloaded. Sensibly enough, the iterator }{\b w}{ can be dereferenced to produce a string that can be used as both an rvalue }{\i and}{ an lvalue:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {*w = ss.str() + ": " + *w;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The fact that you can assign back into the container via the iterator may seem a bit surprising at first, but it\rquote s a tribute to the careful design of the STL.
\par Because the }{\b vector<string>}{ contains the objects themselves, a number of interesting things take place. First, no cleanup is necessary. Even if you were to put addresses of the }{\b string}{ objects as pointers into }{\i other}{ containers, it
\rquote s clear that }{\b strings}{ is the \ldblquote master list\rdblquote  and maintains ownership of the objects.
\par Second, you are effectively using dynamic object creation, and yet you never use }{\b new}{ or }{\b delete}{! That\rquote s because, somehow, it\rquote s all taken care of for you by the }{\b vector}{
 (this is non-trivial. You can try to figure it out by looking at the header files for the STL \endash  all the code is there \endash  but it\rquote s quite an exercise). Thus your coding is significantly cleaned up.
\par The limitation of holding objects instead of pointers inside containers is quite severe: you can\rquote t upcast from derived types, thus you can\rquote t use polymorphism. The problem with upcasting objec
ts by value is that they get sliced and converted until their type is completely changed into the base type, and there\rquote s no remnant of the derived type left. It\rquote s pretty safe to say that you }{\i never}{ want to do this.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776386}Inheriting from STL containers{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776386}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The power of instantly creating a sequence of elements is amazing, and it makes you realize how much time you\rquote 
