\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776387}A plethora of iterators{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776387}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As mentioned earlier, the iterator is the abstraction that allows a piece of code to be }{\i generic}{
, and to work with different types of containers without knowing the underlying structure of those containers.}{\i  }{Every container produces iterators. You must always be able to say:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {ContainerType::iterator
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {ContainerType::const_iterator
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {to produce the types of the iterators produced by that container. Every container has a }{\b begin(\~)}{
 method that produces an iterator indicating the beginning of the elements in the container, and an }{\b end(\~)}{ method that produces an iterator which is the as the }{\i past-the-end value}{ of the container. If the container is }{\b const}{\'b8 }{\b 
begin(\~)}{ and }{\b end(\~)}{ produce }{\b const}{ iterators.
\par Every iterator can be moved forward to the next element using the }{\b operator++}{ (an iterator may be able to do more than this, as you shall see, but it must at least support forward movement with }{\b operator++}{).
\par The basic iterator is only guaranteed to be able to perform }{\b ==}{ and }{\b !=}{ comparisons. Thus, to move an iterator }{\b it}{ forward without running it off the end you say something like:
