\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Where }{\b pastEnd}{ is the past-the-end value produced by the container\rquote s }{\b end(\~)}{ member function.
\par An iterator can be used to produce the element that it is currently selecting within a container by dereferencing the iterator. This can take two forms. If }{\b it }{is an iterator and }{\b f(\~) }{
is a member function of the objects held in the container that the iterator is pointing within, then you can say either:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {(*it).f();
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
