\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {it->f();
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Knowing this, you can create a template that works with any container. Here, the }{\b apply(\~)}{
 function template calls a member function for every object in the container, using a pointer to member that is passed as an argument:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:Apply.cpp
\par // Using basic iterators
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <iterator>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par template<class Cont, class PtrMemFun>
\par void apply(Cont& c, PtrMemFun f) \{
\par   typename Cont::iterator it = c.begin();
\par   while(it != c.end()) \{
\par     (it->*f)(); // Compact form
\par     ((*it).*f)(); // Alternate form
\par     it++;
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par class Z \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   Z(int ii) : i(ii) \{\}
\par   void g() \{ i++; \}
\par   friend ostream& 
\par   operator<<(ostream& os, const Z& z) \{
\par     return os << z.i;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   ostream_iterator<Z> out(cout, " ");
\par   vector<Z> vz;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
\par     vz.push_back(Z(i));
\par   copy(vz.begin(), vz.end(), out);
\par   cout << endl;
\par   apply(vz, &Z::g);
\par   copy(vz.begin(), vz.end(), out);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Because }{\b operator->}{ is defined for STL iterators, it can be used for pointer-to-member dereferencing (in the following chapter you\rquote 
ll learn a more elegant way to handle the problem of applying a member function or ordinary function to every object in a container).
\par Much of the time, this is all you need to know about iterators \endash  that they are produced by }{\b begin(\~)}{ and }{\b end(\~)}{
, and that you can use them to move through a container and select elements. Many of the problems that you solve, and the STL algorithms (covered in the next chapter) will allow you to just flail awa
y with the basics of iterators. However, things can at times become more subtle, and in those cases you need to know more about iterators. The rest of this section gives you the details.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776388}Iterators in reversible containers{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776388}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {All containers must produce the basic }{\b iterator}{. A container may also be }{\i reversible}{
, which means that it can produce iterators that move backwards from the end, as well as the iterators that move forward from the beginning.
\par A reversible container has the methods }{\b rbegin(\~)}{ (to produce a }{\b reverse_iterator}{ selecting the end) and }{\b rend(\~)}{ (to produce a }{\b reverse_iterator}{ indicating \ldblquote one past the beginning\rdblquote ). If the container is }{\b 
const}{ then }{\b rbegin(\~)}{ and }{\b rend(\~)}{ will produce }{\b const_reverse_iterator}{s.
\par All the basic sequence containers }{\b vector}{, }{\b deque}{ and }{\b list}{ are reversible containers. The following example uses }{\b vector}{, but will work with }{\b deque}{ and }{\b list}{ as well:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:Reversible.cpp
\par // Using reversible containers
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   ifstream in("Reversible.cpp");
\par   assure(in, "Reversible.cpp");
\par   string line;
\par   vector<string> lines;
\par   while(getline(in, line))
\par     lines.push_back(line);
\par   vector<string>::reverse_iterator r;
\par   for(r = lines.rbegin(); r != lines.rend(); r++)
\par     cout << *r << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You move backward through the container using the same syntax as moving forward through a container with an ordinary iterator.
\par The associative containers }{\b set}{, }{\b multiset}{, }{\b map}{ and }{\b multimap}{ are also reversible. Using iterators with associative containers is a bit different, however, and will be delayed until those containers are more fully introduced.

\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776389}Iterator categories{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776389}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The iterators are classified into different \ldblquote categories\rdblquote  which describe what 
they are capable of doing. The order in which they are generally described moves from the categories with the most restricted behavior to those with the most powerful behavior.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Input: read-only, one pass
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The only predefined implementations of input iterators are }{\b istream_iterator}{ and }{\b istreambuf_iterator}{, to read from an }{\b istream}{
. As you can imagine, an input iterator can only be dereferenced once for each element that\rquote s selected, just as you can only read a particular portion of an input stream once. They can
 only move forward. There is a special constructor to define the past-the-end value. In summary, you can dereference it for reading (once only for each value), and move it forward.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Output: write-only, one pass
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This is the complement of an input iterator, but for writing rather than reading. The only predefined implementations of output iterators are }{\b ostream_iterator}{ and }{\b ostreambuf_iterator}{
, to write to an }{\b ostream}{, and the less-commonly-used }{\b raw_storage_iterator}{. Again, these can only be dereferenced once fo
r each written value, and they can only move forward. There is no concept of a terminal past-the-end value for an output iterator. Summarizing, you can dereference it for writing (once only for each value) and move it forward.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Forward: multiple read/write
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The forward iterator contains all the functionality of both the input iterator and the output iterator, plus you can dereference an iterator location multiple times, so you can read and write to a value multiple times. As the name implies, you can only mo
ve forward. There are no predefined iterators that are only forward iterators.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Bidirectional: operator--
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The bidirectional iterator has all the functionality of the forward iterator, and in addition it can be moved backwards one location at a time using }{\b operator--}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Random-access: like a pointer
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Finally, the random-access iterator has all the functionality of the bidirectional iterator plus all the functionality of a pointer (a pointer }{\i is}{
 a random-access iterator). Basically, anything you can do with a pointer you can do with a bidirectional iterator, including indexing with }{\b operator[ ]}{
, adding integral values to a pointer to move it forward or backward by a number of locations, and comparing one iterator to another with }{\b <}{, }{\b >=}{,}{\b  }{etc.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Is this really important?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Why do you care about this categorization? When you\rquote 
re just using containers in a straightforward way (for example, just hand-coding all the operations you want to perform on the objects in the container) it usually doesn\rquote t impact you too much. Things either work or they don\rquote 
t. The iterator categories become important when:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls241\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls241\adjustright {
You use some of the fancier built-in iterator types that will be demonstrated shortly. Or you graduate to creating your own iterators (this will also be demonstrated, later in this chapter).
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls241\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls241\adjustright {
You use the STL algorithms (the subject of the next chapter). Each of the algorithms have requirements that they place on the iterators that they work with. Knowledge of the iterator categories is even more important when you create your own reusable a
lgorithm templates, because the iterator category that your algorithm requires determines how flexible the algorithm will be. If you only require the most primitive iterator category (input or output) then your algorithm will work with }{\i everything}{ (
}{\b copy(\~)}{ is an example of this).
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776390}Predefined iterators{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776390}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The STL has a predefined set of iterator classes that can be quite handy. For example, you\rquote ve already seen }{\b reverse_iterator}{ (produced by calling }{\b rbegin(\~)}{ and }{\b rend(\~)}{
 for all the basic containers).
\par The }{\i insertion iterators}{ are necessary because some of the STL algorithms \endash  }{\b copy(\~)}{ for example \endash  use the assignment }{\b operator=}{ in order to place objects in the destination container. This is a problem when you\rquote 
re using the algorithm to }{\i fill}{ the container rather than to overwrite items that are already in the destination container. That is, when the space isn\rquote t already there. What the insert iterators do is change the implementation of the }{\b 
operator=}{ so that instead of doing an assignment, it calls a \ldblquote push\rdblquote  or \ldblquote insert\rdblquote  function for that container, thus causing it to allocate new space. The constructors for both }{\b back_insert_iterator}{ and }{\b 
front_insert_iterator}{ take a basic sequence container object (}{\b vector}{,}{\b  deque}{ or }{\b list}{) as their argument and produce an iterator that calls }{\b push_back(\~)}{ or }{\b push_front(\~)}{
, respectively, to perform assignment. The shorthand functions }{\b back_inserter(\~)}{ and }{\b front_inserter(\~)}{ produce the same objects with a little less typing. Since all the basic sequence containers support }{\b push_back(\~)}{, you will prob
ably find yourself using }{\b back_inserter(\~)}{ with some regularity.
\par The }{\b insert_iterator}{ allows you to insert elements in the middle of the sequence, again replacing the meaning of }{\b operator=}{, but this time with }{\b insert(\~)}{ instead of one of the \ldblquote push\rdblquote  functions. The }{\b insert(\~)}{
 member function requires an iterator indicating the place to insert before, so the }{\b insert_iterator}{ requires this iterator addition to the container object. The shorthand function }{\b inserter(\~)}{ produces the same object.
\par The following example shows the use of the different types of inserters:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:Inserters.cpp
\par // Different types of iterator inserters
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <deque>
\par #include <list>
\par #include <iterator>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int a[] = \{ 1, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, 23 \};
\par 
\par template<class Cont>
\par void frontInsertion(Cont& ci) \{
\par   copy(a, a + sizeof(a)/sizeof(int), 
\par     front_inserter(ci));
\par   copy(ci.begin(), ci.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<int>(cout, " "));
\par   cout << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par template<class Cont>
\par void backInsertion(Cont& ci) \{
\par   copy(a, a + sizeof(a)/sizeof(int), 
\par     back_inserter(ci));
\par   copy(ci.begin(), ci.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<int>(cout, " "));
\par   cout << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par template<class Cont>
\par void midInsertion(Cont& ci) \{
\par   typename Cont::iterator it = ci.begin();
\par   it++; it++; it++;
\par   copy(a, a + sizeof(a)/(sizeof(int) * 2),
\par     inserter(ci, it));
\par   copy(ci.begin(), ci.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<int>(cout, " "));
\par   cout << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   deque<int> di;
\par   list<int>  li;
\par   vector<int> vi;
\par   // Can't use a front_inserter() with vector
\par   frontInsertion(di);
\par   frontInsertion(li);
\par   di.clear();
\par   li.clear();
\par   backInsertion(vi);
\par   backInsertion(di);
\par   backInsertion(li);
\par   midInsertion(vi);
\par   midInsertion(di);
\par   midInsertion(li);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Since }{\b vector}{ does not support }{\b push_front(\~)}{, it cannot produce a }{\b front_insertion_iterator}{. However, you can see that }{\b vector}{
 does support the other two types of insertion (even though, as you shall see later, }{\b insert(\~)}{ is not a very efficient operation for }{\b vector}{).
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {IO stream iterators
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You\rquote ve already seen some use of the }{\b ostream_iterator}{ (an output iterator) in conjuction with }{\b copy(\~)}{
 to place the contents of a container on an output stream. There is a corresponding }{\b istream_iterator}{ (an input iterator) which allows you to \ldblquote iterate\rdblquote 
 a set of objects of a specified type from an input stream. An important difference between }{\b ostream_iterator}{ and }{\b istream_iterator}{ comes from the fact that an output stream doesn\rquote t have any concept of an \ldblquote end,\rdblquote 
 since you can always just keep writing more elements. However, an input stream eventually terminates (for example, when you reach the end of a file) so there needs to be a way to represent that. An }{\b istream_iterator}{
 has two constructors, one that takes an }{\b istream}{ and produces the iterator you actually 
read from, and the other which is the default constructor and produces an object which is the past-the-end sentinel. In the following program this object is named }{\b end}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:StreamIt.cpp
\par // Iterators for istreams and ostreams
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   ifstream in("StreamIt.cpp");
\par   assure(in, "StreamIt.cpp");
\par   istream_iterator<string> init(in), end;
\par   ostream_iterator<string> out(cout, "\\n");
\par   vector<string> vs;
\par   copy(init, end, back_inserter(vs));
\par   copy(vs.begin(), vs.end(), out);
\par   *out++ = vs[0];
\par   *out++ = "That's all, folks!";
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When }{\b in}{ runs out of input (in this case when the end of the file is reached) then }{\b init}{ becomes equivalent to }{\b end}{ and the }{\b copy(\~)}{ terminates.
\par Because }{\b out}{ is an }{\b ostream_iterator<string>}{, you can simply assign any }{\b string}{ object to the dereferenced iterator using }{\b operator=}{ and that }{\b string}{ will be placed on the output stream, as seen in the two assignments to }{
\b out}{. Because }{\b out}{ is defined with a newline as its second argument, these assignments also cause a newline to be inserted along with each assignment.
\par While it is possible to create an }{\b istream_iterator<char>}{ and }{\b ostream_iterator<char>}{, these actually }{\i parse }{the i
nput and thus will for example automatically eat whitespace (spaces, tabs and newlines), which is not desirable if you want to manipulate an exact representation of an }{\b istream}{. Instead, you can use the special iterators }{\b istreambuf_iterator}{
 and }{\b ostreambuf_iterator}{, which are designed strictly to move characters}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{
 These were actually created to abstract the \ldblquote locale\rdblquote  facets away from iostreams, so that locale facets could operate on any sequ
ence of characters, not only iostreams. Locales allow iostreams to easily handle culturally-different formatting (such as representation of money), and are beyond the scope of this book.}}}{
. Although these are templates, the only template arguments they will accept are either }{\b char}{ or }{\b wchar_t}{ (for wide characters). The following example allows you to compare the behavior of the stream iterators vs. the streambuf iterators:

\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:StreambufIterator.cpp
\par // istreambuf_iterator & ostreambuf_iterator
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <iterator>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   ifstream in("StreambufIterator.cpp");
\par   assure(in, "StreambufIterator.cpp");
\par   // Exact representation of stream:
\par   istreambuf_iterator<char> isb(in), end;
\par   ostreambuf_iterator<char> osb(cout);
\par   while(isb != end)
\par     *osb++ = *isb++; // Copy 'in' to cout
\par   cout << endl;
\par   ifstream in2("StreambufIterator.cpp");
\par   // Strips white space:
\par   istream_iterator<char> is(in2), end2;
\par   ostream_iterator<char> os(cout);
\par   while(is != end2)
\par     *os++ = *is++;
\par   cout << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The stream iterators use the parsing defined by }{\b istream::operator>>}{, which is probably not\line what you want if you are parsing characters directly \endash  it\rquote 
s fairly rare that you would want all the whitespace stripped out of your character stream. You\rquote ll virtually always want to use a streambuf iterator when using characters and streams, rather than a stream iterator. In addition, }{\b 
istream::operator>>}{ adds significant overhead for each operation, so it is only appropriate for higher-level operations such as parsing floating-point numbers.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ I am indebted to Nathan Myers for explaining this to me.}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Manipulating raw storage
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This is a little more esoteric and is generally used in the implementation of other Standard Library functions, but it is nonetheless interesting. The }{\b raw_storage_iterator}{ is defined in }{\b 
<algorithm>}{ and is an output iterator. It is provided to
 enable algorithms to store their results into uninitialized memory. The interface is quite simple: the constructor takes an output iterator that is pointing to the raw memory (thus it is typically a pointer) and the }{\b operator=}{
 assigns an object into that raw memory. The template parameters are the type of the output iterator pointing to the raw storage, and the type of object that will be stored. Here\rquote s an example which creates }{\b Noisy}{ objects (you\rquote 
ll be introduced to the }{\b Noisy}{ class shortly; it\rquote s not necessary to know its details for this example):
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:RawStorageIterator.cpp
\par // Demonstrate the raw_storage_iterator
\par #include "Noisy.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <iterator>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   const int quantity = 10;
\par   // Create raw storage and cast to desired type:
\par   Noisy* np = 
\par     (Noisy*)new char[quantity * sizeof(Noisy)];
\par   raw_storage_iterator<Noisy*, Noisy> rsi(np);
\par   for(int i = 0; i < quantity; i++)
\par     *rsi++ = Noisy(); // Place objects in storage
\par   cout << endl;
\par   copy(np, np + quantity,
\par     ostream_iterator<Noisy>(cout, " "));
\par   cout << endl;
\par   // Explicit destructor call for cleanup:
\par   for(int j = 0; j < quantity; j++)
\par     (&np[j])->~Noisy();
\par   // Release raw storage:
\par   delete (char*)np;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To make the }{\b raw_storage_iterator }{template happy, the raw storage must be of the same type as the objects you\rquote re creating. That\rquote s why the pointer from the new array of }{\b char}{
 is cast to a }{\b Noisy*}{. The assignment operator forces the objects into the raw storage using the
 copy-constructor. Note that the explicit destructor call must be made for proper cleanup, and this also allows the objects to be deleted one at a time during container manipulation.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776391}Basic sequences: \line vector, list & deque{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776391}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you take a step back from the STL containers you\rquote ll see that there are really only two types of container: }{\i sequences}{ (including }{\b vector}{, }{\b list}{, }{\b deque}{, }{\b stack}{
, }{\b queue}{, and }{\b priority_queue}{)}{\b  }{and }{\i associations}{ (including }{\b set}{, }{\b multiset}{, }{\b map}{ and }{\b multimap}{). The sequences keep the objects in whatever
 sequence that you establish (either by pushing the objects on the end or inserting them in the middle).
\par Since all the sequence containers have the same basic goal (to maintain your order) they seem relatively interchangeable. However, they differ in the e
fficiency of their operations, so if you are going to manipulate a sequence in a particular fashion you can choose the appropriate container for those types of manipulations. The \ldblquote basic\rdblquote  sequence containers are }{\b vector}{, }{\b list
}{ and }{\b deque}{ \endash  these actually have fleshed-out implementations, while }{\b stack}{, }{\b queue}{ and }{\b priority_queue}{
 are built on top of the basic sequences, and represent more specialized uses rather than differences in underlying structure (}{\b stack}{, for example, can be implemented using a }{\b deque}{, }{\b vector}{ or }{\b list}{).
\par So far in this book I have been using }{\b vector}{ as a catch-all container. This was acceptable because I\rquote ve only used the simplest and safest operations, primarily }{\b push_back(\~)}{ and }{\b operator[ ]}{
. However, when you start making more sophisticated uses of containers it becomes important to know more about their underlying implementations and behavior, so you can make the right choices (and, as you\rquote ll see, stay out of trouble).
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776392}Basic sequence operations{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776392}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Using a template, the following example shows the operations that all the basic sequences (}{\b vector}{, }{\b deque}{ or }{\b list}{
) support. As you shall learn in the sections on the specific sequence containers, not all of these operations make sense for each basic sequence, but they are supported. 
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:BasicSequenceOperations.cpp
\par // The operations available for all the 
\par // basic sequence Containers.
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <deque>
\par #include <list>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par template<typename Container>
\par void print(Container& c, char* s = "") \{
\par   cout << s << ":" << endl;
\par   if(c.empty()) \{
\par     cout << "(empty)" << endl;
\par     return;
\par   \}
\par   typename Container::iterator it;
\par   for(it = c.begin(); it != c.end(); it++)
\par     cout << *it << " ";
\par   cout << endl;
\par   cout << "size() " << c.size() 
\par     << " max_size() "<< c.max_size() 
\par     << " front() " << c.front()
\par     << " back() " << c.back() << endl;
\par \}
\par   
\par template<typename ContainerOfInt>
\par void basicOps(char* s) \{
\par   cout << "------- " << s << " -------" << endl;
\par   typedef ContainerOfInt Ci;
\par   Ci c;
\par   print(c, "c after default constructor");
\par   Ci c2(10, 1); // 10 elements, values all 1
\par   print(c2, "c2 after constructor(10,1)");
\par   int ia[] = \{ 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 \};
\par   const int iasz = sizeof(ia)/sizeof(*ia);
\par   // Initialize with begin & end iterators:
\par   Ci c3(ia, ia + iasz);
\par   print(c3, "c3 after constructor(iter,iter)");
\par   Ci c4(c2); // Copy-constructor
\par   print(c4, "c4 after copy-constructor(c2)");
\par   c = c2; // Assignment operator
\par   print(c, "c after operator=c2");
\par   c.assign(10, 2); // 10 elements, values all 2
\par   print(c, "c after assign(10, 2)");
\par   // Assign with begin & end iterators:
\par   c.assign(ia, ia + iasz);
\par   print(c, "c after assign(iter, iter)");
\par   cout << "c using reverse iterators:" << endl;
\par   typename Ci::reverse_iterator rit = c.rbegin();
\par   while(rit != c.rend())
\par     cout << *rit++ << " ";
\par   cout << endl;
\par   c.resize(4);
\par   print(c, "c after resize(4)");
\par   c.push_back(47);
\par   print(c, "c after push_back(47)");
\par   c.pop_back();
\par   print(c, "c after pop_back()");
\par   typename Ci::iterator it = c.begin();
\par   it++; it++;
\par   c.insert(it, 74);
\par   print(c, "c after insert(it, 74)");
\par   it = c.begin();
\par   it++;
\par   c.insert(it, 3, 96);
\par   print(c, "c after insert(it, 3, 96)");
\par   it = c.begin();
\par   it++;
\par   c.insert(it, c3.begin(), c3.end());
\par   print(c, "c after insert("
\par     "it, c3.begin(), c3.end())");
\par   it = c.begin();
\par   it++;
\par   c.erase(it);
\par   print(c, "c after erase(it)");
\par   typename Ci::iterator it2 = it = c.begin();
\par   it++;
\par   it2++; it2++; it2++; it2++; it2++;
\par   c.erase(it, it2);
\par   print(c, "c after erase(it, it2)");
\par   c.swap(c2);
\par   print(c, "c after swap(c2)");
\par   c.clear();
\par   print(c, "c after clear()");
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   basicOps<vector<int> >("vector");
\par   basicOps<deque<int> >("deque");
\par   basicOps<list<int> >("list");
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The first function template, }{\b print(\~)}{, demonstrates the basic information you can get from any sequence container: whether it\rquote 
s empty, its current size, the size of the largest possible container, the element at the beginning and the element at the end. You can also see that every container has }{\b begin(\~)}{ and }{\b end(\~)}{ methods that return iterators.
\par The }{\b basicOps(\~)}{ function tests everything else (and in turn calls }{\b print(\~)}{), including a variety of constructors: default, copy-constructor, quantity and initial value, and beginning and ending iterators. There\rquote s an assignment }{\b 
operator=}{ and two kinds of }{\b assign(\~)}{ member functions, one which takes a quantity and initial value and the other which take a beginning and ending iterator.
\par All the basic sequence containers are reversible containers, as shown by the use of the }{\b rbegin(\~)}{ and }{\b rend(\~)}{ member functions. A sequence container can be resized, and the entire contents of the container can be removed with }{\b clear(\~
)}{.
\par Using an iterator to indicate where you want to start inserting into any sequence container, you can }{\b insert(\~)}{ a singl
e element, a number of elements that all have the same value, and a group of elements from another container using the beginning and ending iterators of that group. 
\par To }{\b erase(\~)}{ a single element from the middle, use an iterator; to }{\b erase(\~)}{ a range of elements, use a pair of iterators. Notice that since a }{\b list}{
 only supports bidirectional iterators, all the iterator motion must be performed with increments and decrements (if the containers were limited to }{\b vector }{and }{\b deque}{, which produce random-access iterators, then }{\b operator+}{ and }{\b 
operator-}{ could have been used to move the iterators in big jumps).
\par Although both }{\b list}{ and }{\b deque}{ support }{\b push_front(\~)}{ and }{\b pop_front(\~)}{, }{\b vector}{ does not, so the only member functions that work with all three are }{\b push_back(\~)}{ and }{\b pop_back(\~)}{.
\par The naming of the member function }{\b swap(\~)}{ is a little confusing, since there\rquote s also a non-member }{\b swap(\~)}{ algorithm that switches two elements of a container. The member }{\b swap(\~)}{, however, swaps }{\i everything}{
 in one container for another (if the containers hold the same type), effectively swapping the containers themselves. There\rquote s also a non-member version of this function.
\par The following sections on the sequence containers discuss the particulars of each type of container.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776393}vector{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776393}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b vector}{ is intentionally made to look like a souped-up array, since it has array-style indexing but also can expand dynamically. }{\b vector}{
 is so fundamentally useful that it was introduced in a very primitive way early in this book, and used quite regularly in previous examples. This section will give a more in-depth look at }{\b vector}{.
\par To achieve maximally-fast indexing and iteration, the }{\b vector}{ maintains its storage as a single contiguous array of objects. This is a critical point to observe in understanding the behavior of }{\b vector}{
. It means that indexing and iteration are lighting-fast, being basically the same as indexing and iterating over an array of objects. But it also means that inserting an object anywhere but at the end (that is, appending) is not really an acceptable oper
ation for a }{\b vector}{. It also means that when a }{\b vector}{
 runs out of pre-allocated storage, in order to maintain its contiguous array it must allocate a whole new (larger) chunk of storage elsewhere and copy the objects to the new storage. This has a number of unpleasant side effects.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776394}Cost of overflowing allocated storage{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776394}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A }{\b vector }{starts by grabbing a block of storage, as if it\rquote s taking a guess at how many objects you plan to put in it. As long as you don\rquote 
t try to put in more objects than can be held in the initial block of storage, everything is very rapid and efficient (note that if you }{\i do }{know how many objects to expect, you can pre-allocate storage using }{\b reserve(\~)}{
). But eventually you will put in one too many objects and, unbeknownst to you, the }{\b vector }{responds by:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls238\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls238\adjustright {
Allocating a new, bigger piece of storage
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls238\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls238\adjustright {
Copying all the objects from the old storage to the new (using the copy-constructor)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls238\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls238\adjustright {
Destroying all the old objects (the destructor is called for each one)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 4.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls238\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls238\adjustright {Releasing the old memory
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {For complex objects, this copy-construction and destruction can end up being very expensive if you overfill your vector a lot. To see what happens when you\rquote re filling a }{\b vector}{
, here is a class that prints out information about its creations, destructions, assignments and copy-constructions:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:Noisy.h
\par // A class to track various object activities
\par #ifndef NOISY_H
\par #define NOISY_H
\par #include <iostream>
\par 
\par class Noisy \{
\par   static long create, assign, copycons, destroy;
\par   long id;
\par public:
\par   Noisy() : id(create++) \{ 
\par     std::cout << "d[" << id << "]"; 
\par   \}
\par   Noisy(const Noisy& rv) : id(rv.id) \{
\par     std::cout << "c[" << id << "]";
\par     copycons++;
\par   \}
\par   Noisy& operator=(const Noisy& rv) \{
\par     std::cout << "(" << id << ")=[" <<
\par       rv.id << "]";
\par     id = rv.id;
\par     assign++;
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par   friend bool 
\par   operator<(const Noisy& lv, const Noisy& rv) \{
\par     return lv.id < rv.id;
\par   \}
\par   friend bool 
\par   operator==(const Noisy& lv, const Noisy& rv) \{
\par     return lv.id == rv.id;
\par   \}
\par   ~Noisy() \{
\par     std::cout << "~[" << id << "]";
\par     destroy++;
\par   \}
\par   friend std::ostream& 
\par   operator<<(std::ostream& os, const Noisy& n) \{
\par     return os << n.id;
\par   \}
\par   friend class NoisyReport;
\par \};
\par 
\par struct NoisyGen \{
\par   Noisy operator()() \{ return Noisy(); \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // A singleton. Will automatically report the
\par // statistics as the program terminates:
\par class NoisyReport \{
\par   static NoisyReport nr;
\par   NoisyReport() \{\} // Private constructor
\par public:
\par   ~NoisyReport() \{
\par     std::cout << "\\n-------------------\\n"
\par       << "Noisy creations: " << Noisy::create
\par       << "\\nCopy-Constructions: " 
\par       << Noisy::copycons
\par       << "\\nAssignments: " << Noisy::assign
\par       << "\\nDestructions: " << Noisy::destroy
\par       << std::endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Because of these this file can only be used
\par // in simple test situations. Move them to a 
\par // .cpp file for more complex programs:
\par long Noisy::create = 0, Noisy::assign = 0,
\par   Noisy::copycons = 0, Noisy::destroy = 0;
\par NoisyReport NoisyReport::nr;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // NOISY_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Each }{\b Noisy}{ object has its own identifier, and there are }{\b static}{ variables to keep track of all the creations, assignments (using }{\b operator=}{
), copy-constructions and destructions. The }{\b id}{ is initialized using the }{\b create}{ counter inside the default constructor; the copy-constructor and assignment operator take their }{\b id}{ values from the rvalue. Of course, with }{\b operator=}{
 the lvalue is already an initialized object so the old value of }{\b id}{ is printed before it is overwritten with the }{\b id}{ from the rvalue.
\par In order to support certain operations like sorting and searching (which are used implicitly by some of the containers), }{\b Noisy}{ must have an }{\b operator<}{ and }{\b operator==}{. These simply compare the }{\b id}{ values. The }{\b operator<<}{
 for }{\b ostream}{ follows the standard form and simply prints the }{\b id}{.
\par }{\b NoisyGen}{ produces a function object (since it has an }{\b operator(\~)}{) that is used to automatically generate }{\b Noisy}{ objects during testing.
\par }{\b NoisyReport}{ is a type of class called a }{\i singleton}{, which is a \ldblquote design pattern\rdblquote  (these are covered more fully in Chapter XX). Here, the goal is to make sure there is one and only one }{\b NoisyReport}{
 object, because it is responsible for printing out the results at program termination. It has a }{\b private}{ constructor so no one else can make a }{\b NoisyReport}{ object, and a single static instance of }{\b NoisyReport}{ called }{\b nr}{
. The only executable statements are in the destructor, which is called as the program exits and the static destructors are called; this destructor prints out the statistics captured by the }{\b static}{ variables in }{\b Noisy}{.
\par The one snag to this header file is the inclusion of the definitions for the }{\b static}{s at the end. If you include this header in more than one place in your project, you\rquote 
ll get multiple-definition errors at link time. Of course, you can put the }{\b static}{ definitions in a separate}{\b  cpp}{ file and link it in, but that is less convenient, and since }{\b Noisy}{
 is just intended for quick-and-dirty experiments the header file should be reasonable for most situations.
\par Using }{\b Noisy.h}{, the following program will show the behaviors that occur when a }{\b vector}{ overflows its currently allocated storage:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:VectorOverflow.cpp
\par // Shows the copy-construction and destruction
\par // That occurs when a vector must reallocate
\par // (It maintains a linear array of elements)
\par #include "Noisy.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1);
\par   int size = 1000;
\par   if(argc >= 2) size = atoi(argv[1]);
\par   vector<Noisy> vn;
\par   Noisy n;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < size; i++)
\par     vn.push_back(n);
\par   cout << "\\n cleaning up \\n";
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can either use the default value of 1000, or use your own value by putting it on the command-line.
\par When you run this program, you\rquote ll see a single default constructor call (for }{\b n}{), then a lot of copy-constructor calls, then som
e destructor calls, then some more copy-constructor calls, and so on. When the vector runs out of space in the linear array of bytes it has allocated, it must (to maintain all the objects in a linear array, which is an essential part of its job) get a big
ger piece of storage and move everything over, copying first and then destroying the old objects. You can imagine that if you store a lot of large and complex objects, this process could rapidly become prohibitive.
\par There are two solutions to this problem. The nicest one requires that you know beforehand how many objects you\rquote re going to make. In that case you can use }{\b reserve(\~)}{
 to tell the vector how much storage to pre-allocate, thus eliminating all the copies and destructions and making everything very fast (especially random access to the objects with }{\b operator[ ]}{). Note that the use of }{\b reserve(\~)}{
 is different from the using the }{\b vector}{ constructor with an integral first argument; the latter initializes each element using the default copy-constructor.
\par However, in the more general case you won\rquote t know how many objects you\rquote ll need. If }{\b vector}{ reallocations are slowing things down, you can change sequence containers. You could use a }{\b list}{, but}{\b  }{as you\rquote ll see, the }{
\b deque}{ allows speedy insertions at either end of the sequence, and never needs to copy or destroy objects as it expands its storage. The }{\b deque}{ also allows random access with }{\b operator[ ]}{, but it\rquote s not quite as fast as }{\b vector}{
\rquote s }{\b operator[}{ }{\b ]}{. So in the case where you\rquote re creating all your objects in one part of the program and randomly accessing them in another, you may find yourself filling a }{\b deque}{, then creating a }{\b vector}{ from the }{\b 
deque}{ and using the }{\b vector}{ for rapid indexing. Of course, you don\rquote t want to program this way habitually, just be aware of these issues (avoid premature optimization).
\par There is a darker side to }{\b vector}{\rquote s reallocation of memory, however. Because }{\b vector}{ keeps its objects in a nice, neat array (allowing, for one thing, maximally-fast random access), the iterators used by }{\b vector}{ are generally ju
st pointers. This is a good thing \endash  of all the sequence containers, these pointers allow the fastest selection and manipulation. However, consider what happens when you\rquote 
re holding onto an iterator (i.e. a pointer) and then you add the one additional object that causes the }{\b vector}{ to reallocate storage and move it elsewhere. Your pointer is now pointing off into nowhere:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:VectorCoreDump.cpp
\par // How to break a program using a vector
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<int> vi(10, 0);
\par   ostream_iterator<int> out(cout, " ");
\par   copy(vi.begin(), vi.end(), out);
\par   vector<int>::iterator i = vi.begin();
\par   cout << "\\n i: " << long(i) << endl;
\par   *i = 47;
\par   copy(vi.begin(), vi.end(), out);
\par   // Force it to move memory (could also just add
\par   // enough objects):
\par   vi.resize(vi.capacity() + 1);
\par   // Now i points to wrong memory:
\par   cout << "\\n i: " << long(i) << endl;
\par   cout << "vi.begin(): " << long(vi.begin());
\par   *i = 48;  // Access violation
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If your program is breaking mysteriously, look for places where you hold onto an iterator while adding more objects to a }{\b vector}{. You\rquote 
ll need to get a new iterator after adding elements, or use }{\b operator[ ] }{instead for element selections. If you combine the above observation with the awareness of the potential expense of adding new objects to a }{\b vector}{
, you may conclude that the safest way to use one is to fill it up all at once (ideally, knowing first how many objects you\rquote ll need) and then just use it (without adding more objects) elsewhere in the program. This is the way }{\b vector}{
 has been used in the book up to this point.
\par You may observe that using }{\b vector }{as the \ldblquote basic\rdblquote  container the book in earlier chapters may not be the best choice in all cases. This is a fundamental issue in containers, and in data structures in general: the \ldblquote best
\rdblquote  choice varies according to the way the container is used. The reason }{\b vector}{ has been the \ldblquote best\rdblquote  choice up until now is that it looks a lot like an array, and was thus familiar and easy for you to ado
pt. But from now on it\rquote s also worth thinking about other issues when choosing containers.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776395}Inserting and erasing elements{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776395}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b vector}{ is most efficient if:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls239\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls239\adjustright {You }{\b reserve(\~)}{
 the correct amount of storage at the beginning so the }{\b vector}{ never has to reallocate.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls239\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls239\adjustright {Y
ou only add and remove elements from the back end.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {It is possible to insert and erase elements from the middle of a }{\b vector}{ using an iterator, but the following program demonstrates what a bad idea it is:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:VectorInsertAndErase.cpp
\par // Erasing an element from a vector
\par #include "Noisy.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<Noisy> v;
\par   v.reserve(11);
\par   cout << "11 spaces have been reserved" << endl;
\par   generate_n(back_inserter(v), 10, NoisyGen());
\par   ostream_iterator<Noisy> out(cout, " ");
\par   cout << endl;
\par   copy(v.begin(), v.end(), out);
\par   cout << "Inserting an element:" << endl;
\par   vector<Noisy>::iterator it = 
\par     v.begin() + v.size() / 2; // Middle
\par   v.insert(it, Noisy());
\par   cout << endl;
\par   copy(v.begin(), v.end(), out);
\par   cout << "\\nErasing an element:" << endl;
\par   // Cannot use the previous value of it:
\par   it = v.begin() + v.size() / 2;
\par   v.erase(it);
\par   cout << endl;
\par   copy(v.begin(), v.end(), out);
\par   cout << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you run the program you\rquote ll see that the call to }{\b reserve(\~)}{ really does only allocate storage \endash  no constructors are called. The }{\b generate_n(\~)}{
 call is pretty busy: each call to }{\b NoisyGen::operator(\~)}{ results in a construction, a copy-construction (into the }{\b vector}{) and a destruction of the temporary. But when an object is inserted into the }{\b vector}{
 in the middle, it must shove everything down to maintain the linear array and \endash  since there is enough space \endash  it does this with the assignment operator (if the argument of }{\b reserve(\~)}{
 is 10 instead of eleven then it would have to reallocate storage). When an object is erased from the }{\b vector}{
, the assignment operator is once again used to move everything up to cover the place that is being erased (notice that this requires that the assignment operator properly cleans up the lvalue). Lastly, the object on the end of the array is deleted.

\par You can imagine how enormous the overhead can become if objects are inserted and removed from the middle of a }{\b vector}{ if the number of elements is large and the objects are complicated. It\rquote s obviously a practice to avoid.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776396}deque{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776396}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b deque}{ (double-ended-queue, pronounced \ldblquote deck\rdblquote 
) is the basic sequence container optimized for adding and removing elements from either end. It also allows for reasonably fast random access \endash  it has an }{\b operator[ ]}{ like }{\b vector}{. However, it does not have }{\b vector}{\rquote 
s constraint of keeping everything in a single sequential block of memory. Instead, }{\b deque}{ uses multiple blocks of sequential storage (keeping track of all the blocks and their order in a mapping structure). For this reason the overhead for a }{\b 
deque}{ to add or remove elements at either end is very low. In addition, it never needs to copy and destroy contained objects during a new storage allocation (like }{\b vector}{ does) so it is far more efficient than }{\b vector}{
 if you are adding an unknown quantity of objects. This means that }{\b vector}{ is the best choice only if you have a pretty good idea of how many objects you need. In addition, many of the programs shown earlier in this book that use }{\b vector}{ and }
{\b push_back(\~)}{ might be more efficient with a }{\b deque}{. The interface to }{\b deque}{ is only slightly different from a }{\b vector}{ (deque has a }{\b push_front(\~)}{ and }{\b pop_front(\~)}{ while }{\b vector}{
 does not, for example) so converting code from using }{\b vector}{ to using }{\b deque}{ is almost trivial. Consider }{\b StringVector.cpp}{, which can be changed to use }{\b deque}{ by replacing the word \ldblquote vector\rdblquote  with \ldblquote 
deque\rdblquote  everywhere. The following program adds parallel }{\b deque}{ operations to the }{\b vector}{ operations in }{\b StringVector.cpp}{, and performs timing comparisons:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:StringDeque.cpp
\par // Converted from StringVector.cpp
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <string>
\par #include <deque>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <iterator>
\par #include <sstream>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1);
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]);
\par   vector<string> vstrings;
\par   deque<string> dstrings;
\par   string line;
\par   // Time reading into vector:
\par   clock_t ticks = clock();
\par   while(getline(in, line))
\par     vstrings.push_back(line);
\par   ticks = clock() - ticks;
\par   cout << "Read into vector: " << ticks << endl;
\par   // Repeat for deque:
\par   ifstream in2(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in2, argv[1]);
\par   ticks = clock();
\par   while(getline(in2, line))
\par     dstrings.push_back(line);
\par   ticks = clock() - ticks;
\par   cout << "Read into deque: " << ticks << endl;
\par   // Now compare indexing:
\par   ticks = clock();
\par   for(int i = 0; i < vstrings.size(); i++) \{
\par     ostringstream ss;
\par     ss << i;
\par     vstrings[i] = ss.str() + ": " + vstrings[i];
\par   \}
\par   ticks = clock() - ticks;
\par   cout << "Indexing vector: " << ticks << endl;
\par   ticks = clock();
\par   for(int j = 0; j < dstrings.size(); j++) \{
\par     ostringstream ss;
\par     ss << j;
\par     dstrings[j] = ss.str() + ": " + dstrings[j];
\par   \}
\par   ticks = clock() - ticks;
\par   cout << "Indexing deqeue: " << ticks << endl;
\par   // Compare iteration
\par   ofstream tmp1("tmp1.tmp"), tmp2("tmp2.tmp");
\par   ticks = clock();
\par   copy(vstrings.begin(), vstrings.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<string>(tmp1, "\\n"));
\par   ticks = clock() - ticks;
\par   cout << "Iterating vector: " << ticks << endl;
\par   ticks = clock();
\par   copy(dstrings.begin(), dstrings.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<string>(tmp2, "\\n"));
\par   ticks = clock() - ticks;
\par   cout << "Iterating deqeue: " << ticks << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Knowing now what you do about the inefficiency of adding things to }{\b vector}{
 because of storage reallocation, you may expect dramatic differences between the two. However, on a 1.7 Megabyte text file one compiler\rquote s program produced the following (measured in platform/compiler specific clock ticks, not seconds):
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Read into vector: 8350
\par Read into deque: 7690
\par Indexing vector: 2360
\par Indexing deqeue: 2480
\par Iterating vector: 2470
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Iterating deqeue: 2410
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A different compiler and platform roughly agreed with this. It\rquote s not so dramatic, is it? This points out some important issues:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls240\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls240\adjustright {
We (programmers) are typically very bad at guessing where inefficiencies occur in our programs.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls240\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls240\adjustright {
Efficiency comes from a combination of effects \endash  here, reading the lines in and converting them to strings may dominate over the cost of the }{\b vector}{ vs. }{\b deque}{.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls240\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls240\adjustright {The }{\b string}{
 class is probably fairly well-designed in terms of efficiency.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {Of course, this doesn\rquote t mean you shouldn\rquote t use a }{\b deque}{ rather than a }{\b vector}{ when you know that an uncertain number of objects will be pushed
 onto the end of the container. On the contrary, you should \endash  when you\rquote 
re tuning for performance. But you should also be aware that performance issues are usually not where you think they are, and the only way to know for sure where your bottlenecks are is by testing. Later in this chapter there will be a more \ldblquote 
pure\rdblquote  comparison of performance between }{\b vector}{, }{\b deque}{ and }{\b list}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776397}Converting between sequences{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776397}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Sometimes you need the behavior or efficiency of one kind of container for one part of your program, and a different container\rquote 
s behavior or efficiency in another part of the program. For example, you may need the efficiency of a }{\b deque}{ when adding objects to the container but the efficiency of a }{\b vector}{ when indexing them. Each of the basic sequence containers (}{\b 
vector}{, }{\b deque}{ and }{\b list}{) has a two-iterator constructor (indicating the beginning and ending of the sequence to read from when creating a new object) and an }{\b assign(\~)}{
 member function to read into an existing container, so you can easily move objects from one sequence container to another.
\par The following example reads objects into a }{\b deque}{ and then converts to a }{\b vector}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:DequeConversion.cpp
\par // Reading into a Deque, converting to a vector
\par #include "Noisy.h"
\par #include <deque>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   int size = 25;
\par   if(argc >= 2) size = atoi(argv[1]);
\par   deque<Noisy> d;
\par   generate_n(back_inserter(d), size, NoisyGen());
\par   cout << "\\n Converting to a vector(1)" << endl;
\par   vector<Noisy> v1(d.begin(), d.end());
\par   cout << "\\n Converting to a vector(2)" << endl;
\par   vector<Noisy> v2;
\par   v2.reserve(d.size());
\par   v2.assign(d.begin(), d.end());
\par   cout << "\\n Cleanup" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can try various sizes, but you should see that it makes no difference \endash  the objects are simply copy-constructed into the new }{\b vector}{s. What\rquote s interesting is that }{\b v1}{
 does not cause multiple allocations while building the }{\b vector}{, no matter how many elements you use. You might initially think that you must follow the process used for }{\b v2}{
 and preallocate the storage to prevent messy reallocations, but the constructor used for }{\b v1}{ determines the memory need ahead of time so this is unnecessary.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776398}Cost of overflowing allocated storage{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776398}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It\rquote s illuminating to see what happens with a }{\b deque}{ when it overflows a block of storage, in contrast with }{\b VectorOverflow.cpp}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:DequeOverflow.cpp
\par // A deque is much more efficient than a vector
\par // when pushing back a lot of elements, since it
\par // doesn't require copying and destroying.
\par #include "Noisy.h"
\par #include <deque>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   int size = 1000;
\par   if(argc >= 2) size = atoi(argv[1]);
\par   deque<Noisy> dn;
\par   Noisy n;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < size; i++)
\par     dn.push_back(n);
\par   cout << "\\n cleaning up \\n";
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here you will never see any destructors before the words \ldblquote cleaning up\rdblquote  appear. Since the }{\b deque}{ allocates all its storage in blocks instead of a contiguous array like }{\b 
vector}{, it never needs to move existing storage (thus no additional copy-constructions and destructions occur). It simply allocates a new block. For the same reason, the }{\b deque}{ can just as efficiently add elements to the }{\i beginning}{
 of the sequence, since if it runs out of storage it (again) just allocates a new block for the beginning. Insertions in the middle of a }{\b deque}{, however, could be even messier than for }{\b vector}{ (but not as costly).
\par Because a }{\b deque }{never moves its storage, a held iterator never becomes invalid when you add new things to either end of a deque, as it was demonstrated to do with }{\b vector}{ (in }{\b VectorCoreDump.cpp}{). However, it\rquote 
s still possible (albeit harder) to do bad things:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:DequeCoreDump.cpp
\par // How to break a program using a deque
\par #include <queue>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   deque<int> di(100, 0);
\par   // No problem iterating from beginning to end,
\par   // even though it spans multiple blocks:
\par   copy(di.begin(), di.end(), 
\par     ostream_iterator<int>(cout, " "));
\par   deque<int>::iterator i = // In the middle:
\par     di.begin() + di.size() / 2;;
\par   // Walk the iterator forward as you perform 
\par   // a lot of insertions in the middle:
\par   for(int j = 0; j < 1000; j++) \{
\par     cout << j << endl;
\par     di.insert(i++, 1); // Eventually breaks
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Of course, there are two things here that you wouldn\rquote t normally do with a }{\b deque}{: first, elements are inserted in the middle, which }{\b deque}{ allows but isn\rquote 
t designed for. Second, calling }{\b insert(\~)}{ repeatedly with the same iterator would not ordinarily cause an access violation, but the iterator is walked forward after each insertion. I\rquote 
m guessing it eventually walks off the end of a block, but I\rquote m not sure what actually causes the problem.
\par If you stick to what }{\b deque}{ is best at \endash  insertions and removals from either end, reasonably rapid traversals and fairly fast random-access using }{\b operator[ ]}{ \endash  you\rquote ll be in good shape.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776399}Checked random-access{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776399}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Both }{\b vector }{and }{\b deque}{ provide two ways to perform random access of their elements: the }{\b operator[ ]}{, which you\rquote ve seen already, and }{\b at(\~)}{
, which checks the boundaries of the container that\rquote s being indexed and throws an exception if you go out of bounds. It does cost more to use }{\b at(\~)}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:IndexingVsAt.cpp
\par // Comparing "at()" to operator[]
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <deque>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireMinArgs(argc, 1);
\par   long count = 1000;
\par   int sz = 1000;
\par   if(argc >= 2) count = atoi(argv[1]);
\par   if(argc >= 3) sz = atoi(argv[2]);
\par   vector<int> vi(sz);
\par   clock_t ticks = clock();
\par   for(int i1 = 0; i1 < count; i1++)
\par     for(int j = 0; j < sz; j++)
\par       vi[j];
\par   cout << "vector[]" << clock() - ticks << endl;
\par   ticks = clock();
\par   for(int i2 = 0; i2 < count; i2++)
\par     for(int j = 0; j < sz; j++)
\par       vi.at(j);
\par   cout << "vector::at()" << clock()-ticks <<endl;
\par   deque<int> di(sz);
\par   ticks = clock();
\par   for(int i3 = 0; i3 < count; i3++)
\par     for(int j = 0; j < sz; j++)
\par       di[j];
\par   cout << "deque[]" << clock() - ticks << endl;
\par   ticks = clock();
\par   for(int i4 = 0; i4 < count; i4++)
\par     for(int j = 0; j < sz; j++)
\par       di.at(j);
\par   cout << "deque::at()" << clock()-ticks <<endl;
\par   // Demonstrate at() when you go out of bounds:
\par   di.at(vi.size() + 1);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As you\rquote ll learn in the exception-handling chapter, different systems may handle the uncaught exception in different ways, but you\rquote 
ll know one way or another that something went wrong with the program when using }{\b at(\~)}{, whereas it\rquote s possible to go blundering ahead using }{\b operator[ ]}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776400}list{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776400}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A }{\b list}{ is implemented as a doubly-linked list and is thus designed for rapid insertion and removal of elements in the middle of the sequence (whereas for }{\b vector}{ and }{\b deque }{
this is a much more costly operation). A list is so slow when randomly accessing elements that it does not have an }{\b operator[ ]}{. It\rquote s best used when you\rquote 
re traversing a sequence, in order, from beginning to end (or end to beginning) rather than choosing elements randomly from the middle. Even then the traversal is significantly slower than either a }{\b vector }{or a }{\b deque}{, but if you aren\rquote 
t doing a lot of traversals that won\rquote t be your bottleneck.
\par Another thing to be aware of with a }{\b list}{ is the memory overhead of each link, which requires a forward and backward pointer on top of the storage for the actual object. Thus a }{\b list}{ is a better choice when you have larger objects that you
\rquote ll be inserting and removing from the middle of the }{\b list}{. It\rquote s better not to use a }{\b list }{if you think you might be traversing it a lot, looking for objects, since the amount of time it takes to get from the beginning of the }{
\b list}{ \endash  which is the only place you can start unless you\rquote ve already got an iterator to somewhere you know is closer to your destination \endash  to the object of interest is proportional to the number of object
s between the beginning and that object.
\par The objects in a }{\b list }{never move after they are created; \ldblquote moving\rdblquote  a list element means changing the links, but never copying or assigning the actual objects. This means that a held iterator never moves when you add 
new things to a list as it was demonstrated to do in }{\b vector}{. Here\rquote s an example using the }{\b Noisy}{ class:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:ListStability.cpp
\par // Things don't move around in lists
\par #include "Noisy.h"
\par #include <list>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   list<Noisy> l;
\par   ostream_iterator<Noisy> out(cout, " ");
\par   generate_n(back_inserter(l), 25, NoisyGen());
\par   cout << "\\n Printing the list:" << endl;
\par   copy(l.begin(), l.end(), out);
\par   cout << "\\n Reversing the list:" << endl;
\par   l.reverse();
\par   copy(l.begin(), l.end(), out);
\par   cout << "\\n Sorting the list:" << endl;
\par   l.sort();
\par   copy(l.begin(), l.end(), out);
\par   cout << "\\n Swapping two elements:" << endl;
\par   list<Noisy>::iterator it1, it2;
\par   it1 = it2 = l.begin();
\par   it2++;
\par   swap(*it1, *it2);
\par   cout << endl;
\par   copy(l.begin(), l.end(), out);
\par   cout << "\\n Using generic reverse(): " << endl;
\par   reverse(l.begin(), l.end());
\par   cout << endl;
\par   copy(l.begin(), l.end(), out);
\par   cout << "\\n Cleanup" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Operations as seemingly radical as reversing and sorting the list require no copying of objects, because instead of moving the objects, the links are simply changed. However, notice that }{\b sort(\~)
}{ and }{\b reverse(\~)}{ are member functions of }{\b list}{, so they have special knowledge of the internals of }{\b list}{ and can perform the pointer movement instead of copying. On the other hand, the }{\b swap(\~)}{
 function is a generic algorithm, and doesn\rquote t know about }{\b list}{ in particular and so it uses the copying approach for swapping two elements. There are also generic algorithms for }{\b sort(\~)}{ and }{\b reverse(\~)}{
, but if you try to use these you\rquote ll discover that the generic }{\b reverse(\~)}{ performs lots of copying and destruction (so you should never use it with a }{\b list}{) and the generic }{\b sort(\~)}{ simply doesn\rquote t work because it requir
es random-access iterators that }{\b list}{ doesn\rquote t provide (a definite benefit, since this would certainly be an expensive way to sort compared to }{\b list}{\rquote s own}{\b  sort(\~)}{). The generic }{\b sort(\~)}{ and }{\b reverse(\~)}{
 should only be used with arrays, }{\b vector}{s and }{\b deque}{s.
\par If you have large and complex objects you may want to choose a }{\b list}{
 first, especially if construction, destruction, copy-construction and assignment are expensive and if you are doing things like sorting the objects or otherwise reordering them a lot.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776401}Special list operations{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776401}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b list}{ has some special operations that are built-in to make the best use of the structure of the }{\b list}{. You\rquote ve already seen }{\b reverse(\~)}{ and }{\b sort(\~)}{
, and here are some of the others in use:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:ListSpecialFunctions.cpp
\par #include "Noisy.h"
\par #include <list>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par using namespace std;
\par ostream_iterator<Noisy> out(cout, " ");
\par 
\par void print(list<Noisy>& ln, char* comment = "") \{
\par   cout << "\\n" << comment << ":\\n";
\par   copy(ln.begin(), ln.end(), out);
\par   cout << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   typedef list<Noisy> LN;
\par   LN l1, l2, l3, l4;
\par   generate_n(back_inserter(l1), 6, NoisyGen());
\par   generate_n(back_inserter(l2), 6, NoisyGen());
\par   generate_n(back_inserter(l3), 6, NoisyGen());
\par   generate_n(back_inserter(l4), 6, NoisyGen());
\par   print(l1, "l1"); print(l2, "l2");
\par   print(l3, "l3"); print(l4, "l4");
\par   LN::iterator it1 = l1.begin();
\par   it1++; it1++; it1++;
\par   l1.splice(it1, l2);
\par   print(l1, "l1 after splice(it1, l2)");
\par   print(l2, "l2 after splice(it1, l2)");
\par   LN::iterator it2 = l3.begin();
\par   it2++; it2++; it2++;
\par   l1.splice(it1, l3, it2);
\par   print(l1, "l1 after splice(it1, l3, it2)");
\par   LN::iterator it3 = l4.begin(), it4 = l4.end();
\par   it3++; it4--;
\par   l1.splice(it1, l4, it3, it4);
\par   print(l1, "l1 after splice(it1,l4,it3,it4)");
\par   Noisy n;
\par   LN l5(3, n);
\par   generate_n(back_inserter(l5), 4, NoisyGen());
\par   l5.push_back(n);
\par   print(l5, "l5 before remove()");
\par   l5.remove(l5.front());
\par   print(l5, "l5 after remove()");
\par   l1.sort(); l5.sort();
\par   l5.merge(l1);
\par   print(l5, "l5 after l5.merge(l1)");
\par   cout << "\\n Cleanup" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b print(\~)}{ function is used to display results. After filling four }{\b list}{s with }{\b Noisy}{
 objects, one list is spliced into another in three different ways. In the first, the entire list }{\b l2}{ is spliced into }{\b l1}{ at the iterator }{\b it1}{. Notice that after the splice, }{\b l2}{ is empty \endash 
 splicing means removing the elements from the source list. The second splice inserts elements from }{\b l3 }{starting at }{\b it2}{ into }{\b l1 }{starting at }{\b it1}{. The third splice starts at }{\b it1}{ and uses elements from }{\b l4}{ starting at 
}{\b it3}{ and ending at }{\b it4}{ (the seemingly-redundant mention of the source list is because the elements must be erased from the source list as part of the transfer to the destination list)}{\b .
\par }{The output from the code that demonstrates }{\b remove(\~)}{ shows that the list does not have to be sorted in order for all the elements of a particular value to be removed.
\par Finally, if you }{\b merge(\~)}{ one list with another, the merge only works sensibly if the lists have been sorted. What you end up with in that case is a sorted list containing all the elements from both lists (the source list is erased \endash 
 that is, the elements are }{\i moved}{ to the destination list).
\par There\rquote s also a }{\b unique(\~)}{ member function that removes all duplicates, but only if the }{\b list}{ has been sorted first:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:UniqueList.cpp
\par // Testing list's unique() function
\par #include <list>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int a[] = \{ 1, 3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 1, 6, 1 \};
\par const int asz = sizeof a / sizeof *a;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   // For output:
\par   ostream_iterator<int> out(cout, " ");
\par   list<int> li(a, a + asz);
\par   li.unique();
\par   // Oops! No duplicates removed:
\par   copy(li.begin(), li.end(), out);
\par   cout << endl;
\par   // Must sort it first:
\par   li.sort();
\par   copy(li.begin(), li.end(), out);
\par   cout << endl;
\par   // Now unique() will have an effect:
\par   li.unique();
\par   copy(li.begin(), li.end(), out);
\par   cout << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b list}{ constructor used here takes the starting and past-the-end iterator from another container, and it copies all the elements from that container into itself (a sim
ilar constructor is available for all the containers). Here, the \ldblquote container\rdblquote  is just an array, and the \ldblquote iterators\rdblquote 
 are pointers into that array, but because of the design of the STL it works with arrays just as easily as any other container.
\par If you run this program, you\rquote ll see that }{\b unique(\~)}{ will only remove }{\i adjacent}{ duplicate elements, and thus sorting is necessary before calling }{\b unique(\~)}{.
\par There are four additional }{\b list }{member functions that are not demonstrated here: a }{\b remove_if(\~)}{ that takes a predicate which is used to decide whether an object should be removed, a }{\b unique(\~)}{
 that takes a binary predicate to perform uniqueness comparisons, a }{\b merge(\~)}{ that takes an additional argument which performs comparisons, and a }{\b sort(\~)}{ that takes a comparator (to provide a comparison or override the existing one).
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\b list}{ vs. }{\b set}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Looking at the previous example you may note that if you want a sorted list with no duplicates, a }{\b set}{ can give you that, right? }{\b  }{It\rquote 
s interesting to compare the performance of the two containers:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:ListVsSet.cpp
\par // Comparing list and set performance
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <list>
\par #include <set>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par #include <ctime>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Obj \{
\par   int a[20];
\par   int val;
\par public:
\par   Obj() : val(rand() % 500) \{\}
\par   friend bool 
\par   operator<(const Obj& a, const Obj& b) \{
\par     return a.val < b.val;
\par   \}
\par   friend bool 
\par   operator==(const Obj& a, const Obj& b) \{
\par     return a.val == b.val;
\par   \}
\par   friend ostream& 
\par   operator<<(ostream& os, const Obj& a) \{
\par     return os << a.val;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class Container>
\par void print(Container& c) \{
\par   typename Container::iterator it;
\par   for(it = c.begin(); it != c.end(); it++)
\par     cout << *it << " ";
\par   cout << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par struct ObjGen \{
\par   Obj operator()() \{ return Obj(); \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   const int sz = 5000;
\par   srand(time(0));
\par   list<Obj> lo;
\par   clock_t ticks = clock();
\par   generate_n(back_inserter(lo), sz, ObjGen());
\par   lo.sort();
\par   lo.unique();
\par   cout << "list:" << clock() - ticks << endl;
\par   set<Obj> so;
\par   ticks = clock();
\par   generate_n(inserter(so, so.begin()), 
\par     sz, ObjGen());
\par   cout << "set:" << clock() - ticks << endl;
\par   print(lo);
\par   print(so);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you run the program, you should discover that }{\b set}{ is much faster than }{\b list}{. This is reassuring \endash  after all, it }{\i is}{ }{\b set}{\rquote s primary job description!
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776402}Swapping all basic sequences{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776402}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It turns out that all basic sequences have a member function }{\b swap(\~)}{ that\rquote s designed to switch one sequence with another (however, this }{\b swap(\~)}{
 is only defined for sequences of the same type). The member }{\b swap(\~)}{ makes use of its knowledge of the internal structure of the particular container in order to be efficient:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:Swapping.cpp
\par // All basic sequence containers can be swapped
\par #include "Noisy.h"
\par #include <list>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <deque>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par using namespace std;
\par ostream_iterator<Noisy> out(cout, " ");
\par 
\par template<class Cont>
\par void print(Cont& c, char* comment = "") \{
\par   cout << "\\n" << comment << ": ";
\par   copy(c.begin(), c.end(), out);
\par   cout << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par template<class Cont>
\par void testSwap(char* cname) \{
\par   Cont c1, c2;
\par   generate_n(back_inserter(c1), 10, NoisyGen());
\par   generate_n(back_inserter(c2), 5, NoisyGen());
\par   cout << "\\n" << cname << ":" << endl;
\par   print(c1, "c1"); print(c2, "c2");
\par   cout << "\\n Swapping the " << cname 
\par     << ":" << endl;
\par   c1.swap(c2);
\par   print(c1, "c1"); print(c2, "c2");
\par \}  
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   testSwap<vector<Noisy> >("vector");
\par   testSwap<deque<Noisy> >("deque");
\par   testSwap<list<Noisy> >("list");
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you run this, you\rquote ll di
scover that each type of sequence container is able to swap one sequence for another without any copying or assignments, even if the sequences are of different sizes. In effect, you\rquote re completely swapping the memory of one object for another.

\par The STL algorithms also contain a }{\b swap(\~)}{, and when this function is applied to two containers of the same type, it will use the member }{\b swap(\~)}{ to achieve fast performance. Consequently, if you apply the }{\b sort(\~)}{
 algorithm to a container of containers, you will find that the performance is very fast \endash  it turns out that fast sorting of a container of containers was a design goal of the STL.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776403}Robustness of lists{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776403}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To break a }{\b list}{, you have to work pretty hard:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:ListRobustness.cpp
\par // lists are harder to break
\par #include <list>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   list<int> li(100, 0);
\par   list<int>::iterator i = li.begin();
\par   for(int j = 0; j < li.size() / 2; j++)
\par     i++;
\par   // Walk the iterator forward as you perform 
\par   // a lot of insertions in the middle:
\par   for(int k = 0; k < 1000; k++)
\par     li.insert(i++, 1); // No problem
\par   li.erase(i);
\par   i++;
\par   *i = 2; // Oops! It's invalid
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When the link that the iterator }{\b i}{ was pointing to was erased, it was unlinked from the list and thus became invalid. Trying to move forward to the \ldblquote next link\rdblquote 
 from an invalid link is poorly-formed code. Notice that the operation that broke }{\b deque}{ in }{\b DequeCoreDump.cpp}{ is perfectly fine with a }{\b list}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776404}Performance comparison{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776404}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To get a better feel for the differences between the sequence containers, it\rquote s illuminating to race them against each other while performing various operations. 
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:SequencePerformance.cpp
\par // Comparing the performance of the basic
\par // sequence containers for various operations
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <queue>
\par #include <list>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <typeinfo>
\par #include <ctime>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class FixedSize \{
\par   int x[20];
\par   // Automatic generation of default constructor,
\par   // copy-constructor and operator=
\par \} fs;
\par 
\par template<class Cont>
\par struct InsertBack \{
\par   void operator()(Cont& c, long count) \{
\par     for(long i = 0; i < count; i++)
\par       c.push_back(fs);
\par   \}
\par   char* testName() \{ return "InsertBack"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class Cont>
\par struct InsertFront \{
\par   void operator()(Cont& c, long count) \{
\par     long cnt = count * 10;
\par     for(long i = 0; i < cnt; i++)
\par       c.push_front(fs);
\par   \}  
\par   char* testName() \{ return "InsertFront"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class Cont>
\par struct InsertMiddle \{
\par   void operator()(Cont& c, long count) \{
\par     typename Cont::iterator it;
\par     long cnt = count / 10;
\par     for(long i = 0; i < cnt; i++) \{
\par       // Must get the iterator every time to keep
\par       // from causing an access violation with
\par       // vector. Increment it to put it in the
\par       // middle of the container:
\par       it = c.begin();
\par       it++;
\par       c.insert(it, fs);
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par   char* testName() \{ return "InsertMiddle"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class Cont>
\par struct RandomAccess \{ // Not for list
\par   void operator()(Cont& c, long count) \{
\par     int sz = c.size();
\par     long cnt = count * 100;
\par     for(long i = 0; i < cnt; i++)
\par       c[rand() % sz];
\par   \}
\par   char* testName() \{ return "RandomAccess"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class Cont>
\par struct Traversal \{
\par   void operator()(Cont& c, long count) \{
\par     long cnt = count / 100;
\par     for(long i = 0; i < cnt; i++) \{
\par       typename Cont::iterator it = c.begin(),
\par         end = c.end();
\par       while(it != end) it++;
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par   char* testName() \{ return "Traversal"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class Cont>
\par struct Swap \{
\par   void operator()(Cont& c, long count) \{
\par     int middle = c.size() / 2;
\par     typename Cont::iterator it = c.begin(), 
\par       mid = c.begin();
\par     it++; // Put it in the middle
\par     for(int x = 0; x < middle + 1; x++)
\par       mid++;
\par     long cnt = count * 10;
\par     for(long i = 0; i < cnt; i++)
\par       swap(*it, *mid);
\par   \}
\par   char* testName() \{ return "Swap"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class Cont>
\par struct RemoveMiddle \{
\par   void operator()(Cont& c, long count) \{
\par     long cnt = count / 10;
\par     if(cnt > c.size()) \{
\par       cout << "RemoveMiddle: not enough elements"
\par         << endl;
\par       return;
\par     \}
\par     for(long i = 0; i < cnt; i++) \{
\par       typename Cont::iterator it = c.begin();
\par       it++;
\par       c.erase(it);
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par   char* testName() \{ return "RemoveMiddle"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class Cont>
\par struct RemoveBack \{
\par   void operator()(Cont& c, long count) \{
\par     long cnt = count * 10;
\par     if(cnt > c.size()) \{
\par       cout << "RemoveBack: not enough elements"
\par         << endl;
\par       return;
\par     \}
\par     for(long i = 0; i < cnt; i++)
\par       c.pop_back();
\par   \}
\par   char* testName() \{ return "RemoveBack"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par template<class Op, class Container>
\par void measureTime(Op f, Container& c, long count)\{
\par   string id(typeid(f).name());
\par   bool Deque = id.find("deque") != string::npos;
\par   bool List = id.find("list") != string::npos;
\par   bool Vector = id.find("vector") !=string::npos;
\par   string cont = Deque ? "deque" : List ? "list" 
\par     : Vector? "vector" : "unknown";
\par   cout << f.testName() << " for " << cont << ": ";
\par   // Standard C library CPU ticks:
\par   clock_t ticks = clock();
\par   f(c, count); // Run the test
\par   ticks = clock() - ticks;
\par   cout << ticks << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par typedef deque<FixedSize> DF;
\par typedef list<FixedSize> LF;
\par typedef vector<FixedSize> VF;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   srand(time(0));
\par   long count = 1000;
\par   if(argc >= 2) count = atoi(argv[1]);
\par   DF deq;
\par   LF lst;
\par   VF vec, vecres;
\par   vecres.reserve(count); // Preallocate storage
\par   measureTime(InsertBack<VF>(), vec, count);
\par   measureTime(InsertBack<VF>(), vecres, count);
\par   measureTime(InsertBack<DF>(), deq, count);
\par   measureTime(InsertBack<LF>(), lst, count);
\par   // Can't push_front() with a vector:
\par //! measureTime(InsertFront<VF>(), vec, count);
\par   measureTime(InsertFront<DF>(), deq, count);
\par   measureTime(InsertFront<LF>(), lst, count);
\par   measureTime(InsertMiddle<VF>(), vec, count);
\par   measureTime(InsertMiddle<DF>(), deq, count);
\par   measureTime(InsertMiddle<LF>(), lst, count);
\par   measureTime(RandomAccess<VF>(), vec, count);
\par   measureTime(RandomAccess<DF>(), deq, count);
\par   // Can't operator[] with a list:
\par //! measureTime(RandomAccess<LF>(), lst, count);
\par   measureTime(Traversal<VF>(), vec, count);
\par   measureTime(Traversal<DF>(), deq, count);
\par   measureTime(Traversal<LF>(), lst, count);
\par   measureTime(Swap<VF>(), vec, count);
\par   measureTime(Swap<DF>(), deq, count);
\par   measureTime(Swap<LF>(), lst, count);
\par   measureTime(RemoveMiddle<VF>(), vec, count);
\par   measureTime(RemoveMiddle<DF>(), deq, count);
\par   measureTime(RemoveMiddle<LF>(), lst, count);
\par   vec.resize(vec.size() * 10); // Make it bigger
\par   measureTime(RemoveBack<VF>(), vec, count);
\par   measureTime(RemoveBack<DF>(), deq, count);
\par   measureTime(RemoveBack<LF>(), lst, count);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This example makes heavy use of templates to eliminate redundancy, save space, guarantee identical code and improve clarity. Each test is represented by a class that is templatized on the contain
er it will operate on. The test itself is inside the }{\b operator(\~)}{ which, in each case, takes a reference to the container and a repeat count \endash 
 this count is not always used exactly as it is, but sometimes increased or decreased to prevent the test from being too short or too long. The repeat count is just a factor, and all tests are compared using the same value.
\par Each test class also has a member function that returns its name, so that it can easily be printed. You might think that this should be accomplishe
d using run-time type identification, but since the actual name of the class involves a template expansion, this is turns out to be the more direct approach.
\par The }{\b measureTime(\~)}{ function template takes as its first template argument the operation that it\rquote s going to test \endash  which is itself a class template selected from the group defined previously in the listing. The template argument }{\b 
Op}{ will not only contain the name of the class, but also (decorated into it) the type of the container it\rquote s working with. The RTTI }{\b typeid(\~)}{ operation allows the name of the class to be extracted as a }{\b char*}{
, which can then be used to create a }{\b string}{ called }{\b id}{. This }{\b string}{ can be searched using }{\b string::find(\~)}{ to look for }{\b deque}{, }{\b list}{ or }{\b vector}{. The }{\b bool}{ variable that corresponds to the matching }{\b 
string}{ becomes }{\b true}{, and this is used to properly initialize the }{\b string}{ }{\b cont}{ so the container name can be accurately printed, along with the test name.
\par Once the type of test and the container being tested has been printed out, the actual test is quite simple. The Standard C library function }{\b clock(\~)}{
 is used to capture the starting and ending CPU ticks (this is typically more fine-grained than trying to measure seconds). Since }{\b f}{ is an object of type }{\b Op}{, which is a class that has an }{\b operator(\~)}{, the line:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {f(c, count);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {is actually calling the }{\b operator(\~)}{ for the object }{\b f}{.
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{, you can see that each different type of test is run on each type of container, except for the containers that don\rquote t support the particular operation being tested (these are commented out).
\par When you run the program, you\rquote ll get comparative performance numbers for your particular compiler and your particular operating system and platform. Although this is only intended to give you a feel for the various performance feat
ures relative to the other sequences, it is not a bad way to get a quick-and-dirty idea of the behavior of your library, and also to compare one library with another.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776405}set{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776405}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b set}{ produces a container that will accept only one of each thing you place in it; it also sorts the elements (sorting isn\rquote 
t intrinsic to the conceptual definition of a set, but the STL }{\b set}{ stores its elements in a balanced binary tree to provide rapid lookups, thus producing sorted results when you traverse it). The first two examples in this chapter used }{\b set}{s.
}{\b 
\par }{Consider the problem of creating an index for a book. You might like to start with all the words in the book, but you only want one instance of each word and you want them sorted. Of course, a }{\b set}{ is perfect for this, and solves 
the problem effortlessly. However, there\rquote s also the problem of punctuation and any other non-alpha characters, which must be stripped off to generate proper words. One solution to this problem is to use the Standard C library function }{\b strtok(
\~)}{, which produces tokens (in our case, words) given a set of delimiters to strip out:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:WordList.cpp
\par // Display a list of words used in a document
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <string>
\par #include <cstring>
\par #include <set>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par const char* delimiters =
\par   " \\t;()\\"<>:\{\}[]+-=&*#.,/\\\\~";
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1);
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]);
\par   set<string> wordlist;
\par   string line;
\par   while(getline(in, line)) \{
\par     // Capture individual words:
\par     char* s = // Cast probably won\rquote t crash:
\par       strtok((char*)line.c_str(), delimiters);
\par     while(s) \{
\par       // Automatic type conversion:
\par       wordlist.insert(s); 
\par       s = strtok(0, delimiters);
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par   // Output results:
\par   copy(wordlist.begin(), wordlist.end(),
\par        ostream_iterator<string>(cout, "\\n"));
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b strtok(\~) }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C\: Standard C library function strtok(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {strtok(\~)\:
 Standard C library function}}}{takes the starting address of a character buffer (
the first argument) and looks for delimiters (the second argument). It replaces the delimiter with a zero, and returns the address of the beginning of the token. If you call it subsequent times with a first argument of zero it will continue extracting tok
ens from the rest of the string until it finds the end. In this case, the delimiters are those that delimit the keywords and identifiers of C++, so it extracts these keywords and identifiers. Each word is turned into a }{\b string}{ and placed into the }{
\b wordlist}{ vector, which eventually contains the whole file, broken up into words.
\par You don\rquote t have to use a }{\b set}{ just to get a sorted sequence. You can use the }{\b sort(\~)}{ function (along with a multitude of other functions in the STL) on different STL containers. However, it\rquote s likely that }{\b set}{
 will be faster.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776406}Eliminating }{\b strtok(\~)}{{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776406}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Some programmers consider }{\b strtok(\~)}{ to be the poorest design in the Standard C library because it uses a }{\b static}{ buffer to hold its data between function calls. This means:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 1. \tab}}\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls220\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls220\adjustright \fs20\lang1024\cgrid {You can\rquote t use }{\b strtok(\~) }{in two places at the same time
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 2. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls220\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls220\adjustright {You can\rquote t use }{\b strtok(\~) }{in a multithreaded program
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 3. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls220\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls220\adjustright {You can\rquote t use }{\b strtok(\~)}{ in a library that might be used in a multithreaded program
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 4. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls220\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls220\adjustright {\b strtok(\~)}{ modifies the input sequence, which can produce unexpected side effects
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 5. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls220\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls220\adjustright {\b strtok(\~)}{  depends on reading in \ldblquote lines\rdblquote , which means you need a buffer big enough for the longest line. This produces both wastefully-sized buffers, and lines longer than the \ldblquote longest\rdblquote 
 line. This can also introduce security holes. (Notice that the buffer size problem was eliminated in }{\b WordList.cpp}{ by using }{\b string}{ input, but this required a cast so that }{\b strtok(\~)}{ could modify the data in the string \endash 
 a dangerous approach for general-purpose programming).
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {For all these reasons it seems like a good idea to find an alternative for }{\b strtok(\~)}{. The following example will use an }{\b istreambuf_iterator}{
 (introduced earlier) to move the characters from one place (which happens to be an }{\b istream}{) to another (which happens to be a }{\b string}{), depending on whether the Standard C library function }{\b isalpha(\~)}{ is true:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:WordList2.cpp
\par // Eliminating strtok() from Wordlist.cpp
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <string>
\par #include <cstring>
\par #include <set>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <iterator>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   using namespace std;
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1);
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]);
\par   istreambuf_iterator<char> p(in), end;
\par   set<string> wordlist;
\par   while (p != end) \{
\par     string word;
\par     insert_iterator<string> 
\par       ii(word, word.begin());
\par     // Find the first alpha character:
\par     while(!isalpha(*p) && p != end)
\par       p++;
\par     // Copy until the first non-alpha character:
\par     while (isalpha(*p) && p != end)
\par       *ii++ = *p++;
\par     if (word.size() != 0)
\par       wordlist.insert(word);
\par   \} 
\par   // Output results:
\par   copy(wordlist.begin(), wordlist.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<string>(cout, "\\n"));
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This example was suggested by Nathan Myers, who invented the }{\b istreambuf_iterator}{ and its relatives. This iterator extracts information character-by-character from a stream. Although the }{\b 
istreambuf_iterator }{template argument might suggest to you that you could extract, for example, }{\b int}{s instead of }{\b char}{, that\rquote s not the case. The argument must be of some character type \endash  a regular }{\b char}{
 or a wide character.
\par After the file is open, an }{\b istreambuf_iterator}{ called }{\b p}{ is attached to the }{\b istream}{ so characters can be extracted from it. The }{\b set<string>}{ called }{\b wordlist}{ will be used to hold the resulting words.
\par The }{\b while}{ loop reads words until the end of the input stream is found. This is detected using the default constructor for }{\b istreambuf_iterator}{ which produces the past-the-end iterator object }{\b end}{
. Thus, if you want to test to make sure you\rquote re not at the end of the stream, you simply say }{\b p != end}{.
\par The second type of iterator that\rquote s used here is the }{\b insert_iterator}{, which creates an iterator that knows how to insert objects into a container. Here, the \ldblquote container\rdblquote  is the }{\b string}{ called }{\b word}{
 which, for the purposes of }{\b insert_iterator}{, behaves like a container. The constructor for }{\b insert_iterator}{ requires the container and an iterator indicating where it should start inserting the characters. You could also use a }{\b 
back_insert_iterator}{, which requires that the container have a }{\b push_back(\~)}{ (}{\b string}{ does). There is also a }{\b back_insert_iterator}{, but that requires that the container have a }{\b push_back(\~)}{, which }{\b string}{ does not.
\par After the }{\b while}{ loop sets everything up, it begins by looking for the first alpha character, incrementing }{\b start}{ until that chara
cter is found. Then it copies characters from one iterator to the other, stopping when a non-alpha character is found. Each }{\b word}{, assuming it is non-empty, is added to }{\b wordlist}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776407}StreamTokenizer}{: \line a more flexible solution{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776407}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The above program parses its input into strings of words containing only alpha characters, but that\rquote s still a special case compared to the generality of }{\b strtok(\~)}{. What we\rquote 
d like now is an actual replacement for }{\b strtok(\~)}{ so we\rquote re never tempted to use it. }{\b WordList2.cpp}{ can be modified to create a class called }{\b StreamTokenizer}{ that delivers a new token as a }{\b string}{ whenever you call }{\b 
next(\~)}{, according to the delimiters you give it upon construction (very similar to }{\b strtok(\~)}{):
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:StreamTokenizer.h
\par // C++ Replacement for Standard C strtok()
\par #ifndef STREAMTOKENIZER_H
\par #define STREAMTOKENIZER_H
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <iterator>
\par   
\par class StreamTokenizer \{
\par   typedef std::istreambuf_iterator<char> It;
\par   It p, end;
\par   std::string delimiters;
\par   bool isDelimiter(char c) \{
\par     return 
\par       delimiters.find(c) != std::string::npos;
\par   \}
\par public:
\par   StreamTokenizer(std::istream& is, 
\par     std::string delim = " \\t\\n;()\\"<>:\{\}[]+-=&*#"
\par     ".,/\\\\~!0123456789") : p(is), end(It()),
\par     delimiters(delim) \{\}
\par   std::string next(); // Get next token
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif STREAMTOKENIZER_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The default delimiters for the }{\b StreamTokenizer}{
 constructor extract words with only alpha characters, as before, but now you can choose different delimiters to parse different tokens. The implementation of }{\b next(\~)}{ looks similar to }{\b Wordlist2.cpp}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:StreamTokenizer.cpp \{O\}
\par #include "StreamTokenizer.h"
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par string StreamTokenizer::next() \{
\par   string result;
\par   if(p != end) \{
\par     insert_iterator<string>
\par       ii(result, result.begin());
\par     while(isDelimiter(*p) && p != end)
\par       p++;
\par     while (!isDelimiter(*p) && p != end)
\par       *ii++ = *p++;
\par   \}
\par   return result;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The first non-delimiter is found, then characters are copied until a delimiter is found, and the resulting }{\b string}{ is returned. Here\rquote s a test:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:TokenizeTest.cpp
\par //\{L\} StreamTokenizer
\par // Test StreamTokenizer
\par #include "StreamTokenizer.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <set>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1);
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]);
\par   StreamTokenizer words(in);
\par   set<string> wordlist;
\par   string word;
\par   while((word = words.next()).size() != 0)
\par     wordlist.insert(word);
\par   // Output results:
\par   copy(wordlist.begin(), wordlist.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<string>(cout, "\\n"));
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now the tool is more reusable than before, but it\rquote s still inflexible, because it can only work with an }{\b istream}{. This isn\rquote t as bad as it first seems, since a }{\b string}{
 can be turned into an }{\b istream}{ via an }{\b istringstream}{. But in the next section we\rquote ll come up with the most general, reusable tokenizing tool, and this should give you a feeling of what \ldblquote reusable\rdblquote 
 really means, and the effort necessary to create truly reusable code.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776408}A completely reusable tokenizer{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776408}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Since the STL containers and algorithms all revolve around iterators, the most flexible solution will itself be an iterator. You could think of the }{\b TokenIterator}{
 as an iterator that wraps itself around any other iterator that can produce characters. Because it is designed as an input iterator (the most primitive type of iterator) it can be used with any STL algorithm. Not only is it a useful tool in itself, the }
{\b TokenIterator}{ is also a good example of how you can design your own iterators.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ This is 
another example coached by Nathan Myers.}}}{
\par The }{\b TokenIterator}{ is doubly flexible: first, you can choose the type of iterator that will produce the }{\b char}{ input. Second, instead of just saying what characters represent the delimiters, }{\b TokenIterator}{
 will use a predicate which is a function object whose }{\b operator(\~)}{ takes a }{\b char}{
 and decides if it should be in the token or not. Although the two examples given here have a static concept of what characters belong in a token, you could easily design your own function object to change its state as the characters are read
, producing a more sophisticated parser.
\par The following header file contains the two basic predicates }{\b Isalpha}{ and }{\b Delimiters}{, along with the template for }{\b TokenIterator}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:TokenIterator.h
\par #ifndef TOKENITERATOR_H
\par #define TOKENITERATOR_H
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iterator>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par #include <cctype>
\par 
\par struct Isalpha \{ 
\par   bool operator()(char c) \{ 
\par     using namespace std; //[[For a compiler bug]]
\par     return isalpha(c); 
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Delimiters \{
\par   std::string exclude;
\par public:
\par   Delimiters() \{\}
\par   Delimiters(const std::string& excl) 
\par     : exclude(excl) \{\}
\par   bool operator()(char c) \{
\par     return exclude.find(c) == std::string::npos;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par template <class InputIter, class Pred = Isalpha>
\par class TokenIterator: public std::iterator<
\par   std::input_iterator_tag,std::string,ptrdiff_t>\{
\par   InputIter first;
\par   InputIter last;
\par   std::string word;
\par   Pred predicate;
\par public:
\par   TokenIterator(InputIter begin, InputIter end, 
\par     Pred pred = Pred()) 
\par     : first(begin), last(end), predicate(pred) \{
\par       ++*this; 
\par   \}
\par   TokenIterator() \{\} // End sentinel
\par   // Prefix increment:
\par   TokenIterator& operator++() \{
\par     word.resize(0);
\par     first = std::find_if(first, last, predicate);
\par     while (first != last && predicate(*first))
\par       word += *first++;
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par   // Postfix increment
\par   class Proxy \{ 
\par     std::string word;
\par   public:
\par     Proxy(const std::string& w) : word(w) \{\}
\par     std::string operator*() \{ return word; \} 
\par   \};
\par   Proxy operator++(int) \{ 
\par     Proxy d(word);
\par     ++*this; 
\par     return d; 
\par   \}
\par   // Produce the actual value:
\par   std::string operator*() const \{ return word; \}
\par   std::string* operator->() const \{
\par     return &(operator*()); 
\par   \}
\par   // Compare iterators:
\par   bool operator==(const TokenIterator&) \{ 
\par     return word.size() == 0 && first == last; 
\par   \}
\par   bool operator!=(const TokenIterator& rv) \{ 
\par     return !(*this == rv);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // TOKENITERATOR_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b TokenIterator}{ is inherited from the }{\b std::iterator}{ template. It might appear that there\rquote s some kind of functionality that comes with }{\b std::iterator}{
, but it is purely a way of tagging an iterator so that a container that uses it knows what it\rquote s capable of. Here, you can see }{\b input_iterator_tag}{ as a template argument \endash  this tells anyone who asks that a }{\b TokenIterator}{
 only has the capabilities of an input iterator, and cannot be used with algorithms requiring more sophisticated iterators. Apart from the tagging, }{\b std::iterator}{ doesn\rquote 
t do anything else, which means you must design all the other functionality in yourself.
\par }{\b TokenIterator}{ may look a little strange at first, because the first constructor requires both a \ldblquote begin\rdblquote  and \ldblquote end\rdblquote  iterator as arguments, along with the predicate. Remember that this is a \ldblquote wrapper
\rdblquote  iterator that has no idea of how to tell whether it\rquote s at the end of its input source, so
 the ending iterator is necessary in the first constructor. The reason for the second (default) constructor is that the STL algorithms (and any algorithms you write) need a }{\b TokenIterator }{
sentinel to be the past-the-end value. Since all the information necessary to see if the }{\b TokenIterator}{ has reached the end of its input is collected in the first constructor, this second constructor creates a }{\b TokenIterator}{
 that is merely used as a placeholder in algorithms.
\par The core of the behavior happens in }{\b operator++}{. This erases the current value of }{\b word}{ using }{\b string::resize(\~)}{, then finds the first character that satisfies the predicate (thus discovering the beginning of the new token) using }{\b 
find_if(\~)}{ (from the STL algorithms, discussed in the following chapter). The resulting iterator is assigned to }{\b first}{, thus moving }{\b first}{
 forward to the beginning of the token. Then, as long as the end of the input is not reached and the predicate is satisfied, characters are copied into the word from the input. Finally the new token is returned.
\par The postfix increment requires a proxy object to hold the value before the increment, so it can be returned (see the operator overloading chapter for more details of this). Producing the actual value is a straightforward }{\b operator*}{. The only ot
her functions that must be defined for an output iterator are the }{\b operator==}{ and }{\b operator!=}{ to indicate whether the }{\b TokenIterator}{ has reached the end of its input. You can see that the argument for }{\b operator== }{is ignored 
\endash  it only cares about whether it has reached its internal }{\b last}{ iterator. Notice that }{\b operator!=}{ is defined in terms of }{\b operator==}{.
\par A good test of }{\b TokenIterator}{ includes a number of different sources of input characters including a }{\b streambuf_iterator}{, a }{\b char*}{, and a }{\b deque<char>::iterator}{. Finally, the original }{\b Wordlist.cpp}{ problem is solved:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:TokenIteratorTest.cpp
\par #include "TokenIterator.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <deque>
\par #include <set>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   ifstream in("TokenIteratorTest.cpp");
\par   assure(in, "TokenIteratorTest.cpp");
\par   ostream_iterator<string> out(cout, "\\n");
\par   typedef istreambuf_iterator<char> IsbIt;
\par   IsbIt begin(in), isbEnd;
\par   Delimiters 
\par     delimiters(" \\t\\n~;()\\"<>:\{\}[]+-=&*#.,/\\\\");
\par   TokenIterator<IsbIt, Delimiters> 
\par     wordIter(begin, isbEnd, delimiters),
\par     end;
\par   vector<string> wordlist;
\par   copy(wordIter, end, back_inserter(wordlist));
\par   // Output results:
\par   copy(wordlist.begin(), wordlist.end(), out);
\par   *out++ = "-----------------------------------";
\par   // Use a char array as the source:
\par   char* cp = 
\par     "typedef std::istreambuf_iterator<char> It";
\par   TokenIterator<char*, Delimiters>
\par     charIter(cp, cp + strlen(cp), delimiters),
\par     end2;
\par   vector<string> wordlist2;
\par   copy(charIter, end2, back_inserter(wordlist2));
\par   copy(wordlist2.begin(), wordlist2.end(), out);
\par   *out++ = "-----------------------------------";
\par   // Use a deque<char> as the source:
\par   ifstream in2("TokenIteratorTest.cpp");
\par   deque<char> dc;
\par   copy(IsbIt(in2), IsbIt(), back_inserter(dc));
\par   TokenIterator<deque<char>::iterator,Delimiters>
\par     dcIter(dc.begin(), dc.end(), delimiters),
\par     end3;
\par   vector<string> wordlist3;
\par   copy(dcIter, end3, back_inserter(wordlist3));
\par   copy(wordlist3.begin(), wordlist3.end(), out);
\par   *out++ = "-----------------------------------";
\par   // Reproduce the Wordlist.cpp example:
\par   ifstream in3("TokenIteratorTest.cpp");
\par   TokenIterator<IsbIt, Delimiters>
\par     wordIter2(IsbIt(in3), isbEnd, delimiters);
\par   set<string> wordlist4;
\par   while(wordIter2 != end)
\par     wordlist4.insert(*wordIter2++);
\par   copy(wordlist4.begin(), wordlist4.end(), out);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When using an }{\b istreambuf_iterator}{, you create one to attach to the }{\b istream}{ object, and one with the default constructor as the past-the-end marker. Both of these are used to create the }
{\b TokenIterator}{ that will actually produce the tokens; the default constructor produces the faux }{\b TokenIterator}{ past-the-end sentinel (this is just a placeholder, and as mentioned previously is actually ignored). The}{\b  TokenIterator}{
 produces }{\b string}{s that are inserted into a container which must, naturally, be a container of }{\b string}{ \endash  here a }{\b vector<string>}{ is used in all cases except the last (you could also concatenate the results onto a }{\b string}{
). Other than that, a }{\b TokenIterator}{ works like any other input iterator.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776409}stack{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776409}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b stack}{, along with the }{\b queue}{ and }{\b priority_queue}{, are classified as }{\i adapters}{, which means they are implemented using one of the basic sequence containers: }{\b vector}{, 
}{\b list}{ or }{\b deque}{. This, in my opinion, is an unfortunate case of confusing what something does with the details of its underlying implementation \endash  the fact that these are called \ldblquote adapters\rdblquote 
 is of primary value only to the creator of the library. When you use them, you generally don\rquote t care that they\rquote re adapters, but instead that they solve your problem. Admittedly there are times when it\rquote 
s useful to know that you can choose an alternate implementation or build an adapter from an existing container object, but that\rquote s generally one level removed from the adapter\rquote 
s behavior. So, while you may see it emphasized elsewhere that a particular container is an adapter, I shall only point out that fact when it\rquote s useful. Note that each type of adapter has a default container that it\rquote 
s built upon, and this default is the most sensible implementation, so in most cases you won\rquote t need to concern yourself with the underlying implementation.
\par The following example shows }{\b stack<string>}{ implemented in the three possible ways: the default (which uses }{\b deque}{), with a }{\b vector}{ and with a }{\b list}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:Stack1.cpp
\par // Demonstrates the STL stack
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <stack>
\par #include <list>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par // Default: deque<string>:
\par typedef stack<string> Stack1;
\par // Use a vector<string>:
\par typedef stack<string, vector<string> > Stack2;
\par // Use a list<string>:
\par typedef stack<string, list<string> > Stack3;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1); // File name is argument
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]);
\par   Stack1 textlines; // Try the different versions
\par   // Read file and store lines in the stack:
\par   string line;
\par   while(getline(in, line)) 
\par     textlines.push(line + "\\n");
\par   // Print lines from the stack and pop them:
\par   while(!textlines.empty()) \{
\par     cout << textlines.top();
\par     textlines.pop();
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b top(\~)}{ and }{\b pop(\~)}{ operations will probably seem non-intuitive if you\rquote ve used other }{\b stack}{ classes. When you call }{\b pop(\~)}{
 it returns void rather than the top element that you might have expected. If you want the top element, you get a reference to it with }{\b top(\~)}{. It turns out this is more efficient, since a traditional }{\b pop(\~)}{
 would have to return a value rather than a reference, and thus invoke the copy-constructor. When you\rquote re using a }{\b stack}{ (or a }{\b priority_queue}{, described later) you can efficiently refer to }{\b top(\~)}{
 as many times as you want, then discard the top element explicitly using }{\b pop(\~)}{ (perhaps if some other term than the familiar \ldblquote pop\rdblquote  had been used, this would have been a bit clearer).
\par The }{\b stack}{ template has a very simple interface, essentially the member functions you see above. It doesn\rquote t have sophisticated forms of initialization or access, but if you need that you can use the underlying container that the }{\b stack}{
 is implemented upon. For example, suppose you have a function that expects a }{\b stack}{ interface but in the rest of your program you need the objects stored in a }{\b list}{
. The following program stores each line of a file along with the leading number of spaces in that line (you might imagine it as a starting point for performing some kinds of source-code reformatting):
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:Stack2.cpp
\par // Converting a list to a stack
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <stack>
\par #include <list>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par // Expects a stack:
\par template<class Stk>
\par void stackOut(Stk& s, ostream& os = cout) \{
\par   while(!s.empty()) \{
\par     os << s.top() << "\\n";
\par     s.pop();
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par class Line \{
\par   string line; // Without leading spaces
\par   int lspaces; // Number of leading spaces
\par public:
\par   Line(string s) : line(s) \{
\par     lspaces = line.find_first_not_of(' ');
\par     if(lspaces == string::npos)
\par       lspaces = 0;
\par     line = line.substr(lspaces);
\par   \}
\par   friend ostream& 
\par   operator<<(ostream& os, const Line& l) \{
\par     for(int i = 0; i < l.lspaces; i++)
\par       os << ' ';
\par     return os << l.line;
\par   \}
\par   // Other functions here...
\par \};
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1); // File name is argument
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]);
\par   list<Line> lines;
\par   // Read file and store lines in the list:
\par   string s;
\par   while(getline(in, s)) 
\par     lines.push_front(s);
\par   // Turn the list into a stack for printing:
\par   stack<Line, list<Line> > stk(lines);
\par   stackOut(stk);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The function that requires the }{\b stack}{ interface just sends each }{\b top(\~)}{ object to an }{\b ostream}{ and then removes it by calling }{\b pop(\~)}{. The }{\b Line}{
 class determines the number of leading spaces, then stores the contents of the line }{\i without}{ the leading spaces. The }{\b ostream}{ }{\b operator<<}{ re-inse
rts the leading spaces so the line prints properly, but you can easily change the number of spaces by changing the value of }{\b lspaces}{ (the member functions to do this are not shown here).
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{, the input file is read into a }{\b list<Line>}{, then a }{\b stack}{ is wrapped around this list so it can be sent to }{\b stackOut(\~)}{.
\par You cannot iterate through a }{\b stack}{; this emphasizes that you only want to perform }{\b stack}{ operations when you create a }{\b stack}{. You can get equivalent \ldblquote stack\rdblquote  functionality using a }{\b vector}{ and its }{\b back(\~)}{
, }{\b push_back(\~)}{ and }{\b pop_back(\~)}{ methods, and then you have all the additional functionality of the }{\b vector}{. }{\b Stack1.cpp}{ can be rewritten to show this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:Stack3.cpp
\par // Using a vector as a stack; modified Stack1.cpp
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1);
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]);
\par   vector<string> textlines;
\par   string line;
\par   while(getline(in, line)) 
\par     textlines.push_back(line + "\\n");
\par   while(!textlines.empty()) \{
\par     cout << textlines.back();
\par     textlines.pop_back();
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You\rquote ll see this produces the same output as }{\b Stack1.cpp}{, but you can now perform }{\b vector}{ operations as well. Of course, }{\b list}{
 has the additional ability to push things at the front, but it\rquote s generally less efficient than using }{\b push_back(\~)}{ with }{\b vector}{. (In addition, }{\b deque}{ is usually more efficient than }{\b list}{ for pushing things at the front).

\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776410}queue{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776410}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b queue}{ is a restricted form of a }{\b deque}{ \endash  you can only enter elements at one end, and pull them off the other end. Functionally, you could use a }{\b deque}{
 anywhere you need a }{\b queue}{, and you would then also have the additional functionality of the }{\b deque}{. The only reason you need to use a }{\b queue}{ rather than a }{\b deque}{
, then, is if you want to emphasize that you will only be performing queue-like behavior.
\par The }{\b queue}{ is an adapter class like }{\b stack}{, in that it is built on top of another sequence container. As you might guess, the ideal implementation for a }{\b queue}{ is a }{\b deque}{, and that is the default template argument for the }{\b 
queue}{; you\rquote ll rarely need a different implementation.
\par Queues are often used when modeling systems where some elements of the system are waiting to be served by other elements in the system. A classic example of this is the \ldblquote bank-teller problem,\rdblquote 
 where you have customers arriving at random intervals, getting into a line, and then being served by a set of tellers. Since the customers arrive randomly and each take a random amount of time to be served, there\rquote 
s no way to deterministically know how long the line will be at any time. However, it\rquote s possible to simulate the situation and see what happens.
\par A problem in performing this simulation is the fact that, in effect, each customer and teller should be run by a separate process. What we\rquote 
d like is a multithreaded environment, then each customer or teller would have their own thread. However, Standard C++ has no model for multithreading so there is no standard solution to this probl
em. On the other hand, with a little adjustment to the code it\rquote s possible to simulate enough multithreading to provide a satisfactory solution to our problem.
\par Multithreading means you have multiple threads of control running at once, in the same address space (this differs from }{\i multitasking}{
, where you have different processes each running in their own address space). The trick is that you have fewer CPUs than you do threads (and very often only one CPU) so to give the illusion that each thread has its own CPU there is a }{\i time-slicing}{
 mechanism that says \ldblquote OK, current thread \endash  you\rquote ve had enough time. I\rquote m going to stop you and go give time to some other thread.\rdblquote  This automatic stopping and starting of threads is called }{\i pre-emptive }{
and it means you don\rquote t need to manage the threading process at all.
\par An alternative approach is for each thread to voluntarily yield the CPU to the scheduler, which then goes and finds another thread that needs running. This is easier to synthesize, but it still requires a method of \ldblquote swapping\rdblquote 
 out one thread and swapping in another (this usually involves saving the stack frame and using the standard C library functions }{\b setjmp(\~)}{ and }{\b longjmp(\~)}{
; see my article in the (XX) issue of Computer Language magazine for an example). So instead, we\rquote ll build the time-slicing into the classes in the system. In this case, it will be the tellers that represent the \ldblquote threads,\rdblquote 
 (the customers will be passive) so each teller will have an infinite-looping }{\b run(\~)}{ method that will execute for a certain number of \ldblquote time units,\rdblquote 
 and then simply return. By using the ordinary return mechanism, we eliminate the need for any swapping. The resulting program, although small, provides a remarkably reasonable simulation:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:BankTeller.cpp
\par // Using a queue and simulated multithreading
\par // To model a bank teller system
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <queue>
\par #include <list>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Customer \{
\par   int serviceTime;
\par public:
\par   Customer() : serviceTime(0) \{\}
\par   Customer(int tm) : serviceTime(tm) \{\}
\par   int getTime() \{ return serviceTime; \}
\par   void setTime(int newtime) \{
\par     serviceTime = newtime;
\par   \}
\par   friend ostream& 
\par   operator<<(ostream& os, const Customer& c) \{
\par     return os << '[' << c.serviceTime << ']';
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Teller \{
\par   queue<Customer>& customers;
\par   Customer current;
\par   static const int slice = 5;
\par   int ttime; // Time left in slice
\par   bool busy; // Is teller serving a customer?
\par public:
\par   Teller(queue<Customer>& cq) 
\par     : customers(cq), ttime(0), busy(false) \{\}
\par   Teller& operator=(const Teller& rv) \{
\par     customers = rv.customers;
\par     current = rv.current;
\par     ttime = rv.ttime;
\par     busy = rv.busy;
\par     return *this;
\par   \}
\par   bool isBusy() \{ return busy; \}
\par   void run(bool recursion = false) \{
\par     if(!recursion)
\par       ttime = slice;
\par     int servtime = current.getTime();
\par     if(servtime > ttime) \{
\par       servtime -= ttime;
\par       current.setTime(servtime);
\par       busy = true; // Still working on current
\par       return;
\par     \}
\par     if(servtime < ttime) \{
\par       ttime -= servtime;
\par       if(!customers.empty()) \{
\par         current = customers.front();
\par         customers.pop(); // Remove it
\par         busy = true;
\par         run(true); // Recurse
\par       \}
\par       return;
\par     \}
\par     if(servtime == ttime) \{
\par       // Done with current, set to empty:
\par       current = Customer(0);
\par       busy = false;
\par       return; // No more time in this slice
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Inherit to access protected implementation:
\par class CustomerQ : public queue<Customer> \{
\par public:
\par   friend ostream& 
\par   operator<<(ostream& os, const CustomerQ& cd) \{
\par     copy(cd.c.begin(), cd.c.end(), 
\par       ostream_iterator<Customer>(os, ""));
\par     return os;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   CustomerQ customers;
\par   list<Teller> tellers;
\par   typedef list<Teller>::iterator TellIt;
\par   tellers.push_back(Teller(customers));
\par   srand(time(0)); // Seed random number generator
\par   while(true) \{
\par     // Add a random number of customers to the
\par     // queue, with random service times:
\par     for(int i = 0; i < rand() % 5; i++)
\par       customers.push(Customer(rand() % 15 + 1));
\par     cout << '\{' << tellers.size() << '\}' 
\par       << customers << endl;
\par     // Have the tellers service the queue:
\par     for(TellIt i = tellers.begin(); 
\par       i != tellers.end(); i++)
\par       (*i).run();
\par     cout << '\{' << tellers.size() << '\}' 
\par       << customers << endl;
\par     // If line is too long, add another teller:
\par     if(customers.size() / tellers.size() > 2)
\par       tellers.push_back(Teller(customers));
\par     // If line is short enough, remove a teller:
\par     if(tellers.size() > 1 && 
\par       customers.size() / tellers.size() < 2)
\par       for(TellIt i = tellers.begin();
\par         i != tellers.end(); i++)
\par         if(!(*i).isBusy()) \{
\par           tellers.erase(i);
\par           break; // Out of for loop
\par         \}
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Each customer requires a certain amount of service time, which is the number of time units that a teller must spend on the customer in order to serve that customer\rquote 
s needs. Of course, the amount of service time will be different for each customer, and will be determined randomly. In addition, you won\rquote t know how many customers will be arriving in each interval, so this will also be determined randomly. 
\par The }{\b Customer }{objects are kept in a }{\b queue<Customer>}{, and each }{\b Teller}{ object keeps a reference to that queue.}{\b  }{When a }{\b Teller}{ object is finished with its current }{\b Customer}{ object, that }{\b Teller}{ will get another }{
\b Customer}{ from the queue and begin working on the new }{\b Customer}{, reducing the }{\b Customer}{\rquote s service time during each time slice that the }{\b Teller}{ is allotted. All this logic is in the }{\b run(\~)}{
 member function, which is basically a three-way }{\b if}{ statement based on whether the amount of time necessary to serve the customer is less than, greater than or equal to the amount of time left in the teller\rquote 
s current time slice. Notice that if the }{\b Teller}{ has more time after finishing with a }{\b Customer}{, it gets a new customer and recurses into itself.
\par Just as with a }{\b stack}{, when you use a }{\b queue}{, it\rquote s only a }{\b queue}{ and doesn\rquote t have any of the other functionality of the basic sequence containers. This includes the ability to get an iterator in order to step through the }{
\b stack}{. However, the underlying sequence container (that the }{\b queue}{ is built upon) is held as a }{\b protected}{ member inside the }{\b queue}{, and the identifier for this member is specified in the C++ Standard as \lquote }{\b c}{\rquote 
, which means that you can inherit from }{\b queue}{ in order to access the underlying implementation. The }{\b CustomerQ}{ class does exactly that, for the sole purpose of defining an }{\b ostream}{ }{\b operator<<}{ that can iterate through the }{\b 
queue}{ and print out its members.
\par The driver for the simulation is the infinite }{\b while}{ loop in }{\b main(\~)}{
. At the beginning of each pass through the loop, a random number of customers are added, with random service times. Both the number of tellers and the queue contents are displayed so you can see the state of the system. After runni
ng each teller, the display is repeated. At this point, the system adapts by comparing the number of customers and the number of tellers; if the line is too long another teller is added and if it is short enough a teller can be removed. It is in this adap
tation section of the program that you can experiment with policies regarding the optimal addition and removal of tellers. If this is the only section that you\rquote re modifying, you may want to encapsulate policies inside of different objects.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776411}Priority queues{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776411}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you }{\b push(\~)}{ an object onto a }{\b priority_queue}{, that object is sorted into the queue according to a function or function object (you can allow the default }{\b less}{
 template to supply this, or provide one of your own). The }{\b priority_queue}{ ensures that when you look at the }{\b top(\~)}{ element it will be the one with the highest priority. When you\rquote re done with it, you call }{\b pop(\~)}{
 to remove it and bring the next one into place. Thus, the }{\b priority_queue}{ has nearly the same interface as a }{\b stack}{, but it behaves differently.
\par Like }{\b stack}{ and }{\b queue}{, }{\b priority_queue}{ is an adapter which is built on top of one of the basic sequences \endash  the default is }{\b vector}{.
\par It\rquote s trivial to make a }{\b priority_queue}{ that works with }{\b int}{s:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:PriorityQueue1.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <queue>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   priority_queue<int> pqi;
\par   srand(time(0)); // Seed random number generator
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     pqi.push(rand() % 25);
\par   while(!pqi.empty()) \{
\par     cout << pqi.top() << ' ';
\par     pqi.pop();
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This pushes into the }{\b priority_queue }{100 random values from 0 to 24. When you run this program you\rquote 
ll see that duplicates are allowed, and the highest values appear first. To show how you can change the ordering by providing your own function or function object, the following program gives lower-valued numbers the highest priority:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:PriorityQueue2.cpp
\par // Changing the priority
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <queue>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par struct Reverse \{
\par   bool operator()(int x, int y) \{
\par     return y < x;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   priority_queue<int, vector<int>, Reverse> pqi;
\par   // Could also say:
\par   // priority_queue<int, vector<int>, 
\par   //   greater<int> > pqi;
\par   srand(time(0));
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     pqi.push(rand() % 25);
\par   while(!pqi.empty()) \{
\par     cout << pqi.top() << ' ';
\par     pqi.pop();
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Although you can easily use the Standard Library }{\b greater}{ template to produce the predicate, I went to the trouble of creating }{\b Reverse}{
 so you could see how to do it in case you have a more complex scheme for ordering your objects.
\par If you look at the description for }{\b priority_queue}{, you see that the constructor can be handed a \ldblquote Compare\rdblquote  object, as shown above. If you don\rquote t use your own \ldblquote Compare\rdblquote 
 object, the default template behavior the }{\b less }{template function. You might think (as I did) that it would make sense to leave the template instantiation as }{\b priority_queue<int>}{, thus using the default template arguments of }{\b vector<int>}
{ and }{\b less<int>}{. Then you could inherit a new class from }{\b less<int>}{, redefine }{\b operator(\~)}{ and hand an object of that type to the }{\b priority_queue}{
 constructor. I tried this, and got it to compile, but the resulting program produced the same old }{\b less<int>}{ behavior. The answer lies in the }{\b less< > }{template:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {template <class T>
\par struct less : binary_function<T, T, bool> \{
\par   // Other stuff...
\par   bool operator()(const T& x, const T& y) const \{
\par     return x < y;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b operator(\~)}{ is not }{\b virtual}{, so even though the constructor takes your subclass of }{\b less<int>}{ by reference (thus it doesn\rquote t slice it down to a plain }{\b less<int>}{
), when }{\b operator(\~)}{ is called, it is the base-class version that is used. While it is generally reasonable to expect ordinary classes to behave polymorphically, you cannot make this assumption when using the STL.
\par Of course, a }{\b priority_queue}{ of }{\b int}{ is trivial. A more interesting problem is a to-do list, where each object contains a }{\b string}{ and a primary and secondary priority value:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:PriorityQueue3.cpp
\par // A more complex use of priority_queue
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <queue>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class ToDoItem \{
\par   char primary;
\par   int secondary;
\par   string item;
\par public:
\par   ToDoItem(string td, char pri ='A', int sec =1)
\par     : item(td), primary(pri), secondary(sec) \{\}
\par   friend bool operator<(
\par     const ToDoItem& x, const ToDoItem& y) \{
\par     if(x.primary > y.primary) 
\par       return true;
\par     if(x.primary == y.primary)
\par       if(x.secondary > y.secondary) 
\par         return true;
\par     return false;
\par   \}
\par   friend ostream& 
\par   operator<<(ostream& os, const ToDoItem& td) \{
\par     return os << td.primary << td.secondary 
\par       << ": " << td.item;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   priority_queue<ToDoItem> toDoList;
\par   toDoList.push(ToDoItem("Empty trash", 'C', 4));
\par   toDoList.push(ToDoItem("Feed dog", 'A', 2));
\par   toDoList.push(ToDoItem("Feed bird", 'B', 7));
\par   toDoList.push(ToDoItem("Mow lawn", 'C', 3));
\par   toDoList.push(ToDoItem("Water lawn", 'A', 1));
\par   toDoList.push(ToDoItem("Feed cat", 'B', 1));
\par   while(!toDoList.empty()) \{
\par     cout << toDoList.top() << endl;
\par     toDoList.pop();
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b ToDoItem}{\rquote s }{\b operator<}{ must be a non-member function for it to work with }{\b less< >}{. Other than that, everything happens automatically. The output is:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {A1: Water lawn
\par A2: Feed dog
\par B1: Feed cat
\par B7: Feed bird
\par C3: Mow lawn
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {C4: Empty trash
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Note that you cannot iterate through a }{\b priority_queue}{. However, it is possible to emulate the behavior of a }{\b priority_queue}{ using a }{\b vector}{, thus allowing you access to that }{\b 
vector}{. You can do this by looking at the implementation of }{\b priority_queue}{, which uses }{\b make_heap(\~)}{, }{\b push_heap(\~)}{ and }{\b pop_heap(\~)}{ (they are the soul of the }{\b priority_queue}{; in fact you could say that the heap }{\i is
}{ the priority queue and }{\b priority_queue }{is just a wrapper around it). This turns out to be reasonably straightforward, but you might think that a shortcut is possible. Since the container used by }{\b priority_queue}{ is }{\b protected}{
 (and has the identifier, according to the Standard C++ specification, named }{\b c}{) you can inherit a new class which provides access to the underlying implementation:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:PriorityQueue4.cpp
\par // Manipulating the underlying implementation
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <queue>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class PQI : public priority_queue<int> \{
\par public:
\par   vector<int>& impl() \{ return c; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   PQI pqi;
\par   srand(time(0));
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     pqi.push(rand() % 25);
\par   copy(pqi.impl().begin(), pqi.impl().end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<int>(cout, " "));
\par   cout << endl;
\par   while(!pqi.empty()) \{
\par     cout << pqi.top() << ' ';
\par     pqi.pop();
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {However, if you run this program you\rquote ll discover that the }{\b vector}{ doesn\rquote t contain the items in the descending order that you get when you call }{\b pop(\~)}{
, the order that you want from the priority queue. It would seem that if you want to create a }{\b vector}{ that is a priority queue, you have to do it by hand, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:PriorityQueue5.cpp
\par // Building your own priority queue
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <queue>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par template<class T, class Compare>
\par class PQV : public vector<T> \{
\par   Compare comp;
\par public:
\par   PQV(Compare cmp = Compare()) : comp(cmp) \{
\par     make_heap(begin(), end(), comp);
\par   \}
\par   const T& top() \{ return front(); \}
\par   void push(const T& x) \{
\par     push_back(x);
\par     push_heap(begin(), end(), comp);
\par   \}
\par   void pop() \{
\par     pop_heap(begin(), end(), comp);
\par     pop_back();
\par   \}  
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   PQV<int, less<int> > pqi;
\par   srand(time(0));
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     pqi.push(rand() % 25);
\par   copy(pqi.begin(), pqi.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<int>(cout, " "));
\par   cout << endl;
\par   while(!pqi.empty()) \{
\par     cout << pqi.top() << ' ';
\par     pqi.pop();
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {But this program behaves in the same way as the previous one! What you are seeing in the underlying }{\b vector}{ is called a }{\i heap}{.}{\i  }{
This heap represents the tree of the priority queue (stored in the linear structure of the }{\b vector}{), but when you iterate through it you do not get a linear priority-queue order. You might think that you can simply call }{\b sort_heap(\~)}{
, but that only works once, and then you don\rquote t have a heap anymore, but instead a sorted list. This means that to go back to using it as a heap the user must remember to call }{\b make_heap(\~) }{first. This can be encapsulated into your custo
m priority queue:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:PriorityQueue6.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <queue>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par template<class T, class Compare>
\par class PQV : public vector<T> \{
\par   Compare comp;
\par   bool sorted;
\par   void assureHeap() \{
\par     if(sorted) \{
\par       // Turn it back into a heap:
\par       make_heap(begin(), end(), comp);
\par       sorted = false;
\par     \}
\par   \}    
\par public:
\par   PQV(Compare cmp = Compare()) : comp(cmp) \{
\par     make_heap(begin(), end(), comp);
\par     sorted = false;
\par   \}
\par   const T& top() \{
\par     assureHeap();
\par     return front(); 
\par   \}
\par   void push(const T& x) \{
\par     assureHeap();
\par     // Put it at the end:
\par     push_back(x);
\par     // Re-adjust the heap:
\par     push_heap(begin(), end(), comp);
\par   \}
\par   void pop() \{
\par     assureHeap();
\par     // Move the top element to the last position:
\par     pop_heap(begin(), end(), comp);
\par     // Remove that element:
\par     pop_back();
\par   \}
\par   void sort() \{
\par     if(!sorted) \{
\par       sort_heap(begin(), end(), comp);
\par       reverse(begin(), end());
\par       sorted = true;
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   PQV<int, less<int> > pqi;
\par   srand(time(0));
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++) \{
\par     pqi.push(rand() % 25);
\par     copy(pqi.begin(), pqi.end(),
\par       ostream_iterator<int>(cout, " "));
\par     cout << "\\n-----\\n";
\par   \}
\par   pqi.sort();
\par   copy(pqi.begin(), pqi.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<int>(cout, " "));
\par   cout << "\\n-----\\n";
\par   while(!pqi.empty()) \{
\par     cout << pqi.top() << ' ';
\par     pqi.pop();
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If }{\b sorted}{ is true, then the }{\b vector}{ is not organized as a heap, but instead as a sorted sequence. }{\b assureHeap(\~)}{ guarantees that it\rquote 
s put back into heap form before performing any heap operations on it.
\par The first }{\b for}{ loop in }{\b main(\~)}{ now has the additional quality that it displays the heap as it\rquote s being built.
\par The only drawback to this solution is that the user must remember to call }{\b sort(\~) }{
before viewing it as a sorted sequence (although one could conceivably override all the methods that produce iterators so that they guarantee sorting). Another solution is to build a priority queue that is not a }{\b vector}{, but will build you a }{\b 
vector}{ whenever you want one:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:PriorityQueue7.cpp
\par // A priority queue that will hand you a vector
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <queue>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par template<class T, class Compare>
\par class PQV \{
\par   vector<T> v;
\par   Compare comp;
\par public:
\par   // Don't need to call make_heap(); it's empty:
\par   PQV(Compare cmp = Compare()) : comp(cmp) \{\}
\par   void push(const T& x) \{
\par     // Put it at the end:
\par     v.push_back(x);
\par     // Re-adjust the heap:
\par     push_heap(v.begin(), v.end(), comp);
\par   \}
\par   void pop() \{
\par     // Move the top element to the last position:
\par     pop_heap(v.begin(), v.end(), comp);
\par     // Remove that element:
\par     v.pop_back();
\par   \}
\par   const T& top() \{ return v.front(); \}
\par   bool empty() const \{ return v.empty(); \}
\par   int size() const \{ return v.size(); \}
\par   typedef vector<T> TVec;
\par   TVec vector() \{
\par     TVec r(v.begin(), v.end());
\par     // It\rquote s already a heap
\par     sort_heap(r.begin(), r.end(), comp);
\par     // Put it into priority-queue order:
\par     reverse(r.begin(), r.end());
\par     return r;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   PQV<int, less<int> > pqi;
\par   srand(time(0));
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     pqi.push(rand() % 25);
\par   const vector<int>& v = pqi.vector();
\par   copy(v.begin(), v.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<int>(cout, " "));
\par   cout << "\\n-----------\\n"; 
\par   while(!pqi.empty()) \{
\par     cout << pqi.top() << ' ';
\par     pqi.pop();
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b PQV}{ follows the same form as the STL\rquote s }{\b priority_queue}{, but has the additional member }{\b vector(\~)}{, which creates a new }{\b vector}{ that\rquote s a copy of the one in }{\b 
PQV }{(which means that it\rquote s already a heap), then sorts it (thus it leave\rquote s }{\b PQV}{\rquote s }{\b vector}{ untouched), then reverses the order so that traversing the new }{\b vector}{ produces the same effect as popping the elements f
rom the priority queue.
\par You may observe that the approach of inheriting from }{\b priority_queue}{ used in }{\b PriorityQueue4.cpp}{ could be used with the above technique to produce more succinct code:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:PriorityQueue8.cpp
\par // A more compact version of PriorityQueue7.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <queue>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par template<class T>
\par class PQV : public priority_queue<T> \{
\par public:
\par   typedef vector<T> TVec;
\par   TVec vector() \{
\par     TVec r(c.begin(), c.end());
\par     // c is already a heap
\par     sort_heap(r.begin(), r.end(), comp);
\par     // Put it into priority-queue order:
\par     reverse(r.begin(), r.end());
\par     return r;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   PQV<int> pqi;
\par   srand(time(0));
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     pqi.push(rand() % 25);
\par   const vector<int>& v = pqi.vector();
\par   copy(v.begin(), v.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<int>(cout, " "));
\par   cout << "\\n-----------\\n"; 
\par   while(!pqi.empty()) \{
\par     cout << pqi.top() << ' ';
\par     pqi.pop();
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The brevity of this solution makes it the simplest and most desirable, plus it\rquote s guaranteed that the user will not have a }{\b vector}{
 in the unsorted state. The only potential problem is that the }{\b vector(\~)}{ member function returns the }{\b vector<T>}{ by value, which might cause some overhead issues with complex values of the parameter type }{\b T}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776412}Holding bits{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776412}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Most of my computer education was in hardware-level design and programming, and I spent my first few years doing embedded systems development. Because C was a language that purported to be \ldblquote 
close to the hardware,\rdblquote  I have always found it dismaying that there was no native binary representation for numbers. Decimal, of course, and hexadecimal (tolerable only because it\rquote 
s easier to group the bits in your mind), but octal? Ugh. Whenever you read specs for chips you\rquote re trying to program, they don\rquote t describe the chip registers in octal, or even hexadecimal \endash  they use binary. And yet C won\rquote 
t let you say }{\b 0b0101101}{, which is the obvious solution for a language close to the hardware.
\par Although there\rquote s still no native binary representation in C++, things have improved with the addition of two classes: }{\b bitset}{ and }{\b vector<bool>}{
, both of which are designed to manipulate a group of on-off values. The primary differences between these types are:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls242\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls242\adjustright {The }{\b bitset}{
 holds a fixed number of bits. You establish the quantity of bits in the }{\b bitset}{ template argument. The }{\b vector<bool>}{ can, like a regular }{\b vector}{, expand dynamically to hold any number of }{\b bool}{ values.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls242\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls242\adjustright {The }{\b bitset}{
 is explicitly designed for performance when manipulating bits, and not as a \ldblquote regular\rdblquote  container. As such, it has no iterators and it\rquote s most storage-efficient when it contains an integral number of }{\b long}{ values. The }{\b 
vector<bool>}{, on the other hand, is a specialization of a }{\b vector}{, and so has all the operations of a normal }{\b vector}{ \endash  the specialization is just designed to be space-efficient for }{\b bool}{.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {There is no trivial conversion between a }{\b bitset}{ and a }{\b vector<bool>}{, which implies that the two are for very different purposes.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776413}bitset<n>{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776413}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The template for }{\b bitset}{ accepts an integral template argument which is the number of bits to represent. Thus, }{\b bitset<10>}{ is a different type than }{\b bitset<20>}{
, and you cannot perform comparisons, assignments, etc. between the two.
\par A }{\b bitset}{ provides virtually any bit operation that you could ask for, in a very efficient form. However, each }{\b bitset}{ is made up of an integral number of }{\b long}{
s (typically 32 bits), so even though it uses no more space than it needs, it always uses at least the size of a }{\b long}{. This means you\rquote ll use space most efficiently if you increase the size of your }{\b bitset}{
s in chunks of the number of bits in a }{\b long}{. In addition, the only conversion }{\i from}{ a }{\b bitset}{ to a numerical value is to an }{\b unsigned long}{, which means that 32 bits (if your }{\b long }{
is the typical size) is the most flexible form of a }{\b bitset}{.
\par The following example tests almost all the functionality of the }{\b bitset}{ (the missing operations are redundant or trivial).}{\b  }{You\rquote 
ll see the description of each of the bitset outputs to the right of the output so that the bits all line up and you can compare them to the source values. If you still don\rquote t understand bitwise operations, running this program should help.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:BitSet.cpp
\par // Exercising the bitset class
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <bitset>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par const int sz = 32;
\par typedef bitset<sz> BS;
\par 
\par template<int bits>
\par bitset<bits> randBitset() \{
\par   bitset<bits> r(rand());
\par   for(int i = 0; i < bits/16 - 1; i++) \{
\par     r <<= 16;
\par     // "OR" together with a new lower 16 bits:
\par     r |= bitset<bits>(rand()); 
\par   \}
\par   return r;
\par \}  
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   srand(time(0));
\par   cout << "sizeof(bitset<16>) = " 
\par     << sizeof(bitset<16>) << endl;
\par   cout << "sizeof(bitset<32>) = " 
\par     << sizeof(bitset<32>) << endl;
\par   cout << "sizeof(bitset<48>) = " 
\par     << sizeof(bitset<48>) << endl;
\par   cout << "sizeof(bitset<64>) = " 
\par     << sizeof(bitset<64>) << endl;
\par   cout << "sizeof(bitset<65>) = " 
\par     << sizeof(bitset<65>) << endl;
\par   BS a(randBitset<sz>()), b(randBitset<sz>());
\par   // Converting from a bitset:
\par   unsigned long ul = a.to_ulong();
\par   string s = b.to_string();
\par   // Converting a string to a bitset:
\par   char* cbits = "111011010110111";
\par   cout << "char* cbits = " << cbits <<endl;
\par   cout << BS(cbits) << " [BS(cbits)]" << endl;
\par   cout << BS(cbits, 2) 
\par     << " [BS(cbits, 2)]" << endl;
\par   cout << BS(cbits, 2, 11)
\par     << " [BS(cbits, 2, 11)]" << endl;
\par   cout << a << " [a]" << endl;
\par   cout << b << " [b]"<< endl;
\par   // Bitwise AND:
\par   cout << (a & b) << " [a & b]" << endl;
\par   cout << (BS(a) &= b) << " [a &= b]" << endl;
\par   // Bitwise OR:
\par   cout << (a | b) << " [a | b]" << endl;
\par   cout << (BS(a) |= b) << " [a |= b]" << endl;
\par   // Exclusive OR:
\par   cout << (a ^ b) << " [a ^ b]" << endl;
\par   cout << (BS(a) ^= b) << " [a ^= b]" << endl;
\par   cout << a << " [a]" << endl; // For reference
\par   // Logical left shift (fill with zeros):
\par   cout << (BS(a) <<= sz/2) 
\par     << " [a <<= (sz/2)]" << endl;
\par   cout << (a << sz/2) << endl;
\par   cout << a << " [a]" << endl; // For reference
\par   // Logical right shift (fill with zeros):
\par   cout << (BS(a) >>= sz/2) 
\par     << " [a >>= (sz/2)]" << endl;
\par   cout << (a >> sz/2) << endl;
\par   cout << a << " [a]" << endl; // For reference
\par   cout << BS(a).set() << " [a.set()]" << endl;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < sz; i++)
\par     if(!a.test(i)) \{
\par       cout << BS(a).set(i) 
\par         << " [a.set(" << i <<")]" << endl;
\par       break; // Just do one example of this
\par     \}
\par   cout << BS(a).reset() << " [a.reset()]"<< endl;
\par   for(int j = 0; j < sz; j++)
\par     if(a.test(j)) \{
\par       cout << BS(a).reset(j) 
\par         << " [a.reset(" << j <<")]" << endl;
\par       break; // Just do one example of this
\par     \}
\par   cout << BS(a).flip() << " [a.flip()]" << endl;
\par   cout << ~a << " [~a]" << endl;  
\par   cout << a << " [a]" << endl; // For reference
\par   cout << BS(a).flip(1) << " [a.flip(1)]"<< endl;
\par   BS c;
\par   cout << c << " [c]" << endl;
\par   cout << "c.count() = " << c.count() << endl;
\par   cout << "c.any() = " 
\par     << (c.any() ? "true" : "false") << endl;
\par   cout << "c.none() = " 
\par     << (c.none() ? "true" : "false") << endl;
\par   c[1].flip(); c[2].flip();
\par   cout << c << " [c]" << endl;
\par   cout << "c.count() = " << c.count() << endl;
\par   cout << "c.any() = " 
\par     << (c.any() ? "true" : "false") << endl;
\par   cout << "c.none() = " 
\par     << (c.none() ? "true" : "false") << endl;
\par   // Array indexing operations:
\par   c.reset();
\par   for(int k = 0; k < c.size(); k++)
\par     if(k % 2 == 0)
\par       c[k].flip();
\par   cout << c << " [c]" << endl;
\par   c.reset();
\par   // Assignment to bool:
\par   for(int ii = 0; ii < c.size(); ii++)
\par     c[ii] = (rand() % 100) < 25;
\par   cout << c << " [c]" << endl;
\par   // bool test:
\par   if(c[1] == true) 
\par     cout << "c[1] == true"; 
\par   else 
\par     cout << "c[1] == false" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To generate interesting random }{\b bitset}{s, the }{\b randBitset(\~)}{ function is created. The Standard C }{\b rand(\~)}{ function only generates an }{\b int}{, so this function demonstrates }{\b 
operator<<=}{ by shifting each 16 random bits to the left until the }{\b bitset}{ (which is templatized in this function for size) is full. The generated number and each new 16 bits is combined using the }{\b operator|=}{.
\par The first thing demonstrated in }{\b main(\~)}{ is the unit size of a }{\b bitset}{. If it is less than 32 bits, }{\b sizeof}{ produces 4 (4 bytes = 32 bits), which is the size of a single }{\b long}{ on most implementations. If it\rquote 
s between 32 and 64, it requires two }{\b long}{s, greater than 64 requires 3 }{\b long}{s, etc. Thus you make the best use of space if you use a bit quantity that fits in an integral number of }{\b long}{s. However, notice there\rquote 
s no extra overhead for the object \endash  it\rquote s as if you were hand-coding to use a }{\b long}{.
\par Another clue that }{\b bitset}{ is optimized for }{\b long}{s is that there is a }{\b to_ulong(\~)}{ member function that produces the value of the bitset as an }{\b unsigned long}{. There are no other numerical conversions from }{\b bitset}{
, but there is a }{\b to_string(\~)}{ conversion that produces a }{\b string}{ containing ones and zeros, and this can be as long as the actual }{\b bitset}{. However, using }{\b bitset<32>}{ may make your life simpler because of }{\b to_ulong(\~)}{.

\par There\rquote s still no primitive format for binary values, but the next best thing is supported by }{\b bitset}{: a }{\b string}{ of ones and zeros with the least-significant bit (l
sb) on the right. The three constructors demonstrated show taking the entire }{\b string }{(the }{\b char }{array is automatically converted to a }{\b string}{), the }{\b string}{
 starting at character 2, and the string from character 2 through 11. You can write to an }{\b ostream}{ from a }{\b bitset}{ using }{\b operator<< }{and it comes out as ones and zeros. You can also read from an }{\b istream}{ using }{\b operator>>}{
 (not shown here).
\par You\rquote ll notice that }{\b bitset}{ only has three non-member operators: }{\i and}{ (}{\b &}{), }{\i or}{ (}{\b |}{) and }{\i exclusive-or}{ (}{\b ^}{). Each of these create a new }{\b bitset }{
as their return value. All of the member operators opt for the more efficient }{\b &=}{, }{\b |=}{, etc. form where a temporary is not created. However, these forms actually change their lvalue (which is }{\b a}{
 in most of the tests in the above example). To prevent this, I created a temporary to be used as the lvalue by invoking the copy-constructor on }{\b a}{; this is why you see the form }{\b BS(a)}{. The result of each test is printed out, and occasionally 
}{\b a}{ is reprinted so you can easily look at it for reference.
\par The rest of the example should be self-explanatory when you run it; if not you can find the details in your compiler\rquote s documentation or the other documentation mentioned earlier in this chapter.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776414}vector<bool>{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776414}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b vector<bool>}{ is a specialization of the }{\b vector}{ template. A normal }{\b bool}{ variable requires at least one byte, but since a }{\b bool}{ only has two states the ideal implementation of }
{\b vector<bool>}{ is such that each }{\b bool}{ value only requires one bit. This means the iterator must be specially-defined, and cannot be a }{\b bool*}{.
\par The bit-manipulation functions for }{\b vector<bool>}{ are much more limited than those of }{\b bitset}{. The only member function that was added to those already in }{\b vector}{ is }{\b flip(\~)}{, to invert all the bits; there is no }{\b set(\~)}{ or }
{\b reset(\~)}{ as in }{\b bitset}{. When you use }{\b operator[ ]}{, you get back an object of type }{\b vector<bool>::reference}{, which also has a }{\b flip(\~)}{ to invert that individual bit.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:VectorOfBool.cpp
\par // Demonstrate the vector<bool> specialization
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <sstream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <bitset>
\par #include <iterator>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<bool> vb(10, true);
\par   vector<bool>::iterator it;
\par   for(it = vb.begin(); it != vb.end(); it++)
\par     cout << *it;
\par   cout << endl;
\par   vb.push_back(false);
\par   ostream_iterator<bool> out(cout, "");
\par   copy(vb.begin(), vb.end(), out);
\par   cout << endl;
\par   bool ab[] = \{ true, false, false, true, true, 
\par     true, true, false, false, true \};
\par   // There's a similar constructor:
\par   vb.assign(ab, ab + sizeof(ab)/sizeof(bool));
\par   copy(vb.begin(), vb.end(), out);
\par   cout << endl;
\par   vb.flip(); // Flip all bits
\par   copy(vb.begin(), vb.end(), out);
\par   cout << endl;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < vb.size(); i++)
\par     vb[i] = 0; // (Equivalent to "false")
\par   vb[4] = true;
\par   vb[5] = 1;
\par   vb[7].flip(); // Invert one bit
\par   copy(vb.begin(), vb.end(), out);
\par   cout << endl;
\par   // Convert to a bitset:
\par   ostringstream os;
\par   copy(vb.begin(), vb.end(), 
\par     ostream_iterator<bool>(os, ""));
\par   bitset<10> bs(os.str());
\par   cout << "Bitset:\\n" << bs << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The last part of this example takes a }{\b vector<bool>}{ and converts it to a }{\b bitset}{ by first turning it into a }{\b string}{ of ones and zeros. Of course, you must know the size of the }{\b 
bitset}{ at compile-time. You can see that this conversion is not the kind of operation you\rquote ll want to do on a regular basis.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776415}Associative containers{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776415}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b set}{, }{\b map}{, }{\b multiset}{ and }{\b multimap}{ are called }{\i associative containers}{ because they associate }{\i keys}{ with }{\i values}{. Well, at least }{\b map}{s and }{\b 
multimap}{s associate keys to values, but you can look at a }{\b set}{ as a }{\b map}{ that has no values, only keys (and they can in fact be implemented this way), and the same for the relationship between }{\b multiset}{ and }{\b multimap}{
. So, because of the structural similarity }{\b set}{s and }{\b multiset}{s are lumped in with associative containers.
\par The most important basic operations with associative containers are putting things in, and in the case of a }{\b set}{, seeing if something is in the set. In the case of a }{\b map}{, you want to first see if a key is in the }{\b map}{
, and if it exists you want the associated value for that key to be returned. Of course, there are many variations on this theme but that\rquote s the fundamental concept. The following example shows these basics:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:AssociativeBasics.cpp
\par // Basic operations with sets and maps
\par #include "Noisy.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <set>
\par #include <map>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Noisy na[] = \{ Noisy(), Noisy(), Noisy(), 
\par     Noisy(), Noisy(), Noisy(), Noisy() \};
\par   // Add elements via constructor:
\par   set<Noisy> ns(na, na+ sizeof na/sizeof(Noisy));
\par   // Ordinary insertion:
\par   Noisy n;
\par   ns.insert(n);
\par   cout << endl;
\par   // Check for set membership:
\par   cout << "ns.count(n)= " << ns.count(n) << endl;
\par   if(ns.find(n) != ns.end())
\par     cout << "n(" << n << ") found in ns" << endl;
\par   // Print elements:
\par   copy(ns.begin(), ns.end(), 
\par     ostream_iterator<Noisy>(cout, " "));
\par   cout << endl;
\par   cout << "\\n-----------\\n";
\par   map<int, Noisy> nm;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
\par     nm[i]; // Automatically makes pairs
\par   cout << "\\n-----------\\n";
\par   for(int j = 0; j < nm.size(); j++)
\par     cout << "nm[" << j <<"] = " << nm[j] << endl;
\par   cout << "\\n-----------\\n";
\par   nm[10] = n;
\par   cout << "\\n-----------\\n";
\par   nm.insert(make_pair(47, n));
\par   cout << "\\n-----------\\n";
\par   cout << "\\n nm.count(10)= " 
\par     << nm.count(10) << endl;
\par   cout << "nm.count(11)= " 
\par     << nm.count(11) << endl;
\par   map<int, Noisy>::iterator it = nm.find(6);
\par   if(it != nm.end())
\par     cout << "value:" << (*it).second
\par       << " found in nm at location 6" << endl;
\par   for(it = nm.begin(); it != nm.end(); it++)
\par     cout << (*it).first << ":" 
\par       << (*it).second << ", ";
\par   cout << "\\n-----------\\n";
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b set<Noisy>}{ object }{\b ns}{ is created using two iterators into an array of }{\b Noisy}{
 objects, but there is also a default constructor and a copy-constructor, and you can pass in an object that provides an alternate scheme for doing comparisons. Both }{\b set}{s and }{\b map}{s have an }{\b insert(\~)}{
 member function to put things in, and there are a couple of different ways to check to see if an object is already in an associative container: }{\b count(\~)}{, when given a key
, will tell you how many times that key occurs (this can only be zero or one in a }{\b set}{ or }{\b map}{, but it can be more than one with a }{\b multiset}{ or }{\b multimap}{). The }{\b find(\~)}{
 member function will produce an iterator indicating the first occurrence (with }{\b set}{ and }{\b map}{, the }{\i only}{ occurrence) of the key that you give it, or the past-the-end iterator if it can\rquote t find the key. The }{\b count(\~)}{ and }{
\b find(\~)}{ member functions exist for all the associative containers, which makes sense. The associative containers also have member functions }{\b lower_bound(\~)}{, }{\b upper\-_bound(\~)}{ and }{\b equal_range(\~)}{
, which actually only make sense for }{\b multiset}{ and }{\b multimap}{, as you shall see (but don\rquote t try to figure out how they would be useful for }{\b set}{ and }{\b map}{, since they are designed for dealing with a range of 
duplicate keys, which those containers don\rquote t allow).
\par Designing an }{\b operator[ ]}{ always produces a little bit of a dilemma because it\rquote s intended to be treated as an array-indexing operation, so people don\rquote t tend to think about performing a test before they us
e it. But what happens if you decide to index out of the bounds of the array? One option, of course, is to throw an exception, but with a }{\b map}{ \ldblquote indexing out of the array\rdblquote  could mean that you want an entry there, and that\rquote 
s the way the STL }{\b map}{ treats it. The first }{\b for}{ loop after the creation of the }{\b map<int, Noisy> nm}{ just \ldblquote looks up\rdblquote  objects using the }{\b operator[ ]}{, but this is actually creating new }{\b Noisy}{ objects! The }{
\b map}{ creates a new key-value pair (using the default constructor for the value) if you look up a value with }{\b operator[ ]}{ and it isn\rquote 
t there. This means that if you really just want to look something up and not create a new entry, you must use }{\b count(\~)}{ (to see if it\rquote s there) or }{\b find(\~)}{ (to get an iterator to it).
\par The }{\b for}{ loop that prints out the values of the container using }{\b operator[ ]}{ has a number of problems. First, it requires integral keys (which we happen to have in this case). Next and worse, if all the keys are not sequential, you\rquote 
ll end up counting from 0 to the size of the container, and if there are some spots which don\rquote t have key-value pairs you\rquote 
ll automatically create them, and miss some of the higher values of the keys. Finally, if you look at the output from the }{\b for}{ loop you\rquote ll see that things are }{\i very}{ busy, and it\rquote s quite puzzling at firs
t why there are so many constructions and destructions for what appears to be a simple lookup. The answer only becomes clear when you look at the code in the }{\b map}{ template for }{\b operator[ ]}{, which will be something like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {mapped_type& operator[] (const key_type& k) \{
\par   value_type tmp(k,T()); 
\par   return (*((insert(tmp)).first)).second;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Following the trail, you\rquote ll find that }{\b map::value_type}{ is:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {typedef pair<const Key, T> value_type;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now you need to know what a }{\b pair}{ is, which can be found in }{\b <utility>}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {template <class T1, class T2>
\par struct pair \{ 
\par   typedef T1 first_type; 
\par   typedef T2 second_type; 
\par   T1 first; 
\par   T2 second; 
\par   pair(); 
\par   pair(const T1& x, const T2& y) 
\par     : first(x), second(y) \{\}
\par   // Templatized copy-constructor:
\par   template<class U, class V> 
\par    pair(const pair<U, V> &p);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It turns out this is a very important (albeit simple) }{\b struct}{ which is used quite a bit in the STL. All it really does it package together two objects, but it\rquote 
s very useful, especially when you want to return two objects from a function (since a }{\b return}{ statement only takes one object). There\rquote s even a shorthand for creating a pair called }{\b make_pair(\~)}{, which is used in }{\b 
AssociativeBasics.cpp}{.
\par So to retrace the steps, }{\b map::value_type}{ is a }{\b pair}{ of the key and the value of the map \endash  actually, it\rquote s a single entry for the map. But notice that }{\b pair}{
 packages its objects by value, which means that copy-constructions are necessary to get the objects into the }{\b pair}{. Thus, the creation of }{\b tmp}{ in }{\b map::operator[ ]}{ will involve at least a 
copy-constructor call and destructor call for each object in the }{\b pair}{. Here, we\rquote re getting off easy because the key is an }{\b int}{. But if you want to really see what kind of activity can result from }{\b map::operator[ ]}{
, try running this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:NoisyMap.cpp
\par // Mapping Noisy to Noisy
\par #include "Noisy.h"
\par #include <map>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   map<Noisy, Noisy> mnn;
\par   Noisy n1, n2;
\par   cout << "\\n--------\\n";
\par   mnn[n1] = n2;
\par   cout << "\\n--------\\n";
\par   cout << mnn[n1] << endl;
\par   cout << "\\n--------\\n";
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You\rquote ll see that both the insertion and lookup generate a lot of extra objects, and that\rquote s because of the creation of the }{\b tmp}{ object. If you look back up at }{\b map::operator[ ]}{
 you\rquote ll see that the second line calls }{\b insert(\~)}{ passing it }{\b tmp}{ \endash  that is, }{\b operator[ ]}{ does an insertion every time. The return value of }{\b insert(\~)}{ is a different kind of }{\b pair}{, where }{\b first}{
 is an iterator pointing to the key-value }{\b pair}{ that was just inserted, and }{\b second}{ is a }{\b bool}{ indicating whether the insertion took place. You can see that }{\b operator[ ]}{ grabs }{\b first}{
 (the iterator), dereferences it to produce the }{\b pair}{, and then returns the }{\b second}{ which is the value at that location. 
\par So on the upside, }{\b map}{ has this fancy \ldblquote make a new entry if one isn\rquote t there\rdblquote  behavior, but the downside is that you }{\i always}{ get a lot of extra object creations and destructions when you use }{\b map::operator[ ]}{
. Fortunately, }{\b AssociativeBasics.cpp}{ also demonstrates how to reduce the overhead of insertions and deletions, by not using }{\b operator[ ]}{ if you don\rquote t have to. The }{\b insert(\~)}{ member function is slightly more efficient than }{\b 
operator[ ]}{. With a }{\b set}{ you only hold one object, but with a }{\b map}{ you hold key-value pairs, so }{\b insert(\~)}{ requires a }{\b pair}{ as its argument. Here\rquote s where }{\b make_pair(\~)}{ comes in handy, as you can see.
\par For looking objects up in a }{\b map}{, you can use }{\b count(\~)}{ to see whether a key is in the map, or you can use }{\b find(\~)}{ to produce an iterator pointing directly at the key-value pair. Again, since the }{\b map}{ contains }{\b pair}{s that
\rquote s what the iterator produces when you dereference it, so you have to select }{\b first}{ and }{\b second}{. When you run }{\b AssociativeBasics.cpp}{ you\rquote 
ll notice that the iterator approach involves no extra object creations or destructions at all. It\rquote s not as easy to write or read, though.
\par If you use a }{\b map}{ with large, complex objects and discover there\rquote s too much overhead when doing lookups and insertions (don\rquote t assume this from the beginning \endash 
 take the easy approach first and use a profiler to discover bottlenecks), then you can use the counted-handle approach shown in Chapter XX so that you are only passing around small, lightweight objects.
\par Of course, you can also iterate through a }{\b set}{ or }{\b map}{ and operate on each of its objects. This will be demonstrated in later examples.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776416}Generators and fillers \line for associative containers{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776416}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You\rquote ve seen how useful the }{\b fill(\~)}{, }{\b fill_n(\~)}{, }{\b generate(\~)}{ and }{\b generate_n(\~)}{ function templates in }{\b <algorithm>}{
 have been for filling the sequential containers (}{\b vector}{, }{\b list}{ and }{\b deque}{) with data. However, these are implemented by using }{\b operator= }{
to assign values into the sequential containers, and the way that you add objects to associative containers is with their respective }{\b insert(\~)}{ member functions. Thus the default \ldblquote assignment\rdblquote 
 behavior causes a problem when trying to use the \ldblquote fill\rdblquote  and \ldblquote generate\rdblquote  functions with associative containers.
\par One solution is to duplicate the \ldblquote fill\rdblquote  and \ldblquote generate\rdblquote  functions, creating new ones that can be used with associative containers. It turns out that only the }{\b fill_n(\~)}{ and }{\b generate_n(\~)}{
 functions can be duplicated (}{\b fill(\~) }{and }{\b generate(\~) }{copy in between two iterators, which doesn\rquote t make sense with associative containers), but the job is fairly easy, since you have the }{\b <algorithm>}{
 header file to work from (and since it contains templates, all the source code is there):
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:assocGen.h
\par // The fill_n() and generate_n() equivalents 
\par // for associative containers.
\par #ifndef ASSOCGEN_H
\par #define ASSOCGEN_H
\par 
\par template<class Assoc, class Count, class T>
\par void 
\par assocFill_n(Assoc& a, Count n, const T& val) \{
\par   for (; 0 < n; --n)
\par     a.insert(val);
\par \}
\par 
\par template<class Assoc, class Count, class Gen>
\par void assocGen_n(Assoc& a, Count n, Gen g) \{
\par   for (; 0 < n; --n)
\par     a.insert(g());
\par \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // ASSOCGEN_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can see that instead of using iterators, the container class itself is passed (by reference, of course, since you wouldn\rquote 
t want to make a local copy, fill it, and then have it discarded at the end of the scope).
\par This code demonstrates two valuable lessons. The first lesson is that if the algorithms don\rquote t do what you want, copy the nearest thing and modify it. You have the example at hand in the STL header, so most of the work has already been done.
\par The second lesson is more pointed: if you look long enough, there\rquote s probably a way to do it in the STL }{\i without}{ inventing anything new. The present problem can instead be solved by using an }{\b insert_iterator}{ (produced by a call to }{\b 
inserter(\~)}{), which calls }{\b insert(\~)}{ to place items in the container instead of }{\b operator=}{. This is }{\i not}{ simply a variation of }{\b front_insert_iterator}{ (produced by a call to }{\b front_inserter(\~)}{) or }{\b 
back_insert_iterator}{ (produced by a call to }{\b back_inserter(\~)}{), since those iterators use }{\b push_front(\~)}{ and }{\b push_back(\~)}{, respectively. Each of the insert iterators is different by virtue of the member function it uses 
for insertion, and }{\b insert(\~)}{ is the one we need. Here\rquote s a demonstration that shows filling and generating both a }{\b map}{ and a }{\b set}{ (of course, it can also be used with }{\b multimap}{ and }{\b multiset}{
). First, some templatized, simple generators are created (this may seem like overkill, but you never know when you\rquote ll need them; for that reason they\rquote re placed in a header file):
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:SimpleGenerators.h
\par // Generic generators, including
\par // one that creates pairs
\par #include <iostream>
\par 
\par // A generator that increments its value:
\par template<typename T>
\par class IncrGen \{
\par   T i;
\par public:
\par   IncrGen(T ii) : i (ii) \{\}
\par   T operator()() \{ return i++; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // A generator that produces an STL pair<>:
\par template<typename T1, typename T2>
\par class PairGen \{
\par   T1 i;
\par   T2 j;
\par public:
\par   PairGen(T1 ii, T2 jj) : i(ii), j(jj) \{\}
\par   std::pair<T1,T2> operator()() \{ 
\par     return std::pair<T1,T2>(i++, j++); 
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // A generic global operator<< 
\par // for printing any STL pair<>:
\par template<typename Pair> std::ostream&
\par operator<<(std::ostream& os, const Pair& p) \{
\par   return os << p.first << "\\t" 
\par     << p.second << std::endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Both generators expect that }{\b T}{ can be incremented, and they simply use }{\b operator++}{ to generate new values from whatever you used for initialization. }{\b PairGen}{ creates an STL }{\b pair
}{ object as its return value, and that\rquote s what can be placed into a }{\b map}{ or }{\b multimap}{ using }{\b insert(\~)}{.
\par The last function is a generalization of }{\b operator<<}{ for }{\b ostream}{s, so that any }{\b pair}{ can be printed, assuming each element of the }{\b pair }{supports a stream }{\b operator<<}{. As you can see below, this allows the use of }{\b copy(\~
)}{ to output the }{\b map}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:AssocInserter.cpp
\par // Using an insert_iterator so fill_n() and
\par // generate_n() can be used with associative 
\par // containers
\par #include "SimpleGenerators.h"
\par #include <iterator>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par #include <set>
\par #include <map>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   set<int> s;
\par   fill_n(inserter(s, s.begin()), 10, 47);
\par   generate_n(inserter(s, s.begin()), 10, 
\par     IncrGen<int>(12));
\par   copy(s.begin(), s.end(), 
\par     ostream_iterator<int>(cout, "\\n"));
\par   
\par   map<int, int> m;
\par   fill_n(inserter(m, m.begin()), 10, 
\par     make_pair(90,120));
\par   generate_n(inserter(m, m.begin()), 10, 
\par     PairGen<int, int>(3, 9));
\par   copy(m.begin(), m.end(), 
\par     ostream_iterator<pair<int,int> >(cout,"\\n"));
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The second argument to }{\b inserter}{ is an interator, which actually isn\rquote 
t used in the case of associative containers since they maintain their order internally, rather than allowing you to tell them where the element should be inserted. However, an }{\b insert_iterator}{
 can be used with many different types of containers so you must provide the iterator.
\par Note how the }{\b ostream_iterator}{ is created to output a }{\b pair}{; this wouldn\rquote t have worked if the }{\b operator<<}{ hadn\rquote t been created, and since it\rquote s a template it is automatically instantiated for }{\b pair<int, int>}{.

\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776417}The magic of maps{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776417}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {An ordinary array uses an integral value to index into a sequential set of elements of some type. A }{\b map}{ is an }{\i associative array}{, which means you associate one object with another in an
 array-like fashion, but instead of selecting an array element with a number as you do with an ordinary array, you look it up with an object! The example which follows counts the words in a text file, so the index is the }{\b string}{
 object representing the word, and the value being looked up is the object that keeps count of the strings.
\par In a single-item container like a }{\b vector}{ or }{\b list}{, there\rquote s only one thing being held. But in a }{\b map}{, you\rquote ve got two things: the }{\i key}{ (what you look up by, as in }{\b mapname[key]}{) and the }{\i value}{
 that results from the lookup with the key. If you simply want to move through the entire }{\b map}{ and list each key-value pair, you use an iterator, which when dereferenced produces a }{\b pair}{
 object containing both the key and the value. You access the members of a }{\b pair}{ by selecting }{\b first}{ or }{\b second}{.
\par This same philosophy of packaging two items together is also used to insert elements into the map, but the }{\b pair}{ is created as part of the instantiated }{\b map}{ and is called }{\b value_type}{, containing the key and the va
lue. So one option for inserting a new element is to create a }{\b value_type}{ object, loading it with the appropriate objects and then calling the }{\b insert(\~)}{ member function for the }{\b map}{
. Instead, the following example makes use of the aforementioned special feature of }{\b map}{: if you\rquote re trying to find an object by passing in a key to }{\b operator[ ]}{ and that object doesn\rquote t exist, }{\b operator[ ]}{
 will automatically insert a new key-value pair for you, using the default constructor for the value object. With that in mind, consider an implementation of a word counting program:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:WordCount.cpp
\par //\{L\} StreamTokenizer
\par // Count occurrences of words using a map
\par #include "StreamTokenizer.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <string>
\par #include <map>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Count \{
\par   int i;
\par public:
\par   Count() : i(0) \{\}
\par   void operator++(int) \{ i++; \} // Post-increment  
\par   int& val() \{ return i; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par typedef map<string, Count> WordMap;
\par typedef WordMap::iterator WMIter;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1);
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]);
\par   StreamTokenizer words(in);
\par   WordMap wordmap;
\par   string word;
\par   while((word = words.next()).size() != 0)
\par     wordmap[word]++;
\par   for(WMIter w = wordmap.begin(); 
\par       w != wordmap.end(); w++)
\par     cout << (*w).first << ": "
\par       << (*w).second.val() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The need for the }{\b Count}{ class is to contain an }{\b int}{ that\rquote s automatically initialized to zero. This is necessary because of the crucial line:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {wordmap[string(word)]++;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This finds the word that has been produced by }{\b StreamTokenizer }{and increments the }{\b Count}{ object associated with that word, which is fine as long as there }{\i is}{
 a key-value pair for that }{\b string}{. If there isn\rquote t, the }{\b map}{ automatically inserts a key for the word you\rquote re looking up, and a }{\b Count}{ object, which is initialized to zero by the default constructor. Thus, when it\rquote 
s incremented the }{\b Count}{ becomes 1.
\par Printing the entire list requires traversing it with an iterator (there\rquote s no }{\b copy(\~)}{ shortcut for a }{\b map}{ unless you want to write an }{\b operator<< }{for the }{\b pair}{
 in the map). As previously mentioned, dereferencing this iterator produces a }{\b pair}{ object, with the }{\b first}{ member the key and the }{\b second}{ member the value. In this case }{\b second}{ is a }{\b Count}{ object, so its }{\b val(\~)}{ me
mber must be called to produce the actual word count.
\par If you want to find the count for a particular word, you can use the array index operator, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {cout << "the: " << wordmap["the"].val() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can see that one of the great advantages of the }{\b map}{ is the clarity of the syntax; an associative array makes intuitive sense to the reader (note, however, that if \ldblquote the\rdblquote 
 isn\rquote t already in the }{\b wordmap}{ a new entry will be created!).
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {A command-line argument tool
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A problem that often comes up in programming is the management of program arguments that you can specify on the command line. Usually you\rquote 
d like to have a set of defaults that can be changed via the command line. The following tool expects the command line arguments to be in the form }{\b flag1=value1}{ with no spaces around the \lquote }{\b =}{\lquote 
 (so it will be treated as a single argument). The }{\b ProgVal}{ class simply inherits from }{\b map<string, string>}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:ProgVals.h
\par // Program values can be changed by command line
\par #ifndef PROGVALS_H
\par #define PROGVALS_H
\par #include <map>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <string>
\par 
\par class ProgVals 
\par   : public std::map<std::string, std::string> \{
\par public:
\par   ProgVals(std::string defaults[][2], int sz);
\par   void parse(int argc, char* argv[],
\par     std::string usage, int offset = 1);
\par   void print(std::ostream& out = std::cout);
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // PROGVALS_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The constructor expects an array of }{\b string}{ pairs (as you\rquote ll see, this allows you to initialize it with an array of }{\b char*}{) and the size of that array. The }{\b parse(\~)}{
 member function is handed the command-line arguments along with a \ldblquote usage\rdblquote  string to print if the command line is given incorrectly, and the \ldblquote offset\rdblquote 
 which tells it which command-line argument to start with (so you can have non-flag arguments at the beginning of the command line). Finally, }{\b print(\~)}{ displays the values. Here is the implementation:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:ProgVals.cpp \{O\}
\par #include "ProgVals.h"
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par ProgVals::ProgVals(
\par   std::string defaults[][2], int sz) \{
\par   for(int i = 0; i < sz; i++)
\par     insert(make_pair(
\par       defaults[i][0], defaults[i][1]));
\par \}
\par 
\par void ProgVals::parse(int argc, char* argv[],
\par   string usage, int offset) \{
\par   // Parse and apply additional
\par   // command-line arguments:
\par   for(int i = offset; i < argc; i++) \{
\par     string flag(argv[i]);
\par     int equal = flag.find('=');
\par     if(equal == string::npos) \{
\par       cerr << "Command line error: " <<
\par         argv[i] << endl << usage << endl;
\par       continue; // Next argument
\par     \}
\par     string name = flag.substr(0, equal);
\par     string value = flag.substr(equal + 1);
\par     if(find(name) == end()) \{
\par       cerr << name << endl << usage << endl;
\par       continue; // Next argument
\par     \}
\par     operator[](name) = value;
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par void ProgVals::print(ostream& out) \{
\par   out << "Program values:" << endl;
\par   for(iterator it = begin(); it != end(); it++)
\par     out << (*it).first << " = "
\par         << (*it).second << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The constructor uses the STL }{\b make_pair(\~)}{ helper function to convert each pair of }{\b char*}{ into a }{\b pair}{ object that can be inserted into the }{\b map}{. In }{\b parse(\~)}{
, each command-line argument is checked for the existence of the telltale \lquote }{\b =}{\lquote  sign (reporting an error if it isn\rquote t there), and then is broken into two strings, the }{\b name}{ which appears before the \lquote }{\b =}{\lquote 
, and the }{\b value}{ which appears after. The }{\b operator[\~]}{ is then used to change the existing value to the new one.
\par Here\rquote s an example to test the tool:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:ProgValTest.cpp
\par //\{L\} ProgVals
\par #include "ProgVals.h"
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par string defaults[][2] = \{
\par   \{ "color", "red" \},
\par   \{ "size", "medium" \},
\par   \{ "shape", "rectangular" \},
\par   \{ "action", "hopping"\},
\par \};
\par 
\par const char* usage = "usage:\\n"
\par "ProgValTest [flag1=val1 flag2=val2 ...]\\n"
\par "(Note no space around '=')\\n"
\par "Where the flags can be any of: \\n"
\par "color, size, shape, action \\n";
\par 
\par // So it can be used globally:
\par ProgVals pvals(defaults, 
\par   sizeof defaults / sizeof *defaults);
\par 
\par class Animal \{
\par   string color, size, shape, action;
\par public:
\par   Animal(string col, string sz, 
\par     string shp, string act) 
\par     :color(col),size(sz),shape(shp),action(act)\{\}
\par   // Default constructor uses program default
\par   // values, possibly change on command line:
\par   Animal() : color(pvals["color"]), 
\par     size(pvals["size"]), shape(pvals["shape"]),
\par     action(pvals["action"]) \{\}
\par   void print() \{
\par     cout << "color = " << color << endl
\par       << "size = " << size << endl
\par       << "shape = " << shape << endl
\par       << "action = " << action << endl;
\par   \}
\par   // And of course pvals can be used anywhere
\par   // else you'd like.
\par \};
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   // Initialize and parse command line values
\par   // before any code that uses pvals is called:
\par   pvals.parse(argc, argv, usage);
\par   pvals.print();
\par   Animal a;
\par   cout << "Animal a values:" << endl;
\par   a.print();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This program can create }{\b Animal}{ objects with different characteristics, and those characteristics can be established wit
h the command line. The default characteristics are given in the two-dimensional array of }{\b char*}{ called }{\b defaults}{ and, after the }{\b usage}{ string you can see a global instance of }{\b ProgVals}{ called }{\b pvals }{
is created; this is important because it allows the rest of the code in the program to access the values.
\par Note that }{\b Animal}{\rquote s default constructor uses the values in }{\b pvals}{ inside its constructor initializer list. When you run the program you can try creating different animal characteristics.
\par Many command-line programs also use a style of beginning a flag with a hyphen, and sometimes they use single-character flags.
\par The STL }{\b map}{ is used in numerous places throughout the rest of this book.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374082}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776418}Multimaps and duplicate keys{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776418}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A }{\b multimap}{ is a }{\b map}{ that can contain duplicate keys. A
t first this may seem like a strange idea, but it can occur surprisingly often. A phone book, for example, can have many entries with the same name. 
\par Suppose you are monitoring wildlife, and you want to keep track of where and when each type of animal is spotted. Thus, you may see many animals of the same kind, all in different locations and at different times. So if the type of animal is the key, you
\rquote ll need a }{\b multimap}{. Here\rquote s what it looks like:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:WildLifeMonitor.cpp
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <map>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <sstream>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class DataPoint \{
\par   int x, y; // Location coordinates
\par   time_t time; // Time of Sighting
\par public:
\par   DataPoint() : x(0), y(0), time(0) \{\}
\par   DataPoint(int xx, int yy, time_t tm) :
\par     x(xx), y(yy), time(tm) \{\}
\par   // Synthesized operator=, copy-constructor OK
\par   int getX() \{ return x; \}
\par   int getY() \{ return y; \}
\par   time_t* getTime() \{ return &time; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par string animal[] = \{ 
\par   "chipmunk", "beaver", "marmot", "weasel",
\par   "squirrel", "ptarmigan", "bear", "eagle",
\par   "hawk", "vole", "deer", "otter", "hummingbird",
\par \};
\par const int asz = sizeof animal/sizeof *animal;
\par vector<string> animals(animal, animal + asz);
\par 
\par // All the information is contained in a 
\par // "Sighting," which can be sent to an ostream:
\par typedef pair<string, DataPoint> Sighting;
\par 
\par ostream& 
\par operator<<(ostream& os, const Sighting& s) \{
\par   return os << s.first << " sighted at x= " << 
\par     s.second.getX() << ", y= " << s.second.getY()
\par     << ", time = " << ctime(s.second.getTime());
\par \}
\par 
\par // A generator for Sightings:
\par class SightingGen \{
\par   vector<string>& animals;
\par   static const int d = 100;
\par public:
\par   SightingGen(vector<string>& an) :
\par     animals(an) \{ srand(time(0)); \}
\par   Sighting operator()() \{
\par     Sighting result;
\par     int select = rand() % animals.size();
\par     result.first = animals[select];
\par     result.second = DataPoint(
\par       rand() % d, rand() % d, time(0));
\par     return result;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par typedef multimap<string, DataPoint> DataMap;
\par typedef DataMap::iterator DMIter;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   DataMap sightings;
\par   generate_n(
\par     inserter(sightings, sightings.begin()),
\par     50, SightingGen(animals));
\par   // Print everything:
\par   copy(sightings.begin(), sightings.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<Sighting>(cout, ""));
\par   // Print sightings for selected animal:
\par   while(true) \{
\par     cout << "select an animal or 'q' to quit: ";
\par     for(int i = 0; i < animals.size(); i++)
\par       cout <<'['<< i <<']'<< animals[i] << ' ';
\par     cout << endl;
\par     string reply;
\par     cin >> reply;
\par     if(reply.at(0) == 'q') return 0;
\par     istringstream r(reply);
\par     int i;
\par     r >> i; // Converts to int
\par     i %= animals.size();
\par     // Iterators in "range" denote begin, one 
\par     // past end of matching range:
\par     pair<DMIter, DMIter> range = 
\par       sightings.equal_range(animals[i]);
\par     copy(range.first, range.second,
\par       ostream_iterator<Sighting>(cout, ""));
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {All the data about a sighting is encapsulated into the class }{\b DataPoint}{, which is simple enough that it can rely on the synthesized assignment 
and copy-constructor. It uses the Standard C library time functions to record the time of the sighting.
\par In the array of }{\b string}{ }{\b animal}{, notice that the }{\b char*}{ constructor is automatically used during initialization, which makes initializing an array of }{\b string}{ quite convenient. Since it\rquote s easier to use the animal names in a }
{\b vector}{, the length of the array is calculated and a }{\b vector<string>}{ is initialized using the }{\b vector(iterator, iterator)}{ constructor.
\par The key-value pairs that make up a }{\b Sighting}{ are the }{\b string}{ which names the type of animal, and the }{\b DataPoint}{ that says where and when it was sighted. The standard }{\b pair}{
 template combines these two types and is typedefed to produce the }{\b Sighting}{ type. Then an }{\b ostream}{ }{\b operator<<}{ is created for }{\b Sighting}{; this will allow you to iterate through a map or multimap of }{\b Sighting}{s}{\b  }{
and print it out.
\par }{\b SightingGen}{ generates random sightings at random data points to use for testing. It has the usual }{\b operator(\~)}{ necessary for a function object, but it also has a constructor to capture and store a reference to a }{\b vector<string>}{
, which is where the aforementioned animal names are stored.
\par A }{\b DataMap}{ is a }{\b multimap}{ of }{\b string}{-}{\b DataPoint}{ pairs, which means it stores }{\b Sighting}{s. It is filled with 50 }{\b Sighting}{s using }{\b generate_n(\~)}{, and printed out (notice that because there is an }{\b operator<<}{
 that takes a }{\b Sighting}{, an }{\b ostream_iterator}{ can be created). At this point the user is asked to select the animal that they want to see all the sightings for. If you press }{\b \lquote q\rquote }{ the program will quit, but if you s
elect an animal number, then the }{\b equal_range(\~)}{ member function is invoked. This returns an iterator (}{\b DMIter}{
) to the beginning of the set of matching pairs, and one indicating past-the-end of the set. Since only one object can be returned from a function, }{\b equal_range(\~)}{ makes use of }{\b pair}{. Since the }{\b range}{
 pair has the beginning and ending iterators of the matching set, those iterators can be used in }{\b copy(\~)}{ to print out all the sightings for a particular type of animal.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776419}Multisets{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776419}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You\rquote ve seen the }{\b set}{, which only allows one object of each value to be inserted. The }{\b multiset}{
 is odd by comparison since it allows more than one object of each value to be inserted. This seems to go against the whole idea of \ldblquote setness,\rdblquote  where you can ask \ldblquote is \lquote it\rquote  in this set?\rdblquote  If there
 can be more than one of \lquote it\rquote , then what does that question mean?
\par With some thought, you can see that it makes no sense to have more than one object of the same value in a set if those duplicate objects are }{\i exactly}{ the same (with the possible exception of 
counting occurrences of objects, but as seen earlier in this chapter that can be handled in an alternative, more elegant fashion). Thus each duplicate object will have something that makes it unique from the other duplicates \endash 
 most likely different state information that is not used in the calculation of the value during the comparison. That is, to the comparison operation, the objects look the same but they actually contain some differing internal state.
\par Like any STL container that must order its elements, the }{\b multiset}{ template uses the }{\b less}{ template by default to determine element ordering. This uses the contained classes\rquote  }{\b operator<}{
, but you may of course substitute your own comparison function.
\par Consider a simple class that contains one element that is used in the comparison, and another that is not:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:MultiSet1.cpp
\par // Demonstration of multiset behavior
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <set>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class X \{
\par   char c; // Used in comparison
\par   int i; // Not used in comparison
\par   // Don't need default constructor and operator=
\par   X();
\par   X& operator=(const X&);
\par   // Usually need a copy-constructor (but the
\par   // synthesized version works here)
\par public:
\par   X(char cc, int ii) : c(cc), i(ii) \{\}
\par   // Notice no operator== is required
\par   friend bool operator<(const X& x, const X& y) \{
\par     return x.c < y.c;
\par   \}
\par   friend ostream& operator<<(ostream& os, X x) \{
\par     return os << x.c << ":" << x.i;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Xgen \{
\par   static int i;
\par   // Number of characters to select from:
\par   static const int span = 6;
\par public:
\par   Xgen() \{ srand(time(0)); \}
\par   X operator()() \{
\par     char c = 'A' + rand() % span;    
\par     return X(c, i++);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int Xgen::i = 0;
\par 
\par typedef multiset<X> Xmset;
\par typedef Xmset::const_iterator Xmit;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Xmset mset;
\par   // Fill it with X's:
\par   generate_n(inserter(mset, mset.begin()), 
\par     25, Xgen());
\par   // Initialize a regular set from mset:
\par   set<X> unique(mset.begin(), mset.end());
\par   copy(unique.begin(), unique.end(), 
\par     ostream_iterator<X>(cout, " "));
\par   cout << "\\n----\\n";
\par   // Iterate over the unique values:
\par   for(set<X>::iterator i = unique.begin();
\par       i != unique.end(); i++) \{
\par     pair<Xmit, Xmit> p = mset.equal_range(*i);
\par     copy(p.first, p.second, 
\par       ostream_iterator<X>(cout, " "));
\par     cout << endl;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In }{\b X}{, all the comparisons are made with the }{\b char c}{. The comparison is performed with }{\b operator<}{, which is all that is necessary for the }{\b multiset}{
, since in this example the default }{\b less}{ comparison object is used. The class }{\b Xgen}{ is used to randomly generate }{\b X}{ objects, but the comparison value is restricted to the span from }{\b \lquote A}{\rquote  to \lquote }{\b E}{\rquote 
. In }{\b main(\~)}{, a }{\b multiset<X>}{ is created and filled with 25 }{\b X}{ objects using }{\b Xgen}{, guaranteeing that there will be duplicate keys. So that we know what the unique values are, a regular }{\b set<X>}{ is created from the }{\b 
multiset}{ (using the }{\b iterator, iterator}{ constructor). These values are displayed, then each one is used to produce the }{\b equal_range(\~)}{ in the }{\b multiset}{ (}{\b equal_range(\~)}{ has the same meaning here as it does with }{\b multimap}{
: all the elements with matching keys). Each set of matching keys is then printed.
\par As a second example, a (possibly) more elegant version of }{\b WordCount.cpp}{ can be created using }{\b multiset}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:MultiSetWordCount.cpp
\par //\{L\} StreamTokenizer
\par // Count occurrences of words using a multiset
\par #include "StreamTokenizer.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <string>
\par #include <set>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <iterator>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1);
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   assure(in, argv[1]);
\par   StreamTokenizer words(in);
\par   multiset<string> wordmset;
\par   string word;
\par   while((word = words.next()).size() != 0)
\par     wordmset.insert(word);
\par   typedef multiset<string>::iterator MSit;
\par   MSit it = wordmset.begin();
\par   while(it != wordmset.end()) \{
\par     pair<MSit, MSit> p=wordmset.equal_range(*it);
\par     int count = distance(p.first, p.second);
\par     cout << *it << ": " << count << endl;
\par     it = p.second; // Move to the next word
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The setup in }{\b main(\~)}{ is identical to }{\b WordCount.cpp}{, but then each word is simply inserted into the }{\b multiset<string>}{
. An iterator is created and initialized to the beginning of the }{\b multiset}{; dereferencing this iterator produces the current word. }{\b equal_range(\~)}{ produces the starting and ending iterators of the word that\rquote 
s currently selected, and the STL algorithm }{\b distance(\~)}{ (which is in }{\b <iterator>}{) is used to count the number of elements in that range. Then the iterator }{\b it }{is moved forward to the end of the range, which puts it at the next word.
 Although if you\rquote re unfamiliar with the }{\b multiset}{ this code can seem more complex, the density of it and the lack of need for supporting classes like }{\b Count}{ has a lot of appeal.
\par In the end, is this really a \ldblquote set,\rdblquote  or should it be called something else? An alternative is the generic \ldblquote bag\rdblquote 
 that has been defined in some container libraries, since a bag holds anything at all without discrimination \endash  including duplicate objects. This is close, but it doesn\rquote t quite fit since a bag has no specification about how ele
ments should be ordered, while a }{\b multiset}{
 (which requires that all duplicate elements be adjacent to each other) is even more restrictive than the concept of a set, which could use a hashing function to order its elements, in which case they would not be i
n sorted order. Besides, if you wanted to store a bunch of objects without any special criterions, you\rquote d probably just use a }{\b vector}{, }{\b deque}{ or }{\b list}{. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776420}Combining STL containers{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776420}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When using a thesaurus, you have a word and you want to know all the words that are similar. When you look up a word, then, you want a list of words as the result. Here, the \ldblquote multi
\rdblquote  containers (}{\b multimap}{ or }{\b multiset}{) are not appropriate. The solution is to combine containers, which is easily done using the STL. Here, we need a tool that turns out to be a powerful general concept, which is a }{\b map}{ of }{
\b vector}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:Thesaurus.cpp
\par // A map of vectors
\par #include <map>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par typedef map<string, vector<string> > Thesaurus;
\par typedef pair<string, vector<string> > TEntry;
\par typedef Thesaurus::iterator TIter;
\par 
\par ostream& operator<<(ostream& os,const TEntry& t)\{
\par   os << t.first << ": ";
\par   copy(t.second.begin(), t.second.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<string>(os, " "));
\par   return os;
\par \}
\par 
\par // A generator for thesaurus test entries:
\par class ThesaurusGen \{
\par   static const string letters;
\par   static int count;
\par public:
\par   int maxSize() \{ return letters.size(); \}
\par   ThesaurusGen() \{ srand(time(0)); \}
\par   TEntry operator()() \{
\par     TEntry result;
\par     if(count >= maxSize()) count = 0;
\par     result.first = letters[count++];
\par     int entries = (rand() % 5) + 2;
\par     for(int i = 0; i < entries; i++) \{
\par       int choice = rand() % maxSize();
\par       char cbuf[2] = \{ 0 \};
\par       cbuf[0] = letters[choice];
\par       result.second.push_back(cbuf);
\par     \}
\par     return result;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int ThesaurusGen::count = 0;
\par const string ThesaurusGen::letters("ABCDEFGHIJKL"
\par   "MNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz");
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Thesaurus thesaurus;
\par   // Fill with 10 entries:
\par   generate_n(
\par     inserter(thesaurus, thesaurus.begin()), 
\par     10, ThesaurusGen());
\par   // Print everything:
\par   copy(thesaurus.begin(), thesaurus.end(),
\par     ostream_iterator<TEntry>(cout, "\\n"));
\par   // Ask for a "word" to look up:
\par   while(true) \{
\par     cout << "Select a \\"word\\", 0 to quit: ";
\par     for(TIter it = thesaurus.begin(); 
\par       it != thesaurus.end(); it++)
\par       cout << (*it).first << ' ';
\par     cout << endl;
\par     string reply;
\par     cin >> reply;
\par     if(reply.at(0) == '0') return 0; // Quit
\par     if(thesaurus.find(reply) == thesaurus.end())
\par       continue; // Not in list, try again
\par     vector<string>& v = thesaurus[reply];
\par     copy(v.begin(), v.end(), 
\par       ostream_iterator<string>(cout, " "));
\par     cout << endl;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A }{\b Thesaurus}{ maps a }{\b string}{ (the word) to a }{\b vector<string>}{ (the synonyms). A }{\b TEntry }{is a single entry in a }{\b Thesaurus}{. By creating an }{\b ostream operator<<}{ for a }{
\b TEntry}{, a single entry from the }{\b Thesaurus}{ can easily be printed (and the whole }{\b Thesaurus}{ can easily be printed with }{\b copy(\~)}{). The }{\b ThesaurusGen}{ creates \ldblquote words\rdblquote  (which are just single letters) and 
\ldblquote synonyms\rdblquote  for those words (which are just other randomly-chosen single letters) to be used as thesaurus entries. It randomly chooses the number of synonym entries to make, b
ut there must be at least two. All the letters are chosen by indexing into a }{\b static string}{ that is part of }{\b ThesaurusGen}{.
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{, a }{\b Thesaurus}{ is created, filled with 10 entries and printed using the }{\b copy(\~)}{ algorithm. Then the user is requested to choose a \ldblquote word\rdblquote  to look up by typing the letter of that word. The }{\b find(\~)}{
 member function is used to find whether the entry exists in the }{\b map}{ (remember, you don\rquote t want to use }{\b operator[ ]}{ or it will automatically make a new entry if it doesn\rquote t find a match!). If so, }{\b operator[ ]}{
 is used to fetch out the }{\b vector<string>}{ which is displayed.
\par Because templates make the expression of powerful concepts easy, you can take this concept much further, creating a }{\b map}{ of }{\b vector}{s containing }{\b map}{s, etc.{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374082} For that matter, you can c
ombine any of the STL containers this way.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776421}Cleaning up \line containers of pointers{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776421}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In }{\b Stlshape.cpp}{, the pointers did not clean themselves up automatically. It would be convenient to be able to do this easily, rather than writing out the code each time. Here is
 a function template that will clean up the pointers in any sequence container; note that it is placed in the book\rquote s root directory for easy access:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: :purge.h
\par // Delete pointers in an STL sequence container
\par #ifndef PURGE_H
\par #define PURGE_H
\par #include <algorithm>
\par 
\par template<class Seq> void purge(Seq& c) \{
\par   typename Seq::iterator i;
\par   for(i = c.begin(); i != c.end(); i++) \{
\par     delete *i;
\par     *i = 0;
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par // Iterator version:
\par template<class InpIt>
\par void purge(InpIt begin, InpIt end) \{
\par   while(begin != end) \{
\par     delete *begin;
\par     *begin = 0;
\par     begin++;
\par   \}
\par \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // PURGE_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the first version of }{\b purge(\~)}{, note that }{\b typename}{ is absolutely necessary; indeed this is exactly the case that the keyword was added for: }{\b Seq}{ is a template argument, and }{
\b iterator}{ is something that is nested within that template. So what does }{\b Seq::iterator}{ refer to? The }{\b typename}{ keyword specifies that it refers to a type, and not something else.
\par While the container version of purge must work with an STL-style container, the iterator version of }{\b purge(\~)}{ will work with any range, including an array.
\par Here is }{\b Stlshape.cpp}{, modified to use the }{\b purge(\~)}{ function:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:Stlshape2.cpp
\par // Stlshape.cpp with the purge() function
\par #include "../purge.h"
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Shape \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void draw() = 0;
\par   virtual ~Shape() \{\};
\par \};
\par 
\par class Circle : public Shape \{
\par public:
\par   void draw() \{ cout << "Circle::draw\\n"; \}
\par   ~Circle() \{ cout << "~Circle\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Triangle : public Shape \{
\par public:
\par   void draw() \{ cout << "Triangle::draw\\n"; \}
\par   ~Triangle() \{ cout << "~Triangle\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Square : public Shape \{
\par public:
\par   void draw() \{ cout << "Square::draw\\n"; \}
\par   ~Square() \{ cout << "~Square\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par typedef std::vector<Shape*> Container;
\par typedef Container::iterator Iter;
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Container shapes;
\par   shapes.push_back(new Circle);
\par   shapes.push_back(new Square);
\par   shapes.push_back(new Triangle);
\par   for(Iter i = shapes.begin();
\par       i != shapes.end(); i++)
\par     (*i)->draw();
\par   purge(shapes);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When using }{\b purge(\~)}{, you must be careful to consider ownership issues \endash 
 if an object pointer is held in more than one container, then you must be sure not to delete it twice, and you don\rquote t want to destroy the object in the first contain
er before the second one is finished with it. Purging the same container twice is not a problem, because }{\b purge(\~)}{ sets the pointer to zero once it deletes that pointer, and calling }{\b delete}{ for a zero pointer is a safe operation.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776422}Creating your own containers{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776422}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {With the STL as a foundation, it\rquote 
s possible to create your own containers. Assuming you follow the same model of providing iterators, your new container will behave as if it were a built-in STL container.
\par Consider the \ldblquote ring\rdblquote  data structure, which is a circular sequence container. If you reach the end, it just wraps around to the beginning. This can be implemented on top of a }{\b list}{ as follows:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:Ring.cpp
\par // Making a "ring" data structure from the STL
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <list>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par template<class T>
\par class Ring \{
\par   list<T> lst;
\par public:
\par   // Declaration necessary so the following 
\par   // 'friend' statement sees this 'iterator' 
\par   // instead of std::iterator:
\par   class iterator;
\par   friend class iterator;
\par   class iterator : public std::iterator<
\par     std::bidirectional_iterator_tag,T,ptrdiff_t>\{
\par     list<T>::iterator it;
\par     list<T>* r;
\par   public:
\par     // "typename" necessary to resolve nesting:
\par     iterator(list<T>& lst,
\par       const typename list<T>::iterator& i)
\par       : r(&lst), it(i) \{\}
\par     bool operator==(const iterator& x) const \{
\par       return it == x.it;
\par     \}
\par     bool operator!=(const iterator& x) const \{
\par       return !(*this == x);
\par     \}
\par     list<T>::reference operator*() const \{
\par       return *it;
\par     \}
\par     iterator& operator++() \{
\par       ++it;
\par       if(it == r->end())
\par         it = r->begin();
\par       return *this;
\par     \}
\par     iterator operator++(int) \{
\par       iterator tmp = *this;
\par       ++*this;
\par       return tmp;
\par     \}
\par     iterator& operator--() \{
\par       if(it == r->begin())
\par         it = r->end();
\par       --it;
\par       return *this;
\par     \}
\par     iterator operator--(int) \{
\par       iterator tmp = *this;
\par       --*this; 
\par       return tmp;
\par     \}
\par     iterator insert(const T& x)\{
\par       return iterator(*r, r->insert(it, x));
\par     \}
\par     iterator erase() \{
\par       return iterator(*r, r->erase(it));
\par     \}
\par   \};
\par   void push_back(const T& x) \{
\par     lst.push_back(x);
\par   \}
\par   iterator begin() \{
\par     return iterator(lst, lst.begin());
\par   \}
\par  int size() \{ return lst.size(); \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Ring<string> rs;
\par   rs.push_back("one");
\par   rs.push_back("two");
\par   rs.push_back("three");
\par   rs.push_back("four");
\par   rs.push_back("five");
\par   Ring<string>::iterator it = rs.begin();
\par   it++; it++;
\par   it.insert("six");
\par   it = rs.begin();
\par   // Twice around the ring:
\par   for(int i = 0; i < rs.size() * 2; i++)
\par     cout << *it++ << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can see that the iterator is where most of the coding is done. The }{\b Ring}{ }{\b iterator}{ must know how to loop back to the beginning, so it must keep a reference to the }{\b list }{of}{\b  }
{its \ldblquote parent\rdblquote  }{\b Ring}{ object in order to know if it\rquote s at the end and how to get back to the beginning.
\par You\rquote ll notice that the interface for }{\b Ring}{ is quite limited; in particular there is no }{\b end(\~)}{, since a ring just keeps looping. This means that you won\rquote t be able to use a }{\b Ring}{ 
in any STL algorithms that require a past-the-end iterator \endash  which is many of them. (It turns out that adding this feature is a non-trivial exercise). Although this can seem limiting, consider }{\b stack}{, }{\b queue}{ and }{\b priority_queue}{
, which don\rquote t produce any iterators at all!
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776423}Freely-available \line STL extensions{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776423}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Although the STL containers may provide all the functionality you\rquote ll ever need, they are not complete. For example, the standard implementations of }{\b set}{ and }{\b map}{
 use trees, and although these are reasonably fast they may not be fast enough for your needs. In the C++ Standards Committee it was generally agreed that hashed implementations of }{\b set}{ and }{\b map}{
 should have been included in Standard C++, however there was not considered to be enough time to add these components, and thus they were left out.
\par Fortunately, there are freely-available alternatives. One of the nice things about the STL is that it establishes a basic model for creating STL-like classes, so anything built using the same model is easy to understand if 
you are already familiar with the STL.
\par The SGI STL (freely available at http://www.sgi.com/Technology/STL/) is one of the most robust implementations of the STL, and can be used to replace your compiler\rquote s STL if that is found wanting. In addition they\rquote ve a
dded a number of extensions including }{\b hash_set}{, }{\b hash_multiset}{, }{\b hash_map}{, }{\b hash_multimap}{, }{\b slist}{ (a singly-linked list) and }{\b rope}{ (a variant of }{\b string}{
 optimized for very large strings and fast concatenation and substring operations).
\par Let\rquote s consider a performance comparison between a tree-based }{\b map}{ and the SGI }{\b hash_map}{. To keep things simple, the mappings will be from }{\b int}{ to }{\b int}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C20:MapVsHashMap.cpp
\par // The hash_map header is not part of the 
\par // Standard C++ STL. It is an extension that 
\par // is only available as part of the SGI STL:
\par #include <hash_map> 
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <map>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main()\{
\par   hash_map<int, int> hm;
\par   map<int, int> m;
\par   clock_t ticks = clock();
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     for(int j = 0; j < 1000; j++)
\par       m.insert(make_pair(j,j));
\par   cout << "map insertions: " 
\par     << clock() - ticks << endl;
\par   ticks = clock();
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     for(int j = 0; j < 1000; j++)
\par       hm.insert(make_pair(j,j));
\par   cout << "hash_map insertions: " 
\par     << clock() - ticks << endl;
\par   ticks = clock();
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     for(int j = 0; j < 1000; j++)
\par       m[j];
\par   cout << "map::operator[] lookups: " 
\par     << clock() - ticks << endl;
\par   ticks = clock();
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     for(int j = 0; j < 1000; j++)
\par       hm[j];
\par   cout << "hash_map::operator[] lookups: "
\par     << clock() - ticks << endl;
\par   ticks = clock();
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     for(int j = 0; j < 1000; j++)
\par       m.find(j);
\par   cout << "map::find() lookups: "
\par     << clock() - ticks << endl;
\par   ticks = clock();
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
\par     for(int j = 0; j < 1000; j++)
\par       hm.find(j);
\par   cout << "hash_map::find() lookups: " 
\par     << clock() - ticks << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The performance test I ran showed a speed improvement of roughly 4:1 for the }{\b hash_map}{ over the }{\b map}{ in all operations (and as expected, }{\b find(\~)}{ is slightly faster than }{\b 
operator[ ]}{ for lookups for both types of map). If a profiler shows a bottleneck in your }{\b map}{, you should consider a }{\b hash_map}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776424}Summary{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776424}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
The goal of this chapter was not just to introduce the STL containers in some considerable depth (of course, not every detail could be covered here, but you should have enough now that you can look up further information in the other resources). My higher
 hope is that this chapter has made you grasp the incredible power available in the STL, and shown you how much faster and more efficient your programming activities can be by using and understanding the STL.
\par The fact that I could not escape from introducing some of the STL algorithms in this chapter suggests how useful they can be. In the next chapter you\rquote ll get a much more focused look at the algorithms.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776425}Exercises{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776425}
