erase(\~)}{ and a destructor. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 22. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls114\adjustright {(More challenging) Create a little command language. Each command can simply print its name and its arguments, but you may also want to make it perform other activities
 like run programs. The commands will be read from a file that you pass as an command-line argument, or from standard input if no file is given. Each command is on a single line, and lines beginning with \lquote }{\b #}{\rquote 
 are comments. A line begins with the one-word command itself, followed by any number of arguments. Commands and arguments are separated by spaces. Use a }{\b map}{ that maps }{\b string}{
 objects (the name of the command) to object pointers. The object pointers point to objects of a base class }{\b Command}{ that has a virtual }{\b execute(string args)}{ function, where }{\b args}{ contains all the arguments for that command (}{\b 
execute(\~)}{ will parse its own arguments from }{\b args}{). Each different type of command is represented by a class that is inherited from }{\b Command}{.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 23. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls114\adjustright {Add features to the above exercise so that you can have labels, }{\b if}{-}{\b then}{ statements, and the ability to jump program execution to a label.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {\sect }\sectd \margtsxn2707\margbsxn2707\sbkodd\linex0\headery0\footery1440\colsx432\endnhere\titlepg\sectdefaultcl 
\pard\plain \s1\fi-1080\li990\sa360\sl240\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \cbpat8 \f4\fs96\cf1\kerning18\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776426}21: STL Algorithms{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776426}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart STLAlgorithmsChapter}{\*\bkmkend STLAlgorithmsChapter}
The other half of the STL is the algorithms, which are templatized functions designed to work with the containers (or, as you will see, anything that can behave like a container, including arrays}{\b  }{and }{\b string}{ objects).
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The STL was originally designed around the algorithms. The goal was that you use algorithms for almost every piece of code that you write. In this sense it was a
 bit of an experiment, and only time will tell how well it works. The real test will be in how easy or difficult it is for the average programmer to adapt. At the end of this chapter you\rquote 
ll be able to decide for yourself whether you find the algorithms addictive or too confusing to remember. If you\rquote re like me, you\rquote ll resist them at first but then tend to use them more and more.
\par Before you make your judgement, however, there\rquote s one other thing to consider. The STL algorithms provide a }{\i vocabulary}{ with which to describe solutions. That is, once you become familiar with the algorithms you\rquote 
ll have a new set of words with which to discuss what you\rquote re doing, and these words are at a higher level than what you\rquote ve had before. You don\rquote t have to say \ldblquote this loop moves through and assigns from here to there \'85
 oh, I see, it\rquote s copying!\rdblquote  Instead, you say }{\b copy(\~)}{. This is the kind of thing we\rquote ve been doing in computer programming from the beginning \endash  creating more dense ways to express }{\i what}{ we\rquote 
re doing and spending less time saying }{\i how}{ we\rquote re doing it. Whether the STL algorithms and }{\i generic programming }{are a great success in accomplishing this remains to be seen, but that is certainly the objective.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776427}Function objects{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776427}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A concept that is used heavily in the STL algorithms is the }{\i function object}{, which was introduced in the previous chapter. A function object has an overloaded }{\b operator(\~)}{
, and the result is that a template function can\rquote t tell whether you\rquote ve handed it a pointer to a function or an object that has an }{\b operator(\~)}{; all the template function knows is that it can attach an argument list to the object }{\i 
as if}{ it were a pointer to a function:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C21:FuncObject.cpp
\par // Simple function objects
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par template<class UnaryFunc, class T>
\par void callFunc(T& x, UnaryFunc f) \{
\par   f(x);
\par \}
\par 
\par void g(int& x) \{
\par   x = 47;
\par \}
\par 
\par struct UFunc \{
\par   void operator()(int& x) \{
\par     x = 48;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int y = 0;
\par   callFunc(y, g);
\par   cout << y << endl;
\par   y = 0;
\par   callFunc(y, UFunc());
\par   cout << y << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The template }{\b callFunc(\~)}{ says \ldblquote give me an }{\b f}{ and an }{\b x}{, and I\rquote ll write the code }{\b f(x)}{.\rdblquote  In }{\b main(\~)}{, you can see that it doesn\rquote 
t matter if }{\b f}{ is a pointer to a function (as in the case of }{\b g(\~)}{), or if it\rquote s a function object (which is created as a temporary object by the expression }{\b UFunc(\~)}{
). Notice you can only accomplish this genericity with a template function; a non-template function is too particular about its argument types to allow such a thing. The STL algorithms use this flexibility to take either a funct
ion pointer or a function object, but you\rquote ll usually find that creating a function object is more powerful and flexible.
\par The function object is actually a variation on the theme of a }{\i callback}{, which is described in the design patterns chapter. A callback a
llows you to vary the behavior of a function or object by passing, as an argument, a way to execute some other piece of code. Here, we are handing }{\b callFunc(\~)}{ a pointer to a function or a function object.
\par The following descriptions of function objects should not only make that topic clear, but also give you an introduction to the way the STL algorithms work.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776428}Classification of function objects{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776428}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Just as the STL classifies iterators (based on their capabilities), it also classifies function objects based on the number of arguments that their }{\b operator(\~)}{
 takes and the kind of value returned by that operator (of course, this is also true for function pointers when you treat them as function objects). The classification of function objects in the STL is based on whether the }{\b operator(\~)}{
 takes zero, one or two arguments, and if it returns a }{\b bool}{ or non-}{\b bool}{ value.
\par }{\b Generator}{: Takes no arguments, and returns a value of the desired type. A }{\b RandomNumberGenerator}{ is a special case.
\par }{\b UnaryFunction}{: Takes a single argument of any type and returns a value which may be of a different type.
\par }{\b BinaryFunction}{: Takes two arguments of any two types and returns a value of any type.
\par A special case of the unary and binary functions is the }{\i predicate}{, which simply means a function that returns a }{\b bool}{. A predicate is a function you use to make a}{\b  true}{/}{\b false}{ decision.
\par }{\b Predicate}{: This can also be called a }{\b UnaryPredicate}{. It takes a single argument of any type and returns a }{\b bool}{.
\par }{\b BinaryPredicate}{: Takes two arguments of any two types and returns a }{\b bool}{.
\par }{\b StrictWeakOrdering}{: A binary predicate that says that if you have two objects and neither one is less than the other, they can be regarded as equivalent to each other.
\par In addition, there are sometimes qualifications on object types that are passed to an algorithm. These qualifications are given in the template argument type identifier name:
\par }{\b LessThanComparable}{: A class that has a less-than }{\b operator<}{.
\par }{\b Assignable}{: A class that has an assignment }{\b operator=}{ for its own type.
\par }{\b EqualityComparable}{: A class that has an equivalence }{\b operator==}{ for its own type.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776429}Automatic creation of function objects{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776429}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The STL has, in the header file }{\b <functional>}{, a set of templates that will automatically create function objects for you. These generated function objects are admitted
ly simple, but the goal is to provide very basic functionality that will allow you to compose more complicated function objects, and in many situations this is all you\rquote ll need. Also, you\rquote ll see that there are some }{\i 
function object adapters}{ that allow you to take the simple function objects and make them slightly more complicated.
\par Here are the templates that generate function objects, along with the expressions that they effect.
\par }\trowd \trgaph108\trrh361\trleft-108\trhdr\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw10 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw10 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrb
\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10 \cltxlrtb \cellx1260\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10 \cltxlrtb \cellx2790\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrb
