\par }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {multiple inheritance}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\: multiple inheritance (MI)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {MI
\: multiple inheritance}}}{You create a new type by inheriting from more than one base class. The syntax is exactly what you\rquote d expect, and as long as the inheritance diagrams are simple, MI is simple as well.
\par However, MI can introduce a number of ambiguities and strange situations, which are covered in this chapter. But first, it helps to get a perspective on the subject.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593286}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628758}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374097}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776449}Perspective
{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593286}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628758}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374097}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776449}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Before C++, the most successful object-oriented language was Smalltalk}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Smalltalk}}}{
. Smalltalk was created from the ground up as an OO language. It is often referred to as }{\i pure,}{ whereas C++, because it was built on top of C, is called }{\i hybrid}{
. One of the design decisions made with Smalltalk was that all classes would be derived in a single hierarchy, rooted in a single base class (called }{\b Object}{ \endash  this is the model for the }{\i object-based hierarchy}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: object-based hierarchy}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {hierarchy\: object-based hierarchy}}}{
). You cannot create a new class in Smalltalk without inheriting it from an existing class, which is why it takes a certain amount of time to become productive in Smalltalk \endash  you must le
arn the class library before you can start making new classes. So the Smalltalk class hierarchy is always a single monolithic }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {monolithic}}}{tree.
\par Classes in Smalltalk usually have a number of things in common, and always have }{\i some}{ things in common (the characteristics and behaviors of }{\b Object}{
), so you almost never run into a situation where you need to inherit from more than one base class. However, with C++ you can create as many hierarchy trees as you want. Therefore, for logical completeness the langua
ge must be able to combine more than one class at a time \endash  thus the need for multiple inheritance.
\par However, this was not a crystal-clear case of a feature that no one could live without, and there was (and still is) a lot of disagreement about whether MI is really essential in C++. MI was added in AT&T }{\b cfront}{
 release 2.0 and was the first significant change to the language. Since then, a number of other features have been added (notably templates) that change the way we think about programming and place MI 
in a much less important role. You can think of MI as a \ldblquote minor\rdblquote  language feature that shouldn\rquote t be involved in your daily design decisions.
\par One of the most pressing issues that drove MI involved containers. Suppose you want to create a container that everyone can easily use. One approach is to use }{\b void*}{ as the type inside the container, as with }{\b PStash}{ and }{\b Stack}{
. The Smalltalk approach, however, is to make a container that holds }{\b Object}{s. (Remember that }{\b Object}{ is the base type of the entire Smalltalk hierarchy.) Because everything in Smalltalk is ultimately derived from }{\b Object}{
, any container that holds }{\b Object}{s can hold anything, so this approach works nicely.
\par Now consider the situation in C++. Suppose vendor }{\b A}{ creates an object-based hierarchy that includes a useful set of containers including one you want to use called }{\b Holder}{. Now you come across vendor }{\b B}{\rquote 
s class hierarchy that contains some other class that is important to you, a }{\b BitImage}{ class, for example, which holds graphic images. The only way to make a }{\b Holder}{ of }{\b BitImage}{s is to inherit a new class from both }{\b Object}{
, so it can be held in the }{\b Holder}{, and }{\b BitImage}{:
