\ls211\adjustright \fs20\lang1024\cgrid {These exercises will take you step-by-step through the traps of MI. Create a base class }{\b X}{ with a single constructor that takes an }{\b int}{ argument and a member function }{\b f(\~),}{
 that takes no arguments and returns }{\b void}{. Now inherit }{\b X}{ into }{\b Y}{ and }{\b Z}{, creating constructors for each of them that takes a single }{\b int}{ argument. Now multiply inherit }{\b Y}{ and }{\b Z}{ into }{\b A}{
. Create an object of class }{\b A}{, and call }{\b f(\~)}{ for that object. Fix the problem with explicit disambiguation.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 2. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls211\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls211\adjustright {Starting with the results of exercise 1, create a pointer to an }{\b X}{ called }{\b px}{, and assign to it the address of the object of type }{\b A}{ you created before. Fix the problem using a virtual base class. Now fix }{\b X}{
 so you no longer have to call the constructor for }{\b X}{ inside }{\b A}{.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 3. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls211\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
