\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Unfortunately, it\rquote s almost accepted practice to ignore error conditions, as if we\rquote 
re in a state of denial about errors. Some of the reason is no doubt the tediousness and code bloat of checking for many errors. For example, }{\b printf(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {printf(\~)\: error code}}}{ returns the number of arguments that were successf
ully printed, but virtually no one checks this value. The proliferation of code alone would be disgusting, not to mention the difficulty it would add in reading the code.
\par The problem with C\rquote s approach to error handling could be thought of as one of coupling \endash  the user of a function must tie the error-handling code so closely to that function that it becomes too ungainly and awkward to use.
\par One of the major features in C++ is }{\i exception handling}{, which is a better way of thinking about and handling errors. With exception handling}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
exception handling}}}{,
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 1. \tab}}\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1080\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls280\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls280\adjustright \fs20\lang1024\cgrid {Error-handling code is not nearly so tedious to write, and it doesn't become mixed up with your "normal" code. You write the code you }{\i want}{
 to happen; later in a separate section you write the code to cope with the problems. If you make multiple calls to a function, you handle the errors from that function once, in one place.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 2. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1080\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls280\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls280\adjustright {Errors cannot be ignored. If a function needs to send an error message to the caller of that function, it \ldblquote throws\rdblquote  an object representing that error out of the function. If the caller doesn\rquote t \ldblquote catch
\rdblquote  the error and handle it, it goes to the next enclosing scope, and so on until }{\i someone}{ catches the error. 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This chapter examines C\rquote s approach to error handling (such as it is), why it did not work very well for C, and why it won\rquote 
t work at all for C++. Then you\rquote ll learn about }{\b try}{, }{\b throw}{,}{\b  }{and }{\b catch}{, the C++ keywords that support exception handling.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593296}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628768}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374111
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776463}Error handling in C{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593296}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628768}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374111}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776463}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {error\: error handling in C}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C\: error handling in C}}}{In most of the examples in this book, }{\b assert(\~)}{
 was used as it was intended: for debugging during development with code that could be disabled with }{\b #define}{ }{\b NDEBUG}{ for the shipping product. Runtime error checking uses the }{\b require.h}{ functions developed in Chapter XX. These wer
e a convenient way to say, \ldblquote There\rquote s a problem here you\rquote ll probably want to handle with some more sophisticated code, but you don\rquote t need to be distracted by it in this example.\rdblquote  The }{\b require.h}{
 functions may be enough for small programs, but for complicated products you may need to write more sophisticated error-handling code.
\par Error handling is quite straightforward in situations where you check some condition and you know exactly what to do because you have all the necessary information in that context. Of co
urse, you just handle the error at that point. These are ordinary errors and not the subject of this chapter. 
\par The problem occurs when you }{\i don\rquote t}{ have enough information in that context, and you need to pass the error information into a larger context where that information does exist. There are three typical approaches in C to handle this situation.

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 1. \tab}}\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls281\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls281\adjustright \fs20\lang1024\cgrid {Return error information from the function or, if the return value cannot be used this way, set a global error condition flag. (Standard C provides }{\b errno}{ }{\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls281\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls281\adjustright \v\fs20\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {errno}}}{and }{\b perror(\~)}{\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls281\pnrnot0\pndec }
\ls281\adjustright \v\fs20\lang1024\cgrid {\xe {perror(\~)}}}{
 to support this.) As mentioned before, the programmer may simply ignore the error information because tedious and obfuscating error checking must occur with each function call. In addition, returning from a function that
 hits an exceptional condition may not make sense.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 2. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {Use the little-known Standard C library signal-handling system, implemented with the }{\b signal(\~)}{\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls7\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {signal(\~)}}}{ function (to determine what happens when the event occurs) and }{\b raise(\~)}{\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls7\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {raise(\~)}}}{ (to gen
erate an event). Again, this has high coupling because it requires the user of any library that generates signals to understand and install the appropriate signal-handling mechanism; also in large projects the signal numbers from different libraries may c
lash with each other.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 3. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Use the nonlocal goto}{\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls7\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {non-local goto\: setjmp(\~
) and longjmp(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls7\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {goto\: non-local goto, setjmp(\~) and longjmp(\~)}}}{
 functions in the Standard C library: }{\b setjmp(\~)}{\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls7\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {setjmp(\~)}}}{ and }{\b 
longjmp(\~)}{\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls7\adjustright \v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {longjmp(\~)}}}{. With }{\b setjmp(\~)}{ yo
u save a known good state in the program, and if you get into trouble, }{\b longjmp(\~)}{ will restore that state. Again, there is high coupling between the place where the state is stored and the place where the error occurs.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When considering error-handling schemes with C++, there\rquote 
s an additional very critical problem: The C techniques of signals and setjmp/longjmp do not call destructors, so objects aren\rquote t properly cleaned up. This makes it virtually impossible to effectively recover from an exceptional condition
 because you\rquote ll always leave objects behind that haven\rquote t been cleaned up and that can no longer be accessed. The following example demonstrates this with setjmp/longjmp:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C23:Nonlocal.cpp
\par // setjmp() & longjmp()
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <csetjmp>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Rainbow \{
\par public:
\par   Rainbow() \{ cout << "Rainbow()" << endl; \}
\par   ~Rainbow() \{ cout << "~Rainbow()" << endl; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par jmp_buf kansas;
\par 
\par void oz() \{
\par   Rainbow rb;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
\par     cout << "there's no place like home\\n";
\par   longjmp(kansas, 47);
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   if(setjmp(kansas) == 0) \{
\par     cout << "tornado, witch, munchkins...\\n";
\par     oz();
\par   \} else \{
\par     cout << "Auntie Em! "
\par          << "I had the strangest dream..."
\par          << endl;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b setjmp(\~)}{ is an odd function because if you call it directly, it stores all the relevant information about the current processor state in the }{\b 
jmp_buf}{ and returns zero. In that case it has the behavior of an ordinary function. However, if you call }{\b longjmp(\~)}{ using the same }{\b jmp_buf}{, it\rquote s as if you\rquote re returning from }{\b setjmp(\~)}{ again \endash 
 you pop right out the back end of the }{\b setjmp(\~)}{. This time, the value returned is the second argument to }{\b longjmp(\~)}{, so you can detect that you\rquote re actually coming back from a }{\b longjmp(\~)}{. You can imagin
e that with many different }{\b jmp_buf}{s, you could pop around to many different places in the program. The difference between a local }{\b goto}{ (with a label) and this nonlocal goto is that you can go }{\i anywhere}{
 with setjmp/longjmp (with some restrictions not discussed here).
\par The problem with C++ is that }{\b longjmp(\~)}{ doesn\rquote t respect objects; in particular it doesn\rquote t call destructors when it jumps out of a scope.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ You may be surprised when you run the example \endash  some C++ compilers have extended }{\b longjmp(\~)}{
 to clean up objects on the stack. This is nonportable behavior.}}}{ Destructor calls are essential, so this approach won\rquote t work with C++.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593297}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628769}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374112
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776464}Throwing an exception{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593297}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628769}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374112}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776464}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {throwing an exception}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: throwing an exception}}}{If you encounter an exceptional situation in your code \endash  that is, one where you don\rquote 
t have enough information in the current context to decide what to do \endash  you can send information about the error into a larger context by creating an object containing that information and \ldblquote throwing\rdblquote 
 it out of your current context. This is called }{\i throwing an exception}{. Here\rquote s what it looks like:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {throw myerror(\ldblquote something bad happened\rdblquote );
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b myerror}{ is an ordinary class, which takes a }{\b char*}{ as its argument. You can use any type when you throw (including built-in types), but often you
\rquote ll use special types created just for throwing exceptions.
\par The keyword }{\b throw}{ causes a number of relatively magical things to happen. First it creates an object that isn\rquote t there under normal program execution, and of course the constructor is called for that object. Then the object is, in effect, 
\ldblquote returned\rdblquote  from the function, even though that object type isn\rquote t normally what the function is desig
ned to return. A simplistic way to think about exception handling is as an alternate return mechanism, although you get into trouble if you take the analogy too far \endash 
 you can also exit from ordinary scopes by throwing an exception. But a value is returned, and the function or scope exits.
\par Any similarity to function returns ends there because }{\i where}{ you return to is someplace completely different than for a normal function call. (You end up in an appropriate exception handler that may be miles away from where
 the exception was thrown.) In addition, only objects that were successfully created at the time of the exception are destroyed (unlike a normal function return that assumes all the objects in the scope must be destroyed). Of course, the exception object 
itself is also properly cleaned up at the appropriate point.
\par In addition, you can throw as many different types of objects as you want. Typically, you\rquote ll throw a different type for each different type of error. The idea is to store the information in the object and the }{\i type}{
 of object, so someone in the bigger context can figure out what to do with your exception.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593298}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628770}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374113
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776465}Catching an exception{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593298}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628770}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374113}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776465}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If a function throws an exception, it must assume that exception is caught and dealt with. As mentioned before, one of the ad
vantages of C++ exception handling is that it allows you to concentrate on the problem you\rquote re actually trying to solve in one place, and then deal with the errors from that code in another place.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374114}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776466}The }{\b try}{ block
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374114}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776466}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you\rquote re inside a function and you throw an e
xception (or a called function throws an exception), that function will exit in the process of throwing. If you don\rquote t want a }{\b throw }{
to leave a function, you can set up a special block within the function where you try to solve your actual programming problem (and potentially generate exceptions). This is called the }{\i try}{ }{\i block}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {try block}}}{ because you try your various function calls there. The try block is an ordinary scope, preceded by the keyword }{\b try}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {try \{
\par   // Code that may generate exceptions
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you were carefully checking for errors without using exception handling, you\rquote 
d have to surround every function call with setup and test code, even if you call the same function several times. With exception handling, you put everything in a try block without 
error checking. This means your code is a lot easier to write and easier to read because the goal of the code is not confused with the error checking.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374115}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776467}Exception handlers
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374115}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776467}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Of course, the thrown exception must end up someplace. This is the }{\i exception handler}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: exception handler}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {handler, exception}}}{, and there\rquote 
s one for every exception type you want to catch. Exception handlers immediately follow the try block and are denoted by the keyword }{\b catch}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
keyword\: catch}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {catch}}}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {try \{
\par // code that may generate exceptions
\par \} catch(type1 id1) \{
\par   // handle exceptions of type1
\par \} catch(type2 id2) \{
\par   // handle exceptions of type2
\par \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {// etc...
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Each catch clause (exception handler) is like a little function that takes a single argument of one particular type. The identifier (}{\b id1}{, }{\b id2}{
, and so on) may be used inside the handler, just like a function argument, although sometimes there is no identifier because it\rquote s not needed in the handler \endash  the exception type gives you enough information to deal with it.
\par The handlers must appear directly after the try block. If an exception is thrown, the exception-handling mechanism goes hunting for the first handler with an argument that matches the type of the exception. Then it enters that catch clause, and 
the exception is considered handled. (The search for handlers stops once the catch clause is finished.) Only the matching catch clause executes; it\rquote s not like a }{\b switch}{ statement where you need a }{\b break}{ after each }{\b case}{
 to prevent the remaining ones from executing.
\par Notice that, within the try block, a number of different function calls might generate the same exception, but you only need one handler.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Termination vs. resumption
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: termination vs. resumption}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {termination\: vs. resumption, exception handling}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {resumption\: termination vs. resumption, exception handling}}}{There are two basic models in exception-handling theory. In }{\i termination}{ (which is what C++ supports) you assume the error is so critical there\rquote s no way to get back to w
here the exception occurred. Whoever threw the exception decided there was no way to salvage the situation, and they don\rquote t }{\i want}{ to come back.
\par The alternative is called }{\i resumption}{. It means the exception handler is expected to do something to rectify the si
tuation, and then the faulting function is retried, presuming success the second time. If you want resumption, you still hope to continue execution after the exception is handled, so your exception is more like a function call \endash 
 which is how you should set up situations in C++ where you want resumption-like behavior (that is, don\rquote t throw an exception; call a function that fixes the problem). Alternatively, place your }{\b try}{ block inside a }{\b while}{
 loop that keeps reentering the }{\b try}{ block until the result is satisfactory.
\par Historically, programmers using operating systems that supported resumptive exception handling eventually ended up using termination-like code and skipping resumption. So although resumption sounds attractive at first, it seems it isn\rquote t quite so u
seful in practice. One reason may be the distance that can occur between the exception and its handler; it\rquote s one thing to terminate to a handler that\rquote 
s far away, but to jump to that handler and then back again may be too conceptually difficult for large systems where the exception can be generated from many points.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374116}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776468}The exception specification
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374116}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776468}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: specification}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {specification\: exception}}}{You\rquote re not required to inform the person using your function what exceptions you might throw. Ho
wever, this is considered very uncivilized because it means he cannot be sure what code to write to catch all potential exceptions. Of course, if he has your source code, he can hunt through and look for }{\b throw}{
 statements, but very often a library doesn\rquote t come with sources. C++ provides a syntax to allow you to politely tell the user what exceptions this function throws, so the user may handle them. This is the }{\i exception specification}{ and it
\rquote s part of the function declaration, appearing after the argument list.
\par The exception specification reuses the keyword }{\b throw}{, followed by a parenthesized list of all the potential exception types. So your function declaration may look like
