\par Although the }{\b new_handler(\~)}{ function pointer can be null and the system will do something sensible, the }{\b unexpected(\~)}{ function pointer should never be null. The default value is }{\b terminate(\~)}{
 (mentioned later), but whenever you use exceptions and specifications you should write your own }{\b unexpected(\~)}{ to log the error and either rethrow it, throw something new, or terminate the program.
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{, the }{\b try}{ block is within a }{\b for}{ loop so all the possibilities are exercised. Note that this is a way to achieve something like resumption }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {resumption}}}{\endash  nest the }{\b try}{ block inside a }{\b for}{, }{\b while}{, }{\b do}{, or }{\b if}{ and cause any exceptions to attempt to repair the problem; then attempt the }{\b try}{ block again.
\par Only the }{\b Up}{ and }{\b Fit}{ exceptions are caught because those are the only ones the programmer of }{\b f(\~)}{ said would be thrown. Version two of }{\b g(\~)}{ causes }{\b my_unexpected(\~)}{ to be called because }{\b f(\~)}{ then throws an }{\b 
int}{. (You can throw any type, including a built-in type.)
