\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {terminate(\~)\: uncaught exceptions}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {uncaught exceptions}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\:
 uncaught exceptions}}}{If none of the exception handlers following a particular }{\b try}{ block matches an exception, that exception moves to the next-higher context, that is, the function or }{\b try}{ block surrounding the }{\b try}{
 block that failed to catch the exception. (The location of this higher-context }{\b try}{ block is not always obvious at
 first glance.) This process continues until, at some level, a handler matches the exception. At that point, the exception is considered \ldblquote caught,\rdblquote  and no further searching occurs.
\par If no handler at any level catches the exception, it is \ldblquote uncaught\rdblquote  or \ldblquote unhandled.\rdblquote  An uncaught exception also occurs if a new exception is thrown before an existing exception reaches its handler \endash 
 the most common reason for this is that the constructor for the exception object itself causes a new exception.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\b terminate(\~)
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If an exception is uncaught, the special function }{\b terminate(\~)}{ is automatically called. Like }{\b unexpected(\~)}{
, terminate is actually a pointer to a function. Its default value is the Standard C library function }{\b abort(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C\:
 Standard C library function abort(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {abort(\~)\: Standard C library function}}}{
, which immediately exits the program with no calls to the normal termination functions (which means that destructors for global and static objects might not be called).
\par No cleanups occur for an uncaught exception; that is, no destructors are called. If you don\rquote t wrap your code (including, if necessary, all the code in }{\b main(\~)}{)}{\b  }{in a try block followed by handlers and ending with a default handler (}{
\b catch(...)}{) to catch all exceptions, then you will take your lumps. An uncaught exception should be thought of as a programming error.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\b set_terminate(\~)
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can install your own }{\b terminate(\~)}{ function using the standard }{\b set_terminate(\~)}{ }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: set_terminate(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {set_terminate(\~)
}}}{function, which returns a pointer to the }{\b terminate(\~)}{ function you are replacing, so you can restore it later if you want. Your custom }{\b terminate(\~)}{ must take no arguments and have a }{\b void}{ return value. In addition, any }{\b 
terminate(\~)}{ handler you install must not return or throw an exception, but instead must call some sort of program-termination function. If }{\b terminate(\~)}{ is called, it means the problem is unrecoverable.
\par Like }{\b unexpected(\~)}{, the }{\b terminate(\~)}{ function pointer should never be null.
\par Here\rquote s an example showing the use of }{\b set_terminate(\~)}{. Here, the return value is saved and restored so the }{\b terminate(\~)}{ function can be used to help isolate the section of code where the uncaught exception is occurring:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C23:Trmnator.cpp
\par // Use of set_terminate()
\par // Also shows uncaught exceptions
\par #include <exception>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par void terminator() \{
\par   cout << "I'll be back!" << endl;
\par   abort();
\par \}
\par 
\par void (*old_terminate)()
\par   = set_terminate(terminator);
\par 
\par class Botch \{
\par public:
\par   class Fruit \{\};
\par   void f() \{
\par     cout << "Botch::f()" << endl;
\par     throw Fruit();
\par   \}
\par   ~Botch() \{ throw 'c'; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   try\{
\par     Botch b;
\par     b.f();
\par   \} catch(...) \{
\par     cout << "inside catch(...)" << endl;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The definition of }{\b old_terminate}{ looks a bit confusing at first: It not only creates a pointer to a function}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {pointer\: pointer to a function}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\:
 pointer to a function}}}{, but it initializes that pointer to the return value of }{\b set_terminate(\~)}{. Even though you may be familiar with seeing a semicolon right after a pointer-to-function definition, it\rquote 
s just another kind of variable and may be initialized when it is defined.
\par The class }{\b Botch}{ not only throws an exception inside }{\b f(\~)}{, but also in its destructor. This is one of the situations that causes a call to }{\b terminate(\~)}{, as you can see in }{\b main(\~)}{. Even though the exception handler says }{\b 
catch(...)}{, which would seem to catch everything and leave no cause for }{\b terminate(\~)}{ to be called, }{\b terminate(\~) }{is called anyway, because in the process of cleaning up the objects on the stack to handle one exception, the }{\b Botch}{
 destructor is called, and that generates a second exception, forcing a call to }{\b terminate(\~)}{. Thus, a destructor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\:
 destructors}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {destructor\: and exception handling}}}{ that throws an exception or causes one to be thrown is a design error.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593299}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628771}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374121}
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776473}Function-level try blocks{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776473}
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C23:FunctionTryBlock.cpp
\par // Function-level try blocks
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main() try \{
\par     throw "main";
\par \} catch(const char* msg) \{
\par   cout << msg << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
