\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\:
 cleaning up the stack during a throw}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cleaning up the stack during exception handling}}}{
Part of the magic of exception handling is that you can pop from normal program flow into the appropriate exception handler. This wouldn\rquote t be very useful, however, if things weren\rquote 
t cleaned up properly as the exception was thrown. C++ exception handling guarantees that as you leave a scope, all objects in that scope }{\i whose constructors have been completed}{ will have destructors called.
\par Here\rquote s an example that demonstrates that constructors}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: and exception handling}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: constructors}}}{ that aren\rquote t completed don\rquote t have the associated destructors called. It also s
hows what happens when an exception is thrown in the middle of the creation of an array of objects, and an }{\b unexpected(\~)}{ function that rethrows the unexpected exception:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C23:Cleanup.cpp
\par // Exceptions clean up objects
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <exception>
\par #include <cstring>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("cleanup.out");
\par 
\par class Noisy \{
\par   static int i;
\par   int objnum;
\par   enum \{ sz = 40 \};
\par   char name[sz];
\par public:
\par   Noisy(const char* nm="array elem") throw(int)\{
\par     objnum = i++;
\par     memset(name, 0, sz);
\par     strncpy(name, nm, sz - 1);
\par     out << "constructing Noisy " << objnum
\par       << " name [" << name << "]" << endl;
\par     if(objnum == 5) throw int(5);
\par     // Not in exception specification:
\par     if(*nm == 'z') throw char('z');
\par   \}
\par   ~Noisy() \{
\par     out << "destructing Noisy " << objnum
\par       << " name [" << name << "]" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   void* operator new[](size_t sz) \{
\par     out << "Noisy::new[]" << endl;
\par     return ::new char[sz];
\par   \}
\par   void operator delete[](void* p) \{
\par     out << "Noisy::delete[]" << endl;
\par     ::delete []p;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int Noisy::i = 0;
\par 
\par void unexpected_rethrow() \{
\par   out << "inside unexpected_rethrow()" << endl;
\par   throw; // Rethrow same exception
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   set_unexpected(unexpected_rethrow);
\par   try \{
\par     Noisy n1("before array");
\par     // Throws exception:
\par     Noisy* array = new Noisy[7];
\par     Noisy n2("after array");
\par   \} catch(int i) \{
\par     out << "caught " << i << endl;
\par   \}
\par   out << "testing unexpected:" << endl;
\par   try \{
\par     Noisy n3("before unexpected");
\par     Noisy n4("z");
\par     Noisy n5("after unexpected");
\par   \} catch(char c) \{
\par     out << "caught " << c << endl;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The class }{\b Noisy}{ keeps track of objects so you can trace program progress. It keeps a count of the number of objects created with a }{\b static}{
 data member }{\b i}{, and the number of the particular object with }{\b objnum}{, and a character buffer called }{\b name}{
 to hold an identifier. This buffer is first set to zeroes. Then the constructor argument is copied in. (Note that a default argument string is used to indicate array elements, so this constr
uctor also acts as a default constructor.) Because the Standard C library function }{\b strncpy(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C\: Standard C library function strncpy(\~)}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {strncpy(\~)\: Standard C library function strncpy(\~)}}}{stops copying after a null terminator }{\i or}{ the number of cha
racters specified by its third argument, the number of characters copied in is one minus the size of the buffer, so the last character is always zero, and a print statement will never run off the end of the buffer.
\par There are two cases where a }{\b throw}{ can occur in the constructor. The first case happens if this is the fifth object created (not a real exception condition, but demonstrates an exception thrown during array construction). The type thrown is }{\b int
}{, which is the type promised in the exception specification. The second case, also contrived, happens if the first character of the argument string is }{\b \lquote z\rquote }{, in which case a }{\b char}{ is thrown. Because }{\b char}{
 is not listed in the exception specification, this will cause a call to }{\b unexpected(\~)}{.
\par The array versions of }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ are overloaded }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {new\: overloading array new and delete}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {delete\: overloading array new and delete}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overloading\:
 array new and delete }}}{for the class, so you can see when they\rquote re called.
\par The function }{\b unexpected_rethrow(\~)}{ prints a message and rethrows the same exception. It is installed as the }{\b unexpected(\~)}{ function in the first line of }{\b main(\~)}{. Then some objects of type }{\b Noisy}{ are created in a }{\b try}{
 block, but the array causes an exception to be thrown, so the object }{\b n2}{ is never created. You can see the results in the output of the program:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {constructing Noisy 0 name [before array]
\par Noisy::new[]
\par constructing Noisy 1 name [array elem]
\par constructing Noisy 2 name [array elem]
\par constructing Noisy 3 name [array elem]
\par constructing Noisy 4 name [array elem]
\par constructing Noisy 5 name [array elem]
\par destructing Noisy 4 name [array elem]
\par destructing Noisy 3 name [array elem]
\par destructing Noisy 2 name [array elem]
\par destructing Noisy 1 name [array elem]
\par Noisy::delete[]
\par destructing Noisy 0 name [before array]
\par caught 5
\par testing unexpected:
\par constructing Noisy 6 name [before unexpected]
\par constructing Noisy 7 name [z]
\par inside unexpected_rethrow()
\par destructing Noisy 6 name [before unexpected]
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {caught z
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Four array elements are successfully created, but in the middle 
of the constructor for the fifth one, an exception is thrown. Because the fifth constructor never completes, only the destructors for objects 1\endash 4 are called. 
\par The storage for the array is allocated separately with a single call to the global }{\b new}{. Notice that even though }{\b delete}{ is never explicitly called anywhere in the program, the exception-handling system knows it must call }{\b delete}{
 to properly release the storage. This behavior happens only with \ldblquote normal\rdblquote  versions of }{\b operator new}{. If you use the placement syntax}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {new\: placement syntax}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: operator new placement syntax}}}{
 described in Chapter XX, the exception-handling mechanism will not call }{\b delete}{ for that object because then it might release memory that was not allocated on the heap.
\par Finally, object }{\b n1}{ is destroyed, but not object }{\b n2}{ because it was never created.
\par In the section testing }{\b unexpected_rethrow(\~)}{, the }{\b n3}{ object is created, and the constructor of }{\b n4}{ is begun. But before it can complete, an exception is thrown. This exception is of type }{\b char}{
, which violates the exception specification, so the }{\b unexpected(\~)}{ function is called (which is }{\b unexpected_rethrow(\~)}{, in this case). This rethrows the same exception, which is expected this time, because }{\b unexpected_rethrow(\~)}{
 can throw any type of exception. The search begins right after the constructor for }{\b n4}{, and the }{\b char}{ exception handler catches it (after destroying }{\b n3}{, the only successfully created object). Thus, the effect of }{\b 
unexpected_rethrow(\~)}{ is to take any unexpected exception and make it expected; used this way it provides a filter to allow you to track the appearance of unexpected exceptions}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {filtering unexpected exceptions}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: unexpected, filtering exceptions}}}{ and pass them through.

\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593300}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628772}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374122
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776475}Constructors{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593300}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628772}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374122}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776475}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: and exception handling}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: constructors}}}{When writing code with exceptions, it\rquote s particularly important that you always be asking, \ldblquote 
If an exception occurs, will this be properly cleaned up?\rdblquote  Most of the time you\rquote re fairly safe, but in constructors there\rquote 
s a problem: If an exception is thrown before a constructor is completed, the associated destructor will not be called for that object. This means you must be especially diligent while writing your constructor.
\par The general difficulty is allocating resources in constructors. If an exception occurs in the constructor, the destructor doesn\rquote t get a chance to deallocate the resource. This problem occurs most often with \ldblquote naked\rdblquote  pointers}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {naked pointers, and exception handling}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
exception handling\: naked pointers}}}{. For example,
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C23:Nudep.cpp
\par // Naked pointers
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("nudep.out");
\par 
\par class Cat \{
\par public:
\par   Cat() \{ out << "Cat()" << endl; \}
\par   ~Cat() \{ out << "~Cat()" << endl; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Dog \{
\par public:
\par   void* operator new(size_t sz) \{
\par     out << "allocating a Dog" << endl;
\par     throw int(47);
\par   \}
\par   void operator delete(void* p) \{
\par     out << "deallocating a Dog" << endl;
\par     ::delete p;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class UseResources \{
\par   Cat* bp;
\par   Dog* op;
\par public:
\par   UseResources(int count = 1) \{
\par     out << "UseResources()" << endl;
\par     bp = new Cat[count];
\par     op = new Dog;
\par   \}
\par   ~UseResources() \{
\par     out << "~UseResources()" << endl;
\par     delete []bp; // Array delete
\par     delete op;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   try \{
\par     UseResources ur(3);
\par   \} catch(int) \{
\par     out << "inside handler" << endl;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The output is the following:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {UseResources()
\par Cat()
\par Cat()
\par Cat()
\par allocating a Dog
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {inside handler
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b UseResources}{ constructor is entered, and the }{\b Cat}{ constructor is successfully completed for the array objects. However, inside }{\b 
Dog::operator new}{, an exception is thrown (as an example of an out-of-memory error). Suddenly, you end up inside the handler, }{\i without}{ the }{\b UseResources}{ destructor being called. This is correct because the }{\b UseResources}{
 constructor was unable to finish, but it means the }{\b Cat}{ object that was successfully created on the heap is never destroyed.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374123}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776476}Making everything an object
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374123}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776476}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To prevent this, guard against these \ldblquote raw\rdblquote  resource allo
cations by placing the allocations inside their own objects with their own constructors and destructors. This way, each allocation becomes atomic}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
exception handling\: atomic allocations for safety}}}{, as an object, and if it fails, the other resource allocation objects are properly cleaned up. Templates are an excellent way to modify the above example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C23:Wrapped.cpp
\par // Safe, atomic pointers
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("wrapped.out");
\par 
\par // Simplified. Yours may have other arguments.
\par template<class T, int sz = 1> class PWrap \{
\par   T* ptr;
\par public:
\par   class RangeError \{\}; // Exception class
\par   PWrap() \{
\par     ptr = new T[sz];
\par     out << "PWrap constructor" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   ~PWrap() \{
\par     delete []ptr;
\par     out << "PWrap destructor" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   T& operator[](int i) throw(RangeError) \{
\par     if(i >= 0 && i < sz) return ptr[i];
\par     throw RangeError();
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Cat \{
\par public:
\par   Cat() \{ out << "Cat()" << endl; \}
\par   ~Cat() \{ out << "~Cat()" << endl; \}
\par   void g() \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Dog \{
\par public:
\par   void* operator new[](size_t sz) \{
\par     out << "allocating an Dog" << endl;
\par     throw int(47);
\par   \}
\par   void operator delete[](void* p) \{
\par     out << "deallocating an Dog" << endl;
\par     ::delete p;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class UseResources \{
\par   PWrap<Cat, 3> Bonk;
\par   PWrap<Dog> Og;
\par public:
\par   UseResources() : Bonk(), Og() \{
\par     out << "UseResources()" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   ~UseResources() \{
\par     out << "~UseResources()" << endl;
\par   \}
\par   void f() \{ Bonk[1].g(); \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   try \{
\par     UseResources ur;
\par   \} catch(int) \{
\par     out << "inside handler" << endl;
\par   \} catch(...) \{
\par     out << "inside catch(...)" << endl;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The difference is the use of the template to wrap the pointers and make them into objects. The constructors for these objects are called }{\i before}{ the 
body of the }{\b UseResources}{ constructor, and any of these constructors that complete before an exception is thrown will have their associated destructors called.
\par The }{\b PWrap}{ template shows a more typical use of exceptions than you\rquote ve seen so far: A nested class called }{\b RangeError}{ is created to use in }{\b operator[ ]}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {operator\: []}}}{ if its argument is out of range. Because }{\b operator[ ]}{ returns a reference}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling
\: references}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {reference\: and exception handling}}}{ it cannot return zero. (There are no null references.) This is a true exceptional condition 
\endash  you don\rquote t know what to do in the current context, and you can\rquote t return an improbable value. In this example, }{\b RangeError}{ is very simple and assumes all the necessary information is in the class name
, but you may also want to add a member that contains the value of the index, if that is useful.
\par Now the output is
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {Cat()
\par Cat()
\par Cat()
\par PWrap constructor
\par allocating a Dog
\par ~Cat()
\par ~Cat()
\par ~Cat()
\par PWrap destructor
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {inside handler
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Again, the storage allocation for }{\b Dog}{ throws an exception, but this time the array of }{\b Cat}{
 objects is properly cleaned up, so there is no memory leak.
