\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Exceptions aren\rquote t the answer to all problems. In fact, if you simply go looking for something to pound with your new hammer, you\rquote 
ll cause trouble. The following sections point out situations where exceptions are }{\i not}{ warranted.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Not for asynchronous events
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: asynchronous events}}}{
The Standard C }{\b signal(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {signal(\~)}}}{\b  }{
system, and any similar system, handles asynchronous events: events that happen outside the scope of the program, and thus events the program cannot anticipate. C++ exceptions cannot be used to handle asyn
chronous events because the exception and its handler are on the same call stack. That is, exceptions rely on scoping, whereas asynchronous events must be handled by completely separate code that is not part of the normal program flow (typically, interrup
t service routines or event loops).
\par This is not to say that asynchronous events cannot be }{\i associated}{ with exceptions. But the interrupt handler should do its job as quickly as possible and then return. Later, at some well-defined point in the program, an exception might be thrown }{
\i based on}{ the interrupt.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Not for ordinary error conditions
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you have enough information to handle an error, it\rquote 
s not an exception. You should take care of it in the current context rather than throwing an exception to a larger context.
\par Also, C++ exceptions are not thrown for machine-level events like divide-by-zero. It\rquote 
s assumed these are dealt with by some other mechanism, like the operating system or hardware. That way, C++ exceptions can be reasonably efficient, and their use is isolated to program-level exceptional conditions.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Not for flow-of-control
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {An exception looks somewhat like an alternate return mechanism and somewhat like a }{\b switch}{
 statement, so you can be tempted to use them for other than their original intent. This is a
 bad idea, partly because the exception-handling system is significantly less efficient than normal program execution; exceptions are a rare event, so the normal program shouldn\rquote 
t pay for them. Also, exceptions from anything other than error conditions are quite confusing to the user of your class or function.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {You\rquote re not forced to use exceptions
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Some programs are quite simple, many utilities, for example. You may only need to take input and perform some processing. In these programs you might attempt to all
ocate memory and fail, or try to open a file and fail, and so on. It is acceptable in these programs to use }{\b assert(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {assert(\~)}}}{
 or to print a message and }{\b abort(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {abort(\~)}}}{ the program, allowing the system to clean up the mess, rather than to wo
rk very hard to catch all exceptions and recover all the resources yourself. Basically, if you don\rquote t need to use exceptions, you don\rquote t have to.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {New exceptions, old code
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Another situation that arises is the modification of an existing program that doesn\rquote t use exceptions. You may introduce a library that }{\i does}{
 use exceptions and wonder if you need to modify all your code throughout the program. Assuming you have an acceptable error-handling scheme already in place, the most sensible thing to do here is surroun
d the largest block that uses the new library (this may be all the code in }{\b main(\~)}{)}{\b  }{with a }{\b try}{ block, followed by a }{\b catch(...)}{
 and basic error message. You can refine this to whatever degree necessary by adding more specific handlers, but, in any case, the code you\rquote re forced to add can be minimal.
\par You can also isolate your exception-generating code in a try block and write handlers to convert the exceptions into your existing error-handling scheme.
\par It\rquote s truly important to think about exceptions when you\rquote re creating a library for someone else to use, and you can\rquote t know how they need to respond to critical error conditions.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374128}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776481}Typical uses of exceptions
