\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {because, when the object is caught by value, it is }{\i turned into}{ a }{\b Base}{
 object (by the copy-constructor) and must behave that way in all situations, whereas when it\rquote s caught by reference, only the address is passed and the object isn\rquote t truncated, so it behaves like what it really is, a }{\b Derived}{
 in this case.
\par Although you can also throw and catch pointers}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: throwing & catching pointers}}}{, by doing so you introduce more coupling \endash 
 the thrower 
and the catcher must agree on how the exception object is allocated and cleaned up. This is a problem because the exception itself may have occurred from heap exhaustion. If you throw exception objects, the exception-handling system takes care of all stor
age.
