 and exception handling}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: constructors }}}{ has no return value, you\rquote 
ve previously had two choices to report an error during construction:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 13. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls114\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {Set a nonlocal flag and hope the user checks it.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 14. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Return an incompletely created object and hope the user checks it.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
This is a serious problem because C programmers have come to rely on an implied guarantee that object creation is always successful, which is not unreasonable in C where types are so primitive. But continuing execution after construction fails}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: failing}}}{ in a C++ program is a guaranteed disaster, so constructors are one of the most important places to throw exceptions \endash 
 now you have a safe, effective way to handle constructor errors. However, you must also pay attention to pointers inside objects and the way cleanup occurs when an exception is thrown inside a constructor.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Don\rquote t cause exceptions in destructors
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Because destructors }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {destructor\: an
d exception handling}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: destructors}}}{are called in the process of throwing other exceptions, you\rquote 
ll never want to throw an exception in a destructor or cause another exception to be thrown by some action you perform in the destructor. If this happens, it means that a new exception may be thrown }{\i before}{
 the catch-clause for an existing exception is reached, which will cause a call to }{\b terminate(\~)}{.
\par This means that if you call any functions inside a destructor that may throw exceptions, those calls should be within a }{\b try}{ block in the destructor, and the destructor must handle all exceptions itself. None must escape from the destructor.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Avoid naked pointers
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {See }{\b Wrapped.cpp}{. A naked pointer usually means vulnerability in the constructor if resources are allocated for that pointer. A pointer doesn\rquote 
t have a destructor, so those resources won\rquote t be released if an exception is thrown in the constructor.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593304}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628776}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374129
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776482}Overhead{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593304}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628776}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374129}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776482}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {overhead\: exception handling}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: overhead}}}{Of course it costs something for this new feature; when an exception is thrown there\rquote 
s considerable runtime overhead. This is the reason you never want to use exceptions as part of your normal flow-of-control, no matter how tempting and clever it may seem. Exceptions shou
ld occur only rarely, so the overhead is piled on the exception and not on the normally executing code. One of the important design goals for exception handling was that it could be implemented with no impact on execution speed when it }{\i wasn\rquote t}
{ used; that is, as long as you don\rquote t throw an exception, your code runs as fast as it would without exception handling. Whether or not this is actually true depends on the particular compiler implementation you\rquote re using.
\par Exception handling also causes extra information to be put on the stack by the compiler, to aid in stack unwinding.
\par Exception objects are properly passed around like any other objects, except that they can be passed into and out of what can be thought of as a special \ldblquote exception scope\rdblquote  (which may just be the global scope). That\rquote 
s how they go from one place to another. When the exception handler is finished, the exception objects are properly destroyed.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593305}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628777}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374130
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776483}Summary{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593305}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628777}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374130}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776483}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Error recovery is a fundamental concern for every program you write, and it\rquote s especially importan
t in C++, where one of the goals is to create program components for others to use. To create a robust system, each component must be robust.
\par The goals for exception handling in C++ are to simplify the creation of large, reliable programs using less code than currently possible, with more confidence that your application doesn\rquote 
t have an unhandled error. This is accomplished with little or no performance penalty, and with low impact on existing code.
\par Basic exceptions are not terribly difficult to learn, and you should begin using them in your programs as soon as you can. Exceptions are one of those features that provide immediate and significant benefits to your project.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374131}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776484}Exercises
