Run-time type identification (RTTI) lets you find the exact type of an object when you have only a pointer or reference to the base type. 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type\: run-time type identification (RTTI)}}}{This can be thought of as a \ldblquote secondary\rdblquote 
 feature in C++, a pragmatism to help out when you get into messy situations. Normally, you\rquote ll want to intentionally ignore the exact type of an object and let the virtual function mechanism implement the correct beh
avior for that type. But occasionally it\rquote 
s useful to know the exact type of an object for which you only have a base pointer. Often this information allows you to perform a special-case operation more efficiently or prevent a base-class interface from beco
ming ungainly. It happens enough that most class libraries contain virtual functions to produce run-time type information. When exception handling was added to C++, it required the exact type information about objects. It became an easy next step to build
 access to that information into the language.
