\par This is a typical class-hierarchy diagram, with the base class at the top and the derived classes growing downward. The normal goal in object-oriented programming}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {programming, object-oriented}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {object\: object-oriented programming}}}{
 is for the bulk of your code to manipulate pointers to the base type (}{\b Shape}{, in this case) so if you decide to extend the program by adding a new class (}{\b rhomboid}{, derived from }{\b Shape}{
, for example), the bulk of the code is not affected. In this example, the virtual function in the }{\b Shape}{ interface is }{\b draw(\~)}{, so the intent is for the client programmer to call }{\b draw(\~)}{ through a generic }{\b Shape}{ pointer. }{\b 
draw(\~)}{ is redefined in all the derived classes, and because it is a virtual function, the proper behavior will occur even though it is called through a generic }{\b Shape}{ pointer.
\par Thus, you generally create a specific object (}{\b Circle}{, }{\b Square}{, or }{\b Triangle}{), take its address and cast it to a }{\b Shape*}{ (forgetting the specific type of the object), and use that anonymous p
ointer in the rest of the program. Historically, diagrams are drawn as seen above, so the act of casting from a more derived type to a base type is called }{\i upcasting}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: and upcasting}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {upcasting\: and run-time type identification}}}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593309}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628781}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374134
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776487}What is RTTI?{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593309}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628781}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374134}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776487}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {But what if you have a special programming problem that\rquote s easiest to solve if you know the exact type of a generic pointer}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {pointer\: finding exact type of a base pointer}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type\:
 finding exact type of a base pointer}}}{? For exa
mple, suppose you want to allow your users to highlight all the shapes of any particular type by turning them purple. This way, they can find all the triangles on the screen by highlighting them. Your natural first approach may be to try a virtual functio
n like }{\b TurnColorIfYouAreA(\~)}{, which allows enumerated arguments of some type }{\b color}{ and of }{\b Shape::Circle}{, }{\b Shape::Square}{, or }{\b Shape::Triangle}{.
\par To solve this sort of problem, most class library designers put virtual functions in the base class to return type information about the specific object at runtime. You may have seen library member functions with names like }{\b isA(\~)}{ and }{\b typeOf(
\~)}{. These are vendor-defined}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: vendor-defined}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {vendor-defined run-time type identification}}}{ RTTI functions. Using these functions, as you go through the list you can say, \ldblquote If you\rquote re a triangle, turn purple.\rdblquote 
\par When exception handling}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: and exception handling}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: run-time type identification}}}{ was
 added to C++, the implementation required that some run-time type information be put into the virtual function tables. This meant that with a small language extension the programmer could also get the run-time type information about an object. All librar
y vendors were adding their own RTTI }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: RTTI, abbreviation for}}}{anyway, so it was included in the language.
\par RTTI, like exceptions, depends on type information residing in the virtual function table. If you try to use RTTI on a class that has no virtual functions}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {run-time type identification\: without virtual functions}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {function\: run-time type identification without virtual functions}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {virtual\: run-time type identification without virtual functions}}}{, you\rquote ll get unexpected results. 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374135}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776488}Two syntaxes for RTTI
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374135}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776488}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There are two different ways to use RTTI. The first acts like }{\b sizeof(\~)}{ because it looks like a function, but it\rquote 
s actually implemented by the compiler. }{\b typeid(\~) }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {typeid(\~)\: run-time type identification}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: typeid(\~)}}}{takes an argument that\rquote s an object, a reference, or a pointer and returns a reference to a global}{\b  const}{
 object of type }{\b typeinfo}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: typeinfo}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {typeinfo\: run-time type identification}}}{. These can be compared to each other with the }{\b operator==}{ and }{\b operator!=}{, and you can also ask for the }{\b name(\~)}{
 of the type, which returns a string representation of the type name. Note that if you hand }{\b typeid(\~) }{a }{\b Shape*}{, it will say that the type is }{\b Shape*}{
, so if you want to know the exact type it is pointing to, you must dereference the pointer. For example, if }{\b s}{ is a }{\b Shape*}{,
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {cout << typeid(*s).name() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {will print out the type of the object }{\b s}{ points to. 
\par You can also ask a }{\b typeinfo}{ object if it precedes another }{\b typeinfo}{ object in the implementation-defined \ldblquote collation sequence,\rdblquote  using }{\b before(typeinfo&)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: before(\~)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {before(\~)\: run-time type identification}}}{
, which returns true or false. When you say,
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {if(typeid(me).before(typeid(you))) // ...
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {you\rquote re asking if }{\b me}{ occurs before }{\b you}{ in the collation sequence.
\par The second syntax for RTTI is called a \ldblquote type-safe downcast}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {downcast\: type-safe downcast in run-time type identification}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: type-safe downcast}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {type\:
 type-safe downcast in run-time type identification}}}{.\rdblquote  The reason for the term \ldblquote downcast\rdblquote  is (again) the historical arrangement of the class hierarchy diagram. If casting a }{\b Circle*}{ to a }{\b Shape*}{
 is an upcast, then casting a }{\b Shape*}{ to a }{\b Circle*}{ is a downcast. However, you know a }{\b Circle*}{ is also a }{\b Shape*}{,and the compiler freely allows an upcast assignment, but you }{\i don\rquote t}{ know that a }{\b Shape*}{
 is necessarily a }{\b Circle*}{, so the compiler doesn\rquote t allow you to perform a downcast assignment without using an explicit cast. You can of course force your way through using ordinary C-style casts or a C++ }{\b static_cast}{
 (described at the end of this chapter), which says, \ldblquote I hope this is actually a }{\b Circle*}{, and I\rquote m going to pretend it is.\rdblquote  Without some explicit knowledge that it }{\i is}{ in fact a }{\b Circle}{
, this is a totally dangerous thing to do. A common approach in vendor-defined RTTI is to create some function that attempts to assign (for this example) a }{\b Shape*}{ to a }{\b Circle*}{, checking the type in the process.
 If this function returns the address, it was successful; if it returns null, you didn\rquote t have a }{\b Circle*}{.
\par The C++ RTTI typesafe-downcast follows this \ldblquote attempt-to-cast\rdblquote  function form, but it uses (very logically) the template syntax to produce the special function }{\b dynamic_cast}{\pard\plain 
