{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: and templates}}}{Templates generate many different class names, and sometimes you\rquote d
 like to print out information about what class you\rquote re in. RTTI provides a convenient way to do this. The following example revisits the code in Chapter XX to print out the order of constructor and destructor calls}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: order of constructor and destructor calls}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
destructor\: order of constructor and destructor calls}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {order\: of constructor and destructor calls}}}{ without using a preprocessor macro:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C24:ConstructorOrder.cpp
\par // Order of constructor calls
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <typeinfo>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par template<int id> class Announce \{
\par public:
\par   Announce() \{
\par     cout << typeid(*this).name()
\par          << " constructor " << endl;
\par   \}
\par   ~Announce() \{
\par     cout << typeid(*this).name()
\par          << " destructor " << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class X : public Announce<0> \{
\par   Announce<1> m1;
\par   Announce<2> m2;
\par public:
\par   X() \{ cout << "X::X()" << endl; \}
\par   ~X() \{ cout << "X::~X()" << endl; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {int main() \{ X x; \} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b <typeinfo>}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {typeinfo\: TYPEINFO.H}}}{
 header must be included to call any member functions for the }{\b typeinfo}{ object returned by }{\b typeid(\~)}{. The template uses a constant }{\b int}{
 to differentiate one class from another, but class arguments will work as well. Inside both the constructor and destructor, RTTI information is used to produce the name of the class to print. The class }{\b X}{
 uses both inheritance and composition to create a class that has an interesting order of constructor and destructor calls.
\par This technique is often useful in situations when you\rquote re trying to understand how the language works.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593311}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628783}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374143
