{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: and references}}}{
RTTI must adjust somewhat to work with references. The contrast between pointers and references occurs because a reference is always dereferenced for you by the compiler, whereas a pointer\rquote s type }{\i or}{
 the type it points to may be examined. Here\rquote s an example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C24:RTTIwithReferences.cpp
\par #include <cassert>
\par #include <typeinfo>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class B \{
\par public:
\par   virtual float f() \{ return 1.0;\}
\par   virtual ~B() \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class D : public B \{ /* ... */ \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   B* p = new D;
\par   B& r = *p;
\par   assert(typeid(p) == typeid(B*));
\par   assert(typeid(p) != typeid(D*));
\par   assert(typeid(r) == typeid(D));
\par   assert(typeid(*p) == typeid(D));
\par   assert(typeid(*p) != typeid(B));
\par   assert(typeid(&r) == typeid(B*));
\par   assert(typeid(&r) != typeid(D*));
\par   assert(typeid(r.f()) == typeid(float));
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Whereas the type of pointer that }{\b typeid(\~)}{ sees is the base type and not the derived type, the type it sees for the reference is the derived type:

\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {typeid(p) == typeid(B*)
\par typeid(p) != typeid(D*)
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {typeid(r) == typeid(D)
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Conversely, what the pointer points to is the derived type and not the base type, and taking the address of the reference produces the ba
se type and not the derived type:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {typeid(*p) == typeid(D)
\par typeid(*p) != typeid(B)
\par typeid(&r) == typeid(B*)
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {typeid(&r) != typeid(D*)
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Expressions may also be used with the }{\b typeid(\~)}{ operator because they have a type as well:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {typeid(r.f()) == typeid(float)
