}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776497}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you perform a }{\b dynamic_cast}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {dynamic_cast\:
 and exceptions, run-time type identification}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: dynamic_cast, run-time type identification}}}{
 to a reference, the result must be assigned to a reference. But what happens if the cast fails? There are no null references}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {reference\:
 null references}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {null references}}}{, so this is the perfect place to throw an exception; the Standard C++ exception type is }{\b bad_cast}{,}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: bad_cast}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
bad_cast\: and run-time type identification}}}{ but in the following example the ellipses are used to catch any exception:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C24:RTTIwithExceptions.cpp
\par #include <typeinfo>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par class X \{ public: virtual ~X()\{\} \};
\par class B \{ public: virtual ~B()\{\} \};
\par class D : public B \{\};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   D d;
\par   B & b = d; // Upcast to reference
\par   try \{
\par     X& xr = dynamic_cast<X&>(b);
\par   \} catch(...) \{
\par     cout << "dynamic_cast<X&>(b) failed" 
\par          << endl;
\par   \}
\par   X* xp = 0;
\par   try \{
\par     typeid(*xp); // Throws exception
\par   \} catch(bad_typeid) \{
\par     cout << "Bad typeid() expression" << endl;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The failure, of course, is because }{\b b}{ doesn\rquote t actually point to an }{\b X}{ object. If an exception was not thrown here, then }{\b xr}{
 would be unbound, and the guarantee that all objects or references are constructed storage would be broken.
\par An exception is also thrown if you try to dereference a null pointer in the process of calling }{\b typeid(\~)}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {typeid(\~)\: and exceptions}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: typeid(\~)}}}{. The Standard C++ exception is called }{\b bad_typeid}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {exception handling\: bad_typeid}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {bad_typeid\:
 run-time type identification}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: bad_typeid}}}{.
\par Here (unlike the reference example above) you can avoid the exception by checking for a nonzero pointer value before attempting the operation; this is the preferred practice.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593312}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628784}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374145
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776498}Multiple inheritance{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593312}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628784}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374145}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776498}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\:
 multiple inheritance and run-time type identification}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {multiple inheritance\: and run-time type identification}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: and multiple inheritance}}}{
Of course, the RTTI mechanisms must work properly with all the complexities of multiple inheritance, including }{\b virtual}{ base classes:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C24:RTTIandMultipleInheritance.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <typeinfo>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class BB \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void f() \{\}
\par   virtual ~BB() \{\}
\par \};
\par class B1 : virtual public BB \{\};
\par class B2 : virtual public BB \{\};
\par class MI : public B1, public B2 \{\};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   BB* bbp = new MI; // Upcast
\par   // Proper name detection:
\par   cout << typeid(*bbp).name() << endl;
\par   // Dynamic_cast works properly:
\par   MI* mip = dynamic_cast<MI*>(bbp);
\par   // Can't force old-style cast:
\par   //! MI* mip2 = (MI*)bbp; // Compile error
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b typeid(\~) }{properly detects the name of the actual object, even through the }{\b virtual}{ base class pointer. The }{\b dynamic_cast}{
 also works correctly. But the compiler won\rquote t even allow you to try to force a cast the old way:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {MI* mip = (MI*)bbp; // Compile-time error
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {It knows this is never the right thing to do, so it requires that you use a }{\b dynamic_cast}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593313}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628785}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374146
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776499}Sensible uses for RTTI{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593313}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628785}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374146}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776499}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: when to use it}}}{
Because it allows you to discover type information from an anonymous polymorphic pointer, RTTI is ripe for misuse }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\:
 misuse}}}{by the novice because RTTI may make sense before virtual functions do. For many people coming from a procedural background, it\rquote s very difficult not to organize their programs into sets of }{\b switch}{
 statements. They could accomplish this with RTTI and thus lose the very important value of polymorphism }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {polymorphism}}}{
in code development and maintenance. The intent of C++ is that you use virtual functions throughout your code, and you only use RTTI when you must.
\par However, using virtual functions as they are intended requires that you have control of the base-class definition because at some point in the extension of your program you may discover the base class doesn\rquote 
t include the virtual function you need. If the base class comes from a
 library or is otherwise controlled by someone else, a solution to the problem is RTTI: You can inherit a new type and add your extra member function. Elsewhere in the code you can detect your particular type and call that member function. This doesn
\rquote t destroy the polymorphism and extensibility of the program, because adding a new type will not require you to hunt for switch statements. However, when you add new code in your main body that requires your new feature, you\rquote 
ll have to detect your particular type.
\par Putting a feature in a base class might mean that, for the benefit of one particular class, all the other classes derived from that base require some meaningless stub of a virtual function. This makes the interface less clear and annoys those who must r
edefine pure virtual functions when they derive from that base class. For example, suppose that in the }{\b Wind5.cpp}{
 program in Chapter XX you wanted to clear the spit valves of all the instruments in your orchestra that had them. One option is to put a }{\b virtual ClearSpitValve(\~) }{function in the base class }{\b Instrument}{
, but this is confusing because it implies that }{\b Percussion}{ and }{\b electronic}{ instruments also have spit valves. RTTI provides a much more reasonable solution in this case because you can place the function in the specific class (}{\b Wind }{
in this case) where it\rquote s appropriate.
\par Finally, RTTI will sometimes solve efficiency }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: and efficiency}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {efficiency\: run-time type identification}}}{problems. If your code uses polymorphism i
n a nice way, but it turns out that one of your objects reacts to this general-purpose code in a horribly inefficient way, you can pick that type out using RTTI and write case-specific code to improve the efficiency.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374147}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776500}Revisiting the trash recycler
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374147}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776500}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here\rquote s the trash recycling simulation from Chapter XX, rewritten to use RTTI instead of building the information into the class hierarchy:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C24:Recycle2.cpp
\par // Chapter XX example w/ RTTI
\par #include "../purge.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <typeinfo>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("recycle2.out");
\par 
\par class Trash \{
\par   float _weight;
\par public:
\par   Trash(float wt) : _weight(wt) \{\}
\par   virtual float value() const = 0;
\par   float weight() const \{ return _weight; \}
\par   virtual ~Trash() \{ out << "~Trash()\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Aluminum : public Trash \{
\par   static float val;
\par public:
\par   Aluminum(float wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   float value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(int newval) \{
\par     val = newval;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par float Aluminum::val = 1.67;
\par 
\par class Paper : public Trash \{
\par   static float val;
\par public:
\par   Paper(float wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   float value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(int newval) \{
\par     val = newval;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par float Paper::val = 0.10;
\par 
\par class Glass : public Trash \{
\par   static float val;
\par public:
\par   Glass(float wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   float value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(int newval) \{
\par     val = newval;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par float Glass::val = 0.23;
\par 
\par // Sums up the value of the Trash in a bin:
\par template<class Container> void
\par sumValue(Container& bin, ostream& os) \{
\par   typename Container::iterator tally = 
\par     bin.begin();
\par   float val = 0;
\par   while(tally != bin.end()) \{
\par     val += (*tally)->weight() * (*tally)->value();
\par     os << "weight of "
\par         << typeid(*tally).name()
\par         << " = " << (*tally)->weight() << endl;
\par     tally++;
\par   \}
\par   os << "Total value = " << val << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   srand(time(0)); // Seed random number generator
\par   vector<Trash*> bin;
\par   // Fill up the Trash bin:
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 30; i++)
\par     switch(rand() % 3) \{
\par       case 0 :
\par         bin.push_back(new Aluminum(rand() % 100));
\par         break;
\par       case 1 :
\par         bin.push_back(new Paper(rand() % 100));
\par         break;
\par       case 2 :
\par         bin.push_back(new Glass(rand() % 100));
\par         break;
\par     \}
\par   // Note difference w/ chapter 14: Bins hold
\par   // exact type of object, not base type:
\par   vector<Glass*> glassBin;
\par   vector<Paper*> paperBin;
\par   vector<Aluminum*> alBin;
\par   vector<Trash*>::iterator sorter = bin.begin();
\par   // Sort the Trash:
\par   while(sorter != bin.end()) \{
\par     Aluminum* ap =
\par       dynamic_cast<Aluminum*>(*sorter);
\par     Paper* pp =
\par       dynamic_cast<Paper*>(*sorter);
\par     Glass* gp =
\par       dynamic_cast<Glass*>(*sorter);
\par     if(ap) alBin.push_back(ap);
\par     if(pp) paperBin.push_back(pp);
\par     if(gp) glassBin.push_back(gp);
\par     sorter++;
\par   \}
\par   sumValue(alBin, out);
\par   sumValue(paperBin, out);
\par   sumValue(glassBin, out);
\par   sumValue(bin, out);
\par   purge(bin);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593314}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628786}The nature of this problem is that the trash is thrown unclassified into a single bin, so the
 specific type information is lost. But later, the specific type information must be recovered to properly sort the trash, and so RTTI is used. In Chapter XX, an RTTI system was inserted into the class hierarchy, but as you can see here, it\rquote 
s more convenient to use C++\rquote s built-in RTTI.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374148}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776501}
Mechanism & overhead of RTTI{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593314}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628786}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374148}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776501}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: mechanism & overhead}
}}{Typically, RTTI is implemented by placing an additional pointer in the VTABLE}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {VTABLE\: and run-time type identification}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: VTABLE}}}{. This pointer points to the }{\b typeinfo}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {typeinfo\: structure}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: typeinfo}}}{
structure for that particular type. (Only one instance of the }{\b typeinfo}{ structure is created for each new class.) So the effect of a }{\b typeid(\~)}{ expression is quite simple: The VPTR is used to fetch the }{\b typeinfo}{
 pointer, and a reference to the resulting }{\b typeinfo}{ structure is produced. Also, this is a deterministic process \endash  you always know how long it\rquote s going to take.
\par For a }{\b dynamic_cast<destination*>(source_pointer)}{, most cases are quite straightforward: }{\b source_pointer}{\rquote s RTTI information is retrieved, and RTTI information for the type }{\b destination*}{
 is fetched. Then a library routine determines whether }{\b source_pointer}{\rquote s type is of type }{\b destination*}{ or a base class of }{\b destination*}{. The pointer it returns may be slightly adjusted because of multiple inheritance}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\: multiple inheritance and run-time type identification}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {multiple inheritance\: and run-time type identification}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: and multiple inheritance}}}{
 if the base type isn\rquote t the first base of the derived class. The situation is (of course) more complicated with multiple inheritance where a base type may appear more than once in an inheritance hierarchy and where virtual base classes are used.

\par Because the library routine used for }{\b dynamic_cast}{ must check through a list of base classes, the overhead for }{\b dynamic_cast}{ is higher than }{\b typeid(\~)}{ (but of course you get different information, w
hich may be essential to your solution), and it\rquote s nondeterministic because it may take more time to discover a base class than a derived class. In addition, }{\b dynamic_cast}{ allows you to compare any type to any other type; you aren\rquote 
t restricted to comparing types within the same hierarchy. This adds extra overhead to the library routine used by }{\b dynamic_cast}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593315}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628787}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374149
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776502}Creating your own RTTI{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593315}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628787}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374149}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776502}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {run-time type identification\: building your own}}}{
If your compiler doesn\rquote t yet support RTTI, you can build it into your class l
ibraries quite easily. This makes sense because RTTI was added to the language after observing that virtually all class libraries had some form of it anyway (and it was relatively \ldblquote free\rdblquote 
 after exception handling was added because exceptions require exact knowledge of type information).
\par Essentially, RTTI requires only a virtual function to identify the exact type of the class, and a function to take a pointer to the base type and cast it down to the more derived type; this function must produce a pointer to 
the more derived type. (You may also wish to handle references.) There are a number of approaches to implement your own RTTI, but all require a unique identifier for each class and a virtual function to produce type information. The following uses a }{\b 
static}{ member function called }{\b dynacast(\~)}{ that calls a type information function }{\b dynamic_type(\~)}{. Both functions must be defined for each new derivation:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C24:Selfrtti.cpp
\par // Your own RTTI system
\par #include "../purge.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <vector>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Security \{
\par protected:
\par   enum \{ baseID = 1000 \};
\par public:
\par   virtual int dynamic_type(int id) \{
\par     if(id == baseID) return 1;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Stock : public Security \{
\par protected:
\par   enum \{ typeID = baseID + 1 \};
\par public:
\par   int dynamic_type(int id) \{
\par     if(id == typeID) return 1;
\par     return Security::dynamic_type(id);
\par   \}
\par   static Stock* dynacast(Security* s) \{
\par     if(s->dynamic_type(typeID))
\par       return (Stock*)s;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Bond : public Security \{
\par protected:
\par   enum \{ typeID = baseID + 2 \};
\par public:
\par   int dynamic_type(int id) \{
\par     if(id == typeID) return 1;
\par     return Security::dynamic_type(id);
\par   \}
\par   static Bond* dynacast(Security* s) \{
\par     if(s->dynamic_type(typeID))
\par       return (Bond*)s;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Commodity : public Security \{
\par protected:
\par   enum \{ typeID = baseID + 3\};
\par public:
\par   int dynamic_type(int id) \{
\par     if(id == typeID) return 1;
\par     return Security::dynamic_type(id);
\par   \}
\par   static Commodity* dynacast(Security* s) \{
\par     if(s->dynamic_type(typeID))
\par       return (Commodity*)s;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   void special() \{
\par     cout << "special Commodity function\\n";
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Metal : public Commodity \{
\par protected:
\par   enum \{ typeID = baseID + 4\};
\par public:
\par   int dynamic_type(int id) \{
\par     if(id == typeID) return 1;
\par     return Commodity::dynamic_type(id);
\par   \}
\par   static Metal* dynacast(Security* s) \{
\par     if(s->dynamic_type(typeID))
\par       return (Metal*)s;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<Security*> portfolio;
\par   portfolio.push_back(new Metal);
\par   portfolio.push_back(new Commodity);
\par   portfolio.push_back(new Bond);
\par   portfolio.push_back(new Stock);
\par   vector<Security*>::iterator it = 
\par     portfolio.begin();
\par   while(it != portfolio.end()) \{
\par     Commodity* cm = Commodity::dynacast(*it);
\par     if(cm) cm->special();
\par     else cout << "not a Commodity" << endl;
\par     it++;
\par   \}
\par   cout << "cast from intermediate pointer:\\n";
\par   Security* sp = new Metal;
\par   Commodity* cp = Commodity::dynacast(sp);
\par   if(cp) cout << "it's a Commodity\\n";
\par   Metal* mp = Metal::dynacast(sp);
\par   if(mp) cout << "it's a Metal too!\\n";
\par   purge(portfolio);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Each subclass must create its own }{\b typeID}{, redefine the }{\b virtual dynamic_type(\~)}{ function to return that }{\b typeID}{, and define a }{\b static
}{ member called }{\b dynacast(\~)}{, which takes the base pointer (or a pointer at any level in a deeper hierarchy \endash  in that case, the pointer is simply upcast).
\par In the classes derived from }{\b Security}{, you can see that each defines its own }{\b typeID}{ enumeration by adding to }{\b baseID}{. It\rquote s essential that }{\b baseID}{ be directly accessible in the derived class because the }{\b enum}{
 must be evaluated at compile-time, so the usual approach of reading private data with an }{\b inline}{ function would fail. This is a good example of the need for the }{\b protected}{ }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {protected}}}{mechanism.
\par The }{\b enum}{ }{\b baseID}{ establishes a base identifier for all types derived from }{\b Security}{. That way, if an identifier clash ever occurs, you can change all the identifiers by changing the base value. (However, because this scheme doesn
\rquote t compare different inheritance trees, an identifier clash is unlikely). In all the classes, the class identifier number is }{\b protected}{, so it\rquote s directly available to derived classes but not to the end user.
\par This example illustrates what built-in RTTI must cope with. Not only must you be able to determine the exact type, you must also be able to find out whether your exact type is }{\i derived from}{ the type you\rquote re looking for. For example, }{\b 
Metal }{is derived from }{\b Commodity}{,}{\b  }{which has a function called }{\b special(\~)}{, so if you have a }{\b Metal}{ object you can call }{\b special(\~)}{ for it. If }{\b dynamic_type(\~) }{
told you only the exact type of the object, you could ask it if a }{\b Metal}{ were a }{\b Commodity}{, and it would say \ldblquote no,\rdblquote  which is untrue. Therefore, the system must be set up so it will properly cast to intermediate 
types in a hierarchy as well as exact types.
\par The }{\b dynacast(\~) }{function determines the type information by calling the }{\b virtual dynamic_type(\~)}{ function for the }{\b Security}{ pointer it\rquote s passed. This function takes an argument of the }{\b typeID }{for the class you\rquote re t
rying to cast to. It\rquote s a virtual function, so the function body is the one for the exact type of the object. Each }{\b dynamic_type(\~)}{ function first checks to see if the identifier it was passed is an exact match for its own type. If that isn
\rquote t true, it must check to see if it matches a base type; this is accomplished by making a call to the base class }{\b dynamic_type(\~)}{. Just like a recursive function call, each }{\b dynamic_type(\~) }{
checks against its own identifier. If it doesn\rquote t find a match, it returns the result of calling the base class }{\b dynamic_type(\~)}{. When the root of the hierarchy is reached, zero}{\b  }{is returned to indicate no match was found.
\par If }{\b dynamic_type(\~)}{ returns one (for \ldblquote true\rdblquote ) the object pointed to is either the exact type you\rquote re asking about or derived from that type, and }{\b dynacast(\~)}{ takes the }{\b Security}{
 pointer and casts it to the desired type. If the return value is false, }{\b dynacast(\~)}{ returns zero to indicate the cast was unsuccessful. In this way it works just like the C++ }{\b dynamic_cast}{ operator.
\par The C++ }{\b dynamic_cast}{ operator does one more thing the above scheme can\rquote 
t do: It compares types from one inheritance hierarchy to another, completely separate inheritance hierarchy. This adds generality to the system for those unusual cases where you want to compa
re across hierarchies, but it also adds some complexity and overhead.
\par You can easily imagine how to create a DYNAMIC_CAST macro that uses the above scheme and allows an easier transition to the built-in }{\b dynamic_cast}{ operator.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593316}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628788}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374150
}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776503}Explicit cast syntax{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593316}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628788}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374150}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776503}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Whenever you use a cast, you\rquote re breaking the type}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {type\: new cast syntax}}}{ system. }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cast\: new cast syntax}}}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ See Jos\'e9e Lajoie }{\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {Lajoie, Jos\'e9e}}}{, \ldblquote The new cast notation and the bool data type,\rdblquote  C++ Report, September, 1994 pp. 46-51.}}}{ You\rquote 
re telling the compiler that even though you know an object is a certain type, you\rquote re going to pretend it is a different type. This is an inherently dangerous activity, and a clear source of errors.
\par Unfortunately, each cast is different: the name of the pretender type surrounded by parentheses. So if you are given a piece of code that isn\rquote t working correctly and you know you want to examine all casts to see if they\rquote 
re the source of the errors, how can you guarantee that you find all the casts? In a C program, you can\rquote t. For one thing, the C compiler doesn\rquote t always require a cast (it\rquote s possible to assign dissimilar types }{\i through}{
 a void pointer without being forced to use a cast), and the casts all look different, so you can\rquote t know if you\rquote ve searched for every one.
\par To solve this problem, C++ provides a consistent casting syntax using four reserved words: }{\b dynamic_cast}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cast\: dynamic_cast}}}{ (the subje
ct of the first part of this chapter), }{\b const_cast}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const_cast}}}{, }{\b static_cast, }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {static_cast}}}{and}{\b  reinterpret_cast}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {reinterpret_cast}
}}{. This window of opportunity opened up when the need for }{\b dynamic_cast}{ arose \endash  the meaning of the existing cast syntax was already far too overloaded to support any additional functionality.
\par By using these casts instead of the }{\b (newtype)}{ syntax, you can easily search for}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cast\: searching for}}}{
 all the casts in any program. To support existing code, most compilers have various levels of error/warning generation that can be turned on and off. But if you turn on full errors for the explicit cast syntax, you can be guaranteed that you\rquote 
ll find all the places in your project where casts occur, which will make bug-hunting much easier.
\par The following table describes the different forms of casting}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cast\: casting away const and/or volatile}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: casting away const and/or volatile}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {volatile\:
 casting away const and/or volatile}}}{:
\par }\trowd \trgaph108\trleft2160\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clvertalt\clbrdrt
\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cltxlrtb \cellx4050\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr
\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cltxlrtb \cellx7668\pard \sa120\widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b static_cast}{\cell For \ldblquote well-behaved\rdblquote  and \ldblquote reasonably well-behaved\rdblquote 
 casts, including things you might now do without a cast (e.g., an upcast or automatic type conversion).\cell }\pard \widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\row }\trowd \trgaph108\trleft2160\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 
\trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb
\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cltxlrtb \cellx4050\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cltxlrtb \cellx7668\pard 
\sa120\widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b const_cast}{\cell To cast away }{\b const}{ and/or }{\b volatile}{.\cell }\pard \widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\row }\pard 
\sa120\widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b dynamic_cast}{\cell For type-safe downcasting (described earlier in the chapter).\cell }\pard \widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\row 
}\trowd \trgaph108\trleft2160\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clvertalt\clbrdrt
\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cltxlrtb \cellx4050\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr
\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cltxlrtb \cellx7668\pard \sa120\widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b reinterpret_cast}{\cell To cast to a completely different meaning. The key is that you\rquote 
ll need to cast back to the original type to use it safely. The type you cast to is typically used only for bit twiddling or some other mysterious purpose. This is the most dangerous of all the casts.\cell }\pard 
\widctlpar\intbl{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\row }\pard \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {The three explicit casts will be described more completely in the following sections.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374151}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776504}static_cast
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374151}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776504}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {static_cast}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cast\: static_cast}}}{A }{\b static_cast}{ is used for all conversions that are well-defined. These include \ldblquote safe\rdblquote  convers
ions that the compiler would allow you to do without a cast and less-safe conversions that are nonetheless well-defined. The types of conversions covered by }{\b static_cast}{
 include typical castless conversions, narrowing (information-losing) conversions, forcing a conversion from a }{\b void*}{, implicit type conversions, and static navigation of class hierarchies:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C24:Statcast.cpp
\par // Examples of static_cast
\par 
\par class Base \{ /* ... */ \};
\par class Derived : public Base \{
\par public:
\par   // ...
\par   // Automatic type conversion:
\par   operator int() \{ return 1; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par void func(int) \{\}
\par 
\par class Other \{\};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   int i = 0x7fff; // Max pos value = 32767
\par   long l;
\par   float f;
\par   // (1) typical castless conversions:
\par   l = i;
\par   f = i;
\par   // Also works:
\par   l = static_cast<long>(i);
\par   f = static_cast<float>(i);
\par 
\par   // (2) narrowing conversions:
\par   i = l; // May lose digits
\par   i = f; // May lose info
\par   // Says "I know," eliminates warnings:
\par   i = static_cast<int>(l);
\par   i = static_cast<int>(f);
\par   char c = static_cast<char>(i);
\par 
\par   // (3) forcing a conversion from void* :
\par   void* vp = &i;
\par   // Old way produces a dangerous conversion:
\par   float* fp = (float*)vp;
\par   // The new way is equally dangerous:
\par   fp = static_cast<float*>(vp);
\par 
\par   // (4) implicit type conversions, normally
\par   // Performed by the compiler:
\par   Derived d;
\par   Base* bp = &d; // Upcast: normal and OK
\par   bp = static_cast<Base*>(&d); // More explicit
\par   int x = d; // Automatic type conversion
\par   x = static_cast<int>(d); // More explicit
\par   func(d); // Automatic type conversion
\par   func(static_cast<int>(d)); // More explicit
\par 
\par   // (5) Static Navigation of class hierarchies:
\par   Derived* dp = static_cast<Derived*>(bp);
\par   // ONLY an efficiency hack. dynamic_cast is
\par   // Always safer. However:
\par   // Other* op = static_cast<Other*>(bp);
\par   // Conveniently gives an error message, while
\par   Other* op2 = (Other*)bp;
\par   // Does not.
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In Section (1), you see the kinds of conversions you\rquote re used to doing in C, with or without a cast. Promoting from an }{\b int}{ to a }{\b long}{ or }
{\b float}{ is not a problem because the latter can always hold every value that an }{\b int}{ can contain. Although it\rquote s unnecessary, you can use }{\b static_cast}{ to highlight these promotions.
\par Converting back the other way is shown in (2). Here, you can lose data because an }{\b int}{ is not as \ldblquote wide\rdblquote  as a }{\b long}{ or a }{\b float}{ \endash  it won\rquote t hold numbers of the same size. Thus these are called \ldblquote 
narrowing conversions}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {narrowing conversions}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe 
{conversion\: narrowing conversions}}}{.\rdblquote  The compiler will still perform these, but will often give you a warning. You can eliminate this warning and indicate that you really did mean it using a cast.
\par Assigning from a }{\b void*}{ is not allowed without a cast in C++ (unlike C), as seen in (3). This is dangerous and requires that a programmer know what he\rquote s doing. The }{\b static_cast}{, at least, is easi
er to locate than the old standard cast when you\rquote re hunting for bugs.
\par Section (4) shows the kinds of implicit type conversions that are normally performed automatically by the compiler. These are automatic and require no casting, but again }{\b static_cast}{ highlights the action in case you want to make it clear what
\rquote s happening or hunt for it later.
\par If a class hierarchy has no }{\b virtual}{ functions or if you have other information that allows you to safely downcast, it\rquote s slightly faster to do the downcast}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {downcast\: static}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {static\: downcast}}}{ statically than with }{\b dynamic_cast}{, as shown in (5). In addition, }{\b static_cast
}{ won\rquote t allow you to cast out of the hierarchy, as the traditional cast will, so it\rquote s safer. However, statically navigating class hierarchies is always risky and you should use }{\b dynamic_cast}{ unless you have a special situation.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374152}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776505}const_cast
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374152}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776505}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const_cast}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cast\: const_cast}}}{If you want to convert from a }{\b const}{ to a non}{\b const}{ or from a }{\b volatile}{ to a non}{\b volatile}{, you use }{\b const_cast}{
. This is the }{\i only}{ conversion allowed with }{\b const_cast}{; if any other conversion is involved it must be done separately or you\rquote ll get a compile-time error.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C24:Constcst.cpp
\par // Const casts
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   const int i = 0;
\par   int* j = (int*)&i; // Deprecated form
\par   j  = const_cast<int*>(&i); // Preferred
\par   // Can't do simultaneous additional casting:
\par //! long* l = const_cast<long*>(&i); // Error
\par   volatile int k = 0;
\par   int* u = const_cast<int*>(&k);
\par \}
\par 
\par class X \{
\par   int i;
\par // mutable int i; // A better approach
\par public:
\par   void f() const \{
\par     // Casting away const-ness:
\par     (const_cast<X*>(this))->i = 1;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}; ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If you take the address of a }{\b const}{ object, you produce a pointer to a }{\b const}{, and this cannot be assigned to a non}{\b const}{
 pointer without a cast. The old-style cast will accomplish this, but the }{\b const_cast}{ is the appropriate one to use. The same holds true for }{\b volatile}{.
\par If you want to change a class member inside a }{\b const}{ member function, the traditional approach is to cast away constness}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cast\:
 casting away const}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {const\: casting away const}}}{ by saying }{\b (X*)this}{. You can still cast away constness using the better }{\b const_cast}{
, but a superior approach is to make that particular data member }{\b mutable}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {mutable}}}{, so it\rquote 
s clear in the class definition, and not hidden away in the member function definitions, that the member may change in a }{\b const}{ member function.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {\b {\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374153}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776506}reinterpret_cast
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374153}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776506}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {reinterpret_cast}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {cast\: reinterpret cast}}}{This is the least safe of the casting mechanisms, and the one most likely to point to bugs. A
t the very least, your compiler should contain switches to allow you to force the use of }{\b const_cast}{ and }{\b reinterpret_cast}{, which will locate the most unsafe of the casts.
\par A }{\b reinterpret_cast}{ pretends that an object is just a bit pattern that can be treated (for some dark purpose) as if it were an entirely different type of object. This is the low-level bit twiddling that C is notorious for. You\rquote 
ll virtually always need to }{\b reinterpret_cast}{ back to the original type before doing anything else with it.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C24:Reinterp.cpp
\par // Reinterpret_cast
\par // Example depends on VPTR location,
\par // Which may differ between compilers.
\par #include <cstring>
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("reinterp.out");
\par 
\par class X \{
\par   enum \{ sz = 5 \};
\par   int a[sz];
\par public:
\par   X() \{ memset(a, 0, sz * sizeof(int)); \}
\par   virtual void f() \{\}
\par   // Size of all the data members:
\par   int membsize() \{ return sizeof(a); \}
\par   friend ostream&
\par     operator<<(ostream& os, const X& x) \{
\par       for(int i = 0; i < sz; i++)
\par         os << x.a[i] << ' ';
\par       return os;
\par   \}
\par   virtual ~X() \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   X x;
\par   out << x << endl; // Initialized to zeroes
\par   int* xp = reinterpret_cast<int*>(&x);
\par   xp[1] = 47;
\par   out << x << endl; // Oops!
\par 
\par   X x2;
\par   const int vptr_size= sizeof(X) - x2.membsize();
\par   long l = reinterpret_cast<long>(&x2);
\par   // *IF* the VPTR is first in the object:
\par   l += vptr_size; // Move past VPTR
\par   xp = reinterpret_cast<int*>(l);
\par   xp[1] = 47;
\par   out << x2 << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593317}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628789}The }{\b class}{ }{\b X}{ contains some data and a }{\b virtual}{ member function. In }{\b main(\~)}{
, an }{\b X}{ object is printed out to show that it gets initialized to zero, and then its address is cast to an }{\b int*}{ using a }{\b reinterpret_cast}{. Pretending it\rquote s an }{\b int*}{
, the object is indexed into as if it were an array and (in theory) element one is set to 47. But here\rquote s the output:}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ For this particular compiler. Yours will probably be different.}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {0 0 0 0 0 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {47 0 0 0 0 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Clearly, it\rquote 
s not safe to assume that the data in the object begins at the starting address of the object. In fact, this compiler puts the VPTR at the beginning of the object, so if }{\b xp[0]}{ had been selected instead of }{\b xp[1]}{
, it would have trashed the VPTR.
\par To fix the problem, the size of the VPTR is calculated by subtracting the size of the data members from the size of the object. Then the address of the object is cast (again, with }{\b reinterpret_cast}{)}{\b  }{to a }{\b long}{
, and the starting address of the actual data is established, }{\i assuming}{ the VPTR is placed at the beginning of the object. The resulting number is cast back to an }{\b int*}{ and the indexing now produces the desired result:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {0 47 0 0 0 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Of course, this is inadvisable and nonportable programming. That\rquote s the kind of thing that a }{\b reinterpret_cast}{ indicates, but it\rquote 
s available when you decide you have to use it.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374154}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776507}Summary
{\*\bkmkend _Toc305593317}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628789}{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374154}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776507}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {RTTI is a convenient extra feature, a bit of icing on the cake. Although normally you upcast a point
er to a base class and then use the generic interface of that base class (via virtual functions), occasionally you get into a corner where things can be more effective if you know the exact type of the object pointed to by the base pointer, and that
\rquote s what RTTI provides. Because some form of virtual-function-based RTTI has appeared in almost all class libraries, this is a useful feature because it means
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 1. \tab}}\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls214\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls214\adjustright \fs20\lang1024\cgrid {You don\rquote t have to build it into your own libraries.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 2. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls214\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls214\adjustright {You don\rquote t have to worry whether it will be built into someone else\rquote s library.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 3. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls214\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls214\adjustright {You don\rquote t have the extra programming overhead of maintaining an RTTI scheme during inheritance.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 4. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls214\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls214\adjustright {The syntax is consistent, so you don\rquote t have to figure out a new one for each library.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {While RTTI is a convenience, like most features i
n C++ it can be misused by either a naive or determined programmer. The most common misuse may come from the programmer who doesn\rquote 
t understand virtual functions and uses RTTI to do type-check coding instead. The philosophy of C++ seems to be to provide you
 with powerful tools and guard for type violations and integrity, but if you want to deliberately misuse or get around a language feature, there\rquote s nothing to stop you. Sometimes a slight burn is the fastest way to gain experience.
\par The explicit cast syntax will be a big help during debugging because casting opens a hole into your type system and allows errors to slip in. The explicit cast syntax will allow you to more easily locate these error entryways.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374155}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776508}Exercises
