\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 1. \tab}}\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls277\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls277\adjustright \fs20\lang1024\cgrid {Create a heap compactor for all dynamic memory in a particular program. This will require that you control how objects are dynamically created and used (do you overload }{\b operator new}{
 or does that approach work?). The typically heap-compaction scheme requires that all pointers are doubly-indirected (that is, pointers to pointers) so the \ldblquote middle tier\rdblquote 
 pointer can be manipulated during compaction. Consider overloading }{\b operator->}{ to accomplish this, since that operator has special behavior which will probably benefit your heap-compaction scheme. Write a program to test your he
ap-compaction scheme.
\par }\pard\plain \s1\fi-1080\li990\sa360\sl240\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \cbpat8 \f4\fs96\cf1\kerning18\cgrid {\sect }\sectd 
\margtsxn2491\margbsxn2563\binfsxn-121\binsxn-121\psz1\sbkodd\linex0\headery0\footery1620\colsx432\endnhere\titlepg\sectdefaultcl \pard\plain \s1\fi-1080\li990\sa360\sl240\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \cbpat8 
\f4\fs96\cf1\kerning18\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart DesignPatterns}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776516}{\*\bkmkend DesignPatterns}25: Design pa}{t}{terns{\*\bkmkend _Toc375545412}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407441460}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018793}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776516}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {\ldblquote \'85
 describes a problem which occurs over and over again in our environment, and then describes the core of the solution to that problem, in such a way that you can use this solution a million times over, without ever doing it the same way twice\rdblquote   
\endash  Christopher Alexander
\par This chapter introduces the important and yet non-traditional \ldblquote patterns\rdblquote  approach to program design. 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {[[ Much of the prose in this chapter still needs work, but the examples all compile. Also, more patterns and examples are forthcoming ]]
\par Probably the most important step forward in object-oriented design is the \ldblquote design patterns\rdblquote  movement, chronicled in }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {design patterns}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {patterns, design patterns}}}{\i Design Patterns}{, by Gamma, Helm, Johnson & Vlissides (Addison-Wesley 1995).}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ Conveniently, the examples are in C++.}}}{
 That book shows 23 different solutions to particular classes of problems. In this chapter, the basic concepts of design patterns will be introduced along with examples. This should whet your appetite to read }{\i Design Patterns}{
 (a source of what has now become an essential, almost mandatory, vocabulary for OOP programmers).
\par The latter part of this chapter contains an example of the design evolution process, starting with an initial solution and moving through the logic and pr
ocess of evolving the solution to more appropriate designs. The program shown (a trash recycling simulation) has evolved over time, and you can look at that evolution as a prototype for the way your own design can start as an adequate solution to a partic
ular problem and evolve into a flexible approach to a class of problems.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018794}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776517}The pattern concept{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018794}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776517
}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Initially, you can think of a pattern as an especially clever and insightful way of solving a particular class of problems. That is, it looks like a lot of people h
ave worked out all the angles of a problem and have come up with the most general, flexible solution for it. The problem could be one you have seen and solved before, but your solution probably didn\rquote t have the kind of completeness you\rquote 
ll see embodied in a pattern.
\par Although they\rquote re called \ldblquote design patterns,\rdblquote  they really aren\rquote 
t tied to the realm of design. A pattern seems to stand apart from the traditional way of thinking about analysis, design, and implementation. Instead, a pattern e}{m}{bodies a complete idea 
within a program, and thus it can sometimes appear at the analysis phase or high-level design phase. This is interesting because a pattern has a direct implementation in code and so you might not expect it to show up before low-level design or implementat
ion (and in fact you might not realize that you need a particular pattern until you get to those phases).
\par The basic concept of a pattern can also be seen as the basic concept of pr}{o}{gram design: adding layers of }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {abstraction\: in program design}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {design\: abstraction in program design}}}{abstraction. Whenever you abstract something you\rquote re isolating particular details, and one of the most compelling motiv}{a}{tions behind this is to }{\i 
separate things that change from things that stay the same}{. Another way to put this is that once you find some part of your pr}{o}{gram that\rquote s likely to change for one reason or another, you\rquote 
ll want to keep those changes from propagating other modifications throughout your code. Not only does this make the code much cheaper to maintain, but it also turns out that it is usually simpler to understand (which results in lowered costs).
\par Often, the most difficult part of developing an elegant and cheap-to-maintain design is in discovering what I call \ldblquote the }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {vector of change}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {change\: vector of change}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {design patterns\: vector of change}}}{vector of change.\rdblquote  (Here, \ldblquote vector\rdblquote 
 refers to the maximum gradient and not a container class.) This means finding the most important thing that changes in your system, or put another way, di}{s}{
covering where your greatest cost is. Once you discover the vector of change, you have the focal point around which to structure your design.
\par So the goal of design patterns is to isolate changes in your code. If you look at it this way, you\rquote ve been seeing some design patterns already in this book. For example, }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {inheritance\:
 and design patterns}}}{inheritance could be thought of as a design pattern (albeit one implemented by the compiler). It allows you to express differences in b}{e}{havior (that\rquote s the thing that changes) in objects that all have the same inte}{r}{
face (that\rquote s what stays the same). }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {composition\: and design patterns}}}{Composition could also be considered a pattern, since it allows you to change \endash  dynamically or statically 
\endash  the objects that implement your class, and thus the way that class works. Normally, however, features that are directly supported by a programming language are not classified as design patterns.
\par You\rquote ve also already seen another pattern that appears in }{\i Design Patterns}{: the }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {iterator}}}{\i iterator}{
. This is the fundamental tool used in the design of the STL; it hides the particular implementation of the container as you\rquote re stepping through and selecting the elements one by one. The iterator allows you to write generic code t
hat performs an operation on all of the elements in a range without regard to the container that holds the range. Thus your generic code can be used with any container that can pr}{o}{duce iterators.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018795}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776518}The singleton{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018795}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776518}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Possibly the simplest design pattern is the }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {singleton}}}{\i singleton}{
, which is a way to provide one and only one instance of an object:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:SingletonPattern.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Singleton \{
\par   static Singleton s;
\par   int i;
\par   Singleton(int x) : i(x) \{ \}
\par   void operator=(Singleton&);
\par   Singleton(const Singleton&);
\par public:
\par   static Singleton& getHandle() \{
\par     return s;
\par   \}
\par   int getValue() \{ return i; \}
\par   void setValue(int x) \{ i = x; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par Singleton Singleton::s(47);
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Singleton& s = Singleton::getHandle();
\par   cout << s.getValue() << endl;
\par   Singleton& s2 = Singleton::getHandle();
\par   s2.setValue(9);
\par   cout << s.getValue() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The key to creating a singleton is to prevent the client programmer from having any way to create an object except the ways you provide. To do this, you must declare all }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: private constructor}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {private\: constructor}}}{constructors as }{\b private}{, and you must}{\b  }{create at least one co}{n}{
structor to prevent the compiler from }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {default constructor\: synthesized by the compiler}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\:
 default constructor synthesized by the compiler}}}{synthesizing a default constructor for you.
\par At this point, you decide how you\rquote re going to create your object. Here, it\rquote s created statically, but you can also wait until the client programmer asks for one and create it on demand. In any case, the object should be stored pr}{i}{
vately. You provide access through public methods. Here, }{\b getHandle(\~)}{ pr}{o}{duces a reference to the }{\b Singleton}{ object. The rest of the interface (}{\b getValue(\~)}{ and }{\b setValue(\~)}{) is the regular class interface.
\par Note that you aren\rquote t restricted to creating only one object. This tec}{h}{nique easily supports the creation of a limited pool of objects. In that situation, however, you can be confronted with the problem
 of sharing objects in the pool. If this is an i}{s}{sue, you can create a solution involving a check-out and check-in of the shared objects.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Variations on singleton
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Any static member object inside a class is an expression of singleton: one and only one will b
e made. So in a sense, the language has direct support for the idea; we certainly use it on a regular basis. However, there\rquote s a problem associated with static objects (member or not), and that\rquote 
s the order of initialization, as described earlier in this book. If one static object depends on another, it\rquote s important that the order of initialization proceed correctly.
\par Fortunately, there\rquote s another language feature that allows you to control many aspects of initialization order, and that\rquote s a static object defined
 inside a function. This delays the initialization of the object until the first time the function is called. If the function returns a reference to the static object, it gives you the effect of a singleton while removing much of the worry of static initi
alization. For example, suppose you want to create a logfile upon the first call to a function which returns a reference to that logfile. This header file will do the trick:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:LogFile.h
\par #ifndef LOGFILE_H
\par #define LOGFILE_H
\par #include <fstream>
\par 
\par inline std::ofstream& logfile() \{
\par   static std::ofstream log("Logfile.log");
\par   return log;
\par \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // LOGFILE_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Since it\rquote s }{\b inline}{, the compiler and linker are responsible for guaranteeing there\rquote s only one actual instance of the function definition. The }{\b log}{ 
object will not be created until the first time }{\b logfile(\~)}{ is called. So if you use the function in one file:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:UseLog1.cpp \{O\}
\par #include "LogFile.h"
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par void f() \{
\par   logfile() << __FILE__ << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {And again in another file (for simplicity I didn\rquote t create a header file for }{\b UseLog1.cpp}{):
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:UseLog2.cpp
\par //\{L\} UseLog1
\par #include "LogFile.h"
\par using namespace std;
\par void f(); // In lieu of a header file
\par 
\par void g() \{
\par   logfile() << __FILE__ << endl;
\par \} 
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   f();
\par   g();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Then the }{\b log }{object doesn\rquote t get created until the first call to }{\b f(\~)}{.
\par You can easily combine the creation of the static object inside a member function with the singleton class. }{\b SingletonPattern.cpp}{ can be modified to use this approach:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:SingletonPattern2.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Singleton \{
\par   int i;
\par   Singleton(int x) : i(x) \{ \}
\par   void operator=(Singleton&);
\par   Singleton(const Singleton&);
\par public:
\par   static Singleton& getHandle() \{
\par     static Singleton s(47);
\par     return s;
\par   \}
\par   int getValue() \{ return i; \}
\par   void setValue(int x) \{ i = x; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Singleton& s = Singleton::getHandle();
\par   cout << s.getValue() << endl;
\par   Singleton& s2 = Singleton::getHandle();
\par   s2.setValue(9);
\par   cout << s.getValue() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {An especially interesting case is if two of these singletons depend on each other, like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:FunctionStaticSingleton.cpp
\par 
\par class Singleton1 \{
\par   Singleton1() \{\}
\par public:
\par   static Singleton1& ref() \{
\par     static Singleton1 single;
\par     return single;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Singleton2 \{
\par   Singleton1& s1;
\par   Singleton2(Singleton1& s) : s1(s) \{\}
\par public:
\par   static Singleton2& ref() \{
\par     static Singleton2 single(Singleton1::ref());
\par     return single;
\par   \}
\par   Singleton1& f() \{ return s1; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Singleton1& s1 = Singleton2::ref().f();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When }{\b Singleton2::ref(\~)}{ is called, it causes its sole }{\b Singleton2}{ object to be created. In the process of this creation, }{\b Singleton1::ref(\~)}{ is called, and that causes the sole }{
\b Singleton1}{ object to be created. Because this technique doesn\rquote t rely on the order of linking or loading, the programmer has much better control over initialization, leading to less problems.
\par You\rquote ll see further examples of the singleton pattern in the rest of this chapter.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018796}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776519}Classifying patterns{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018796}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776519}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\i Design Patterns}{ book discusses 23 different patterns, classified under three pu}{r}{poses (all of which revolve around the particular aspect that can vary). The three purposes are:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls250\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls250\adjustright {\pard\plain 
\fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls250\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls250\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {creational design patterns}}}{\pard\plain \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls250\pnrnot0
\pndec }\ls250\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {design patterns\: creational}}}{\b Creational}{: how an object can be created. This often involves isolating the details of object creation so your code isn\rquote t d}{e}{
pendent on what types of objects there are and thus doesn\rquote t have to be changed when you add a new type of object. The aforementioned }{\i Singleton}{ is classified as a creational pattern, and later in this chapter you\rquote ll see examples of }{
\i Factory Method}{ and }{\i Prototype}{.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls250\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls250\adjustright {\pard\plain 
\fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls250\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls250\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {design patterns\: structural}}}{\pard\plain \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls250\pnrnot0
\pndec }\ls250\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {structural design patterns}}}{\b Structural}{: designing objects to satisfy particular proje
ct constraints. These work with the way objects are connected with other objects to ensure that changes in the system don\rquote t require changes to those connections.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls250\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls250\adjustright {\pard\plain 
\fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls250\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls250\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {design patterns\: behavioral}}}{\pard\plain \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls250\pnrnot0
\pndec }\ls250\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {behavioral design patterns}}}{\b Behavioral}{: objects tha
t handle particular types of actions within a program. These encapsulate processes that you want to perform, such as interpreting a language, fulfilling a r}{e}{quest, moving through a sequence (as in an iterator), or i}{m}{
plementing an algorithm. This chapter contains examples of the }{\i Observer}{ and the }{\i Visitor}{ patterns.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {The }{\i Design Patterns}{ book has a section on each of its 23 patterns along with one or more examples for each, typically in C++ but sometimes in Smalltalk. This book will not repeat all the details of the pa}{t}{
terns shown in }{\i Design Patterns}{ since that book stands on its own and should be studied separately. The catalog and exa}{m}{
ples provided here are intended to rapidly give you a grasp of the patterns, so you can get a decent feel for what patterns are about and why they are so important.
\par [[ Describe different form of categorization, based on what you want to accomplish rather than the way the patterns look. More categories, but should result in easier-to-understand, faster selection ]]]
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc375545413}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018797}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776520}Features, idioms, patterns
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776520}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {How things have gotten confused; conflicting pattern descriptions, na\'efve \ldblquote patterns,\rdblquote 
 patterns are not trivial nor are they represented by features that are built into the language, nor are they things that you do almost all the time. Constructors and destructors, for example, could be called the \ldblquote 
guaranteed initialization and cleanup design pattern.\rdblquote  This is an important and essential idea, but it\rquote s built into the language.
\par Another example comes from various forms of aggregation. Aggregation is a c
ompletely fundamental principle in object-oriented programming: you make objects out of other objects [[ make reference to basic tenets of OO ]]. Yet sometimes this idea is classified as a pattern, which tends to confuse the issue. This is unfortunate bec
ause it pollutes the idea of the design pattern and suggest that anything that surprises you the first time you see it should be a design pattern.
\par Another misguided example is found in the Java language; the designers of the \ldblquote JavaBeans\rdblquote  specification decided to refer to a simple naming convention as a design pattern (you say }{\b getInfo(\~)}{
 for a member function that returns an }{\b Info}{ property and }{\b setInfo(\~)}{ for one that changes the internal }{\b Info}{ property; the use of the \ldblquote get\rdblquote  and \ldblquote set\rdblquote  strings is what they decided
 constituted calling it a design pattern).
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776521}Basic complexity hiding{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776521}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You\rquote ll often find that messy code can be cleaned up by putting it inside a class. This is more than fastidiousness \endash 
 if nothing else, it aids readability and therefore maintainability, and it can often lead to reusability.
\par Simple Veneer (fa\'e7ade, Adapter (existing system), Bridge (designed in), 
\par Hiding types (polymorphism, iterators, proxy)
\par Hiding connections (mediator,)
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776522}Factories: encapsulating object creation{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776522}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you discover that you need
 to add new types to a system, the most sensible first step to take is to use polymorphism to create a common interface to those new types, thus separating the rest of the code in your system from the knowledge of the specific types that you are adding. T
his way, new types may be added without disturbing exising code \'85
 or so it seems. At first it would appear that the only place you need to change the code in such a design is the place where you inherit a new type, but this is not quite true. You must still
 create an object of your new type, and at the point of creation you must specify the exact constructor to use. Thus, if the code that creates objects is distributed throughout your application, you have the same problem when adding new types \endash 
 you must still chase down all the points of your code where it type matters. It happens to be the }{\i creation}{ of the type that matters in this case rather than the }{\i use}{
 of the type (which is taken care of by polymorphism), but the effect is the same: adding a new type can cause problems.
\par The solution is to force the creation of objects to occur through a common }{\i factory}{ rather than to allow the creational code to be spread throughout your system. If all the code in your program must go through this factory whenever it need
s to create one of your objects, then all you must do when you add a new object is to modify the factory. 
\par As an example, let\rquote s revisit the }{\b Shape}{ system. One approach is to make the factory a }{\b static}{ method of the base class:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:ShapeFactory1.cpp
\par #include "../purge.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <exception>
\par #include <vector>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Shape \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void draw() = 0;
\par   virtual void erase() = 0;
\par   virtual ~Shape() \{\}
\par   class BadShapeCreation : public exception \{
\par     string reason;
\par   public:
\par     BadShapeCreation(string type) \{
\par       reason = "Cannot create type " + type;
\par     \}
\par     const char *what() const \{ 
\par       return reason.c_str(); 
\par     \}
\par   \};
\par   static Shape* factory(string type) 
\par     throw(BadShapeCreation);
\par \};
\par 
\par class Circle : public Shape \{
\par   Circle() \{\} // Private constructor
\par   friend class Shape;
\par public:
\par   void draw() \{ cout << "Circle::draw\\n"; \}
\par   void erase() \{ cout << "Circle::erase\\n"; \}
\par   ~Circle() \{ cout << "Circle::~Circle\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Square : public Shape \{
\par   Square() \{\}
\par   friend class Shape;
\par public:
\par   void draw() \{ cout << "Square::draw\\n"; \}
\par   void erase() \{ cout << "Square::erase\\n"; \}
\par   ~Square() \{ cout << "Square::~Square\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par Shape* Shape::factory(string type) 
\par   throw(Shape::BadShapeCreation) \{
\par   if(type == "Circle") return new Circle();
\par   if(type == "Square") return new Square();
\par   throw BadShapeCreation(type);
\par \}
\par 
\par char* shlist[] = \{ "Circle", "Square", "Square",
\par   "Circle", "Circle", "Circle", "Square", "" \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<Shape*> shapes;
\par   try \{
\par     for(char** cp = shlist; **cp; cp++)
\par       shapes.push_back(Shape::factory(*cp));
\par   \} catch(Shape::BadShapeCreation e) \{
\par     cout << e.what() << endl;
\par     return 1;
\par   \}
\par   for(int i = 0; i < shapes.size(); i++) \{
\par     shapes[i]->draw();
\par     shapes[i]->erase();
\par   \}
\par   purge(shapes);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b factory(\~)}{ takes an argument that allows it to determine what type of  }{\b Shape}{ to create; it happens to be a }{\b string}{ in this case but it could be any set of data. The }{\b 
factory(\~)}{ is now the only other code in the system that needs to be changed when a new type of }{\b Shape }{
is added (the initialization data for the objects will presumably come from somewhere outside the system, and not be a hard-coded array as in the above example).
\par To ensure that the creation can only happen in the }{\b factory(\~)}{, the constructors for the specific types of }{\b Shape}{ are made }{\b private}{, and }{\b Shape}{ is declared a }{\b friend}{ so that }{\b factory(\~)}{
 has access to the constructors (you could also declare only }{\b Shape::factory(\~)}{ to be a }{\b friend}{, but it seems reasonably harmless to declare the entire base class as a }{\b friend}{).
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776523}Polymorphic factories{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776523}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b static factory(\~)}{ method in the previous example forces all the creation operations to be focused in one spot, to that\rquote s the only place you need to 
change the code. This is certainly a reasonable solution, as it throws a box around the process of creating objects. However, the }{\i Design Patterns}{ book emphasizes that the reason for the }{\i Factory Method}{
 pattern is so that different types of factories can be subclassed from the basic factory (the above design is mentioned as a special case). However, the book does not provide an example, but instead just repeats the example used for the }{\i 
Abstract Factory}{. Here is }{\b ShapeFactory1.cpp}{ modified so the factory methods are in a separate class as virtual functions:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:ShapeFactory2.cpp
\par // Polymorphic factory methods
\par #include "../purge.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <exception>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <map>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Shape \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void draw() = 0;
\par   virtual void erase() = 0;
\par   virtual ~Shape() \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class ShapeFactory \{
\par   virtual Shape* create() = 0;
\par   static map<string, ShapeFactory*> factories;
\par public:
\par   virtual ~ShapeFactory() \{\}
\par   friend class ShapeFactoryInizializer;
\par   class BadShapeCreation : public exception \{
\par     string reason;
\par   public:
\par     BadShapeCreation(string type) \{
\par       reason = "Cannot create type " + type;
\par     \}
\par     const char *what() const \{ 
\par       return reason.c_str(); 
\par     \}
\par   \};
\par   static Shape* 
\par   createShape(string id) throw(BadShapeCreation)\{
\par     if(factories.find(id) != factories.end())
\par       return factories[id]->create();
\par     else
\par       throw BadShapeCreation(id);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Define the static object:
\par map<string, ShapeFactory*> 
\par   ShapeFactory::factories;
\par 
\par class Circle : public Shape \{
\par   Circle() \{\} // Private constructor
\par public:
\par   void draw() \{ cout << "Circle::draw\\n"; \}
\par   void erase() \{ cout << "Circle::erase\\n"; \}
\par   ~Circle() \{ cout << "Circle::~Circle\\n"; \}
\par   class Factory;
\par   friend class Factory;
\par   class Factory : public ShapeFactory \{
\par   public:
\par     Shape* create() \{ return new Circle; \}
\par   \};
\par \};
\par 
\par class Square : public Shape \{
\par   Square() \{\}
\par public:
\par   void draw() \{ cout << "Square::draw\\n"; \}
\par   void erase() \{ cout << "Square::erase\\n"; \}
\par   ~Square() \{ cout << "Square::~Square\\n"; \}
\par   class Factory;
\par   friend class Factory;
\par   class Factory : public ShapeFactory \{
\par   public:
\par     Shape* create() \{ return new Square; \}
\par   \};
\par \};
\par 
\par // Singleton to initialize the ShapeFactory:
\par class ShapeFactoryInizializer \{
\par   static ShapeFactoryInizializer si;
\par   ShapeFactoryInizializer() \{
\par     ShapeFactory::factories["Circle"] =
\par       new Circle::Factory;
\par     ShapeFactory::factories["Square"] =
\par       new Square::Factory;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Static member definition:
\par ShapeFactoryInizializer
\par   ShapeFactoryInizializer::si;
\par 
\par char* shlist[] = \{ "Circle", "Square", "Square",
\par   "Circle", "Circle", "Circle", "Square", "" \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<Shape*> shapes;
\par   try \{
\par     for(char** cp = shlist; **cp; cp++)
\par       shapes.push_back(
\par         ShapeFactory::createShape(*cp));
\par   \} catch(ShapeFactory::BadShapeCreation e) \{
\par     cout << e.what() << endl;
\par     return 1;
\par   \}
\par   for(int i = 0; i < shapes.size(); i++) \{
\par     shapes[i]->draw();
\par     shapes[i]->erase();
\par   \}
\par   purge(shapes);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now the factory method appears in its own class, }{\b ShapeFactory}{, as the }{\b virtual create(\~)}{. This is a }{\b private}{
 method which means it cannot be called directly, but it can be overridden. The subclasses of }{\b Shape}{ must each create their own subclasses of }{\b ShapeFactory}{ and override the }{\b shape(\~)}{
 method to create an object of their own type. The actual creation of shapes is performed by calling }{\b ShapeFactory::createShape(\~)}{, which is a static method that uses the }{\b map}{ in }{\b ShapeFactory}{ to find the appropriate factory object
 based on an identifier that you pass it. The factory is immediately used to create the shape object, but you could imagine a more complex problem where the appropriate factory object is returned and then used by the caller to create an object in a more s
ophisticated way. However, it seems that much of the time you don\rquote t need the intricacies of the polymorphic factory method, and a single static method in the base class (as shown in }{\b ShapeFactory1.cpp}{) will work fine.
\par Notice that the }{\b ShapeFactory}{ must be initialized by loading its }{\b map}{ with factory objects, which takes place in the singleton }{\b ShapeFactoryInizializer}{
. So to add a new type to this design you must inherit the type, create a factory, and modify }{\b ShapeFactoryInizializer}{ so that an instance of your factory is inserted in the map. This extra complexity again suggests the use of a }{\b static}{
 factory method if you don\rquote t need to create individual factory objects.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305593322}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc305628794}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312374165}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776524}Abstract factories
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776524}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\i Abstract Factory}{ pattern looks like the factory objects we\rquote ve seen previous
ly, with not one but several factory methods. Each of the factory methods creates a different kind of object. The idea is that at the point of creation of the factory object, you decide how all the objects created by that factory will be used. The example
 given in }{\i Design Patterns}{ implements portability across various graphical user interfaces (GUIs): you create a factory object appropriate to the GUI that you\rquote 
re working with, and from then on when you ask it for a menu, button, slider, etc. it will automatically create the appropriate version of that item for the GUI. Thus you\rquote 
re able to isolate, in one place, the effect of changing from one GUI to another.
\par As another example suppose you are creating a general-purpose gaming environment and you want to be able to support different types of games. Here\rquote s how it might look using an abstract factory:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:AbstractFactory.cpp
\par // A gaming environment
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Obstacle \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void action() = 0;
\par \};
\par 
\par class Player \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void interactWith(Obstacle*) = 0;
\par \};
\par 
\par class Kitty: public Player \{
\par   virtual void interactWith(Obstacle* ob) \{
\par     cout << "Kitty has encountered a ";
\par     ob->action();
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class KungFuGuy: public Player \{
\par   virtual void interactWith(Obstacle* ob) \{
\par     cout << "KungFuGuy now battles against a ";
\par     ob->action();
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Puzzle: public Obstacle \{
\par public:
\par   void action() \{ cout << "Puzzle\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class NastyWeapon: public Obstacle \{
\par public:
\par   void action() \{ cout << "NastyWeapon\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // The abstract factory:
\par class GameElementFactory \{
\par public:
\par   virtual Player* makePlayer() = 0;
\par   virtual Obstacle* makeObstacle() = 0;
\par \};
\par 
\par // Concrete factories:
\par class KittiesAndPuzzles : 
\par   public GameElementFactory \{
\par public:
\par   virtual Player* makePlayer() \{ 
\par     return new Kitty;
\par   \}
\par   virtual Obstacle* makeObstacle() \{
\par     return new Puzzle;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class KillAndDismember : 
\par   public GameElementFactory \{
\par public:
\par   virtual Player* makePlayer() \{ 
\par     return new KungFuGuy;
\par   \}
\par   virtual Obstacle* makeObstacle() \{
\par     return new NastyWeapon;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class GameEnvironment \{
\par   GameElementFactory* gef;
\par   Player* p;
\par   Obstacle* ob;
\par public:
\par   GameEnvironment(GameElementFactory* factory) :
\par     gef(factory), p(factory->makePlayer()), 
\par     ob(factory->makeObstacle()) \{\}
\par   void play() \{
\par     p->interactWith(ob);
\par   \}
\par   ~GameEnvironment() \{
\par     delete p;
\par     delete ob;
\par     delete gef;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   GameEnvironment 
\par     g1(new KittiesAndPuzzles),
\par     g2(new KillAndDismember);
\par   g1.play();
\par   g2.play();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In this environment, }{\b Player}{ objects interact with }{\b Obstacle}{ objects, but there are different types of players and obstacles depending on what kind of game you\rquote 
re playing. You determine the kind of game by choosing a particular }{\b GameElementFactory}{, and then the }{\b GameEnvironment}{ controls the setup and play of the game. In this example, the setup and play is very simple, but those activities (the }{\i 
initial conditions}{ and the }{\i state change}{) can determine much of the game\rquote s outcome. Here, }{\b GameEnvironment}{ is not designed to be inherited, although it could very possibly make sense to do that.
\par This also contains examples of }{\i Double Dispatching}{ and the }{\i Factory Method}{, both of which will be explained later.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776525}Virtual constructors}{\cs29\b\v\f17\fs16\lang1033 {\*\bkmkend _Toc305593322}{\*\bkmkend _Toc305628794}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374165}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776525}{\*\atnid BE}{\*\atnauthor Bruce Eckel}\chatn {\*\annotation \pard\plain \s31\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst {PAGE \\# "'Page: '#'
\line '"}{\cs29\fs16   }}{\fldrslt }}{\cs29\fs16 \chatn }{Show simpler version of virtual constructor scheme, letting the user create the object with new. Probably make constructor for objects private and use a maker function to for
ce all objects on the heap.}}}{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {One of the primary goals of using a factory is so that }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {simulating virtual constructors}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {virtual\: simulating virtual constructors}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: simulating virtual constructors}}}{you can organize your code so you don\rquote 
t have to select an exact type of constructor when creating an object. That is, you can say, \ldblquote I don\rquote t know precisely what type of object you are, but here\rquote s the information: Create yourself.\rdblquote  
\par In addition, during a constructor call the virtual mechanism does not operate (early binding occurs). Sometimes this is awkward. For example, in the }{\b Shape }{program it seems logical that inside the constructor for a }{\b Shape}{
 object, you would want to set everything up and then }{\b draw(\~)}{ the }{\b Shape}{. }{\b draw(\~)}{ should be a virtual function, a message to the }{\b Shape}{ that it should draw itsel
f appropriately, depending on whether it is a circle, square, line, and so on. However, this doesn\rquote t work inside the constructor, for the reasons given in Chapter XX: Virtual functions}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
virtual\: virtual functions inside constructors}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: virtual functions inside constructors}}}{ resolve to the \ldblquote local\rdblquote  function bodies when called in constructors.

\par If you want to be able to call a virtual function inside the constructor and have it do the right thing, you must use a technique to }{\i simulate}{ a virtual constructor (which is a variation of the }{\i Factory Method}{
). This is a conundrum. Remember the idea of a virtual function is that you send a message to an object and let the object figure out the right thing to do. But a constructor builds an object. So a virtual constructor would be like saying, \ldblquote 
I don\rquote t know exactly what type of object you are, but build yourself anyway.\rdblquote  In an ordinary constructor, the compiler must know which VTABLE }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {VTABLE}}}{
address to bind to the VPTR}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {VPTR}}}{, and if it existed, a virtual constructor couldn\rquote t do this because it doesn\rquote 
t know all the type information at compile-time. It makes sense that a constructor can\rquote t be virtual because it is the one function that absolutely must know everything about the type of the object.
\par And yet there are times when you want something approximating the behavior of a virtual constructor.
\par In the }{\b Shape}{ example, it would be nice to hand the }{\b Shape}{ constructor some specific information in the argument list and let the constructor create a specific type of }{\b Shape}{ (a }{\b Circle}{, }{\b Square}{
) with no further intervention. Ordinarily, you\rquote d have to make an explicit call to the }{\b Circle}{, }{\b Square}{ constructor yourself.
\par Coplien}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{James O. Coplien}{\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {Coplien, James}}}{, }{\i Advanced C++ Programming Styles and Idioms}{, Addison-Wesley, 1992.}}}{ calls his solution to this problem \ldblquote envelope and letter classes.\rdblquote  The \ldblquote envelope\rdblquote 
 class is the base class, a shell that contains a pointer to an object of the base class. The constructor for the \ldblquote envelope\rdblquote 
 determines (at runtime, when the constructor is called, not at compile-time, when the type checking is normally done) what specific type to m
ake, then creates an object of that specific type (on the heap) and assigns the object to its pointer. All the function calls are then handled by the base class through its pointer. So the base class is acting as a proxy for the derived class:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:VirtualConstructor.cpp
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <exception>
\par #include <vector>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Shape \{
\par   Shape* s;
\par   // Prevent copy-construction & operator=
\par   Shape(Shape&);
\par   Shape operator=(Shape&);
\par protected:
\par   Shape() \{ s = 0; \};
\par public:
\par   virtual void draw() \{ s->draw(); \}
\par   virtual void erase() \{ s->erase(); \}
\par   virtual void test() \{ s->test(); \};
\par   virtual ~Shape() \{
\par     cout << "~Shape\\n";
\par     if(s) \{
\par       cout << "Making virtual call: ";
\par       s->erase(); // Virtual call
\par     \}
\par     cout << "delete s: ";
\par     delete s; // The polymorphic deletion
\par   \}
\par   class BadShapeCreation : public exception \{
\par     string reason;
\par   public:
\par     BadShapeCreation(string type) \{
\par       reason = "Cannot create type " + type;
\par     \}
\par     const char *what() const \{ 
\par       return reason.c_str(); 
\par     \}
\par   \};
\par   Shape(string type) throw(BadShapeCreation);
\par \};
\par 
\par class Circle : public Shape \{
\par   Circle(Circle&);
\par   Circle operator=(Circle&);
\par   Circle() \{\} // Private constructor
\par   friend class Shape;
\par public:
\par   void draw() \{ cout << "Circle::draw\\n"; \}
\par   void erase() \{ cout << "Circle::erase\\n"; \}
\par   void test() \{ draw(); \}
\par   ~Circle() \{ cout << "Circle::~Circle\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Square : public Shape \{
\par   Square(Square&);
\par   Square operator=(Square&);
\par   Square() \{\}
\par   friend class Shape;
\par public:
\par   void draw() \{ cout << "Square::draw\\n"; \}
\par   void erase() \{ cout << "Square::erase\\n"; \}
\par   void test() \{ draw(); \}
\par   ~Square() \{ cout << "Square::~Square\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par Shape::Shape(string type) 
\par   throw(Shape::BadShapeCreation) \{
\par   if(type == "Circle") 
\par     s = new Circle();
\par   else if(type == "Square")
\par     s = new Square();
\par   else throw BadShapeCreation(type);
\par   draw();  // Virtual call in the constructor
\par \}
\par 
\par char* shlist[] = \{ "Circle", "Square", "Square",
\par   "Circle", "Circle", "Circle", "Square", "" \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<Shape*> shapes;
\par   cout << "virtual constructor calls:" << endl;
\par   try \{
\par     for(char** cp = shlist; **cp; cp++)
\par       shapes.push_back(new Shape(*cp));
\par   \} catch(Shape::BadShapeCreation e) \{
\par     cout << e.what() << endl;
\par     return 1;
\par   \}
\par   for(int i = 0; i < shapes.size(); i++) \{
\par     shapes[i]->draw();
\par     cout << "test\\n";
\par     shapes[i]->test();
\par     cout << "end test\\n";
\par     shapes[i]->erase();
\par   \}
\par   Shape c("Circle"); // Create on the stack
\par   cout << "destructor calls:" << endl;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < shapes.size(); i++) \{
\par     delete shapes[i];
\par     cout << "\\n------------\\n";
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The base class }{\b Shape}{ contains a pointer to an object of type }{\b Shape}{ as its only data member. When you build a \ldblquote virtual constructor\rdblquote 
 scheme, you must exercise special care to ensure this pointer is always initialized to a live object.
\par Each time you derive a new subtype from }{\b Shape}{, you must go back and add the creation for that type in one place, inside the \ldblquote virtual constructor\rdblquote  in the }{\b Shape}{
 base class. This is not too onerous a task, but the disadvantage is you now have a dependency between the }{\b Shape}{ class and all classes derived from it (a reasonable trade-off, it seems). Also, because it is a proxy, the base-class interface is 
truly the only thing the user sees.
\par In this example, the information you must hand the virtual constructor about what type to create is very explicit: It\rquote s a }{\b string}{ that names the type. However, your scheme may use other information \endash  for example, in a par
ser the output of the scanner may be handed to the virtual constructor, which then uses that information to determine which token to create.
\par The virtual constructor }{\b Shape(type)}{ can only be declared inside the class; it cannot be defined until after all the derived classes have been declared. However, the default constructor}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
default\: constructor}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {constructor\: default constructor}}}{ can be defined inside }{\b class Shape}{, but it should be made }{\b protected}{ so temporary }{\b Shape}{
 objects cannot be created. This default constructor is only called by the constructors of derived-class objects. You are forced to explicitly create a default constructor because the compiler will create one for you automatically only if there are }{\i 
no}{ constructors defined. Because you must define }{\b Shape(type)}{, you must also define }{\b Shape(\~)}{.
\par The default constructor in this scheme has at least one very important chore \endash  it must set the value of the }{\b s}{ pointer to zero. This sounds strange at first, but remember that the default constructor will b
e called as part of the construction of the }{\i actual object}{ \endash  in Coplien\rquote s terms, the \ldblquote letter,\rdblquote  not the \ldblquote envelope.\rdblquote  However, the \ldblquote letter\rdblquote  is derived from the \ldblquote 
envelope,\rdblquote  so it also inherits the data member }{\b s}{. In the \ldblquote envelope,\rdblquote  }{\b s}{ is important because it points to the actual object, but in the \ldblquote letter,\rdblquote  }{\b s}{
 is simply excess baggage. Even excess baggage should be initialized, however, and if }{\b s}{ is not set to zero by the default constructor called for the \ldblquote letter,\rdblquote  bad things happen (as you\rquote ll see later).
\par The virtual constructor takes as its argument information that completely determines the type of the object. Notice, though, that this type information isn\rquote 
t read and acted upon until runtime, whereas normally the compiler must know the exact type at compile-time (one other reason this system effectively imitates virtual constructors).
\par Inside the virtual constructor there\rquote s a }{\b switch}{ statement that uses the argument to construct the actual (\ldblquote letter\rdblquote ) object, which is then assigned to the pointer inside the \ldblquote envelope.\rdblquote 
 At that point, the construction of the \ldblquote letter\rdblquote  has been completed, so any virtual calls will be properly directed.
\par As an example, consider the call to }{\b draw(\~)}{ inside the virtual constructor. If you trace this call (either by hand or with a debugger), you can see that it starts in the }{\b draw(\~)}{ function in the base class, }{\b Shape}{
. This function calls }{\b draw(\~)}{ for the \ldblquote envelope\rdblquote  }{\b s}{ pointer to its \ldblquote letter.\rdblquote  All types derived from }{\b Shape}{ share the same interface, so this virtual call is properly executed, even thou
gh it seems to be in the constructor. (Actually, the constructor for the \ldblquote letter\rdblquote  has already completed.) As long as all virtual calls in the base class simply make calls to identical virtual function through the pointer to the 
\ldblquote letter,\rdblquote  the system operates properly.
\par To understand how it works, consider the code in }{\b main(\~)}{. To fill the }{\b vector shapes}{, \ldblquote virtual constructor\rdblquote  calls are made to }{\b Shape}{
. Ordinarily in a situation like this, you would call the constructor for the actual type, and the VPTR for that type would be installed in the object. Here, however, the VPTR used in each case is the one for }{\b Shape}{, not the one for the specific }{
\b Circle}{, }{\b Square}{, or }{\b Triangle}{.
\par In the }{\b for}{ loop where the }{\b draw(\~)}{ and }{\b erase(\~) }{functions are called for each }{\b Shape}{, the virtual function call resolves, through the VPTR, to the corresponding type. However, this is }{\b Shape}{
 in each case. In fact, you might wonder why }{\b draw(\~)}{ and }{\b erase(\~) }{were made }{\b virtual}{ at all. The reason shows up in the next step: The base-class version of }{\b draw(\~)}{ makes a call, through the \ldblquote letter\rdblquote 
 pointer }{\b s}{, to the }{\b virtual}{ function }{\b draw(\~)}{ for the \ldblquote letter.\rdblquote  This time the call resolves to the actual type of the object, not just the base class }{\b Shape}{
. Thus the runtime cost of using virtual constructors is one more virtual call every time you make a virtual function call.
\par In order to create any function that is overridden, such as }{\b draw(\~)}{, }{\b erase(\~)}{ or }{\b test(\~)}{, you must proxy all calls to the }{\b s}{ pointer in the base class implementation, as shown above. This is because, whe
n the call is made, the call to the envelope\rquote s member function will resolve as being to }{\b Shape}{, and not to a derived type of }{\b Shape}{. Only when you make the proxy call to }{\b s}{ will the virtual behavior take place. In }{\b main(\~)}{
, you can see that everything works correctly, even when calls are made inside constructors and destructors.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Destructor operation
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The activities of destruction in this scheme are also tricky. To understand, let\rquote s verbally walk through what happens when you call }{\b delete}{ for a pointer to a }{\b Shape}{ object \endash 
 specifically, a }{\b Square}{ \endash  created on the heap. (This is more complicated than an object created on the stack.) This will be a }{\b delete}{ through the polymorphic interface, as in the statement }{\b delete shapes[i]}{ in }{\b main(\~)}{.

\par The type of the pointer }{\b shapes[i]}{ is of the base class }{\b Shape}{, so the compiler makes the call through }{\b Shape}{. Normally, you might say that it\rquote s a virtual call, so }{\b Square}{\rquote 
s destructor will be called. But with the virtual constructor scheme, the compiler is creating actual }{\b Shape}{ objects, even though the constructor initializes the letter pointer to a specific type of }{\b Shape}{. The virtual mechanism }{\i is}{
 used, but the VPTR inside the }{\b Shape}{ object is }{\b Shape}{\rquote s VPTR, not }{\b Square}{\rquote s. This resolves to }{\b Shape}{\rquote s destructor, which calls }{\b delete}{ for the letter pointer }{\b s}{, which actually points to a }{\b 
Square}{ object. This is again a virtual call, but this time it resolves to }{\b Square}{\rquote s destructor.
\par With a destructor, however, C++ guarantees, via the compiler, that all destructors in the hierarchy are called. }{\b Square}{\rquote s de
structor is called first, followed by any intermediate destructors, in order, until finally the base-class destructor is called. This base-class destructor has code that says }{\b delete s}{. When this destructor was called originally, it was for the 
\ldblquote envelope\rdblquote  }{\b s}{, but now it\rquote s for the \ldblquote letter\rdblquote  }{\b s}{, which is there because the \ldblquote letter\rdblquote  was inherited from the \ldblquote envelope,\rdblquote 
 and not because it contains anything. So }{\i this}{ call to }{\b delete}{ should do nothing.
\par The solution to the problem is to make the \ldblquote letter\rdblquote  }{\b s}{ pointer zero. Then when the \ldblquote letter\rdblquote  base-class destructor is called, you get }{\b delete 0}{
, which by definition does nothing. Because the default constructor is protected, it will be called }{\i only}{ during the construction of a \ldblquote letter,\rdblquote  so that\rquote s the only situation where }{\b s}{ is set to zero.
\par Your most common tool for hiding construction will probably be ordinary factory methods rather than the more complex approaches. The idea of adding new types with minimal effect on the rest of the system will be further explored later in this chapter.

\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776526}Callbacks{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776526}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Decoupling code behavior
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776527}Functor/Command{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776527}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776528}Strategy{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776528}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776529}Observer{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776529}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Like the other forms of callback, this contains a hook point where you can change code. The difference is in the observer\rquote s completely dynamic nature. It is often used for the spec
ific case of changes based on other object\rquote s change of state, but is also the basis of event management. Anytime you want to decouple the source of the call from the called code in a completely dynamic way.
\par The }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {design patterns\: observer}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {observer design pattern}}}{observer pattern solves a fairly common problem: What if a group of o}
{b}{jects needs to update themselves when some other object changes state? This can be seen in the \ldblquote model-view\rdblquote  aspect of Smalltalk\rquote s MVC (model-view-controller), or the almost-equivalent \ldblquote Document-View Architecture.
\rdblquote  Suppose that you have some data (the \ldblquote document\rdblquote ) and more than one view, say a plot and a textual view. When you change the data, the two views must know to update themselves, and that\rquote s what the observer fa
cilitates. 
\par There are two types of objects used to implement the observer pattern in the following code. The }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Observable}}}{\b Observable}{ class keeps track of everybody who wants to be i}{n}{
formed when a change happens, whether the \ldblquote state\rdblquote  has changed or not. When someone says \ldblquote OK, everybody should check and potentially update themselves,\rdblquote  the }{\b Observable}{ class performs this task by calling the }
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {notifyObservers(\~)}}}{\b notifyObservers(\~)}{ member function for each observer on the list. The }{\b notifyObservers(\~)}{ member function is part of the base class }{\b Observable}{.
\par There are actually two \ldblquote things that change\rdblquote  in the observer pattern: the quantity of observing objects and the way an update occurs. That is, the o}{b}{server pattern allows you to modify both of these without affecting the su}{r}{
rounding code.
\par There are a number of ways to implement the observer pattern, but the code shown here will create a framework from which you can build your own observer code, following the example. First, this interface describes what an observer looks like:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:Observer.h
\par // The Observer interface
\par #ifndef OBSERVER_H
\par #define OBSERVER_H
\par 
\par class Observable;
\par class Argument \{\};
\par 
\par class Observer \{
\par public:
\par   // Called by the observed object, whenever 
\par   // the observed object is changed:
\par   virtual void 
\par   update(Observable* o, Argument * arg) = 0;
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // OBSERVER_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Since }{\b Observer}{ interacts with }{\b Observable}{ in this approach, }{\b Observable}{ must be declared first. In addition, the }{\b Argument}{ class is empty and only acts as a base class for
 any type of argument you wish to pass during an update. If you want, you can simply pass the extra argument as a }{\b void*}{; you\rquote ll have to downcast in either case but some folks find }{\b void*}{ objectionable.
\par }{\b Observer}{ is an \ldblquote interface\rdblquote  class that only has one member function, }{\b update(\~)}{. This function is called by the object that\rquote s being observed, when that object decides its time to update all it\rquote 
s observers. The arguments are optional; you could have an }{\b update(\~)}{ with no arguments and that would still fit the observer pattern; however this is more general \endash 
 it allows the observed object to pass the object that caused the update (since an }{\b Observer }{may be registered with more than one observed object) and any extra information if that\rquote s helpful, rather than forcing the }{\b Observer}{
 object to hunt around to see who is updating and to fetch any other information it needs.
\par The \ldblquote observed object\rdblquote  that decides when and how to do the updating will be called the }{\b Observable}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:Observable.h
\par // The Observable class
\par #ifndef OBSERVABLE_H
\par #define OBSERVABLE_H
\par #include "Observer.h"
\par #include <set>
\par 
\par class Observable \{
\par   bool changed;
\par   std::set<Observer*> observers;
\par protected:
\par   virtual void setChanged() \{ changed = true; \}
\par   virtual void clearChanged()\{ changed = false; \}
\par public:
\par   virtual void addObserver(Observer& o) \{
\par     observers.insert(&o);
\par   \}
\par   virtual void deleteObserver(Observer& o) \{
\par     observers.erase(&o);
\par   \}
\par   virtual void deleteObservers() \{
\par     observers.clear();
\par   \}
\par   virtual int countObservers() \{
\par     return observers.size();
\par   \}
\par   virtual bool hasChanged() \{ return changed; \}
\par   // If this object has changed, notify all
\par   // of its observers:
\par   virtual void notifyObservers(Argument* arg=0) \{
\par     if(!hasChanged()) return;
\par     clearChanged(); // Not "changed" anymore
\par     std::set<Observer*>::iterator it;
\par     for(it = observers.begin(); 
\par       it != observers.end(); it++)
\par       (*it)->update(this, arg);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // OBSERVABLE_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Again, the design here is more elaborate than is necessary; as long as there\rquote s a way to register an }{\b Observer}{ with an }{\b Observable}{ and for the }{\b Observable}{ to update its }{\b 
Observer}{s, the set of member functions doesn\rquote t matter. However, this design is intended to be reusable (it was lifted from the design used in the Java standard library). As
 mentioned elsewhere in the book, there is no support for multithreading in the Standard C++ libraries, so this design would need to be modified in a multithreaded environment.
\par }{\b Observable}{ has a flag to indicate whether it\rquote s been changed. In a simpler design, there would be no flag; if something happened, everyone would be notified. The flag allows you to wait, and only notify the }{\b Observer}{
s when you decide the time is right. Notice, however, that the control of the flag\rquote s state is }{\b protected}{, so that only an inheritor can decide what constitutes a change, and not the end user of the resulting derived }{\b Observer}{ class.

\par The collection of }{\b Observer}{ objects is kept in a }{\b set<Observer*>}{ to prevent duplicates; the }{\b set insert(\~)}{, }{\b erase(\~)}{, }{\b clear(\~)}{ and }{\b size(\~)}{ functions are exposed to allow }{\b Observer}{
s to be added and removed at any time, thus providing runtime flexibility.
\par Most of the work is done in }{\b notifyObservers(\~)}{. If the }{\b changed}{ flag has not been set, this does nothing. Otherwise, it first clears the }{\b changed}{ flag so repeated calls to }{\b notifyObservers(\~)}{ won\rquote 
t waste time. This is done before notifying the observers in case the calls to }{\b update(\~)}{ do anything that causes a change back to this }{\b Observable}{ object. Then it moves through the }{\b set}{ and calls back to the }{\b update(\~)}{
 member function of each }{\b Observer}{. 
\par At first it may appear that you can use an ordinary }{\b Observable}{ object to manage the updates. But this doesn\rquote t work; to get an effect, you }{\i must}{ inherit from }{\b Obser}{\b v}{\b able}{ and somewhere in your derived-class code call }
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {setChanged(\~)}}}{\b setChanged(\~)}{. This is the member function that sets the \ldblquote changed\rdblquote  flag, which means that when you call }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {notifyObservers(\~)}}}{\b notifyObservers(\~)}{ all of the observers will, in fact, get notified. }{\i Where}{ you call }{\b setChanged(\~)}{ depends on the logic of your program.
\par Now we encounter a dilemma. An object that should notify its observers about things that happen to it \endash  events or changes in state \endash  might have more than one such item of interest. For example, if you\rquote re dealing with a graphical u
ser interface (GUI) item \endash  a button, say \endash  the items of interest might be the mouse clicked the button, the mouse moved over the button, and (for some reason) the button changed its color. So we\rquote 
d like to be able to report all of these events to different observers, each of which is interested in a different type of event.
\par The problem is that we would normally reach for multiple inheritance in such a situation: \ldblquote I\rquote ll inherit from }{\b Observable}{ to deal with mouse clicks, and I\rquote ll \'85 er \'85 inherit from }{\b Observable}{ 
to deal with mouse-overs, and, well, \'85 hmm, that doesn\rquote t work.\rdblquote 
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {The \ldblquote interface\rdblquote  idiom
\par The \ldblquote inner class\rdblquote  idiom
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here\rquote s a situation where we do actually need to (in effect) upcast to more than one type, but in this case we need to provide several }{\i different}{ im
plementations of the same base type. The solution is something I\rquote ve lifted from Java, which takes C++\rquote s nested class one step further. Java has a built-in feature called }{\i inner classes}{, which look like C++\rquote 
s nested classes, but they do two other things:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls248\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls248\adjustright {A J
ava inner class automatically has access to the private elements of the class it is nested within.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls248\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls248\adjustright {
An object of a Java inner class automatically grabs the \ldblquote this\rdblquote  to the outer class object it was created within. In Java, the \ldblquote outer this\rdblquote  is implicitly dereferenced whenever you name an element of the outer class.

\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {[[ Insert the definition of a closure ]]. So to implement the inner class idiom in C++, we must do these things by hand. Here\rquote s an example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:InnerClassIdiom.cpp
\par // Example of the "inner class" idiom
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Poingable \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void poing() = 0;
\par \};
\par 
\par void callPoing(Poingable& p) \{
\par   p.poing();
\par \}
\par 
\par class Bingable \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void bing() = 0;
\par \};
\par 
\par void callBing(Bingable& b) \{
\par   b.bing();
\par \}
\par 
\par class Outer \{
\par   string name;
\par   // Define one inner class:
\par   class Inner1;
\par   friend class Outer::Inner1;
\par   class Inner1 : public Poingable \{
\par     Outer* parent;
\par   public:
\par     Inner1(Outer* p) : parent(p) \{\}
\par     void poing() \{
\par       cout << "poing called for "
\par         << parent->name << endl;
\par       // Accesses data in the outer class object
\par     \}
\par   \} inner1;
\par   // Define a second inner class:
\par   class Inner2;
\par   friend class Outer::Inner2;
\par   class Inner2 : public Bingable \{
\par     Outer* parent;
\par   public:
\par     Inner2(Outer* p) : parent(p) \{\}
\par     void bing() \{
\par       cout << "bing called for "
\par         << parent->name << endl;
\par     \}
\par   \} inner2;
\par public:
\par   Outer(const string& nm) : name(nm), 
\par     inner1(this), inner2(this) \{\}
\par   // Return reference to interfaces
\par   //  implemented by the inner classes:
\par   operator Poingable&() \{ return inner1; \}
\par   operator Bingable&() \{ return inner2; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Outer x("Ping Pong");
\par   // Like upcasting to multiple base types!:
\par   callPoing(x);
\par   callBing(x);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The example begins with the }{\b Poingable}{ and }{\b Bingable }{interfaces, each of which contain a single member function. The services provided by }{\b callPoing(\~)}{ and }{\b callBing(\~) }{
require that the object they receive implement the }{\b Poingable}{ and }{\b Bingable }{interfaces, respectively, but they put no other requirements on that object so as to maximize the flexibility of using }{\b callPoing(\~)}{ and }{\b callBing(\~)}{
. Note the lack of }{\b virtual}{ destructors in either interface \endash  the intent is that you never perform object destruction via the interface.
\par }{\b Outer}{ contains some private data (}{\b name}{) and it wishes to provide both a }{\b Poingable}{ interface and a }{\b Bingable }{interface so it can be used with }{\b callPoing(\~) }{and }{\b callBing(\~)}{. Of course, in this situation we }{\i 
could}{ simply use multiple inheritance. This example is just intended to show the simplest syntax for the idiom; we\rquote ll see a real use shortly. To provide a }{\b Poingable}{ object without inheriting }{\b Outer}{ from }{\b Poingable}{
, the inner class idiom is used. First, the declaration }{\b class Inner}{ says that, somewhere, there is a nested class of this name. This allows the }{\b friend}{
 declaration for the class, which follows. Finally, now that the nested class has been granted access to all the private elements of }{\b Outer}{, the class can be defined. Notice that it keeps a pointer to the }{\b Outer}{
 which created it, and this pointer must be initialized in the constructor. Finally, the }{\b poing(\~)}{ function from }{\b Poingable}{ is implemented. The same process occurs for the second inner class which implements }{\b Bingable}{
. Each inner class has a single }{\b private}{ instance created, which is initialized in the }{\b Outer}{ constructor. By creating the member objects and returning references to them, issues of object lifetime are eliminated.
\par Notice that both inner class definitions are }{\b private}{, and in fact the client programmer doesn\rquote t have any access to details of the implementation, since the two access methods }{\b operator Poingable&(\~)}{ and }{\b operator Bingable&(\~)}{
 only return a reference to the upcast interface, not to the object that implements it. In fact, since the two inner classes are }{\b private}{
, the client programmer cannot even downcast to the implementation classes, thus providing complete isolation between interface and implementation.
\par Just to push a point, I\rquote ve taken the extra liberty here of defining the automatic type conversion operators }{\b operator Poingable&(\~)}{ and }{\b operator Bingable&(\~)}{. In }{\b main(\~)}{
, you can see that these actually allow a syntax that looks like }{\b Outer}{ is multiply inherited from }{\b Poingable}{ and }{\b Bingable}{. The difference is that the casts in this case are one way. You can get the effect of an upcast to }{\b Poingable
}{ or }{\b Bingable}{, but you cannot downcast back to an }{\b Outer}{. In the following example of observer, you\rquote ll see the more typical approach: you provide access to the inner class objects using ordinary
 member functions, not automatic type conversion operations.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {The observer example
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Armed with the }{\b Observer}{ and }{\b Observable }{header files and the inner class idiom, we can look at an example of the observer pattern:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:ObservedFlower.cpp
\par // Demonstration of "observer" pattern
\par #include "Observable.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Flower \{
\par   bool isOpen;
\par public:
\par   Flower() : isOpen(false), 
\par     openNotifier(this), closeNotifier(this) \{\}
\par   void open() \{ // Opens its petals
\par     isOpen = true;
\par     openNotifier.notifyObservers();
\par     closeNotifier.open();
\par   \}
\par   void close() \{ // Closes its petals
\par     isOpen = false;
\par     closeNotifier.notifyObservers();
\par     openNotifier.close();
\par   \}
\par   // Using the "inner class" idiom:
\par   class OpenNotifier;
\par   friend class Flower::OpenNotifier;
\par   class OpenNotifier : public Observable \{
\par     Flower* parent;
\par     bool alreadyOpen;
\par   public:
\par     OpenNotifier(Flower* f) : parent(f), 
\par       alreadyOpen(false) \{\}
\par     void notifyObservers(Argument* arg=0) \{
\par       if(parent->isOpen && !alreadyOpen) \{
\par         setChanged();
\par         Observable::notifyObservers();
\par         alreadyOpen = true;
\par       \}
\par     \}
\par     void close() \{ alreadyOpen = false; \}
\par   \} openNotifier;
\par   class CloseNotifier;
\par   friend class Flower::CloseNotifier;
\par   class CloseNotifier : public Observable \{
\par     Flower* parent;
\par     bool alreadyClosed;
\par   public:
\par     CloseNotifier(Flower* f) : parent(f), 
\par       alreadyClosed(false) \{\}
\par     void notifyObservers(Argument* arg=0) \{
\par       if(!parent->isOpen && !alreadyClosed) \{
\par         setChanged();
\par         Observable::notifyObservers();
\par         alreadyClosed = true;
\par       \}
\par     \}
\par     void open() \{ alreadyClosed = false; \}
\par   \} closeNotifier;
\par \};
\par 
\par class Bee \{
\par   string name;
\par   // An "inner class" for observing openings:
\par   class OpenObserver;
\par   friend class Bee::OpenObserver;
\par   class OpenObserver : public Observer \{
\par     Bee* parent;
\par   public:
\par     OpenObserver(Bee* b) : parent(b) \{\}
\par     void update(Observable*, Argument *) \{
\par       cout << "Bee " << parent->name 
\par         << "'s breakfast time!\\n";
\par     \}
\par   \} openObsrv;
\par   // Another "inner class" for closings:
\par   class CloseObserver;
\par   friend class Bee::CloseObserver;
\par   class CloseObserver : public Observer \{
\par     Bee* parent;
\par   public:
\par     CloseObserver(Bee* b) : parent(b) \{\}
\par     void update(Observable*, Argument *) \{
\par       cout << "Bee " << parent->name 
\par         << "'s bed time!\\n";
\par     \}
\par   \} closeObsrv;
\par public:
\par   Bee(string nm) : name(nm), 
\par     openObsrv(this), closeObsrv(this) \{\}
\par   Observer& openObserver() \{ return openObsrv; \}
\par   Observer& closeObserver() \{ return closeObsrv;\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Hummingbird \{
\par   string name;
\par   class OpenObserver;
\par   friend class Hummingbird::OpenObserver;
\par   class OpenObserver : public Observer \{
\par     Hummingbird* parent;
\par   public:
\par     OpenObserver(Hummingbird* h) : parent(h) \{\}
\par     void update(Observable*, Argument *) \{
\par       cout << "Hummingbird " << parent->name 
\par         << "'s breakfast time!\\n";
\par     \}
\par   \} openObsrv;
\par   class CloseObserver;
\par   friend class Hummingbird::CloseObserver;
\par   class CloseObserver : public Observer \{
\par     Hummingbird* parent;
\par   public:
\par     CloseObserver(Hummingbird* h) : parent(h) \{\}
\par     void update(Observable*, Argument *) \{
\par       cout << "Hummingbird " << parent->name 
\par         << "'s bed time!\\n";
\par     \}
\par   \} closeObsrv;
\par public:
\par   Hummingbird(string nm) : name(nm), 
\par     openObsrv(this), closeObsrv(this) \{\}
\par   Observer& openObserver() \{ return openObsrv; \}
\par   Observer& closeObserver() \{ return closeObsrv;\}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   Flower f;
\par   Bee ba("A"), bb("B");
\par   Hummingbird ha("A"), hb("B");
\par   f.openNotifier.addObserver(ha.openObserver());
\par   f.openNotifier.addObserver(hb.openObserver());
\par   f.openNotifier.addObserver(ba.openObserver());
\par   f.openNotifier.addObserver(bb.openObserver());
\par   f.closeNotifier.addObserver(ha.closeObserver());
\par   f.closeNotifier.addObserver(hb.closeObserver());
\par   f.closeNotifier.addObserver(ba.closeObserver());
\par   f.closeNotifier.addObserver(bb.closeObserver());
\par   // Hummingbird B decides to sleep in:
\par   f.openNotifier.deleteObserver(hb.openObserver());
\par   // Something changes that interests observers:
\par   f.open();
\par   f.open(); // It's already open, no change.
\par   // Bee A doesn't want to go to bed:
\par   f.closeNotifier.deleteObserver(
\par     ba.closeObserver());
\par   f.close();
\par   f.close(); // It's already closed; no change
\par   f.openNotifier.deleteObservers();
\par   f.open();
\par   f.close();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The events of interest are that a }{\b Flower}{ can open or close. Because of the use of the inner class idiom, both these events can be separately-observable phenomena. }{\b OpenNotifier}{ and }{\b 
CloseNotifier}{ both inherit }{\b Observable}{, so they have access to }{\b setChanged(\~)}{ and can be handed to anything that needs an }{\b Observable}{. You\rquote ll notice that, contrary to }{\b InnerClassIdiom.cpp}{, the }{\b Observable}{
 descendants are }{\b public}{. This is because some of their member functions must be available to the client programmer. There\rquote s nothing that says that an inner class must be }{\b private}{; in }{\b InnerClassIdiom.cpp}{
 I was simply following the design guideline \ldblquote make things as private as possible.\rdblquote  You could make the classes }{\b private}{ and expose the appropriate methods by proxy in }{\b Flower}{, but it wouldn\rquote t gain much.
\par The inner class idiom also comes in handy to define more than one kind of }{\b Observer}{, in }{\b Bee}{ and }{\b Hummingbird}{, since both those classes may want to independently observe }{\b Flower}{
 openings and closings. Notice how the inner class idiom provides something that has most of the benefits of inheritance (the ability to access the private data in the outer class, for example) without the same restrictions.
\par In }{\b main(\~)}{, you can see one of the prime benefits of the observer pattern: the ability to change behavior at runtime by dynamically registering and un-registering }{\b Observer}{s with }{\b Observable}{s. 
\par If you study the code above you\rquote ll see that }{\b OpenNotifier }{and }{\b CloseNotifier}{ use the basic }{\b Observable}{ interface. This means that you could inherit other completely different }{\b Obser}{\b v}{\b er}{
 classes; the only connection the }{\b Observer}{s have with }{\b Flower}{s is the }{\b Observer}{ interface.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776530}Multiple dispatching{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776530}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When dealing with multiple 
types which are interacting, a program can get particularly messy. For example, consider a system that parses and executes mathematical expressions. You want to be able to say }{\b Number + Number}{, }{\b Number * Number}{, etc., where }{\b Number}{
 is the base class for a family of numerical objects. But when you say }{\b a + b}{, and you don\rquote t know the exact type of either }{\b a}{ or }{\b b}{, so how can you get them to interact properly?
\par The answer starts with something you probably don\rquote t think about: C++ performs only single dispatching. That is, if you are performing an operation on more than one object whose type is unknown, C++ can invoke the dynamic bin}{d}{
ing mechanism on only one of those types. This doesn\rquote t solve the problem, so you end up detecting some types manually and effectively producing your own dynamic binding behavior.
\par The solution is called }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {multiple dispatching}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {dispatching\: multiple dispatching}}}{\i multiple dispatching}{
. Remember that polymorphism can occur only via member function calls, so if you want double dispatchi
ng to occur, there must be two member function calls: the first to determine the first unknown type, and the second to determine the second unknown type. With multiple dispatching, you must have a virtual call to determine each of the types. Generally, yo
u\rquote ll set up a config}{u}{
ration such that a single member function call produces more than one dynamic member function call and thus determines more than one type in the process. To get this effect, you need to work with more than one virtual function: you\rquote ll n
eed a virtual function call for each dispatch. The virtual functions in the following example are called }{\b compete(\~) }{and }{\b eval(\~)}{
, and are both members of the same type. (In this case there will be only two dispatches, which is referred to as }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {double dispatching}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {dispatching\: double dispatching}}}{\i double dispatching}{). If you are working with two different type hierarchies that are interacting, then you\rquote ll have to have a virtual call in each hierarchy.
\par Here\rquote s an example of multiple dispatching:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:PaperScissorsRock.cpp
\par // Demonstration of multiple dispatching
\par #include "../purge.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Paper;
\par class Scissors;
\par class Rock;
\par 
\par enum Outcome \{ win, lose, draw \};
\par 
\par ostream& 
\par operator<<(ostream& os, const Outcome out) \{
\par   switch(out) \{
\par     default:
\par     case win: return os << "win";
\par     case lose: return os << "lose";
\par     case draw: return os << "draw";
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par class Item \{
\par public:
\par   virtual Outcome compete(const Item*) = 0;
\par   virtual Outcome eval(const Paper*) const = 0;
\par   virtual Outcome eval(const Scissors*) const= 0;
\par   virtual Outcome eval(const Rock*) const = 0;
\par   virtual ostream& print(ostream& os) const = 0;
\par   virtual ~Item() \{\}
\par   friend ostream& 
\par   operator<<(ostream& os, const Item* it) \{
\par     return it->print(os);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Paper : public Item \{
\par public:
\par   Outcome compete(const Item* it) \{
\par     return it->eval(this);
\par   \}
\par   Outcome eval(const Paper*) const \{
\par     return draw;
\par   \}
\par   Outcome eval(const Scissors*) const \{
\par     return win;
\par   \}
\par   Outcome eval(const Rock*) const \{
\par     return lose;
\par   \}
\par   ostream& print(ostream& os) const \{
\par     return os << "Paper   ";
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Scissors : public Item \{
\par public:
\par   Outcome compete(const Item* it) \{
\par     return it->eval(this);
\par   \}
\par   Outcome eval(const Paper*) const \{
\par     return lose;
\par   \}
\par   Outcome eval(const Scissors*) const \{
\par     return draw;
\par   \}
\par   Outcome eval(const Rock*) const \{
\par     return win;
\par   \}
\par   ostream& print(ostream& os) const \{
\par     return os << "Scissors";
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Rock : public Item \{
\par public:
\par   Outcome compete(const Item* it) \{
\par     return it->eval(this);
\par   \}
\par   Outcome eval(const Paper*) const \{
\par     return win;
\par   \}
\par   Outcome eval(const Scissors*) const \{
\par     return lose;
\par   \}
\par   Outcome eval(const Rock*) const \{
\par     return draw;
\par   \}
\par   ostream& print(ostream& os) const \{
\par     return os << "Rock    ";
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par struct ItemGen \{
\par   ItemGen() \{ srand(time(0)); \}
\par   Item* operator()() \{
\par     switch(rand() % 3) \{
\par       default:
\par       case 0:
\par         return new Scissors();
\par       case 1:
\par         return new Paper();
\par       case 2:
\par         return new Rock();
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par struct Compete \{
\par   Outcome operator()(Item* a, Item* b) \{
\par     cout << a << "\\t" << b << "\\t";
\par     return a->compete(b);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   const int sz = 20;
\par   vector<Item*> v(sz*2);
\par   generate(v.begin(), v.end(), ItemGen());
\par   transform(v.begin(), v.begin() + sz, 
\par     v.begin() + sz, 
\par     ostream_iterator<Outcome>(cout, "\\n"), 
\par     Compete());
\par   purge(v);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776531}Visitor, a type of multiple dispatching{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776531}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The assumption is that you have a primary class hierarchy that is fixed; perhaps it\rquote s from another vendor and you can\rquote t make changes to that hierarchy. However, you\rquote 
d like to add new polymorphic methods to that hierarchy, which means that normally you\rquote d have to add something to the base class interface. So the dilemma is that you need to add methods to the base class, but you can\rquote 
t touch the base class. How do you get around this?
\par The design pattern that solves this kind of problem is called a \ldblquote visitor\rdblquote  (the final one in the }{\i Design Patterns}{ book), and it builds on the double}{\i  }{dispatching scheme shown in the last section.
\par The }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {visitor pattern}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {design patterns\: visitor}}}{visitor pattern allows you to extend the interface of 
the primary type by creating a separate class hierarchy of type }{\b Visitor }{to virtualize the operations performed upon the primary type. The objects of the primary type simply \ldblquote accept\rdblquote  the visitor, then call the visitor\rquote 
s dynamically}{\b -}{bound member function.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:BeeAndFlowers.cpp
\par // Demonstration of "visitor" pattern
\par #include "../purge.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class Gladiolus;
\par class Renuculus;
\par class Chrysanthemum;
\par 
\par class Visitor \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void visit(Gladiolus* f) = 0;
\par   virtual void visit(Renuculus* f) = 0;
\par   virtual void visit(Chrysanthemum* f) = 0;
\par   virtual ~Visitor() \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Flower \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void accept(Visitor&) = 0;
\par   virtual ~Flower() \{\}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Gladiolus : public Flower \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void accept(Visitor& v) \{
\par     v.visit(this);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Renuculus : public Flower \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void accept(Visitor& v) \{
\par     v.visit(this);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class Chrysanthemum : public Flower \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void accept(Visitor& v) \{
\par     v.visit(this);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Add the ability to produce a string:
\par class StringVal : public Visitor \{
\par   string s;
\par public:
\par   operator const string&() \{ return s; \}
\par   virtual void visit(Gladiolus*) \{
\par     s = "Gladiolus";
\par   \}
\par   virtual void visit(Renuculus*) \{
\par     s = "Renuculus";
\par   \}
\par   virtual void visit(Chrysanthemum*) \{
\par     s = "Chrysanthemum";
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Add the ability to do "Bee" activities:
\par class Bee : public Visitor \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void visit(Gladiolus*) \{
\par     cout << "Bee and Gladiolus\\n";
\par   \}
\par   virtual void visit(Renuculus*) \{
\par     cout << "Bee and Renuculus\\n";
\par   \}
\par   virtual void visit(Chrysanthemum*) \{
\par     cout << "Bee and Chrysanthemum\\n";
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par struct FlowerGen \{
\par   FlowerGen() \{ srand(time(0)); \}
\par   Flower* operator()() \{
\par     switch(rand() % 3) \{
\par       default:
\par       case 0: return new Gladiolus();
\par       case 1: return new Renuculus();
\par       case 2: return new Chrysanthemum();
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<Flower*> v(10);
\par   generate(v.begin(), v.end(), FlowerGen());
\par   vector<Flower*>::iterator it;
\par   // It's almost as if I added a virtual function
\par   // to produce a Flower string representation:
\par   StringVal sval;
\par   for(it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); it++) \{
\par     (*it)->accept(sval);
\par     cout << string(sval) << endl;
\par   \}
\par   // Perform "Bee" operation on all Flowers:
\par   Bee bee;
\par   for(it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); it++)
\par     (*it)->accept(bee);
\par   purge(v);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776532}Efficiency{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776532}
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776533}Flyweight{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776533}
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018798}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776534}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018797}The composite{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776534}

\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776535}Evolving a design: the trash recycler{\*\bkmkend _Toc375545413}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018798}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776535}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The nature of this problem (modeling a trash recycling system) is that the trash is thrown unclassified into a si}{n}{gle bin, so the specific type information is lost. But later, the specific type i}
{n}{formation must be recovered to properly sort the trash. In the initial solution, RTTI (described in Chapter XX) is used.
\par This is not a trivial design because it has an added constraint. That\rquote s what makes it interesting \endash  it\rquote s more like the messy problems you\rquote re likely to e}{n}{counter in your work. The extra constrai
nt is that the trash arrives at the trash recycling plant all mixed together. The program must model the sorting of that trash. This is where RTTI comes in: you have a bunch of anonymous pieces of trash, and the program figures out exactly what type they 
are.
\par One of the objectives of this program is to sum up the weight and value of the different types of trash. The trash will be kept in (potentially different types of) containers, so it makes sense to templatize the \ldblquote summation\rdblquote 
 function on the container holding it (assuming that container exhibits basic STL-like behavior), so the function will be maximally flexible:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:sumValue.h
\par // Sums the value of Trash in any type of STL
\par // container of any specific type of Trash:
\par #ifndef SUMVALUE_H
\par #define SUMVALUE_H
\par #include <typeinfo>
\par #include <vector>
\par 
\par template<typename Cont>
\par void sumValue(Cont& bin) \{
\par   double val = 0.0f;
\par   typename Cont::iterator tally = bin.begin();
\par   while(tally != bin.end()) \{
\par     val +=(*tally)->weight() * (*tally)->value();
\par     out << "weight of "
\par         << typeid(*(*tally)).name()
\par         << " = " << (*tally)->weight() 
\par         << endl;
\par     tally++;
\par   \}
\par   out << "Total value = " << val << endl;
\par \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // SUMVALUE_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you look at a piece of code like this, it can be initially disturbing because you might wonder \ldblquote how can the compiler know that the member functions I\rquote m calling here are valid?
\rdblquote  But of course, all the template says is \ldblquote generate this code on demand,\rdblquote  and so only when you call the function will type checking come into play. This enforces that }{\b *tally}{
 produces an object that has member functions }{\b weight(\~)}{ and }{\b value(\~)}{, and that }{\b out}{ is a global }{\b ostream}{.
\par The }{\b sumValue(\~)}{ function is templatized on the type of container that\rquote s holding the }{\b Trash }{pointers. Notice there\rquote s nothing in the template signature that says \ldblquote 
this container must behave like an STL container and must hold }{\b Trash*}{\rdblquote ; that is all implied in the code that\rquote s generated which uses the container.
\par The first version of the example takes the straightforward approach: creating a }{\b vector<Trash*>}{, filling it with }{\b Trash}{ objects, then using RTTI to sort them out:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:Recycle1.cpp 
\par // Recycling with RTTI
\par #include "sumValue.h"
\par #include "../purge.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <typeinfo>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("Recycle1.out");
\par 
\par class Trash \{
\par   double _weight;
\par   static int _count; // # created
\par   static int _dcount; // # destroyed
\par   // disallow automatic creation of
\par   // assignment & copy-constructor:
\par   void operator=(const Trash&);
\par   Trash(const Trash&);
\par public:
\par   Trash(double wt) : _weight(wt) \{ 
\par     _count++; 
\par   \}
\par   virtual double value() const = 0;
\par   double weight() const \{ return _weight; \}
\par   static int count() \{ return _count; \}
\par   static int dcount() \{ return _dcount;\}
\par   virtual ~Trash() \{ _dcount++; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int Trash::_count = 0;
\par int Trash::_dcount = 0;
\par 
\par class Aluminum : public Trash \{
\par   static double val;
\par public:
\par   Aluminum(double wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   double value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(double newval) \{
\par     val = newval;
\par   \}
\par   ~Aluminum() \{ out << "~Aluminum\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par double Aluminum::val = 1.67F;
\par 
\par class Paper : public Trash \{
\par   static double val;
\par public:
\par   Paper(double wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   double value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(double newval) \{
\par     val = newval;
\par   \}
\par   ~Paper() \{ out << "~Paper\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par double Paper::val = 0.10F;
\par 
\par class Glass : public Trash \{
\par   static double val;
\par public:
\par   Glass(double wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   double value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(double newval) \{
\par     val = newval;
\par   \}
\par   ~Glass() \{ out << "~Glass\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par double Glass::val = 0.23F;
\par 
\par class TrashGen \{
\par public:
\par   TrashGen() \{ srand(time(0)); \}
\par   static double frand(int mod) \{
\par     return static_cast<double>(rand() % mod);
\par   \}
\par   Trash* operator()() \{
\par     for(int i = 0; i < 30; i++)
\par       switch(rand() % 3) \{
\par         case 0 :
\par           return new Aluminum(frand(100));
\par         case 1 :
\par           return new Paper(frand(100));
\par         case 2 :
\par           return new Glass(frand(100));
\par       \}
\par     return new Aluminum(0);
\par     // Or throw exeception...
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<Trash*> bin;
\par   // Fill up the Trash bin:
\par   generate_n(back_inserter(bin), 30, TrashGen());
\par   vector<Aluminum*> alBin;
\par   vector<Paper*> paperBin;
\par   vector<Glass*> glassBin;
\par   vector<Trash*>::iterator sorter = bin.begin();
\par   // Sort the Trash:
\par   while(sorter != bin.end()) \{
\par     Aluminum* ap = 
\par       dynamic_cast<Aluminum*>(*sorter);
\par     Paper* pp = dynamic_cast<Paper*>(*sorter);
\par     Glass* gp = dynamic_cast<Glass*>(*sorter);
\par     if(ap) alBin.push_back(ap);
\par     if(pp) paperBin.push_back(pp);
\par     if(gp) glassBin.push_back(gp);
\par     sorter++;
\par   \}
\par   sumValue(alBin);
\par   sumValue(paperBin);
\par   sumValue(glassBin);
\par   sumValue(bin);
\par   out << "total created = "
\par       << Trash::count() << endl;
\par   purge(bin);
\par   out << "total destroyed = "
\par       << Trash::dcount() << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This uses the classic structure of virtual functions in the base class that are redefined in the derived class. In addition, there are two }{\b static }{data members in the base class:}{\b  }{_}{\b 
count }{to indicate the number of }{\b Trash }{objects that are created, and }{\b _dcount }{to keep track of the number that are destroyed. This verifies that proper memory management occurs. To support this, the }{\b operator=}{ and copy-constructor ar
e disallowed by declaring them }{\b private}{ (no definitions are necessary; this simply prevents the compiler from synthesizing them). Those operations would cause problems with the count, and if they were allowed you\rquote 
d have to define them properly.
\par The }{\b Trash}{ objects are created, for the sake of this example, by the generator }{\b TrashGen}{, which uses the random number generator to choose the type of }{\b Trash}{, and also to provide it with a \ldblquote weight\rdblquote 
 argument. The return value of the generator\rquote s }{\b operator(\~) }{is upcast to }{\b Trash*}{, so all the specific type information is lost.}{\b  }{In }{\b main(\~)}{, a }{\b vector<Trash*>}{ called }{\b bin }{
is created and then filled using the STL algorithm }{\b generate_n(\~)}{. To perform the sorting, three }{\b vector}{s are created, each of which holds a different type of }{\b Trash*}{. An iterator moves through }{\b bin}{
 and RTTI is used to determine which specific type of }{\b Trash}{ the iterator is currently selecting, placing each into the appropriate typed bin. Finally, }{\b sumValue(\~)}{ is applied to each of the containers, and the }{\b Trash }{objects are
 cleaned up using }{\b purge(\~)}{ (defined in Chapter XX). The creation and destruction counts ensure that things are properly cleaned up.
\par Of course, it seems silly to upcast the types of }{\b Trash}{ into a container holding base type pointers, and then to turn around 
and downcast. Why not just put the trash into the appropriate receptacle in the first place? (indeed, this is the whole enigma of recycling). In this program it might be easy to repair, but sometimes a system\rquote 
s structure and flexibility can benefit greatly from downcasting.
\par The program satisfies the design requirements: it works. This may be fine as long as it\rquote s a one-shot solution. However, a good program will evolve over time, so you must ask: what if the situation changes? For example, cardboard is now a
 valuable recyclable commodity, so how will that be integrated into the system (especially if the program is large and complicated). Since the above type-check coding in the }{\b switch}{
 statement and in the RTTI statements could be scattered throughout the program, you\rquote d have to go find all that code every time a new type was added, and if you miss one the compiler won\rquote t help you.
\par The key to the misuse of RTTI here is that }{\i every type is tested}{. If you\rquote re only looking for a subset of types because that subset needs special treatment, that\rquote s probably fine. But if you\rquote re hunting for every type inside a }{
\b switch}{ statement, then you\rquote re probably missing an important point, and definitely making your code less maintainable. In the next section we\rquote ll look at how this progr
am evolved over several stages to become much more flexible. This should prove a valuable example in program design.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc375545414}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018799}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776536}Improving the design
{\*\bkmkend _Toc375545414}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018799}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776536}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The solutions in }{\i Design Patterns}{ are organized around the question \ldblquote What will change as this program evolves?\rdblquote  This is usually the most important que}{s}{
tion that you can ask about any design. If you can build your system around the answer, the results will be two-pronged: not only will your system allow easy (and inexpensive) maintenance, but you might also produce componen
ts that are reusable, so that other systems can be built more cheaply. This is the promise of object-oriented programming, but it doesn\rquote t happen automatically; it requires thought and insight on your part. In this section we\rquote 
ll see how this process can happen during the refinement of a system.
\par The answer to the question \ldblquote What will change?\rdblquote  for the recycling system is a common one: more types will be added to the system. The goal of the design, then, is to make this addition of types as painless as possible.
 In the recycling program, we\rquote 
d like to encapsulate all places where specific type information is mentioned, so (if for no other reason) any changes can be localized inside those encapsulations. It turns out that this process also cleans up the rest of the code considerably.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc375545415}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018800}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776537}\ldblquote Make more objects\rdblquote 
{\*\bkmkend _Toc375545415}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018800}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776537}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This brings up a general object-oriented design principle that I first heard spoken by }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Booch, Grady}}}{Grady Booch: \ldblquote 
If the design is too complicated, make more o}{b}{jects.\rdblquote  This is simultaneously counterintuitive and ludicrously simple, and yet it\rquote s the most useful guideline I\rquote ve found. (You might observe that \ldblquote make more objects
\rdblquote  is often equivalent to \ldblquote add another level of indirection.\rdblquote ) In general, if you find a place with messy code, consider what sort of class 
would clean things up. Often the side effect of cleaning up the code will be a system that has better structure and is more flexible.
\par Consider first the place where }{\b Trash}{ objects are created. In the above example, we\rquote re conveniently using a generator to cr
eate the objects. The generator nicely encapsulates the creation of the objects, but the neatness is an illusion because in general we\rquote 
ll want to create the objects based on something more than a random number generator. Some information will be available which will determine what kind of }{\b Trash}{
 object this should be. Because you generally need to make your objects by examining some kind of information, if you\rquote re not paying close attention you may end up with }{\b switch}{ statements (as in }{\b TrashGen}{) or cascaded }{\b if }{
statements scattered throughout your code. This is definitely messy, and also a place where you must change code whe}{n}{
ever a new type is added. If new types are commonly added, a better solution is a single member function that takes all of the necessary information and produces an object of the correct type, already upcast to a }{\b Trash}{ pointer. In }{\i D}{\i e}{\i 
sign Patterns}{ this is broadly referred to as a }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {creational design patterns}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {design patterns\: creational}}}{\i 
creational pattern}{ (of which there are several). The specific pattern that will be applied here is a variant of the }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {factory method}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid 
{\xe {design patterns\: factory method}}}{\i Factory Method}{ (\ldblquote method\rdblquote  being a more OOPish way to refer to a member function). Here, the factory method will be a }{\b static}{ member of }{\b Trash}{
, but more commonly it is a member function that is overridden in the derived class.
\par The idea of the factory method is that you pass it the essential information it needs to know to create your object, then stand back and wait for the poi
nter (already upcast to the base type) to pop out as the return value. From then on, you treat the object polymorphically. Thus, you never even need to know the exact type of object that\rquote 
s created. In fact, the factory method hides it from you to prevent accidental misuse. If you want to use the object without polymorphism, you must explicitly use RTTI and casting.
\par But there\rquote s a little problem, especially when you use the more complicated approach (not shown here) of making the factory method in the base cl
ass and overriding it in the derived classes. What if the information required in the derived class requires more or different arguments? \ldblquote Creating more o}{b}{jects\rdblquote  solves this problem. To implement the factory method, the }{\b Trash}
{ class gets a new member function called }{\b factory(\~)}{. To hide the creational data, there\rquote s a new class called }{\b Info}{ that contains all of the necessary information for the }{\b factory(\~)}{ method to create the appropriate }{\b Trash}
{ object. Here\rquote s a simple implement}{a}{tion of }{\b Info}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  class Info \{
\par     int type;
\par     // Must change this to add another type:
\par     static const int maxnum = 3;
\par     double data;
\par   public:
\par     Info(int typeNum, double dat)
\par       : type(typeNum % maxnum), data(dat) \{\}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  \};
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {An }{\b Info}{ object\rquote s only job is to hold information for the }{\b factory(\~)}{ method. Now, if there\rquote s a situation in which }{\b factory(\~)}{ needs more or different inform}{a}{
tion to create a new type of }{\b Trash}{ object, the }{\b factory(\~)}{ interface doesn\rquote t need to be changed. The }{\b Info}{ class can be changed by adding new data and new const
ructors, or in the more typical object-oriented fashion of subclassing.
\par Here\rquote s the second version of the program with the factory method added. The object-counting code has been removed; we\rquote ll assume proper cleanup will take place in all the rest of the examples.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:Recycle2.cpp
\par // Adding a factory method
\par #include "sumValue.h"
\par #include "../purge.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <typeinfo>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("Recycle2.out");
\par 
\par class Trash \{
\par   double _weight;
\par   void operator=(const Trash&);
\par   Trash(const Trash&);
\par public:
\par   Trash(double wt) : _weight(wt) \{ \}
\par   virtual double value() const = 0;
\par   double weight() const \{ return _weight; \}
\par   virtual ~Trash() \{\}
\par   // Nested class because it's tightly coupled
\par   // to Trash:
\par   class Info \{
\par     int type;
\par     // Must change this to add another type:
\par     static const int maxnum = 3;
\par     double data;
\par     friend class Trash;
\par   public:
\par     Info(int typeNum, double dat)
\par       : type(typeNum % maxnum), data(dat) \{\}
\par   \};
\par   static Trash* factory(const Info& info);
\par \};
\par 
\par class Aluminum : public Trash \{
\par   static double val;
\par public:
\par   Aluminum(double wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   double value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(double newval) \{
\par     val = newval;
\par   \}
\par   ~Aluminum() \{ out << "~Aluminum\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par double Aluminum::val = 1.67F;
\par 
\par class Paper : public Trash \{
\par   static double val;
\par public:
\par   Paper(double wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   double value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(double newval) \{
\par     val = newval;
\par   \}
\par   ~Paper() \{ out << "~Paper\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par double Paper::val = 0.10F;
\par 
\par class Glass : public Trash \{
\par   static double val;
\par public:
\par   Glass(double wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   double value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(double newval) \{
\par     val = newval;
\par   \}
\par   ~Glass() \{ out << "~Glass\\n"; \}
\par \};
\par 
\par double Glass::val = 0.23F;
\par 
\par // Definition of the factory method. It must know
\par // all the types, so is defined after all the
\par // subtypes are defined:
\par Trash* Trash::factory(const Info& info) \{
\par   switch(info.type) \{
\par     default: // In case of overrun
\par     case 0:
\par       return new Aluminum(info.data);
\par     case 1:
\par       return new Paper(info.data);
\par     case 2:
\par       return new Glass(info.data);
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par // Generator for Info objects:
\par class InfoGen \{
\par   int typeQuantity;
\par   int maxWeight;
\par public:
\par   InfoGen(int typeQuant, int maxWt)
\par     : typeQuantity(typeQuant), maxWeight(maxWt) \{
\par     srand(time(0)); 
\par   \}
\par   Trash::Info operator()() \{
\par     return Trash::Info(rand() % typeQuantity, 
\par       static_cast<double>(rand() % maxWeight));
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<Trash*> bin;
\par   // Fill up the Trash bin:
\par   InfoGen infoGen(3, 100);
\par   for(int i = 0; i < 30; i++)
\par     bin.push_back(Trash::factory(infoGen()));
\par   vector<Aluminum*> alBin;
\par   vector<Paper*> paperBin;
\par   vector<Glass*> glassBin;
\par   vector<Trash*>::iterator sorter = bin.begin();
\par   // Sort the Trash:
\par   while(sorter != bin.end()) \{
\par     Aluminum* ap = 
\par       dynamic_cast<Aluminum*>(*sorter);
\par     Paper* pp = dynamic_cast<Paper*>(*sorter);
\par     Glass* gp = dynamic_cast<Glass*>(*sorter);
\par     if(ap) alBin.push_back(ap);
\par     if(pp) paperBin.push_back(pp);
\par     if(gp) glassBin.push_back(gp);
\par     sorter++;
\par   \}
\par   sumValue(alBin);
\par   sumValue(paperBin);
\par   sumValue(glassBin);
\par   sumValue(bin);
\par   purge(bin); // Cleanup
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the factory method }{\b Trash::factory(\~)}{, the determination of the exact type of object is simple, but you can imagine a more complicated system in which }{\b factory(\~)}{
 uses an elaborate alg}{o}{rithm. The point is that it\rquote s now hidden away in one place, and you know to come to this place to make changes when you add new types.
\par The creation of new objects is now more general in }{\b main(\~)}{, and depends on \ldblquote real\rdblquote  data (albeit created by another generator, driven by random numbers). The generator object is created, telling it the maximum 
type number and the largest \ldblquote data\rdblquote  value to produce. Each call to the generator creates an }{\b Info}{ object which is passed into }{\b Trash::factory(\~)}{, which in turn pr}{o}{duces some kind of }{\b Trash}{
 object and returns the pointer that\rquote s added to the }{\b vector<Trash*>}{ }{\b bin}{. 
\par The constructor for the }{\b Info }{object is very specific and restrictive in this example. However, you could also imagine a }{\b vector}{ of arguments into the }{\b Info }{constructor (or directly into a }{\b factory(\~)}{
 call, for that matter). This requires that the arg}{u}{ments be parsed and checked at runtime, but it does provide the greatest flexibility.
\par You can see from this code what \ldblquote }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {vector of change}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {change\: vector of change}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {design patterns\: vector of change}}}{vector of change\rdblquote  problem the factory is r}{e}{s
ponsible for solving: if you add new types to the system (the change), the only code that must be modified is within the factory, so the factory isolates the effect of that change.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc375545418}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018801}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776538}A pattern for prototyping creation
{\*\bkmkend _Toc375545418}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018801}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776538}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A problem with the above design is that it still requires a central location where all the types of the objects must be known: inside the }{\b factory(\~)}{
 method. If new types are regularly being added to the system, the }{\b factory(\~)}{ method must be changed for each new type. When you discover something like this, it is useful to try to go one step further and move }{\i all}{
 of the activities involving that specific type \endash  including its creation \endash  into the class representing that type. This way, the only thing you need to do to add a new type to the sy}{s}{tem is to inherit a single class.
\par To move the information concerning type creation into each specific type of }{\b Trash}{,}{\b  }{the \ldblquote }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {prototype}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
design patterns\: prototype}}}{prototype\rdblquote  pattern will be used. The general idea is that you have a master container of objects, one of each type you\rquote re interested in making. The \ldblquote prototype objects\rdblquote 
 in this container are used }{\i only}{ for making new objects. In this case, we\rquote ll name the object-creation member function }{\b clone(\~)}{.}{\b  }{When you\rquote re ready to make a new object, presumably
 you have some sort of information that establishes the type of object you want to create. The }{\b factory(\~)}{ method (it\rquote 
s not required that you use factory with prototype, but they comingle nicely) moves through the master container comparing your information with whatever appropriate information is in the prototype objects in the master container. When a match is found, }
{\b factory(\~) }{returns a clone of that object.
\par In this scheme there is no hard-coded information for creation. Each object knows how to expose appropriate information to allow matching, and how to clone itself. Thus, the }{\b factory(\~)}{ method doesn\rquote 
t need to be changed when a new type is added to the system. 
\par The prototypes will be contained in a }{\b static}{ }{\b vector<Trash*>}{ called }{\b prototypes}{. This is a }{\b private}{ member of the base class }{\b Trash}{. The }{\b friend }{class }{\b TrashPrototypeInit }{is responsible for putting the }{\b 
Trash*}{ prototypes into the prototype list.
\par You\rquote ll also note that the }{\b Info}{ class has changed. It now uses a }{\b string}{ to act as type identification information. As you shall see, this will allow us to read object information from a file when creating }{\b Trash}{ objects.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:Trash.h
\par // Base class for Trash recycling examples
\par #ifndef TRASH_H
\par #define TRASH_H
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <exception>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <string>
\par 
\par class TypedBin; // For a later example
\par class Visitor; // For a later example
\par 
\par class Trash \{
\par   double _weight;
\par   void operator=(const Trash&);
\par   Trash(const Trash&);
\par public:
\par   Trash(double wt) : _weight(wt) \{\}
\par   virtual double value() const = 0;
\par   double weight() const \{ return _weight; \}
\par   virtual ~Trash() \{\}
\par   class Info \{
\par     std::string _id;
\par     double _data;
\par   public:
\par     Info(std::string ident, double dat)
\par       : _id(ident), _data(dat) \{\}
\par     double data() const \{ return _data; \}
\par     std::string id() const \{ return _id; \}
\par     friend std::ostream& operator<<(
\par       std::ostream& os, const Info& info) \{
\par       return os << info._id << ':' << info._data;
\par     \}
\par   \};
\par protected:
\par   // Remainder of class provides support for
\par   // prototyping:
\par   static std::vector<Trash*> prototypes;
\par   friend class TrashPrototypeInit;
\par   Trash() : _weight(0) \{\}
\par public:
\par   static Trash* factory(const Info& info);
\par   virtual std::string id() = 0;  // type ident
\par   virtual Trash* clone(const Info&) = 0;
\par   // Stubs, inserted for later use:
\par   virtual bool 
\par   addToBin(std::vector<TypedBin*>&) \{\}
\par   virtual void accept(Visitor&) \{\};
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // TRASH_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The basic part of the }{\b Trash}{ class remains as before. The rest of the class supports the prototyping pattern. The }{\b id(\~)}{ member function returns a }{\b string}{
 that can be compared with the }{\b id(\~)}{ of an }{\b Info}{ object to determine whether this is the prototype that should be cloned (of course, the evaluation can be much more sophisticated than that if you need it). Both }{\b id(\~)}{ and }{\b clone(
\~)}{ are pure }{\b virtual}{ functions so they must be overridden in derived classes.
\par The last two member functions, }{\b addToBin(\~)}{ and }{\b accept(\~)}{, are \ldblquote stubs\rdblquote  which will be used in later versions of the trash sorting problem. It\rquote s necessary to have these virtu
al functions in the base class, but in the early examples there\rquote s no need for them, so they are not pure virtuals so as not to intrude.
\par The }{\b factory(\~) }{member function has the same declaration, but the definition is what handles the prototyping. Here is the implementation file:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:Trash.cpp \{O\}
\par #include "Trash.h"
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par Trash* Trash::factory(const Info& info) \{
\par   vector<Trash*>::iterator it;
\par   for(it = prototypes.begin();
\par     it != prototypes.end(); it++) \{
\par     // Somehow determine the new type
\par     // to create, and clone one:
\par     if (info.id() == (*it)->id())
\par       return (*it)->clone(info);
\par   \}
\par   cerr << "Prototype not found for "
\par     << info << endl;
\par   // "Default" to first one in the vector:
\par   return (*prototypes.begin())->clone(info);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b string}{ inside the }{\b Info }{object contains the type name of the }{\b Trash }{to be created; this }{\b string}{ is compared to the }{\b id(\~)}{ values of the objects in }{\b prototypes}{
. If there\rquote s a match, then that\rquote s the object to create.
\par Of course, the appropriate prototype object might not be in the }{\b prototypes}{ list. In this case, the }{\b return}{ in the inner loop is never executed and you\rquote 
ll drop out at the end, where a default value is created. It might be more appropriate to throw an exception here.
\par As you can see from the code, there\rquote s nothing that knows about specific types of }{\b Trash}{. The beauty of this design is that this code doesn\rquote t need to be changed, regardless of the different situations it will be used in.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {\b Trash}{ subclasses
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To fit into the prototyping scheme, each new subclass of }{\b Trash}{ must follow some rules. First, it must create a }{\b protected}{ default constructor, so that no one but }{\b TrashPrototypeInit }
{may use it. }{\b TrashPrototypeInit }{is a singleton, creating one and only one prototype}{\b  }{object for each subtype. This guarantees that the }{\b Trash}{ subtype will be properly represented in the }{\b prototypes}{ container.
\par After defining the \ldblquote ordinary\rdblquote  member functions and data that the }{\b Trash}{ object will actually use, the class must also override the }{\b id(\~)}{ member (which in this case returns a }{\b string}{ for comparison) and the }{\b 
clone(\~)}{ function, which must know how to pull the appropriate information out of the }{\b Info}{ object in order to create the object correctly.
\par Here are the different types of }{\b Trash}{, each in their own file.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:Aluminum.h
\par // The Aluminum class with prototyping
\par #ifndef ALUMINUM_H
\par #define ALUMINUM_H
\par #include "Trash.h"
\par 
\par class Aluminum : public Trash \{
\par   static double val;
\par protected:
\par   Aluminum() \{\}
\par   friend class TrashPrototypeInit;
\par public:
\par   Aluminum(double wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   double value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(double newVal) \{
\par     val = newVal;
\par   \}
\par   std::string id() \{ return "Aluminum"; \}
\par   Trash* clone(const Info& info) \{
\par     return new Aluminum(info.data());
\par   \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // ALUMINUM_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:Paper.h
\par // The Paper class with prototyping
\par #ifndef PAPER_H
\par #define PAPER_H
\par #include "Trash.h"
\par 
\par class Paper : public Trash \{
\par   static double val;
\par protected:
\par   Paper() \{\}
\par   friend class TrashPrototypeInit;
\par public:
\par   Paper(double wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   double value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(double newVal) \{
\par     val = newVal;
\par   \}
\par   std::string id() \{ return "Paper"; \}
\par   Trash* clone(const Info& info) \{
\par     return new Paper(info.data());
\par   \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // PAPER_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:Glass.h
\par // The Glass class with prototyping
\par #ifndef GLASS_H
\par #define GLASS_H
\par #include "Trash.h"
\par 
\par class Glass : public Trash \{
\par   static double val;
\par protected:
\par   Glass() \{\}
\par   friend class TrashPrototypeInit;
\par public:
\par   Glass(double wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   double value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(double newVal) \{
\par     val = newVal;
\par   \}
\par   std::string id() \{ return "Glass"; \}
\par   Trash* clone(const Info& info) \{
\par     return new Glass(info.data());
\par   \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // GLASS_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {And here\rquote s a new type of }{\b Trash}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:Cardboard.h
\par // The Cardboard class with prototyping
\par #ifndef CARDBOARD_H
\par #define CARDBOARD_H
\par #include "Trash.h"
\par 
\par class Cardboard : public Trash \{
\par   static double val;
\par protected:
\par   Cardboard() \{\}
\par   friend class TrashPrototypeInit;
\par public:
\par   Cardboard(double wt) : Trash(wt) \{\}
\par   double value() const \{ return val; \}
\par   static void value(double newVal) \{
\par     val = newVal;
\par   \}
\par   std::string id() \{ return "Cardboard"; \}
\par   Trash* clone(const Info& info) \{
\par     return new Cardboard(info.data());
\par   \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // CARDBOARD_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The static }{\b val}{ data members must be defined and initialized in a separate code file:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:TrashStatics.cpp \{O\}
\par // Contains the static definitions for 
\par // the Trash type's "val" data members
\par #include "Trash.h"
\par #include "Aluminum.h"
\par #include "Paper.h"
\par #include "Glass.h"
\par #include "Cardboard.h"
\par 
\par double Aluminum::val = 1.67;
\par double Paper::val = 0.10;
\par double Glass::val = 0.23;
\par double Cardboard::val = 0.14;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There\rquote s one other issue: initialization of the static data members. }{\b TrashPrototypeInit }{must create the prototype objects and add them to the }{\b static Trash::prototypes }{vector. So it
\rquote s }{\i very}{ important that you control the order of initialization of the }{\b static}{ objects, so the }{\b prototypes}{ vector is created before any of the prototype objects, which depend on the prior existence of }{\b prototypes}{
. The most straightforward way to do this is to put all the definitions in a single file, in the order in which you want them initialized.
\par }{\b TrashPrototypeInit}{ must be defined separately because it inserts the actual prototypes into the }{\b vector}{, and throughout the chapter we\rquote ll be inheriting new types of }{\b Trash}{
 from the existing types. By making this one class in a separate file, a different version can be created and linked in for the new situations, leaving the rest of the code in the system alone.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:TrashPrototypeInit.cpp \{O\}
\par // Performs initialization of all the prototypes.
\par // Create a different version of this file to
\par // make different kinds of Trash.
\par #include "Trash.h"
\par #include "Aluminum.h"
\par #include "Paper.h"
\par #include "Glass.h"
\par #include "Cardboard.h"
\par 
\par // Allocate the static member object:
\par std::vector<Trash*> Trash::prototypes;
\par 
\par class TrashPrototypeInit \{
\par   Aluminum a;
\par   Paper p;
\par   Glass g;
\par   Cardboard c;
\par   TrashPrototypeInit() \{
\par     Trash::prototypes.push_back(&a);
\par     Trash::prototypes.push_back(&p);
\par     Trash::prototypes.push_back(&g);
\par     Trash::prototypes.push_back(&c);
\par   \}
\par   static TrashPrototypeInit singleton;
\par \};
\par 
\par TrashPrototypeInit 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  TrashPrototypeInit::singleton; ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This is taken a step further by making }{\b TrashPrototypeInit}{ a singleton (the constructor is }{\b private}{
), even though the class definition is not available in a header file so it would seem safe enough to assume that no one could accidentally make a second instance.
\par Unfortunately, this is one more separate piece of code you must maintain whenever you add a new type to the system. However, it\rquote s not too bad since the linker should give you an error message if you forget (since }{\b prototypes }{
is defined in this file as well). The really difficult problems come when you }{\i don\rquote t}{ get any warnings or errors if you do something wrong.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Parsing }{\b Trash}{ from an external file
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The information about }{\b Trash}{ objects will be read from an outside file. The file has all of the necessary information about each piece of trash in a single entry in the form }{\b Trash:weight}{
. There are multiple entries on a line, separated by commas:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\cgrid0 //:! C25:Trash.dat
\par Glass:54, Paper:22, Paper:11, Glass:17,
\par Aluminum:89, Paper:88, Aluminum:76, Cardboard:96,
\par Aluminum:25, Aluminum:34, Glass:11, Glass:68,
\par Glass:43, Aluminum:27, Cardboard:44, Aluminum:18,
\par Paper:91, Glass:63, Glass:50, Glass:80,
\par Aluminum:81, Cardboard:12, Glass:12, Glass:54,
\par Aluminum:36, Aluminum:93, Glass:93, Paper:80,
\par Glass:36, Glass:12, Glass:60, Paper:66,
\par Aluminum:36, Cardboard:22,
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\cgrid0 ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\cgrid0 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {To parse this, the line is read and the }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {String\: indexOf(\~)}}}{\b string }{member function }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright 
\b\v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {\b indexOf(\~)}}}{\b find(\~)}{ produces the index of the \lquote }{\b :}{\rquote . This is first used with the }{\b string }{member function }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {substring(\~)}}}
{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {String\:substring(\~)}}}{\b substr(\~) }{to extract the name of the trash type, and next to get the weight that is turned into a }{\b double }{with the }{\b atof(\~) }{function (from }{\b 
<cstdlib>}{).
\par The }{\b Trash }{file parser is placed in a separate file since it will be reused throughout this chapter. To facilitate this reuse, the function }{\b fillBin(\~)}{
 which does the work takes as its first argument the name of the file to open and read, and as its second argument a reference to an object of type }{\b Fillable}{. This uses what I\rquote ve named the \ldblquote interface\rdblquote  idiom at the begi
nning of the chapter, and the only attribute for this particular interface is that \ldblquote it can be filled,\rdblquote  via a member function }{\b addTrash(\~)}{. Here\rquote s the header file for }{\b Fillable}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:Fillable.h
\par // Any object that can be filled with Trash
\par #ifndef FILLABLE_H
\par #define FILLABLE_H
\par 
\par class Fillable \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void addTrash(Trash* t) = 0;
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // FILLABLE_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that it follows the interface idiom of having no non-static data members, and all pure }{\b virtual}{ member functions.
\par This way, any class which implements this interface (typically using multiple inheritance) can be filled using }{\b fillBin(\~)}{. Here\rquote s the header file:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:fillBin.h
\par // Open a file and parse its contents into
\par // Trash objects, placing each into a vector
\par #ifndef FILLBIN_H
\par #define FILLBIN_H
\par #include "Fillablevector.h"
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <string>
\par 
\par void 
\par fillBin(std::string filename, Fillable& bin);
\par 
\par // Special case to handle vector:
\par inline void fillBin(std::string filename, 
\par   std::vector<Trash*>& bin) \{
\par   Fillablevector fv(bin);
\par   fillBin(filename, fv);
\par \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // FILLBIN_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The overloaded version will be discussed shortly. First, here is the implementation:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:fillBin.cpp \{O\}
\par // Implementation of fillBin()
\par #include "fillBin.h"
\par #include "Fillable.h"
\par #include "../C17/trim.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par void fillBin(string filename, Fillable& bin) \{
\par   ifstream in(filename.c_str());
\par   assure(in, filename.c_str());
\par   string s;
\par   while(getline(in, s)) \{
\par     int comma = s.find(',');
\par     // Parse each line into entries:
\par     while(comma != string::npos) \{
\par       string e = trim(s.substr(0,comma));
\par       // Parse each entry:
\par       int colon = e.find(':');
\par       string type = e.substr(0, colon);
\par       double weight = 
\par         atof(e.substr(colon + 1).c_str());
\par       bin.addTrash(
\par         Trash::factory(
\par           Trash::Info(type, weight)));
\par       // Move to next part of line:
\par       s = s.substr(comma + 1);
\par       comma = s.find(',');
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
After the file is opened, each line is read and parsed into entries by looking for the separating comma, then each entry is parsed into its type and weight by looking for the separating colon. Note the convenience of using the }{\b trim(\~)}{
 function from chapter 17 to remove the white space from both ends of a }{\b string}{. Once the type and weight are discovered, an }{\b Info }{object is created from that data and passed to the }{\b factory(\~)}{. The result of this call is a }{\b Trash*}
{ which is passed to the}{\b  addTrash(\~)}{ function of the }{\b bin}{ (which is the only function, remember, that a }{\b Fillable}{ guarantees).
\par Anything that supports the }{\b Fillable}{ interface can be used with }{\b fillBin(\~)}{. Of course, }{\b vector}{ doesn\rquote t implement }{\b Fillable}{, so it won\rquote t work. Since }{\b vector}{ is used in most of the example
s, it makes sense to add the second overloaded }{\b fillBin(\~)}{ function that takes a }{\b vector}{, as seen previously in }{\b fillBin.h}{. But how to make a }{\b vector<Trash*> }{adapt to the }{\b Fillable }{interface, which says it must have an }{\b 
addTrash(\~) }{member function? The key is in the word \ldblquote adapt\rdblquote ; we use the adapter pattern to create a class that has a }{\b vector }{and is also }{\b Fillable}{.
\par By saying \ldblquote is also }{\b Fillable}{,\rdblquote  the hint is strong (is-a) to inherit from }{\b Fillable}{. But what about the }{\b vector<Trash*>}{? Should this new class inherit from that? We don\rquote 
t actually want to be making a new kind of }{\b vector}{, which would force everyone to only use our }{\b vector}{ in this situation. Instead, we want someone to be able to have their own }{\b vector}{ and say \ldblquote please fill this.\rdblquote 
 So the new class should just keep a reference to that }{\b vector}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:Fillablevector.h
\par // Adapter that makes a vector<Trash*> Fillable
\par #ifndef FILLABLEVECTOR_H
\par #define FILLABLEVECTOR_H
\par #include "Trash.h"
\par #include "Fillable.h"
\par #include <vector>
\par 
\par class Fillablevector : public Fillable \{
\par   std::vector<Trash*>& v;
\par public:
\par   Fillablevector(std::vector<Trash*>& vv) 
\par     : v(vv) \{\}
\par   void addTrash(Trash* t) \{ v.push_back(t); \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // FILLABLEVECTOR_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You can see that the only job of this class is to connect }{\b Fillable}{\rquote s }{\b addTrash(\~)}{ member function to }{\b vector}{\rquote s }{\b push_back(\~)}{ (that\rquote s the \ldblquote 
adapter\rdblquote  motivation). With this class in hand, the overloaded }{\b fillBin(\~)}{ member function can be used with a }{\b vector}{ in }{\b fillBin.h}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {inline void fillBin(std::string filename, 
\par   std::vector<Trash*>& bin) \{
\par   Fillablevector fv(bin);
\par   fillBin(filename, fv);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Notice that the adapter object }{\b fv}{ only exists for the duration of the function call, and it wraps }{\b bin}{ in an interface that works with the other }{\b fillBin(\~)}{ function.
\par This approach works for any container class that\rquote s used frequently. Altern}{a}{tively, the container can multiply inherit from }{\b Fill}{\b a}{\b ble}{. (You\rquote ll see this later, in }{\b DynaTrash.cpp}{.)
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Recycling with prototyping
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now you can see the new version of the recycling solution using the }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {prototype\: design pattern}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {design patterns\: prototype}}}{prototyping technique:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:Recycle3.cpp
\par //\{L\} TrashPrototypeInit
\par //\{L\} fillBin Trash TrashStatics
\par // Recycling with RTTI and Prototypes
\par #include "Trash.h"
\par #include "Aluminum.h"
\par #include "Paper.h"
\par #include "Glass.h"
\par #include "fillBin.h"
\par #include "sumValue.h"
\par #include "../purge.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <vector>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("Recycle3.out");
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<Trash*> bin;
\par   // Fill up the Trash bin:
\par   fillBin("Trash.dat", bin);
\par   vector<Aluminum*> alBin;
\par   vector<Paper*> paperBin;
\par   vector<Glass*> glassBin;
\par   vector<Trash*>::iterator it = bin.begin();
\par   while(it != bin.end()) \{
\par     // Sort the Trash:
\par     Aluminum* ap = 
\par       dynamic_cast<Aluminum*>(*it);
\par     Paper* pp = dynamic_cast<Paper*>(*it);
\par     Glass* gp = dynamic_cast<Glass*>(*it);
\par     if(ap) alBin.push_back(ap);
\par     if(pp) paperBin.push_back(pp);
\par     if(gp) glassBin.push_back(gp);
\par     it++;
\par   \}
\par   sumValue(alBin);
\par   sumValue(paperBin);
\par   sumValue(glassBin);
\par   sumValue(bin);
\par   purge(bin);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The process of opening the data file containing }{\b Trash}{ descriptions and the parsing of that file have been wrapped into }{\b fillBin(\~)}{, so now it\rquote 
s no longer a part of our design focus. You will see that throughout the rest of the chapter, no matter what new classes are added, }{\b fillBin(\~)}{ will continue to work without change, which indicates a good design.
\par In terms of object creation, this design does indeed severely localize the changes you need to make to add a new type to the system. However, there\rquote 
s a significant problem in the use of RTTI that shows up clearly here. The program seems to run fine, and yet it never detects any cardboard, even though there is cardboard in the list of trash data! This happens }{\i because}{ of the use of RTTI, which
 looks for only the types that you tell it to look for. The clue that }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {RTTI\: misuse of RTTI}}}{RTTI is being misused is that }{\i every type in the system }{
is being tested, rather than a single type or subset of types. But if you forget to test for your new type, the compiler has nothing to say about it. 
\par As you will see later, there are ways to use polymorphism instead when you\rquote re testing for every type. But if you use RTTI a lot in this fashion, and you add a new type to your system, you can easily forget t
o make the necessary changes in your program and produce a difficult-to-find bug. So it\rquote s worth trying to eliminate RTTI in this case, not just for aesthetic reasons \endash  it produces more maintainable code.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc375545416}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018802}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776539}Abstracting usage{\*\bkmkend _Toc375545416
}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018802}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776539}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {With creation out of the way, it\rquote s time to tackle the remainder of the design: where the classes are used. Since it\rquote s the act of sorting into bins that\rquote s pa}{r}{
ticularly ugly and exposed, why not take that process and hide it inside a class? This is simple \ldblquote complexity hiding,\rdblquote  the principle of \ldblquote If you must do something ugly, at least localize the ugliness.\rdblquote 
 In an OOP language, the best place to hide complexity is inside a class. Here\rquote s a first cut:
