pending on what type of }{\b Trash}{ the current }{\b vector}{ is holding. That is, each }{\b vector}{ must be aware of the type it holds. This \ldblquote awareness\rdblquote  is accomplished with a }{\b virtual }{function, the }{\b grab(\~)}{
 function, which thus eliminates at least the outward appearance of the use of RTTI. The implementation of }{\b grab(\~) }{\i does}{ use RTTI, but it\rquote s templatized so as long as you put a new }{\b TrashBin}{ in the }{\b TrashSorter}{
 when you add a type, everything else is taken care of.
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright {Memory is managed by denoting }{\b bin}{ as the \ldblquote master container,\rdblquote  the one responsible for cleanup. With this rule in place, calling }{\b purge(\~)}{ for }{\b bin}{ cleans up all the }{\b Trash}{
 objects. In addition, }{\b TrashSorter}{ assumes that it \ldblquote owns\rdblquote  the pointers it holds, and cleans up all the }{\b TrashBin}{ objects during destruction.
\par A basic OOP design principle is \ldblquote Use data members for variation in state, use }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {polymorphism}}}{polymorphism for variation in behavior.\rdblquote  Your first thought might be that the 
}{\b grab(\~)}{ member function certainly behaves differently for a }{\b vector}{ that holds }{\b Paper}{ than for one that holds }{\b Glass}{. But what it does is strictly dependent on the type, and nothing else.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\b\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls249\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls249\adjustright {\b TbinList}{ holds a set of }{
\b Tbin}{ pointers, so that }{\b sort(\~)}{ can ite}{r}{ate through the }{\b Tbin}{s when it\rquote s looking for a match for the }{\b Trash}{ object you\rquote ve handed it.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\b\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls249\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls249\adjustright {\b sortBin(\~)}{
 allows you to pass an entire }{\b Tbin}{ in, and it moves through the }{\b Tbin}{, picks out each piece of }{\b Trash}{, and sorts it into the appropriate specific }{\b Tbin}{. Notice the genericity of this code: it doesn\rquote t change at all if new ty
pes are added. If the bulk of your code doesn\rquote t need changing when a new type is added (or some other change occurs) then you have an ea}{s}{ily-extensible system.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls249\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls249\adjustright {
Now you can see how easy it is to add a new type. Few lines must be changed to support the addition. If it\rquote s really important, you can squeeze out even more by further m}{a}{nipulating the design.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs20\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 4.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls249\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls249\adjustright {
One member function call causes the contents of }{\b bin}{ to be sorted into the respective specifically-typed bins.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc375545417}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018803}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776540}Applying double dispatching
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018803}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776540}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The above design is c
ertainly satisfactory. Adding new types to the system consists of adding or modifying distinct classes without causing code changes to be propagated throughout the system. In addition, RTTI is not as \ldblquote misused\rdblquote  as it was in }{\b 
Recycle1.cpp}{. However, it\rquote s possible to go one step further and eliminate RTTI alt}{o}{gether from the operation of sorting the trash into bins.
\par To accomplish this, you must first take the perspective that all type-dependent activities \endash  such as detecting the type of a piece of trash and pu}{t}{ting it into the appropriate bin \endash 
 should be controlled through polymorphism and dynamic binding.
\par The previous examples first sorted by type, then acted on sequences of el}{e}{ments that were all of a particular type. But whenever you find yourself picking out particular types, stop and think. The whole idea of polymo}{r}{
phism (dynamically-bound member function calls) is to handle type-specific inform}{a}{tion for you. So why are you hunting for types?
\par The multiple-dispatch pattern demonstrated at the beginning of this chapter uses }{\b virtual}{ functions to determine all type information, thus eliminating RTTI.
