The new hierarchy is }{\b TypedBin}{, and it contains its own member function called }{\b add(\~)}{ that is also used polymorphically. But here\rquote s an additional twist: }{\b add(\~)}{ is }{\i overloaded}{ to take arguments of the different types of }
{\b Trash}{. So an essential part of the double dispatching scheme also involves overloading (or at least having a group of virtual functions to call; overloading happens to be particularly convenient here).
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:TypedBin.h
\par #ifndef TYPEDBIN_H
\par #define TYPEDBIN_H
\par #include "Trash.h"
\par #include "Aluminum.h"
\par #include "Paper.h"
\par #include "Glass.h"
\par #include "Cardboard.h"
\par #include <vector>
\par 
\par // Template to generate double-dispatching
\par // trash types by inheriting from originals:
\par template<class TrashType> 
\par class DD : public TrashType \{
\par protected:
\par   DD() : TrashType(0) \{\}
\par   friend class TrashPrototypeInit;
\par public:
\par   DD(double wt) : TrashType(wt) \{\}
\par   bool addToBin(std::vector<TypedBin*>& tb) \{
\par     for(int i = 0; i < tb.size(); i++)
\par       if(tb[i]->add(this))
\par         return true;
\par     return false;
\par   \}
\par   // Override clone() to create this new type:
\par   Trash* clone(const Trash::Info& info) \{
\par     return new DD(info.data());
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // vector<Trash*> that knows how to 
\par // grab the right type
\par class TypedBin : public std::vector<Trash*> \{
\par protected:
\par   bool addIt(Trash* t) \{
\par     push_back(t);
\par     return true;
\par   \}
\par public:
\par   virtual bool add(DD<Aluminum>*) \{
\par     return false;
\par   \}
\par   virtual bool add(DD<Paper>*) \{
\par     return false;
\par   \}
\par   virtual bool add(DD<Glass>*) \{
\par     return false;
\par   \}
\par   virtual bool add(DD<Cardboard>*) \{
\par     return false;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Template to generate specific TypedBins:
\par template<class TrashType>
\par class BinOf : public TypedBin \{
\par public:
\par   // Only overrides add() for this specific type:
\par   bool add(TrashType* t) \{ return addIt(t); \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // TYPEDBIN_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In each particular subtype of }{\b Aluminum}{, }{\b Paper}{, }{\b Glass,}{ and }{\b Cardboard}{, the }{\b addToBin(\~)}{ member function is implemented, but it }{\i looks}{
 like the code is exactly the same in each case. The code in each }{\b addToBin(\~) }{calls }{\b add(\~)}{ for each }{\b TypedBin}{ object in the a}{r}{ray. But notice the argument: }{\b this}{. The type of }{\b this}{ is different for each su}{b}{
class of }{\b Trash}{, so the code is different. So this is the first part of the double dispatch, because once you\rquote re inside this member function you know you\rquote re }{\b Aluminum}{, or }{\b Paper}{, etc. During the call to }{\b add(\~)}{
, this information is passed via the type of }{\b this}{. The compiler resolves the call to the proper overloaded version of }{\b add(\~)}{. But}{\b  }{since }{\b tb[i]}{ produces a pointer to the base type }{\b TypedBin}{,}{\b  }{
this call will end up calling a different member function depending on the type of }{\b TypedBin}{ that\rquote s currently selected. That is the second di}{s}{patch.
\par You can see that the overloaded }{\b add(\~)}{ methods all return }{\b false}{. If the member function is not overloaded in a derived class, it will continue to r}{e}{turn }{\b false}{, and the caller (}{\b addToBin(\~)}{
, in this case) will assume that the current }{\b Trash}{ object has not been added successfully to a container, and continue searching for the right container.
\par In each of the subclasses of }{\b TypedBin}{, only one overloaded member function is ove}{r}{ridden, according to the type of bin that\rquote s being created. For example, }{\b Car}{\b d}{\b boardBin}{ overrides }{\b add(DD<Cardboard>)}{
. The overridden member function adds the }{\b Trash}{ pointer to its container and returns }{\b true}{, while all the rest of the }{\b add(\~) }{methods in }{\b CardboardBin }{continue to return }{\b false}{, since they haven\rquote 
t been overridden. With }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C++\: template}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {template\: in C++}}}{C++ }{\b template}{s, you don\rquote 
t have to explicitly write the subclasses or place the }{\b addToBin(\~)}{ member function in }{\b Trash}{. 
\par To set up for prototyping the new types of trash, there must be a different initializer file:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:DDTrashPrototypeInit.cpp \{O\}
\par #include "TypedBin.h"
\par #include "Aluminum.h"
\par #include "Paper.h"
\par #include "Glass.h"
\par #include "Cardboard.h"
\par 
\par std::vector<Trash*> Trash::prototypes;
\par 
\par class TrashPrototypeInit \{
\par   DD<Aluminum> a;
\par   DD<Paper> p;
\par   DD<Glass> g;
\par   DD<Cardboard> c;
\par   TrashPrototypeInit() \{
\par     Trash::prototypes.push_back(&a);
\par     Trash::prototypes.push_back(&p);
\par     Trash::prototypes.push_back(&g);
\par     Trash::prototypes.push_back(&c);
\par   \}
\par   static TrashPrototypeInit singleton;
\par \};
\par 
\par TrashPrototypeInit 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  TrashPrototypeInit::singleton; ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here\rquote s the rest of the program:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:DoubleDispatch.cpp
\par //\{L\} DDTrashPrototypeInit
\par //\{L\} fillBin Trash TrashStatics
\par // Using multiple dispatching to handle more than
\par // one unknown type during a member function call
\par #include "TypedBin.h"
\par #include "fillBin.h"
\par #include "sumValue.h"
\par #include "../purge.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("DoubleDispatch.out");
\par 
\par class TrashBinSet : public vector<TypedBin*> \{
\par public:
\par   TrashBinSet() \{
\par     push_back(new BinOf<DD<Aluminum> >());
\par     push_back(new BinOf<DD<Paper> >());
\par     push_back(new BinOf<DD<Glass> >());
\par     push_back(new BinOf<DD<Cardboard> >());
\par   \};
\par   void sortIntoBins(vector<Trash*>& bin) \{
\par     vector<Trash*>::iterator it;
\par     for(it = bin.begin(); it != bin.end(); it++)
\par       // Perform the double dispatch:
\par       if(!(*it)->addToBin(*this))
\par         cerr << "Couldn't add " << *it << endl;
\par   \}
\par   ~TrashBinSet() \{ purge(*this); \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<Trash*> bin;
\par   TrashBinSet bins;
\par   // fillBin() still works, without changes, but
\par   // different objects are cloned:
\par   fillBin("Trash.dat", bin);
\par   // Sort from the master bin into the
\par   // individually-typed bins:
\par   bins.sortIntoBins(bin);
\par   TrashBinSet::iterator it;
\par   for(it = bins.begin(); it != bins.end(); it++)
\par     sumValue(**it);
\par   // ... and for the master bin
\par   sumValue(bin);
\par   purge(bin);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b TrashBinSet}{ encapsulates all of the different types of }{\b TypedBin}{s, along with the }{\b sortIntoBins(\~)}{
 member function, which is where all the double dispatching takes place. You can see that once the structure is set up, sorting into the various }{\b Type}{\b d}{\b Bin}{s is remarkably easy. In addition, the efficiency of 
two virtual calls and the double dispatch is probably better than any other way you could sort.
\par Notice the ease of use of this system in }{\b main(\~)}{, as well as the complete i}{n}{dependence of any specific type information within }{\b main(\~)}{. All other met}{h}{ods that talk only to the }{\b Trash}{
 base-class interface will be equally invulnerable to changes in }{\b Trash}{ types.
\par The changes necessary to add a new type are relatively isolated: you i}{n}{herit the new type of }{\b Trash}{ with its }{\b addToBin(\~)}{ member function, then make a small modification to }{\b TypedBin}{
, and finally you add a new type into the vector in }{\b TrashBinSet}{ and modify }{\b DDTrashPrototypeInit.cpp}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018805}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776542}Applying the visitor pattern{\*\bkmkend _Toc375545417}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018805}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776542}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now consider applying a design pattern with an entirely different goal to the trash-sorting problem. As demonstrated earlier in this chapter, the visitor pattern\rquote 
s goal is to allow the addition of new polymorphic operations to a frozen inheritance hierarchy.
