{\sp{\sn fCalloutMinusX}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fCalloutMinusY}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fCalloutDropAuto}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fCalloutLengthSpecified}{\sv 0}}}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypara\dodhgt8199\dppolygon\dppolycount4\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx6711\dppty0
\dpptx6711\dppty5211\dpptx0\dppty5211\dpx3086\dpy0\dpxsize6711\dpysize5211\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr255\dpfillbgcg255\dpfillbgcb255\dpfillpat1\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{Now, if }{\b t}{ is a }{\b Trash }{
pointer to an }{\b Aluminum}{ object, the code:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {PriceVisitor pv;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {t->accept(pv);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {causes two polymorphic member function calls: the first one to select }{\b Aluminum}{\rquote s version of }{\b accept(\~)}{, and the second one within }{\b accept(\~)}{ when the specific version of }{
\b visit(\~)}{ is called dynamically using the base-class }{\b Visitor}{ pointer }{\b v}{.
\par This configuration means that new functionality can be added to the system in the form of new subclasses of }{\b Visitor}{. The }{\b Trash }{hierarchy doesn\rquote 
t need to be touched. This is the prime benefit of the visitor pattern: you can add new polymorphic functionality to a class hierarchy without touching that hiera}{r}{chy (once the }{\b accept(\~)}{
 methods have been installed). Note that the benefit is helpful here but not exactly what we started out to accomplish, so at first blush you might decide that this isn\rquote t the desired solution.
\par But look at one thing that\rquote s been accomplished: the visitor solution avoids sorting from the master }{\b Trash}{
 sequence into individual typed sequences. Thus, you can leave everything in the single master sequence and simply pass through that sequence using the appropriate visitor to accomplish th
e goal. Although this behavior seems to be a side effect of visitor, it does give us what we want (avoiding RTTI).
\par The }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {double dispatching}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {dispatching\: double dispatching}}}{
double dispatching in the visitor pattern takes care of determining both the type of }{\b Trash }{and the type of }{\b Visitor}{.}{\b  }{In the following example, there are two implementations of }{\b Visitor}{: }{\b PriceVisitor}{
 to both determine and sum the price, and }{\b WeightVisitor}{ to keep track of the weights.
\par You can see all of this implemented in the new, improved version of the rec}{y}{cling program. As with }{\b DoubleDispatch.cpp}{, the }{\b Trash}{ class has had an extra member function stub (}{\b accept(\~)}{)}{\b  }{
inserted in it to allow for this example.
\par Since there\rquote s nothing concrete in the }{\b Visitor}{ base class, it can be created as an }{\b interface}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:Visitor.h
\par // The base interface for visitors
\par // and template for visitable Trash types
\par #ifndef VISITOR_H
\par #define VISITOR_H
\par #include "Trash.h"
\par #include "Aluminum.h"
\par #include "Paper.h"
\par #include "Glass.h"
\par #include "Cardboard.h"
\par 
\par class Visitor \{
\par public:
\par   virtual void visit(Aluminum* a) = 0;
\par   virtual void visit(Paper* p) = 0;
\par   virtual void visit(Glass* g) = 0;
\par   virtual void visit(Cardboard* c) = 0;
\par \};
\par 
\par // Template to generate visitable 
\par // trash types by inheriting from originals:
\par template<class TrashType> 
\par class Visitable : public TrashType \{
\par protected:
\par   Visitable () : TrashType(0) \{\}
\par   friend class TrashPrototypeInit;
\par public:
\par   Visitable(double wt) : TrashType(wt) \{\}
\par   // Remember "this" is pointer to current type:
\par   void accept(Visitor& v) \{ v.visit(this); \}
\par   // Override clone() to create this new type:
\par   Trash* clone(const Trash::Info& info) \{
\par     return new Visitable(info.data());
\par   \}
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // VISITOR_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {As before, a different version of the initialization file is necessary:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:VisitorTrashPrototypeInit.cpp \{O\}
\par #include "Visitor.h"
\par 
\par std::vector<Trash*> Trash::prototypes;
\par 
\par class TrashPrototypeInit \{
\par   Visitable<Aluminum> a;
\par   Visitable<Paper> p;
\par   Visitable<Glass> g;
\par   Visitable<Cardboard> c;
\par   TrashPrototypeInit() \{
\par     Trash::prototypes.push_back(&a);
\par     Trash::prototypes.push_back(&p);
\par     Trash::prototypes.push_back(&g);
\par     Trash::prototypes.push_back(&c);
\par   \}
\par   static TrashPrototypeInit singleton;
\par \};
\par 
\par TrashPrototypeInit 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {  TrashPrototypeInit::singleton; ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The rest of the program creates specific }{\b Visitor}{ types and sends them through a single list of }{\b Trash}{ objects:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:TrashVisitor.cpp
\par //\{L\} VisitorTrashPrototypeInit
\par //\{L\} fillBin Trash TrashStatics 
\par // The "visitor" pattern
\par #include "Visitor.h"
\par #include "fillBin.h"
\par #include "../purge.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("TrashVisitor.out");
\par 
\par // Specific group of algorithms packaged
\par // in each implementation of Visitor:
\par class PriceVisitor : public Visitor \{
\par   double alSum; // Aluminum
\par   double pSum; // Paper
\par   double gSum; // Glass
\par   double cSum; // Cardboard
\par public:
\par   void visit(Aluminum* al) \{
\par     double v = al->weight() * al->value();
\par     out << "value of Aluminum= " << v << endl;
\par     alSum += v;
\par   \}
\par   void visit(Paper* p) \{
\par     double v = p->weight() * p->value();
\par     out << 
\par       "value of Paper= " << v << endl;
\par     pSum += v;
\par   \}
\par   void visit(Glass* g) \{
\par     double v = g->weight() * g->value();
\par     out << 
\par       "value of Glass= " << v << endl;
\par     gSum += v;
\par   \}
\par   void visit(Cardboard* c) \{
\par     double v = c->weight() * c->value();
\par     out << 
\par       "value of Cardboard = " << v << endl;
\par     cSum += v;
\par   \}
\par   void total(ostream& os) \{
\par     os <<
\par       "Total Aluminum: $" << alSum << "\\n" <<
\par       "Total Paper: $" << pSum << "\\n" <<
\par       "Total Glass: $" << gSum << "\\n" <<
\par       "Total Cardboard: $" << cSum << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class WeightVisitor : public Visitor \{
\par   double alSum; // Aluminum
\par   double pSum; // Paper
\par   double gSum; // Glass
\par   double cSum; // Cardboard
\par public:
\par   void visit(Aluminum* al) \{
\par     alSum += al->weight();
\par     out << "weight of Aluminum = "
\par         << al->weight() << endl;
\par   \}
\par   void visit(Paper* p) \{
\par     pSum += p->weight();
\par     out << "weight of Paper = " 
\par       << p->weight() << endl;
\par   \}
\par   void visit(Glass* g) \{
\par     gSum += g->weight();
\par     out << "weight of Glass = "
\par         << g->weight() << endl;
\par   \}
\par   void visit(Cardboard* c) \{
\par     cSum += c->weight();
\par     out << "weight of Cardboard = "
\par         << c->weight() << endl;
\par   \}
\par   void total(ostream& os) \{
\par     os << "Total weight Aluminum:"
\par        << alSum << endl;
\par     os << "Total weight Paper:"
\par        << pSum << endl;
\par     os << "Total weight Glass:"
\par        << gSum << endl;
\par     os << "Total weight Cardboard:" 
\par        << cSum << endl;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   vector<Trash*> bin;
\par   // fillBin() still works, without changes, but
\par   // different objects are prototyped:
\par   fillBin("Trash.dat", bin);
\par   // You could even iterate through
\par   // a list of visitors!
\par   PriceVisitor pv;
\par   WeightVisitor wv;
\par   vector<Trash*>::iterator it = bin.begin();
\par   while(it != bin.end()) \{
\par     (*it)->accept(pv);
\par     (*it)->accept(wv);
\par     it++;
\par   \}
\par   pv.total(out);
\par   wv.total(out);
\par   purge(bin);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Note that the shape of }{\b main(\~)}{ has changed again. Now there\rquote s only a single }{\b Trash}{ bin. The two }{\b Visitor}{ objects are accepted into every element in the s}{e}{
quence, and they perform their operations. The visitors keep their own inte}{r}{nal data to tally the total weights and prices.
\par Finally, there\rquote s no run-time type identification other than the inevitable cast to }{\b Trash}{ when pulling things out of the sequence. 
\par One way you can distinguish this solution from the double dispatching sol}{u}{tion described previously is to note that, in the double dispatching solution, only one of the overloaded methods, }{\b add(\~)}{, was overridden when each su}{b}{
class was created, while here }{\i each}{ one of the overloaded }{\b visit(\~)}{ methods is overridden in every subclass of }{\b Visitor}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {More coupling?
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There\rquote s a lot more code here, and there\rquote s definite coupling between the }{\b Trash}{ hierarchy and the }{\b Visitor}{ hierarchy. However, there\rquote 
s also high cohesion within the respective sets of classes: they each do only one thing (}{\b Trash }{d}{e}{scribes trash, while }{\b Visitor }{describes actions performed on }{\b Trash}{), which is an indicator of a good des
ign. Of course, in this case it works well only if you\rquote re adding new }{\b Visitor}{s, but it gets in the way when you add new types of }{\b Trash}{.
\par Low coupling between classes and high cohesion within a class is definitely an important design goal. Applied mindlessly, though, it can prevent you from achieving a more elegant design. It seems that some classes i}{n}{
evitably have a certain intimacy with each other. These often occur in pairs that could perhaps be called }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {couplet}}}{\i couplets}{, for example, containers and iterators. The }{\b 
Trash-Visitor}{ pair above appears to be another such couplet.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc375545419}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018806}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776543}RTTI considered harmful?
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018806}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776543}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Various designs in this chapter attempt to remove RTTI, which might give you the impression that it\rquote s \ldblquote considered harmful\rdblquote  (the condemnation used for poor }{\b goto}{
). This isn\rquote t true; it is the }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {RTTI\: misuse of RTTI}}}{\i misuse}{ of RTTI that is the problem. The reason our designs removed RTTI is b}{e}{
cause the misapplication of that feature prevented }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {extensible}}}{extensibility, which contravened the stated goa
l of adding a new type to the system with as little impact on surrounding code as possible. Since RTTI is often misused by having it look for every single type in your system, it causes code to be non-extensible: when you add a new type, you have to go hu
nting for all the code in which RTTI is used, and if you miss any you won\rquote t get help from the compiler.
\par However, RTTI doesn\rquote t automatically create non-extensible code. Let\rquote s revisit the trash recycler once more. This time, a new tool will be introduced, which I call a }{\b TypeMap}{. It inherits from a }{\b map}{ that holds a variant of }{\b 
type_info }{object as the key, and }{\b vector<Trash*>}{ as the value. The interface is simple: you call }{\b addTrash(\~)}{ to add a new }{\b Trash}{ pointer, and the }{\b map }{class provides the rest of the interface
. The keys represent the types contained in the associated }{\b vector}{. The beauty of this design (suggested by Larry O\rquote Brien) is that the }{\b TypeMap}{
 dynamically adds a new key-value pair whenever it encounters a new type, so whenever you add a new type to the system (even if you add the new type at runtime), it adapts.
\par The example will again build on the structure of the }{\b Trash}{ types, and will use }{\b fillBin(\~) }{to parse and insert the values into the }{\b TypeMap}{. However, }{\b TypeMap}{ is not a }{\b vector<Trash*>}{, and so it must be
 adapted to work with }{\b fillBin(\~)}{ by multiply inheriting from }{\b Fillable}{. In addition, the Standard C++ }{\b type_info}{ class is too restrictive to be used as a key, so a kind of wrapper class }{\b TypeInfo}{
 is created, which simply extracts and stores the }{\b type_info}{ }{\b char*}{ representation of the type (making the assumption that, within the realm of a single compiler, this representation will be unique for each type).
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C25:DynaTrash.cpp
\par //\{L\} TrashPrototypeInit
\par //\{L\} fillBin Trash TrashStatics
\par // Using a map of vectors and RTTI
\par // to automatically sort Trash into
\par // vectors. This solution, despite the
\par // use of RTTI, is extensible.
\par #include "Trash.h"
\par #include "fillBin.h"
\par #include "sumValue.h"
\par #include "../purge.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <map>
\par #include <typeinfo>
\par using namespace std;
\par ofstream out("DynaTrash.out");
\par 
\par // Must adapt from type_info in Standard C++,
\par // since type_info is too restrictive:
\par template<class T> // T should be a base class
\par class TypeInfo \{
\par   string id;
\par public:
\par   TypeInfo(T* t) : id(typeid(*t).name()) \{\}
\par   const string& name() \{ return id; \}
\par   friend bool operator<(const TypeInfo& lv,
\par     const TypeInfo& rv)\{
\par     return lv.id < rv.id;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class TypeMap : 
\par   public map<TypeInfo<Trash>, vector<Trash*> >,
\par   public Fillable \{
\par public:
\par   // Satisfies the Fillable interface:
\par   void addTrash(Trash* t) \{
\par     (*this)[TypeInfo<Trash>(t)].push_back(t);
\par   \}
\par   ~TypeMap() \{
\par     for(iterator it = begin(); it != end(); it++)
\par       purge((*it).second);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   TypeMap bin;
\par   fillBin("Trash.dat", bin); // Sorting happens
\par   TypeMap::iterator it;
\par   for(it = bin.begin(); it != bin.end(); it++)
\par     sumValue((*it).second);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
