\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b TypeInfo}{ is templatized because }{\b typeid(\~)}{ does not allow the use of }{\b void*}{, which would be 
the most general way to solve the problem. So you are required to work with some specific class, but this class should be the most base of all the classes in your hierarchy. }{\b TypeInfo}{ must define an }{\b operator<}{ because a }{\b map}{
 needs it to order its keys.
\par Although powerful, the definition for }{\b TypeMap}{ is simple; the }{\b addTrash(\~)}{ member function does most of the work. When you add a new }{\b Trash }{pointer, the a }{\b TypeInfo<Trash> }{
object for that type is generated. This is used as a key to determine whether a }{\b vector}{ holding objects of that type is already present in the }{\b map}{. If so, the }{\b Trash }{pointer is added to that }{\b vector}{. If not, the }{\b TypeInfo }{
object and a new }{\b vector}{ are added as a key-value pair.
\par An iterator to the map, when dereferenced, produces a }{\b pair }{object where the key}{\b  }{(}{\b TypeInfo}{)}{\b  }{is the }{\b first}{ member, and the value (}{\b Vector<Trash*>}{)}{\b  }{is the }{\b second }{member. And that\rquote 
s all there is to it.
\par The }{\b TypeMap }{takes advantage of the design of }{\b fillBin(\~)}{, which doesn\rquote t just try to fill a }{\b vector}{ but instead an}{y}{thing that implements the }{\b Fillable}{ interface with its }{\b addTrash(\~)}{ member function. Since }{\b 
TypeMap }{is multiply inherited from }{\b Fill}{\b a}{\b ble}{, it can be used as an argument to }{\b fillBin(\~)}{ like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 \adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {fillBin("Trash.dat", bin);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {An interesting thing about this design is that even though it wasn\rquote t created to handle the sorting, }{\b fillBin(\~)}{ is performing a sort every time it inserts a }{\b Trash}{ pointer into }{
\b bin}{. When the }{\b Trash}{ is thrown into }{\b bin}{ it\rquote s immediately sorted by }{\b TypeMap}{\rquote s internal sorting mechanism. Stepping through the }{\b TypeMap}{ and operating on each individual }{\b vector}{
 becomes a simple matter, and uses ordinary STL syntax.
\par As you can see, adding a new type to the system won\rquote t affect this code at all, nor the code in }{\b TypeMap}{. This is certainly the smallest solution to the problem, and arguably 
the most elegant as well. It does rely heavily on RTTI, but notice that each key-value pair in the }{\b map}{ is looking for only one type. In a}{d}{dition, there\rquote s no way you can \ldblquote forget\rdblquote 
 to add the proper code to this system when you add a new type, since there isn\rquote t any code you need to add, other than that which supports the prototyping process (and you\rquote ll find out right away if you forget that).
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018807}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776544}Summary{\*\bkmkend _Toc375545419}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018807}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776544}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Coming up with a design such as }{\b TrashVisitor.cpp }{that contains a larger amount of code than the earlier designs can seem at first to be counterpr}{o}{ductive. It pays to notice what you\rquote 
re trying to accomplish with various d}{e}{signs. Design patterns in general strive to }{\i separate the things that change from the things that stay the same}{. The \ldblquote things that change\rdblquote  can refer to many di}{f}{
ferent kinds of changes. Perhaps the change occurs because the program is placed into a new environment or because something in the current enviro}{n}{ment changes (this could be: \ldblquote The user wants to add a new shape to the di}{a}{
gram currently on the screen\rdblquote ). Or, as in this case, the change could be the evolution of the code body. While previous versions of the trash-sorting example emphasized the addition of new }{\i types}{ of }{\b Trash }{to the system, }{\b 
TrashVis}{\b i}{\b tor.cpp}{ allows you to easily add new }{\i functionality}{ without disturbing the }{\b Trash}{ hierarchy. There\rquote s more code in }{\b TrashVisitor.cpp}{, but adding new functionality to }{\b Visitor}{
 is cheap. If this is something that happens a lot, then it\rquote s worth the extra effort and code to make it happen more easily.
\par The discovery of the }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {vector of change}}}{vector of change is no trivial matter; it\rquote 
s not something that an analyst can usually detect before the program sees its initial design. The necessary information will probably not appear until later phases in the project: sometimes only at the design or implementation phases do you di}{s}{
cover a deeper or more subtle need in your system. In the case of adding new types (which was the focus of most of the \ldblquote recycle\rdblquote  examples) you might r}{e}{alize that you need a particular inherita
nce hierarchy only when you are in the maintenance phase and you begin extending the system!
\par One of the most important things that you\rquote ll learn by studying design patterns seems to be an about-face from what has been promoted so far in this book. That is: \ldblquote OOP is all about polymorphism.\rdblquote  This statement can produce the 
\ldblquote two-year-old with a hammer\rdblquote  syndrome (everything looks like a nail). Put another way, it\rquote s hard enough to \ldblquote get\rdblquote  polymorphism, and once you do, you try to cast all your designs into that one particular mold.

\par What design patterns say is that OOP isn\rquote t just about polymorphism. It\rquote s about \ldblquote separating the things that change from the things that stay the same.\rdblquote  }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {
polymorphism}}}{Polymorphism is an especially important way to do this, and it turns out to be helpful if the programming language directly supports polymo}{r}{phism (so you don\rquote 
t have to wire it in yourself, which would tend to make it prohibitively expensive). But design patterns in general show }{\i other}{ ways to accomplish the basic goal, and 
once your eyes have been opened to this you will begin to search for more creative designs.
\par Since the }{\i Design Patterns}{ book came out and made such an impact, people have been searching for other patterns. You can expect to see more of these appear as time goes on. Here are some sites recommended by Jim Coplien, of C++ fame (}{\i 
http://www.bell-labs.com/~cope}{), who is one of the main proponents of the patterns movement:
\par }\pard\plain \s47\sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \i\fs20\cgrid {http://st-www.cs.uiuc.edu/users/patterns\line http://c2.com/cgi/wiki\line http://c2.com/ppr\line http://www.bell-labs.com/people/cope/Patterns/Process/index.html\line 
http://www.bell-labs.com/cgi-user/OrgPatterns/OrgPatterns\line http://st-www.cs.uiuc.edu/cgi-bin/wikic/wikic\line http://www.cs.wustl.edu/~schmidt/patterns.html\line http://www.espinc.com/patterns/overview.html
\par }\pard\plain \s33\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {Also no
te there has been a yearly conference on design patterns, called PLOP, that produces a published proceedings. The third one of these proceedings came out in late 1997 (all published by Addison-Wesley).
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc375545420}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018808}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776545}Exercises{\*\bkmkend _Toc375545420}
