\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The code for this book is aut
omatically extracted directly from the ASCII text version of this book. The book is normally maintained in a word processor capable of producing camera-ready copy, automatically creating the table of contents and index, etc. To generate the code files, th
e
 book is saved into a plain ASCII text file, and the program in this section automatically extracts all the code files, places them in appropriate subdirectories, and generates all the makefiles. The entire contents of the book can then be built, for each
 compiler, by invoking a single }{\b make}{
 command. This way, the code listings in the book can be regularly tested and verified, and in addition various compilers can be tested for some degree of compliance with Standard C++ (the degree to which all the examples
 in the book can exercise a particular compiler, which is not too bad). 
\par The code in this book is designed to be as generic as possible, but it is only tested under two operating systems: 32-bit Windows and Linux (using the Gnu C++ compiler }{\b g++}{, which mean
s it should compile under other versions of Unix without too much trouble). You can easily get the latest sources for the book onto your machine by going to the web site }{\b www.BruceEckel.com}{
 and downloading the zipped archive containing all the code files and makefiles. If you unzip this you\rquote ll have the book\rquote 
s directory tree available. However, it may not be configured for your particular compiler or operating system. In this case, you can generate your own using the ASCII text file for the book (available at }{\b www.BruceEckel.com}{) and the }{\b 
ExtractCode.cpp }{program in this section. Using a text editor, you find the }{\b CompileDB.txt}{ file inside the ASCII text file for the book, edit it (leaving it the book\rquote s text file) to adapt it to your compiler and operating system,
 and then hand it to the ExtractCode program to generate your own source tree and makefiles.
\par You\rquote ve seen that each file to be extracted contains a starting marker (which includes the file name and path) and an ending marker. Files can be of any type, and if the colon after the comment is directly followed by a \lquote !\rquote 
 then the starting and ending marker lines are not reproduced in the generated file. In addition, you\rquote ve seen the other markers }{\b \{O\}}{, }{\b \{L\}}{, and }{\b \{T\}}{ that have been placed inside comments; these are us
ed to generate the makefile for each subdirectory.
\par If there\rquote s a mistake in the input file, then the program must report the error, which is the }{\b error(\~)}{ function at the beginning of the program. In addition, directory manipulation is not supported by the s
tandard libraries, so this is hidden away in the class }{\b OSDirControl}{. If you discover that this class will not compile on your system, you must replace the non-portable function calls in }{\b OSDirControl}{ with eqivalent calls from your library.

\par Although this program is very useful for distributing the code in the book, you\rquote ll see that it\rquote 
s also a useful example in its own right, since it partitions everything into sensible objects and also makes heavy use of the STL and the standard }{\b string}{ class. You may note tha
t one or two pieces of code might be duplicated from other parts of the book, and you might observe that some of the tools created within the program might have been broken out into their own reusable header files and }{\b cpp}{
 files. However, for easy unpacking of the book\rquote s source code it made more sense to keep everything lumped together in a single file.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:ExtractCode.cpp
\par // Automatically extracts code files from
\par // ASCII text of this book.
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <map>
\par #include <set>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par string copyright =
\par   "// From Thinking in C++, 2nd Edition\\n"
\par   "// Available at http://www.BruceEckel.com\\n"
\par   "// (c) Bruce Eckel 1999\\n"
\par   "// Copyright notice in Copyright.txt\\n";
\par 
\par string usage =
\par   " Usage:ExtractCode source\\n"
\par   "where source is the ASCII file containing \\n"
\par   "the embedded tagged sourcefiles. The ASCII \\n"
\par   "file must also contain an embedded compiler\\n"
\par   "configuration file called CompileDB.txt \\n"
\par   "See Thinking in C++, 2nd ed. for details\\n";
\par 
\par // Tool to remove the white space from both ends:
\par string trim(const string& s) \{
\par   if(s.length() == 0)
\par     return s;
\par   int b = s.find_first_not_of(" \\t");
\par   int e = s.find_last_not_of(" \\t");
\par   if(b == -1) // No non-spaces
\par     return "";
\par   return string(s, b, e - b + 1);
\par \}
\par 
\par // Manage all the error messaging:
\par void error(string problem, string message) \{
\par   static const string border(
\par   "-----------------------------------------\\n");
\par   class ErrReport \{
\par     int count;
\par     string fname;
\par   public:
\par     ofstream errs;
\par     ErrReport(char* fn = "ExtractCodeErrors.txt") 
\par       : count(0),fname(fn),errs(fname.c_str()) \{\}
\par     void operator++(int) \{ count++; \}
\par     ~ErrReport() \{
\par       errs.close();
\par       // Dump error messages to console
\par       ifstream in(fname.c_str());
\par       cerr << in.rdbuf() << endl;
\par       cerr << count << " Errors found" << endl;
\par       cerr << "Messages in " << fname << endl;
\par     \}
\par   \};
\par   // Created on first call to this function;
\par   // Destructor reports total errors:
\par   static ErrReport report;
\par   report++;
\par   report.errs << border << message << endl
\par     << "Problem spot: " << problem << endl;
\par \}
\par 
\par ///// OS-specific code, hidden inside a class:
\par #ifdef __GNUC__  // For egcs under Linux/Unix
\par #include <unistd.h>
\par #include <sys/stat.h>
\par #include <stdlib.h>
\par class OSDirControl \{
\par public:
\par   static string getCurrentDir() \{
\par     char path[PATH_MAX];
\par     getcwd(path, PATH_MAX);
\par     return string(path);
\par   \}
\par   static void makeDir(string dir) \{
\par     mkdir(dir.c_str(), 0777);
\par   \}
\par   static void changeDir(string dir) \{
\par     chdir(dir.c_str());
\par   \}
\par \};
\par #else // For Dos/Windows:
\par #include <direct.h>
\par class OSDirControl \{
\par public:
\par   static string getCurrentDir() \{
\par     char path[_MAX_PATH];
\par     getcwd(path, _MAX_PATH);
\par     return string(path);
\par   \}
\par   static void makeDir(string dir) \{
\par     mkdir(dir.c_str());
\par   \}
\par   static void changeDir(string dir) \{
\par     chdir(dir.c_str());
\par   \}
\par \};
\par #endif ///// End of OS-specific code
\par 
\par class PushDirectory \{
\par   string oldpath;
\par public:
\par   PushDirectory(string path);
\par   ~PushDirectory() \{
\par     OSDirControl::changeDir(oldpath);
\par   \}
\par   void pushOneDir(string dir) \{
\par     OSDirControl::makeDir(dir);
\par     OSDirControl::changeDir(dir);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par PushDirectory::PushDirectory(string path) \{
\par   oldpath = OSDirControl::getCurrentDir();
\par   while(path.length() != 0) \{
\par     int colon = path.find(':');
\par     if(colon != string::npos) \{
\par       pushOneDir(path.substr(0, colon));
\par       path = path.substr(colon + 1);
\par     \} else \{
\par       pushOneDir(path);
\par       return;
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par //--------------- Manage code files -------------
\par // A CodeFile object knows everything about a
\par // particular code file, including contents, path
\par // information, how to compile, link, and test 
\par // it, and which compilers it won't compile with.
\par enum TType \{header, object, executable, none\};
\par 
\par class CodeFile \{
\par   TType _targetType;
\par   string _rawName, // Original name from input
\par     _path, // Where the source file lives
\par     _file, // Name of the source file
\par     _base, // Name without extension
\par     _tname, // Target name
\par     _testArgs; // Command-line arguments
\par   vector<string>
\par     lines, // Contains the file
\par     _compile, // Compile dependencies
\par     _link; // How to link the executable
\par   set<string>
\par     _noBuild; // Compilers it won't compile with
\par   bool writeTags; // Whether to write the markers
\par   // Initial makefile processing for the file:
\par   void target(const string& s);
\par   // For quoted #include headers:
\par   void headerLine(const string& s);
\par   // For special dependency tag marks:
\par   void dependLine(const string& s);
\par public:
\par   CodeFile(istream& in, string& s);
\par   const string& rawName() \{ return _rawName; \}
\par   const string& path() \{ return _path; \}
\par   const string& file() \{ return _file; \}
\par   const string& base() \{ return _base; \}
\par   const string& targetName() \{ return _tname; \}
\par   TType targetType() \{ return _targetType; \}
\par   const vector<string>& compile() \{
\par     return _compile;
\par   \}
\par   const vector<string>& link() \{
\par     return _link;
\par   \}
\par   const set<string>& noBuild() \{
\par     return _noBuild;
\par   \}
\par   const string& testArgs() \{ return _testArgs; \}
\par   // Add a compiler it won't compile with:
\par   void addFailure(const string& failure) \{
\par     _noBuild.insert(failure);
\par   \}
\par   bool compilesOK(string compiler) \{
\par     return _noBuild.count(compiler) == 0;
\par   \}
\par   friend ostream&
\par   operator<<(ostream& os, const CodeFile& cf) \{
\par     copy(cf.lines.begin(), cf.lines.end(),
\par       ostream_iterator<string>(os, ""));
\par     return os;
\par   \}
\par   void write() \{
\par     PushDirectory pd(_path);
\par     ofstream listing(_file.c_str());
\par     listing << *this;  // Write the file
\par   \}
\par   void dumpInfo(ostream& os);
\par \};
\par 
\par void CodeFile::target(const string& s) \{
\par   // Find the base name of the file (without
\par   // the extension):
\par   int lastDot = _file.find_last_of('.');
\par   if(lastDot == string::npos) \{
\par     error(s, "Missing extension");
\par     exit(1);
\par   \}
\par   _base = _file.substr(0, lastDot);
\par   // Determine the type of file and target:
\par   if(s.find(".h") != string::npos ||
\par      s.find(".H") != string::npos) \{
\par     _targetType = header;
\par     _tname = _file;
\par     return;
\par   \}
\par   if(s.find(".txt") != string::npos
\par       || s.find(".TXT") != string::npos
\par       || s.find(".dat") != string::npos
\par       || s.find(".DAT") != string::npos) \{
\par     // Text file, not involved in make
\par     _targetType = none;
\par     _tname = _file;
\par     return;
\par   \}
\par   // C++ objs/exes depend on their own source:
\par   _compile.push_back(_file);
\par   if(s.find("\{O\}") != string::npos) \{
\par     // Don't build an executable from this file
\par     _targetType = object;
\par     _tname = _base;
\par   \} else \{
\par     _targetType = executable;
\par     _tname = _base;
\par     // The exe depends on its own object file:
\par     _link.push_back(_base);
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par void CodeFile::headerLine(const string& s) \{
\par   int start = s.find('\\"');
\par   int end = s.find('\\"', start + 1);
\par   int len = end - start - 1;
\par   _compile.push_back(s.substr(start + 1, len));
\par \}
\par 
\par void CodeFile::dependLine(const string& s) \{
\par   const string linktag("//\{L\} ");
\par   string deps = trim(s.substr(linktag.length()));
\par   while(true) \{
\par     int end = deps.find(' ');
\par     string dep = deps.substr(0, end);
\par     _link.push_back(dep);
\par     if(end == string::npos) // Last one
\par       break;
\par     else
\par       deps = trim(deps.substr(end));
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par CodeFile::CodeFile(istream& in, string& s) \{
\par   // If false, don't write begin & end tags:
\par   writeTags = (s[3] != '!');
\par   // Assume a space after the starting tag:
\par   _file = s.substr(s.find(' ') + 1);
\par   // There will always be at least one colon:
\par   int lastColon = _file.find_last_of(':');
\par   if(lastColon == string::npos) \{
\par     error(s, "Missing path");
\par     lastColon = 0; // Recover from error
\par   \}
\par   _rawName = trim(_file);
\par   _path = _file.substr(0, lastColon);
\par   _file = _file.substr(lastColon + 1);
\par   _file =_file.substr(0,_file.find_last_of(' '));
\par   cout << "path = [" << _path << "] "
\par     << "file = [" << _file << "]" << endl;
\par   target(s); // Determine target type
\par   if(writeTags)\{
\par     lines.push_back(s + '\\n');
\par     lines.push_back(copyright);
\par   \}
\par   string s2;
\par   while(getline(in, s2)) \{
\par     // Look for specified link dependencies:
\par     if(s2.find("//\{L\}") == 0) // 0: Start of line
\par       dependLine(s2);
\par     // Look for command-line arguments for test:
\par     if(s2.find("//\{T\}") == 0) // 0: Start of line
\par       _testArgs = s2.substr(strlen("//\{T\}") + 1);
\par     // Look for quoted includes:
\par     if(s2.find("#include \\"") != string::npos) \{
\par       headerLine(s2); // Grab makefile info
\par     \}
\par     // Look for end marker:
\par     if(s2.find("//" "/:~") != string::npos) \{
\par       if(writeTags)
\par         lines.push_back(s2 + '\\n');
\par       return;  // Found the end
\par     \}
\par     // Make sure you don't see another start:
\par     if(s2.find("//" ":") != string::npos
\par        || s2.find("/*" ":") != string::npos) \{
\par       error(s, "Error: new file started before"
\par         " previous file concluded");
\par       return;
\par     \}
\par     // Write ordinary line:
\par     lines.push_back(s2 + '\\n');
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par void CodeFile::dumpInfo(ostream& os) \{
\par   os << _path << ':' << _file << endl;
\par   os << "target: " << _tname << endl;
\par   os << "compile: " << endl;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < _compile.size(); i++)
\par     os << '\\t' << _compile[i] << endl;
\par   os << "link: " << endl;
\par   for(int i = 0; i < _link.size(); i++)
\par     os << '\\t' << _link[i] << endl;
\par   if(_noBuild.size() != 0) \{
\par     os << "Won't build with: " << endl;
\par     copy(_noBuild.begin(), _noBuild.end(),
\par       ostream_iterator<string>(os, "\\n"));
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par //--------- Manage compiler information ---------
\par class CompilerData \{
\par   // Information about each compiler:
\par   vector<string> rules; // Makefile rules
\par   set<string> fails; // Non-compiling files
\par   string objExtension; // File name extensions
\par   string exeExtension;
\par   // For OS-specific activities:
\par   bool _dos, _unix;
\par   // Store the information for all the compilers:
\par   static map<string, CompilerData> compilerInfo;
\par   static set<string> _compilerNames;
\par public:
\par   CompilerData() : _dos(false), _unix(false) \{\}
\par   // Read database of various compiler's 
\par   // information and failure listings for 
\par   // compiling the book files:
\par   static void readDB(istream& in);
\par   // For enumerating all the compiler names:
\par   static set<string>& compilerNames() \{
\par     return _compilerNames;
\par   \}
\par   // Tell this CodeFile which compilers
\par   // don't work with it:
\par   static void addFailures(CodeFile& cf);
\par   // Produce the proper object file name
\par   // extension for this compiler:
\par   static string obj(string compiler);
\par   // Produce the proper executable file name
\par   // extension for this compiler:
\par   static string exe(string compiler);
\par   // For inserting a particular compiler's
\par   // rules into a makefile:
\par   static void 
\par   writeRules(string compiler, ostream& os);
\par   // Change forward slashes to backward 
\par   // slashes if necessary:
\par   static string 
\par   adjustPath(string compiler, string path);
\par   // So you can ask if it's a Unix compiler:
\par   static bool isUnix(string compiler) \{
\par     return compilerInfo[compiler]._unix;
\par   \}
\par   // So you can ask if it's a dos compiler:
\par   static bool isDos(string compiler) \{
\par     return compilerInfo[compiler]._dos;
\par   \}
\par   // Display information (for debugging):
\par   static void dump(ostream& os = cout);
\par \};
\par 
\par // Static initialization:
\par map<string,CompilerData> 
\par   CompilerData::compilerInfo;
\par set<string> CompilerData::_compilerNames;
\par 
\par void CompilerData::readDB(istream& in) \{
\par   string compiler; // Name of current compiler
\par   string s;
\par   while(getline(in, s)) \{
\par     if(s.find("#//" "/:~") == 0)
\par       return; // Found end tag
\par     s = trim(s);
\par     if(s.length() == 0) continue; // Blank line
\par     if(s[0] == '#') continue; // Comment
\par     if(s[0] == '\{') \{ // Different compiler
\par       compiler = s.substr(0, s.find('\}'));
\par       compiler = trim(compiler.substr(1));
\par       if(compiler.length() != 0)
\par         _compilerNames.insert(compiler);
\par       continue; // Changed compiler name
\par     \}
\par     if(s[0] == '(') \{ // Object file extension
\par       string obj = s.substr(1);
\par       obj = trim(obj.substr(0, obj.find(')')));
\par       compilerInfo[compiler].objExtension =obj;
\par       continue;
\par     \}
\par     if(s[0] == '[') \{ // Executable extension
\par       string exe = s.substr(1);
\par       exe = trim(exe.substr(0, exe.find(']')));
\par       compilerInfo[compiler].exeExtension =exe;
\par       continue;
\par     \}
\par     if(s[0] == '&') \{ // Special directive
\par       if(s.find("dos") != string::npos)
\par         compilerInfo[compiler]._dos = true;
\par       else if(s.find("unix") != string::npos)
\par         compilerInfo[compiler]._unix = true;
\par       else
\par         error("Compiler Information Database", 
\par           "unknown special directive: " + s);
\par       continue;
\par     \}
\par     if(s[0] == '@') \{ // Makefile rule
\par       string rule(s.substr(1)); // Remove the @
\par       if(rule[0] == ' ') // Space means tab
\par         rule = '\\t' + trim(rule);
\par       compilerInfo[compiler].rules
\par         .push_back(rule); 
\par       continue;
\par     \}
\par     // Otherwise, it's a failure line:
\par     compilerInfo[compiler].fails.insert(s);
\par   \}
\par   error("CompileDB.txt","Missing end tag");
\par \}
\par 
\par void CompilerData::addFailures(CodeFile& cf) \{
\par   set<string>::iterator it = 
\par     _compilerNames.begin();
\par   while(it != _compilerNames.end()) \{
\par     if(compilerInfo[*it]
\par        .fails.count(cf.rawName()) != 0)
\par       cf.addFailure(*it);
\par     it++;
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par string CompilerData::obj(string compiler) \{
\par   if(compilerInfo.count(compiler) != 0) \{
\par     string ext(
\par       compilerInfo[compiler].objExtension);
\par     if(ext.length() != 0)
\par       ext = '.' + ext; // Use '.' if it exists
\par     return ext;
\par   \} else
\par     return "No such compiler information";
\par \}
\par 
\par string CompilerData::exe(string compiler) \{
\par   if(compilerInfo.count(compiler) != 0) \{
\par     string ext(
\par       compilerInfo[compiler].exeExtension);
\par     if(ext.length() != 0)
\par       ext = '.' + ext; // Use '.' if it exists
\par     return ext;
\par   \} else
\par     return "No such compiler information";
\par \}
\par 
\par void CompilerData::writeRules(
\par   string compiler, ostream& os) \{
\par   if(_compilerNames.count(compiler) == 0) \{
\par     os << "No info on this compiler" << endl;
\par     return;
\par   \}
\par   vector<string>& r = 
\par     compilerInfo[compiler].rules;
\par   copy(r.begin(), r.end(), 
\par     ostream_iterator<string>(os, "\\n"));
\par \}
\par 
\par string CompilerData::adjustPath(
\par   string compiler, string path) \{
\par   // Use STL replace() algorithm:
\par   if(compilerInfo[compiler]._dos)
\par     replace(path.begin(), path.end(), '/', '\\\\');
\par   return path;
\par \}
\par 
\par void CompilerData::dump(ostream& os) \{
\par   ostream_iterator<string> out(os, "\\n");
\par   *out++ = "Compiler Names:";
\par   copy(_compilerNames.begin(), 
\par     _compilerNames.end(), out);
\par   map<string, CompilerData>::iterator compIt;
\par   for(compIt = compilerInfo.begin(); 
\par     compIt != compilerInfo.end(); compIt++) \{
\par     os << "******************************\\n";
\par     os << "Compiler: [" << (*compIt).first <<
\par       "]" << endl;
\par     CompilerData& cd = (*compIt).second;
\par     os << "objExtension: " << cd.objExtension
\par       << "\\nexeExtension: " << cd.exeExtension 
\par       << endl;
\par     *out++ = "Rules:";
\par     copy(cd.rules.begin(), cd.rules.end(), out);
\par     cout << "Won't compile with: " << endl;
\par     copy(cd.fails.begin(), cd.fails.end(), out);
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par // ---------- Manage makefile creation ----------
\par // Create the makefile for this directory, based
\par // on each of the CodeFile entries:
\par class Makefile \{
\par   vector<CodeFile> codeFiles;
\par   // All the different paths 
\par   // (for creating the Master makefile):
\par   static set<string> paths;
\par   void 
\par   createMakefile(string compiler, string path);
\par public:
\par   Makefile() \{\}
\par   void addEntry(CodeFile& cf) \{
\par     paths.insert(cf.path()); // Record all paths
\par     // Tell it what compilers don't work with it:
\par     CompilerData::addFailures(cf);
\par     codeFiles.push_back(cf);
\par   \}
\par   // Write the makefile for each compiler:
\par   void writeMakefiles(string path);
\par   // Create the master makefile:
\par   static void writeMaster(string flag = "");
\par \};
\par 
\par // Static initialization:
\par set<string> Makefile::paths;
\par 
\par void Makefile::writeMakefiles(string path) \{
\par   if(trim(path).length() == 0)
\par     return; // No makefiles in root directory
\par   PushDirectory pd(path);
\par   set<string>& compilers = 
\par     CompilerData::compilerNames();
\par   set<string>::iterator it = compilers.begin();
\par   while(it != compilers.end())
\par     createMakefile(*it++, path);
\par \}
\par 
\par void Makefile::createMakefile(
\par   string compiler, string path) \{
\par   string // File name extensions:
\par     exe(CompilerData::exe(compiler)),
\par     obj(CompilerData::obj(compiler));
\par   string filename(compiler + ".makefile");
\par   ofstream makefile(filename.c_str());
\par   makefile << 
\par     "# From Thinking in C++, 2nd Edition\\n"
\par     "# At http://www.BruceEckel.com\\n"
\par     "# (c) Bruce Eckel 1999\\n"
\par     "# Copyright notice in Copyright.txt\\n"
\par     "# Automatically-generated MAKEFILE \\n"
\par     "# For examples in directory "+ path + "\\n"
\par     "# using the " + compiler + " compiler\\n"
\par     "# Note: does not make files that will \\n"
\par     "# not compile with this compiler\\n"
\par     "# Invoke with: make -f " 
\par     + compiler + ".makefile\\n"
\par     << endl;
\par   CompilerData::writeRules(compiler, makefile);
\par   vector<string> makeAll, makeTest, 
\par     makeBugs, makeDeps, linkCmd;
\par   // Write the "all" dependencies:
\par   makeAll.push_back("all: ");
\par   makeTest.push_back("test: all ");
\par   makeBugs.push_back("bugs: ");
\par   string line;
\par   vector<CodeFile>::iterator it;
\par   for(it = codeFiles.begin(); 
\par     it != codeFiles.end(); it++) \{
\par     CodeFile& cf = *it;
\par     if(cf.targetType() == executable) \{
\par       line = "\\\\\\n\\t"+cf.targetName()+ exe + ' ';
\par       if(cf.compilesOK(compiler) == false) \{
\par         makeBugs.push_back(
\par           CompilerData::adjustPath(
\par             compiler,line));
\par       \} else \{
\par         makeAll.push_back(
\par           CompilerData::adjustPath(
\par             compiler,line));
\par         line = "\\\\\\n\\t" + cf.targetName() + exe +
\par           ' ' + cf.testArgs() + ' ';
\par         makeTest.push_back(
\par           CompilerData::adjustPath(
\par             compiler,line));
\par       \}
\par       // Create the link command:
\par       int linkdeps = cf.link().size();
\par       string linklist;
\par       for(int i = 0; i < linkdeps; i++)
\par         linklist += 
\par           cf.link().operator[](i) + obj + " ";
\par       line = cf.targetName() + exe + ": "
\par         + linklist + "\\n\\t$(CPP) $(OFLAG)"
\par         + cf.targetName() + exe
\par         + ' ' + linklist + "\\n\\n";
\par       linkCmd.push_back(
\par         CompilerData::adjustPath(compiler,line));
\par     \}
\par     // Create dependencies
\par     if(cf.targetType() == executable
\par       || cf.targetType() == object) \{
\par       int compiledeps = cf.compile().size();
\par       string objlist(cf.base() + obj + ": ");
\par       for(int i = 0; i < compiledeps; i++)
\par         objlist += 
\par           cf.compile().operator[](i) + " ";
\par       makeDeps.push_back(
\par         CompilerData::adjustPath(
\par           compiler, objlist) +"\\n");
\par     \}      
\par   \}
\par   ostream_iterator<string> mkos(makefile, "");
\par   *mkos++ = "\\n";
\par   // The "all" target:
\par   copy(makeAll.begin(), makeAll.end(), mkos);
\par   *mkos++ = "\\n\\n";
\par   // Remove continuation marks from makeTest:
\par   vector<string>::iterator si = makeTest.begin();
\par   int bsl;
\par   for(; si != makeTest.end(); si++)
\par     if((bsl= (*si).find("\\\\\\n")) != string::npos)
\par       (*si).erase(bsl, strlen("\\\\"));
\par   // Now print the "test" target:
\par   copy(makeTest.begin(), makeTest.end(), mkos);
\par   *mkos++ = "\\n\\n";
\par   // The "bugs" target:
\par   copy(makeBugs.begin(), makeBugs.end(), mkos);
\par   if(makeBugs.size() == 1)
\par     *mkos++ = "\\n\\t@echo No compiler bugs in "
\par       "this directory!";
\par   *mkos++ = "\\n\\n";
\par   // Link commands:
\par   copy(linkCmd.begin(), linkCmd.end(), mkos);
\par   *mkos++ = "\\n";
\par   // Demendencies:
\par   copy(makeDeps.begin(), makeDeps.end(), mkos);
\par   *mkos++ = "\\n";
\par \}
\par 
\par void Makefile::writeMaster(string flag) \{
\par   string filename = "makefile";
\par   if(flag.length() != 0)
\par     filename += '.' + flag;
\par   ofstream makefile(filename.c_str());
\par   makefile << "# Master makefile for "
\par     "Thinking in C++, 2nd Ed. by Bruce Eckel\\n"
\par     "# at http://www.BruceEckel.com\\n"
\par     "# Compiles all the code in the book\\n"
\par     "# Copyright notice in Copyright.txt\\n\\n"
\par     "help: \\n"
\par     "\\t@echo To compile all programs from \\n"
\par     "\\t@echo Thinking in C++, 2nd Ed., type\\n"
\par     "\\t@echo one of the following commands,\\n"
\par     "\\t@echo according to your compiler:\\n";
\par   set<string>& n = CompilerData::compilerNames();
\par   set<string>::iterator nit;
\par   for(nit = n.begin(); nit != n.end(); nit++)
\par     makefile << 
\par       string("\\t@echo make " + *nit + "\\n");
\par   makefile << endl;
\par   // Make for each compiler:
\par   for(nit = n.begin(); nit != n.end(); nit++) \{
\par     makefile << *nit << ":\\n";
\par     for(set<string>::iterator it = paths.begin();
\par       it != paths.end(); it++) \{
\par       // Ignore the root directory:
\par       if((*it).length() == 0) continue;
\par       makefile << "\\tcd " << *it;
\par       // Different commands for unix vs. dos:
\par       if(CompilerData::isUnix(*nit))
\par         makefile << "; ";
\par       else
\par         makefile << "\\n\\t";
\par       makefile << "make -f " << *nit 
\par         << ".makefile";
\par       if(flag.length() != 0) \{
\par         makefile << ' ';
\par         if(flag == "bugs")
\par           makefile << "-i ";
\par         makefile << flag;
\par       \}
\par       makefile << "\\n";
\par       if(CompilerData::isUnix(*nit) == false)
\par         makefile << "\\tcd ..\\n";
\par     \}
\par     makefile << endl;
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   if(argc < 2) \{
\par     error("Command line error", usage);
\par     exit(1);
\par   \}
\par   // For development & testing, leave off notice:
\par   if(argc == 3)
\par     if(string(argv[2]) == "-nocopyright")
\par       copyright = "";
\par   // Open the input file to read the compiler
\par   // information database:
\par   ifstream in(argv[1]);
\par   if(!in) \{
\par     error(string("can't open ") + argv[1],usage);
\par     exit(1);
\par   \}
\par   string s;
\par   while(getline(in, s)) \{
\par     // Break up the strings to prevent a match when
\par     // this code is seen by this program:
\par     if(s.find("#:" " :CompileDB.txt") 
\par       != string::npos) \{
\par       // Parse the compiler information database:
\par       CompilerData::readDB(in);
\par       break; // Out of while loop
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par   if(in.eof())
\par     error("CompileDB.txt", "Can't find data");
\par   in.seekg(0, ios::beg); // Back to beginning
\par   map<string, Makefile> makeFiles;
\par   while(getline(in, s)) \{
\par     // Look for tag at beginning of line:
\par     if(s.find("//" ":") == 0
\par        || s.find("/*" ":") == 0
\par        || s.find("#" ":") == 0) \{
\par       CodeFile cf(in, s);
\par       cf.write();  // Tell it to write itself
\par       makeFiles[cf.path()].addEntry(cf);
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par   // Write all the makefiles, telling each
\par   // the path where it belongs:
\par   map<string, Makefile>::iterator mfi;
\par   for(mfi = makeFiles.begin(); 
\par     mfi != makeFiles.end(); mfi++)
\par     (*mfi).second.writeMakefiles((*mfi).first);
\par   // Create the master makefile:
\par   Makefile::writeMaster();
\par   // Write the makefile that tries the bug files:
\par   Makefile::writeMaster("bugs");
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The first tool you see is }{\b trim(\~)}{, which was lifted from the }{\b string}{
s chapter earlier in the book. It removes the whitespace from both ends of a }{\b string}{ object. This is followed by the }{\b usage}{ string which is printed whenever something goes wrong with the program.
\par The }{\b error(\~)}{ function is global because it uses a trick with static members of functions. }{\b error(\~) }{is designed so that if it is never called, no error reporting occurs, but if it is called one or more times then an
 error file is created and the total number of errors is reported at the end of the program execution. This is accomplished by creating a nested class }{\b ErrReport }{and making a }{\b static}{ }{\b ErrReport }{object inside }{\b error(\~)}{.}{\b  }{
That way, an }{\b ErrReport}{ object is only created the first time }{\b error(\~)}{ is called, so if }{\b error(\~)}{ is never called no error reporting will occur. }{\b ErrReport}{ creates an }{\b ofstream}{ to write the errors to, and the }{\b 
ErrReport }{destructor closes the }{\b ofstream}{, then re-opens it and dumps it to }{\b cerr}{. This way, if the error report is too long and scrolls off the screen, you can use an editor to look at it. The count of the number of errors is held in }{\b 
ErrReport}{, and this is also reported upon program termination.
\par The job of a }{\b PushDirectory}{ object is to capture the current directory, then created and move down each directory in the path (the path can be arbitrarily long). Each subdirectory in the file\rquote s path description is separated by a \lquote }{\b 
:}{\rquote  and the }{\b mkdir(\~)}{ and }{\b chdir(\~)}{ (or the equivalent on your system) are used to move into only one directory at a time, so the actual character that\rquote 
s used to separate directory paths is safely ignored. The destructor returns the path to the one that was captured before all the creating and moving took place.
\par Unfortunately, there are no functions in Standard C or Standard C++ to control directory creation and movement, so this is captured in the class }{\b OSDirControl}{. After reading the design patterns chapter, your first impulse might be to use the full 
\ldblquote Bridge\rdblquote  pattern. However, there\rquote s
 a lot more going on here. Bridge generally works with things that are already classes, and here we are actually creating the class to encapsulating operating system directory control. In addition, this requires }{\b #ifdef}{s and }{\b #include}{
s for each different operating system and compiler. However, the basic idea is that of a Bridge, since the rest of the code (}{\b PushDirectory }{is actually the only thing that uses this, and thus it acts as the Bridge abstraction) treats an }{\b 
OsDirControl}{ object as a standard interface.
\par All the information about a particular source code file is encapsulated in a }{\b CodeFile}{ object. This includes the type of target the file should produce, variations on the name of the file including the name of the target file it\rquote 
s supposed to produce. The entire contents of the file is contained in the }{\b vector<string> lines}{. In addition, the file\rquote 
s dependencies (the files which, if they change, should cause a recompilation of the current file) and the files on the linker command line are also }{\b vector<string>}{ objects. The }{\b CodeFile}{ object keeps all the compilers it won\rquote 
t work with in }{\b _noBuild}{, which is a }{\b set<string>}{ because it\rquote s easier to look up an element in a }{\b set}{. The }{\b writeTags}{ flag indicates whether the beginning and ending markers from the book listing shou
ld actually be output to the generated file.
\par The three private helper functions }{\b target(\~)}{, }{\b headerLine(\~)}{ and }{\b dependLine(\~)}{ are used by the }{\b CodeFile}{ constructor while it is parsing the input stream. In fact, the }{\b CodeFile}{ constructor does much of the work an
d most of the rest of the member functions simply return values that are stored in the }{\b CodeFile}{ object. Exceptions to this are }{\b addFailure(\~)}{ which stores a compiler that won\rquote t work, and }{\b compilesOK(\~)}{
 which, when given a compiler tells whether this file will compile successfully with that compiler. The }{\b ostream operator<<}{ uses the STL }{\b copy(\~)}{ algorithm and }{\b write(\~)}{ uses }{\b operator<<}{
 to write the file into a particular directory and file name.
\par Looking at the implementation, you\rquote ll see that the helper functions }{\b target(\~)}{, }{\b headerLine(\~)}{ and }{\b dependLine(\~)}{ are just using }{\b string}{
 functions in order to search and manipulate the lines. The constructor is what initiates everything. The idea is that the main program opens the file and reads it until it sees the starting marker for a code file. At that point it makes a }{\b CodeFile}{
 object and hands the constructor the }{\b istream}{ (so the constructor can read the rest of the code file) and the first line that was already read, since it contains valuable information. This first line i
s dissected for the file name information and the target type. The beginning of the file is written (source and copyright information is added) and the rest of the file is read, until the ending tag. The top few lines may contain information about link de
pendencies and command line arguments, or they may be files that are }{\b #included}{ using quotes rather than angle brackets. Quotes indicate they are from local directories and should be added to the makefile dependency.
\par You\rquote ll notice that a number of the markers strings in this program are broken up into two adjacent character strings, relying on the preprocessor to concatenate those strings. This is to prevent them from causing the }{\b ExtractCode}{
 program from accidentally mistaking the strings embedded in the program with the end marker, when }{\b ExtractCode}{ is extracting it\rquote s own source code.
\par The goal of }{\b CompilerData }{is to capture and make available all the information about particular compiler idiosyncrasies. At first glance, the }{\b CompilerData}{ class appears to be a container of }{\b static}{
 member functions, a library of functions wrapped in a class. Actually, the class contains two }{\b static}{ data members; the simpler one is a }{\b set<string> }{that holds all the compiler names, but }{\b compilerInfo}{ is a }{\b map}{ that maps }{\b 
string}{ objects (the compiler name) to }{\b CompilerData}{ objects. Each individual }{\b CompilerData}{ object in }{\b compilerInfo}{ contains a }{\b vector<string>}{ which is the \ldblquote rules\rdblquote 
 that are placed in the makefile (these rules are different for different compilers) and a }{\b set<string>}{ which indicates the files that won\rquote 
t compile with this particular compiler. Also, each compiler creates different extensions for object files and executable files, and these are also stored. There are two flags which indicate if this is a \ldblquote dos\rdblquote  or \ldblquote unix
\rdblquote  style environment (this causes differences in path information and command styles for the resulting makefiles).
\par The member function }{\b readDB(\~)}{ is responsible for taking an }{\b istream}{ and parsing it into a series of }{\b CompilerData}{ objects which are stored in }{\b compilerInfo}{. By ch
oosing a relatively simple format (which you can see in Appendix D) the parsing of this configuration file is fairly simple: the first character on a line determines what information the line contains; a \lquote }{\b #}{\rquote  sign is a comment, a 
\lquote }{\b \{}{\lquote  indicates that the next compiler configuration is beginning and this is the new compiler name, a \lquote }{\b (}{\lquote  is used to establish the object file extension name, a \lquote }{\b &}{\rquote  indicates the \ldblquote 
dos\rdblquote  or \ldblquote unix\rdblquote  directive, and \lquote @\rquote  is a makefile rule which is placed verbatim at the beginning of the ma
kefile. If there is no special character at the beginning of the line, the it must be a file that fails to compile.
\par The }{\b addFailures(\~)}{ member function takes it\rquote s }{\b CodeFile}{ argument (by reference, so it can modify the outside object) and checks each compiler to see if it works with that particular code file; if not, it adds that compiler to the }{
\b CodeFile}{ object\rquote s failure list.
\par Both }{\b obj(\~)}{ and }{\b exe(\~)}{ return the appropriate file extension for a particular compiler. Note that some situations don\rquote t expect extensions, and so the \lquote }{\b .}{\rquote  is added only if there is an extension.
\par When the makefile is being created, one of the first things to do is add the various }{\b make}{ rules, such as the prefixes and target rules (see Appendix D for examples). This is accomplished with }{\b writeRules(\~)}{. Note the use of the STL }{\b 
copy(\~)}{ algorithm.
\par Although dos compilers have no trouble with forward slashes as part of the paths of }{\b #include}{ files, most dos }{\b make}{ programs expect backslashes as part of paths in dependency lists. To adjust for this, the }{\b adjustPath(\~)}{
 function checks to see if this is a dos compiler, and if so it uses the STL }{\b replace(\~)}{ algorithm, treating the }{\b path}{ string object as a container, to replace forward-slash characters with backward slashes.
\par The last class, }{\b Makefile}{, is used to create all the makefiles, including the master makefile that moves into each subdirectory and calls the other makefiles. Each }{\b Makefile}{ contains a group of }{\b CodeFile}{ objects, stored in a }{\b vector}
{. You call }{\b addEntry(\~)}{ to put a new }{\b CodeFile}{ into the }{\b Makefile}{; this also adds the failure list to the }{\b CodeFile}{. In addition, there is a }{\b static set<string>}{
 which contains all the different paths where all the different makefiles will be written; this is used to build the master makefile so it can call all the makefiles in all the subdirectories. The }{\b addEntry(\~)}{
 function also updates this set of paths.
\par To write the makefile for a particular path (once the entire book file has been read), you call }{\b writeMakefiles(\~)}{ and hand it the path you want it to write the makefile for. This function simply iterates through all the compilers in }{\b compilers
}{ and calls }{\b createMakefile(\~)}{ for each one, passing it the compiler name and the path. The latter function is where the real work gets done. First the file name extensions are captured into local }{\b string}{
 objects, then the file name is created from the name of the compiler with \ldblquote .makefile\rdblquote  concatenated (you can use a file with a name other than \ldblquote makefile\rdblquote  by using the }{\b make -f}{
 flag). After writing the header comments and the rules for that particular compiler/operating-system combination (remember, these rules come from the compiler configuration file), a }{\b vector<string>}{
 is created to hold all the different regions of the makefile: the master target list }{\b makeAll}{, the testing commands }{\b makeTest}{, the dependencies }{\b makeDeps}{, and}{\b  }{the commands for linking into executables }{\b linkCmd}{
. The reason it\rquote s necessary to have lists for these four regions is that each }{\b CodeFile}{ object causes entries into each region, so the regions are built as the list of }{\b CodeFile}{
s is traversed, and then finally each region is written in its proper order. This is the function which decides whether a file is going to be included, and also calls }{\b adjustPath(\~)}{ to conditionally change forward slashes to backward slashes.

\par To write the master makefile in }{\b writeMaster(\~)}{, the initial comments are written. The default target is called \ldblquote help,\rdblquote  and it is used if you simply type }{\b make}{
. This provides very simple help to the first time user, including the options for }{\b make}{ that this makefile suppor
ts (that is, all the different compilers the makefile is set up for). Then it creates the list of commands for each compiler, which basically consists of: descending into a subdirectory, call }{\b make}{
 (recursively) on the appropriate makefile in that subdirectory, and then rising back up to the book\rquote s root subdirectory. Makefiles in unix and dos work very differently from each other in this situation: in unix, you }{\b cd}{
 to the directory, followed by a semicolon and then the command you want to execute \endash  returning to the root directory happens automatically. While in dos, you must }{\b cd}{ both down and then back up again, all on separate lines. So the }{\b 
writeMaster(\~)}{ function must interrogate to see if a compiler is running under Unix and write different commands accordingly.
\par Because of the work done in designing the classes (and this was an iterative process; it didn\rquote t just pop out this way), }{\b main(\~)}{ is quite straightforward to read. After opening the input file, the }{\b getline(\~)}{
 function is used to read each input line until the line containing }{\b CompileDB.txt}{ is found; this indicates the beginning of the compiler database listing. Once that has been parsed, }{\b seekg(\~)}{
 is used to move the file pointer back to the beginning so all the code files can be extracted.
\par Each line is read and if one of the start markers is found in the line, a }{\b CodeFile}{
 object is created using that line (which has essential information) and the input stream. The constructor returns when it finishes reading its file, and at that point you can turn around and call }{\b write(\~)}{
 for the code file, and it is automatically written to the correct spot (an earlier version of this program collected all the }{\b CodeFile}{ objects first and put them in a container, then wrote one directory at a time, but the approach shown abo
ve has code that\rquote s easier to understand and the performance impact is not really significant for a tool like this.
\par For makefile management, a }{\b map<string, Makefile>}{ is created, where the }{\b string}{ is the path where the makefile exists. The nice thing about this approach is that the }{\b Makefile}{
 objects will be automatically created whenever you access a new path, as you can see in the line
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {makeFiles[cf.path()].addEntry(cf);
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
