\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The Standard C library }{\b assert(\~)}{ macro is brief, to the point and portable. In addition, when you\rquote 
re finished debugging you can remove all the code by defining }{\b NDEBUG}{, either on the command-line or in code.
\par Also, }{\b assert(\~)}{ can be used while roughing out the code. Later, the calls to }{\b assert(\~)}{ that are actually providing information to the end user can be replaced with more civilized messages.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776550}Trace macros{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776550}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Sometimes it\rquote s very helpful to print the code of each statement before it is executed, either to }{\b cout}{ or to a trace file. Here\rquote 
s a preprocessor macro to accomplish this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#define TRACE(ARG) cout << #ARG << endl; ARG
