\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Which isn\rquote t what you want. Thus, this technique must be used carefully.
\par A variation on the }{\b TRACE(\~)}{ macro is this:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#define D(a) cout << #a "=[" << a << "]" << nl;
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If there\rquote s an expression you want to display, you simply put it inside a call to }{\b D(\~)}{
 and the expression will be printed, followed by its value (assuming there\rquote s an overloaded operator }{\b <<}{ for the result type). For example, you can say }{\b D(a + b)}{
. Thus you can use it anytime you want to test an intermediate value to make sure things are OK.
\par Of course, the above two macros are actually just the two most fundamental things you d
o with a debugger: trace through the code execution and print values. A good debugger is an excellent productivity tool, but sometimes debuggers are not available, or it\rquote 
s not convenient to use them. The above techniques always work, regardless of the situation.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776551}Trace file{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776551}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This code allows you to easily create a trace file and send all the output that would normally go to }{\b cout}{ into the file. All you have to do is }{\b 
#define}{ TRACEON and include the header file (of course, it\rquote s fairly easy just to write the two key lines right into your file):
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:Trace.h
\par // Creating a trace file
\par #ifndef TRACE_H
\par #define TRACE_H
\par #include <fstream>
\par 
