\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here\rquote s a simple test of the above file:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:Tracetst.cpp
\par // Test of trace.h
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par #define TRACEON
\par #include "Trace.h"
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   ifstream f("Tracetst.cpp");
\par   assure(f, "Tracetst.cpp");
\par   cout << f.rdbuf();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This also uses the }{\b assure(\~)}{ function defined earlier in the book.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776552}Abstract base class for debugging{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776552}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the Smalltalk tradition, you can create your own object-based hierarchy, and install pure virtual functions to perform debuggi
ng. Then everyone on the team must inherit from this class and redefine the debugging functions. All objects in the system will then have debugging functions available.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776553}Tracking }{\b new}{/}{\b delete}{ & }{\b malloc}{/}{\b free}{
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776553}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Common problems with memory allocation include calling }{\b delete}{ for things you have }{\b malloc}{ed, calling }{\b free}{ for things you allocated with }
{\b new}{, forgetting to release objects from the free store, and releasing them more than once. This section provides a system to help you track these kinds of problems down.
\par To use the memory checking system, you simply link the }{\b obj}{ file in and all the calls to }{\b malloc(\~)}{, }{\b realloc(\~)}{, }{\b calloc(\~)}{, }{\b free(\~)}{, }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{
 are intercepted. However, if you also include the following file (which is optional), all the calls to }{\b new}{ will store information about the file and line where they were called. This is accomplished with a use of the }{\i placement syntax}{ for }{
\b operator new}{ (this trick was suggested by Reg Charney of the C++ Standards Committee). The placement syntax is intended for situations where you need to place objects at a specific point in memory. However, it allows you to create an }{\b 
operator new}{ with any number of arguments. This is used to advantage here to store the results of the }{\b __FILE__}{ and }{\b __LINE__}{ macros whenever }{\b new}{ is called:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:MemCheck.h
\par // Memory testing system
\par // This file is only included if you want to
\par // use the special placement syntax to find
\par // out the line number where "new" was called.
\par #ifndef MEMCHECK_H
\par #define MEMCHECK_H
\par #include <cstdlib>  // size_t
\par 
\par // Use placement syntax to pass extra arguments.
\par // From an idea by Reg Charney:
\par void* operator new(
\par   std::size_t sz, char* file, int line);
\par #define new new(__FILE__, __LINE__)
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // MEMCHECK_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In the following file containing the function definitio
ns, you will note that everything is done with standard IO rather than iostreams. This is because, for example, the }{\b cout}{ constructor allocates memory. Standard IO ensures against cyclical conditions that can lock up the system.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:MemCheck.cpp \{O\}
\par // Memory allocation tester
\par #include <cstdlib>
\par #include <cstring>
\par #include <cstdio>
\par using namespace std;
\par // MemCheck.h must not be included here
\par 
\par // Output file object using cstdio
\par // (cout constructor calls malloc())
\par class OFile \{
\par   FILE* f;
\par public:
\par   OFile(char* name) : f(fopen(name, "w")) \{\}
\par   ~OFile() \{ fclose(f); \}
\par   operator FILE*() \{ return f; \}
\par \};
\par extern OFile memtrace;
\par // Comment out the following to send all the
\par // information to the trace file:
\par #define memtrace stdout
\par 
\par const unsigned long _pool_sz = 50000L;
\par static unsigned char _memory_pool[_pool_sz];
\par static unsigned char* _pool_ptr = _memory_pool;
\par 
\par void* getmem(size_t sz) \{
\par   if(_memory_pool + _pool_sz - _pool_ptr < sz) \{
\par     fprintf(stderr,
\par            "Out of memory. Use bigger model\\n");
\par     exit(1);
\par   \}
\par   void* p = _pool_ptr;
\par   _pool_ptr += sz;
\par   return p;
\par \}
\par 
\par // Holds information about allocated pointers:
\par class MemBag \{ 
\par public:
\par   enum type \{ Malloc, New \};
\par private:
\par   char* typestr(type t) \{
\par     switch(t) \{
\par       case Malloc: return "malloc";
\par       case New: return "new";
\par       default: return "?unknown?";
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par   struct M \{
\par     void* mp;  // Memory pointer
\par     type t;     // Allocation type
\par     char* file; // File name where allocated
\par     int line;  // Line number where allocated
\par     M(void* v, type tt, char* f, int l)
\par       : mp(v), t(tt), file(f), line(l) \{\}
\par   \}* v;
\par   int sz, next;
\par   enum \{ increment = 50 \};
\par public:
\par   MemBag() : v(0), sz(0), next(0) \{\}
\par   void* add(void* p, type tt = Malloc,
\par             char* s = "library", int l = 0) \{
\par     if(next >= sz) \{
\par       sz += increment;
\par       // This memory is never freed, so it
\par       // doesn't "get involved" in the test:
\par       const int memsize = sz * sizeof(M);
\par       // Equivalent of realloc, no registration:
\par       void* p = getmem(memsize);
\par       if(v) memmove(p, v, memsize);
\par       v = (M*)p;
\par       memset(&v[next], 0,
\par              increment * sizeof(M));
\par     \}
\par     v[next++] = M(p, tt, s, l);
\par     return p;
\par   \}
\par   // Print information about allocation:
\par   void allocation(int i) \{
\par     fprintf(memtrace, "pointer %p"
\par       " allocated with %s",
\par       v[i].mp, typestr(v[i].t));
\par     if(v[i].t == New)
\par       fprintf(memtrace, " at %s: %d",
\par         v[i].file, v[i].line);
\par     fprintf(memtrace, "\\n");
\par   \}
\par   void validate(void* p, type T = Malloc) \{
\par     for(int i = 0; i < next; i++)
\par       if(v[i].mp == p) \{
\par         if(v[i].t != T) \{
\par           allocation(i);
\par           fprintf(memtrace,
\par           "\\t was released as if it were "
\par           "allocated with %s \\n", typestr(T));
\par         \}
\par         v[i].mp = 0;  // Erase it
\par         return;
\par       \}
\par     fprintf(memtrace,
\par     "pointer not in memory list: %p\\n", p);
\par   \}
\par   ~MemBag() \{
\par     for(int i = 0; i < next; i++)
\par       if(v[i].mp != 0) \{
\par         fprintf(memtrace,
\par         "pointer not released: ");
\par         allocation(i);
\par       \}
\par   \}
\par \};
\par extern MemBag MEMBAG_;
\par 
\par void* malloc(size_t sz) \{
\par   void* p = getmem(sz);
\par   return MEMBAG_.add(p, MemBag::Malloc);
\par \}
\par 
\par void* calloc(size_t num_elems, size_t elem_sz) \{
\par   void* p = getmem(num_elems * elem_sz);
\par   memset(p, 0, num_elems * elem_sz);
\par   return MEMBAG_.add(p, MemBag::Malloc);
\par \}  
\par 
\par void* realloc(void* block, size_t sz) \{
\par   void* p = getmem(sz);
\par   if(block) memmove(p, block, sz);
\par   return MEMBAG_.add(p, MemBag::Malloc);
\par \}
\par 
\par void free(void* v) \{ 
\par   MEMBAG_.validate(v, MemBag::Malloc);
\par \}
\par 
\par void* operator new(size_t sz) \{
\par   void* p = getmem(sz);
\par   return MEMBAG_.add(p, MemBag::New);
\par \}
\par 
\par void*
\par operator new(size_t sz, char* file, int line) \{
\par   void* p = getmem(sz);
\par   return MEMBAG_.add(p, MemBag::New, file,line);
\par \}
\par 
\par void operator delete(void* v) \{
\par   MEMBAG_.validate(v, MemBag::New);
\par \}
\par 
\par MemBag MEMBAG_;
\par // Placed here so the constructor is called
\par // AFTER that of MEMBAG_ :
\par #ifdef memtrace
\par #undef memtrace
\par #endif
\par OFile memtrace("memtrace.out");
\par // Causes 1 "pointer not in memory list" message
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b OFile}{ is a simple wrapper around a }{\b FILE*}{; the constructor opens the file and the destructor closes it. The }{\b operator FILE*(\~)}{
 allows you to simply use the }{\b OFile}{ object anyplace you would ordinarily use a }{\b FILE*}{ (in the }{\b fprintf(\~)}{ statements in this example). The }{\b #define}{
 that follows simply sends everything to standard output, but if you need to put it in a trace file you simply comment out that line.
\par Memory is allocated from an array called }{\b _memory_pool}{. The }{\b _pool_ptr}{ is moved forward every time storage is allocated. For simplicity, the storage is never reclaimed, and }{\b realloc(\~)}{ doesn\rquote 
t try to resize the storage in the same place.
\par All the storage allocation functions call }{\b getmem(\~)}{ which ensures there is enough space left and moves the }{\b _pool_ptr}{ to allocate your storage. Then they store the pointer in a special container of class }{\b MemBag}{ called }{\b MEMBAG_}{
, along with pertinent information (notice the two versions of }{\b operator new}{; one which just stores the pointer and the other which stores the file and line number). The }{\b MemBag}{ class is the heart of the system.
\par You will see many similarities to }{\b xbag}{ in }{\b MemBag}{. A distinct difference is }{\b realloc(\~)}{ is replaced by a call to }{\b getmem(\~)}{ and }{\b memmove(\~)}{, so that storage allocated for the }{\b MemBag}{
 is not registered. In addition, the }{\b type}{ }{\b enum}{ allows you to store the way the memory was allocated; the }{\b typestr(\~)}{ function takes a type and produces a string for use with printing.
\par The nested }{\b struct M}{ holds the pointer, the type, a pointer to the file name (which is assumed to be statically allocated) and the line where the allocation occurred. }{\b v}{ is a pointer to an array of }{\b M}{ objects \endash 
 this is the array which is dynamically sized.
\par The }{\b allocation(\~)}{ function prints out a different message depending on whether the storage was allocated with }{\b new}{ (where it has line and file information) or }{\b malloc(\~)}{ (where it doesn\rquote t). This function is used inside }{\b 
validate(\~)}{, which is called by }{\b free(\~)}{ and }{\b delete(\~)}{ to ensure everything is OK, and in the destructor, to ensure the pointer was cleaned up (note that in }{\b validate(\~)}{ the pointer value }{\b v[i].mp}{
 is set to zero, to indicate it has been cleaned up).
\par The following is a simple test using the memcheck facility. The }{\b MemCheck.obj}{ file must be linked in for it to work:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:MemTest.cpp
\par //\{L\} MemCheck
\par // Test of MemCheck system
\par #include "MemCheck.h"
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   void* v = std::malloc(100);
\par   delete v;
\par   int* x = new int;
\par   std::free(x);
\par   new double;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The trace file created in }{\b MemCheck.cpp}{ causes the generation of one "pointer not in memory list" m
essage, apparently from the creation of the file pointer on the heap. [[ This may not still be true \endash  test it ]]
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018776}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776554}CGI
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408018776} programming in C++{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776554}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The World-Wide Web has become the common tongue of connectivity on planet earth. It began as simply a way to publish pri
mitively-formatted documents in a way that everyone could read them regardless of the machine they were using. The documents are created in }{\i hypertext markup language}{
 (HTML) and placed on a central server machine where they are handed to anyone who asks. The documents are requested and read using a }{\i web browser}{ that has been written or ported to each particular platform.
\par Very quickly, just reading a document was not enough and people wanted to be able to collect information from the clients, for example to take orders or allow database lookups from the server. Many different approaches to }{\i client-side}{ }{\i 
programming}{ have been tried such as Java applets, Javascript, and other scripting or programming languages. Unfortunately, whenever you publish something on the I
nternet you face the problem of a whole history of browsers, some of which may support the particular flavor of your client-side programming tool, and some which won\rquote t. The only reliable and well-established solution}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn 
{\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ Actually, Java Servlets look like a much better solution than CGI, but \endash  at le
ast at this writing \endash  Servlets are still an up-and-coming solution and you\rquote re unlikely to find them provided by your typical ISP.}}}{ to this problem is to use straight HTM
L (which has a very limited way to collect and submit information from the client) and }{\i common gateway interface}{
 (CGI) programs that are run on the server. The Web server takes an encoded request submitted via an HTML page and responds by invoking a CGI program and handing it the encoded data from the request. }{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {CGI\: connecting Java to CGI}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {network programming\:
 connecting Java to CGI}}}{This request is classified as either a \ldblquote }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {GET}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {CGI\: GET}}}{GET\rdblquote  or a \ldblquote }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {POST}}}{\pard\plain 
