\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {That is, the special character is turned into a }{\b \lquote %}{\rquote 
 followed by its ASCII value in hex. Fortunately, the web browser automatically performs all encoding for you.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408018779}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776556}The CGI {\*\bkmkend _Toc408018779}
parser{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776556}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {CGI\: programming in C++}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C++\: CGI programming in C++}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {network programming\:
 CGI programming in C++}}}{There are many examples of CGI pr}{o}{grams written using Standard C. One argument for doing this is that Standard C can be found virtually everywhere. However, C++ has become quite ubiqu}{i}{tous, especially in the form of the 
}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {GNU C++ Compiler}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C++\: GNU C++ Compiler}}}
{GNU C++ Compiler}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ GNU stands for \ldblquote Gnu\rquote 
s Not Unix.\rdblquote  The project, created by the Free Software Found}{a}{tion, was originally intended to replace the Unix operating system with a free version of that OS. Linux appears to have replaced this initiative,
 but the GNU tools have played an integral part in the development of Linux, which comes packaged with many GNU components.}}}{ (}{\b g++}{)}{\b  }{
that can be downloaded free from the Internet for virtually any platform (and often comes pre-installed with operating systems such as Linux). As you will see, this means that you can get the benefit of object-oriented programming in a CGI program.
\par Since what we\rquote re concerned with when parsing the CGI information is the field name-value pairs, one class (}{\b CGIpair}{)}{\b  }{will be used to represent a single name-value pair and a second class (}{\b CGImap}{) will use }{\b CGIpair}{
 to parse each name-value pair that is submitted from the HTML form into keys and values that it will hold in a }{\b map}{ of }{\b strings}{ so you can easily fetch the value for each field at your leisure.
\par One of the reasons for using C++ here is the convenience of the }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C++\: Standard Template Library (STL).}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {template\: C++ Standard Template Library (STL)}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {STL\:
 C++ Standard Template Library}}}{STL, in particular the }{\b map}{ class. Since }{\b map }{has the }{\b operator[ ], }{you have a nice syntax for extracting the data for each field. The }{\b map}{ template will be used in the creation of }{\b CGImap}{
, which you\rquote ll see is a fairly short definition considering how powerful it is.
\par The project will start with a reusable portion, which consists of }{\b CGIpair}{ and }{\b CGImap}{ in a header file. Normally you should avoid cramming this much code into a header file, but for these examples it\rquote s convenient and it doesn\rquote 
t hurt anything:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:CGImap.h
\par // Tools for extracting and decoding data from 
\par // from CGI GETs and POSTs.
\par #include <string>
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <iostream>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class CGIpair : public pair<string, string> \{
\par public:
\par   CGIpair() \{\}
\par   CGIpair(string name, string value) \{
\par     first = decodeURLString(name);
\par     second = decodeURLString(value);
\par   \}
\par   // Automatic type conversion for boolean test:
\par   operator bool() const \{
\par     return (first.length() != 0);
\par   \} 
\par private:  
\par   static string decodeURLString(string URLstr) \{
\par     const int len = URLstr.length();
\par     string result;
\par     for(int i = 0; i < len; i++) \{
\par       if(URLstr[i] == '+')
\par         result += ' ';
\par       else if(URLstr[i] == '%') \{
\par         result +=
\par           translateHex(URLstr[i + 1]) * 16 +
\par           translateHex(URLstr[i + 2]);
\par         i += 2; // Move past hex code
\par       \} else // An ordinary character
\par         result += URLstr[i];
\par     \}
\par     return result;
\par   \}
\par   // Translate a single hex character; used by
\par   // decodeURLString():
\par   static char translateHex(char hex) \{
\par     if(hex >= 'A')
\par       return (hex & 0xdf) - 'A' + 10;
\par     else
\par       return hex - '0';
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Parses any CGI query and turns it into an
\par // STL vector of CGIpair which has an associative
\par // lookup operator[] like a map. A vector is used
\par // instead of a map because it keeps the original
\par // ordering of the fields in the Web page form.
\par class CGImap : public vector<CGIpair> \{
\par   string gq;
\par   int index;
\par   // Prevent assignment and copy-construction:
\par   void operator=(CGImap&);
\par   CGImap(CGImap&);
\par public:
\par   CGImap(string query): index(0), gq(query)\{
\par     CGIpair p;
\par     while((p = nextPair()) != 0)
\par       push_back(p); 
\par   \}
\par   // Look something up, as if it were a map:
\par   string operator[](const string& key) \{
\par     iterator i = begin();
\par     while(i != end()) \{
\par       if((*i).first == key)
\par         return (*i).second;
\par       i++;
\par     \}
\par     return string(); // Empty string == not found
\par   \}
\par   void dump(ostream& o, string nl = "<br>") \{
\par     for(iterator i = begin(); i != end(); i++) \{
\par       o << (*i).first << " = "
\par         << (*i).second << nl;
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par private:
\par   // Produces name-value pairs from the query 
\par   // string. Returns an empty Pair when there's 
\par   // no more query string left:
\par   CGIpair nextPair() \{
\par     if(gq.length() == 0)
\par       return CGIpair(); // Error, return empty
\par     if(gq.find('=') == -1)
\par       return CGIpair(); // Error, return empty
\par     string name = gq.substr(0, gq.find('='));
\par     gq = gq.substr(gq.find('=') + 1);
\par     string value = gq.substr(0, gq.find('&'));
\par     gq = gq.substr(gq.find('&') + 1);
\par     return CGIpair(name, value);
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Helper class for getting POST data:
\par class Post : public string \{
\par public: 
\par   Post() \{
\par     // For a CGI "POST," the server puts the
\par     // length of the content string in the 
\par     // environment variable CONTENT_LENGTH:
\par     char* clen = getenv("CONTENT_LENGTH");
\par     if(clen == 0) \{
\par       cout << "Zero CONTENT_LENGTH, Make sure "
\par         "this is a POST and not a GET" << endl;
\par       return;
\par     \}
\par     int len = atoi(clen);
\par     char* s = new char[len];
\par     cin.read(s, len); // Get the data
\par     append(s, len); // Add it to this string
\par     delete []s;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}; ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b CGIpair}{ class starts out quite simply: it inherits from the standard library }{\b pair}{ template to create a }{\b pair}{ of}{\b  string}{
s, one for the name and one for the value. The second constructor calls the member function }{\b decodeURLString(\~)}{ which produces a }{\b string}{
 after stripping away all the extra characters added by the browser as it submitted the CGI request. There is no need to provide functions to select each individual element \endash  because }{\b pair }{is inherited publicly, you can just select the }{\b 
first }{and }{\b second}{ elements of the }{\b CGIpair}{.}{\b  
\par }{The }{\b operator bool}{ provides automatic type conversion to }{\b bool}{. If you have a }{\b CGIpair}{ object called }{\b p}{ and you use it in an expression where a Boolean result is expected, such as 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {if(p) \{ //...
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {then the compiler will recognize that it has a }{\b CGIpair}{ and it needs a Boolean, so it will automatically call }{\b operator bool}{
 to perform the necessary conversion.
\par Because the }{\b string}{ objects take care of themselves, you don\rquote t need to explicitly define the copy-constructor, }{\b operator=}{ or destructor \endash  the default versions synthesized by the compiler do the right thing.
\par The remainder of the }{\b CGIpair}{ class consists of the two methods }{\b decodeURLString(\~)}{ and a helper member function }{\b translateHex(\~)}{ which is used by }{\b decodeURLString(\~)}{. (Note that }{\b translateHex(\~)}{
 does not guard against bad input such as \ldblquote %1H.\rdblquote ) }{\b decodeURLString(\~)}{ moves through and replaces each \lquote }{\b +}{\rquote  with a space, and each hex code (beginning with a \lquote }{\b %}{\rquote 
) with the appropriate character. It\rquote s worth noting here and in }{\b CGImap}{ the power of the }{\b string}{ class \endash  you can index into a }{\b string}{ object using }{\b operator[ ]}{, and you can use methods like }{\b find(\~)}{ and }{\b 
substring(\~)}{.
\par }{\b CGImap}{ parses and holds all the name-value pairs submitted from the form as part of a CGI request. You might think that anything that has the word \ldblquote map\rdblquote  in it\rquote s name should be inherited from the STL }{\b map}{, but }{\b 
map}{ has it\rquote s own way of ordering the elements it stores whereas here it\rquote s useful to keep the elements in the order that they appear on the Web page. So }{\b CGImap}{ is inherited from }{\b vector<CGIpair>}{, and }{\b operator[ ] }{
is overloaded so you get the associative-array lookup of a }{\b map}{.
\par You can also see that }{\b CGImap}{ has a copy-constructor and an }{\b operator=}{, but they\rquote re both declared as }{\b private}{. This is to prevent the compiler from synthesizing the two functions (which it will do if you don\rquote 
t declare them yourself), but it also prevents the client progra}{m}{mer from passing a }{\b CGImap}{ by value or from using assignment.
\par }{\b CGImap}{\rquote s job is to take the input data and parse it into name-value pairs, which it will do with the aid of }{\b CGIpair}{ (effectively, }{\b CGIpair}{
 is only a helper class, but it also seems to make it easier to understand the code). After copying the query string (you\rquote ll see where the query string comes from later) into a local }{\b string}{ object }{\b gq}{, the }{\b nextPair(\~)}{
 member function is used to parse the string into raw name-value pairs, delimited by \lquote }{\b =}{\lquote  and}{\b  }{\lquote }{\b &}{\rquote  signs. Each resulting }{\b CGIpair}{ object is added to the }{\b vector}{ using the standard }{\b 
vector::push_back(\~)}{. When }{\b nextPair(\~)}{ runs out of input from the query string, it returns zero.
\par The }{\b CGImap::operator[ ]}{ takes the brute-force approach of a linear search through the elements. Since the }{\b CGImap}{ is intentionally not sorted and they tend to be small, this is not too terrible. The }{\b dump(\~)}{
 function is used for testing, typically by sending information to the resulting Web page, as you might guess from the default value of }{\b nl}{, which is an HTML \ldblquote break line\rdblquote  token.
\par }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {POST\: CGI}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {CGI\: POST}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {network programming\: CGI POST}}}{
Using GET can be fine for many applications. However, GET passes its data to the CGI program through an environment variable (called }{\b QUERY_STRING}{), and operating systems typically run out of environment space with long GET stri
ngs (you should start worrying at about 200 characters). CGI provides a solution for this: POST. With POST, the data is encoded and concatenated the same way as with GET, but POST uses standard input to pass the encoded query string to the CGI pr}{o}{
gram and has no length limitation on the input. All you have to do in your CGI program is determine the length of the query string. This length is stored in the environment variable }{\b CONTENT_LENGTH}{
. Once you know the length, you can allocate storage and read the pre
cise number of bytes from standard input. Because POST is the less-fragile solution, you should probably prefer it over GET, unless you know for sure that your input will be short. In fact, one might surmise that the only reason for GET is that it is slig
htly easier to code a CGI program in C using GET. However, the last class in }{\b CGImap.h}{ is a tool that makes handling a POST just as easy as handling a GET, which means you can always use POST.
\par The }{\b class Post}{ inherits from a string and only has a constructor. The job of the constructor is to get the query data from the POST into itself (a }{\b string}{). It does this by reading the }{\b CONTENT_LENGTH}{
 environment variable using the Standard C library function }{\b getenv(\~)}{. This comes back as a pointer to a C character string
. If this pointer is zero, the CONTENT_LENGTH environment variable has not been set, so something is wrong. Otherwise, the character string must be converted to an integer using the Standard C library function }{\b atoi(\~)}{
. The resulting length is used with }{\b new }{to allocate enough storage to hold the query string (plus its null terminator), and then }{\b read(\~)}{ is called for }{\b cin}{. The }{\b read(\~)}{
 function takes a pointer to the destination buffer and the number of bytes to read. The resulting buffer is inserted into the current }{\b string}{ using }{\b string::append(\~)}{. At this point, the POST data is just a }{\b string}{
 object and can be easily used without further concern about where it came from.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Testing the CGI parser
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Now that the basic tools are defined, they can easily be used in a C
GI program like the following which simply dumps the name-value pairs that are parsed from a GET query. Remember that an iterator for a }{\b CGImap}{ returns a }{\b CGIpair}{ object when it is dereferenced, so you must select the }{\b first}{ and }{\b 
second}{ parts of that }{\b CGIpair}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:CGI_GET.cpp
\par // Tests CGImap by extracting the information
\par // from a CGI GET submitted by an HTML Web page.
\par #include "CGImap.h"
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   // You MUST print this out, otherwise the 
\par   // server will not send the response:
\par   cout << "Content-type: text/plain\\n" << endl;
\par   // For a CGI "GET," the server puts the data
\par   // in the environment variable QUERY_STRING:
\par   CGImap query(getenv("QUERY_STRING"));
\par   // Test: dump all names and values
\par   for(CGImap::iterator it = query.begin();
\par     it != query.end(); it++) \{
\par     cout << (*it).first << " = "
