\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {When you use the GET approach (which is controlled by the HTML page with the METHOD tag of the FORM directive), the Web server grabs everything after the 
\lquote ?\rquote  and puts in into the operating-system environment variable }{\b QUERY_STRING}{. So to read that information all you have to do is get the }{\b QUERY_STRING}{. You do this with the standard C library function }{\b getenv(\~)}{
, passing it the identifier of the environment variable you wish to fetch. In }{\b main(\~)}{,}{\b  }{notice how simple the act of parsing the }{\b QUERY_STRING}{ is: you just hand it to the constructor for the }{\b CGImap}{ object called }{\b query }{
and all the work is done for you. Although an iterator is used here, you can also pull out the names and values from }{\b query}{ using }{\b CGImap::operator[ ]}{.
\par Now it\rquote s important to understand something about CGI. A CGI program is handed its input in one of two ways: through QUERY_STRING during a GET (as in the above case) or through standard input during a POST.
 But a CGI program only returns its results through standard output, via }{\b cout}{. Where does this output go? Back to the Web server, which decides what to do with it. The server makes this decision based on the }{\b content-type}{
 header, which means that if the }{\b content-type}{ header isn\rquote t the first thing it sees, it won\rquote t know what to do with the data. Thus it\rquote s essential that you start the output of all CGI programs with the }{\b content-type}{ header.

\par In this case, we want the server to feed all the information directly back to the client program. The information should be unchanged, so the }{\b content-type}{ is }{\b text/plain}{
. Once the server sees this, it will echo all strings right back to the client as a simple text Web page.
