Managing an email list is the kind of problem many people need to solve for their Web site. As it is turning out to be the case for everything on the Internet, the simplest approach is a
lways the best. I learned this the hard way, first trying a variety of Java applets (which some firewalls do not allow) and even JavaScript (which isn\rquote 
t supported uniformly on all browsers). The result of each experiment was a steady stream of email from the folks who couldn\rquote t get it to work. When you set up a Web site, your goal should be to never get email from anyone complaining that it doesn
\rquote t work, and the best way to produce this result is to use plain HTML (which, with a little work, can be made to look quite decent).
\par The second problem was on the server side. Ideally, you\rquote d like all your email addresses to be added and removed from a single master file, but this presents a problem. Most operating systems allow more than one program to open a file. Wh
en a client makes a CGI request, the Web server starts up a new invocation of the CGI program, and since a Web server can handle many requests at a time, this means that you can have many instances of your CGI program running at once. If the CGI program o
pens a specific file, then you can have many programs running at once that open that file. This is a problem if they are each reading and writing to that file.
\par There may be a function for your operating system that \ldblquote locks\rdblquote  a file, so that other invocations 
of your program do not access the file at the same time. However, I took a different approach, which was to make a unique file for each client. Making a file unique was quite easy, since the email name itself is a unique character string. The filename for
 each request is then just the email name, followed by the string \ldblquote .add\rdblquote  or \ldblquote .remove\rdblquote 
. The contents of the file is also the email address of the client. Then, to produce a list of all the names to add, you simply say something like (in Unix):
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {cat *.add > addlist
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {(or the equivalent for your system). For removals, you say:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {cat *.remove > removelist
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Once the names have been combined into a list you can archive or remove the files.
\par The HTML code to place on your Web page becomes fairly straightforward. This particular example takes an email address to be added or removed from my C++ mailing list:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {<h1 align="center"><font color="#000000">
\par The C++ Mailing List</font></h1>
\par <div align="center"><center>
\par 
\par <table border="1" cellpadding="4" 
\par cellspacing="1" width="550" bgcolor="#FFFFFF">
\par   <tr>
\par     <td width="30" bgcolor="#FF0000">&nbsp;</td>
\par     <td align="center" width="422" bgcolor="#0">
\par     <form action="/cgi-bin/mlm.exe" method="GET">
\par     <input type="hidden" name="subject-field"
\par     value="cplusplus-email-list">
\par     <input type="hidden" name="command-field"
\par     value="add"><p>
\par     <input type="text" size="40" 
\par     name="email-address"> 
\par     <input type="submit" name="submit"
\par     value="Add Address to C++ Mailing List">
\par     </p></form></td>
\par     <td width="30" bgcolor="#FF0000">&nbsp;</td>
\par   </tr>
\par   <tr>
\par     <td width="30" bgcolor="#000000">&nbsp;</td>
\par     <td align="center" width="422" 
\par     bgcolor="#FF0000">
\par     <form action="/cgi-bin/mlm.exe" method="GET">
\par     <input type="hidden" name="subject-field"
\par     value="cplusplus-email-list">
\par     <input type="hidden" name="command-field"
\par     value="remove"><p>
\par     <input type="text" size="40" 
