\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Each form contains one data-entry field called }{\b email-address}{, as well as a couple of hidden fields which don\rquote 
t provide for user input but carry information back to the server nonetheless. The }{\b subject-field}{ tells the CGI program the subdirectory where the resulting file should be placed. The }{\b command-field}{
 tells the CGI program whether the user is requesting that they be added or removed from the list. From the }{\b action}{, you can see that a GET is used with a program called }{\b mlm.exe}{ (for \ldblquote mailing list manager\rdblquote ). Here it is:

\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:mlm.cpp
\par // A GGI program to maintain a mailing list
\par #include "CGImap.h"
\par #include <fstream>
\par using namespace std;
\par const string contact("Bruce@EckelObjects.com");
\par // Paths in this program are for Linux/Unix. You
\par // must use backslashes (two for each single 
\par // slash) on Win32 servers:
\par const string rootpath("/home/eckel/");
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   cout << "Content-type: text/html\\n"<< endl;
\par   CGImap query(getenv("QUERY_STRING"));
\par   if(query["test-field"] == "on") \{
\par     cout << "map size: " << query.size() << "<br>";
\par     query.dump(cout, "<br>");
\par   \}
\par   if(query["subject-field"].size() == 0) \{
\par     cout << "<h2>Incorrect form. Contact " <<
\par     contact << endl;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   string email = query["email-address"];
\par   if(email.size() == 0) \{
\par     cout << "<h2>Please enter your email address"
\par       << endl;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   if(email.find_first_of(" \\t") != string::npos)\{
\par     cout << "<h2>You cannot use white space "
\par       "in your email address" << endl;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   if(email.find('@') == string::npos) \{
\par     cout << "<h2>You must use a proper email"
\par       " address including an '@' sign" << endl;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   if(email.find('.') == string::npos) \{
\par     cout << "<h2>You must use a proper email"
\par       " address including a '.'" << endl;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   string fname = email;
\par   if(query["command-field"] == "add")
\par     fname += ".add";
\par   else if(query["command-field"] == "remove")
\par     fname += ".remove";
\par   else \{  
\par     cout << "error: command-field not found. Contact "
\par       << contact << endl;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   string path(rootpath + query["subject-field"] 
\par     + "/" + fname);
\par   ofstream out(path.c_str());
\par   if(!out) \{
\par     cout << "cannot open " << path << "; Contact"
\par       << contact << endl;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   out << email << endl;
\par   cout << "<br><H2>" << email << " has been ";
\par   if(query["command-field"] == "add")
\par     cout << "added";
\par   else if(query["command-field"] == "remove")
\par     cout << "removed";
\par   cout << "<br>Thank you</H2>" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Again, all the CGI work is done by the }{\b CGImap}{. From then on it\rquote 
s a matter of pulling the fields out and looking at them, then deciding what to do about it, which is easy because of the way you can index into a }{\b map}{ and also because of the tools available for standard }{\b string}{
s. Here, most of the programming has to do with checking for a valid email address. Then a file name is created with the email address as the name and \ldblquote .add\rdblquote  or \ldblquote .remove\rdblquote  as the extension, a
nd the email address is placed in the file.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Maintaining your list
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Once you have a list of names to add, you can just paste them to end of your list. However, you might get some duplicates so you need a program to remove those. Because your names may differ only by upper and lowercase, it\rquote 
s useful to create a tool that will read a list of names from a file and place them into a container of strings, forcing all the names to lowercase as it does:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:readLower.h
\par // Read a file into a container of string, 
\par // forcing each line to lower case.
\par #ifndef READLOWER_H
\par #define READLOWER_H
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par #include <cctype>
\par 
\par inline char downcase(char c) \{
\par   using namespace std; // Compiler bug
\par   return tolower(c);
\par \}
\par 
\par std::string lcase(std::string s) \{
\par   std::transform(s.begin(), s.end(),
\par     s.begin(), downcase);
\par   return s;
\par \}
\par 
\par template<class SContainer> 
\par void readLower(char* filename, SContainer& c) \{
\par   std::ifstream in(filename);
\par   assure(in, filename);
\par   const int sz = 1024;
\par   char buf[sz];
\par   while(in.getline(buf, sz))
\par     // Force to lowercase:
\par     c.push_back(string(lcase(buf)));
\par \} 
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {#endif // READLOWER_H ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Since it\rquote s a }{\b template}{, it will work with any container of }{\b string}{ that supports }{\b push_back(\~)}{.}{\b  }{
Again, you may want to change the above to the form }{\b readln(in, s)}{ instead of using a fixed-sized buffer, which is more fragile.
\par Once the names are read into the list and forced to lowercase, removing duplicates is trivial:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:RemoveDuplicates.cpp
\par // Remove duplicate names from a mailing list
\par #include "readLower.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <vector>
\par #include <algorithm>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 2);
\par   vector<string> names;
\par   readLower(argv[1], names); 
\par   long before = names.size();
\par   // You must sort first for unique() to work:
\par   sort(names.begin(), names.end()); 
\par   // Remove adjacent duplicates:
\par   unique(names.begin(), names.end());
\par   long removed = before - names.size();
\par   ofstream out(argv[2]);
\par   assure(out, argv[2]);
\par   copy(names.begin(), names.end(),
\par        ostream_iterator<string>(out,"\\n"));
\par   cout << removed << " names removed" << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {A }{\b vector }{is used here instead of a }{\b list }{because sorting requires random-access which is much faster in a }{\b vector}{. (A }{\b list}{
 has a built-in }{\b sort(\~)}{ so that it doesn\rquote t suffer from the performance that would result from applying the normal }{\b sort(\~)}{ algorithm shown above).
\par The sort must be performed so that all duplicates are adjacent to each other. Then }{\b unique(\~)}{ can remove all the adjacent duplicates. The program also keeps track of how many duplicate names were removed.
\par When you have a file of names to remove from your list, }{\b readLower(\~)}{ comes in handy again:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:RemoveGroup.cpp
\par // Remove a group of names from a list
\par #include "readLower.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <list>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par typedef list<string> Container;
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 3);
\par   Container names, removals;
\par   readLower(argv[1], names);
\par   readLower(argv[2], removals);
\par   long original = names.size();
\par   Container::iterator rmit = removals.begin();
\par   while(rmit != removals.end())
\par     names.remove(*rmit++); // Removes all matches
\par   ofstream out(argv[3]);
\par   assure(out, argv[3]);
\par   copy(names.begin(), names.end(),
\par        ostream_iterator<string>(out,"\\n"));
\par   long removed = original - names.size();
\par   cout << "On removal list: " << removals.size()
\par     << "\\n Removed: " << removed << endl;  
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here, a }{\b list }{is used instead of a }{\b vector }{(since }{\b readLower(\~) }{is a }{\b template}{, it adapts). Although there is a }{\b remove(\~)}{
 algorithm that can be applied to containers, the built-in }{\b list::remove(\~)}{ seems to work better.}{\b  }{The second command-line argument is the file containing the list of na
mes to be removed. An iterator is used to step through that list, and the }{\b list::remove(\~)}{ function removes every instance of each name from the master list. Here, the list doesn\rquote t need to be sorted first.
\par Unfortunately, that\rquote s not all there is to it. The messiest part about maintaining a mailing list is the bounced messages. Presumably, you\rquote 
ll just want to remove the addresses that produce bounces. If you can combine all the bounced messages into a single file, the following program has a pretty good chance of extracting the email addresses; then you can use }{\b RemoveGroup}{
 to delete them from your list.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:ExtractUndeliverable.cpp
\par // Find undeliverable names to remove from 
\par // mailing list from within a mail file 
\par // containing many messages
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <cstdio>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <set>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par char* start_str[] = \{
\par   "following address",
\par   "following recipient",
\par   "following destination",
\par   "undeliverable to the following",
\par   "following invalid",
\par \};
\par 
\par char* continue_str[] = \{
\par   "Message-ID",
\par   "Please reply to",
\par \};
\par 
\par // The in() function allows you to check whether
\par // a string in this set is part of your argument.
\par class StringSet \{
\par   char** ss;
\par   int sz;
\par public:
\par   StringSet(char** sa, int sza):ss(sa),sz(sza) \{\}
\par   bool in(char* s) \{
\par     for(int i = 0; i < sz; i++)
\par       if (strstr(s, ss[i]) != 0)
\par         return true;
\par     return false;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par // Calculate array length:
\par #define ALEN(A) ((sizeof A)/(sizeof *A))
\par 
\par StringSet 
\par   starts(start_str, ALEN(start_str)),
\par   continues(continue_str, ALEN(continue_str));
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 2, 
\par     "Usage:ExtractUndeliverable infile outfile");
\par   FILE* infile = fopen(argv[1], "rb");
\par   FILE* outfile = fopen(argv[2], "w");
\par   require(infile != 0); require(outfile != 0);
\par   set<string> names;
\par   const int sz = 1024;
\par   char buf[sz];
\par   while(fgets(buf, sz, infile) != 0) \{
\par     if(starts.in(buf)) \{
\par       puts(buf);
\par       while(fgets(buf, sz, infile) != 0) \{
\par         if(continues.in(buf)) continue;
\par         if(strstr(buf, "---") != 0) break;
\par         const char* delimiters= " \\t<>():;,\\n\\"";
\par         char* name = strtok(buf, delimiters);
\par         while(name != 0) \{
\par           if(strstr(name, "@") != 0)
\par             names.insert(string(name));
\par           name = strtok(0, delimiters);
\par         \}
\par       \}
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par   set<string>::iterator i = names.begin();
\par   while(i != names.end())
\par     fprintf(outfile, "%s\\n", (*i++).c_str());
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The first thing you\rquote ll notice about this program is that contains some C functions, including
 C I/O. This is not because of any particular design insight. It just seemed to work when I used the C elements, and it started behaving strangely with C++ I/O. So the C is just because it works, and you may be able to rewrite the program in more 
\ldblquote pure C++\rdblquote  using your C++ compiler and produce correct results.
\par A lot of what this program does is read lines looking for string matches. To make this convenient, I created a }{\b StringSet}{ class with a member function }{\b in(\~)}{ that tells you whether any of the strings in the set are in the argument. The }{\b 
StringSet}{ is initialized with a constant two-dimensional of strings and the size of that array. Although the }{\b StringSet}{ makes the code easier to read, it\rquote s also easy to add new strings to the arrays.
\par Both the input file and the output file in }{\b main(\~)}{ are manipulated with standard I/O, since it\rquote s not a good idea to mix I/O types in a program. Each line is read using }{\b fgets(\~)}{, and if one of them matches with the }{\b starts}{ }{
\b StringSet}{, then what follows will contain email addresses, until you see some dashes (I figured this out empirically, by hunting through a file full of bounced email). The }{\b continues}{ }{\b StringSet}{
 contains strings whose lines should be ignored. For each of the lines that potentially contains an addresses, each address is extracted using the Standard C Library function }{\b strtok(\~)}{ and then it is added to the }{\b set<string>}{ called }{\b 
names}{. Using a }{\b set}{ eliminates duplicates (you may have duplicates based on case, but those are dealt with by }{\b RemoveGroup.cpp}{. The resulting }{\b set}{ of names is then printed to the output file.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\li720\sa120\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel3\adjustright \f4\fs32\lang1024\cgrid {Mailing to your list
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {There are a number of ways to connect to your system\rquote 
s mailer, but the following program just takes the simple approach of calling an external command (\ldblquote fastmail,\rdblquote  which is part of Unix) using the Standard C library function }{\b system(\~)}{
. The program spends all its time building the external command.
\par When people don\rquote t want to be on a list anymore they will often ignore instructions and just reply to the message. This can be a problem if the email address they\rquote re replying with is different than the one that\rquote 
s on your list (sometimes it has been routed to a new or aliased address). To solve the problem, this program prepends the text file with a message that informs them that they can remove themselves
 from the list by visiting a URL. Since many email programs will present a URL in a form that allows you to just click on it, this can produce a very simple removal process. If you look at the URL, you can see it\rquote s a call to the }{\b mlm.exe}{
 CGI program, includ
ing removal information that incorporates the same email address the message was sent to. That way, even if the user just replies to the message, all you have to do is click on the URL that comes back with their reply (assuming the message is automaticall
y copied back to you).}{\i 
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:Batchmail.cpp
\par // Sends mail to a list using Unix fastmail
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <string>
\par #include <strstream>
\par #include <cstdlib> // system() function
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par string subject("New Intensive Workshops");
\par string from("Bruce@EckelObjects.com");
\par string replyto("Bruce@EckelObjects.com");
\par ofstream logfile("BatchMail.log");
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 2,
\par     "Usage: Batchmail namelist mailfile");
\par   ifstream names(argv[1]);
\par   assure(names, argv[1]);
\par   string name;
\par   while(getline(names, name)) \{
\par     ofstream msg("m.txt");
\par     assure(msg, "m.txt");
\par     msg << "To be removed from this list, "
\par       "DO NOT REPLY TO THIS MESSAGE. Instead, \\n"
\par       "click on the following URL, or visit it "
\par       "using your Web browser. This \\n"
\par       "way, the proper email address will be "
\par       "removed. Here's the URL:\\n"
\par       << "http://www.mindview.net/cgi-bin/"
\par       "mlm.exe?subject-field=workshop-email-list"
\par       "&command-field=remove&email-address="
\par       << name << "&submit=submit\\n\\n"
\par       "------------------------------------\\n\\n";
\par     ifstream text(argv[2]);
\par     assure(text, argv[1]);
\par     msg << text.rdbuf() << endl;
\par     msg.close();
\par     string command("fastmail -F " + from + 
\par       " -r " + replyto + " -s \\"" + subject + 
\par       "\\" m.txt " + name);
\par     system(command.c_str());
\par     logfile << command << endl;
\par     static int mailcounter = 0;
\par     const int bsz = 25; 
\par     char buf[bsz];
\par     // Convert mailcounter to a char string:
\par     ostrstream mcounter(buf, bsz);
\par     mcounter << mailcounter++ << ends;
\par     if((++mailcounter % 500) == 0) \{
\par       string command2("fastmail -F " + from + 
\par         " -r " + replyto + " -s \\"Sent " +
\par         string(buf) + 
\par         " messages \\" m.txt eckel@aol.com");
\par       system(command2.c_str());
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The first command-line argument is the list of email addresses, one per line. The names are read one at a time into the }{\b string }{called }{\b name}{
 using }{\b getline(\~)}{. Then a temporary file called }{\b m.txt}{
 is created to build the customized message for that individual; the customization is the note about how to remove themselves, along with the URL. Then the message body, which is in the file specified by the second command-line argument, is appended to }{
\b m.txt}{. Finally, the command is built inside a }{\b string}{: the \ldblquote -F\rdblquote  argument to }{\b fastmail}{ is who it\rquote s from, the \ldblquote -r\rdblquote  argument is who to reply to. The \ldblquote -s\rdblquote 
 is the subject line, the next argument is the file containing the mail and the last argument is the email address to send it to.
\par You can start this program in the background and tell Unix not to stop the program when you sign off of the server. However, it takes a while to run for a long list (this isn\rquote t because of the program itself, but
 the mailing process). I like to keep track of the progress of the program by sending a status message to another email account, which is accomplished in the last few lines of the program.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776559}A general information-extraction \line CGI program
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776559}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {One of the problems 
with CGI is that you must write and compile a new program every time you want to add a new facility to your Web site. However, much of the time all that your CGI program does is capture information from the user and store it on the server. If you could us
e
 hidden fields to specify what to do with the information, then it would be possible to write a single CGI program that would extract the information from any CGI request. This information could be stored in a uniform format, in a subdirectory specified b
y a hidden field in the HTML form, and in a file that included the user\rquote s email address \endash  of course, in the general case the email address doesn\rquote 
t guarantee uniqueness (the user may post more than one submission) so the date and time of the submission can b
e mangled in with the file name to make it unique. If you can do this, then you can create a new data-collection page just by defining the HTML and creating a new subdirectory on your server. For example, every time I come up with a new class or workshop,
 all I have to do is create the HTML form for signups \endash  no CGI programming is required.
\par The following HTML page shows the format for this scheme. Since a CGI POST is more general and doesn\rquote t have any limit on the amount of information it can send, it will always be used instead of a GET for the }{\b ExtractInfo.cpp}{
 program that will implement this system. Although this form is simple, yours can be as complicated as you need it.
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//:! C26:INFOtest.html
\par <html><head><title>
\par Extracting information from an HTML POST</title>
\par </head>
\par <body bgcolor="#FFFFFF" link="#0000FF" 
\par vlink="#800080"> <hr>
\par <p>Extracting information from an HTML POST</p>
\par <form action="/cgi-bin/ExtractInfo.exe" 
\par   method="POST">
\par <input type="hidden" name="subject-field"
\par value="test-extract-info">
\par <input type="hidden" name="reminder"
\par value="Remember your lunch!">
\par <input type="hidden" name="test-field" 
\par value="on">
\par <input type="hidden" name="mail-copy" 
\par value="Bruce@EckelObjects.com;eckel@aol.com">
\par <input type="hidden" name="confirmation" 
\par value="confirmation1">
\par <p>Email address (Required): <input
\par type="text" size="45" name="email-address" >
\par </p>Comment:<br>
\par <textarea name="Comment" rows="6" cols="55"> 
\par </textarea>
\par <p><input type="submit" name="submit"> 
\par <input type="reset" name="reset"</p>
\par </form><hr></body></html>
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Right after the form\rquote s }{\b action}{ statement, you see
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {<input type="hidden"
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
This means that particular field will not appear on the form that the user sees, but the information will still be submitted as part of the data for the CGI program.
\par The value of this field named \ldblquote subject-field\rdblquote  is used by }{\b ExtractInfo.cpp }{to determine the subdirectory in which to place the resulting file (in this case, the subdirectory will be \ldblquote test-extract-info\rdblquote 
). Because of this technique and the generality of the program, the only thing you\rquote ll usually need to do to start a new database of data is to create the subdirectory on the server and then create an HTML page like the one above. The }{\b 
ExtractInfo.cpp}{ program will do the rest for you by creating a unique file for each submission. Of course, you can always change the program if you want it to do something more unusual, but the system as shown will work most of the time.
\par The contents of the \ldblquote reminder\rdblquote  field will be displayed on the form that is sent back to the user when their data is accepted. The \ldblquote test-field\rdblquote 
 indicates whether to dump test information to the resulting Web page. If \ldblquote mail-copy\rdblquote  exists and contains anything other than \ldblquote no\rdblquote  the value string will be parsed for mailing addresses separated by \lquote ;\rquote 
 and each of these addresses will get a mail message with the data in it. The \ldblquote email-address\rdblquote  field is required in each case and the email address will be checked to ensure that it conforms to some basic standards.
\par The \ldblquote confirmation\rdblquote  field causes a second program to 
be executed when the form is posted. This program parses the information that was stored from the form into a file, turns it into human-readable form and sends an email message back to the client to confirm that their information was received (this is use
ful because the user may not have entered their email address correctly; if they don\rquote t get a confirmation message they\rquote ll know something is wrong). The design of the \ldblquote confirmation\rdblquote 
 field allows the person creating the HTML page to select more than one type of confirmation. Your first solution to this may be to simply call the program directly rather than indirectly as was done here, but you don\rquote 
t want to allow someone else to choose \endash  by modifying the web page that\rquote s downloaded to them \endash  what programs they can run on your machine.
\par Here is the program that will extract the information from the CGI request:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:ExtractInfo.cpp
\par // Extracts all the information from a CGI POST
\par // submission, generates a file and stores the
\par // information on the server. By generating a 
\par // unique file name, there are no clashes like
\par // you get when storing to a single file.
\par #include "CGImap.h"
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <cstdio>
\par #include <ctime>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par const string contact("Bruce@EckelObjects.com");
\par // Paths in this program are for Linux/Unix. You
\par // must use backslashes (two for each single 
\par // slash) on Win32 servers:
\par const string rootpath("/home/eckel/");
\par 
\par void show(CGImap& m, ostream& o);
\par // The definition for the following is the only
\par // thing you must change to customize the program
\par void 
\par store(CGImap& m, ostream& o, string nl = "\\n");
\par 
\par int main() \{
\par   cout << "Content-type: text/html\\n"<< endl;
\par   Post p; // Collect the POST data
\par   CGImap query(p);
\par   // "test-field" set to "on" will dump contents
\par   if(query["test-field"] == "on") \{
\par     cout << "map size: " << query.size() << "<br>";
\par     query.dump(cout);
\par   \}
\par   if(query["subject-field"].size() == 0) \{
\par     cout << "<h2>Incorrect form. Contact " <<
\par     contact << endl;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   string email = query["email-address"];
\par   if(email.size() == 0) \{
\par     cout << "<h2>Please enter your email address"
\par       << endl;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   if(email.find_first_of(" \\t") != string::npos)\{
\par     cout << "<h2>You cannot include white space "
\par       "in your email address" << endl;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   if(email.find('@') == string::npos) \{
\par     cout << "<h2>You must include a proper email"
\par       " address including an '@' sign" << endl;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   if(email.find('.') == string::npos) \{
\par     cout << "<h2>You must include a proper email"
\par       " address including a '.'" << endl;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   // Create a unique file name with the user's
\par   // email address and the current time in hex
\par   const int bsz = 1024;
\par   char fname[bsz];
\par   time_t now;
\par   time(&now); // Encoded date & time
\par   sprintf(fname, "%s%X.txt", email.c_str(), now);
\par   string path(rootpath + query["subject-field"] +
\par      "/" + fname);
\par   ofstream out(path.c_str());
\par   if(!out) \{
\par     cout << "cannot open " << path << "; Contact"
\par       << contact << endl;
\par     return 0;
\par   \}
\par   // Store the file and path information:
\par   out << "///\{" << path << endl;
\par   // Display optional reminder:
\par   if(query["reminder"].size() != 0)
\par     cout <<"<H1>" << query["reminder"] <<"</H1>";
\par   show(query, cout); // For results page
\par   store(query, out); // Stash data in file
\par   cout << "<br><H2>Your submission has been "
\par     "posted as<br>" << fname << endl 
\par     << "<br>Thank you</H2>" << endl;
\par   out.close();
\par   // Optionally send generated file as email
\par   // to recipients specified in the field:
\par   if(query["mail-copy"].length() != 0 &&
\par      query["mail-copy"] != "no") \{
\par     string to = query["mail-copy"];
\par     // Parse out the recipient names, separated 
\par     // by ';', into a vector.
\par     vector<string> recipients;
\par     int ii = to.find(';');
\par     while(ii != string::npos) \{
\par       recipients.push_back(to.substr(0, ii));
\par       to = to.substr(ii + 1);
\par       ii = to.find(';');
\par     \}
\par     recipients.push_back(to); // Last one
\par     // "fastmail" only available on Linux/Unix:
\par     for(int i = 0; i < recipients.size(); i++) \{
\par       string cmd("fastmail -s"" \\"" +
\par         query["subject-field"] + "\\" " +
\par         path + " " + recipients[i]);
\par       system(cmd.c_str());
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par   // Execute a confirmation program on the file.
\par   // Typically, this is so you can email a
\par   // processed data file to the client along with
\par   // a confirmation message:
\par   if(query["confirmation"].length() != 0) \{
\par     string conftype = query["confirmation"];
\par     if(conftype == "confirmation1") \{
\par       string command("./ProcessApplication.exe "+
\par         path + " &");
\par       // The data file is the argument, and the
\par       // ampersand runs it as a separate process:
\par       system(command.c_str());
\par       string logfile("Extract.log");
\par       ofstream log(logfile.c_str());
\par     \}
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par // For displaying the information on the html 
\par // results page:
\par void show(CGImap& m, ostream& o) \{
\par   string nl("<br>");
\par   o << "<h2>The data you entered was:"
\par     << "</h2><br>"
\par     << "From[" << m["email-address"] << ']' <<nl;
\par   for(CGImap::iterator it = m.begin();
\par     it != m.end(); it++) \{
\par     string name = (*it).first, 
\par       value = (*it).second;
\par     if(name != "email-address" && 
\par        name != "confirmation" &&
\par        name != "submit" &&
\par        name != "mail-copy" &&
\par        name != "test-field" &&
\par        name != "reminder")
\par       o << "<h3>" << name << ": </h3>" 
\par         << "<pre>" << value << "</pre>";
\par   \}
\par \}
\par 
\par // Change this to customize the program:
\par void store(CGImap& m, ostream& o, string nl) \{
\par   o << "From[" << m["email-address"] << ']' <<nl;
\par   for(CGImap::iterator it = m.begin();
\par     it != m.end(); it++) \{
\par     string name = (*it).first, 
\par       value = (*it).second;
\par     if(name != "email-address" && 
\par        name != "confirmation" &&
\par        name != "submit" &&
\par        name != "mail-copy" &&
\par        name != "test-field" &&
\par        name != "reminder")
\par       o << nl << "[\{[" << name << "]\}]" << nl
\par         << "[([" << nl << value << nl << "])]"
\par         << nl;
\par     // Delimiters were added to aid parsing of
\par     // the resulting text file.
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The pro
gram is designed to be as generic as possible, but if you want to change something it is most likely the way that the data is stored in a file (for example, you may want to store it in a comma-separated ASCII format so that you can easily read it into a s
preadsheet). You can make changes to the storage format by modifying }{\b store(\~)}{, and to the way the data is displayed by modifying }{\b show(\~)}{.
\par }{\b main(\~)}{ begins using the same three lines you\rquote ll start with for any POST program. The rest of the program is similar to }{\b mlm.cpp}{ because it looks at the \ldblquote test-field\rdblquote  and }{\b \ldblquote }{email-address\rdblquote 
 (checking it for correctness). The file name combines the user\rquote s email address and the current date and time in hex \endash  notice that }{\b sprintf(\~)}{ is used because it has a convenient way to conver
t a value to a hex representation. The entire file and path information is stored in the file, along with all the data from the form, which is tagged as it is stored so that it\rquote s easy to parse (you\rquote 
ll see a program to parse the files a bit later). All the information is also sent back to the user as a simply-formatted HTML page, along with the reminder, if there is one. If \ldblquote mail-copy\rdblquote  exists and is not \ldblquote no,\rdblquote 
 then the names in the \ldblquote mail-copy\rdblquote  value are parsed and an email is sent to each one containing the tagged data. Finally, if there is a \ldblquote confirmation\rdblquote  field, the value selects the type of confirmation (there\rquote 
s only one type implemented here, but you can easily add others) and the command is built that passes the generated data file to the program (called }{\b ProcessApplication.exe}{). That program will be created in the next section.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\li360\sb120\sa60\sl-480\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f4\fs44\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776560}Parsing the data files{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776560}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {You now have a lot of data files accumulating on your Web site, as people sign up for whatever you\rquote re offering. Here\rquote 
s what one of them might look like:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//:! C23:TestData.txt
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {///\{
/home/eckel/super-cplusplus-workshop-registration/Bruce@EckelObjects.com35B589A0.txt 
\par From[Bruce@EckelObjects.com]
\par 
\par [\{[subject-field]\}]
