\par [([
\par 415-555-1212
\par ])]
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This is a brief example, but there are as many fields as you have on your HTML form. Now, if your event is compelling you\rquote 
ll have a whole lot of these files and what you\rquote d like to do is automatically extract the information from them and put that data in any format you\rquote d like. For example, the }{\b ProcessApplication.exe}{
 program mentioned above will use the data in an email confirmation message. You\rquote ll also probably want to put the data in a form that can be easily brought into a spreadsheet. So it makes sense to start by creating a general-purpos
e tool that will automatically parse any file that is created by }{\b ExtractInfo.cpp}{:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:FormData.h
\par #include <string>
\par #include <iostream>
\par #include <fstream>
\par #include <vector>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par class DataPair : public pair<string, string> \{
\par public:
\par   DataPair() \{\}
\par   DataPair(istream& in) \{ get(in); \}
\par   DataPair& get(istream& in);
\par   operator bool() \{
\par     return first.length() != 0;
\par   \}
\par \};
\par 
\par class FormData : public vector<DataPair> \{
\par public:
\par   string filePath, email;
\par   // Parse the data from a file:
\par   FormData(char* fileName);
\par   void dump(ostream& os = cout);
\par   string operator[](const string& key);
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}; ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b DataPair}{ class looks a bit like the }{\b CGIpair}{ class, but it\rquote s simpler. When you create a }{\b DataPair}{, the constructor calls }{\b 
get(\~)}{ to extract the next pair from the input stream. The }{\b operator bool}{ indicates an empty }{\b DataPair}{, which usually signals the end of an input stream.
\par }{\b FormData}{ contains the path where the original file was placed (this path information is stored within the file), the email address of the user, and a }{\b vector<DataPair>}{ to hold the information. The }{\b operator[ ]}{
 allows you to perform a map-like lookup, just as in }{\b CGImap}{.
\par Here are the definitions:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:FormData.cpp \{O\}
\par #include "FormData.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par 
\par DataPair& DataPair::get(istream& in) \{
\par   first.erase(); second.erase();
\par   string ln;
\par   getline(in,ln);
\par   while(ln.find("[\{[") == string::npos)
\par     if(!getline(in, ln)) return *this; // End
\par   first = ln.substr(3, ln.find("]\}]") - 3);
\par   getline(in, ln); // Throw away [([
\par   while(getline(in, ln))
\par     if(ln.find("])]") == string::npos)
\par       second += ln + string(" ");
\par     else
\par       return *this;
\par \}
\par 
\par FormData::FormData(char* fileName) \{
\par   ifstream in(fileName);
\par   assure(in, fileName);
\par   require(getline(in, filePath) != 0);
\par   // Should be start of first line:
\par   require(filePath.find("///\{") == 0); 
\par   filePath = filePath.substr(strlen("///\{"));
\par   require(getline(in, email) != 0);
\par    // Should be start of 2nd line:
\par   require(email.find("From[") == 0);
\par   int begin = strlen("From[");
\par   int end = email.find("]");
\par   int length = end - begin;
\par   email = email.substr(begin, length);
\par   // Get the rest of the data:
\par   DataPair dp(in);
\par   while(dp) \{
\par     push_back(dp);
\par     dp.get(in);
\par   \}
\par \} 
\par 
\par string FormData::operator[](const string& key) \{
\par   iterator i = begin();
\par   while(i != end()) \{
\par     if((*i).first == key)
\par       return (*i).second;
\par     i++;
\par   \}
\par   return string(); // Empty string == not found
\par \}
\par 
\par void FormData::dump(ostream& os) \{
\par   os << "filePath = " << filePath << endl;
\par   os << "email = " << email << endl;
\par   for(iterator i = begin(); i != end(); i++)
\par     os << (*i).first << " = " 
\par        << (*i).second << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b DataPair::get(\~)}{ function assumes you are using the same }{\b DataPair}{ over and over (which is the case, in }{\b FormData::FormData(\~)}{
) so it first calls }{\b erase(\~)}{ for its }{\b first}{ and }{\b second}{ }{\b string}{s. Then it begins parsing the lines for the key (which is on a single line and is denoted by the \ldblquote }{\b [\{[}{\rdblquote  and \ldblquote }{\b ]\}]}{
\rdblquote ) and the value (which may be on multiple lines and is denoted by a begin-marker of \ldblquote }{\b [([}{\rdblquote  and an end-marker of \ldblquote }{\b ])]}{\rdblquote ) which it places in the }{\b first}{ and }{\b second}{
 members, respectively.
\par The }{\b FormData}{ constructor is given a file name to open and read. The }{\b FormData}{ object always expects there to be a file path and an email address, so it reads those itself before getting the rest of the data as }{\b DataPair}{s.
\par With these tools in hand, extracting the data becomes quite easy:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:FormDump.cpp
\par //\{L\} FormData
\par #include "FormData.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1);
\par   FormData fd(argv[1]);
\par   fd.dump();
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The only reason that }{\b ProcessApplication.cpp}{ is busier is that it is building the email reply. Other than that, it just relies on }{\b FormData}{:

\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:ProcessApplication.cpp
\par //\{L\} FormData
\par #include "FormData.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par const string from("Bruce@EckelObjects.com");
\par const string replyto("Bruce@EckelObjects.com");
\par const string basepath("/home/eckel");
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   requireArgs(argc, 1);
\par   FormData fd(argv[1]);
\par   char tfname[L_tmpnam];
\par   tmpnam(tfname); // Create a temporary file name
\par   string tempfile(basepath + tfname + fd.email);
\par   ofstream reply(tempfile.c_str());
\par   assure(reply, tempfile.c_str());
\par   reply << "This message is to verify that you "
\par     "have been added to the list for the "
\par     << fd["subject-field"] << ". Your signup "
\par     "form included the following data; please "
\par     "ensure it is correct. You will receive "
\par     "further updates via email. Thanks for your "
\par     "interest in the class!" << endl;
\par   FormData::iterator i;
\par   for(i = fd.begin(); i != fd.end(); i++)
\par     reply << (*i).first << " = " 
\par        << (*i).second << endl;
\par   reply.close();
\par   // "fastmail" only available on Linux/Unix:
\par   string command("fastmail -F " + from + 
\par     " -r " + replyto + " -s \\"" + 
\par     fd["subject-field"] + "\\" " +
\par     tempfile + " " + fd.email);
\par   system(command.c_str()); // Wait to finish
\par   remove(tempfile.c_str()); // Erase the file
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This program first creates a temporary file to build the email message in. Although it uses the Standard C library function }{\b tmpnam(\~)}{
 to create a temporary file name, this program takes the paranoid step of assuming that, since there can be many instances of this program running at once, it\rquote s possible that a t
emporary name in one instance of the program could collide with the temporary name in another instance. So to be extra careful, the email address is appended onto the end of the temporary file name.
\par The message is built, the }{\b DataPair}{s are added to the end of the message, and once again the Linux/Unix }{\b fastmail}{ command is built to send the information. An interesting note: if, in Linux/Unix, you add an ampersand (}{\b &}{
) to the end of the command before giving it to }{\b system(\~)}{, then this command will be spawned as a background process and }{\b system(\~)}{ will immediately return (the same effect can be achieved in Win32 with }{\b start}{
). Here, no ampersand is used, so }{\b system(\~)}{ does not return until the command is finished \endash  which is a good thing, since the next operation is to delete the temporary file which is used in the command.
\par The final operation in this project is to extract the data into an easily-usable form. A spreadsheet is a useful way to handle this kind of information, so this program will put the data into a form that\rquote s easily readable by a spreadsheet program:

\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {//: C26:DataToSpreadsheet.cpp
\par //\{L\} FormData
\par #include "FormData.h"
\par #include "../require.h"
\par #include <string>
\par using namespace std;
\par 
\par string delimiter("\\t");
\par 
\par int main(int argc, char* argv[]) \{
\par   for(int i = 1; i < argc; i++) \{
\par     FormData fd(argv[i]);
\par     cout << fd.email << delimiter;
\par     FormData::iterator i;
\par     for(i = fd.begin(); i != fd.end(); i++)
\par       if((*i).first != "workshop-suggestions")
\par         cout << (*i).second << delimiter;
\par     cout << endl;
\par   \}
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\} ///:~
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Common data interchange formats use various delimiters to separate fields of information. Here, a tab is used but you can easily change it to something else. Also note that I have checked for the \ldblquote workshop-suggestions\rdblquote 
 field and specifically excluded that, because it tends to be too long for the information I want in a spreadsheet. You can make another version of this program that only extracts the \ldblquote workshop-suggestions\rdblquote  field.
\par This program assumes that all the file names are expanded on the command line. Using it under Linux/Unix is easy since file-name global expansion (\ldblquote globbing\rdblquote ) is handled for you. So you say:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {DataToSpreadsheet *.txt >> spread.out
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {In Win32 (at a DOS prompt) it\rquote s a bit more involved, since you must do the \ldblquote globbing\rdblquote  yourself:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {For %f in (*.txt) do DataToSpreadsheet %f >> spread.out
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {This technique is generally useful for writing Win32/DOS command lines.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776561}Summary{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776561}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776562}Exercises{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776562}
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 1. \tab}}\pard\plain \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls217\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls217\adjustright \fs20\lang1024\cgrid {In }{\b ExtractInfo.cpp}{, change }{\b store(\~)}{ so it stores the data in comma-separated ASCII format
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s43 \f4\lang1024\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 2. \tab}}\pard \s43\fi-360\li1440\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls217\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang{\pntxta . }}
\ls217\adjustright {(This exercise may require a little research and ingenuity, but you\rquote ll have a good idea of how server-side programming works when you\rquote 
