\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {
This appendix is not about indenting and placement of parentheses and curly braces, although that will be mentioned. This is about the general guidelines used in this book for organizing the code listings.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
Although many of these issues have been introduced throughout the book, this appendix appears at the end so it can be assumed that every topic is fair game, and if you don\rquote t understand something you can look it up in the appropriate section.
\par All the decisions about coding style in this book have been deliberately made and considered, sometimes over a period of years. Of course, everyone has their reasons for organizing code the way they do, and I\rquote 
m just trying to tell you how I arrived at mine and the constraints and environmental factors that brought me to those decisions.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc312373824}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776564}File names
{\*\bkmkend _Toc312373824}{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776564}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {file\: names}}}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {name\: file}}}{In C, it has been traditional to name header files (containing declarations) with an extension of }{\b .h}{
 and implementation files (that cause storage to be allocated and code to be generated) with an extension of }{\b .c}{. C++ went through an evolution. It was first developed on Unix, where the operating system was aware of upper and low
er case in file names. The original file names were simply capitalized versions of the C extensions: }{\b .H}{ and }{\b .C}{. This of course didn\rquote t work for operating systems that didn\rquote 
t distinguish upper and lower case, like DOS. DOS C++ vendors used extensions of }{\b hxx}{ and }{\b cxx}{ for header files and implementation files, respectively, or }{\b hpp}{ and }{\b cpp}{
. Later, someone figured out that the only reason you needed a different extension for a file was so the compiler could determine whether to compile it as a C or C++ file. Becau
se the compiler never compiled header files directly, only the implementation file extension needed to be changed. The custom, virtually across all systems, has now become to use }{\b cpp}{ for implementation files and }{\b h}{ for header files.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776565}Begin and end comment tags{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776565}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
A very important issue with this book is that all code that you see in the book must be automatically extractable and compilable, so it can be verified to be correct (with at least one compiler). To facilitate this, all code listings that are meant to b
e compiled (as opposed to code fragments, of which there are few) have comment tags at the beginning and end. These tags are used by the code-extraction tool }{\b ExtractCode.cpp}{
 in chapter 23 to pull each code listing out of the plain-ASCII text version of this book (which you can find on the Web site http://www.BruceEckel.com).
\par The end-listing tag simply tells }{\b ExtractCode.cpp}{ that it\rquote s the end of the listing, but the begin-listing tag is followed by information about what subdirectory the file belongs in (gene
rally organized by chapters, so a file that belongs in Chapter 8 would have a tag of }{\b C08}{), followed by a colon and the name of the listing file.
\par Because }{\b ExtractCode.cpp}{ also creates a }{\b makefile}{ for each subdirectory, information about how a program is made and the command-line used to test it is also incorporated into the listings. If a program is stand alone (it doesn\rquote 
t need to be linked with anything else) it has no extra information. This is also true for header files. However, if it doesn\rquote t contain a }{\b main(\~)}{ and is meant to be linked with something else, then it has an }{\b \{O\}}{
 after the file name. If this listing is meant to be the main program but needs to be linked with other components, there\rquote s a separate line that begins with }{\b //\{L\}}{ and continues with all 
the files that need to be linked (without extensions, since those can vary from platform to platform).
\par Here\rquote s an example of a stand-alone program:
\par Here\rquote s a more complicated example that involves two header files, two implementation files and a main program that requires a link line:
\par Here\rquote s the }{\b makefile}{ that }{\b ExtractCode.cpp}{ generated for this appendix:
\par If a file should be extracted but the begin- and end-tags should not be included in the extracted file (for example, if it\rquote s a file of test data) then the begin-tag is immediately followed by a \lquote }{\b !}{\rquote , like this:
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776566}Parens, braces and indentation{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776566
}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
You may notice the formatting style in this book is different from many traditional C styles. Of course, everyone feels their own style is the most rational. However, the st
yle used here has a simple logic behind it, which will be presented here mixed in with ideas on why some of the other styles developed.
\par The formatting style is motivated by one thing: presentation, both in print and in live seminars. You may feel your needs are different because you don\rquote 
t make a lot of presentations, but working code is read much more than it is written, so it should be easy for the reader to perceive. My two most important criteria are \ldblquote scannability\rdblquote  (how easy it is for the reader to grasp
 the meaning of a single line) and the number of lines that can fit on a page. This latter may sound funny, but when you are giving a live presentation, it\rquote 
s very distracting to shuffle back and forth between slides, and a few wasted lines can cause this.
\par Everyone seems to agree that code inside braces should be indented. What people don\rquote t agree on, and the place where there\rquote 
s the most inconsistency within formatting styles is this: where does the opening brace go? This one question, I feel, is what causes 
such inconsistencies among coding styles (For an enumeration of coding styles, see C++ Programming Guidelines, by Tom Plum & Dan Saks, Plum Hall 1991). I\rquote ll try to convince you that many of today\rquote 
s coding styles come from pre-Standard C constraints (before function prototypes) and are thus inappropriate now.
\par First, my answer to the question: the opening brace should always go on the same line as the \ldblquote precursor\rdblquote  (by which I mean \ldblquote whatever the body is about: a class, function, object definition, if statement, etc.\rdblquote 
). This is a single, consistent rule I apply to all the code I write, and it makes formatting much simpler. It makes the \ldblquote scannability\rdblquote  easier \endash  when you look at this line:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void func(int a);
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {you know, by the semicolon at the end of the line, that this is a declaration and it goes no further, but when you see the line:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void func(int a) \{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {you immediately know it\rquote s a definition because the line finishes with an opening brace, and not a semicolon. Similarly, for a class:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class Thing;
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {is a class name declaration, and
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {class Thing \{
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {is a class declaration. You can tell by looking at the single line in all cases whether it\rquote 
s a declaration or definition. And of course, putting the opening brace on the same line, instead of a line by itself, allows you to fit more lines on a page. 
\par So why do we have so many other styles? In particular, you\rquote ll notice that most people create classes following the above style (which Stroustrup uses in all editions of his book }{\i The C++ Programming Language}{ from Addison-Wesley) but cre
ate function definitions by putting the opening brace on a single line by itself (which also engenders many different indentation styles). Stroustrup does this except for short inline functions. With the approach I describe here, everything is consistent 
\endash  you name whatever it is (}{\b class}{, function, }{\b enum}{
, etc) and on that same line you put the opening brace to indicate that the body for this thing is about to follow. Also, the opening brace is the same for short inlines and ordinary function definitions.
\par I assert that the style of function definition used by many folks comes from pre-function-prototyping C, where you had to say:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {void bar()
\par  int x;
\par  float y;
\par \{
\par  /* body here */
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Here, it would be quite ungainly to put the opening brace on the same line, so no 
one did it. However, they did make various decisions about whether the braces should be indented with the body of the code, or whether they should be at the level of the \ldblquote precursor.\rdblquote  Thus we got many different formatting styles. 

\par There are other arguments for putting for placing the brace on the line immediately following the declaration (of a class, struct, function, etc.). The following came from a reader, and are presented here so you know what the issues are:
\par }\pard \li720\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {Experienced \lquote vi\rquote  (vim) users know that typing the \lquote ]\rquote  key twice will take the user to the next occurance of \lquote \{\lquote 
 (or ^L) in column 0. This feature is extremely useful in navigating code (jumping to the next function or class definition). [ My comment: when I was initially working under Unix, Gnu emacs was just appearing and I became enmeshed in that. As a result, 
\lquote vi\rquote  has never made sense to me, and thus I do not think in terms of \ldblquote column 0 locations.\rdblquote  However, there is a fair contingent of \lquote vi\rquote  users out there, and they are affected by this issue]
\par Placing the \lquote \{\lquote  on the next line eliminates some confusing code in complex conditionals, aiding in the scannability. Example:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li1080\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {if(cond1
\par    && cond2
\par    && cond3) \{
\par    statement;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li1080\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li1080\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \li720\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The above [asserts the reader] has poor scannability. However,
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li1080\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {if (cond1
\par && cond2
\par && cond3
\par \{
\par statement;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li1080\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li1080\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \li720\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {breaks up the \lquote if\rquote  from the body, resulting in better readability.
\par Finally, it\rquote s much easier to visually align braces when they are aligned in the same column. They visually "stick out" much better. [ End of reader comment ]
\par }\pard \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {The issue of where to put the opening curly brace is probably the most discordant issue. I\rquote ve learned to scan both forms, and in the end it comes down to what you
\rquote ve grown comfortable with. However, I note that the official Java coding standard (found on Sun\rquote s Java Web site) is effectively the same as the one I present here \endash 
 since more folks are programming in both languages, the consistency between coding styles may be helpful.
\par The approach I use removes all the exceptions and special cases, and logically produces a single style of indentation, as well. Even within a function body, the consistency holds, as in:
\par }\pard\plain \s24\li360\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {for(int i = 0; i < 100; i++) \{
\par   cout << i << endl;
\par   cout << x * i << endl;
\par }\pard\plain \s25\li360\sa60\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp200 {\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs20\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {\}
\par }\pard\plain \s26\li360\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f6\fs6\lang1024\kerning18\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The style is very easy to teach and remember \endash  you use a single, consi
stent rule for all your formatting, not one for classes, one for functions and possibly others for for loops, if statements, etc. The consistency alone, I feel, makes it worthy of consideration. Above all, C++ is a new language and (although we must make 
many concessions to C) we shouldn\rquote 
t be carrying too many artifacts with us that cause problems in the future. Small problems multiplied by many lines of code become big problems. (For a thorough examination of the subject, albeit in C, see David Straker: C 
Style: Standards and Guidelines, Prentice-Hall 1992).
\par The other constraint I must work under is the line width, since the book has a limitation of 50 characters. What happens when something is too long to fit on one line? Well, again I strive to have a con
sistent policy for the way lines are broken up, so they can be easily viewed. As long as something is all part of a single definition, it should \'85 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776567}Order of header inclusion{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776567}

\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Headers are included from \ldblquote the most specific to the most general.\rdblquote  That is, any he
ader files in the local directory are included first, then any of my own \ldblquote tool\rdblquote  headers such as }{\b require.h}{ or }{\b purge.h}{
, then any third-party library headers, then the standard C++ library headers, and finally the C library headers.
\par The justification for this comes from John Lakos in }{\i Large-Scale C++ Software Design}{ (Addison-Wesley, 1996):
\par }\pard\plain \s49\li720\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \i\fs20\cgrid {
Latent usage errors can be avoided by ensuring that the .h file of a component parses by itself -- without externally-provided declarations or definitions... Including the .h
 file as the very first line of the .c file ensures that no critical piece of information intrinsic to the physical interface of the component is missing from the .h file (or, if there is, that you will find out about it as soon as you try to compile the 
.c file).
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {If the order of header inclusion goes \ldblquote from most specific to most general,\rdblquote  then it\rquote s more likely that if your header doesn
\rquote t parse by itself, you\rquote ll find out about it sooner and prevent annoyances down the road.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776568}Include guards on header files{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776568
}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {Include guards are always used in headers files [more detail here].
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776569}Use of namespaces{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776569}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {[More detail will be given here]. In header files, any \ldblquote pollution\rdblquote 
 of the namespace in which the header is included must be scrupulously avoided, so no }{\b using}{ declarations of any kind are allowed outside of function definitions. 
\par In }{\b cpp}{ files, any global }{\b using}{ definitions will only affect that file, and so they are generally used for ease of reading and writing code, especially in small programs.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776570}Use of }{\b require(\~)}{ and }{\b assure(\~)}{
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776570}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {The }{\b require(\~)}{ and }{\b assure(\~)}{ functions in }{\b require.h}{
 are used consistently throughout most of the book, so that they may properly report problems. [more detail here]
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {\sect }\sectd \margtsxn2707\margbsxn2707\sbkodd\linex0\headery0\footery1440\colsx432\endnhere\titlepg\sectdefaultcl \pard\plain 
\s1\fi-1080\li990\sa360\sl240\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright \cbpat8 \f4\fs96\cf1\kerning18\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776571}B: Programming guidelines{\*\bkmkend _Toc312374163}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776571}
\par }\pard\plain \s15\li720\sa180\sl270\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs28\cgrid {This appendix}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ This appendix was suggested by Andrew Binstock}{\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0
\pndec }\adjustright \v\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {Binstock, Andrew}}}{, editor of Unix Review, as an article for that magazine.}}}{ is a collection of suggestions for C++ programming. They\rquote 
ve been collected over the course of my teaching and programming experience and
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {also from the insights of friends including Dan Saks}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe 
{Saks, Dan}}}{ (co-author with Tom Plum}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Plum, Tom}}}{ of }{\i C++ Programming Guidelines}{, Plum Hall, 1991), Scott Meyers}{\pard\plain 
\sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Meyers, Scott}}}{ (author of }{\i Effective C++}{, Addison-Wesley, 1992), and Rob Murray}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {Murray, Rob}}}{ (author of }{\i C++ Strategies & Tactics}{, Addison-Wesley, 1993). Many of these tips are summarized from the pages of this book. }{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }
\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {C++\: programming guidelines}}}{\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \v\fs20\cgrid {\xe {guidelines\: C++ programming guidelines}}}{
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 4. \tab}}\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls114\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls114\adjustright \f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {Don\rquote t automatically rewrite all your existing C code in C++ unless you need to significantly change its functionality (that is, don\rquote t fix it if it isn\rquote t broken). }{\i 
Recompiling}{ in C++ is a very valuable a
ctivity because it may reveal hidden bugs. However, taking C code that works fine and rewriting it in C++ may not be the most valuable use of your time, unless the C++ version will provide a lot of opportunities for reuse as a class.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 5. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Separate the class creator from the class user (}{\i client programmer}{). The class user is the \ldblquote customer\rdblquote  and doesn\rquote t need or want to know what\rquote 
s going on behind the scenes of the class. The class creator must be the expert in class design and write the class so it can be used by the most novice programmer possible, yet still work robustly in the application. Library use will be easy only if it
\rquote s transparent.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 6. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {When you create a class, make your names as clear as possible. Your goal should be to make the user\rquote s interface conceptually s
imple. To this end, use function overloading and default arguments to create a clear, easy-to-use interface.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 7. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Data hiding allows you (the class creator) to change as much as possible in the future without damaging client code in which the class is used. In this light, keep everything as }{\b private}{
 as possible, and make only the class interface }{\b public}{, always using functions rather than data. Make data }{\b public}{ only when forced. If class users don\rquote t need to access a function, make it }{\b private}{. If a part of your cla
ss must be exposed to inheritors as }{\b protected}{, provide a function interface rather than expose the actual data. In this way, implementation changes will have minimal impact on derived classes.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 8. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Don\rquote t fall into analysis paralysis. Some things you don\rquote t learn
 until you start coding and get some kind of system working. C++ has built-in firewalls; let them work for you. Your mistakes in a class or set of classes won\rquote t destroy the integrity of the whole system.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 9. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Your analysis and design must produce, at minimum, t
he classes in your system, their public interfaces, and their relationships to other classes, especially base classes. If your method produces more than that, ask yourself if all the elements have value over the lifetime of the program. If they do not, ma
intaining them will cost you. Members of development teams tend not to maintain anything that does not contribute to their productivity; this is a fact of life that many design methods don\rquote t account for.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 10. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Remember the fundamental rule of software engineering: }{\i All problems can be simplified by introducing an extra level of conceptual indirection.}{\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0
\widctlpar\adjustright \f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\cs23\fs16\up6 \chftn }{ Explained to me by Andrew Koenig.}{\pard\plain \s34\li720\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\adjustright \v\f4\fs18\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {Koenig, Andrew}}}}}{
 This one idea is the basis of abstraction, the primary feature of object-oriented programming.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 11. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Make classes as atomic as possible; that is, give each class a single, clear purpose. If your classes or your system design grows too complicated, break complex classes into simpler ones.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 12. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {From a design standpoint, look for and separate things that change from things that stay the same. That is, search for the elements in a s
ystem that you might want to change without forcing a redesign, then encapsulate those elements in classes.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 13. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Watch out for }{\i variance}{. Two semantically different objects may have identical actions, or responsibilities, and there is a natural temptation to try
 to make one a subclass of the other just to benefit from inheritance. This is called variance}{\pard\plain \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls7\adjustright 
\v\f4\fs20\kerning18\cgrid {\xe {variance}}}{, but there\rquote s no real justification to force a superclass/subclass relationship where it doesn\rquote t exist. A better solution is to create a general ba
se class that produces an interface for both as derived classes \endash  it requires a bit more space, but you still benefit from inheritance and will probably make an important discovery about the natural language solution.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 14. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Watch out for }{\i limitation}{ during inheri
tance. The clearest designs add new capabilities to inherited ones. A suspicious design removes old capabilities during inheritance without adding new ones. But rules are made to be broken, and if you are working from an old class library, it may be more 
efficient to restrict an existing class in its subclass than it would be to restructure the hierarchy so your new class fits in where it should, above the old class.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 15. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Don\rquote t extend fundamental functionality by subclassing. If an interface element is essential to a class it should be in the base class, not added during derivation. If you\rquote 
re adding member functions by inheriting, perhaps you should rethink the design.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 16. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Start with a minimal interface to a class, as small and simple as you need. As the class is used, you\rquote 
ll discover ways you must expand the interface. However, once a class is in use you cannot shrink the interface without disturbing client code. If you need to add more functions, that\rquote s fine; it won\rquote 
t disturb code, other than forcing recompiles. But
 even if new member functions replace the functionality of old ones, leave the existing interface alone (you can combine the functionality in the underlying implementation if you want). If you need to expand the interface of an existing function by adding
 more arguments, leave the existing arguments in their current order, and put default values on all the new arguments; this way you won\rquote t disturb any existing calls to that function.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 17. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Read your classes aloud to make sure they\rquote re logical, referring to base classes as \ldblquote is-a\rdblquote  and member objects as \ldblquote has-a..\rdblquote 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 18. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {When deciding between inheritance and composition, ask if you need to upcast to the base type. If not, prefer composition (member objects) to inheritance. This can eliminate the perceived need for multiple in
heritance. If you inherit, users will think they are supposed to upcast.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 19. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Sometimes you need to inherit in order to access }{\b protected}{ members of the base class. This can lead to a perceived need for multiple inheritance. If you don\rquote t need to upcast, first de
rive a new class to perform the protected access. Then make that new class a member object inside any class that needs to use it, rather than inheriting.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 20. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Typically, a base class will only be an interface to classes derived from it. When you create a base c
lass, default to making the member functions pure virtual. The destructor can also be pure virtual (to force inheritors to explicitly redefine it), but remember to give the destructor a function body, because all destructors in a hierarchy are always call
ed.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 21. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {When you put a }{\b virtual}{ function in a class, make all functions in that class }{\b virtual}{, and put in a }{\b virtual}{ destructor. Start removing the }{\b virtual}{ keyword when you\rquote 
re tuning for efficiency. This approach prevents surprises in the behavior of the interface.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 22. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Use data members for variation in value and }{\b virtual}{
 functions for variation in behavior. That is, if you find a class with state variables and member functions that switch behavior on those variables, you should probably redesign it to express the differences in behavior within subclasses and }{\b virtual
}{ functions.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 23. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {If you must do something nonportable, make an abstraction for that service and localize it within a class. This extra level of indirection prevents the nonportability from being distributed throughout your program.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 24. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Avoid multiple inheritance. It\rquote s for getting you out of bad situations, especially repairing class interfaces where you don\rquote 
t have control of the broken class (see Chapter XX). You should be an experienced programmer before designing multiple inheritance into your system.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 25. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Don\rquote t use private inheritance. Although it\rquote 
s in the language and seems to have occasional functionality, it introduces  significant ambiguities when combined with run-time type identification. Create a private member object instead of using private inheritance.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 26. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {If two classes are associated with each other in some functional way (such as containers and iterators) try to make one a }{\b public }{nested }{\b friend}{
 class of the other, as the STL does with iterators inside containers. This not only emphasizes the association between the classes, but it allows the class name to be reused by nesting it within another class. Again, the STL does this by placing }{\b 
iterator}{ inside each container class, thereby providing them with a common interface.\line The other reason you\rquote ll want to nest a class is as part of the }{\b private }{
implementation. Here, nesting is beneficial for implementation hiding rather than class association and the prevention of namespace pollution as above.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 27. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Operator overloading is only \ldblquote syntactic sugar\rdblquote : a different way to make a function call. If overloading an operator doesn\rquote t make the class interface clearer and easier to use, don\rquote 
t do it. Create only one automatic type conversion operator for a class. In general, follow the guidelines and format given in Chapter XX when overloading operators.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 28. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {First make a program work, then optimize it. In particular, don\rquote t worry about writing }{\b inline}{ functions, making some functions non}{\b virtual}{, or tweaking code to be efficient when you are first cons
tructing the system. Your primary goal should be to prove the design, unless the design requires a certain efficiency.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 29. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Don\rquote t let the compiler create the constructors, destructors, or the }{\b operator=}{ for you. Those are training wheels. Class designers should 
always say exactly what the class should do and keep the class entirely under control. If you don\rquote t want a copy-constructor or }{\b operator=}{, declare them private.}{\b  }{
Remember that if you create any constructor, it prevents the default constructor from being synthesized.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 30. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {If your class contains pointers, you must create the copy-constructor, }{\b operator=}{, and destructor for the class to work properly.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 31. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {When you write a copy-constructor for a derived class, remember to call the base-class copy-constructor explicitly. If you don\rquote t, the default constructor will be called for the base class and that probably isn\rquote 
t what you want. To call the base-class copy-constructor, pass it the derived object you\rquote re copying from:\line }{\b\f2 Derived(const Derived& d) : base(d) \{ // ...}{
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 32. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {To minimize recompiles during development of a large project, use the handle class/Cheshire cat technique demonstrated in Chapter XX, and remove it only if runtime efficiency is a problem.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 33. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Avoid the preprocessor. Always use }{\b const}{ for value substitution and }{\b inline}{s for macros.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 34. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {
Keep scopes as small as possible so the visibility and lifetime of your objects are as small as possible. This reduces the chance of using an object in the wrong context and hiding a difficult-to-find bug. For example, suppose you have a container 
and a piece of code that iterates through it. If you copy that code to use with a new container, you may accidentally end up using the size of the old container as the upper bound of the new one. If, however, the old container is out of scope, the error w
ill be caught at compile time.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 35. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Avoid global variables. Always strive to put data inside classes. Global functions are more likely to occur naturally than global variables, although you may later discover that a global function may fit better as a }{\b 
static }{member of a class.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 36. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {If you need to declare a class or function from a library, always do so by including a header file. For example, if you want to create a function to write to an }{\b ostream}{, never declare }{\b ostream}{
 yourself using an incomplete type specification like this,\line }{\b\f2 class ostream;\line }{This approach leaves your code vulnerable to changes in representation. (For example, }{\b ostream}{ could actually be a }{\b typedef}{
.) Instead, always use the header file:\line }{\b\f2 #include <iostream>\line }{When creating your own classes, if a library is b
ig, provide your users an abbreviated form of the header file with incomplete type specifications (that is, class name declarations) for cases where they only need to use pointers. (It can speed compilations.)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 37. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {When choosing the return type of an overloaded operator, think about chaining expressions together. When defining }{\b operator=}{, remember x=x. Return a copy or reference to the lvalue (}{\b return *this}{
) so it can be used in a chained expression (}{\b A = B = C}{).
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 38. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {When writing a function, pass arguments by }{\b const}{ reference as your first choice. As long as you don\rquote 
t need to modify the object being passed in, this practice is best because it has the simplicity of pass-by-value syntax but doesn\rquote t require expensive constructions and destructions to create a local object, 
which occurs when passing by value. Normally you don\rquote t want to be worrying too much about efficiency issues when designing and building your system, but this habit is a sure win.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 39. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Be aware of temporaries}{\b .}{ When tuning for performance, watch out for temporary
 creation, especially with operator overloading. If your constructors and destructors are complicated, the cost of creating and destroying temporaries can be high. When returning a value from a function, always try to build the object \ldblquote in place
\rdblquote  with a constructor call in the return statement: \line }{\b\f2 return MyType(i, j);\line }{rather than\line }{\b\f2 MyType x(i, j);\line return x;\line }{The former return statement eliminates a copy-constructor call and destructor call.

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 40. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {When creating constructors, consider exceptions. In the best case, the constructor won\rquote 
t do anything that throws an exception. In the next-best scenario, the class will be composed and inherited from robust classes only, so they will automatically clean themselves up if an exception is thrown. If you must have naked pointers, y
ou are responsible for catching your own exceptions and then deallocating any resources pointed to before you throw an exception in your constructor. If a constructor must fail, the appropriate action is to throw an exception.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 41. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Do only what is minimally nec
essary in your constructors. Not only does this produce a lower overhead for constructor calls (many of which may not be under your control) but your constructors are then less likely to throw exceptions or cause problems.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 42. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {The responsibility of the destructor is to release resources allocated during the lifetime of the object, not just during construction.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 43. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Use exception hierarchies, preferably derived from the Standard C++ exception hierarchy and nested as public classes within the class that throws the exc
eptions. The person catching the exceptions can then catch the specific types of exceptions, followed by the base type. If you add new derived exceptions, client code will still catch the exception through the base type.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 44. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Throw exceptions by value and catch
 exceptions by reference. Let the exception-handling mechanism handle memory management. If you throw pointers to exceptions created on the heap, the catcher must know to destroy the exception, which is bad coupling. If you catch exceptions by value, you 
cause extra constructions and destructions; worse, the derived portions of your exception objects may be sliced during upcasting by value.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 45. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Don\rquote t write your own class templates unless you must. Look first in the Standard Template Library, then to vendors who create special-purpose tools. Become proficient with their use and you\rquote 
ll greatly increase your productivity.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 46. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {When creating templates, watch for code that does not depend on type and put that code in a nontemplate base class to prevent needless code bloat
. Using inheritance or composition, you can create templates in which the bulk of the code they contain is type-dependent and therefore essential.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 47. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Don\rquote t use the }{\b stdio.h}{ functions such as }{\b printf(\~)}{. Learn to use iostreams instead; they are type-safe and typ
e-extensible, and significantly more powerful. Your investment will be rewarded regularly (see Chapter XX). In general, always use C++ libraries in preference to C libraries.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 48. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Avoid C\rquote s built-in types. They are supported in C++ for backward compatibility, but they are much less robust than C++ classes, so your bug-hunting time will increase.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 49. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Whenever you use built-in types as globals or automatics, don\rquote t define them until you can also initialize them. Define variables one per line along with their initialization. When defining pointers, put the \lquote }{
\b *}{\rquote  next to the type name. You can safely do this if you define one variable per line. This style tends to be less confusing for the reader.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 50. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Guarantee that initialization occurs in all aspects of your code. Perform all
 member initialization in the constructor initializer list, even built-in types (using pseudo-constructor calls). Use any bookkeeping technique you can to guarantee no uninitialized objects are running around in your system. Using the constructor initiali
zer list is often more efficient when initializing subobjects; otherwise the default constructor is called, and you end up calling other member functions \endash  probably }{\b operator=}{ \endash 
 on top of that in order to get the initialization you want.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 51. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Don\rquote t use the form }{\b MyType a = b;}{ to define an object. This one feature is a major source of confusion because it calls a constructor instead of the }{\b operator=}{
. For clarity, always be specific and use the form }{\b MyType a(b);}{ instead. The results are identical, but other programmers won\rquote t be confused.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 52. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Use the explicit casts in C++. A cast overrides the normal typing system and is a potential error spot. Since the explicit casts divide C\rquote 
s one-cast-does-all into classes of well-marked casts, anyone debugging and maintaining the code can easily find all the places where logical errors are most likely to happen.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 53. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {For a program to be robust, each component must be robust. Use all the tools provided by C++: implementation hiding, exceptions, const-correctness, type checking, and so on in 
each class you create. That way you can safely move to the next level of abstraction when building your system.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 54. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Build in }{\b const}{-correctness. This allows the compiler to point out bugs that would otherwise be subtle and difficult to find. This practice takes
 a little discipline and must be used consistently throughout your classes, but it pays off.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 55. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Use compiler error checking to your advantage. Perform all compiles with full warnings, and fix your code to remove all warnings. Write code that utilizes the comp
iler errors and warnings rather than that which causes runtime errors (for example, don\rquote t use variadic argument lists, which disable all type checking). Use }{\b assert(\~)}{ for debugging, but use exceptions to work with runtime errors.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 56. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Prefer compile-time errors
 to runtime errors. Try to handle an error as close to the point of its occurrence as possible. Prefer dealing with the error at that point to throwing an exception. Catch any exceptions in the nearest handler that has enough information to deal with them
. Do what you can with the exception at the current level; if that doesn\rquote t solve the problem, rethrow the exception.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 57. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {If you\rquote re using exception specifications, install your own }{\b unexpected(\~)}{ function using }{\b set_unexpected(\~)}{. Your }{\b unexpected(\~)}{ should log the
 error and rethrow the current exception. That way, if an existing function gets redefined and starts throwing exceptions, you will have a record of the culprit and can modify your calling code to handle the exception. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 58. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Create a user-defined }{\b terminate(\~)}{ (indicating a programmer error) to log the error that caused the exception, then release system resources, and exit the program.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 59. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {If a destructor calls any functions, those functions may throw exceptions. A destructor cannot throw an exception (this can result in a call to }{\b terminate(\~)}{
, which indicates a programming error), so any destructor that calls functions must catch and manage its own exceptions.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 60. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Don\rquote t create your own \ldblquote decorated\rdblquote  private data member names, unless you have a lot of pre-existing global values; otherwise, let classes and namespaces do that for you.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 61. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {If you\rquote re going to use a loop variable after the end of a }{\b for}{ loop, define the variable }{\i before}{ the }{\b for}{ control expression. This way, you won\rquote 
t have any surprises when implementations change to limit the lifetime of variables defined within }{\b for}{ control-expressions to the controlled expression.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 62. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Watch for overloading. A function should not conditionally execute code based on the value of an argument, default or not. In this case, you should create two or more overloaded functions instead.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 63. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Hide your pointers inside container classes. Bring them out only when you are going to immediately perform operations on them. Pointers have always been a major source of bugs. When you use }{\b new}{, try to drop the resu
lting pointer into a container.}{\b  }{Prefer that a container \ldblquote own\rdblquote  its pointers so it\rquote 
s responsible for cleanup. If you must have a free-standing pointer, always initialize it, preferably to an object address, but to zero if necessary. Set it to zero when you delete it to prevent accidental multiple deletions.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 64. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Don\rquote t overload global }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{
; always do it on a class-by-class basis. Overloading the global versions affects the entire client programmer project, something only the creators of a project should control. When overloading }{\b new}{ and }{\b delete}{ for classes, don\rquote 
t assume you know the size of the object; someone may be inheriting from you. Use the provided argument. If you do anything special, consider the effect it could have on inheritors.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 65. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Don\rquote t repeat your
self. If a piece of code is recurring in many functions in derived classes, put that code into a single function in the base class and call it from the derived class functions. Not only do you save code space, you provide for easy propagation of changes. 
This is possible even for pure virtual functions (see Chapter XX). You can use an inline function for efficiency. Sometimes the discovery of this common code will add valuable functionality to your interface.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 66. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Prevent object slicing. It virtually never makes sense to upcast an object by value. To prevent this, put pure virtual functions in your base class.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s17 \f4\kerning18\cgrid \hich\af4\dbch\af0\loch\f4 67. \tab}}\pard \s17\fi-360\li1440\sa180\sl240\slmult0\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1440{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnf4\pnfs24\pnstart1\pnindent1440\pnhang
{\pntxta . }}\ls7\adjustright {Sometimes simple aggregation does the job. A \ldblquote passenger comfort system\rdblquote  on an airline consists of disconnected elements: seat, air conditioning, video, etc
., and yet you need to create many of these in a plane. Do you make private members and build a whole new interface? No \endash 
 in this case, the components themselves are also part of the public interface, so you should create public member objects. Those objects have their own private implementations, which are still safe.
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {
