\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart RecommendedReading}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776573}{\*\bkmkend RecommendedReading}C{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776573}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b Thinking in C: Foundations for Java & C++}{, by Chuck Allison (a MindView, Inc. Seminar on CD ROM, 1999, available at http://www.MindView.net). A course including lec
tures and slides in the foundations of the C Language to prepare you to learn Java or C++. This is not an exhaustive course in C; only the necessities for moving on to the other languages are included. An extra section covering features for the C++ progra
mmer is included. Prerequisite: experience with a high-level programming language, such as Pascal, BASIC, FORTRAN, or LISP. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-810\li720\sb120\sa120\sl-640\slmult0\keep\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \f4\fs60\lang1024\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart _Toc451776574}General C++{\*\bkmkend _Toc451776574}
\par }\pard\plain \sa120\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\b The C++ Programming Language, 3}{\b\super rd}{\b  edition}{, by Bjarne Stroustrup (Addison-Wesley 1997). To some degree, the goal of the book that you\rquote 
re currently holding is to allow you to use Bjarne\rquote s book as a reference. Since his book contains the description of the language by the author of that language, it\rquote s typically the place where you\rquote 
ll go to resolve any uncertainties about what C++ is or isn\rquote t supposed to do. When you get the knack of the language and are ready to get serious, you\rquote ll need it.
\par }{\b C++ Primer, 3}{\b\super rd}{\b  Edition}{, by Stanley Lippman and Josee Lajoie (Addison-Wesley 1998). Not that much of a primer anymore; it\rquote s evolved into a 
thick book filled with lots of detail, and the one that I reach for along with Stroustrup\rquote s when trying to resolve an issue. }{\i Thinking in C++}{  should provide a basis for understanding the }{\i C++ Primer }{as well as Stroustrup\rquote s book.

\par }{\b C & C++ Code Capsules}{, by Ch
uck Allison (Prentice-Hall, 1998). Assumes that you already know C and C++, and covers some of the issues that you may be rusty on, or that you may not have gotten right the first time. This book fills in C gaps as well as C++ gaps.
\par The C++ ANSI/ISO Standard. (Availability? Legality of use? Can this be put on the CD Rom?).
\par Large Scale C++ (?) by John Lakos.
\par C++ Gems, Stan Lippman, editor. SIGS publications.
\par The Design & Evolution of C++, by Bjarne Stroustrup
